#include<stdio.h>
#include<vector>
using namespace std;
vector<int> unique(vector<int> l){

Your task is to write the unique function.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is the complete function that sorts a vector of integers and removes duplicates. This function uses no in-built sort or unique function. It utilizes a selection sort algorithm to sort the vector and manually removes duplicates.

```cpp
vector<int> unique(vector<int> l){
    // size of the vector
    int n = l.size();

    // selection sort algorithm
    for(int i = 0; i < n-1; i++){
        int min_idx = i;
        for(int j = i+1; j < n; j++){
            if(l[j] < l[min_idx])
                min_idx = j;
        }
        // swap
        int temp = l[min_idx];
        l[min_idx] = l[i];
        l[i] = temp;
    }

    // removing duplicates
    vector<int> unique_vec;
    unique_vec.push_back(l[0]);

    for(int i = 1; i < n; i++){
        if(l[i]!=l[i-1])
            unique_vec.push_back(l[i]);
    }

    return unique_vec;
}
```
When the 'unique' function is called with a vector, it first sorts that vector using a selection sort algorithm. Then it begins to build a new vector, starting by adding the first element of the sorted vector. It then checks each successive element of the sorted vector, only adding it to the new vector if it is not equal to the current last element of the new vector. This check removes duplicates. The function finally returns the new, sorted, duplicate-free vector.
<|end|>


