I need type hints for the sake of autocomplete in VSCODE. I have following:

# a.py

class A:
    def __init__(self):
        self.B = B(self)



# b.py

from a import A
# I NEED TO IMPORT A HERE SO I CAN REFER TO A IN TYPE HINT

class B:
    def __init__(self, a: A):
        self.A = a


This causes circular import. I have read, that it is allowed to type hint in between single quotation marks, but this removes the purpose for why I need it. It is VSCODE autocomplete. Once A is in quotation marks, VSCODE does not know what it is. I have read about stub files, but these wont work for me, because I would have to edit these files every time I change A class and I plan to have it a large class.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can use the `from __future__ import annotations` to postpone evaluation of type hints. From Python 3.7 onwards, this was introduced to help solving circular imports related to type hinting.

You can use it like this:

```python
# a.py
from __future__ import annotations
from b import B

class A:
    def __init__(self):
        self.B = B(self)


# b.py
from __future__ import annotations
from a import A

class B:
    def __init__(self, a: A):
        self.A = a
```

With `from __future__ import annotations`, Python will handle the type hints as strings and won't attempt to resolve the actual types until it needs to do so (typically at runtime when doing type checking). 

VSCode will still provide helpful autocompletion while typing, and mypy (or similar) will still be able to check the types correctly.

Please note that the `annotations` future import will become the default behavior in Python 3.11.
<|end|>


