Unraveling quantum entanglement commences with a leap back into the early 1930s, in the midst of a fervent debate between two of theoretical physics' most luminary minds - Albert Einstein and Niels Bohr. It was here the notoriety of quantum entanglement was birthed, originating from Einstein's dismissal of the concept as "spooky action at a distance". Bohr vehemently defended it, affirming its pertinence to the quantum realm.

