<|asst|>
Yes, of course. Here's a C++ equivalent of your Python function:

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>

int five_mult_div_seq(int n, int start_range, int end_range, int m)
{
    std::vector<int> ascending_sequence, descending_sequence;
    // Loop for ascending sequence
    for (int num = start_range; num <= n; ++num)
    {
        // Check if any of the number is divisible by 9, 14, or m
        if (num % 9 == 0 || num % 14 == 0 || num % m == 0)
        {
            ascending_sequence.push_back(num);
        }
    }

    // Loop for descending sequence
    for (int num = end_range; num >= n; --num)
    {
        // Check if any of the number is divisible by 9, 14, or m
        if (num % 9 == 0 || num % 14 == 0 || num % m == 0)
        {
            descending_sequence.push_back(num);
        }
    }

    // Combine the ascending and descending sequences
    ascending_sequence.insert(ascending_sequence.end(), descending_sequence.begin(), descending_sequence.end());

    int count = 0;
    for (const int& num : ascending_sequence)
    {
        // Convert number to std::string
        std::string strNum = std::to_string(num);
        // Count the occurrence of '5' in current std::string
        count += std::count(strNum.begin(), strNum.end(), '5');
    }

    return count; // Return the count
}
```

This code mainly replicates the logic in your Python function. There are loops to generate two sequences, ascending from `start_range` to `n` and descending from `end_range` to `n`. Any numbers in the sequences that are divisible by `9`, `14`, or `m` are selected. The sequences are combined, and for each number in the resulting sequence, the code converts it to a string and counts the occurrences of the character `'5'`. The total count is then returned.
