Design a MATLAB subroutine that computes the summation of two two-dimensional numerical arrays, ensuring the parallel processing capability. The function should be able to handle arrays of different sizes, by padding missing elements with zeroes.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In this problem, a MATLAB subroutine or function is proposed here to add two input 2D arrays `Array1` and `Array2` of different sizes, ensuring parallel computing. The function uses `padarray` to extend the size of the smaller array with zeroes to match the size of the larger array. The MATLAB Parallel Computing Toolbox is required to use the `parfor` loop for parallel computing.

The MATLAB function can be implemented as follows:

```matlab
function result = add_arrays_parallel(Array1, Array2)
% Function to add two 2D arrays of different sizes in parallel

    % Obtain the sizes of the input arrays
    [rows1, cols1] = size(Array1);
    [rows2, cols2] = size(Array2);

    % Determine the largest dimensions
    max_rows = max([rows1, rows2]);
    max_cols = max([cols1, cols2]);

    % Extend Array1 with zero padding, if necessary
    if rows1 < max_rows || cols1 < max_cols
        Array1 = padarray(Array1, [max_rows - rows1, max_cols - cols1], 'post');
    end

    % Extend Array2 with zero padding, if necessary
    if rows2 < max_rows || cols2 < max_cols
        Array2 = padarray(Array2, [max_rows - rows2, max_cols - cols2], 'post');
    end

    % Initialize the result array
    result = zeros(max_rows, max_cols);

    % Use a parfor loop for parallel computing
    parfor i = 1:max_rows
        for j = 1:max_cols
            result(i, j) = Array1(i, j) + Array2(i, j);
        end
    end
end
```

The function can then be used to add two 2D arrays using parallel computing as follows:

```matlab
% Define two 2D arrays of different sizes
Array1 = [1, 2, 3; 4, 5, 6];
Array2 = [7, 8; 9, 10; 11, 12];

% Use the function to add the arrays in parallel
result = add_arrays_parallel(Array1, Array2);

% Display the result
disp(result);
```

The `disp(result);` line will output the following:

```
     8    10     3
    13    15     6
    11    12     0
```

Which are the results of adding the two 2D arrays, padding the smaller array with zeroes as needed.
