Conceive a function in C++ language whose sole mandate is to assimilate a provided array filled with integer constituents and arrange them in a strictly ascending progression based on their numerical worth.
unsorted_arr = {45, 21, 12, 56, 7, 47, 34}
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, here is a simple function implementing Bubble sort algorithm in C++ to sort your array. This algorithm works by repeatedly swapping the adjacent elements if they are in wrong order.

```c++
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void sortArray(int arr[], int arr_size) {
    int i, j;
    for (i = 0; i < arr_size-1; i++) {
        for (j = 0; j < arr_size-i-1; j++) {  
            if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {
                // Swap arr[j] and arr[j+1]
                int temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j+1];
                arr[j+1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    // Here is your array
    int unsorted_arr[] = {45, 21, 12, 56, 7, 47, 34};
    int arr_size = sizeof(unsorted_arr)/sizeof(unsorted_arr[0]);

    sortArray(unsorted_arr, arr_size);

    // Print the sorted array
    for (int i=0; i < arr_size; i++) {
        cout << unsorted_arr[i] << " ";
    }

    return 0;
}
```

When you run this code, the output will be:

```bash
7 12 21 34 45 47 56
```

The function "sortArray" works by iterating over the array multiple times and on each iteration, it compares each pair of adjacent elements and swap them if they are in the wrong order. This process continues until the array is sorted. The main function takes an array and its size as parameters and calls the "sortArray" function to sort the array. Then it prints the sorted array.
<|end|>


