Create a basic React application that displays a search bar. When a user enters a query, the application should display all items in the store which match the query.
{
    "items": [
        {
            "name": "Apples",
            "price": 10
        },
        {
            "name": "Oranges",
            "price": 15
        },
        {
            "name": "Bananas",
            "price": 20
        }
    ]
}
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, you can use react hooks to handle this condition.

Here is a simple way to implement a search function in a React app:

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const App = () => {
  const items = [
    {
      "name": "Apples",
      "price": 10
    },
    {
      "name": "Oranges",
      "price": 15
    },
    {
      "name": "Bananas",
      "price": 20
    }
  ];

  const [query, setQuery] = useState("");
  const [matchItems, setMatchItems] = useState(items);

  const onSearch = (event) => {
    setQuery(event.target.value);
    if (!event.target.value) {
      setMatchItems(items);
      return;
    }
    const filteredItems = items.filter(item => item.name.toLowerCase().includes(event.target.value.toLowerCase()));
    setMatchItems(filteredItems);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <input type="text" placeholder="Search..." value={query} onChange={onSearch} />
      <div>
        {matchItems.map((item, index) => 
          <div key={index}>
            <h2>Name: {item.name}</h2>
            <p>Price: {item.price}</p>
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
};

export default App;
```

In this React component, we maintain the search query and matched items in the state using React hooks. By using the `onChange` prop, we can respond to changes in the search bar text. When the value of the input changes, we filter the items based on whether their name contains the search query. If the search bar is empty, we simply show all the items.
<|end|>


