Is it possible to let reflections only influence the colors of an object's screen pixels, but not their brightness'?
Meaning, let reflections be treated exactly like a Material's Albedo (texture)?
 
Rationale:
If a Material's Metallic value is cranked up to 1, all surfaces of objects using this Material become uniformly fully lit, even surfaces which receive little or no light.
There might be a real-world-physics reason for this which makes sense in some scenarios, but in others - especially dark environments - this just looks plain wrong.
I've tried to customize the Standard shader (using the one from 'builtin_shaders.zip') accordingly, but due to close to zilch knowledge about shader programming not to much avail.
 
If anyone can offer any help about this, it would be highly appreciated.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In a physically accurate rendering model, increasing a surface's metallic ratio to 1 implies that it mainly reflects its environment rather than the direct light it receives. This reflection value, called specular reflection, influences both the color and the brightness of rendered pixels. To put it simply, both color and brightness are entwined and can't be separated in this context.

Reflections are essentially a simulation of how light is being bounced off the surface and into your eye (or camera), which inherently includes both color and brightness aspects. This is fundamentally how perception of surface characteristics work, not just in Unity's standard shader, but in any render engine that uses physically-based surfacing.

That's not to say it would be impossible to create a custom shader that artificially separates these values, but it would not be physically accurate, and likely cause more visual issues than it solves.

In your case of creating a more pleasing result in a darker environments, it might be more practical to adjust either the lighting setup or the properties of the material. You could try lowering the "smoothness" property of the material so it does not reflect as much environmental light. Alternatively, you could try other workarounds like using a darker reflection probe or depending on the indoor scene, use additional light sources. If you want a more advanced solution, creating a custom shader that uses different rendering technique might be something you want to consider. 

Starting point for custom shader:
1. https://docs.unity3d.com/Manual/SL-SurfaceShaders.html
2. Shader Graph (More Visual) : https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.shadergraph@10.0/manual/index.html

For a more detailed understanding, you might want to read up on Physically Based Rendering and how it mimics the interplay of light and materials.
https://www.marmoset.co/posts/basic-theory-of-physically-based-rendering/
<|end|>


