For a regression problem, where I'm doing hyperparameter tuning for LGBMRegressor, I was wondering whether I can first tune max_depth and n_estimators and then (in another GridSearchCV round) the regularisation parameters (reg_lambda, learning_rate, reg_alpha)? This will help me save time.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, this is a common approach to hyperparameter tuning. The general principle is that you tune the parameters that have the biggest impact on the model's performance first and then refine your model with the others later.

This allows you to explore the hyperparameter space more efficiently. For example, if a certain max_depth and n_estimators configuration is already performing poorly, it might not be worth it to test all possible combinations of reg_lambda, learning_rate, and reg_alpha for this configuration.

Once you have a "short list" of max_depth and n_estimators configurations that give you the best performance, you can then fine-tune your model by exploring different combinations of the remaining hyperparameters.

This is also in line with the principle of 'coarse to fine' tuning, where one would first tune parameters with larger values, which doesn't need to be quite precise, and then tune the parameters with smaller values or ones that are more sensitive to value changes.

However, it's important to remember that there may be interactions between these parameters. So, the optimal parameter settings identified in each step may not be the globally optimal set after tuning other parameters. But, the two-stage strategy is often a good practical compromise between computational efficiency and performance.
<|end|>


