Let's say you wrote a totally awesome function in C# and want to use it in your PowerShell script. You can do this like so:

$Source = @"
public class BasicTest
{
  public static int AddNumbers(int a, int b)
    {
        return (a + b);
    }
}
"@

Add-Type -TypeDefinition $Source

[BasicTest]::AddNumbers(4, 3)
# output: 7


The question is, how to call this function without `[BasicTest]::` part? Like this:

AddNumbers(4, 3)


What I tried (and it didn't work)

using namespace BasicTest



Set-Alias -Name AddNumbers -Value [BasicTest]::AddNumber
<|end|>
<|asst|>
When you compile C# code in PowerShell using Add-Type, the resultant type is essentially a .NET type. Just like any other .NET object, you will always need to qualify static methods with the class name.

Powershell does not support directly handling compiled C# code without '[BasicTest]::' part. You have no choice but to use it. 

That being said, it's possible to wrap the call to the static method with a PowerShell function. Here is an example on how to do it:

```powershell
function AddNumbers($a, $b) {
    [BasicTest]::AddNumbers($a, $b)
}
```

Now, you can call the function 'AddNumbers' directly:

```powershell
AddNumbers(4, 3)
```

By doing this, you’re not really calling the C# function directly, but you’re calling a PowerShell function that wraps the C# function. This can make your PowerShell code more readable if you are frequently calling the C# method.
<|end|>


