I am trying to find a way to rollback all database (mysql) changes (transactions) once after my tests finished. Not after each test method but after all of them done. I read that `DatabaseTransactions` trait is used for this kind of approach but it rolls db transactions back after each test method. Operation going like:

Run Test1 -> Perform DB transactions -> Rollback -> Run Test2 ... -> Run LastTest


But what I need:

Run Test1 -> Perform DB transactions -> Run Test2 ..... -> Run LastTest -> Rollback


PS: I know I should use different database for testing and can use `RefreshDatabase` trait for it. But in my case I can't use different database. Please don't ask why)
<|end|>
<|asst|>
If you are using PHPUnit for testing, you can use the setUpBeforeClass() and tearDownAfterClass() static methods for setting up and tearing down your environment.

Here's a crude example:

```php
<?php

class TransactionTest extends TestCase {

    public static function setUpBeforeClass(): void
    {
        // Begin transaction
        DB::beginTransaction();
    }

    public function testOne() {
        // Perform some db operations
    }

    public function testTwo() {
        // Perform some db operations
    }

    // Other test methods

    public static function tearDownAfterClass(): void
    {
        // Rollback Transaction
        DB::rollBack();
    }
}
```
This method begins a transaction before the first test runs, and a rollback happens only after the last test runs.

NOTE: Be careful with this approach as PHPUnit run tests in separate process and it might cause unexpected behavior with connections and transactions in some cases. And it should not be used if your test cases are not affecting same data or same connections. It is often more effective to have each test case clean after themselves, either using database transactions or the `RefreshDatabase` trait.

Alternatively, perhaps you could use database snapshots. Snapshots allow you to "save" the state of the database at a certain point in time and then "reset" it back to that state at will. However, its usefulness will depend on how big your database is and how complex are the transactions being made in your tests. This approach will require additional storage as well.
<|end|>


