Construct an HTML webpage in which a square box is showcased, saturated in a dense red hue. This task should be accomplished employing the detailed precision of CSS styling, bringing into play intricate attributes and properties to manipulate the layout and color scheme.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is a simple example of how you might construct an HTML webpage that displays a red square box using CSS for styling.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Red Box Page</title>
    <style>
        .red-box {
            width: 200px;
            height: 200px;
            background-color: red;
            margin: 0 auto;
            top: 50%;
            left: 50%;
            position: absolute;
            transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="red-box"></div>
</body>
</html>
```

In this example, the HTML contains a div element with the class red-box. The CSS in the `head` adds the styling to the square:

- `width` and `height` set the size of the square to 200 pixels. 
- `background-color` sets the square's color to red.
- `margin` centers the square horizontally.
- `top` and `left` position the top-left corner of the square to the center of the page.
- `position` sets the type of positioning used for the square (absolute is relative to the closest positioned ancestor).
- `transform` lets you rotate, scale, skew, or translate an element. It modifies the coordinate space of the CSS visual formatting model. translate(-50%, -50%) is used to perfectly center an element both vertically and horizontally in its container.
<|end|>


