I have a Next.js 13 website running with proprietary images.
Some of them have [IPTC](https://www.iptc.org/standards/photo-metadata/) Photo Metadata but [Next.js Image optimization](https://nextjs.org/docs/basic-features/image-optimization) by default is removing them.
Is it possible to customize Next.js app to keep them or even better to add a predefined set of IPTC metadata on all images of the website by customizing the optimization step?
I tried to look in [Next.js Image Component API](https://nextjs.org/docs/api-reference/next/image) documentation
I see that [sharp can read IPTC Metadata](https://sharp.pixelplumbing.com/api-input#metadata) so I imagine it could be possible to customize the image optimization step to add IPTC metadata?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
As of the time of writing (Next.js version 13.2), there isn't built-in support in Next.js's Image optimization for preserving or adding custom IPTC metadata. The Image Optimization API by default optimizes images for better performance by removing unnecessary metadata and compressing the image. 

However, as you already know, `sharp`, the library used by Next.js for image optimization, does allow reading and writing IPTC metadata. So, technically it's possible, but implementing it would involve extending or customizing the part of Next.js that deals with image optimization, which isn't a small task and may involve "hacking" into Next.js.

There are few other possibilities you can consider:

1. You could use a custom loader with the Next.js Image component. This allows you to handle the optimization and (presumably) attach or keep the metadata you need. The downside is this may involve setting up a custom server for handling these requests, which could add complexity to your app. You can find more information about custom loaders in the [Next.js Image Optimization documentation](https://nextjs.org/docs/basic-features/image-optimization#loader).

2. Serve the images statically (without using Next.js image optimization) from a separate location/server where you control the optimization process entirely. This way, you can use `sharp` directly or any other image processing library that suits your needs. You may lose some of the performance benefits that come with Next.js's Image Optimization, but gain full control over the process.

Remember to keep an eye on the Next.js GitHub and community for updates, as this support may be added in future versions or as a plugin. Feel free to create a feature request in their GitHub repository as well!
<|end|>


