Let's assume you are working with JavaScript & HTML. You would first create your navigation links and then use JavaScript to add click event listeners on each one, ensuring that only the clicked one has the "active" class. Here is an example:

HTML:
```html
<nav>
  <ul id="navLinks">
    <li><a class="nav-link active" href="#">Link 1</a></li>
    <li><a class="nav-link" href="#">Link 2</a></li>
    <li><a class="nav-link" href="#">Link 3</a></li>
  </ul>
</nav>
```
JavaScript:
```javascript
window.onload = function() {
  let navLinks = document.querySelectorAll('.nav-link');
  
  navLinks.forEach(link => {
    link.addEventListener('click', function(){
      //Remove the current active class
      document.querySelector('.active').classList.remove('active');

      //Add the active class to the clicked link
      this.classList.add('active');
    });
  });
}
```
In the above code, on window load we get all the navigation links and attach a click event listener to them. When a link is clicked, we find the current active link, remove the active class, and then add the active class to the clicked link.

Remember to apply the active class style in your CSS file.

CSS:
```css
.active {
    color: blue;
}
```
