I derived the pdf using the butterfly prices and the curve looks like gamma of an option at every strike. [](https://i.stack.imgur.com/A3pBa.png) Is that the case or am I missing something to get the pricing of an option?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In the context of options pricing, butterfly prices are often used as an effective way to derive the underlying probability density function (PDF) of the forward price of the underlying asset. The graphical representation or curve you mentioned can indeed resemble a gamma distribution, but can also assume different shapes depending on the volatility skew and kurtosis in the market.

To be precise, the shape of the curve shows the market's implied probability density function for the future price of the again underlying. It doesn't represent the gamma of an option for different strikes, although the gamma of an option is an input into forming the PDF.

Moreover, even though you have derived the PDF of the underlying's future price, you are still missing some ingredients for the pricing of an option. Option pricing models would typically also require the risk-free rate of interest, time to expiry, underlying price, the strike price and the volatility, which isn't directly observable from the PDF. There are also assumptions related to the behavior of the underlying and the market that have to hold for an option pricing model to work, some famous models being Black-Scholes and binomial tree models. 

The butterfly prices help in gaining insight about implied volatility skew and the particularities of the distribution are priced in options, they are not directly giving you the option prices.
<|end|>


