What is the difference between p(x,y) and p(x;y)?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
p(x,y) usually represents the joint probability of x and y, meaning x and y happening at the same time.

p(x;y) usually represents the conditional probability of x given y, meaning the probability of x happening given that y has already happened.
<|end|>


