Can you adapt this code to Rust?
script
const charCount = (text) => {
    const result = {};
    for(let i = 0; i < text.length; i++) {
        const char = text[i];
        if(result[char]) {
            result[char]++;
        } else {
            result[char] = 1;
        }
    }
    return result;
};
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In Rust, you would use a HashMap to store character occurrences. Here's how you could implement the equivalent functionality:

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;

fn char_count(text: &str) -> HashMap<char, i32> {
    let mut result = HashMap::new();

    for c in text.chars() {
        *result.entry(c).or_insert(0) += 1;
    }

    result
}

fn main() {
    let text = "Hello, World!";
    let char_counts = char_count(text);
    for (c, count) in &char_counts {
        println!("{}: {}", c, count);
    }
}
```

This code firstly creates a new mutable HashMap result. Then it iterates over all characters of the input string. For each character, it inserts it into the map and increments the associated value or initializes it with 1 if it's not yet in the map. The `entry()` function gives a mutable reference to the value for this key and `or_insert(0)` creates a new entry if it doesn't already exist. Finally, the `*` dereferences this mutable reference, so += 1 can increase the value. 

The `main` function is just an example of how to use `char_count`. It prints out each character of the string "Hello, World!" and its corresponding count.
<|end|>


