Modify and debug a given erroneous piece of code that should delete all special characters from a string in C++. Analyze the input string and implement the correct algorithm to remove any non-alphabetic character.

String = "Hello!&@ This#%^$ is a string"

### Given Erroneous Code ###
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>

bool is_special(char c) {
    return !isalpha(c);
}

std::string remove_special_characters(std::string s) {
    std::string result(s);
    std::remove_copy_if(s.begin(), s.end(), result.begin(), is_special);
    return result;
}

int main() {
    std::string str = "Hello!&@ This#%^$ is a string";
    std::string result = remove_special_characters(str);
    std::cout << result << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```
### Task ###
- Identify the issues in the given code and modify it to correctly remove special characters.
- Write a function that accepts a string as input and returns a modified string without special characters.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The main issue in the given code is that it doesn't properly handle the removal of elements from the string when using the `std::remove_copy_if`.

The `std::remove_copy_if` function works by copying elements from one range (in this case, our original string) to another range (our result string) if a certain condition (in this case, our `is_special` function) evaluates to false. Consequently, it doesn't perform in-place removal of characters from the string. 

Also, 'std::remove_copy_if' doesn't actually resize the destination container; it just returns an iterator pointing one past the last element that wasn't removed.

To fix this, you can use the 'std::remove_if' function in conjunction with the `std::string::erase`.

Here is the corrected code.
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>

std::string remove_special_characters(std::string s) {
    s.erase(std::remove_if(s.begin(), s.end(), 
               []( unsigned char const c ) { return !std::isalpha(c); }), s.end());
    return s;
}

int main() {
    std::string str = "Hello!&@ This#%^$ is a string";
    std::string result = remove_special_characters(str);
    std::cout << result << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

In this corrected code, we modify `remove_special_characters` function to use `std::remove_if` with a predicate that defines every non-alphabetic character as 'to remove', and then we call `std::string:: erase` to actually remove the characters. To do this, we use a lambda expression (the parameter to `std::remove_if`) that has the same functionality as the `is_special` function in the erroneous code. The `std::isalpha` function checks if a character is alphabetic. This is a simpler and more straightforward way to achieve our goal: removing non-alphabetic characters from a string.
<|end|>


