Create a circular linked list in Java, where each node stores a unique identifier and its corresponding numerical value, with a specific node capacity. Also, implement methods to insert, delete, and search for nodes by using the reference of the unique identifier in the linked list. The linked list should be thread-safe and support concurrent access.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a Java solution using a ConcurrentHashMap to achieve thread-safe and concurrent access.

Firstly, Node class:

```java
public class Node {
  String key;
  int value;
  Node next;

  public Node(String key, int value) {
    this.key = key;
    this.value = value;
  }
}
```

Now, the Circular Linked list:

```java

import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class CircularLinkedList {
  Node last;
  int size;
  int capacity;
  Map<String, Node> nodeMap;

  public CircularLinkedList(int capacity) {
    nodeMap = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    this.capacity = capacity;
  }

  // Insert Method
  public boolean insert(String key, int value) {
    synchronized (this) {
      if (size == capacity) {
        return false;
      }

      Node node = new Node(key, value);
      nodeMap.put(key, node);

      if (last == null) {
        node.next = node;
      } else {
        node.next = last.next;
        last.next = node;
      }
      last = node;
      size++;
      return true;
    }
  }

  // Delete Method
  public boolean delete(String key) {
    synchronized (this) {
      if (!nodeMap.containsKey(key)) {
        return false;
      }

      Node node = nodeMap.get(key);
      Node p = last;
      while (p.next != node) {
        p = p.next;
      }

      p.next = node.next;
      if (last == node) {
        if (last == p) {
          last = null;
        } else {
          last = p;
        }
      }
      nodeMap.remove(key);
      size--;
      return true;
    }
  }

  // Search Method
  public Integer search(String key) {
    if (!nodeMap.containsKey(key)) {
      return null;
    }

    return nodeMap.get(key).value;
  }
}
```

The `ConcurrentHashMap` ensures thread-safe and support concurrent access to reading the nodes. For all writing operations (`insert` and `delete` methods), it uses `synchronized` keyword to make sure that these operations are thread-safe.

Remember, concurrent modification conflict can still occur at the individual node data, which means this solution is only safe when manipulating the relationships between the nodes. If individual nodes data are modified concurrently, there can be synchronization problems.
<|end|>


