There is a rich scripting model for Microsoft Office, but not so with Apple iWork, and specifically the word processor Pages. While there are some AppleScript hooks, it looks like the best approach is to manipulate the underlying XML data.

This turns out to be pretty ugly because (for example) page breaks are stored in XML. So for example, you have something like:


... we hold these truths to be self evident, that </page>
<page>all men are created equal, and are ...



So if you want to add or remove text, you have to move the start/end tags around based on the size of the text on the page. This is pretty impossible without computing the number of words a page can hold, which seems wildly inelegant.

Anybody have any thoughts on this?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Manipulating the raw XML data for things like Pages documents can be tricky and quite cumbersome, mainly because the underlying data structure was not necessarily designed to be human-friendly, but rather to be efficient for the computer to process. 

One suggestion to manage this kind of task would be to use a high-level programming language library or utility that abstracts some of these lower level details. One possible option could be using a library such as PyObjC, which bridges Python and the Objective-C language that AppleScript is based on. This could allow you to interact with the Pages API more directly, instead of working with the raw XML. 

There are probably other third-party libraries or plugins out there that have been designed specifically for interacting with Pages documents, but you might have to do some searching to find these. 

Another option entirely would be to consider a different word processing tool or service that provides more comprehensive scripting or API options. For instance, Google Docs provides a quite extensive scripting model which can be used for manipulating text, formatting, and other features of a document.

Lastly, it might be worth providing feedback to Apple about this limitation in Pages. If enough users are running into this issue, it might encourage them to improve the scripting model in future versions.
<|end|>


