Firstly, this method won't work if you don't declare it in your AndroidManifest file. Open your AndroidManifest.xml and add android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize" inside your Activity tag which you are using.

```xml
<activity android:name=".YourActivity"
          android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize">
</activity>
```

This method will tell the Android system that you're going to handle orientation changes in your own way, and it will not restart your Activity when orientation changes.

In terms of Kotlin ImageView locking the orientation, you can directly apply the fixed orientation to ImageView.

```kotlin
val rotation = if (resources.configuration.orientation == Configuration.ORIENTATION_PORTRAIT) 90f else 0f

yourImageView.animate().rotation(rotation).setDuration(2000).start()
```

However, given your need to change the background image based on the orientation, there is another approach: use different resources for different orientations without any code. Android supports resource qualifiers, and you can provide different resources for different screen orientations. Here I provide an example: 

1. Firstly, add a new Android Resource Directory in your app module. Named it layout-land.

    * This directory will be used when your device in a landscape orientation.
 
2. Then, copy your current ImageView layout to this new directory and change its settings to adapt to landscape orientation.
 
The system will then automatically choose the correct resource based on the current screen orientation. You don't need add any Java or Kotlin code. 

This is easier to maintain and more in line with the Android development ideology.
