Manipulating SVG with animations requires understanding of SVG properties and CSS animations, transitions, and keyframes. We can animate the SVG to make it look more oceanic by using the `transform` property for rotation and the `animation` property to control its duration, delay, and iteration. Here's a basic way to implement an SVG that gives the feeling of swaying in the ocean currents:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <style>
        svg {
            animation: sway 3s ease-in-out infinite;
        }
        
        @keyframes sway {
            0% { transform: rotate(0deg); }
            25% { transform: rotate(-2deg); }
            50% { transform: rotate(0deg); }
            75% { transform: rotate(2deg); }
            100% { transform: rotate(0deg); }
        }
        
        .bubble {
            animation: bubble 5s ease-in infinite;
        }
        
        @keyframes bubble {
            0% { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(0); }
            50% { opacity: 1; }
            100% { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(-800%); }
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <!-- Add your SVG here -->

    <!-- Bubbles can be SVGs or simply circular divs -->
    <div class="bubble" style="background: white; width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 50%; position: absolute; left: 50%; bottom: 0;"></div>
    <!-- Repeat the bubble div as many times as you want, varying the left and bottom properties to position them and the width/height to vary their sizes. Also vary the animation delay and duration so they increase and decrease at different rates -->
</body>
</html>
```

Please note that you should put your SVG code where indicated. This code provides a sway effect to the SVG like it is being moved by ocean currents. An endless vertical moving effect is also applied to the bubble class. You can adjust the transform degrees, the timings and other properties depending on your needs.

This code does not work on platforms that do not support HTML or CSS, such as text editors or plaintext environments. It is designed for usage in an HTML document viewed in a web browser.
<|end|>


