Devise an Elasticsearch query that not only scours the database for every entry with a "location" attribute possessing a value specifically signifying "Los Angeles", but also sorts these entries on the basis of the numerical value of an "age" attribute. Additionally, incorporate provisions for pagination that allow for the retrieval of these sorted entries in sets of 20. Also consider optimizing your solution for performance.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here are steps to follow to achieve this purpose. Here is the Python code that uses the Elasticsearch module:

```python
from elasticsearch import Elasticsearch

es = Elasticsearch()

body = {
    "size": 20,
    "query": {
        "match": {
            "location": "Los Angeles"
        }
    },
    "sort": [
        {
            "age": {
                "order": "asc"
            }
        }
    ]
}

response = es.search(index="your_index", body=body)

# To paginate through the results, use the "from" parameter
body["from"] = 20  # skip the first 20 results
response = es.search(index="your_index", body=body)
```

You replace "your_index" with your index in Elasticsearch.

This query will return the first 20 documents where the "location" field is "Los Angeles", sorted by the "age" field in ascending order. 

The performance will be optimized if your Elasticsearch data is indexed on "location" and "age". This allows Elasticsearch to retrieve and sort the results efficiently. Without indexing, it might need to scan the full dataset, which can slow down your query as your dataset grows. 

Using pagination with the "from" and "size" parameters will also improve performance by retrieving a manageable subset of results. If you don't use pagination and your query matches a lot of documents, Elasticsearch needs to retrieve all those documents at once, which can be slower and use more memory. It can even cause your query to fail if Elasticsearch doesn't have enough memory to store all the matching documents.
<|end|>


