To make this code work in Rust, you would need to use the AWS SDK for Rust which is in the developer preview phase currently. You will have to pull the latest version from their GitHub repository. Here's how it would look in Rust:

```rust
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::BufReader;
use serde::Serialize;
use csv::ReaderBuilder;
use rusoto_core::{Region, RusotoError};
use rusoto_dynamodb::{DynamoDb, DynamoDbClient, PutItemInput, PutItemError, AttributeValue};

#[derive(Serialize)]
struct Item {
    // Define your item structure here
}

fn main() {
    let client = DynamoDbClient::new(Region::UsWest2);
    let table_name = "your_table_name".to_string();
    
    let file = File::open("your_data_file.csv").unwrap();
    let reader = BufReader::new(file);
    let mut csv_reader = ReaderBuilder::new().delimiter(b',').from_reader(reader);
    
    for result in csv_reader.deserialize() {
        let item: Item = match result {
            Ok(item) => item,
            Err(error) => {
                eprintln!("Invalid row: {}. Skipping...", error.to_string());
                continue;
            }
        };
        
        let item = serde_json::to_string(&item).unwrap();
        let item: std::collections::HashMap<String, AttributeValue> = serde_json::from_str(&item).unwrap();
        
        let put_item_input = PutItemInput {
            table_name: table_name.clone(),
            item,
            ..Default::default()
        };
        
        match client.put_item(put_item_input).sync() {
            Ok(_) => println!("Item {:#?} inserted successfully.", item),
            Err(RusotoError::Service(PutItemError::RequestLimitExceeded(_))) => {
                eprintln!("Error: Request limit exceeded");
            },
            Err(RusotoError::Unknown(response)) => {
                eprintln!("Error: {}", response.body_as_str().unwrap_or("Unknown error"));
            },
            Err(error) => eprintln!("Error: {}", error),
        }
    }
}
```
This Rust code does the same thing as the original Python code: 
1. It connects to DynamoDB. 
2. Opens the CSV file. 
3. Reads each row and converts it into an `Item` struct (which you need to define). 
4. Writes each item to the specified DynamoDB table.

Please make sure you have properly configured your AWS SDKs in your Rust environment. The AWS credentials should be store in your environment variables (`AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` and `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`).

Note: AWS SDK for Rust is currently in developer preview and the above code may not compile due to changes in the SDK in the future. If you encounter any problem, please refer to the latest AWS SDK documentation or the AWS SDK github repository.
