Complete the C++ code below. You'll need to form a function that accepts a vector containing two strings. These strings will only have '(' or ')' characters. The function is tasked to find out if joining the two strings in a particular order could result in a good string. A string is deemed good if it contains balanced parentheses. For instance, "(())()" is a good string, whereas "())" isn't. If a good string can be formed, the function should return "Yes", otherwise it must return "No".

Illustrations:
match_parens({"()(", ")"}) is expected to return "Yes"
match_parens({")", ")"}) is expected to return "No"
The function prototype is:
```c++
#include<stdio.h>
#include<vector>
#include<string>
using namespace std;
string match_parens(vector<string> lst){
```
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a complete version of the provided C++ function to solve this problem. This function works by iterating over each character in both strings. It uses a counter to keep track of the number of open parentheses that have not been closed. If it encounters a closed parenthesis without a corresponding open parenthesis, it instantly returns "No". If it completes the iteration without finding any unclosed parentheses, it returns "Yes". 

```c++
#include<vector>
#include<string>
using namespace std;

string match_parens(vector<string> lst) {
    int count = 0; // Initialize counter for parentheses
    for(const string& s : lst) {   // Iterate each string in the vector
        for(char c : s) {          // Iterate each character in string
            if(c == '(') {
                count++;            // If open parenthesis, increment count
            } else if(c == ')') {
                if(count == 0) {    
                    return "No";     // If close parenthesis with no open, return "No"
                }
                count--;            // Else, decrement count
            }
        }
    }
    return (count == 0) ? "Yes" : "No";  // If count is zero, return "Yes" else return "No"
}
```
This function assumes that the provided strings only contain parentheses, as stated in the problem. The function will not behave as expected if given strings with other characters. Additionally, the function considers empty strings and strings with no characters as valid "good" strings.
<|end|>


