<|user|>
I'm setting up replication for a server which runs a bunch of databases (one per client) and plan on adding more all the time, on my.cnf, Instead of having:


binlog-do-db  = databasename 1
binlog-do-db  = databasename 2
binlog-do-db  = databasename 3
...
binlog-do-db  = databasename n



can I rather just have 


binlog-ignore-db  = mysql
binlog-ignore-db  = informationschema



(and no database to log specified) and assume that everything else is logged?

EDIT: actually if I remove all my binlog-do-db entries, it seemingly logs everything (as you see the binary log file change position when you move the database), but on the slave server, nothing gets picked up! (perhaps, this is the case to use replicate-do-db? this would kill the idea; i guess I cant have MySQL automagically detect which databases to replicate).
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, you can definitely use `binlog-ignore-db` option to exclude certain databases from being logged in the binary logs. This option is often used to ignore "mysql", "performance_schema", and "information_schema" databases which usually don't need to be replicated. 

However, you should be aware of how MySQL handles database selection for binary and replication logs. The `binlog-do-db` and `binlog-ignore-db` options are considered only for the default database, the database that the client specifies when it connects to the MySQL server.

So, if your changes are performed on multiple databases within a single connection (i.e, using `USE database_name` command to switch between databases) or you're running statements that update tables from multiple databases, the changes might not get logged/replayed as you expect. 

If you're going to add databases all the time, you might need to restart the MySQL server each time you add a database, so that you can update the `my.cnf` configuration file. 

You're correct that removing all `binlog-do-db` entries will cause everything to be logged, but you need to ensure that the replicate / slave server is set to read and apply transactions from all databases. You can achieve this by removing any `replicate-do-db` or `replicate-ignore-db` entries from the configuration of the slave server. But be careful: this means your slave will replicate all changes from the master, unless you specify otherwise.

To answer your edited question: Yes, you can use `replicate-do-db` on slave to specify which databases you want to replicate. But you have to manually update the configuration each time you add a new database on the master. There is no automatic way for MySQL to detect new databases for replication. 

In conclusion: While you can use binlog-ignore-db to exclude certain databases from replication, handling replication in a multi-database environment can be complex and requires good understanding of how MySQL replication works. If possible, you might want to consider a different design for your application that does not require constantly adding new databases.
<|end|>


