It seems that anyone can snoop on incoming/outgoing .NET web service SOAP messages just by dropping in a simple SoapExtension into the bin folder and then plumbing it in using:


<soapExtensionTypes>
  <add type="MyLoggingSoapExtension, SoapLoggingTools" priority="0" group="High" />
<soapExtensionTypes>



Is there a way to prevent SOAP extensions from loading or to be asked in my app (through an event or some such mechanism) whether it's ok to load ?

@Hurst: thanks for the answer. I know about message level encryption/WS-Security and was hoping not to have to go that road. We have classic ASP clients using the service and that opens a small world of pain. There are SSL certs on the site running the web service but I was kinda hoping that I could discourage the client from tinkering with soap extensions as they have developers who have some ability to 'play'.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
We can prevent SOAP extensions from loading using the following steps:

1. Modify the machine.config file: .NET configuration can be controlled at machine level using the machine.config file. By modifying this, we can prevent SOAP extensions at the machine level.

2. Configuring ASP.NET trust levels: ASP.NET allows configuring application trust levels through the <trust> element in the web.config file. Setting this to Low or Medium will restrict Web Applications from performing operations like calling unmanaged code and will prevent SOAP extensions from loading.

Do note that these solutions might have implications on other apps running on the same machine, so they should be taken into careful consideration and probably discussed with the system administrator before applying.

For security, the best approach to prevent anyone from snooping on SOAP messages is to ensure that they are encrypted using SSL/TLS. This can be augmented with message-level security using WS-Security standards. If clients are unauthorized to view certain parts of the message, encrypt only those portions of the message.

If the concern is more on unauthorized alteration of SOAP messages rather than just viewing, you can look into applying digital signatures to the SOAP messages. This guarantees that the messages have not been tampered with in transit.

Unfortunately, if your concern is about technically savvy users on the client side, no amount of server-side control will mitigate the risk entirely. It's generally best to operate using the principle of least privilege, granting users (and their applications) only those capabilities strictly necessary for their legitimate needs. Anything sensitive should be encrypted, and you should limit the potential for damage in the event they venture into realms they shouldn't.
<|end|>


