Create a class called “Car” in Java with the following attributes and methods, as well as a custom exception class to handle specific errors.

Attributes:
- make (String)
- model (String)
- year (int)
- fuelEfficiency (double)
- distanceTravelled (double)

Methods:
- accelerate: Increase the car's speed.
- decelerate: Decrease the car's speed.
- refuel: Add fuel to the car's tank.
- calculateFuelConsumption(): Calculate the fuel consumed based on distance travelled and fuel efficiency.

Custom Exception Class:
- Create a custom exception class called 'CarException' to handle errors in the following situations:
  - The year must be between 1900 and the current year.
  - The fuel efficiency must be a positive value.
  - The distance travelled must not be negative.

Include appropriate constructors and class methods for the Car class. Also, provide a main method that demonstrates the functionality of the Car class and handles the custom exception class appropriately.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
```java
import java.util.Calendar;

// Custom Exception Class
class CarException extends Exception {
    public CarException(String errorMessage) {
        super(errorMessage);
    }
}

// Car Class
public class Car {

    // Attributes
    protected String make;
    protected String model;
    protected int year;
    protected double fuelEfficiency;
    protected double distanceTravelled;

    // Constructors
    public Car(String make, String model, int year, double fuelEfficiency, double distanceTravelled) {
        this.make = make;
        this.model = model;
        this.year = year;
        this.fuelEfficiency = fuelEfficiency;
        this.distanceTravelled = distanceTravelled;
    }

    // Methods
    public void accelerate() {
        System.out.println("Car accelerating.");
    }

    public void decelerate() {
        System.out.println("Car decelerating.");
    }

    public void refuel(double fuelAmount) {
        System.out.println("Adding " + fuelAmount + " litres of fuel to the tank.");
    }

    public double calculateFuelConsumption() {
        return distanceTravelled / fuelEfficiency;
    }

    // Input validation methods
    public void checkYear() throws CarException {
        int currentYear = Calendar.getInstance().get(Calendar.YEAR);
        if (year < 1900 || year > currentYear) {
            throw new CarException("Invalid year! The year must be between 1900 and current year.");
        }
    }

    public void checkFuelEfficiency() throws CarException {
        if (fuelEfficiency <= 0) {
            throw new CarException("Invalid fuel efficiency! Fuel efficiency must be a positive value.");
        }
    }

    public void checkDistanceTravelled() throws CarException {
        if (distanceTravelled < 0) {
            throw new CarException("Invalid distance travelled! Distance travelled must not be negative.");
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a Car object
        Car car = new Car("Toyota", "Corolla", 2010, 15, 500);

        // Demonstrate functionality
        car.accelerate();
        car.decelerate();
        car.refuel(50);

        // Validate input and handle exceptions
        try {
            car.checkYear();
        } catch (CarException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }

        try {
            car.checkFuelEfficiency();
        } catch (CarException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }

        try {
            car.checkDistanceTravelled();
        } catch (CarException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }

        // Calculate fuel consumption
        System.out.println("Fuel consumed: " + car.calculateFuelConsumption() + " litres.");
    }
}
```
