<|user|>
I'm trying to ftp a folder using the command line ftp client, but so far I've only been able to use 'get' to get individual files.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The command-line FTP client does not support recursive file transfers. It only allows you to transfer individual files one at a time.

However, using other command line tools like "wget" or "ncftp" can fulfill this task.

1. Wget:

This is a free utility that can non-interactively download files from the web. It supports HTTP, HTTPS, and FTP protocols. You can use it in this way:

```bash
wget -m ftp://username:password@servername/path/to/directory
```

The -m option stands for mirror, so wget will make a local mirror of the directory you are downloading.

2. Ncftp:

This is a free set of programs that use the File Transfer Protocol. You can use it in this way:

First, install ncftp using one of the following commands based on your OS:

- Ubuntu, Debian, and derivatives: `sudo apt-get install ncftp`
- RHEL, CentOS and derivatives: `sudo yum install ncftp`

Next, you can download a complete directory:

```bash
ncftpget -R -v -u "ftpuser" ftp.server.com /local/directory /remote/directory
```
Refer to the man pages (`man wget` and `man ncftpget`) for more options and usage information.
<|end|>


