<|user|>
Create a Java program that stores employee information in a database. The program should be able to add, remove, and update an employee record in the database.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is a simple Java program that uses JDBC (Java Database Connectivity) to interact with a MySQL database:

```java
import java.sql.*;

public class EmployeeDatabase {
    private static final String url = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/EmployeeDB";
    private static final String user = "root";
    private static final String password = "password";

    static Connection con = null;
    static Statement stmt = null;
    static ResultSet rs = null;

    public static void connectToDB() {
        try {
            con = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, password);
            stmt = con.createStatement();
        } catch (SQLException sqlEx) {
            sqlEx.printStackTrace();
        } 
    }

    public static void addEmployee(int id, String name, String department) {
        try {
            String query = "INSERT INTO employees (id, name, department) VALUES (" + id + ", '" + name + "', '" + department + "');";
            stmt.executeUpdate(query);
        } catch (SQLException sqlEx) {
            sqlEx.printStackTrace();
        } 
    }

    public static void removeEmployee(int id) {
        try {
            String query = "DELETE FROM employees WHERE id = " + id + ";";
            stmt.executeUpdate(query);
        } catch (SQLException sqlEx) {
            sqlEx.printStackTrace();
        } 
    }

    public static void updateEmployee(int id, String name, String department) {
        try {
            String query = "UPDATE employees SET name = '" + name + "', department = '" + department + "' WHERE id = " + id + ";";
            stmt.executeUpdate(query);
        } catch (SQLException sqlEx) {
            sqlEx.printStackTrace();
        } 
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        connectToDB();
        addEmployee(1, "John Doe", "Engineering");
        addEmployee(2, "Jane Doe", "Marketing");
        updateEmployee(1, "John Doe", "Sales");
        removeEmployee(2);

        // It's always good practice to close connections that you've opened once you're done with them
        try { con.close(); } catch(SQLException se) { /* can't do anything */ }
        try { stmt.close(); } catch(SQLException se) { /* can't do anything */ }
        try { rs.close(); } catch(SQLException se) { /* can't do anything */ }
    }
}
```

This Java program needs to be in the same local network as the MySQL server and the necessary JDBC driver should be included in your classpath. You also need to replace user, password, and url with your actual MySQL database credentials. 

Also, please make sure you have the appropriate table representation in the MySQL database. This example assumes a table named 'employees' with columns 'id', 'name', and 'department' in a database named 'EmployeeDB'.

Note: This code is for demonstration purposes only and doesn't include best practices like hashing passwords, prepared statements, etc.
<|end|>


