Develop a JavaScript-based React component, simulating an intricate social media platform environment, that efficiently processes and displays an input string as a sophisticated status update on a user's profile, demanding a multistage evaluation process.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
If you want to create a simple social media status update component in React, here's a simple example. However, for a full-fledged application, you need to handle server communication, user authentication, error handling, and much more which is beyond the scope of a simple example.

This example has a `StatusUpdate` component which contains a form for entering and submitting the status update, and a `StatusDisplay` component for displaying the status.

```jsx
import React, { Component } from 'react';

class StatusUpdate extends Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { value: '' };

    this.handleChange = this.handleChange.bind(this);
    this.handleSubmit = this.handleSubmit.bind(this);
  }

  handleChange(event) {
    this.setState({ value: event.target.value });
  }

  handleSubmit(event) {
    event.preventDefault();
    this.props.onSubmit(this.state.value);
  }

  render() {
    return (
      <form onSubmit={this.handleSubmit}>
        <label>
          Status Update:
          <input type="text" value={this.state.value} onChange={this.handleChange} />
        </label>
        <input type="submit" value="Submit" />
      </form>
    );
  }
}

class StatusDisplay extends Component {
  render() {
    const statusUpdate = this.props.statusUpdate;
    return <p>{statusUpdate}</p>;
  }
}

class App extends Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { statusUpdate: '' };
    
    this.handleStatusUpdate = this.handleStatusUpdate.bind(this);
  }
  
  handleStatusUpdate(statusUpdate) {
    // This would be a place where you evaluate and process the status update
    // Depending how complex this processing is, you might want to move it to a web worker or the server
    this.setState({ statusUpdate });
  }

  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        <StatusUpdate onSubmit={this.handleStatusUpdate} />
        <StatusDisplay statusUpdate={this.state.statusUpdate} />
      </div>
    );
  }
}

export default App;
```
This is a very basic example without any sophisticated features or validation. In reality, you would want to check the input's length, sanitize it to prevent code injection, and possibly add more features like mentions or hashtags.
You could also display status updates in real time by setting up a socket connection to your server and listening for new updates.
<|end|>


