<|user|>
A polygonal prism has a base in the shape of a regular hexagon with sides measuring 10cm, and a height of 25cm. The hexagon's sides are all equal in length and meet at 120-degree angles. What is the volume of the prism? Note: You will have to derive the formula for the area of a regular hexagon yourself, the standard library functions for this are not allowed.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To derive a formula for the area of a regular hexagon, we will split the hexagon into six equilateral triangles (the regular hexagon can be divided into six equilateral triangles by connecting the center point to each of the six vertices).

The formula for the area of an equilateral triangle is (sqrt(3)/4) * side^2. In this case, each equilateral triangle therefore has an area of (sqrt(3)/4) * 10^2 = 25sqrt(3) cm^2.

As there are six such triangles in a regular hexagon, the total area is 6 * 25sqrt(3) = 150sqrt(3) cm^2.

The volume of a prism is given by the base area times the height, so the volume of this prism is 150sqrt(3) cm^2 * 25cm = 3750sqrt(3) cubic centimeters.
<|end|>


