Create a program that receives an HTML document as its input and generates a list of all the unique HTML elements in the document that are used for formatting only. Additionally, provide a brief description of each element's usage in formatting.

Note: Consider unique elements as elements with distinct opening and closing tags. For example, if an HTML document has multiple `<strong>` tags, list it only once in the output list with its usage description.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a Python program using BeautifulSoup, which is a well-known library for web scraping, to solve this problem. For the descriptions, we'll use a dictionary of some common HTML formatting elements.

```python
from bs4 import BeautifulSoup

def get_unique_formatting_elements(html_doc):
    # dictionary containing descriptions
    formatting_elements = {
        'b': 'Defines bold text.',
        'em': 'Defines emphasized text.',
        'i': 'Defines italic text.',
        'strong': 'Defines important text.',
        'small': 'Defines smaller text.',
        'mark': 'Defines marked/highlighted text.',
        'del': 'Defines deleted text.',
        'ins': 'Defines inserted text.',
        'sub': 'Defines subscripted text.',
        'sup': 'Defines superscripted text.',
    }
    
    # Create a BeautifulSoup object
    soup = BeautifulSoup(html_doc, 'html.parser')
    
    # Find all tags
    tags = soup.find_all(True)
    
    # Get unique tags which are also in the formatting tags dictionary
    unique_formatting_tags = set(tag.name for tag in tags if tag.name in formatting_elements)
    
    # For each unique tag, print the tag and its description
    for tag in unique_formatting_tags:
        print(f'{tag}: {formatting_elements[tag]}')

# Test it
html_doc = """
    <html><body><h1>My First Heading</h1><p>My first paragraph <b>bold text</b> and <i>italic text</i>.</p></body></html>
"""

get_unique_formatting_elements(html_doc)
```

This script analyzes an HTML document and extracts any usage of the specified formatting tags (`'b'`, `'em'`, `'i'`, `'strong'`, `'small'`, `'mark'`, `'del'`, `'ins'`, `'sub'`, `'sup'`). For each unique tag found, it outputs the tag's name and the description, based on the formatting elements dictionary.

Please note that BeautifulSoup just allows us to parse the HTML. If you're interested in exploring elements that affect formatting in a live environment, you'll need to use a more powerful tool, such as Puppeteer or Selenium, to get the tags after the CSS and JavaScript effects are applied. Other HTML tags not included in this list can also be used for formatting purposes.
<|end|>


