Develop a movie genre predictor utilizing the k-Nearest Neighbors technique, considering movie runtimes and box office revenues as additional influencing factors. Enable the predictor to deal with missing data values, without affecting the overall prediction accuracy. 
[['Inception', 'Sci-Fi', 148, 825532764],
['The Godfather', 'Crime', 175, 246120974],
['The Conjuring', 'Horror', 112, 319494638],
['Titanic', 'Romance', 195, 2187463944]]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This type of problem is beyond the capabilities of text-based AI and can only be handled by a computer with a suitable programming environment. Below is a Python solution using `pandas` for data preprocessing and `sklearn` for K-Nearest Neighbors algorithm.

```python
import pandas as pd
from sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier
from sklearn.preprocessing import LabelEncoder, Imputer

# Our movie data
data = [['Inception', 'Sci-Fi', 148, 825532764],
        ['The Godfather', 'Crime', 175, 246120974],
        ['The Conjuring', 'Horror', 112, 319494638],
        ['Titanic', 'Romance', 195, 2187463944]]

# Create a dataframe
df = pd.DataFrame(data, columns=['Title', 'Genre', 'Runtime', 'BoxOffice'])

# Handle missing values
df['Runtime'].fillna(df['Runtime'].mean(), inplace=True)
df['BoxOffice'].fillna(df['BoxOffice'].mean(), inplace=True)

# Handle the categorical data
labelencoder = LabelEncoder()
df['Genre_Cat'] = labelencoder.fit_transform(df['Genre'])

# Train the model
knn = KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=3)
knn.fit(df[['Runtime', 'BoxOffice']], df['Genre_Cat'])

# Now you can predict the genre of a new movie
new_movie = pd.DataFrame({'Runtime':[120], 'BoxOffice':[500000000]})
predicted_genre_cat = knn.predict(new_movie)

# Translate category back to string genre
predicted_genre = labelencoder.inverse_transform(predicted_genre_cat)

print(predicted_genre)
```

Please note, the above code is only illustrative and needs more data to make effective predictions. Additionally, the sample data does not include any missing values, even though the code can handle it. It predicts the genre of a hypothetical new movie with a runtime of 120 minutes and box office revenue of 500000000. In a real-world scenario, it's important to have robust data preprocessing to deal with noise and outliers, and to fine tune the KNN algorithm (e.g., choosing the right number of neighbors) to achieve the best prediction accuracy.
