Prefixing variable and method names with an underscore is a common convention for marking things as private. Why does all the methods on the page tracker class in the Google Analytics tracking code (`ga.js`) start with an underscore, even the ones that are clearly public, like `_getTracker` and `_trackPageView`?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In Google's analytics.js, the use of the underscore prefix is not adhering to the traditional "private method" convention. Instead, all Google Analytics methods are prefixed with an underscore to help prevent conflicts with other JavaScript code that a site might be using. By using an uncommon naming convention, the likelihood of overriding or being overridden by other JavaScript functions is reduced.
