I am writing a PowerShell script that examines a collection of objects and counts how many times various properties are seen.  In Python, I would use a Counter object, but PowerShell doesn't seem to have a standard counter/bag/multiset object. My need is fairly simple, so I could use a defaultdict, but that also doesn't seem to exist.  Is there a standard way to do this?  Thanks!
<|end|>
<|asst|>
There isn't a built-in bag or counter object in PowerShell, but you can use a HashTable as a simple counter. Here is how you can emulate a counter using a HashTable:

Here is an example:

```powershell
# Initialize Hashtable
$counter = @{}

# Array of items to count
$items = "apple","banana","apple","cherry","banana","banana"

foreach ($item in $items){
    if ($counter.ContainsKey($item)){
        # Item already in counter Hashtable; increment 
        $counter[$item]++
    } else {
        # Item not yet in counter Hashtable; add 
        $counter.Add($item,1)
    }
}

# Display counts
$counter
```

When run, this script counts the occurrences of each string in the `$items` array and stores the counts in the `$counter` Hashtable. You can adapt this code for any object properties as needed. In the end, it would output something like this:

```
Name                           Value
----                           -----
apple                          2
banana                         3
cherry                         1
```

As you can see, it's easy to mimic the functionality of a Python Counter using a PowerShell Hashtable. You check if an item is in the Hashtable using `ContainsKey()`, increment existing counts directly, and add new items to the Hashtable using `Add()`.
<|end|>


