Create a Node.js server that handles incoming requests for displaying the current UNIX timestamp.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a basic example of a Node.js server using the `http` module which responds to all requests with the current UNIX timestamp.

```javascript
// load http module
const http = require('http');

// create http server
http.createServer((req, res) => {
    // write response header
    res.writeHead(200, {'Content-Type': 'text/plain'});

    // write response content - current UNIX timestamp
    res.end(String(Math.floor(new Date().getTime()/1000)));

}).listen(8080, () => {
    console.log('Server running at http://localhost:8080/');
});
```
This script creates a server that listens for HTTP requests on port 8080. Every time a request is received, a callback function is invoked which writes the HTTP 200 response header (indicating a successful request), and then writes the current UNIX timestamp in the response body.

To run this, you'll need to have Node.js installed on your machine, save this script to a .js file, navigate to the directory containing this file in your terminal, and type `node filename.js`. The server will start, and you can navigate to `http://localhost:8080/` in a web browser to view the current UNIX timestamp.

Remember, UNIX timestamp is the number of seconds that have passed since Thursday, January 1, 1970 00:00:00 UTC minus the leap seconds. Because of this, it's a widely used time format in computing.

Also note that the port number (8080 in this example) can be any valid port number, but if you choose a port number below 1024, you may need special permissions to start the server.
<|end|>


