Combine all of the given numbers in an array using the reduce() method in JavaScript.
numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can use the reduce() method to combine all of the numbers in an array by adding them together in JavaScript. The reduce() method applies a function against an accumulator and each element in the array (from left to right) to reduce it to a single output value.

Here is a simple script that will do this:

```javascript
let numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]

let sum = numbers.reduce(function(accumulator, currentValue) {
  return accumulator + currentValue;
});

console.log(sum)  // it will log 15
```

In the script above, the reduce() function will take each value from the array one by one (from left to right). For each value, it will execute the function that adds the current value to the accumulator. The result of this function will be saved in the accumulator for the next iteration or will be returned if there are no more values in the array. The initial value of the accumulator is the first element in the array if no initial value is provided.

This script can also be written with arrow function as:

```javascript
let numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]

let sum = numbers.reduce((accumulator, currentValue) => accumulator + currentValue);

console.log(sum)  // it will log 15
```
<|end|>


