<|asst|>
Engineering a RESTful API exploiting Node.js technology for the propagation of real-time data regarding ambient temperature and relative humidity based on user-specified geographical coordinates is a complex task. For the purpose of this, we will use Express, a popular web application framework for Node.js, and MongoDB, a popular NoSQL database.

Here's a basic version of what that could look like:

```
const express = require('express');
const mongoose = require('mongoose');

const app = express();

// Connect to MongoDB
mongoose.connect("mongodb://localhost/climateData", { useNewUrlParser: true, useUnifiedTopology: true });

// Data Model
const ClimateData = mongoose.model('ClimateData', {
   lat: Number,
   lon: Number,
   temperature: Number,
   humidity: Number
});

// Middleware
app.use(express.json());

// Routes
app.post('/data', async (req, res) => {
   // Error handling and data feed consistency required here.
   try {
      const data = new ClimateData(req.body);
      await data.save();
      res.status(200).send({ message: 'Data received and stored' });
   } catch(error) {
      res.status(500).send(error);
   }
});

app.get('/data', async (req, res) => {
   try {
      if (!req.query.lat || !req.query.lon) {
         res.status(400).send({ message: 'Request must include lat and lon params' });
         return;
      }
      const data = await ClimateData.find({
         lat: req.query.lat,
         lon: req.query.lon,
      });
      res.status(200).send(data);
   } catch (error) {
      res.status(500).send(error);
   }
});

// Start Server
app.listen(3000, () => {
   console.log('Server is running on port 3000');
});
```
This example assumes that you have a MongoDB instance running locally and that the data being sent and stored includes the latitude, longitude, temperature, and humidity. Data is sent to `/data` as a POST request and is retrieved with a GET request to the same endpoint. Note that you would also need appropriate error handling and protections in place, especially for production-level code.

As it is indicated, this is a simplified version. Further implementation can include validation for data consistency, additional error handling, tests, authentication for data posting, among others.

For example, the data object received from the POST request could include more properties, or the latitude, longitude, temperature, and humidity values might need validation to verify they're within appropriate ranges. Also, the GET request could potentially return multiple sets of climate data if the same lat/lon has been used multiple times, so you may want to limit it to the most recent or provide a way for users to specify the time frame they're interested in.

Remember, in a final implementation each of these details should be well planned and thoroughly tested.
