Develop a Python program that identifies the most frequently occurring word and its synonym (using a provided synonym dictionary) in a given textual sequence. The word should not only have the highest count in the text but also the combined count of the word and its synonym should exceed other word counts. The program should also print out the frequency count of the word and its synonym separately. If more than one word has the same highest frequency, return all such words, including their synonyms. 

Your solution should account for case sensitivity and punctuation. Your solution should handle for text containing up to 1 million words. 

This is your first sentence of a sample text.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a Python solution that uses built-in Python libraries, including collections for frequency counts, re for regex operations, and pandas to handle data operations:

```python
import collections
import re
import pandas as pd

def find_most_frequent_synonym(text, synonym_dict):
    # Tokenize words, account for punctuation and case sensitivity
    words = re.findall(r'\b\w+\b', text.lower())

    # Get frequency of each word
    word_counts = dict(collections.Counter(words))

    # convert word_counts and synonym_dict into DataFrames
    word_counts_df = pd.DataFrame(list(word_counts.items()), columns=["word", "count"])
    synonym_df = pd.DataFrame(list(synonym_dict.items()), columns=["word", "synonym"])

    # merge word_counts_df and synonym_df 
    merged_df = pd.merge(word_counts_df, synonym_df, on="word").set_index("word")

    # calculate combined count with synonyms
    for word in merged_df.index:
        synonym = merged_df.at[word, 'synonym']
        if synonym in word_counts:
            merged_df.at[word, 'count'] += word_counts[synonym]

    # find synonym/s having the max combined count
    max_count = merged_df['count'].max()
    most_frequent_words = merged_df[merged_df['count'] == max_count]

    # print out the frequency count of the word and its synonym separately
    for word, row in most_frequent_words.iterrows():
        print(f"The word '{word}' (synonym: '{row['synonym']}') has the highest combined frequency of {row['count']} in the text. The word '{word}' has a frequency count of {word_counts[word]} and its synonym '{row['synonym']}' has a frequency count of {word_counts[row['synonym']]}.")

synonym_dict = {'quick': 'fast', 'lazy': 'idle', 'dog': 'hound'}
text = "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog. The fast brown fox jumps over the lazy hound."

find_most_frequent_synonym(text, synonym_dict)
```

You can replace `text` and `synonym_dict` in the last two lines with your text and synonym dictionary, respectively.

This solution meets the problem's requirement of handling a text containing up to 1 million words provided enough memory available. As mainly vectorized operations are used, it's relatively efficient. It has a time complexity of O(n) where n is the number of words in the text.

Please note, in regular cases, a word should not be a synonym to itself, but above hardcoded example has such cases just for demonstration. If your synonym dictionary doesn't have such cases, the function doesn't need to be changed and would still work as intended.
<|end|>


