As an AI, I can't code directly, but I will give you a detailed guide on how to create a RESTful API using Flask, one of the most popular Python web microframeworks, and deploy it with Google Cloud Endpoints. This integration will streamline authentication, monitoring, and logging.

Step 1: Install dependencies.

`pip install flask flask-restful google-auth google-auth-oauthlib google-auth-httplib2`

Step 2: Construct a RESTful API using Flask and Flask-RESTful.

```Python
from flask import Flask
from flask_restful import Resource, Api

app = Flask(__name__)
api = Api(app)

class HelloWorld(Resource):
    def get(self):
        return {'hello': 'world'}

api.add_resource(HelloWorld, '/')

if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.run(debug=True)
```
This is a very basic example of how to create an API endpoint with a single GET method which returns 'Hello, World!' when accessing the root URL.

Step 3: Deploy it by using Google Cloud Endpoints.

1. Create a project in Google Cloud Console and enable Cloud Endpoints and Cloud Deployment Manager APIs.
2. Install and initialize Google Cloud SDK.
3. Spin up a server using Google App Engine to host your application.
4. Create an OpenAPI specification document `openapi.yaml` for your API.
5. Deploy your API configuration using `gcloud endpoints services deploy openapi.yaml`.
6. Deploy your API using `gcloud app deploy`.

With Google Cloud Endpoints, you will gain the benefits of features such as authentication and access control, monitoring and logging, quota management, and developer portal services.

For handling multi-step reasoning and complex problem solving, you may add more routes implementing different logic and problem solving processes. Also remember, depending on complexity of requirements, you may want to integrate databases, automated tasks (Celery or RQ), user authentication, and more.

This is a simplified guide, and creating a production-grade and robust API would require handling many more concerns like error handling, security, rate-limiting, caching etc.
