Interpret the provided XML document and alter the numeric value of a specific element by combining the integer values of 2 other elements. Here's the XML document:
<root>
  <element1>10</element1>
  <element2>20</element2>
  <element3>0</element3>
</root>
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You want to alter the value of element3 by combining (I assume you mean adding) the values of element1 and element2. This can be done using Python's xml.etree.ElementTree module, which provides functionality for parsing and creating XML documents.

Here's the code to do it:

```python
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET

xml_data = """
<root>
  <element1>10</element1>
  <element2>20</element2>
  <element3>0</element3>
</root>
"""

# parse the XML document into an element tree
root = ET.fromstring(xml_data)

# find the elements we're interested in
element1 = root.find('element1')
element2 = root.find('element2')
element3 = root.find('element3')

# combine the values of element1 and element2 and alter the value of element3
element3.text = str(int(element1.text) + int(element2.text))

# print the modified XML document
ET.dump(root)
```

This will output:

```xml
<root>
  <element1>10</element1>
  <element2>20</element2>
  <element3>30</element3>
</root>
```

If the XML data is in a file instead of a string, you can use ET.parse instead of ET.fromstring to read and parse the file. If you need to write the modified XML tree back to a file, you can use ET.ElementTree(root).write(filename).
<|end|>


