<|end|>
<|asst|>
The AWS environment can be quite versatile and adaptable depending on a project's needs. To host a scalable WordPress site, we can use the following setup:

1. Amazon EC2 (Elastic Compute Cloud): Launch EC2 instances which serves as virtual servers to run applications on AWS. Enable Auto Scaling feature to automatically adjust capacity to maintain steady and predictable performance.

2. Amazon RDS (Relational Database Service) for MySQL: Store all your WordPress data in managed relational database suitable for WordPress. 

3. Amazon S3 (Simple Storage Service): Use this for backup and recovery processes. 

4. Amazon CloudFront: Deploy this Content Delivery Network to cache content and deliver it to your users from the nearest geographical location.

5. ELB (Elastic Load Balancer): Distribute incoming application traffic across multiple targets, such as Amazon EC2 instances.

6. Amazon Route 53: For reliable and cost-effective domain name system (DNS) web service.

For budget monitoring and optimization:

1. Use AWS Cost Explorer to analyze your costs and usage. It provides default reports that help you understand your costs, and it allows you to create custom reports.

2. Enable AWS Budgets which gives you the ability to set custom cost and usage budgets that alert you when your costs or usage exceed (or are forecasted to exceed) your budgeted amount.

3. Use "Savings Plans" and "Reserved Instances" for cost saving on longer-term workloads.

4. Regularly review and terminate unused instances, clean up unused Amazon EBS volumes and snapshots.

Code or specific architecture can’t be provided for such a setup as it requires using the AWS Management Console or AWS CLI (Command Line Interface) tool for setup and configurations. The above-mentioned pointers should give a good start to tailor an AWS environment for WordPress hosting with scalability and cost efficiency. 

Always ensure you follow AWS Well-Architected Framework to build secure, high-performing, resilient, and efficient infrastructure for your WordPress application.
