Here's a fairly simple solution in C#:

We'll first take an input integer from the user. Then we'll use two nested loops to generate the multiplication table. 

Please note that you will need to have user interaction to receive the input, that part is not covered in this algorithm and strongly depends on the type of application (Console, WFP, Asp.Net, etc).

```csharp
using System;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        Console.Write("Enter the number for the multiplication table: ");
        if (int.TryParse(Console.ReadLine(), out int number) && number > 0)
        {
            GenerateAndDisplayMultiplicationTable(number);
        }
        else
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Please enter a valid positive integer number.");
        }
    }

    public static void GenerateAndDisplayMultiplicationTable(int number)
    {
        if(number > 0)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"\nMultiplication Table For {number}:\n");
            for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++)
            {
                for (int j = 1; j <= number; j++)
                {
                    Console.Write("{0}x{1}={2}\t", j, i, (j * i));
                }
                Console.WriteLine();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This code will generate a multiplication table for the numbers from 1 to the number entered by the user. Essentially, the table will be a grid where the value at each cell is the product of its row and column number.

Please note that the upper limit for the multiplication table is 10 (since typical school multiplication tables go up to 10), but you can increase this or make it dynamic too, in the event the user wants a larger table.

Also, always ensure to validate user inputs in real scenarios and handle exceptions to avoid crashes.
