Write a MySQL query to find the top three students with the highest grades in the class. Additionally, if the top three students have the same average grade, organize them alphabetically by their last name. If they have the same last name as well, sort them by their first names.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Assuming there is a table named `students` with columns `first_name`, `last_name`, and `grade`.

The SQL query would be:

```sql
SELECT first_name, last_name, grade
FROM students
ORDER BY grade DESC, last_name ASC, first_name ASC
LIMIT 3;
```

This query sorts by `grade` in a descending order, so students with higher grades come first. If two students have the same grade, it then sorts by `last_name` in ascending order. If they also have the same last name, it sorts by their `first_name` in ascending order. The `LIMIT 3` clause then restricts the output to the top 3 rows (students) in the sorted list.
<|end|>


