Develop a complex SQL query to retrieve all the customer data with the customer age more than 25 years and have made purchases within the last year. Furthermore, prioritize customers who have spent more than 1000 USD, grouping them by their spending categories and displaying the total and average amount spent per spending category.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a complex SQL query based on your requirements assuming hypothetical table names and relationships:

```sql
SELECT c.*, s.category, COUNT(p.id) AS purchase_count, SUM(p.amount) AS total_spent, AVG(p.amount) AS average_spent
FROM Customers c
JOIN Purchases p ON c.id = p.customer_id
JOIN SpendingCategories s ON p.category_id = s.id
WHERE YEAR(CURDATE()) - YEAR(c.date_of_birth) > 25 AND p.purchase_date > DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 1 YEAR)
GROUP BY c.id, s.category
HAVING total_spent > 1000
ORDER BY total_spent DESC;
```

Here is the table structure:

- Customers (`id`, `name`, `date_of_birth`, ...)
- Purchases (`id`, `customer_id`, `category_id`, `purchase_date`, `amount`, ...)
- SpendingCategories (`id`, `category`, ...)

Explanation of the Query:

- Join Customers, SpendingCategories, and Purchases tables.
- Filter out customers who are 25 years old and have made purchases within the last year.
- Group the result by customer id and spending category.
- Calculate the total amount spent and average amount spent per customer per spending category.
- Filter out groups where the total amount spent is more than 1000 USD.
- Order the result by the total amount spent in descending order.

Please modify the table names, column names, and relationships according to your database structure.
<|end|>


