I have data that very unclear, but I have been tidying it up. I am now in a situation where every two rows is the complete data and should be one row, but each row contains NAs and no key.
an example:

df <- data.frame(Name=c("Steve",NA,"Jim",NA,"Patty",NA),Age=c(NA,26,NA,41,NA,37),Gender=c(NA,"M",NA,"M",NA,"F"))


So we see something like:
|Name...Age...Gender
|
|Steve....NA...... NA
|NA........ 26........ M
But what I need is
Steve 26 M
So the data is there, but I am not sure how to fill it down...I could possible fill down the Name column where is NA. Any help would be greatly appreciated
<|end|>
<|asst|>
One way to solve this is by using the "tidyverse", particularly "dplyr" and "zoo" packages in R.

```R
library(dplyr)
library(zoo)

df <- data.frame(Name=c("Steve",NA,"Jim",NA,"Patty",NA),Age=c(NA,26,NA,41,NA,37),Gender=c(NA,"M",NA,"M",NA,"F"))

df %>%
  mutate(Name = zoo::na.locf(Name)) %>%
  filter(!is.na(Age)) %>%
  select(Name, Age, Gender)
```
Here are the steps:
1. We are using mutate() from dplyr to change the Name column. 
2. The na.locf() function from the zoo package is used to fill NA values with the most recent non-NA prior value.
3. We then filter out the rows where Age is NA since these are the second rows that have been filled with the Name information but Now do not contain any new data.
4. Finally, we use select to order the columns.
<|end|>


