    return 0;
}
```

This program generates a random number and prints it. The random number is generated by `std::rand()` function provided by `cstdlib` library. `std::srand(std::time(0))` is used to ensure that we don't get the same sequence of random numbers on every program run. This statement sets the seed of the random number generator algorithm, and the value of the seed is got from the system time (thus ensuring different seeds on each run).

If you want a random number within a specific range, you can use the modulus operator (%) :

```C++
// generate random number between 1 and 100
int random_number = (std::rand() % 100) + 1;
```
In this case, `(std::rand() % 100)` generates a random number between 0 and 99. By adding 1 we get a number between 1 and 100.
