I have generated results for Cox proportional hazards model, and Fine and Gray competing risk model, which gives me the hazard ratio and sub-distributional hazard ratio for each of the independent variables. Is there a way to compute the cumulative incidence function for each of the independent variables from the estimated hazard ratios?
I found a [reference](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11657-020-00748-x), where they have compared the cumulative incidence of re-fracture using the Kaplan-Meier model and the Fine-Gray model. I am also trying to figure out how they calculated it.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The cumulative incidence function (CIF) for each variable cannot be computed solely based on the estimated hazard ratios. The hazard ratio is a measure of the relative risk of an event occurring given an individual's status on a particular independent variable. However, it doesn't provide information regarding the overall proportion of individuals who experience the event over time, as the CIF does.

In the Fine and Gray competing risk model, estimating the cumulative incidence function relies on data about the time to the event and censoring, as well as the status of the independent variables. The Fine and Gray model estimates the cumulative incidence function in the presence of competing risks by integrating the subdistribution hazard over the time period of interest.

To visualize CIF in the context of Fine and Gray model, the approach is commonly to estimate and plot CIFs by subject's status on the independent variable of interest (like treatment group). This requires not only the subdistribution hazard ratio but also the raw data.

In the reference you've provided, the authors calculated cumulative incidence using both the standard Kaplan-Meier estimator and the Fine-Gray model for their particular dataset, which includes not only the status of each patient's fractures but also the time-to-event (or censoring). They likely plotted cumulative incidence curves for different levels of important variables like age group, sex, and bone mineral density.

In conclusion, to perform similar analyses, you'll need to have available (or generate) more than just the hazard ratios; you'll also need your original survival data, which includes the status of independent variable of interest and outcome data (time to event or censoring). Also, software like R with packages like "survival" or "cmprsk" could be useful for performing such analyses.
<|end|>


