Develop a Python script that outputs every single day within the standard Gregorian weekly framework.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, this can be done using the built-in `datetime` and `timedelta` modules in Python. Here is a simple script:

```python
from datetime import datetime, timedelta

# start from today
start_date = datetime.today()

# for the next 7 days
for i in range(7):
    # add i days to the start date
    date = start_date + timedelta(days=i)
    # print the date
    print(date.strftime("%A, %d %B %Y"))
```

This script will print the date for the next 7 days, starting from today, in the format Day, DD Month YYYY. You can replace `7` with any value to get the dates for that many days.

If you want to loop through all days indefinitely,

```python
from datetime import datetime, timedelta

# start from today
date = datetime.today()

while True:
    print(date.strftime("%A, %d %B %Y"))
    date += timedelta(days=1)
```

This script will print the date for each day, starting from today, indefinitely. Be careful with this script as it will loop forever unless you manually stop it.
