Enhance the complexity of the current programming task:

/* From a given list of integers, generate a list of rolling maximum element found until given moment
   in the sequence while considering negative numbers and don't use built-in Javascript max function.
   Input: [1, 2, 3, 2, 3, 4, 2]
   Output: [1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4]
*/
const rollingMax = (numbers) => {
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a programmed solution of the problem in JavaScript:

```
const rollingMax = (numbers) => {
    let maxNum = numbers[0];
    return numbers.map((currentNum) => {
        if(currentNum > maxNum) {
            maxNum = currentNum;
        } 
        return maxNum;
    });
}
```
This script maps through every element in the given array, compares it with the current maximum number and, if it is higher, updates the current maximum number. Finally, it returns the maximum number found until that moment.

This solution has a complexity of O(n), where n is the size of the input array. This is because it only loops through the elements of the array once. By way of using `map` function, the space complexity is also O(n), because a new array is being created as a result of mapping operation.

The enhanced complexity version of this problem could be to find the rolling maximum over a "window" of the last k elements instead of all the previous elements. Here's how to do that:

```
const rollingMax = (numbers, k) => {
    const queue = [];
    const res = [];
    for(let i=0; i<numbers.length; i++){
        while(queue.length && queue[0] === i - k){
            queue.shift();
        }
        while(queue.length && numbers[queue[queue.length-1]] < numbers[i]){
          queue.pop();
        }
        queue.push(i);
        if(i>=k-1){
            res.push(numbers[queue[0]]);
        }
    }
    return res;
}
```
For example with input array as [1, 2, 3, 2, 3, 4, 2] and window k as 3, output will be [3, 3, 3, 4, 4]. Here, we are using a dequeue to keep track of potential max values within the window. The first element of dequeue is always the max value. For every element, we are removing elements from the back of dequeue which are less than current element because they cannot be max in presence of current element. Also removing element from the front if it is out of window. If we have passed the first k-1 number, we start pushing max values into the result array. This approach has O(n) time complexity and O(k) space complexity.
<|end|>


