Below you will find a piece of JavaScript code, which supposedly compares two provided strings. However, it contains an error. Your task is to identify the problem in the code, correct it, and then optimize the function to handle case-sensitive comparison as well as ignoring special characters and spaces. Bear in mind that your solution should not use any built-in string comparison methods, and should operate with a time complexity no worse than O(n).

```javascript
function compareStrings(stringA, stringB) {
    if (stringA.length != stringB.length) return false;
    for (let i = 0; i < stringA.length; i++) {
        if (stringA[i] != stringB[i]) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}
```
<|end|>
<|asst|>
At first glance, it seems that the initial code compares two strings accurately. However, the issue arises when it comes to comparing special characters and spaces. The existing code doesn't ignore these characters, and it also doesn't handle case sensitivity.

We'll use the ASCII code to ignore the case, spaces, and special characters. Because letters 'a' to 'z' are assigned ASCII values from 97 to 122, and 'A' to 'Z' from 65 to 90, we'll check if the characters are within the range for letters. If they are, we'll convert uppercase letters to lowercase by adding 32 to their ASCII values, and we'll simply ignore characters outside the ranges.

```javascript
function compareStrings(stringA, stringB) {
    var indexA = 0, 
        indexB = 0;

    while (indexA < stringA.length || indexB < stringB.length) {
        if (indexA < stringA.length && (stringA.charCodeAt(indexA) < 65 || (stringA.charCodeAt(indexA) > 90 && stringA.charCodeAt(indexA) < 97 ) || stringA.charCodeAt(indexA) > 122)) {
            indexA++;
            continue;
        }
   
        if (indexB < stringB.length && (stringB.charCodeAt(indexB) < 65 || (stringB.charCodeAt(indexB) > 90 && stringB.charCodeAt(indexB) < 97 ) || stringB.charCodeAt(indexB) > 122)) {
            indexB++;
            continue;
        }
   
        if (indexA === stringA.length || indexB === stringB.length){
            return false;
        }

        let charA = stringA.charCodeAt(indexA) <= 90 ? stringA.charCodeAt(indexA) + 32 : stringA.charCodeAt(indexA);
        let charB = stringB.charCodeAt(indexB) <= 90 ? stringB.charCodeAt(indexB) + 32 : stringB.charCodeAt(indexB);

        if (charA != charB) {
            return false;
        }

        indexA++;
        indexB++;
    }

    return true;
}
```
The function achieves an execution time of O(n), as it goes through each string only once. While it seems convoluted because of the multiple checks, it should meet the specifications provided in the prompt.
<|end|>


