Is there a way to change the appearance of an icon (ie. contrast / luminosity) when I hover the cursor, without requiring a second image file (or without requiring a hidden portion of the image)?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, it is possible to change the appearance of an icon on hover without requiring a second image file using CSS. There are CSS filters, like brightness, contrast, invert, and grayscale, which can be used to change the appearance of the image when you hover over it.

Here's an example:

```html
<style>
.icon:hover {
    filter: brightness(50%);
}
</style>

<img class="icon" src="your_icon.png"/>
```
In this example, the brightness of the icon will decrease by 50% when you hover over it.

Here is an example using contrast:

```html
<style>
.icon:hover {
    filter: contrast(200%);
}
</style>

<img class="icon" src="your_icon.png"/>
```
In this example, the contrast of the icon will increase by 200% when you hover over it.
  
Note: The filter property is not supported in Internet Explorer.
<|end|>


