Wikipedia says SQL Server Express Edition is limited to "one processor, 1 GB memory and 4 GB database files".  Does anyone have practical experience with how well this scales?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
While I don't have personal experience with it, from various sources it can be seen that overall, SQL Server Express Edition can perform quite well with these limitations depending on your needs. For small-to-medium applications, it should handle the load without any problems.

However, keep in mind these limitations:

1. One Processor: Express Edition can only use a single processor. If your server has multiple processors, Express Edition will only utilize one of them. This could limit performance in scenarios with high concurrent usage.

2. 1 GB Memory: The amount of memory that SQL Server Express can use is 1 GB. If your database is larger than this or has complex queries, performance can be negatively impacted due to frequent disk I/O operations.

3. 4 GB/10GB Database Size: This is perhaps the most significant limitation. If your database grows beyond the 4 GB limit for the 2005 and 2008 versions of SQL Server Express, or 10 GB for the 2008 R2 version and onwards, you won't be able to store any more data. For many applications, this size constraint may be the determining factor to upgrade to a paid version of SQL Server.

So, for smaller applications or services with moderate usage and data sizes, SQL Server Express can be a cost-effective database solution. For larger applications, services with heavy simultaneous usage, or rapidly growing databases, the limitations can quickly become prohibitive, and a more scalable platform would be advisable.
<|end|>


