This problem seems quite complex and large. Here we are asked to implement multiple functionality and each functionality has multiple issues to solve. So I am dividing this problem into multiple versions and here is the first version.

Python Code :

```python
import heapq

def min_heapify(arr):
    heapq.heapify(arr)
    return arr

def sortArray(a, n):
    for i in range(n, -1, -1):
        heapify(a, n, i)
    for i in range(n - 1, 0, -1):
        a[i], a[0] = a[0], a[i]
        heapify(a, i, 0)
    return a

def heapify(a, n, i):
    largest = i
    l = 2 * i + 1
    r = 2 * i + 2
    if l < n and a[i] < a[l]:
        largest = l
    if r < n and a[largest] < a[r]:
        largest = r
    if largest != i:
        a[i], a[largest] = a[largest], a[i]
        heapify(a, n, largest)
        
def max_heapify(arr):
    n = len(arr)
    arr = sortArray(arr, n)
    return arr
```

Here two functions are defined min_heapify() and max_heapify() - to convert array to min_heap and max_heap respectively. 

min_heapify() uses python's in-built library heapq's heapify function to convert array to min heap. This in-built function internally uses the sift down approach and time complexity of this approach is O(n).

To create max heap, I've used the sift-up approach. In max_heapify(), I first rearranged all elements so that largest value gets to the root node then moved the largest element end of the array and heapified remaining unsorted elements. This process repeated until all elements are in heap order. Time complexity of this approach is O(nlogn).

Next version will focus on handling arrays with duplicate values, negative numbers, zero, and nullify values. Also explaining how to handle arrays with non-integer, positive and negative infinity values. 

Last version will illustrate about construction of method to convert the array into a balanced binary search tree and red-black tree. Test cases will also be provided at the end to verify the solution.
