To build the responsive, horizontally aligned navigation bar with a dropdown menu, you will need to use a combination of HTML5, CSS3, and ARIA roles/properties for accessibility.

Consider the following example, it utilizes flexbox for alignment and positioning, :hover pseudo-classes to show the dropdown menu, and ARIA roles and properties to make it accessible.

HTML5 Code:
```html
<nav role="navigation" aria-label="Main Menu">
  <ul class="navbar">
    <li><a href="#">Home</a></li>
    <li><a href="#">About</a></li>
    <li class="dropdown">
      <a href="#" aria-haspopup="true" aria-expanded="false">Services</a>
      <div class="dropdown-content" aria-label="submenu">
        <a href="#">Web Design</a>
        <a href="#">SEO</a>
        <a href="#">Digital Marketing</a>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li><a href="#">Contact</a></li>
  </ul>
</nav>
```

CSS3 Code:
```css
.navbar {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;
}
.navbar li {
  list-style: none;
}
.dropdown {
  position: relative;
}
.dropdown-content {
  display: none;
  position: absolute;
  top: 100%;
  left: 0;
  background-color: #f9f9f9;
  min-width: 160px;
}
.dropdown-content a {
  display: block;
  padding: 12px 16px;
  color: black;
  text-decoration: none;
}
.dropdown:hover .dropdown-content {
  display: block;
}
```

In the HTML, `aria-haspopup="true"` informs assistive technologies that this link has a submenu, and `aria-expanded="false"` indicates that the submenu is currently collapsed. When the user interacts with the dropdown, the script updates the `aria-expanded` attribute to `true`.

The CSS provides the dropdown behavior. The `.dropdown-content` is hidden by default (`display: none`). Then, when `.dropdown` is hovered over (`:hover`), `.dropdown-content` is revealed (`display: block`).

To make this navigation bar responsive, you could use CSS media queries to adjust the styles based on the screen size. For example, you might stack the links vertically below a certain screen width.

Note: This approach assumes a light usage of CSS, and it's quite basic and has room for enhancements. If you have a more complex navigation or a large project generally you should probably use JavaScript or a JavaScript library such as jQuery.
