To create theme of clouds and a sky for your SVG, you can follow these few steps:

1. Change the colors: You can change the fill attributes of your paths to colors that represent a sky/cloud theme. For clouds, you can use different shades of blue and white.

2. Add gradients: Real skies and clouds aren't one solid color - they have gradients. You can add `linearGradient` or `radialGradient` elements to your SVG to create this effect.

3. Add a sky background: You can create a `rect` element, give it a blue fill, and place it at the very back of your SVG to represent a sky background.

4. Apply blur filter: Clouds have blurry edges, you can apply this effect to your cloud elements using `filter` or 'flood-opacity' tags in your SVG.

An example for point 1 and 2:

```html
<svg ... >
    <defs>
        <linearGradient id="cloudGradient" x1="0" y1="0" x2="1" y2="1">
            <stop offset="0%" style="stop-color:rgb(255,255,255);stop-opacity:1" />
            <stop offset="100%" style="stop-color:rgb(200,200,200);stop-opacity:1" />
        </linearGradient>
    </defs>
    
    <path ... fill="url(#cloudGradient)" />
    ...
</svg>
```
For point 3:

```html
<svg ... >
    <rect x="0" y="0" width="32" height="32" fill="lightblue" />
    <!-- your existing paths here -->
</svg>
```

For point 4:

```html
<svg ... >
    <defs>
        <filter id="blurMe">
            <feGaussianBlur in="SourceGraphic" stdDeviation="2" />
        </filter>
    </defs>
    <path ... filter="url(#blurMe)" />
</svg>
```

Note: Numbers in 'stdDeviation' determines the amount of blur. Adjust according to your preference.
<|end|>


