Yes, you're on the right track. GARCH and ARCH models are often used in financial time series analysis because they do a good job of capturing a common phenomenon in such data, namely, volatility clustering. This means that large changes in prices tend to be followed by large changes (either increases or decreases), and small changes tend to be followed by small changes.

There are different ways to test for the presence of conditional heteroskedasticity (a key assumption of these models) in your data. One common approach is to use the Lagrange Multiplier Test. This test checks the null hypothesis of no autoregressive conditional heteroskedasticity.

For a more intuitive understanding, you can simply plot the absolute or squared values of your return series. If you observe clustering of high and low volatility periods, this might suggest the presence of conditional heteroskedasticity.

To perform this in Python, you'll want to use the 'arch' package, which supports both ARCH and GARCH models. You may also want to use the 'statsmodels' package for exploring the data and testing for autocorrelation, stationarity, and other important properties.

Remember that like any model, ARCH/GARCH has its limitations. While they can capture the volatility clustering phenomenon, they might not do well when facing sharp and sudden market changes or 'black swan' events. 

Since you're also interested in determining what factors influence copper prices, you might want to explore additional techniques like machine learning, distributed lag models, or vector autoregressive models, which can help you understand relationships between variables and their lags. You might also want to look into how economic indicators, like GDP, inflation rates, industrial production and others affect copper prices. 

It is also worth mentioning that you will need to treat your data for seasonality and trend, if these are present before applying the models. 

While applying time-series model remember the most important assumptions of time series modelling are:
- The series must be stationary.
- Observations must be independent.

Check for these before starting your modelling journey! Good luck!
<|end|>


