An array in C can store either int, float, char, or pointer types, but not strings directly. However, you can create an array of char pointers (which are strings basically) to store the strings. Here’s an equivalent C code using array:

```C
char* messages[3] = {"A is pressed", "B is pressed", "C is pressed"};
if (c >= 'A' && c <= 'C') {
    printf("%s\n", messages[c - 'A']);
}
```

In the above code, we initialize the array `messages` to contain the corresponding strings. Note that in C and C++, characters are represented internally using ASCII codes. The ASCII value of 'A' is 65, 'B' is 66, and 'C' is 67. So `c - 'A'` will give an integer between 0, 1, or 2 which can be used to index into the `messages` array. If `c` is 'A', `c - 'A'` will be 0, if `c` is 'B', `c - 'A'` will be 1, and so on.

The if statement `if (c >= 'A' && c <= 'C') { ... }` is used to check whether `c` is a character between 'A' and 'C'. We don't check this in the switch statement version, but it's a good practice to check the validity of `c` because `c - 'A'` could give a negative number or a number larger than the size of the array if `c` is not a valid character.
