Conceptualize a Perl programming script designed to transmute audio files into their representative hexadecimal format.
The audio file for conversion: sample.wav
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is a simplistic Perl script that converts an audio file into hexadecimal format. It reads the file in binary mode, then use 'unpack' function to interpret the binary data into hexadecimal format. 

Make sure to replace "sample.wav" with your own file's path if it's not in the same directory. 

```Perl
#!/usr/bin/perl
use strict;
use warnings;

sub main {
    my $file = "sample.wav";
    open(my $fh,'<:raw',$file) or die "Could not open file '$file' $!";
    
    while (read $fh, my $buffer, 4) {
        print(unpack("H*", $buffer), "\n"); 
    } 
       
    close $fh;
}

main();
```
Every four bytes are read from the file and then converted into hexadecimal. If the audio file is large, this will provide controlled output.

Don't forget to handle file operations' exceptions, such as failing to open a file (which can be due to reasons like the file not existing or lacking permissions), as those operations may fail and throw exceptions that could crash your script if not properly handled.

Lastly, always close a file handle when done with it. In some cases, the file handle might remain open, leading to file corruption or other issues.

Note: This code needs to be run in an environment where Perl is installed. If it's not installed you can download it from `https://www.perl.org/get.html` based on your system requirements. Then you can run the script from the command line like so: `perl script.pl`. This script won't produce any output file, instead it will print the hexadecimal data on the console. If you want to save the output to a file you can use a file handle and write the output to the file.
