<|asst|>
You can use a mocking framework like MOQ to generate an `IQueryable<T>` that returns specific values when accessed.

Here's how you can do it:

```csharp
using Moq;
using NHibernate;
using NHibernate.Linq;
using System.Linq;

public class NHibernateRepositoryTests
{
    [Test]
    public void TestRepositoryQuery()
    {
        var mockSession = new Mock<ISession>();

        // Arrange
        var data = new List<T> { ... } // your test data
        var queryable = data.AsQueryable();

        mockSession.Setup(_ => _.Linq<T>()).Returns(queryable);

        var repo = new NHibernateRepository<T>(mockSession.Object);

        // Act
        var result = repo.Query();

        // Assert
        Assert.True(result.SequenceEqual(data)); // or whatever checks you need.
    }
}
```

In this way, when you call `NHibernateRepository<T>.Query()` in your tests, the returned `IQueryable<T>` will yield the test data you provided.
<|end|>


