<|asst|>
Upgrading the compatibility level from 80 (SQL Server 2000) to 90 (SQL Server 2005) may improve performance on the SQL Server 2005 servers. This is because SQL Server 2005 introduces several new features and improvements over SQL Server 2000, including improvements in query optimization and execution, indexing, and other functionality that might impact performance.

Having said that, the effect on performance can depend greatly on the specific workloads, schema, data, queries, and usage patterns of your application. Changing the compatibility level also changes the behavior of the SQL Server in various ways which can impact not only performance, but also correctness and compatibility of your application. Care should be taken to thoroughly test your application after changing the compatibility level. 

In some cases, certain queries or operations might become slower after changing the compatibility level. You may have to review and update such queries or parts of your application for optimal performance under the new compatibility level.

Lastly, using an outdated and unsupported version like SQL Server 2000 or 2005 presents various risks and disadvantages including lack of support, missing out on improvements and features of later versions, and potential security vulnerabilities. Upgrading to a more recent version of SQL Server is strongly recommended.
<|end|>


