Construct two functions in R programming language. The first function will generate a DataFrame with random numbers of different data types (at least 3 different data types) and missing values. The second function will then be used to illustrate a histogram visualization of these numerical data types separately, handle missing values and outliers accordingly. Additionally, the histogram should include suitable labels for axes, a legend, and a title. Further, provide a succinct text description of each histogram which includes key metrics like mean, median, mode, and standard deviation.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's how you can approach this problem. Note that R is a statistical programming language and outliers are identified and handled using statistical techniques. There are many ways to define outliers but for this example I use the "1.5 interquartile range rule". missing data and outliers are filled and replaced with median value of the respective columns.

```R
# Load necessary libraries
library(dplyr)
library(tidyr)
library(ggplot2)

# Function to create a DataFrame with random numbers of different data types
create_dataframe <- function() {
  df <- data.frame(
    column1 = runif(1000, min=-100, max=100), # uniform distribution
    column2 = rnorm(1000, mean=0, sd=10), # normal distribution
    column3 = sample(1:10, 1000, replace = TRUE), # discrete uniform distribution
    column4 = sample(c('A', 'B', 'C', 'D'), 1000, replace = TRUE) # categorical
  )
  
  # Add missing values randomly
  df <- df %>% mutate_all(function(x) {x[sample(1:n(), 50)] <- NA; x})
  
  return(df)
}

# create the dataframe
df <- create_dataframe()

# Inspect dataframe
head(df)

# Function to create histograms, handle missing values by inserting
# median and handle outliers by converting them to median
create_histogram <- function(df) {
    df %>% purrr::map_if(is.numeric, function(x) {
      x[is.na(x)] <- median(x, na.rm = TRUE) # fill missing values
      qnt <- quantile(x, probs=c(.25, .75), na.rm = T) # calculate quantiles
      H <- 1.5 * IQR(x, na.rm = T) # calculate 1.5*IQR
      x[x<(qnt[1]-H) | x>(qnt[2]+H)] <- median(x, na.rm = TRUE) # replace outliers
      return(x)
    }) -> df
    
    # Generate histograms for numeric columns
    df %>% purrr::map_if(is.numeric, function(x) {
      ggplot2::qplot(
        x,
        binwidth = 30,
        main = paste0("Histogram for Numerical Variable\nMean: ", round(mean(x), 1),
                      "\nMedian: ", median(x),
                      "\nMode: ", as.numeric(names(which.max(table(x)))),
                      "\nSD: ", round(sd(x), 1)),
        xlab = "Value",
        ylab = "Frequency",
        fill=I("blue"),
        col=I("red"),
        alpha=I(.2)
      ) -> pl
      print(pl)
    })
}

create_histogram(df)
```
This code randomly generates a data frame with 4 columns where the first 3 columns have different numerical distributions and the 4th is categorical with a mixture of integers and characters. It randomly inserts NAs in the data and handles NAs & outliers in the numerical data when visualizing the histograms. It calculates and includes labels for: mean, median, mode, and standard deviation in the title of the histograms. It only plots histograms for numerical data following the standard statistical view of histograms.
<|end|>


