I have an `API .net 7.0` project which I am attempting to run as an `Azure web service`. I have a linux app service defined, and I have deployed a zip file created by zipping the output of `dotnet publish` to the service using the command

az webapp deployment source config-zip --resource-group <resource group> --name <app service> --src publish.zip


But when I attempt to access the webapp, I simply get

Your web app is running and waiting for your content.


When accessing the webapp using ssh, I can see that `/site/wwwroot` is empty while `/home/Data/SitePackages` contains a zip file.
I have set `WEBSITE_RUN_FROM_PACKAGE=1` under application settings. Startup command is set to `dotnet <name>.dll`
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Your `WEBSITE_RUN_FROM_PACKAGE=1` setting is correct. When you set `WEBSITE_RUN_FROM_PACKAGE=1`, the zip package is mounted as a read-only file system at /home/site/wwwroot.

However, it seems the application doesn't run as expected. The possible issue might be related to the startup command you have set. Make sure the `<name>.dll` you set in command is the same as the dll file name in your publish folder, which should be your application's main assembly.

Make sure the application is published with

`dotnet publish --configuration Release --output ./publish`

Finally pack up everything in the `publish` directory (not the directory itself) into `.zip` format file, and then deploy that `.zip` package to Azure.

After doing this, SSH to your web application and the command `ls /home/site/wwwroot` should show your application files.

If the above solutions are not working, you should check the application logs for more detail about what is happening. 

You can enable logging on azure portal following this steps:
- Go to your App Service’s management page in Azure
- On the left, scroll down and select the 'App Service logs' item
- Turn 'Application Logging (Filesystem)' and 'Detailed error messages' to on and then save. 

Then access your application again and later download and check the logs (also in 'App Service logs').
<|end|>


