<|user|>
Create a React application that queries a REST API and displays the data received. The application should query the API when it first loads and render the results.
API Endpoint: https://example.com/cards
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To achieve this, you can use React library along with Axios or the Fetch API which are commonly used to send HTTP requests. Here's a simple example using Axios.

Firstly, install axios to your project:
```bash
npm install axios
```
Then implement your application like this:

```jsx
import React, { useState, useEffect } from "react";
import axios from "axios";

function App() {
  const [data, setData] = useState([]); // initialize data as an empty array

  useEffect(() => {
    // send a GET request on component mount
    axios.get("https://example.com/cards")
      .then(response => {
        // handle success
        setData(response.data);
      })
      .catch(error => {
        // handle error
        console.log(error);
      });
  }, []); // empty dependency array means this effect will only run once (like componentDidMount in classes)

  return (
    <div>
      {data.length > 0 ? (
        data.map((item, index) => (
          <div key={index}>
            <h1>{item.title}</h1>
            <p>{item.description}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      ) : (
        <p>Loading data...</p>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

Here, we use the `useEffect` hook to send the GET request when the component mounts. The axios.get method returns a promise that resolves to the response of the request. If the request is successful, we use `setData` to update our state with the response data.

Then in our JSX, we check if data has been loaded by checking its length. If it has, we map over the data and for each item, display it in a div. If the data is still loading (i.e. `data.length` is 0), we display a loading message.

This is a basic example and has no error handling besides logging any error to the console. In a real world application, you would want to handle errors properly by maybe displaying a message to the user, and you would probably not want to render all the data as soon as it's loaded, instead using pagination or infinite scrolling.

You also may want to put the API call in a separate function and call that function in the `useEffect`, especially if the API call logic gets more complicated.
<|end|>


