In Java, create a method that systematically sifts through an array of integers and determines the index of the smallest numerical element. Utilize multi-step reasoning for this implementation. Example: [7, 9, 5, 4, 1, 3]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a Java method that implements the description. It iterates through the given array of integers and checks each element to determine if it's the smallest so far. If it's indeed the smallest, then it updates the minimum and its index. By the end of this process, it would have identified the index of the smallest element.

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] array = {7, 9, 5, 4, 1, 3};
        System.out.println("Index of the smallest element is: " + findSmallestIndex(array));
    }

    public static int findSmallestIndex(int[] numArray) {
        if(numArray == null || numArray.length == 0) {
            return -1; // Return -1 if the array is null or empty
        }
        
        int smallestIndex = 0; // Initially assume the first element is the smallest
        int smallestNum = numArray[0];

        for(int i = 1; i < numArray.length; i++) {
            if(numArray[i] < smallestNum) {
                smallestNum = numArray[i]; // update smallest number
                smallestIndex = i; // update smallest number index
            }
        }

        return smallestIndex; // return index of smallest number
    }
}
```

For the given array, the method will output: 4 which is the index of the smallest number 1.
<|end|>


