can this be written in perl?
def twofold_transmutation(sequence: str) -> dict:
    """ Given a specific sequence of characters, invert the case of the letters, round the numeric parts to the 
    nearest even number, double the special symbols, and tally the frequency of 
    each character post transformation in the sequence.
    """
    modified_sequence = ''
    
    for s in sequence:
        if s.isalpha():
            modified_sequence += s.swapcase()
        elif s.isdigit():
            digit = int(s)
            digit = digit + 1 if digit % 2 != 0 else digit
            modified_sequence += str(digit)
        else:
            modified_sequence += s * 2

    frequency_count = {}
    for s in modified_sequence:
        if s in frequency_count:
            frequency_count[s] += 1
        else:
            frequency_count[s] = 1

    return modified_sequence, frequency_count

# Testing the function
print(twofold_transmutation('Hello4!'))
please add some explanatory comments in the perl code.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Absolutely, a similar function can be written in Perl. Perl is very versatile for text manipulation tasks. Here is a similar function written in Perl:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl 

sub twofold_transmutation{
   my $sequence = $_;              # Assign the input parameter to a variable sequence
   my $modified_sequence = "";     # Initialize an empty string for modified_sequence

   # Loop through each character of the sequence
   foreach my $s (split //, $sequence) {
       if($s =~ /[a-zA-Z]/){       # If s is alphabetic
           $s =~ tr/a-zA-Z/A-Za-z/; # Swap the case
       }
       elsif($s =~ /[0-9]/){       # If s is numeric
           if((int($s) % 2) != 0){
               $s = int($s) + 1;   # Round to the nearest even number if odd
           }
       }
       else{
           $s = $s x 2;            # Double the special symbols
       }
       $modified_sequence .= $s;   # Append to the modified_sequence
   }

   # Initialize an empty hash to count the frequency of characters
   my %frequency_count = ();
   # Split the sequence into individual characters and loop through
   foreach my $s (split //, $modified_sequence){
       if (exists $frequency_count{$s}){
           $frequency_count{$s} += 1;  # Increase the frequency count
       }
       else {
           $frequency_count{$s} = 1;   # If the character is not in the hash yet, set the count to 1
       }
   }

   # Print the modified sequence and frequency counts
   print "$modified_sequence\n";
   foreach my $key (keys %frequency_count){
       print "$key : $frequency_count{$key}\n";
   }
}

twofold_transmutation('Hello4!');
```
Run the script and replace `'Hello4!'` with any string you prefer. This script returns a string that has undergone several transformations and the frequency count of each character in that string. It inverts the case of the letters, rounds the numerical parts to the nearest even number, doubles the special symbols, and displays the frequency count of each character post these transformations.
<|end|>


