I want to emulate the delete confirmation page behavior before saving 
certain models in the admin.  In my case if I change one object, 
certain others should be deleted as they depend upon the object's now 
out-of-date state. 

I understand where to implement the actual cascaded updates (inside 
the parent model's save method), but I don't see a quick way to ask 
the user for confirmation (and then rollback if they decide not to 
save). I suppose I could implement some weird confirmation logic 
directly inside the save method (sort of a two phase save) but that 
seems...ugly. 

Any thoughts, even general pointers into the django codebase? 

Thanks!
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can achieve this using Django's messages framework and overriding the `save_model` method on the Django admin model. If certain conditions are met (i.e changes made require deletion of other objects), then you display a warning message and don't save the changes. 

If the warning is acknowledged, you can pass a URL parameter that confirms the user wants to continue with the changes, and then proceed with the save and delete necessary objects.

Here is a rough example of how you'd implement it:

```python
from django.contrib import messages
from django.http import HttpResponseRedirect
from django.urls import reverse

class MyModelAdmin(admin.ModelAdmin):
    def save_model(self, request, obj, form, change):
        # Check logic if the other model instances need to be deleted
        should_delete =  # Add your check logic here...

        if should_delete and "_confirm_changes" not in request.POST:
            # Display warning message and don't save changes
            messages.warning(request, 'Saving this object will delete other objects. Are you sure you want to proceed?')
            
            # Redirect to the same page with a confirmation flag
            return HttpResponseRedirect("{}?_confirm_changes=True".format(
                reverse('admin:app_label_model_change', args=[obj.id]))
            )
        
        # Delete other model instances, if conditions met and changes confirmed 
        if should_delete and "_confirm_changes" in request.POST:
            # Define deletion logic here...

        super().save_model(request, obj, form, change)
```

This simply adds a warning message to the messages stack, and redirects to the form page. If the "confirm_changes" parameter is found in the POST data, it executes the save and delete logic. Note that actual deletion or save logic is not provided in this example. You will have to add your own business logic.
