Optimize an existing code snippet by introducing caching.
function getData() {
 return axios.get('https://example.com/data');
}
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Caching is a way to store value/data for reuse. It decreases the number of requests to the server and thus makes the application faster and more efficient. In JavaScript, one commonly used caching technique is memoization.

We can use a simple object to store data that has been fetched before. Here's how the refactored function would look:

```javascript
let cache = {};

function getData() {
  // check if data exists in cache
  if (cache.data) {
    return new Promise(resolve => resolve(cache.data));
  }
  // otherwise, fetch data and store in cache
  else {
    return axios.get('https://example.com/data')
      .then(res => {
        cache.data = res;
        return res;
      });
  }
}  
```
Note: This kind of caching is simple but it won't manage itself over time. More sophisticated caches will have things like expiration times, maximum sizes or other forms of controlling what is kept in cache.

Important points to be considered:
- Cached data becomes stale after updates. So, refetch and update cache when data is updated.
- Clear cache when it's no longer needed to avoid memory leaks.
<|end|>


