I'm slowly learning Objective-C and Cocoa, and the only way I see so far to capture key events in Text Views is to use delegation, but I'm having trouble finding useful documentation and examples on how to implement such a solution. Can anyone point me in the right direction or supply some first-hand help?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You're right that delegation is the way to go to capture key events in Text Views in Objective-C. To give you a simple example, here's how you can implement it.

Suppose you have a `NSTextView` instance and you call it `myTextView`.

1. Make sure your class conforms to the `NSTextViewDelegate` protocol.

```objective-c
@interface MyClass : NSObject <NSTextViewDelegate>
```

Also, ensure that the class where you plan to monitor the events has been set as the delegate of the NSTextView. To do this, you can usually set it either programatically or in the Interface Builder.

```objective-c
[myTextView setDelegate:self];
```

Or if you're using Interface Builder, control-drag from the `NSTextView` to the object you want to be the delegate and select "delegate".

2. Implement the key event delegate methods. For a `NSTextView`, the delegate methods you might most want to implement are `-textDidChange:` or `-textShouldEndEditing:`. 

Example of `-textDidChange:`:

```objective-c
- (void)textDidChange:(NSNotification *)notification {
   NSTextView *textView = notification.object;
   NSLog(@"Text view content: %@", textView.string);
}
```

You can see some of the methods the delegate can implement in the `NSControl` documentation and more specifically `NSTextView` delegate methods in the NSTextViewDelegate documentation.

Remember, not all these methods are required, only implement the ones you need for your specific use case.

For more specific key events, you might need to subclass `NSTextView` and override `-keyDown:` or some of the other `NSResponder` methods. Make sure to call the superclass implementation if you do override any of these.
<|end|>


