class Foo
{
   static bool Bar(Stream^ stream);
};

class FooWrapper
{
   bool Bar(LPCWSTR szUnicodeString)
   {
       return Foo::Bar(??);
   }
};



`MemoryStream` will take a `byte[]` but I'd  to do this without copying the data if possible.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
If you want to get a Stream from a string without copying the data, you might consider using a StringReader, although it will not yield a Stream but a TextReader. If you want to convert a wide string (LPCWSTR) into a Stream, you will need to convert it first into a byte array. However, there's no way to do it without copying the data in C++\/CLI.

Here's how to do it:

```cpp
class FooWrapper
{
public:
    bool Bar(LPCWSTR szUnicodeString)
    {
        std::wstring ws(szUnicodeString);
        std::string str(ws.begin(), ws.end());
        System::IO::MemoryStream^ ms = gcnew System::IO::MemoryStream();

        for(auto c : str)
        {
            ms->WriteByte(c);
        }

        ms->Position = 0;
        return Foo::Bar(ms);
    }
};
```

This code will copy the contents of the string into a MemoryStream byte by byte. After WriteByte, it sets the Position of the MemoryStream back to 0. This action allows the Stream to start reading from the beginning. Finally, it calls Foo::Bar using the MemoryStream.

Remember that all strings are sequences of bytes. Therefore, “transferring” a string to a stream must involve copying those bytes. If the string is mutable and large, it might be possible to "transfer" the buffer of that string to a stream by moving rather than copying data, but C# strings are immutable and small, so any such "transfer" will only be a copy under the hood. Therefore, there's no way to avoid copying a string to a Stream in C++/CLI, C#, or .NET in general.
<|end|>


