I was trying to remove all the lines of a file except the last line but the following command did not work, although file.txt is not empty.


$cat file.txt |tail -1 > file.txt

$cat file.txt



Why is it so?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The reason your command isn't working is because of the order in which the shell processes the command. When you run the command, the shell is first seeing the redirection (`> file.txt`) and immediately truncates the file, i.e., it clears all its contents before performing the rest of the operations. When `cat` and `tail` commands are then run, `file.txt` is already empty, making it look like your command is not working.

Instead, you can use a temporary file to hold the last line, then rename it back to file.txt, like this:

```bash
tail -1 file.txt > temp.txt && mv temp.txt file.txt
```

Or, you can use 'sponge', a command from the moreutils package that soaks up all its input before writing the output file:

```bash
cat file.txt | tail -1 | sponge file.txt
```

However, the 'sponge' command is not installed on all systems by default. You'll need to install the moreutils package if you want to use it.
<|end|>


