<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a simple JavaScript code that completes the task. This function smallestNumInString input three parameters a, b, and c. The values are converted to integers using the parseInt() function. Next, we check if the values are numbers using isNaN() function. In the end, we return the smallest number using Math.min() function. 

```javascript
function smallestNumInString(a, b, c) {
  // Convert strings to integers
  var a = parseInt(a);
  var b = parseInt(b);
  var c = parseInt(c);
  
  // Check if the inputs are numbers
  if(isNaN(a) || isNaN(b) || isNaN(c)) {
    return 'Please enter valid numbers.';
  }
  
  // Find and return the smallest number
  return Math.min(a, b, c);
}

console.log(smallestNumInString('5', '10', '15'));  // Returns 5
console.log(smallestNumInString('apple', '10', '15'));  // Returns "Please enter valid numbers."
```

This script will return the smallest number if the inputs are valid numerical strings. If not, it will return an error message saying "Please enter valid numbers."
