The provided code snippet is in PowerShell, which is typically used in Windows. To run it on a macOS, it would need to be converted into bash or another script that a Unix-based system could understand, as macOS doesn't support Windows Management Instrumentation (WMI). Below is a comparable bash script that can be used on a macOS system:

```bash
#!/bin/bash

ComputerName=$(hostname)
OS=$(sw_vers -productName)
Version=$(sw_vers -productVersion)
Model=$(sysctl -n hw.model)
Manufacturer="Apple"  # MacOS runs only on Apple hardware
Processor=$(sysctl -n machdep.cpu.brand_string)
RAM=$(sysctl -n hw.memsize | awk '{print $0/1024/1024/1024}')
TimeZone=$(systemsetup -gettimezone | cut -d ':' -f2 | xargs)  # remove leading spaces

echo "ComputerName: $ComputerName"
echo "OS: $OS $Version"
echo "Model: $Model"
echo "Manufacturer: $Manufacturer"
echo "Processor: $Processor"
echo "RAM: $RAM GB"
echo "TimeZone: $TimeZone"
```

This code uses the `systemsetup`, `sysctl`, and `sw_vers` commands to get system information such as the computer name, operating system, model, manufacturer, processor, total physical memory, and timezone, equivalent to the original PowerShell script. 

You can save this script to a file then run it with `bash filename.sh` command in the terminal.
