I am facing a challenge with the following task: My Calendar III. The task details are as follows: A `k`-booking is defined as an occurrence when `k` events share a non-empty intersection (meaning, there is a certain duration that is common to all `k` events.)
You are provided with a series of events delineated as `[start, end)`, after each event is given, you are required to return an integer `k` that signifies the maximum `k`-booking among all the preceding events.

You are to construct the `MyCalendarThree` class:
`MyCalendarThree()` This initializes the object.

`int book(int start, int end)` This returns an integer `k` that represents the highest integer for which a `k`-booking exists in the calendar.

Example 1:
Input
[&#34;MyCalendarThree&#34;, &#34;book&#34;, &#34;book&#34;, &#34;book&#34;, &#34;book&#34;, &#34;book&#34;, &#34;book&#34;]
[[], [10, 20], [50, 60], [10, 40], [5, 15], [5, 10], [25, 55]]
Output
[null, 1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 3]
Explanation
MyCalendarThree myCalendarThree = new MyCalendarThree();
myCalendarThree.book(10, 20); // return 1, The initial event can be booked and is disjoint, hence the maximum k-booking is a 1-booking.

myCalendarThree.book(50, 60); // return 1, The subsequent event can be booked and is disjoint, hence the maximum k-booking is a 1-booking.

myCalendarThree.book(10, 40); // return 2, The third event [10, 40) intersects the initial event, and the maximum k-booking is a 2-booking.

myCalendarThree.book(5, 15); // return 3, The remaining events result in the maximum K-booking being only a 3-booking.

myCalendarThree.book(5, 10); // return 3
myCalendarThree.book(25, 55); // return 3

Constraints:
`0 &lt;= start &lt; end &lt;= 109`
A maximum of `400` calls will be made to `book`.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This problem is all about tracking overlapping intervals, and there are multiple ways to solve this. However, one of the simpler methods is to use a TreeMap, that keeps track of the start and end times of events. 

Here is the solution in Java:

```java
import java.util.TreeMap;

public class MyCalendarThree {
    TreeMap<Integer, Integer> calendar;
    
    public MyCalendarThree() {
        calendar = new TreeMap<>();
    }

    public int book(int start, int end) {
        calendar.put(start, calendar.getOrDefault(start, 0) + 1); // increment the counter at start time of the event
        calendar.put(end, calendar.getOrDefault(end, 0) - 1);  // decrement the counter at end time of the event

        int ongoing = 0, max = 0;
        for (int v : calendar.values()) {
            ongoing += v;
            max = Math.max(max, ongoing);
        }
        
        return max;
    }
}
```
Explanation:
In the constructor we initialize the TreeMap. We add 1 at the start time of an event and subtract 1 at end time of an event. For each added event we keep a running sum ("ongoing") and keep track of the maximum of this sum. This will be the maximum k-booking. We use a TreeMap so that the start and end times are automatically sorted.

The time complexity of this solution is O(n log n) where n is the number of events booked, because TreeMap operations take log n. The space complexity is O(n) because we store each start and end time in the TreeMap.
<|end|>


