Construct an HTML document to exhibit the supplied data in the form of a meticulously formatted table. The table must include a sort function for each column, and a search bar which filters results in real-time.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Creating an interactive table in pure HTML won't be sufficient because HTML does not support real time search or sorting functionality on its own. For those features, we'll need to use JavaScript. Below is a basic example of how you could do this using a client-sided JavaScript library known as `jQuery` along with `jQuery DataTables` plugin:

Here's an HTML structure for your page:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <script src="https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/jquery/3.5.1/jquery.min.js"></script>
    <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="https://cdn.datatables.net/1.10.22/css/jquery.dataTables.css">
    <script type="text/javascript" charset="utf8" src="https://cdn.datatables.net/1.10.22/js/jquery.dataTables.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
    <table id="dataTable" class="display">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Column 1</th>
                <th>Column 2</th>
                <!-- Add more columns as needed -->
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Row 1 Data 1</td>
                <td>Row 1 Data 2</td>
                <!-- Add more data as needed -->
            </tr>
            <!-- Add more rows as needed -->
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <script src="script.js"></script> 
</body>
</html>
```

And then JavaScript/jQuery to initialise the DataTables plugin:

```javascript
$(document).ready( function () {
    $('#dataTable').DataTable();
} );
```

You need to include jQuery and the DataTables plugin in your `<head>` section. In this example, I include them from a CDN (Content Delivery Network), but you could also download and host them on your own server.

The `$(document).ready()` function ensures that the script only runs once the page is fully loaded.

Inside of this function, `$('#dataTable').DataTable();` initializes the DataTables plugin on your table.

The `script.js` should be linked at the end of the `body` tag. This file should contain the JavaScript code.

The above lines of code are for basic functionality of sorting and real-time search. You may want to customize it further as per your requirements by following the official DataTables documentation. 

Please note that you should replace `Column 1`, `Column 2`, `Row 1 Data 2`, etc. with your own table headers and data. The DataTables plugin will automatically handle creating the search bar and enabling sort functionality for each column.
<|end|>


