3.2GHz P4 Hyperthreaded, 2GB RAM. Running Outlook, Perforce, IE7, directory browsers. Usually have 1-3 instances of VS running. It's much slower than VC6, say. It seems to take a long time to load projects and close down. I'm interested in if people know reasons why this happens, because of the way VS is written. Is it using .net internally and GC slows it down?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
There could be various reasons. Here are a few things to consider:

- Larger projects require more resources: Visual Studio (VS) needs to load metadata about every class, structure, enum, method in your code into memory. The larger your project, the more time it will need to load or quit.
- Plugins: The more plugins you have installed into your VS, the more time it requires to load or quit. Some of them can be particularly resource-hungry.
- 3rd Party Tools: Some tools (like some AntiVirus), can significantly slow down Visual Studio by scanning each file when VS opens them.
- Computer configuration: Although you mentioned your PC’s configuration, and it seems good enough, the problem could be related to the overall health of your PC. If your PC is running other resource-heavy programs in the background, there might not be enough resources available for VS to perform efficiently. Consider checking your HDD or SSD's health as well.
- Visual Studio is itself a big application and thus, requires an ample amount of computational resources to function properly.
- Network shares: If your projects are on a network share or in a virtualized folder (like Dropbox folder), this can also slow things down.
- Visual Studio uses .NET for many of its functionalities and yes, if garbage collector (GC) kicks in, it can slow it down.

Make sure that your computer is optimized for Visual Studio, disable not-so-required extensions or services, and try to isolate the behavior by using a different machine, if possible, to see if that improves performance. Also, upgrading to a newer version of VS can sometimes help, as Microsoft continually makes improvements and optimizations to their software.
<|end|>


