Formulate a responsive CSS Grid layout that meticulously arranges the elements of the provided multi-dimensional array in a row-column fashion. The grid should automatically adjust its rows and columns based on the window size. Also, construct a function that could add a unique hover effect to each cell and a click event that alerts the data within the cell.

In addition, ensure that the first row of the grid is marked as a header with a distinct style compared to the other cells. Also handle cases for data scalability, i.e., the grid design should accommodate future data add-ons to the array. The array is shown below:

array_data = [["name, favorite color, pet"], ["John Doe, blue, dog"], ["Jane Doe, green, cat"]] 

For additional complexity, devise a way to sort the array data based on name, favorite color, or pet when the respective header is clicked.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a quick implementation of the problem using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.

```html
<div id="grid"></div>

<script>
  // This parses the CSV-like input and formats it as an array of object
  const arrayData = [["name", "favorite color", "pet"], ["John Doe", "blue", "dog"], ["Jane Doe", "green", "cat"]];

  const handleCellClick = (data) => {
    alert(data);
  };

  const handleHeaderClick = (key) => {
    arrayData.sort((a, b) => (a[key] > b[key]) ? 1 : ((b[key] > a[key]) ? -1 : 0));
    populateGrid(arrayData); // Re-render the grid
  };

  const populateGrid = (data) => {
    let output = `${data.map((row, rowIndex) =>
          `${row.map((col, colIndex) =>
            `<div onclick="handleCellClick('${col}')" style="${rowIndex === 0 ? 'background: grey;' : ''}">
                 ${rowIndex === 0 ? `<a href="#" onclick="handleHeaderClick('${col}')">${col}</a>` : col}
             </div>`).join('')}`).join('')}`;

    document.getElementById('grid').innerHTML = output;
  };

  populateGrid(arrayData);
</script>
```

```css
#grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(100px, 1fr));
  grid-gap: 10px;
}

#grid > div {
  padding: 20px;
  border: 1px solid black;
}

#grid > div:hover {
  background-color: yellow;
}
``` 

This script creates a grid from the given array data. When the data in a cell is clicked, its value is alerted. This grid content adjusts according to the window size to maintain the responsiveness. The first row has a different style to indicate it as a header. Future data can easily be added to the 'arrayData' variable, and the grid would adjust accordingly.

Upon clicking the header cells, sorting of the respective columns takes place. But, this is a very basic implementation, to fully handle CSV data parsing, and to provide a full feature sorting you might want to use dedicated libraries or plugins like d3.js, jQuery DataTables etc. 

Also, it is strongly recommended to sanitize and validate your data properly before using it in production code to prevent XSS and other attacks.
<|end|>


