Write a procedure in PL/SQL to count the number of employees in a company, considering only full-time employees who have been working for at least 6 months.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Assuming that we have an "Employees" table with fields like "full_time" (indicating whether the employee is full-time or not) and "hire_date" (indicating when the employee was hired), the following is a PL/SQL procedure that will count the number of employees satisfying above conditions:

```PL/SQL
CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE Count_FullTime_Employees AS
    v_count NUMBER(8);
BEGIN
   SELECT COUNT(*) INTO v_count
   FROM Employees
   WHERE full_time ='Yes' AND hire_date <= SYSDATE - INTERVAL '6' MONTH;

   DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Number of Full Time Employees working for at least 6 months: ' || v_count);
EXCEPTION
   WHEN OTHERS THEN
      DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('An error has occurred: ' || SQLERRM);
END Count_FullTime_Employees;
/
```

In this procedure, I've decided to name it as "Count_FullTime_Employees". It calculates the number of full-time employees with a hire_date earlier than or equal to 6 months before the current date and stores that count in v_count variable. DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE is used to display that count. If any error occurs during programme execution, an exception block catches that error and an error message is printed.
<|end|>


