I was looking to create Android MotionEvents to simulate a pinch (scale) gesture.  There wasn't any good documentation, so I am adding my solution below.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here are the steps needed to manually create the MotionEvent events necessary to perform a pinch using instrumentation:

1. Determine the start and end points for each finger. These will be `(startX1,startY1)`, `(endX1,endY1)`, `(startX2,startY2)`, `(endX2,endY2)`.
2. Get the current time in ms: `long downTime = SystemClock.uptimeMillis();`
3. Create `ACTION_DOWN` event for first finger

   ```java
   MotionEvent downEvent = MotionEvent.obtain(downTime, downTime, 
                                       MotionEvent.ACTION_DOWN, startX1, startY1, 0);
   ```

4. Create `ACTION_POINTER_DOWN` event for second finger

   ```java
   MotionEvent.PointerProperties[] properties = new MotionEvent.PointerProperties[2];
   MotionEvent.PointerProperties pp1 = new MotionEvent.PointerProperties();
   pp1.id = 0;
   pp1.toolType = MotionEvent.TOOL_TYPE_FINGER;

   MotionEvent.PointerProperties pp2 = new MotionEvent.PointerProperties();
   pp2.id = 1;
   pp2.toolType = MotionEvent.TOOL_TYPE_FINGER;

   MotionEvent.PointerCoords[] pointerCoords = new MotionEvent.PointerCoords[2];
   MotionEvent.PointerCoords pc1 = new MotionEvent.PointerCoords();
   pc1.x = startX1;
   pc1.y = startY1;
   pc1.pressure = 1;
   pc1.size = 1;

   MotionEvent.PointerCoords pc2 = new MotionEvent.PointerCoords();
   pc2.x = startX2;
   pc2.y = startY2;
   pc2.pressure = 1;
   pc2.size = 1;

   properties[0] = pp1;
   properties[1] = pp2;

   pointerCoords[0] = pc1;
   pointerCoords[1] = pc2;

   MotionEvent event = MotionEvent.obtain(downTime, SystemClock.uptimeMillis(), 
                    MotionEvent.ACTION_POINTER_DOWN + (pp2.id << MotionEvent.ACTION_POINTER_INDEX_SHIFT), 
                                        2, properties, pointerCoords, 
                                        0,  0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0);
   ```

5. Create intermediate `ACTION_MOVE` events

    You can generate these in a similar way to how you created the `ACTION_POINTER_DOWN` event. You will want to generate many of these events, each time increasing the x,y values for each pointer until they reach their respective end x,y values.

6. Create `ACTION_POINTER_UP` event for the first finger. Make sure you shift the index correctly. If you are lifting the second finger, you need to shift the index to the second place. 

7. Create `ACTION_UP` event for the second finger.

Make sure to inject these events using `Instrumentation.sendPointerSync(motionEvent)`. To make the gesture smoother, you can sleep the thread for a little while between sending each event. In addition, make sure to recycle your `MotionEvent`s once you have sent them to release their native resources.
<|end|>


