Design a function that transforms a singly linked list into a balanced binary search tree (BST), ensuring the resultant structure adheres to the BST properties. This function should be capable of handling nested singly linked lists, converting them into nested BSTs, and should also be proficient in dealing with a mixture of singly linked lists and other data types within the same BST.

Additionally, the function should be adept at handling singly linked lists where nodes themselves are singly linked lists, converting these into BSTs where the root node is a BST representation of the node singly linked list and the subsequent node is the value. The function should also be proficient in handling situations where the values in the singly linked list are singly linked lists of singly linked lists, converting these into BSTs of BSTs.

The function should be prepared to handle any level of nesting and should be proficient in handling singly linked lists of any size. The function should also be prepared to handle scenarios where the singly linked lists contain recursive references.

The function should be designed in a way that is efficient in terms of both time and space complexity. The BST should be balanced and the function should also return the height of the BST. The function should also be proficient in handling duplicate values in the singly linked list, and position them in the BST according to the rules of a BST.

Furthermore, the function should be proficient in handling circular singly linked lists and singly linked lists with null values. The function should also be proficient in handling singly linked lists with negative values and sort them accordingly in the BST.

To increase the difficulty, the function should also be able to handle singly linked lists with alphanumeric values, special characters, boolean values, floating point numbers, complex numbers, and sort them accordingly in the BST. 

Moreover, the function should be able to handle singly linked lists with custom objects and sort them according to a provided comparator function. The function should also be proficient in handling singly linked lists with multiple data types and sort them according to a provided sorting rule. The function should also be proficient in handling singly linked lists with non-comparable data types and throw an appropriate error. The function should also be proficient in handling singly linked lists with missing values and sort them accordingly in the BST.

To further increase the difficulty, the function should be able to handle singly linked lists with data types that are not inherently sortable, such as custom objects without a natural ordering or comparison method. In these cases, the function should accept a custom comparison function as an argument, which defines the ordering of these custom objects. If no such function is provided, the function should throw an appropriate error. 

Additionally, the function should be able to handle and sort linked lists with multiple levels of nesting and should be able to handle and sort linked lists with a mixture of data types, including but not limited to, integers, strings, booleans, and custom objects.
