Say I have $N$ samples of 100 numbers all drawn IID from the same distribution $\mathcal{D}$. For each sample, I take the 95th quantile to get $N$ sample quantiles $\hat{q}_n$. Will taking the average of these sample quantiles $\mathbb{E}[\hat{q}_n]$ estimate the true quantile of the population distribution $\mathcal{D}$?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, taking the average of the sample quantiles should give you a good estimate of the true population quantile. 

This approach to estimating quantiles is based on the law of large numbers, a fundamental theorem of probability and statistics that states that the average of the results obtained from a large number of trials should be close to the expected value, and will tend to become closer as more trials are performed. 

So, if you take numerous samples from the population, calculate the 95th quantile for each, and then average these values, you should expect to get a value that's very close to the true 95th quantile of the population distribution. 

However, this doesn't imply that the estimate will be exact or even unbiased. In practice, the accuracy and bias of this approach would depend on the specific characteristics of the distribution (such as skewness and kurtosis) and the number of samples N. It may be better or worse than other estimation methods depending on these conditions. So it is often recommended to use other robust statistical methods and tools available. 

Lastly, it's not only mean of sample quantiles that can be used for the estimation. Median, for instance, can give you a more robust estimation - less sensitive to outliers.
<|end|>


