Almost every Python web framework has a simple server that runs a wsgi application and automatically reloads the imported modules every time the source gets changed. I know I can look at the code and see how it's done, but that may take some time and I'm asking just out of curiosity. Does anyone have any idea how this is implemented?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Python web frameworks such as Flask or Django generally use the Werkzeug WSGI utility library for their development server which has the feature of automatically reloading the server when source code changes.

This hot-reload feature is implemented by periodically polling all imported Python modules file timestamp or a hash of their content to detect any changes (addition/modification/deletion). If a change is detected, the server will restart, hence reload all Python modules.

To achieve this, when the server is first started, it forks off a child process. This child process is the actual server. The parent process then continuously monitors the source code files. If it detects any changes, it will terminate the child process and fork a new one. This ensures all the Python modules are reloaded implicitly because it's brand new Python interpreter process.

Werkzeug and the Django Development Server take advantages of libraries like inotify on Linux and ReadDirectoryChangesW on Windows to accomplish this task very efficiently, only when there are file system events.

For Werkzeug specifically, it's implemented in the `werkzeug.serving.run_with_reloader()` function along with `werkzeug._reloader_reloader_loop()` and `_iter_module_files()` functions.

Also, note that this feature is supposed to be used only in the development servers. In a production environment, you would not want to use it because it has the overhead of periodically polling the filesystem. In a production environment, you'd use a WSGI server like gunicorn or uWSGI instead, possibly behind a reverse proxy like Nginx.
<|end|>


