<|user|>
Using a paired t-test, is it possible to test only one group against all other groups instead of testing each pair of groups against each other? If yes, would bonferroni correction work the same way as for comparing each pair of groups (simply dividing $\alpha$ by the number of performed tests)?
Example: I want to compare group A to group B, C and D using paired tests, which are A-B, A-C, A-D. My global $\alpha$ value has to be corrected by dividing it by 3.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In the scenario you outlined, the corrected value of alpha would indeed be $\alpha$ divided by 3 (the number of tests) if you’re using Bonferroni correction. However, it’s important to note that the Bonferroni correction controls the familywise error rate (the probability of making at least one Type I error) and not the per-comparison error rate.

Keep in mind that while this approach is statistically valid, it is not without its caveats. The Bonferroni correction is known to be quite conservative. If you have a relatively large number of comparisons that you're making, it can greatly increase the odds of a type II error (failing to reject the null hypothesis when it's actually false). This can be a particular issue when your comparisons are not all independent. 

As for the first part of your question, yes, in principle you could do a series of paired t-tests to compare one group to each other group one at a time. However, this is generally not the most statistically power or precise way to do such comparisons. 

A better approach is to use a one-way ANOVA if the data are normally distributed or a Kruskal-Wallis test for data that are not normally distributed, followed by post hoc comparisons with appropriate correction for multiple testing, if the overall test is significant. These tests would analyze all the data together and provide a more sensitive and accurate analysis overall. 

So, while the approach you describe can be used in some cases, it won't yield the most reliable or comprehensive results in more complex scenarios, and it is better to use a more rigorous statistical method if possible.
<|end|>


