Design a function that takes in three lists as parameters. The first two lists consist of integer elements, while the third list contains a series of operations represented as strings. These operations could be "merge", "common_elements", "unique_elements", "sort_in_descending", "element_difference", "length_of_list", "sum_of_elements", "average_of_elements", "max_element", "min_element", "flatten_nested_list", "remove_duplicates", "find_median", "find_mode", "find_range". The function should perform the operations on the first two lists sequentially as they appear in the third list. The result of each operation should be displayed in descending order of elements. If an operation is not possible due to the nature of the lists (for example, unique_elements operation when the first list is identical to the second), the function should return an appropriate error message.

Additionally, the function should handle incorrect inputs and edge cases. For instance, if the operation list includes an operation that is not defined, the function should return an error message. If the operation list does not contain any operations, the function should return the original lists. If the first two lists do not contain any elements, the function should return an error message.

The function should also be able to handle nested lists and perform operations on them. For example, if the first list is [1, 2, [3, 4]], the function should be able to perform operations on the nested list as well.

To increase the difficulty, provide a piece of incorrect code as a reference to create confusion. The candidate should be able to identify and correct the errors in the code. The function should also be able to handle large lists efficiently without causing a system crash or taking too much time. The function should also be able to handle lists with mixed data types (integers, floats, strings) and perform operations accordingly.
