Combining SVGs involves placing the paths of both SVGs, along with any other properties they have, within the same SVG tag. Below is a step-by-step guide to doing this:

Step 1: Obtain your second SVG.
To provide a distinct example, let's take the following SVG:

```xml
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="40" height="40" fill="currentColor" class="bi bi-arrow-right-circle" viewBox="0 0 16 16">
  <path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M8 0a8 8 0 1 0 0 16A8 8 0 0 0 8 0zm3.146 7.646a.5.5 0 0 1 0 .708l-4 4a.5.5 0 0 1-.708-.708L10.293 8l-3.647-3.646a.5.5 0 0 1 .708-.708l4 4z"/>
</svg>
```

Step 2: Open the SVG tag.
The tag is opened with `<svg>`. Inside this tag, specify any attributes that apply to the entire SVG. If the sizes or views of the two SVGs are different, you may have to adjust them to fit them together. 

```xml
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="40" height="40" fill="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 16 16">
```

Step 3: Add the paths from both SVGs.

Below the opening svg tag, include the paths from the original SVG and the second one:

```xml
<path d="M4.08027 5C2.37925 5 1 6.37934 1 8.08034V26.0154C1 26.7697 1.17005 .../>
<path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M8 0a8 8 0 1 0 0 16A8 8 0 0 0 8 0zm3.146 7.646a.5.5 0 0 1 .../>
```
Step 4: Close the SVG tag.

Finally, end the SVG with a closing `</svg>` tag:

```xml
</svg>
```
The final code should look like this:

```xml
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="40" height="40" fill="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 16 16">
<path d="M4.08027 5C2.37925 5 1 6.37934 1 8.08034V26.0154C1 26.7697 1.17005 .../>
<path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M8 0a8 8 0 1 0 0 16A8 8 0 0 0 8 0zm3.146 7.646a.5.5 0 0 1 .../>
</svg>
```

Please remember that combining SVGs like this will overlay them on top of each other. If you want to position them side-by-side or in any particular manner, additional steps may be required.
