Improve the following code:
The function should take a square matrix as input, return a new square matrix where odd rows are the same as the original, but the even rows are sorted in ascending order. Additionally, only use C++ STL and no helper functions. For example...
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's how you can improve code using C++ STL to handle this requirement:

```c++ 
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

std::vector<std::vector<int>> sortEvenRows(std::vector<std::vector<int>> matrix) {
    for(int i = 0; i < matrix.size(); i++)
        if(i % 2 == 1)
            std::sort(matrix[i].begin(), matrix[i].end());

    return matrix;
}
```

This function starts with an iteration over all rows of the input matrix. It checks if the row index is even or not by getting the remainder when divided by 2. If the remainder is 1 (which means the index is even from a 0-based perspective) it sorts the elements in that row in ascending order using the std::sort function. After processing all rows, it returns the created matrix.

Please note, it follows a 0-based index so even rows become odd rows and vice versa in 1-based index perspective.
I've not created any helper functions according to the problem requirement.
<|end|>


