Develop an algorithm that can parse arbitrary XML data structures into JSON objects, whilst maintaining all attributes, text fields, and hierarchical relationships present in the original XML. The solution should be able to handle edge cases such as self-closing tags, nested tags, and multiple attributes.

Consider the following example:

```XML
<Root>
  <items type="letters">
    <item id="1">A</item>
    <item id="2">B</item>
    <item id="3">
      <subItem>C1</subItem>
      <subItem>C2</subItem>
    </item>
  </items>
  <items type="numbers">
    <item id="1">1</item>
    <item id="2">2</item>
    <item id="3">3</item>
  </items>
</Root>
```

The algorithm should not be limited to this particular XML structure and should be able to parse different XML formats. The efficiency of your parsing algorithm will be part of the evaluation criteria.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
I will use Python to solve this problem using xml.etree.ElementTree library to parse XML and json to generate JSON object.

Here is the Python code:

```python
import json
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET

def xmlToJson(element):
    node = {}

    text = getattr(element, 'text', None)
    if text is not None:
        node['text'] = text.strip()

    attributes = element.attrib
    if attributes:
        node['attributes'] = attributes

    children = list(element)
    if children:
        node['children'] = [xmlToJson(child) for child in children]

    return {element.tag: node}

def parse(xml_data):
    root = ET.fromstring(xml_data)
    json_data = json.dumps(xmlToJson(root), indent=4)
    return json_data
```

This script first parses the XML to a tree-like structure using ElementTree library. Then it recursively goes through each element(node) and its children in the tree and collects its tag, attributes, text, and children (if any) into a dictionary. Finally, this function dumps the dictionary into a JSON string with a pretty indentation of 4 spaces.

To run this function on the given XML data, you can use the following code:

```python
xml_data = """
<Root>
  <items type="letters">
    <item id="1">A</item>
    <item id="2">B</item>
    <item id="3">
      <subItem>C1</subItem>
      <subItem>C2</subItem>
    </item>
  </items>
  <items type="numbers">
    <item id="1">1</item>
    <item id="2">2</item>
    <item id="3">3</item>
  </items>
</Root>
"""

print(parse(xml_data))
```
  
This script is general enough to parse various XML structures. The efficiency mainly depends on Python's built-in xml.etree.ElementTree library and json library. The time complexity is O(n), where n is the number of elements(nodes) in the XML data.

Notice: This script does not handle XML attributes with complex nested structures. For XML with advanced feature such as namespaces, there needs a more complex parser.
<|end|>


