Also, for unit testing, you can use Jest and Enzyme. This descriptive pseudo-code will give you an idea of how to structure and write the code for your table component:

1. Create your redux store:
```javascript
//store.js
import { createStore, applyMiddleware } from 'redux';
import thunk from 'redux-thunk';
import rootReducer from './reducers';

const store = createStore(
   rootReducer,
   applyMiddleware(thunk)
);

export default store;
```

2. Create your action to fetch data from your source:
```javascript
// actions/cityActions.js
export const fetchCities = () => async (dispatch) => {
  // fetch data from source
  const response = await fetch('your data source url');
  const data = await response.json();

  dispatch({
    type: 'FETCH_CITIES',
    payload: data,
  });
};
```

3. Create your reducer to handle state:
```javascript
// reducers/cityReducer.js
const cityReducer = (state = [], action) => {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'FETCH_CITIES':
      return action.payload;
    default:
      return state;
  }
};

export default cityReducer;

//Then combine your reducers
// reducers/index.js
import { combineReducers } from 'redux';
import cities from './cityReducer';

const rootReducer = combineReducers({
  cities //Your other reducers if any,
});

export default rootReducer;
```

4. Create your table component to display fetched cities:
```javascript
// components/CityTable.js
import React, { useEffect } from 'react';
import { connect } from 'react-redux';
import { fetchCities } from '../actions/cityActions';

const CityTable = ({ fetchCities, cities }) => {
  useEffect(() => {
    fetchCities();
  }, [fetchCities]);

  return ( /* code to display sortable, filterable table */ );
};

const mapStateToProps = state => ({
  cities: state.cities
});

const mapDispatchToProps = dispatch => ({
  fetchCities: () => dispatch(fetchCities())
});

export default connect(
  mapStateToProps,
  mapDispatchToProps
)(CityTable);
```

5. For unit testing you can use a testing library like Jest:
```javascript
// tests/CityTable.test.js
import React from 'react';
import { shallow } from 'enzyme';
import { CityTable } from '../components/CityTable';

it('renders without crashing', () => {
  shallow(<CityTable />);
});

// Add additional tests as needed.
```

Remember to replace 'your data source url' with your actual data source url. The basic concept here is to fetch data with a Redux action creator, store it in your Redux store, then connect your React component to the Redux store enabling your component to access and display the data. You may need to modify some parts of the code to fully meet your own needs or set up, as this is just a snippet rather than a fully working solution.
