Given the following erroneous code: 

```R
wrong_df <- data.frame(Name = c("John", "Jane", "Bob", 10), 
                       Age = c(25, "Mike", 34, 45), 
                       Country = "US", "Canada", "Japan", "Australia")
                       
print(wrong_df)
```

When you try to run it, it won't work as expected due to inappropriate data typing and syntactical errors.

Your task is to correct this code in R programming language. Create a dataframe that contains three columns: 'Name', 'Age', and 'Country', and populate these columns with five specific data entries each. Moreover, once the data frame has been created, add a new row to this dataframe and then implement a function that will sort data entries according to the 'Age' column in descending order. After that, filter out all rows that have an age above 35.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To solve the problem, first, we will define the correct dataframe structure and then we will add a new row. After that, we will create a function that sorts the dataframe by the column 'Age' in a descending order. In end, we will filter the rows according to the 'Age' column. 

Here is the correct code:

```R
# Load dplyr for bind_rows and filter functions
library(dplyr)

# Correct code for creating dataframe
correct_df <- data.frame(Name = c("John", "Jane", "Bob", "Mike", "Alice"), 
                         Age = c(25, 30, 34, 45, 50), 
                         Country = c("US", "Canada", "Japan", "Australia", "UK"), 
                         stringsAsFactors=FALSE)

# Add a new row
new_row <- data.frame(Name = "Sara", Age = 37, Country = "Germany", stringsAsFactors=FALSE)
correct_df <- bind_rows(correct_df, new_row)

# Create a function to sort data entries according to 'Age'
sort_df <- function(df){
  sorted_df <- df[order(-df$Age),]
  return(sorted_df)
}

# Sort the 'correct_df'
correct_df <- sort_df(correct_df)

# Filter out all rows that have an age above 35
correct_df <- correct_df %>%
  filter(Age <= 35)

# Print the dataframe
print(correct_df)
```
You can modify this code according to your needs. Note that 'bind_rows' function from the dplyr package is used to add a new row to a dataframe and 'filter' function is used to filter the rows.
