It seems you're trying to break apart an SVG (Scalable Vector Graphic) into different components. Here's a general approach on how to do it:

1. **Understand SVG elements:** SVGs are made from simple shapes like `<circle>`, `<rect>`, `<polyline>`, `<polygon>`, etc. Each of these shapes are defined within `<path>` elements.

2. **Inspect SVG:** You can open your SVG code in a text editor and see that each part of the image is defined within `<path>` tags. Each `<path>` is a separate part of the SVG.

To break your SVG into individual parts, you can separate each `<path>` element into its own SVG file. Remember to keep the SVG header (up to `xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">`) and closing tag `</svg>` intact for each file.

3. **Separate `<path>` elements:** Once you have separated each `<path>` into its own file, you now have individual parts of your SVG. You can now manipulate each part separately as an individual SVG file.

4. **Consolidation:** If you want to use all these parts together in a webpage, consolidate all the parts in HTML by giving different `id` attributes to each SVG and controlling them through JavaScript or CSS. 

Remember, working with SVGs can get complex as they can contain a lot of path commands, and modifying them manually would require a thorough understanding of these commands. Consider using a SVG editor like Adobe Illustrator, Inkscape, or an online SVG editor for easier manipulation of the SVG paths. You can also use JavaScript libraries such as Snap.svg or Raphaël for SVG manipulation in webpages.
