Show me how this would look in C++:
def custom_histogram(test):
    # removing punctuation and making it lowercase
    test = ''.join(e for e in test.lower() if e.isalnum() or e.isspace())
    
    word_count = {}
    for word in test.split():
        # incrementing count if already present else inserting it
        word_count[word + '_' + str(test.count(word))] = test.count(word)

    return word_count
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a translation of your Python code into C++:

```cpp
#include <map>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>
#include <sstream>

std::map<std::string, int> custom_histogram(std::string test) {
    // lower case
    std::transform(test.begin(), test.end(), test.begin(),
                   [](unsigned char c){ return std::tolower(c); });

    // remove non-alphanumeric characters
    test.erase(std::remove_if(test.begin(), test.end(),
                              [](char c) { return !std::isalnum(c) && !std::isspace(c); }),
               test.end());

    std::map<std::string, int> word_count;

    std::string word;
    std::istringstream iss(test);
    while (iss >> word) {
        int count = std::count(test.begin(), test.end(), word);
        word_count[word + "_" + std::to_string(count)] = count;
    }
    
    return word_count;
}
```
Note: One limitation of this implementation is that the word count isn't accurate. This version counts individual instances of a string anywhere in the text, not just whole words – so it would treat "bob" and "bobby" as having the same word "bob" in them, and increase the counts accordingly.

A more accurate way to find the words and their counts would require a more complex algorithm, perhaps involving first splitting the string into words, and then counting how many times each word appears in the resulting list of words. But such implementation might leave us with a solution not as clean as in Python, mostly because C++ Standard Library doesn't offer all Python's facilities in its libraries, like the 'split' method in Python.
<|end|>


