<|user|>
On IIS 6, what does an IIS reset do? 

Please compare to recycling an app pool and stopping and starting an ASP.NET web site.

If you replace a DLL or edit/replace the web.config on an ASP.NET web site is that the same as stopping and starting that web site?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
IIS Reset is a command line tool that restarts the Internet Information Services (IIS) as well as all its associated services such as SMTP, NNTP, and FTP. This is advantageous when you want all changes to be applied immediately; however, this also means that internet services will be temporarily unavailable to users.

On the other hand, recycling an app pool is specific to a particular application pool and does not affect other app pools running to other sites. This is more efficient as it only reloads the specific application pool rather than all the app pools, providing minimal disruption to users.

Stopping and starting an ASP.NET website is another way of applying updates or changes to a website, and it only affects the specific website which is stopped and started, not the whole server. Hence, it's less disruptive than an IIS Reset but more disruptive than app pool recycling since it stops the entire site.

Finally, when you replace a DLL or edit/replace the web.config on an ASP.NET website, it does automatically force a restart of the app domain, which is similar to an app pool recycle or stopping and starting that web site. However, it does not affect any other app domains or websites since the process is isolated to the changed web site only.
<|end|>


