Here is a rough outline of how to approach this task. Since this is a complex problem and requires a deep understanding of both the domain and programming, I won't be providing complete code. You can always refer to this outline while developing the solution.

```python
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split
from sklearn.preprocessing import MinMaxScaler
from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression
from sklearn.metrics import r2_score, mean_squared_error
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

# Loading data
df = pd.read_csv('https://github.com/owid/co2-data')

# -------------------------Data Preprocessing-------------------------
# Check for missing values
df.isnull().sum()

# Depending on the number of missing values, you can either drop them or fill them using various strategies. 
df.dropna(inplace=True)

# Convert date/time data to a format useful for regression analysis
df['date'] = pd.to_datetime(df['date'])
df['year'] = df['date'].dt.year

# Split data into training and testing sets
train_df, test_df = train_test_split(df, test_size=0.20, random_state=42)

# Define features and target
features = df.columns.tolist()
features.remove('co2')

X_train = train_df[features]
y_train = train_df['co2']

X_test = test_df[features]
y_test = test_df['co2']

# Scale the features
scaler = MinMaxScaler()
X_train_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X_train)
X_test_scaled = scaler.transform(X_test)

#-------------------------Model Selection-------------------------
# As a beginner, we can start modelling data using simple linear regression 
lin_reg = LinearRegression()
lin_reg.fit(X_train_scaled, y_train)

#-------------------------Model Evaluation-------------------------
# Use appropriate metrics to evaluate model's performance. These can include R-square, Mean Squared Error etc.

# Check the coefficient of determination (R²) to see how well our model is fitting the training data
print('R^2 score on training set: ', lin_reg.score(X_train_scaled, y_train))

# Predict on test dataset and evaluate
y_pred = lin_reg.predict(X_test_scaled)

# computing metrics
print("R^2 score: ", r2_score(y_test, y_pred))
print("Mean Squared Error: ", mean_squared_error(y_test, y_pred))

#-------------------------Data Visualization-------------------------
# Combine actual and predicted values into a single DataFrame for visualization
true_vs_predicted = pd.DataFrame({'Actual': y_test, 'Predicted': y_pred})

# plotting
true_vs_predicted.plot(kind='line', figsize=(10,8))
plt.grid(which='both')
plt.title('Actual vs Predicted CO2 Emissions')
plt.xlabel('Years')
plt.ylabel('CO2 Emissions')
plt.show()
```

**NOTE:** This is a simplified and beginner-friendly approach to the task, and it serves merely to illustrate how you may want to approach the problem. Real-world data, depending on their complexity, may require more sophisticated techniques, such as handling of outliers, feature scaling, more advanced predictive models, fine-tuning of the model, etc. It's important to always begin with exploratory data analysis to understand the nature and characteristics of the data.
