Write a RUST program that determines the second highest value in an array of signed 32-bit integers.
[23,45,1,2,6,89,99,34]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a simple solution to the problem in RUST.

Firstly, we will sort the array and then print the second highest value. RUST uses 'slice' instead of array. So, we will convert the array to slice before sorting.

```rust
fn main() {
    let mut numbers = [23, 45, 1, 2, 6, 89, 99, 34];
    numbers.sort_unstable();  // sorts the array in increasing order
    let second_highest = numbers[numbers.len() - 2];  // -2 since index starts from 0
    println!("{}", second_highest);
}
```

In this script above, we firstly declare and initialize a mutable variable as array. Later, we sort the array in increasing order. Finally, we find the second largest number in the sorted array by using the length of the array minus 2 (since the array index starts from 0). 

The second highest number is the second number from the right in a array sorted in increasing order. 

Please note that we sort the array in-place (without allocating new memory) which is more efficient. If your array is large, this will be significantly faster and use less memory. 

Understand that there's the unstable sort to avoid unnecessary work. If you don't care about stability, it's recommended to just use the unstable sort.
<|end|>


