Sure, here's a Java version of the Python function you provided:

```java
import java.util.List;

public class Main{
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Test the method
        List<Integer> list1 = Arrays.asList(1,2,3,4,5);
        List<Integer> list2 = Arrays.asList(6,7,8,9,10);
        System.out.println(modification(list1, list2));
    }
    
    public static String modification(List<Integer> list1, List<Integer> list2){
        int sum1 = sumList(list1);
        int sum2 = sumList(list2);
        
        if ((sum1 + sum2) % 2 != 0){
            return "NO";
        } else {
            return "YES";
        }
    }

    public static int sumList(List<Integer> list){
        int sum = 0;

        for(Integer i : list){
            sum += i;
        }

        return sum;
    }
}
```
How this code works:

- `modification()` method: The Java version takes in two generic `List<Integer>`’s as their parameters, list1 and list2.  Each list will be separately passed to the `sumList` method where the sum will be calculated. The sum of both lists will then be added together. If that sum is not evenly divisible by 2 (i.e., `if ((sum1 + sum2) % 2 != 0)`), then "NO" is returned. If it is evenly divisible by 2, then "YES" is returned.

- `sumList()` method: This function accepts a generic `List<Integer>` as an argument and iterates through each element in the list, adding its value to the 'sum' variable. Once all elements have been added, the total sum is returned.

Please note that Java doesn't have built-in sum function like Python for collections. So, we have to create our own function to sum the list items.

