Devise an iterative algorithm in C#, following the principles of recursion, to display all unique, natural numbers greater than 1, with only two distinct positive divisors, 1 and the number itself, within the numerical range from 1 to 100. Additionally, calculate and print their sum and the total number of these prime numbers.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a C# implementation that performs the task:

```csharp
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        int sumOfPrimes = 0;
        int countOfPrimes = 0;
      
        for(int i = 2; i <= 100; i++)
        {
            if(isPrime(i))
            {
                Console.WriteLine(i);
                sumOfPrimes += i;
                countOfPrimes++;
            }
        }

        Console.WriteLine("Sum of primes: " + sumOfPrimes);
        Console.WriteLine("Count of primes: " + countOfPrimes);
    }

    static bool isPrime(int number)
    {
        if (number == 1) return false;
        if (number == 2) return true;

        for (int i = 2; i <= Math.Sqrt(number); i++)
        {
            if (number % i == 0) return false;
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

This program calculates prime numbers in the range 1 to 100 using the IsPrime function. Sum and count of primes are calculated inside the main loop and displayed after the loop. The IsPrime function checks if a number is prime by trying to divide it from 2 to its square root (inclusive). If any division returns a remainder of zero, it indicates that the number is not prime; otherwise, it is prime. In the main method, each number between 2 and 100 (inclusive) is checked for primality, and if so, printed, added to the sum, and counted. Finally, the sum and count of primes are printed.
<|end|>


