I am seeking a method to rearrange the columns of a 2D numpy array in a specific, arbitrary order. For instance, consider the array `a`:
array([[10, 20, 30, 40, 50],
       [ 6,  7,  8,  9, 10]])
I aim to transform it into:
array([[10, 30, 50, 40, 20],
       [ 6,  8, 10,  9,  7]])
by applying the permutation
0 -&gt; 0
1 -&gt; 4
2 -&gt; 1
3 -&gt; 3
4 -&gt; 2
to the columns. In the transformed matrix, the first column remains the same, the second column becomes the last, and so forth. Is there a specific numpy function that can accomplish this? Given the large size of my matrix, which is expected to grow, I require a swift and in-place solution (permutation matrices are not feasible).
Appreciate your help.
A:
&lt;code&gt;
import numpy as np
a = np.array([[10, 20, 30, 40, 50],
       [ 6,  7,  8,  9, 10]])
permutation = [0, 4, 1, 3, 2]
&lt;/code&gt;
a = ... # put solution in this variable
BEGIN SOLUTION
&lt;code&gt;
<|end|>
<|asst|>
a = a[:, permutation]
&lt;/code&gt;
END SOLUTION
This code reorders the columns according to the specified permutation. It is swift because it leverages numpy's advanced indexing functionality. However, note that it does not provide an in-place solution: a new array is created and the original array is left unmodified. At present, numpy does not support in-place reordering of columns (or rows) using advanced indexing.
