```java
import com.rabbitmq.client.*;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeoutException;

public class QueueConsumer {
    private final static String QUEUE_NAME = "queue-name";

    public static void main(String[] argv) {
        ConnectionFactory factory = new ConnectionFactory();
        factory.setHost("localhost"); // Update this based on your configuration

        try {
            Connection connection = factory.newConnection();
            Channel channel = connection.createChannel();
            channel.queueDeclare(QUEUE_NAME, false, false, false, null);

            DeliverCallback deliverCallback = (consumerTag, delivery) -> {
                String message = new String(delivery.getBody(), "UTF-8");
                // TODO: Interpret and execute the message appropriately
                System.out.println(" [x] Received '" + message + "'");
            };
            channel.basicConsume(QUEUE_NAME, true, deliverCallback, consumerTag -> {});
        } catch (IOException | TimeoutException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

This example shows a basic consumer that receives messages from a specific queue. When it gets a message, it will print out the string representation of that message. 

You will note that the interpretation and execution of the message is not implemented in this code. To add that, you would replace the TODO comment with code that interprets and performs the necessary actions based on the content of the message. 

This implementation will automatically acknowledge messages after they are received. It's important to note that if your message interpretation and execution code could throw exceptions, you should modify this code to only acknowledge messages when they have been successfully interpreted and executed. Failure to do so could cause a message that can't be processed to be removed from the queue, which might not be the desired behavior.

Also, please ensure you have the RabbitMQ Java Client library installed in your project. If not, you can add the following to your Maven `pom.xml` file:
```xml
<dependency>
  <groupId>com.rabbitmq</groupId>
  <artifactId>amqp-client</artifactId>
