Given a piece of erroneous CSS code segment, rectify and enhance it to customize the visual aesthetic of hyperlinks within the context of a specified webpage. Additionally, include code to manage the state of hyperlinks (like hover, visited and active). Ensure that your solution considers a responsive design for mobile platforms.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Rectifying a CSS code segment requires the erroneous code in question. However, below is an exemplar solution of CSS styles for hyperlinks, taking into consideration hover, visited, active states, and responsiveness for mobile platforms.

```css
/* Mobile first design */
a {
    color: #000000;
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 500;
}

a:visited {
    color: #555555;
}

a:hover {
    color: #FF0000;
    text-decoration: underline;
}

a:active {
    color: #0000FF;
}

/* For landscape phones and down */
@media (max-width: 480px) {
    a {
        font-size: 16px;
    }
}

/* For tablets and up */
@media (min-width: 768px) {
    a {
        font-size: 18px;
    }
}

/* For desktops and up */
@media (min-width: 992px) {
    a {
        font-size: 20px;
    }
}
```

This CSS code customizes the look of hyperlinks:

1. Hyperlinks have a color of black (`#000000`), no text decoration, and a font weight of `500` by default.
2. When a hyperlink has been visited, the color changes to a darker grey (`#555555`).
3. When hovering, the color becomes red (`#FF0000`) and an underline appears.
4. During the click (also called ‘active’ state), the color becomes blue (`#0000FF`).

For responsiveness (i.e., different screen sizes):

1. Landscape phones and smaller screens will have a font size of `16px`.
2. Tablets and larger screens will have a font size of `18px`.
3. Desktops and larger screens will have a font size of `20px`.

Remember to adjust the pixel values according to your design requirements.
<|end|>


