<|asst|>
Here's a revision of the Java code to solve this problem:

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.lang.*;

class Solution {
    public List<String> oddCount(List<String> lst) {
        List<String> resultList = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < lst.size(); i++) {
            String currStr = lst.get(i);
            int count = 0;

            // Count the number of odd digits
            for (char c : currStr.toCharArray()) {
                int digit = Character.getNumericValue(c);
                if (digit % 2 != 0) {
                    count++;
                }
            }

            // Generate the new string
            String newStr = "the number of odd elements " + count + "n the str" + count + "ng " + count + " of the " + count + "nput.";
            resultList.add(newStr);
        }

        return resultList;
    }
}
```
Here, `oddCount` method processes each individual string in the list. For each string, it counts the number of odd digits, forms the new string that needs to be added to the result list (where it replaces each 'i' with the corresponding count of odd digits), then adds this new string to the result list. 

The resulting list of strings is then returned.
<|end|>


