In JavaScript, we can solve this problem as shown below:

```javascript
const isPalindrome = (text) => {
    // Convert text to lowercase and remove non-alphanumeric characters and spaces
    text = text.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]/gi, '');
  
    // Reverse the processed text
    let reversedText = text.split("").reverse().join("");
  
    // Check if processed text and its reversed form are same
    return text === reversedText;
}

console.log(isPalindrome('Able was I ere I saw Elba'));  // Returns true
console.log(isPalindrome('Was it a car or a cat I saw?'));  // Returns true
console.log(isPalindrome('Red roses'));  // Returns false
```

In this solution, we first convert the given string to lowercase and remove all non-alphanumeric characters (such as spaces, punctuation etc.) as these should not affect whether a phrase is a palindrome or not. Then, we reverse this processed string and compare it with the original string. If they are same, it means the given string is a palindrome.
