Craft a Ruby program that transforms a binary tree data structure into a doubly-linked list.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a Ruby solution for transforming a binary tree data structure into a doubly-linked list. The main idea behind the solution is to perform an in-order traversal of the tree and attach the previous node with the currently traversed node. 

In this program I'm assuming that the each node in the binary tree has left, right and data attributes. Similarly for the doubly linked list the Node has next, prev and data attributes.

```ruby
class Node
  attr_accessor :data, :left, :right, :next, :prev
  def initialize(data)
    @data = data
    @left = nil
    @right = nil
    @next = nil
    @prev = nil
  end
end

def binaryTreeToDLL(root)
  return nil if root.nil?
 
  n = Node.new(root.data)
  
  if root.left
    n.prev = binaryTreeToDLL(root.left)
    n.prev.next = n if n.prev
  end

  if root.right
    n.next = binaryTreeToDLL(root.right)
    n.next.prev = n if n.next
  end
  
  return n
end

def print_dll(head)
  while head
    prev_data = head.prev ? head.prev.data : nil
    next_data = head.next ? head.next.data : nil
    puts "Data: #{head.data} Previous: #{prev_data} Next: #{next_data}"
    head = head.next
  end
end

# driver program
root = Node.new(1)
root.left = Node.new(2)
root.right = Node.new(3)
root.left.left = Node.new(4)
root.left.right = Node.new(5)

head = binaryTreeToDLL(root)
print_dll(head)
```

In this code, `binaryTreeToDLL` function is used to convert binary tree to doubly linked list. It does that through recursive calls for the left and right subtrees of the root. It then creates a new DLL node with the data of the current root node, and sets prev pointer to the DLL equivalent of the left subtree and next pointer to the DLL equivalent of the right subtree. Finally, it returns the created DLL node.

The `print_dll` function is used to print the data in each node of the doubly linked list from head to tail.
<|end|>


