I want to replace a single file inside a msi. How to do it?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Replacing a single file inside an .MSI (Microsoft Installer Package) isn't quite as simple as replacing files inside of regular archive types, like ZIP or RAR, but it's certainly possible. Here is a general guideline on how to achieve this.

You'll need a tool that can edit or modify MSI files - an example would be Orca (a database table editor for creating and editing Windows Installer packages and merge modules).

Steps to edit an MSI file:

1. Download and install Orca from this link (Microsoft official website, Note: Orca is part of Microsoft SDK). https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/download/details.aspx?id=48159 

2. Run the Orca tool.
  
3. Open your MSI file with Orca: Go to "File > Open" and navigate to the location of the MSI file.

4. Locate the file you want to replace: In the left panel, click on the "File" table. This will bring up a list of files in the MSI. Find the one you want to replace - it might take some searching if the MSI contains many files.

5. Note the key name of the file: You'll need the exact key name to add the replacement file.

6. Remove the old file: Right-click the file you want to replace and choose "Drop Row" to remove it.

7. Add the new file: Go to the "Binary" table and choose "Import" to add your replacement file. When asked for the key name, enter the key name of the file you removed.

8. Update the file details: Return to the "File" table and insert a row with the same name as your removed file but containing details of your new file. The details should include the size of your new file.

9. Save your changes: Go to "File > Save" to save the updated MSI. 

Please note that tampering with MSI files may sometimes lead to unpredictable behavior or the installer not working properly if not done correctly.

Ensure that you have a backup of your original MSI file in case things go wrong.

Make sure editing an MSI file in this way is allowed - some companies might not allow you to make changes to their installer packages.
<|end|>


