I'd like to install xmla in Python 3.10 to access an SSAS db but I get the following error:

pip install xmla
ImportError: cannot import name 'MutableMapping' from 'collections' (C:\Users\xx\AppData\Local\Programs\Python\Python310\lib\collections\__init__.py)


Any help welcome
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The library you're trying to use (xmla) is not compatible with Python 3.10 because of how it uses collections.MutableMapping.

This is because, as of Python 3.10, collections.MutableMapping can only be imported from collections.abc, not collections directly. This is stated in the Python 3.3 documentation (quote):

MutableMapping
A MutableMapping is a generic container for associating key/value pairs.

This class can be used as a base class, with a similar use to Mapping. It has the following additional abstract methods:

`__setitem__(k, v)`, `__delitem__(k)`

And the following mixin methods:

`__setitem__(k, v)[source]`
Set self[k] to v.

`__delitem__(k)[source]`
Remove self[k].

`clear()->None.  Remove all items from D.`
New in version 3.9: Starting with Python 3.9, the collections.abc module can be accessed directly in collections. Older Python versions can use import collections.abc.

You can try installing an older version of Python (such as Python 3.8 or 3.9) and see if that helps. If you want to stay on Python 3.10, you will have to find a different library that is compatible, or wait for the authors of xmla to update their software.

1. Uninstall Python 3.10
2. Install Python 3.9 from here
3. Then reinstall xmla via pip

If you can't downgrade your Python version, another alternative is to fix the library yourself by replacing every import of MutableMapping from collections with collections.abc. But it's a good idea to ask permission from the original author or fork the repository and apply your changes there.
<|end|>


