Devise an intricate regular expression pattern that is capable of pinpointing and retrieving email addresses concealed within an assortment of textual strings.
text_list = ["contact me at jack@example.com for more information", "reach out to jill@example.com for a discussion"]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In Python, you can use the `re` module for regular expression matching. The following program features a regular expression pattern that attempts to match email addresses:

```python
import re

#List of strings
text_list = ["contact me at jack@example.com for more information", 
             "reach out to jill@example.com for a discussion"]

#Regular expression pattern to match email addresses
pattern = r'[\w\.-]+@[\w\.-]+\.\w+'

#Empty list to store the email addresses
emails = []

#Loop through each string in the list
for text in text_list:
    #Find all matches of the pattern in the current string
    matches = re.findall(pattern, text)
    #Extend the email list with the matches
    emails.extend(matches)

#Print the email addresses
for email in emails:
    print(email)
```
This script identifies email addresses by looking for one or more word characters (letters, numbers, or underscores) or periods or dashes, followed by an '@' symbol, followed by one or more word characters or periods or dashes, followed by a period, followed by one or more word characters.

Remember that this is a simple email matcher and doesn't cover all possible email variants - emails are complex and can contain a variety of characters and formats that a single regular expression might not capture. Please use a library or a verified regular expression if you want to handle real-world emails.
<|end|>


