def reverse_last_three(my_list):
    return my_list[:-3] + my_list[-3:][::-1]

my_list = ["apple", "banana", "cherry", "date", "fig", "grape"]

print(reverse_last_three(my_list))
```

When you run this function with your list, it will return `['apple', 'banana', 'cherry', 'grape', 'fig', 'date']`. The function achieves this by concatenating all items in the list except the last three (`my_list[:-3]`) with the last three items reversed (`my_list[-3:][::-1]`). The `-3:` slice gets the last three items and `[::-1]` reverses them.
