What's the recommended source control system for a very small team (one developer)?
Price does not matter. Customer would pay :-)
I'm working on Vista32 with VS 2008 in C++ and later in C# and with WPF. Setting up an extra (physical) server for this seems overkill to me. Any opinions?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
For a small team or a single developer, Git is a great choice. It is a distributed version control system, which means every clone of the repository fully contains the entire history. This is a great advantage if you don't have a dedicated server - any copy of the repository, including your local one, is a fully functioning repository.

Git has gained a huge popularity in the past years, especially within the open source community. It is used by big projects like the Linux kernel, Ruby on Rails, and many others. GitHub, a web-based hosting service for Git repositories, is wildly popular and hosts hundreds of thousands of projects.

Another good choice would be Mercurial, which is very similar in functionality to Git. It is also a distributed version control system, which means it shares many of the benefits. 

Both Git and Mercurial can be used from the command line or with a graphical user interface, and both have excellent support for a variety of platforms, including Vista. They also integrate well with Visual Studio.

Finally, both Git and Mercurial are free and open source.
<|end|>


