Engineer a jQuery extension facilitating the incorporation of Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) encryption methodology, specifically targeting data transmissions following form submissions, with an added emphasis on maintaining data integrity and confidentiality.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To create this solution, you'll need a service or library that offers AES encryption in JavaScript. One option is Crypto-JS, an open-source library that provides basic cryptographic functions for JavaScript. 

The first task is to include both jQuery and Crypto-JS in your project. You can download the libraries or use a CDN.

```html
<script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.6.0.min.js" ></script>

<!-- Minified version of crypto-js/rollups/aes.js -->
<script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/crypto-js/4.0.0/aes.min.js">
</script>
```
Now, we are ready to create jQuery extension as shown below:

```javascript
$.fn.encryptSubmit = function(secret) {

  $(this).submit(function(e) {
    e.preventDefault();
    
    // iterate over form inputs
    var encryptedData = {};
    $(this).find(":input").each(function(i, v) {
      var name = $(this).prop("name");
      var data = $(this).val();

      // simple AES encryption 
      var encrypted = CryptoJS.AES.encrypt(JSON.parse(data), secret);
      encryptedData[name] = encrypted.toString();

    });

    // create new form data for encrypted transmission
    var encryptedForm = $('<form></form>').attr('method', 'POST');
  
    $.each(encryptedData, function(key, value) {
       encryptedForm.append($('<input>').attr('type', 'hidden').attr('name', key).val(value));
    });

    $(body).append(encryptedForm);
    encryptedForm.submit();
  
  });
  
};
```

This jQuery extension enhances form submission by ensuring form data is encrypted before it's transmitted over the network. It targets AES encryption of form fields and submits the encrypted version as a separate form. This helps to ensure integrity and confidentiality of sensitive form data. 

Use case:

```javascript
$('#myform').encryptSubmit('my-secret-key');
```

Note: Data encryption is a complex topic, and this simplified example only scratches the surface. The code example above uses a static encryption key, which is not secure. You need a secure method for managing and distributing your encryption keys. Also note that you will need to have a corresponding decryption mechanism on the server side to decrypt the data received.

Please treat the code as a proof of concept and not ready for production grade implementation. If you are dealing with sensitive or regulated data, you should consider using secure transport like HTTPS and modern security standards like TLS for transmission and storage. This code has not been tested against any platform or under any environment whether development or production. 

Always follow industry best practices when dealing with encryption and never roll out your solution for securing information. Competent consultation with security experts is very recommended.
<|end|>


