In Ruby, trying to print out the individual elements of a String is giving me trouble. Instead of seeing each character, I'm seeing their ASCII values instead: 


>> a = "0123"
=> "0123"
>> a[0]
=> 48



I've looked online but can't find any way to get the original "0" back out of it. I'm a little new to Ruby to I know it has to be something simple but I just can't seem to find it.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In Ruby, strings can be treated like an array for certain purposes. For instance, you can access the characters of a string using their index, just like an array. However, in Ruby versions before 1.9, when you access a string by an index, it returns ASCII value of the character. That's why you're seeing 48 instead of "0".

In Ruby versions 1.9 and later, accessing a string by an index would give you the character itself. Your Ruby interpreter is probably running a version of Ruby prior to 1.9.

If you are using a version prior to 1.9 and want to see the character instead of the ASCII value, you should use the chr method as follows:

```ruby
a = "0123"
character = a[0].chr    # "0"
```

The chr method returns a string containing the character represented by the int's value according to ASCII.

If you're using a version 1.9 and later of Ruby and still see ASCII values instead of actual characters, it could be possible your string is not properly encoded. Ensure the string encoding is UTF-8 or ASCII, based on your needs, using the force_encoding method as below:

```ruby
a = "0123"
a = a.force_encoding('UTF-8')   # Ensures the encoding is UTF-8
character = a[0]    # "0"
```

The a[0] should then give you the character "0", not its ASCII value.
<|end|>


