Enhance the subsequent Python function:
def extract_even_integer(number: float) -> int:
    """ Given a positive real number, it can invariably be partitioned into an integral
    segment and a fractional component. Alter the function to yield only when the integral segment is an even number. 
    If the integral segment of the real number isn't even, the function ought to return None.    

    >>> extract_even_integer(4.7)
    4
    """

<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's an enhancement of your function:

```python
def extract_even_integer(number: float) -> int:
    """ Given a positive real number, it is divided into an integral segment and a fractional component. 
    Returns the integral segment only when it is an even number. If the integral part of the real number 
    is not even, the function will return None.

    >>> extract_even_integer(4.7)
    4
    >>> extract_even_integer(5.7)
    None
    """
    integral_part = int(number)
    if integral_part % 2 == 0:
        return integral_part
    else:
        return None
```
Before, your function was not checking if the integral part of the number is even or not. In this version, we use the modulus operator (%) to check if the division of the integral part of the number with 2 has a remainder. If the remainder is 0, then the number is even and we return it. If not, we return None.
<|end|>


