Create an Angular component that includes a text field and multiple checkboxes. When the user inputs text and selects any number of checkboxes, generates a unique ID for each checked option, and displays a list of the user's inputs and corresponding IDs when a button is clicked. Additionally, ensure that the generated IDs persist after page refresh using local storage. Optimize for efficient read/write to local storage.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You'll want to ensure you have Angular installed and set up with the Angular CLI before beginning this project. 

Let's create an `CheckboxComponent` in Angular with its corresponding `checkbox.component.ts`, `checkbox.component.html` , `checkbox.component.css`, and `AppModule` update.

checkbox.component.ts: 

```Typescript
import { Component, OnInit } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-checkbox',
  templateUrl: './checkbox.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./checkbox.component.css']
})

export class CheckboxComponent implements OnInit {
  checkboxes = [{name: 'Option 1', checked: false, id: null},
                {name: 'Option 2', checked: false, id: null},
                {name: 'Option 3', checked: false, id: null}];
  textInput = '';
  checkedItems = [];

  ngOnInit() {
    const savedData = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('savedCheckboxes'));
    if (savedData) {
      this.checkboxes = savedData;
    }
  }

  generateIds() {
    this.checkedItems = [];
    this.checkboxes.forEach((item, index) => {
      if (item.checked && item.id === null) {
        item.id = this.textInput + '_' + index;
      }
      if (item.checked) {
        this.checkedItems.push(item);
      }
    });
    localStorage.setItem('savedCheckboxes', JSON.stringify(this.checkboxes));
  }
}
```
checkbox.component.html:

```HTML
<div>
  <input [(ngModel)]="textInput" placeholder="Enter text here" >

  <div *ngFor="let checkbox of checkboxes; let i = index">
    <input type="checkbox" [(ngModel)]="checkbox.checked" > {{checkbox.name}}
  </div>

  <button (click)="generateIds()">
    Generate IDs and save
  </button>

  <h3>Saved Checkboxes:</h3>
  <div *ngFor="let item of checkedItems">
    <p>Text: {{textInput}}, ID: {{item.id}}</p>
  </div>
</div>
```
checkbox.component.css: 
```CSS
/* You can put any css styles for your component here */
```
In `AppModule` (`app.module.ts`), remember to import the required modules and also the `CheckboxComponent`:

```Typescript
import { BrowserModule } from '@angular/platform-browser';
import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';
import { FormsModule } from '@angular/forms';
import { CheckboxComponent } from './checkbox/checkbox.component';

@NgModule({
  declarations: [CheckboxComponent ],
  imports: [BrowserModule, FormsModule], // FormsModule is necessary for [(ngModel)] to work
  providers: [],
  bootstrap: [CheckboxComponent]
})
export class AppModule { }
```

In the input and checkbox fields, `[(ngModel)]` two-way data binding is used to keep track of their values. When button is clicked, `generateIds()` function is called which assigns unique id to checked checkboxes. This id is combination of text entered in the inputfield and index of the checkbox in checkboxes array. The updated checkboxes are stored in the localStorage after generating ids. Also, only the checked checkboxes are filtered to display to the user. 

Please replace `'Option 1'`, `'Option 2'` and `'Option 3'` with your actually values. You can also dynamically create checkboxes according to your requirements. 

Remember, the key used in `localStorage.setItem()` and `localStorage.getItem()` should always be the same ('savedCheckboxes' in this case) to access the same data. The value is stored in the form of string and should be parsed into JSON to access as a Javascript object.
