Assuming the results (admitted and rejected) of all applicants  are available, I'd like to design a regression or statistical test to examine if the admission is discriminative regarding the gender or race of the applicants. The available explanatory variables include both continuous variables like sat score, and categorical variables that depict the qualification of the students.
Any comments are very well appreciated.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The first step in your analysis should be exploratory data analysis. Look at the distribution of your outcome variable (admission result) and your predictor variables (like SAT scores, gender, race, and qualification). Use methods such as box-plots, histograms, and t-tests or Chi-square tests to understand if there are any initial differences in these groups.

Next, you can make use of logistic regression to figure out the relationship of your predictor variables (both continuous and categorical) with the outcome variable (admission decision).

Logistic regression is chosen because the admission decision is binary (admitted/rejected). Its output can be interpreted as the odds of a student being admitted relative to being rejected, given the student's characteristics (like gender, race, SAT scores, etc.).

Include the variables of interest (gender and race) and control for other potentially confounding variables (like SAT scores and qualifications). 

In particular, for your test:

Outcome Variable: admission result (admitted = 1, rejected = 0)
Predictor Variables: sat scores, qualification, gender, race

The model would look something like this:

logit(Pr(admission result = 1)) = β0 + β1*(SAT score) + β2*(qualification) + β3*(gender) + β4*(race)

Then, look at the p-values associated with the coefficients for gender and race. A significant p-value (usually less than 0.05) would indicate a statistically significant relationship between these predictors and the odds of being admitted.

Remember, this analysis can only tell you if there is an association, not causation. It is also essential that you ensure your data meets all the assumptions of logistic regression, and that the model is a good fit for your data.
<|end|>


