Write a SQL query to list the names of suppliers and the associated product ID they provide, but also add a condition: only include suppliers that provide more than five different products and products that have been ordered more than 50 times. Sort the result by supplier name in ascending order and then by product ID in descending order. Include additional fields - total products supplied by a supplier and total orders of each product. Ensure your query can handle large datasets efficiently.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
SQL queries do not handle efficiency on large datasets - this is up to the database engine design and index settings. However, depending on the database structure, a possible SQL query for your problem could look something like this:

```
SELECT 
    S.supplier_name, 
    P.product_id,
    COUNT(DISTINCT P.product_id) OVER (PARTITION BY S.supplier_id) as total_products_supplied,
    COUNT(O.order_id) OVER (PARTITION BY P.product_id) as total_orders
FROM 
    Suppliers AS S
JOIN 
    Products AS P ON S.supplier_id = P.supplier_id
JOIN 
    Orders AS O ON P.product_id = O.product_id
GROUP BY 
    S.supplier_name, P.product_id
HAVING 
    COUNT(DISTINCT P.product_id) > 5
AND 
    COUNT(O.order_id) > 50 
ORDER BY 
    S.supplier_name ASC, P.product_id DESC
```

Please note, in the above SQL query, names of tables (Suppliers, Products, Orders) and their attribute names (like supplier_name, product_id, order_id, supplier_id) are assumed. You will need to change these as per your database schema.

Also, this query assumes that there is a many-to-many relationship between Products and Suppliers which is further related with an Orders table. 

The 'GROUP BY' clause is used to group the common suppliers and products together. The 'HAVING' clause is used to filter out the groups which do not satisfy the condition where the suppliers provide more than 5 products and products ordered more than 50 times. The 'ORDER BY' clause is used to sort the suppliers and products as per the condition described. 

The window function COUNT is used to calculate the total product supplied by supplier and total orders for each product. The 'OVER' clause is used to perform the calculation for each group of rows that have the same supplier_id in the total_products_supplied column and the same product_id in the total_orders column.
<|end|>


