I've searched on the Internet for comparisons between [F#](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F_Sharp_%28programming_language%29) and [Haskell](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haskell_%28programming_language%29) but haven't found anything really definitive. What are the primary differences and why would I want to choose one over the other?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
F# and Haskell are both functional programming languages, but there are also many differences between them both in design philosophy and practical use. Here's a general comparison:

Design Philosophy:

1. Strict vs Lazy: One major difference is that F# is strictly evaluated whereas Haskell is lazily evaluated. This means that in F#, expressions are evaluated as they are bound, while in Haskell, expressions are evaluated when they are needed.

2. Purity: Haskell is a purely functional language. Functions in Haskell have no side effects, and mutable state is handled via monads. In contrast, F# allows for both pure functions and side effects, and it has mutable variables.

3. Syntax: Syntax in F# is somewhat similar to that of other C-style languages (like C# or Java) which makes it easier for programmers with an imperative programming background. Haskell's syntax is quite unique and might be hard for people coming from imperative programming background to adjust to.

Real-world usage:

1. Interoperability: F# is a .NET language and can effortlessly work with other .NET languages like C#, and it can access all the .NET Framework and .NET Core. Haskell does not interoperate as smoothly with other ecosystems.

2. Libraries: Even though Haskell has a rich set of libraries, the .NET ecosystem around F# is more extensive, covering a broad range of needs in both commercial and open-source software.

3. Tooling: F# has excellent tool support being part of the Visual Studio ecosystem, while historically Haskell's tooling has been weaker, despite recent improvements.

Why choose one over the other?

It depends on your specific use case:

1. If you need a lot of math and/or scientific computation, Haskell provides a lot of abstraction with its type system without sacrificing performance. Haskell is also a good choice if you want to learn more about functional programming in a more pure way.

2. If you're working in a .NET environment, or want to develop something that's easy to deploy in a commercial/production setting, F# is probably a better choice because of its strong interoperability with .NET languages and frameworks.

3. If you need a language that is more flexible in allowing both functional and imperative programming, F# would be a better fit.

In the end, your choice might be also influenced by your personal preference and the kind of project that you are working on. Both languages have their strengths and are well-suited to specific types of problems and environments.
<|end|>


