I have several projects which I document with Sphinx. The contents of `index.rst` are identical in each of them (I use some substitutions for project name and include another rst file with the list of modules). So I thought I could remove this file from most projects and only keep it in one shared project to which the others can find an absolute path during build.
However, the `master_doc` config does not let me enter an absolute path, and it even rejects relative paths that go "out of scope of the single project". I even tried adding the shared folder in `sys.path`, but it still did not help.
I've seen many questions asking the opposite, i.e. using multiple index.rst files in a single project. But that's not my case.
Do you have any ideas on how to get this working?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Since Sphinx does not allow giving absolute path in `master_doc`, one way to solve your problem is to use symbolic links or hard links. Here is what you can do:

1. Keep the `index.rst` file in a shared location.
2. In all your projects, you should create a symbolic link or a hard link to this shared `index.rst`.

On Unix-like systems, you can create symbolic link using the `ln` command:

```bash
ln -s /full/path/to/your/shared/index.rst /full/path/to/your/project/index.rst
```

On Windows systems, the equivalent command to create symbolic link is `mklink`:

```bat
mklink Link Target
```

Make sure to check if Sphinx is able to resolve any relative paths in `index.rst` correctly. 

Although this is not the most elegant solution, it should work since Sphinx will think that `index.rst` exists in the project's root directory, so the `master_doc` config won't need to change. 

Please ensure to handle version control correctly with this setup, ideally the symlink should be version controlled and not the resolved file. 

Also to note, this method still means a copy of `index.rst` appears to exist in each project, just without the need of manually synchronizing changes between these copies.
<|end|>


