Write an SQL query to modify the department of the employee with Id 9 to 'Marketing'. Also, ensure to write an update-trigger in SQL that logs each change in the department with the employee id, old department name, new department name, and timestamp of the change into a separate table called 'DepartmentChanges'.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is how you can approach this task.

First, run a SQL UPDATE command to update the department of the employee with Id 9:

```sql
UPDATE Employees 
SET Department = 'Marketing' 
WHERE Id = 9;
```

Now we're going to create a trigger named `log_department_changes` which will be fired immediately after any UPDATE operation on the department of the Employees table.

```sql
CREATE TRIGGER log_department_changes
AFTER UPDATE OF Department ON Employees
FOR EACH ROW
BEGIN
   INSERT INTO DepartmentChanges(EmployeeId, OldDepartment, NewDepartment, ChangeTime)
   VALUES(OLD.Id, OLD.Department, NEW.Department, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);
END;
```

This trigger works in the following way:

1. `AFTER UPDATE OF Department ON Employees`: The trigger is going to fire after there are any updates on the Department column in the Employees table.

2. `FOR EACH ROW`: It specifies that the trigger will fire for each row updated.

3. `BEGIN ... END;`: Contains the SQL commands to be executed whenever the trigger fires. In this case, it is inserting a new row into the `DepartmentChanges` table which records the id of the employee (`OLD.Id`), the department before the update (`OLD.Department`), the new department after the update (`NEW.Department`), and the timestamp of the change (`CURRENT_TIMESTAMP`).

Please remember that the implementation might be slightly different depending on the specific SQL databases you are using. SQL standards are implemented differently amongst different databases. Be sure to check the documentation of your specific SQL database when using triggers.

Also, this provided SQL statements use the tables `Employees` and `DepartmentChanges`, but your actual table names might be different. Change `Employees` and `DepartmentChanges` to your actual table names.

Before running this SQL queries, make sure your `DepartmentChanges` table does have columns `EmployeeId`, `OldDepartment`, `NewDepartment` and  `ChangeTime`.
<|end|>


