If X is a random variable from the uniform distribution on the interval [0,θ], how would one construct a 95% confidence interval for the method of moments estimator of θ?
I have found that [](https://i.stack.imgur.com/cQFYs.png)
and also its asymptotic distribution
[](https://i.stack.imgur.com/w1s9G.png)
but I do not understand how to find a confidence interval for it. I know that I have to use the central limit theorem but I do not know how to apply it in this case.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
For a uniform distribution on the interval [0, θ], the method of moments estimator of θ is 2X̄ (where X̄ is the sample mean). This is because the expected value of a uniform distribution on [0, θ] is θ/2, so we set this equal to the sample mean and solve for θ.

Under the Central Limit Theorem, as n tends to infinity, X̄ follows a normal distribution with mean θ/2 and variance (θ^2)/12n.

This implies that sqrt(n) * (X̄ - θ/2) follows a normal distribution with mean 0 and variance (θ^2)/12. Consequently, 2 sqrt(n) * (X̄ - θ/2) / θ follows a normal distribution with mean 0 and variance 1/3.

Now, let's denote by Q the quantity 2 sqrt(n) * (X̄ - θ/2) / θ. We can then rewrite Q as (2 sqrt(n) * X̄ - sqrt(n) * θ) / (θ/2). This can be further simplified to (4 sqrt(n) * X̄ - 2 sqrt(n) * θ)/θ.

Then, a 95% confidence interval for the parameter θ can be obtained as:

θ ∈ {4 sqrt(n) * X̄ - Z_(α/2) * 2 sqrt(n) * θ : 4 sqrt(n) * X̄ + Z_(α/2) * 2 sqrt(n) * θ}

where Z_(α/2) is a quantile from the standard normal distribution that amounts to 1.96 for α=0.05.

Note that this interval contains the unknown parameter θ with probability 0.95 no matter the true value of θ.

This approach assumes that the sample size n is large enough for the Central Limit Theorem to apply. If the sample size is small, it might be appropriate to use a different approach to confidence interval construction, such as a bootstrap method.
<|end|>


