Convert the given Python data structure (set) into a properly formatted XML document.

{ "Apple", "Banana", "Cherry" }
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can solve this problem by using the built-in xml.etree.ElementTree module to create XML documents. Here is a simple Python script that creates an XML document from the given Python set:

```python
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET

# Python set
fruits = { "Apple", "Banana", "Cherry" }

# create the root element
root = ET.Element("root")

# iterate through the set and create XML elements
for fruit in fruits:
    elem = ET.SubElement(root, "fruit")
    elem.text = fruit

# create a tree from the root element
tree = ET.ElementTree(root)

# write the tree to an XML file
tree.write("fruits.xml")
```

This script creates an XML document with a root element named "root". Each item in the set is converted to an XML element named "fruit". The text content of each "fruit" element is the fruit name from the set. The resulting XML document will look something like this (the order of the "fruit" elements may vary because sets are unordered):

```xml
<root>
  <fruit>Cherry</fruit>
  <fruit>Banana</fruit>
  <fruit>Apple</fruit>
</root>
```

This document is saved in a file named "fruits.xml".
<|end|>


