Could you elucidate on the concept of kernel trick, particularly its role in the realm of computational learning theory, with a special emphasis on its application in Support Vector Machines?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The kernel trick is a mathematical technique in machine learning that involves the use of special functions, called kernels, to map the input data from a low-dimensional space to a much higher dimensional space. It is a crucial concept in the field of computational learning theory and is primarily used in methods such as Support Vector Machines (SVMs).

The idea is to transform the input data in such a way that it becomes easier to learn with linear methods. For example, when data isn't linearly separable (which means you can't draw a straight line to separate different classes of data), the kernel trick can map the data to a higher dimension where it is linearly separable. 

Now focusing on its application in Support Vector Machines:

SVMs are a kind of linear classifiers that aim to find the best hyperplane which can segregate the data into its respective classes. But when the data is not linearly separable, SVMs alone can't work effectively. This is where the 'kernel trick' comes into play. 

In SVMs, the kernel trick is used to transform the input space into a higher-dimensional feature space where the data is more likely to be linearly separable, allowing SVMs to find a hyperplane in the new dimensioned space that can separate the classes effectively. Once the hyperplane is determined in the higher-dimensional space, it is then transformed back into the original input space. This could end up being a more complex decision boundary, like a curve or a circle.

Common types of kernels used in SVMs include linear, polynomial, radial basis function (RBF), and sigmoid kernels. 

The main advantage of the kernel trick is that it allows complex decision boundaries to be formed, even when we're working with linear models. This gives it a wide application in various machine learning tasks – from pattern recognition and text categorization to bioinformatics – basically anywhere, where non-linear decision boundaries are necessary.

Finally, the kernel trick avoids the explicit computation of the coordinates of the points in the high dimensional space by calculating only the inner products between the images of all pairs of data in the feature space. This makes the whole process computationally efficient and is the primary reason why SVMs, along with Kernel trick, can handle high dimensional data effectively.
<|end|>


