You are correct that most existing swapping models focus on faces because the facial swapping concept has been extensively researched and experimented. They exploit features like symmetry, structure, and the consistent appearance of faces. Nonetheless, the fundamental concept – that is, to learn a representation of an object and replace it with another – can be applied to objects other than faces.

Adapting something like the FaceSwap model to other items would involve retraining the model with your desired object types. However, one major issue would be data collection: you'd have to gather large amounts of images of the objects you want to swap, which could be time consuming and even impractical.

One possible approach could be utilizing Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs), which have been successful in a variety of tasks, such as image synthesis, image-to-image translation, etc.

Here are few models that might be helpful:

1. CycleGAN ([https://github.com/junyanz/CycleGAN](https://github.com/junyanz/CycleGAN)) might be a good starting point. It's a model developed for object transfiguration (like turning horses into zebras) and style transfer.

2. StyleGAN ([https://github.com/NVlabs/stylegan](https://github.com/NVlabs/stylegan)) is another promising option. It is capable of generating very realistic images and could potentially be adapted for your needs.

3. The pix2pix model ([https://github.com/junyanz/pytorch-CycleGAN-and-pix2pix](https://github.com/junyanz/pytorch-CycleGAN-and-pix2pix)) is somewhat similar to CycleGAN but uses paired data.

4. Also, [DeepArt.io](https://deepart.io) generates new images by using a style image and a content image. They don't exactly swap objects, but the technology is similar and you may find it interesting.

Remember that these models might require significant computation resources (GPUs) and might not work perfectly on all types of objects. You will need to experiment with different architectures and settings to find what works best for your specific task.
