  <head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>Accessible Page</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
  </head>
  <body>
    <header>
      <h1 id="header-text">Accessible Web Page</h1>
    </header>
    <nav>
      <ul id="navigation">
        <li><a href="#">Home</a></li>
        <li><a href="#">About</a></li>
        <li><a href="#">Contact</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <main>
      <p id="content">This is a paragraph of text.</p>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
```

CSS (styles.css):

```css
body {
  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}

header {
  background: lightblue;
}

#header-text {
  color: white;
  text-align: center;
  padding: 10px 0;
}

#navigation {
  list-style: none;
  padding: 0;
}

#navigation li {
  display: inline-block;
  margin-right: 10px;
}

#content {
  font-size: 1.2em;
  color: black;
}
```

In the above code:

- The HTML document structure is defined with a `<header>`, a `<nav>` for navigation, and a `<main>` for the main content.
- The `<header>` and `<p>` tags are given descriptive id's to make it easier to style them with CSS.
- The CSS in the `styles.css` file applies the desired styles. The text alignment, color, and padding of the header are set, the bullets from the navigation items are removed and some margin is added, and the font size and color of the paragraph text are set.
- Both the header and paragraph have unique id's in the CSS to help with specific style.
  
For accessibility:

- Use proper semantic HTML tags (header, nav, main) for improved screen reader support.
- Use clear and concise language for navigation and content.
- Choose colors with sufficient contrast so that colorblind users can distinguish between different components on the page. In this case, the contrast between light blue and white and black is quite strong.
  
Please note: This is a rather simple webpage. In a real-world scenario, you might want to provide much more in the way of accessibility features, such as skip links, alt text for any images, and WAI-ARIA roles and properties where needed.
