<|asst|>
Here is a version of your code translated into Go. Go does not support complex numbered median computations and does not have a built-in sort function for complex types like in Python, so it does not sort the slice of complex numbers. It only supports for slice of floats or integers.

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sort"
)

func median(slice []float64) float64 {
    // Check if slice is empty
	if len(slice) == 0 {
		return 0
	}

    // Sort the slice
	sort.Float64s(slice)

	middle := len(slice) / 2

    // If length of the slice is even
	if len(slice)%2 == 0 {
		return (slice[middle-1] + slice[middle]) / 2
	}
    // If length of the slice is odd
	return slice[middle]
}

func main() {
    slice := []float64{3,1,2,4,5}
	fmt.Println(median(slice))
    slice = []float64{-10, 4, 6, 1000, 10, 20}
	fmt.Println(median(slice))
    slice = []float64{1, 2, 2, 4, 5}
	fmt.Println(median(slice))
}
```

The type conversion from set to list, which is in the original python code, does not exist in the Go version because Go doesn't have a set data type like Python. Also, Go does not support list of multiple data types (floats, ints, complex), the list only supports single data type. 

The `if __name__ == "__main__"` part of the code in Python which allows the script to be run from the command-line does not apply in Go. In Go, the `main()` function serves the same purpose.

The `doctest.testmod(verbose=True)` part of your Python code tests your functions using your provided comments. This functionality does not exist in Go - you would need to write separate test functions.
<|end|>


