In the Java programming language, construct a program that generates an instance of the "Car" class. Your program should also uphold the standard Java convention rules for class creation, object instantiation, and method encapsulation. Furthermore, expand the "Car" class such that it includes properties and methods that are common for a car–like manufacturer, model, color, speed, and behaviors like accelerate and brake. Show how encapsulation is used and explain why this is important in object-oriented programming.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a sample Java program that meets the requirements. 

```java
// Class creation
public class Car {
    // Properties
    private String manufacturer;
    private String model;
    private String color;
    private int speed;
    
    // Car class constructor
    public Car(String manufacturer, String model, String color) {
        this.manufacturer = manufacturer;
        this.model = model;
        this.color = color;
        this.speed = 0;
    }

    // Method encapsulation
    public String getManufacturer() {
        return manufacturer;
    }

    public String getModel() {
        return model;
    }

    public String getColor() {
        return color;
    }

    public int getSpeed() {
        return speed;
    }

    // Behaviours
    public void accelerate() {
        speed += 10;
    }

    public void brake() {
        if (speed > 0)
            speed -= 10;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Object instantiation
        Car myCar = new Car("Tesla", "Model 3", "Red");
        
        // Test accelerate method
        myCar.accelerate();
        System.out.println(myCar.getSpeed()); // should print 10

        // Test brake method
        myCar.brake();
        System.out.println(myCar.getSpeed()); // should print 0
    }
}
```

In this example, encapsulation is used as the class properties (manufacturer, model, color, and speed) are set as private, so they can't be directly accessed and modified. Instead, getter methods are used to retrieve these properties' values. This means the class has full control over what information is read (the getter methods) and how to read it.

On the behavior side, the increase and decrease of the speed are done through the methods `accelerate` and `brake`, instead of allowing a direct manipulation of the `speed` property.

Encapsulation acts like a protective shell that prevents the code and data being arbitrarily accessed by other code defined outside the class. It is important because it helps maintain the integrity of the object state by hiding its implementation details. It allows modification or addition of new methods or data inside the class without affecting other parts of the code using this class. Encapsulation promotes data-hiding and application security.
<|end|>


