Create a program that accepts two multidimensional arrays as input in Scala. The function should check if the dimensions of the two matrices are equal. If yes, compute the sum for each corresponding pair of elements in both arrays and create a new multidimensional array with the results of these sums. Assume the multidimensional arrays contain only integers.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a simple Scala program that implements the functionality you want.

```scala
def addMatrices(matrix1: Array[Array[Int]], matrix2: Array[Array[Int]]): Array[Array[Int]] = {
  require(matrix1.length == matrix2.length)                                      // checks if the rows numbers agree 
  require(matrix1.head.length == matrix2.head.length)                            // checks if the columns numbers agree
  for ((row1, row2) <- matrix1 zip matrix2) yield {                              // iterates over rows of both matrices
    require(row1.length == row2.length)                                          // checks if the rows lengths agree
  }
  (matrix1, matrix2).zipped.map((row1, row2) => (row1, row2).zipped.map(_ + _)) // iterates over pairs of rows and sums them
}
```

You can use the `addMatrices` method as follows:

```scala
// Define two matrices
val matrix1 = Array(Array(1, 2, 3), Array(4, 5, 6), Array(7, 8, 9))
val matrix2 = Array(Array(10, 20, 30), Array(40, 50, 60), Array(70, 80, 90))

// Call addMatrices method
val result = addMatrices(matrix1, matrix2)

// Print the result
result.foreach(row => println(row.mkString(", ")))
```

This program will output:

```shell
11, 22, 33
44, 55, 66
77, 88, 99
```

This program uses array transformations and the high-order function `zip` to add all elements of the input matrices (column by column, row by row), and returns a new matrix with the summed elements. If the matrices don't have the same dimensions, it throws an InvalidArgumentException.
<|end|>


