<|user|>
Code an SQL function that will take three tables as input and return the common elements among all three. tableA, tableB, tableC.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Since SQL doesn't truly support functions that accept tables as a parameter, this solution assumes that the table names are constants within the query. Also, this solution assumes that each of the three tables has the same column structure and that we are looking for common rows based on all columns. 

Assuming each table just has a single column named 'column' for simplicity:

```sql
SELECT * FROM tableA
WHERE EXISTS (
  SELECT 1 FROM tableB
  WHERE tableB.column = tableA.column
)
AND EXISTS (
  SELECT 1 FROM tableC
  WHERE tableC.column = tableA.column
)
```

If the tables have multiple columns:

```sql
SELECT * FROM tableA
WHERE EXISTS (
  SELECT 1 FROM tableB
  WHERE 
    tableB.column1 = tableA.column1
    AND tableB.column2 = tableA.column2
    -- Repeat for as many columns as necessary
)
AND EXISTS (
  SELECT 1 FROM tableC
  WHERE 
    tableC.column1 = tableA.column1
    AND tableC.column2 = tableA.column2
    -- Repeat for as many columns as necessary
)
```

This is just a simplistic case and actual scenario may vary based on the specific requirements. You may need to adjust the queries accordingly, for example if you only need to find common elements based on certain columns but not all, or if the columns have different names in different tables, etc.
<|end|>


