Create a class in Java that not only generates a set from a given array of numbers but also performs the following:

- The class should ensure that the input array does not have duplicates and should return an error message if it does.
- Create a method that receives an array of integers and checks if this is a subset of our initial set. 
- Additionally, create another method that will fetch the minimum and maximum element from the set.
- The class should also have a method to determine if a specified integer is present in the set. 

Test your code with the following array: [2, 3, 5, 4, 2, 3]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is the code which meets your criteria. The class SetOperator has constructor which takes an array of integers to make a set. It has a method isSubset which checks if a given set is a subset of our set. It also has a method minMax which gives us the minimum and maximum of our set. It also has another method which verifies if an integer exists in our set:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

public class SetOperator {
    private Set<Integer> set;

    public SetOperator(int[] data) {
        Set<Integer> tempSet = new HashSet<>();
        for (int value : data) {
            if (!tempSet.add(value)) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Error: Duplicate values are not allowed.");
            }
        }
        set = tempSet;
    }

    public boolean isSubset(int[] subset) {
        Set<Integer> subsetSet = new HashSet<>();
        for (int value : subset) subsetSet.add(value);

        return set.containsAll(subsetSet);
    }

    public int[] minMax() {
        if (set.isEmpty()) return new int[]{};

        int min = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        for (int value : set) {
            if (value < min) min = value;
            if (value > max) max = value;
        }
        return new int[]{min, max};
    }

    public boolean contains(int value) {
        return set.contains(value);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            SetOperator sop = new SetOperator(new int[] {2, 3, 5, 4, 2, 3});
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }

        SetOperator sop1 = new SetOperator(new int[] {2, 3, 5, 4});
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(sop1.minMax())); // [2, 5]
        System.out.println(sop1.isSubset(new int[] {3, 5, 2})) ; // true
        System.out.println(sop1.contains(3)); // true
        System.out.println(sop1.contains(10)); // false
    }
}
```

The first call in the main function creates an instance of the class `SetOperator` with a repeated value in the array, which is not allowed. So it will throw an exception printing `Error: Duplicate values are not allowed`. The other calls for minMax, isSubset and contains will be based on the valid SetOperator object.

Finally, it's a good practice in Java to separate data structures (a `SetOperator`) from a client that uses them (the `main` method here), typically in separate classes. But for this simple use case, this should be enough.
<|end|>


