<|user|>
I am looking at HDBSCAN and wondering whether it is Divisive or Agglomerative? I understand the two approaches, but I cannot seem to grasp which HDBSCAN utilises. Looking for some elaboration.
[https://hdbscan.readthedocs.io/en/latest/](https://hdbscan.readthedocs.io/en/latest/)
<|end|>
<|asst|>
HDBSCAN (Hierarchical Density-Based Spatial Clustering of Applications with Noise) is an agglomerative clustering algorithm. This means that it works by gradually combining smaller clusters into larger ones.

More specifically, HDBSCAN starts by transforming the space according to the density/sparsity of the data points then finds the minimum spanning tree of the distance weighted graph. This tree is then condensed by transforming it into a set of clusters. These clusters are progressively merged in order of their stability. This process involves creating a hierarchy of clusters at different scales, then selecting a set of clusters that best represents the data at a range of scales.

But unlike many hierarchical clustering algorithms, HDBSCAN does not force a data point to be in a cluster, hence, it has the ability to express the concept of noise or outliers. This makes it somewhat different from traditional agglomerative hierarchical clustering, but it is still considered an agglomerative method.
<|end|>


