<|asst|>
You can create this desired effect by adding a nested loop in your code that iterates from 1 to the current n-count of emojis in this scenario. You will also want to add a counter for the number of times the loop executed. The following is how you can use for and while loops to execute this.

```python
import random
import time

# Define your list of emojis.
fish = ['🐠', '🐡', '🦈']

i = 1
while True:
    for _ in range(i):
        print(random.choice(fish), end='')
    print()  # to add a new line after printing the emojis
    i += 1
    time.sleep(.1)
```
In this script, you have a counter i that dictates the number of fish emojis that are printed on each line. After the emojis are printed, i is incremented by 1, and a newline is printed so that the next round of emojis can be printed on a new line.

Note that here while True is used to make the infinite loop, this is the more Pythonic way to make an infinite loop than while 1==1 that you already used. And `end=''` in the print function is used to prevent a new line being started after each emoji.

Please be careful with running an infinite loop script like this one: it'll keep going forever or until it runs out of resources to keep executing, unless you interrupt it (for instance, by pushing `CTRL+C` in the terminal).
<|end|>


