Design a programming function that integrates underscores in place of spaces that separate words ending with punctuation symbols in a provided text string. This function should be proficient at handling multiple punctuation marks and a variety of sentence structures, including but not limited to intricate sentences, compound sentences, and compound-complex sentences. Furthermore, the function should manage nested punctuation and disregard punctuation enclosed within parentheses or quotes. The function should also accommodate multiple languages, including those employing non-Latin scripts, and should be capable of handling and preserving emojis within the text.

To increase the complexity, the function should also be capable of handling and preserving HTML tags within the text. The function should refrain from altering the spaces within the HTML tags. Additionally, the function should be capable of handling and preserving URLs within the text. The function should refrain from altering the spaces within the URLs. 

Moreover, the function should also be able to handle and preserve markdown syntax within the text. The function should refrain from altering the spaces within the markdown syntax. 

The function should also be able to handle and preserve LaTeX syntax within the text. The function should refrain from altering the spaces within the LaTeX syntax. 

For your reference, consider the following flawed code that endeavours to solve the problem but fails to correctly handle HTML tags, URLs, markdown syntax and LaTeX syntax:

```python
def underscore_punctuation(text):
    words = text.split(&amp;amp;#39; &amp;amp;#39;)
    for i in range(len(words)):
        if any(char in string.punctuation for char in words[i]):
            words[i] = words[i].replace(&amp;amp;#39; &amp;amp;#39;, &amp;amp;#39;_&amp;amp;#39;)
    return &amp;amp;#39; &amp;amp;#39;.join(words)
```

This code erroneously replaces spaces with underscores within HTML tags, URLs, markdown syntax and LaTeX syntax, which is not the intended behavior. Your assignment is to rectify this flawed code and implement the additional stipulations.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Solving this problem requires expertise in regular expressions and understanding of various edge-cases involved. This is a pretty involved problem, the provided function is not enough. The following Python code is a more extensive solution. 

```python
import re

def handle_url_and_tag(text):
    urls = re.findall(r'(https?://[^\s]+)', text)
    html_tags = re.findall(r'(<[^>]+>)', text)
    markdown_tags = re.findall(r'(!?\[[^\]]*\]\([^\)]*\))', text)
    latex_tags = re.findall(r'(\$[^$]*\$)', text)
    
    for url in urls:
        text = text.replace(url, url.replace(' ', '__'))
    
    for tag in html_tags:
        text = text.replace(tag, tag.replace(' ', '__'))
    
    for tag in markdown_tags:
        text = text.replace(tag, tag.replace(' ', '__'))
    
    for tag in latex_tags:
        text = text.replace(tag, tag.replace(' ', '__'))
    
    return text

def underscore_punctuation(text):
    text = handle_url_and_tag(text)
    words = text.split('&amp;amp;#39; &amp;amp;#39;')
    for i in range(len(words)):
        if any(char in string.punctuation for char in words[i]):
            words[i] = words[i].replace(&amp;amp;#39; &amp;amp;#39;, &amp;amp;#39;_&amp;amp;#39;)
    return &amp;amp;#39; &amp;amp;#39;.join(words).replace('__', ' ')
```
This code takes care of removing spaces within urls and html tags before the main string operation, and replaces them back after, reducing the overall complexity of regex checks in a single execution. This code also caters to markdown and LaTeX syntax. But it may not handle all edge cases, depending on the complexity of the contents within the regexes. You might need to refine it further according to the use-case. Test it with a variety of inputs to make sure it meets your requirements. 

The complexity of the code would be even higher if we need to handle nested scenarios, for example, a URL within an HTML tag. Such a scenario is not addressed in this algorithm.
<|end|>


