Given three pieces of text, identify the lengthiest mutually occurring sequence of characters within all of them. Also, determine the starting index of the common sequence in each piece of text.

s1 = "Hello, world!"
s2 = "world, goodbye!"
s3 = "Oh, the world is beautiful!"
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Python's difflib library was created for this type of comparison, so we can write a function to find the longest common substring (which must be in all three strings) and then find the first occurrence index in each string. 
We will first construct a function for determining the longest common substring and later a second function for determining the indices:

```python
from difflib import SequenceMatcher

# Determine longest common substring
def longest_common_substring(s1, s2):
    longest_match = SequenceMatcher(None, s1, s2).find_longest_match(0, len(s1), 0, len(s2))
    if longest_match.size != 0:
        return s1[longest_match.a: longest_match.a + longest_match.size] 

# Compare longest common substring in all three strings
def common_in_all(s1, s2, s3):
    return longest_common_substring(longest_common_substring(s1, s2), s3)

# Find index in each string
def index_of_substring(s1, s2, s3):
    common_substring = common_in_all(s1, s2, s3)
    return ([s1.index(common_substring), s2.index(common_substring), s3.index(common_substring)])

s1 = "Hello, world!"
s2 = "world, goodbye!"
s3 = "Oh, the world is beautiful!"

print("Common Sequence: ", common_in_all(s1, s2, s3))
print("Starting Indices: ", index_of_substring(s1, s2, s3))
```

The result will show the longest common substring and the start indices in all three strings. Note, this code does not handle the case where there are multiple common substrings of the same maximum length. It only returns the first one it finds.
<|end|>


