Given the root of a potentially malformed Binary Search Tree, calculate the maximum depth. A tree is defined as malformed if one or more children are incorrectly placed according to BST rules i.e., for each node, every value in the left subtree is less, and every value in the right subtree is greater. Also, identify and mark those malformed nodes.

TreeNode* root = new TreeNode(3);
root->left = new TreeNode(2);
root->right = new TreeNode(7);
root->left->left = new TreeNode(1);
root->left->right = new TreeNode(5); // This is a malformed node, it should not be on the left of 2.
root->right->left = new TreeNode(6);
root->right->right = new TreeNode(8);
root->right->left->right = new TreeNode(4); // This is a malformed node, it should not be on the right of 6.

Note: The TreeNode class is defined as follows:
```c++
class TreeNode
{
  public:
      int val;
      TreeNode* left;
      TreeNode* right;
      bool isMalformed = false;
      TreeNode(int);
};
```                             
Please return a pair<int, int> where the first element represents the max depth of the tree, and the second element represents the number of malformed nodes.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can solve this problem by depth-first traversal of the tree. You start at the root and recursively compare each node's value with its parent's. The maximum depth records how deep the recursion goes. The code below provides a solution for this problem in C++.

C++ Code:
```C++
#include <algorithm>
#include <climits>
using namespace std;

pair<int, int> findDepthAndMalformations(TreeNode* node, int min_val, int max_val, int depth){
    if (node == nullptr) return pair<int, int>{depth, 0};
    
    int malformed = 0;
    if (node->val < min_val || node->val > max_val) { 
        node->isMalformed = true;
        malformed = 1;
    }

    pair<int, int> left_res = findDepthAndMalformations(node->left, min_val, node->val, depth+1);
    pair<int, int> right_res = findDepthAndMalformations(node->right, node->val, max_val, depth+1);

    return pair<int, int> {
        max(left_res.first, right_res.first), 
        malformed + left_res.second + right_res.second
    };
}

pair<int, int> getDepthAndMalformations(TreeNode* root){
    return findDepthAndMalformations(root, INT_MIN, INT_MAX, 0);
}
```
This function 'getDepthAndMalformations' take the root of the binary tree as input and returns the pair of maximum depth of the tree and the number of malformed nodes.

Nodes are marked as malformed and counted during the depth-first search itself. Nodes are checked whether they are within the allowable range from their parent; if not, they are marked as malformed. Min and max values are updated as we navigate down the tree. The depth + 1 is passed recursively to track the maximum depth. Note that this code modifies the input tree, marking the malformed nodes.

Kindly note that the input tree must be properly constructed prior to calling this function, and that integers not exceeding standard limits (INT_MIN and INT_MAX in this case) must be used.
<|end|>


