Create a class in JavaScript to encapsulate user information, but the data should not be directly accessible from the class. Use JavaScript proxies to control access to the data. Furthermore, the class should include functions to validate the user's email and date of birth during the creation of the user object.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is the solution in JavaScript:

```javascript
class User {
    constructor(email, dob) {
        let _user = { email: '', dob: '' }; // private properties

        // Email validation
        const validateEmail = (email) => {
            let re = /\S+@\S+\.\S+/;
            return re.test(email);
        }

        // DateOfBirth validation 
        const validateDOB = (dob) => {
            return !isNaN(Date.parse(dob));
        }

        // Setters and getters for email and date of birth
        const handlers = {
            set: (user, property, value) => {
                switch (property) {
                    case 'email':
                        if (!validateEmail(value)) {
                            throw new Error('Invalid email format.');
                        }
                        break;
                    case 'dob':
                        if (!validateDOB(value)) {
                            throw new Error('Invalid date format.');
                        }
                        break;
                    default:
                        break;
                }
                user[property] = value;
                return true;
            },
            get: (user, property) => {
                return user[property];
            }
        }

        // Proxy object
        this._user = new Proxy(_user, handlers);
        this._user.email = email;
        this._user.dob = dob;
    }

    get email() {
        return this._user.email;
    }

    get dob() {
        return this._user.dob;
    }

    set email(value) {
        this._user.email = value;
    }

    set dob(value) {
        this._user.dob = value;
    }
}

// Test
let user1 = new User('john.doe@gmail.com', '1990-01-01');
console.log(user1.email); // 'john.doe@gmail.com'
console.log(user1.dob); // '1990-01-01'

user1.email = 'john.doe2@gmail.com';
user1.dob = '1991-01-01';
console.log(user1.email); // 'john.doe2@gmail.com'
console.log(user1.dob); // '1991-01-01'
```
In the above code, an instance of the User class can be constructed with email and date of birth parameters. The User class makes use of the "private" `_user` object to hold these property values and uses a Proxy to control access to these properties. When the values are set, they undergo a validation process; if they fail, an error will be thrown.
<|end|>


