You can use `spyOnProperty` to mock the `getElementById` method on the document object, as well as the `print` method on the contentWindow. Here is a simple example with Jasmine:

```typescript
// Import necessary functions
import { ComponentFixture, TestBed } from '@angular/core/testing';
import { By } from '@angular/platform-browser';
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

// Test component
@Component({
  template: `<iframe id="iframe0"></iframe><button (click)="print(0)">Print</button>`
})
class TestComponent {
  print(i) {
    (document.getElementById("iframe" + i) as any).contentWindow.print();
  }
}

describe('Component: TestComponent', () => {
  let component: TestComponent;
  let fixture: ComponentFixture<TestComponent>;
  let printSpy: jasmine.Spy;
  let iframeEl: HTMLIFrameElement;

  beforeEach(() => {

    TestBed.configureTestingModule({
      declarations: [ TestComponent ],
    });

    fixture = TestBed.createComponent(TestComponent);
    component = fixture.componentInstance;

    iframeEl = fixture.debugElement.query(By.css('iframe')).nativeElement;
    printSpy = spyOn(iframeEl.contentWindow, 'print');

    fixture.detectChanges();
          
    spyOnProperty(document, 'getElementById').and.returnValue(iframeEl);
  });

  it('should call print method on iframe contentWindow', () => {
    component.print(0);  
    expect(printSpy).toHaveBeenCalled();
  });
});
```

Don't forget to ensure your tests are operating in a safe environment where actual calls to the `print` method aren't made. Mocking, spying, or stubbing these methods can help achieve this.

