Is it plausible to assert that the exhaustive aggregation of all four-sided polygons with perpendicular angles in a planar realm, inclusive of those not parallel to the axis, holds the capacity to fragment a group of five points? In addition, does the Vapnik-Chervonenkis dimension of a k-Nearest Neighbour classifier ascend to an infinite value when k is congruent to a solitary entity?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Assuming that by "fragment a group of five points", you're referring to being able to divide or subset any possible set of five points into two groups, the answer might be yes. If the five points are chosen such that they do not all lie on the same line, it's feasible (though not guaranteed) that you could find some configuration of four-sided polygons with perpendicular angles (essentially, rectangles) that could divide the points. However, this highly depends on the positions of the points, and in many cases may not be possible.

As for the Vapnik-Chervonenkis (VC) dimension of a k-Nearest Neighbour classifier, the VC dimension does not become infinite when k equals one. In fact, the VC dimension of a 1-nearest neighbour classifier is one. The k-nearest neighbour algorithm classifies a test point based on the majority class of the 'k' points closest to it, and thus its complexity and capacity to segment the instance space does not radically improve with lower k. The VC dimension primarily deals with the question of how many points can be shattered, or correctly classified, regardless of their arrangement. A 1-NN can at most correctly classify an arbitrary arrangement of 1 point, hence its VC dimension is one.
<|end|>


