I need to convert SAS data tables into flat files (or "ASCII files" as they were called once, as opposed to binary files). And only one flat file for each original SAS table.
The challenging thing is that I want the flat file to contain some structural information of the original SAS table also, specifically:

- - - - - - 

Additional information:

- - - 

The standard SAS tables, transport files, XPORT files, etc are all binary format files, and the standard XML table format in SAS and CSV-files don't preserve table structure. So obviously these options don't help.

What is my best option?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Generally, you will not be able to include all the information you require within one flat file per SAS table. Flat files typically contain only data, no metadata like variable labels, formats etc.

However, one thing you could do is export all the required information into separate files. You can create a flat data file, and separate files for the metadata you need (variable labels, format, etc.). This can be done using PROC DATASETS or PROC CONTENTS procedures and writing the output to text files.

Here is an example:

```
/* Export data */
DATA _null_;
file 'path\to\datafile.txt';
set library.dataset;
put (_all_) (:);
run;

/* Export variable labels */
ods listing file="path\to\labels.txt";
proc contents data=library.dataset varnum;
ods output variables=work.my_vars_label(where=(label ne ' '));
run;

data _null_;
set work.my_vars_label;
file 'path\to\labels.txt' mod;
put name $32. +(-1) label $50.;
run;

/* Export formats and informats */
ods listing file="path\to\formats.txt";
proc contents data=library.dataset varnum;
ods output variables=work.my_vars_fmtinf(fmt='character');
run;

data _null_;
set work.my_vars_fmtinf(where=(format ne ' ' or informat ne ' '));
file 'path\to\formats.txt' mod;
put name $32. +(-1) format $16. +(-1) informat $16.;
run;
```

This script exports the data into one file, the variable labels into another file and formats/informats into a third file. Paths of the files need to be updated. It also filters out variables which don't have a format, informat or label.

Remember that the files will need to be handled together in order to reconstruct the original SAS table. 

If you plan to use another platform to read these files, make sure that it supports the kind of separation and script you're using (for example, use of spaces vs tabs in the scripts above, which are easier for humans to interpret than binary). 

If the receiving platform supports a specific data exchange format which preserves metadata, like SPSS or Stata formats, consider converting your SAS data set to this format instead.

Finally, note that this will create files in ASCII format, not EBCDIC (used on mainframes).
<|end|>


