Develop a programming function that not only examines the supplied HTML script and produces a selective array containing solely the paragraph markers indicated by <p> tags, but also identifies and extracts any specific emphasized text within these paragraphs marked by <em> tags. This function should handle nested tags correctly and should ignore any <a> anchor tags.

<html>
    <p>This is a <em>test</em>.</p>
    <a href="">link</a>
    <p>Another <em>test</em>.</p>
    <p>Final <em>testing</em> phase.</p>
</html>
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Python has the built-in HTML parser `html.parser` which can be used to parse the HTML. However, for simplicity, you can use third-party libraries like Beautiful Soup.

Here is the Python code by using the Beautiful Soup library.

```python
from bs4 import BeautifulSoup

html = """
<html>
    <p>This is a <em>test</em>.</p>
    <a href="">link</a>
    <p>Another <em>test</em>.</p>
    <p>Final <em>testing</em> phase.</p>
</html>
"""

def extract_em_text(html):
    soup = BeautifulSoup(html, 'html.parser')
    p_tags = soup.find_all('p')
    
    em_texts_in_p = []
    for tag in p_tags:
        em_tags = tag.find_all('em')
        for em_tag in em_tags:
            em_texts_in_p.append(em_tag.text)

    return em_texts_in_p

print(extract_em_text(html))
```

This solution first finds all 'p' tags in the given HTML. Then, it iterates over each 'p' tag and find all 'em' tags within these 'p' tags. It extracts the text in these 'em' tags and append it to the list `em_texts_in_p`. At the end, this list is returned.

Do note that, the function `find_all` of Beautiful Soup object can return all tags that match the given filter. The `text` property of a tag returns the text within that tag.

Please remember to install Beautiful Soup library (if not done already) by using the command pip install beautifulsoup4.
<|end|>


