Suppose I would like to investigate how multiple variables $[ x_1,\ldots,x_k]$ contribute to another variable $[ y ]$, I had intended to use a simple linear regression.
However, to see the contributions against a thrid variable [ Age ], any idea if it would be methodologically-wise to compute the regression analysis independently across Age and plot the $\beta$ values aginst Age ? (no need to worry about the sample size)
Else, would it be advised to group Age into Age Cohorts, and perform probably a Linear Mixed Model analysis instead ?
Suppose that I would like to achieve in somthing conceptually similar to the following graph:
[](https://i.stack.imgur.com/FAMAw.png)
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The most appropriate methodology will depend on the specific nature of your data and your research goals. However, given your description, a few approaches could be suitable.

1. Running separate regressions for each age: This could be a good approach if there are non-linear or non-monotonic relationships between the predictor variables and the outcome variable at different ages. However, this reduces the sample size for each regression, which could increase the risk of overfitting or underfitting your model. Moreover, the results may be challenging to interpret if the coefficients for the predictor variables differ drastically across ages.

2. Age cohorts: Grouping individuals into age cohorts can be a advantageous way to simplify your analysis, especially if you have reasons to believe that the relationship between the predictor variables and the outcome variable may change at specific age thresholds. However, it will also reduce the resolution of your age variable and could potentially introduce age cohort effects into your results. 

3. Incorporating age in the model: Instead of analyzing separately by age or age cohorts, one could consider including age as a predictor variable in the regression analysis. This would allow to analyze the independent effects of age and the other variables on the outcome. The model would be:

$$ y = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_1 + \ldots + \beta_k x_k + \beta_{k+1} \text{Age} + \epsilon $$

The coefficient $\beta_{k+1}$ would indicate how the response variable $[ y ]$ changes with age, adjusting for the effects of the other predictor variables.

4. Interaction terms: Including interaction terms in your model can be a useful way to investigate how the effects of the predictor variables on the outcome variable may change with age. 

The Linear Mixed Model (LMM) approach is a powerful tool when you have repeated measures on the same individuals, which may or may not be the case here. 

A better understanding of the specifics of your study would allow for more tailored advice. However, these suggestions should provide you with some potential strategies to consider.
<|end|>


