I'm new to python and I'm working on a small autoclicker that you can turn on & off using the letter 's', however when I press it the autoclicker starts but I can't turn it off unless I use the letter 'k' which is used to stop the program completely, this is what my code looks like:

from pynput.mouse import Controller, Button
import keyboard as kb
import time
import multiprocessing

def click():
    
    isToggled = False
    mouse = Controller()

    while True:
        if isToggled:
            mouse.click(Button.left)
            time.sleep(0.01)
        
        if kb.is_pressed('s'):
            if isToggled:
                isToggled = False
            else:
                isToggled = True

        elif kb.is_pressed('k'):
            break

if __name__ == '__main__':
    p = multiprocessing.Process(target=click)
    p.start()
    p.join()
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The reason that you can't stop the autoclicker with the letter 's' is because you check whether 's' has been pressed and consequently set `isToggled` to True or False only once per cycle of the while loop. For the rest of the cycle, `isToggled` remains either True or False and this condition consequently executes or skips `mouse.click(Button.left)`.

If you want to be able to stop the autoclicker with 's', you should check whether 's' has been pressed in each iteration of the while loop. You can do this by testing whether 's' has been pressed in every iteration of the inner while loop, like so:

```python
from pynput.mouse import Controller, Button
import keyboard as kb
import time
import multiprocessing


def click():

    isToggled = False
    mouse = Controller()

    while True:
        while isToggled:
            if kb.is_pressed('s'):
                isToggled = False
                break
            mouse.click(Button.left)
            time.sleep(0.01)

        if kb.is_pressed('s'):
            isToggled = True
        elif kb.is_pressed('k'):
            break


if __name__ == '__main__':
    p = multiprocessing.Process(target=click)
    p.start()
    p.join()
```

This way, you check whether 's' has been pressed either right after you set `isToggled = True` in the outer while loop or after each mouse click in the inner while loop. This way, the autoclicker will stop as soon as you press 's', but not after some delay as in your original version of the program.
<|end|>


