Let (B, S) a multi period binomial model that is arbitrage free.
I would like to prove that the unique optimal exercise time for an American call option is the maturity time T. My idea is to prove this result by contradiction. I want to find an exercise time t<T, such that there exists an arbitrage opportunity, so that the value of the portfolio at time t is greater than zero, starting with an initial value equal to zero. Is this a good idea, and how can I find such strategy explicitely?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The idea to prove by contradiction is indeed a good one. Here is a general outline of how to do that:

Assume that the optimal exercise time for an American call option is at some time t<T.

1. At time t, we have a call option with a strike price of X. Therefore, we have the right to buy the underlying asset at this price. If the underlying asset's price at this time is S(t)<X, we will choose not to exercise the option and let it expire worthless. If S(t)>X, we will exercise the option and immediately sell the underlying asset for S(t) to obtain a profit of S(t)-X.

2. However, if we had chosen not to exercise the option at time t, we would still hold a European option with maturity T and strike price X that would be worth more than zero. This is because European options can only be exercised at their expiration date, and their value is influenced by the potential for the underlying asset's price to increase above the strike price in the remaining time until expiration. Therefore, we have a positive value for the option at time t (no matter if S(t)>X or S(t)<X), which contradicts the assumption that it was optimal to exercise the call option at this time.

3. Thus, we have an arbitrage opportunity: we can start off by buying the call option for zero cost, wait until time t, and either exercise the option if S(t)>X or keep holding it if S(t)<X. In both cases, we end up with a value greater than zero, meaning we made a profit without any investment, which is arbitrage.

This contradiction shows that the assumption cannot be true. Therefore, the conclusion is that the unique optimal exercise time, which maximizes the price of the American call option, must be at the maturity time T.
<|end|>


