After completing a difference-in-differences matched cohort analysis to estimate the pre/post treatment effect of a home health nursing intervention compared to a control group, my employers are interested in dissecting the matched data.
In particular, do we achieve different treatment effects looking at subsets of the original matched data (e.g., high vs. low risk scores, 0 vs. 1+ ER visits in the baseline time period)?
Is this a valid form of analysis or are we getting into trouble with the [Table 2 Fallacy](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6103824/#:%7E:text=A%20%E2%80%9CTable%202%20Fallacy%2C%E2%80%9D,together%20in%20a%20single%20table.)?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Taking subsets of your original matched data to see if different treatment effects are realized does indeed sound like a valid form of analysis. It is a common practice in medical research to explore treatment effects in subgroups of patients defined by baseline characteristics.

However, there are a few caveats and precautions to keep in mind. This kind of exploratory subgroup analysis can lead to the "Table 2 fallacy" where one starts to attribute relationships between baseline covariates and outcomes where none exist. In other words, it is a risk to interpret the association between treatment and outcomes within strata of the covariate as a measure of the effect modification of the treatment by the covariate. 

The statistical power of such analysis also becomes a concern, given it generally leads to smaller sample sizes. It’s also worth noting that results may be more subject to false positives due to multiple comparisons. 

Thus, such subgroup analysis should always be interpreted cautiously and ideally be hypothesis-generating rather than confirmatory. It's best practice to pre-specify these subgroup analyses and account for them in your statistical analysis plan to avoid the pitfalls of data dredging and p-hacking.

If you're interested in truly understanding effect modification or interaction, it might be more appropriate to include interaction terms in your regression model, which formally tests whether the treatment effect differs by levels of a third variable. However, it requires a large sample size and, like subgroup analysis, should also be pre-specified to guard against false discoveries. 

In conclusion, exploring subsets of the matched data is not intrinsically invalid, but it must be done carefully and interpreted with caution.
<|end|>


