So, when I was a comparative novice to the novice I am right now, I used to think that these two things were syntactic sugar for each other, i.e. that using one over the other was simply a personal preference. Over time, I'm come to find that these two are not the same thing, even in a default implementation (see [this](http://web.archive.org/web/20081205081158/http://blogs.msdn.com:80/jmstall/archive/2005/03/06/386064.aspx) and [this](http://web.archive.org/web/20090710210823/http://blogs.msdn.com:80/jmstall/archive/2005/03/12/394645.aspx)). To further confuse the matter, each can be overridden/overloaded separately to have completely different meanings. 

Is this a good thing, what are the differences, and when/why should you use one over the other?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You seem to have not provided the exact concepts or codes that you are comparing. However, based on the linked blogs, I think you are interested in the difference between the `==` operator and `Equals()` method in .NET or similar framework.

Here are the key differences between them:

1. `Equals()`: This is a method defined in the root Object class in .NET which is used for determining if two objects are similar. By default, it does reference equality checking (i.e. are they the same object in memory). However, it can be overridden in any derived class to imply value equality instead (i.e. do they have same value).

2. `==` Operator: On the other hand, `==` is an operator. For reference types, by default it also does reference equality check as `Equals()` method. But for value types, it does value equality check by default. Like `Equals()`, `==` operator can also be overridden.

Now, Coming to your questions:

- **Is it a good thing?**
Yes. Having both `Equals()` and `==` gives flexibility to developers. The choice depends on the particular requirements of the code.

- **Should you use one over the other?**
There is no hard and fast rule about when to use `Equals()` versus `==`. That said, commonly `Equals()` is used when you explicitly want to check the object's value equality. And `==` is often used for reference equality checks, especially when one of the operands could be null.
  
Remember, if you override one, it's generally a good practice to override the other, to ensure they remain consistent with each other.

This is true in context of .NET, C#, and similar languages. The exact semantics could vary in different programming languages. Always consult your language's particular documentation to understand these nuances.
<|end|>


