To handle your case, you can use JavaScript's Fetch API to fetch the data from the URL and then console the `@timestamp` field. The example below is written in JavaScript:

```javascript
fetch('https://run.mocky.io/v3/1e7e5b56-1ae7-4212-92a1-1b233707f495')
  .then(response => response.json())
  .then(data => console.log(data['@timestamp']))
  .catch((error) => {
    console.error('Error:', error)
  });
```

In this code example, Fetch API makes a request to the provided URL. The response of the fetch request is a Promise that resolves to the Response object representing the response to the request.

`response.json()` method is used, which returns a Promise that resolves with the result of parsing the body text as JSON, which could be any kind of JSON value.

In the next `then`, the `@timestamp` field from the data is logged in the console. If there is an error in any of the promises, it will be logged in the console by the `catch`. 

Please remember that if `@timestamp` field is not a direct child of your JSON object and it's nested, you need to access it with the correct path. For example if it's inside a `data` object inside your returned JSON, then you would access it like this: `console.log(data['data']['@timestamp'])`

Also, replace `'https://run.mocky.io/v3/1e7e5b56-1ae7-4212-92a1-1b233707f495'` URL with the URL you're working with.
