Considering the given context that two different processors, namely M-5 and M-7, implement the same instruction set, where M-5 utilizes a 5-stage pipeline and a clock cycle of 10 nanoseconds, while M-7 employs a 7-stage pipeline with a clock cycle of 7.5 nanoseconds, could you ascertain which of the following propositions are accurate? 
I. The maximum throughput of M-7’s pipeline surpasses that of M-5’s pipeline.
II. When comparing a single instruction's latency, M-7’s pipeline is more expedient than M-5’s pipeline.
III. Irrespective of the nature of the program, execution on M-7 will always yield faster results than execution on M-5.
A) Only I is true 
B) Only II is true 
C) Both I and III are true
D) Both II and III are true
<|end|>
<|asst|>
I. The maximum throughput of a pipeline is determined by the number of stages and the length of the clock cycle. For M-5, the throughput is one instruction every 10 nanoseconds, or 0.1 instructions per nanosecond. For M-7, the throughput is one instruction every 7.5 nanoseconds, or approximately 0.133 instructions per nanosecond. Therefore, M-7 has a higher throughput than M-5. This statement is true.

II. The latency of a single instruction is determined by the number of pipeline stages and the length of the clock cycle. This is equivalent to the total time it takes for a single instruction to move through the entire pipeline. For M-5, the latency is 5 stages * 10 nanoseconds/stage = 50 nanoseconds. For M-7, the latency is 7 stages * 7.5 nanoseconds/stage = 52.5 nanoseconds. Therefore, M-5 has a lower latency than M-7, and this statement is false.

III. While M-7 has a higher throughput, the nature of the program can affect the overall execution time. Certain programs may not benefit from a longer pipeline if there are many data dependencies or other factors that prevent instructions from being continuously fed into the pipeline. Therefore, this statement is false.

So, the answer would be A) Only I is true.
<|end|>


