Predicting soccer results involves both regression (predicting scores) and classification (predicting win/loss/draw). The project also involves working with time-series data as the outcomes of matches likely depend on the outcomes of previous matches. As you have various control variables, so the prediction model would become more suitable if it can handle high dimensional data.

Given the complexity of the project, a Neural Network algorithm indeed seems like a suitable approach, and more specifically, a type of neural network called Recurrent Neural Network (RNN). RNNs are particularly effective when it comes to time-series data.

If your class has not covered neural networks yet, getting a good understanding of them could take some time. An alternative approach could be using a Random Forest or Gradient Boosting algorithm. Both of these are capable of handling high-dimensional data and can do both classification and regression. They also have the added benefit of providing feature importance, which could be useful for understanding which variables had the biggest impact on the prediction.

Remember that it's best to experiment with several different models, and compare their performances on a validation set to choose the best performing one. You may want to try out simpler models earlier in the project, then move into more complex models as you get a grip on what is working and what's not. You may also consider ensemble methods to combine outputs from several models. 

Lastly, bear in mind that the quality of your predictions will be largely determined by the features you have, rather than the specific algorithm you choose. Feature engineering, pre-processing and domain knowledge are key to successful predictive modeling for sports results.
