I have an Interface called `IStep` that can do some computation (See "[Execution in the Kingdom of Nouns](http://steve-yegge.blogspot.com/2006/03/execution-in-kingdom-of-nouns.html)"). At runtime, I want to select the appropriate implementation by class name.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a simple solution where `IStep` implementations are mapped to their class names in a `Map`. This map is then used to retrieve the appropriate `IStep` implementation at runtime.

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class StepFactory {
    private Map<String, IStep> steps = new HashMap<>();

    public StepFactory() {
        // Register your classes here. These might be loaded from some kind of configuration in a real case.
        steps.put(Step1.class.getName(), new Step1());
        steps.put(Step2.class.getName(), new Step2());
        // And so on...
    }

    public IStep getStep(String className) {
        IStep step = steps.get(className);
        if (step == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("No IStep implementation found for class: " + className);
        }
        return step;
    }
}
```

You might want to consider whether the class name is the best identifier for your `IStep` implementations. Depending on what types of computations they do, perhaps some kind of computation type identifier would be more appropriate.

Also, be aware that if the `IStep` implementations are stateful (i.e. they store some kind of data that is modified during their computation), the above solution will not work as all clients will share the same instances. In this case, you will probably need to create new instances of each class every time `getStep()` is called, which you can achieve with Java reflection.

```java
public IStep getStep(String className) {
    try {
        Class<?> clazz = Class.forName(className);
        return (IStep) clazz.newInstance();
    } catch (ClassNotFoundException | InstantiationException | IllegalAccessException e) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Problem creating a new instance of class: " + className, e);
    }
}
```

This will create a new instance of the class every time it is requested. The class must have a no-args constructor for this to work.

Please remember, it is a better idea to limit the scope of reflection use within an application. It should usually be constrained to code that's dealing with components or plugins, config/bootstrap code, etc. not as a general programming tool. Seems your application's design leading you towards reflection, which might sign of some design issues.
<|end|>


