You can declare and initialize an array of pointers to integers in C as follows:

```c
int* ptrArray[10]; // declares an array of 10 int pointers.
```

Next, you want to allocate memory for these pointers using dynamic memory allocation methods. You can do so using the `malloc()` function in C. 

Here is a simple piece of code that firstly declares an array of 10 int pointers, dynamically allocates memory for integers, and then assigns each integer pointer in the array to point to the allocated memory:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

void allocateMemoryForPointers(int** array, int arraySize)
{
    for(int i = 0; i < arraySize; i++)
    {
        array[i] = (int*)malloc(sizeof(int)); // allocate memory 
        if(array[i] == NULL)
        {
            // handle error 
            printf("Memory could not be allocated for int pointer at index %d\t", i);
            exit(0);
        }
    }
}

int main()
{
    int* ptrArray[10]; // an array of 10 pointers to integer
    allocateMemoryForPointers(ptrArray, 10);
    // insert the clean up code here
    return 0;
}
```

Don't forget that after using dynamically-allocated memory, you should free it when it's no longer needed. Here's the cleanup code:

```c
for(int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
{
    free(ptrArray[i]);
}
```
