Construct a Java software module that not only validates if a supplied numerical input qualifies as an Armstrong number, but also prints out all the Armstrong numbers within a provided range (e.g., between 1 to the given number). Also, if the numerical input does not qualify as an Armstrong number, the module should print the closest Armstrong number (above or below). The application should handle potential exceptions and errors, such as non-numeric or negative input, maintaining its robustness.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is a simple Java class to validate if a numerical input is an Armstrong number, prints out all the Armstrong numbers within a range (i.e., between 1 to the input number), and if the input is not an Armstrong number, prints the closest Armstrong number (above or below).

```java
public class ArmstrongNumberFinder {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int input;
        try {
            input = Integer.parseInt(args[0]);
            if(input < 1) {
                System.out.println("Input number must be positive.");
                return;
            }
        }
        catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            System.out.println("Invalid input. Please input a positive integer.");
            return;
        }
        catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("An error occurred.");
            return;
        }

        boolean isArmstrong = isArmstrong(input);

        System.out.println();
        // Check if the input number is Armstrong
        if(isArmstrong) {
            System.out.println(input + " is an Armstrong number.");
            findArmstrongsInRange(1, input);
        }
        else {
            System.out.println(input + " is not an Armstrong number.");
            findArmstrongsInRange(1, input);
            // Look for closest Armstrong number
            findClosestArmstrong(input);
        }
    }

    private static boolean isArmstrong(int num) {
        int originalNum, remainder, result = 0, n = 0;
        originalNum = num;

        for (;originalNum != 0; originalNum /= 10, ++n);

        originalNum = num;
        for (;originalNum != 0; originalNum /= 10) {
            remainder = originalNum % 10;
            result += Math.pow(remainder, n);
        }

        return result == num;
    }

    private static void findArmstrongsInRange(int start, int end) {
        System.out.println("Armstrong numbers in range " + start + " - " + end + " are: ");
        for(int i = start; i <= end; i++) {
            if(isArmstrong(i)) {
                System.out.print(i + " ");
            }
        }
        System.out.println();
    }

    private static void findClosestArmstrong(int num) {
        // Assume largest possible nearest Armstrong number initially
        int nearestArmstrong = 1000000000;
        // Iterate to find a closer Armstrong number
        for(int i = num; i > 0; i--) {
            if(i <= nearestArmstrong && isArmstrong(i)) {
                nearestArmstrong = i;
            }
        }

        // Iterate above the number as well
        for(int i = num+1; i < nearestArmstrong*2; i++) {
            if(isArmstrong(i)) {
                nearestArmstrong = i;
                break; // Exit loop as we've found a closer number
            }
        }

        System.out.println("The nearest Armstrong number is: " + nearestArmstrong);
    }
}
```

Run the program as `java ArmstrongNumberFinder 1000`. This Java program reads an integer from the command-line input, checks if the input number is an Armstrong number, finds all Armstrong numbers up to the input number, and lastly, finds the closest Armstrong number if the input number is not an Armstrong number. The program handles any exceptions and inappropriate input gracefully. 
Make sure to compile the java file before running it. Use `javac ArmstrongNumberFinder.java` to compile.
<|end|>


