<|user|>
I am currently building a wordpress plugin for the dashboard.
Everything works so far, but at the moment I handle everything with jQuery.
I would now like to integrate React instead of jQuery for practice.
For this I have first created a React app in my plugin

npx create-react-app reactapp


In my dashboard I created a page with the following code:

<?php

echo "<div id='root'></div>";


Now I wanted to load React:

wp_register_script('react-js', plugin_dir_url(__FILE__). "templates/reactapp/src/index.js" , array(),null, true);
wp_enqueue_script('react-js');


But there is now the following error in the console if I open the page in the Dashboard:

What is the best way to proceed?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The issue is that you are trying to load a React.js app into a Wordpress plugin without actually building the React library. Your index.js file is not the whole React library, but just the entry point, which uses ES6 and JSX that is not recognized by browser. 

The correct way to use a React app with Wordpress is to first "compile" your React application back to Javascript before using it within WordPress. This process is called "transpiling". 

Here are the general steps with Create React App:

1. Open command line terminal, navigate to your "reactapp" directory (inside your plugin).

2. Run `npm run build`. This builds the app for production to the `build` folder. 

3. After you ran `npm run build`, you'll see a new "build" folder inside your "reactapp" directory.

4. Now, instead of enqueuing `index.js`, you should enqueue main.js file that generated in build/static/js

```PHP
// example
wp_register_script('react-js', plugin_dir_url(__FILE__). "templates/reactapp/build/static/js/main.js" , array(), null, true);
wp_enqueue_script('react-js');
```

5. Inside your main PHP file where you're echoing the `<div id="root"></div>`, you should include your main.css as well. 

```PHP
// example
wp_enqueue_style('main-css', plugin_dir_url(__FILE__) . 'templates/reactapp/build/static/css/main.css');
wp_enqueue_script('react-js', plugin_dir_url(__FILE__) . 'templates/reactapp/build/static/js/main.js', array(), null, true );
```

Make sure to rerun `npm run build` in your development process every time you make changes to the React code.

Remember to enqueue the React and ReactDOM libraries in WordPress if they are not included in the build file.

P.S: You may also consider setting up a development environment with Webpack and Babel to directly transpile and bundle your React code. This setup is more complex but gives you more control and is more "WordPress-like" as your source files are in your plugin, not in a separate Create React App environment.
<|end|>


