I was going through the proof about the equality of forward and futures price (assuming constant interest rate) in a book. Somewhere, the authors used the fact that suppose we start with two capitals - say A and B and we end up with the same final capital S, then A=B otherwise there will be arbitrage opportunity. Why arbitrage arises if A and B are not same?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Arbitrage arises when there's a discrepancy in the pricing between two identical assets or identical cash flows in different markets. If A and B are two different initial capitals but they both yield the same final result S, that would mean the cost of investment would be more profitable if you choose the lesser initial capital. 

Arbitrageurs would exploit this opportunity by borrowing money at the lower initial capital (say A if A<B), invest it to get the final result S (as per your assumption), and then return the borrowing with interest while keeping the excess amount as risk-free profit. This action would in turn drive the price up of the lower initial capital and/or drive the price down of the higher initial capital until no arbitrage opportunity remains, which would then balance the system into A=B. Hence, A is supposed to be equal to B, otherwise arbitrage will occur.  

Keep in mind that this holds assuming that the markets are efficient, meaning all trading participants have the same timely and complete information and can act upon it quickly enough to balance out mispricing. In reality, markets can have inefficiencies where such arbitrage opportunities might exist for certain periods of time.
<|end|>


