I would like to know the best way to use a datagrid control linked to a combination (join) of data tables in a way that both simply allows both display, creation of new rows in underlying tables and deletion.

The datagrid appears to offer the latter capabilities but I have not found a way that I am happy with to do more than just display on one grid and offer specific separate edit create and delete facilities.

Suppose for the sake of illustration that the database contains:-

Customer Table
* CustomerID
* CustomerName

Order Table
* CustomerID
* OrderLineItem
* OrderLineQuanity

And that I want to lose the CustomerID for display purposes but would like to be able to create new customers and delete existing ones, perhaps with a confirmatory dialog.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
One of the best ways to use a datagrid control for multiple tables and allowing CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations with joined entities is to leverage the use of Master-Detail views. This implies using two datagrids. One showing the parent entity (in this case, the Customers) and another one, related details (in this case, Orders).

Below is an outline of how to achieve this:

1. Master Grid: It is used to display the main entity (Customers). In your case, CustomerID and CustomerName. You can allow users to add or delete rows here, and this will handle the creation or deletion of Customers.

2. Detail Grid: It is bound to the selected row in the Master grid, to display the corresponding detail data (Orders). The underlying data source of the Detail grid will be a list of Orders belonging to the selected Customer in the Master grid. When you add or delete rows in the Detail Grid, this handles the creation or deletion of Orders for a selected customer.

3. ForeignKey column: Now, if you want to not display the CustomerID in the Order table but you need it to attach orders to customers, you can use a hidden column in your grid. Your Foreign key column (CustomerID in Order table) can be hidden so it is not displayed, but you can still access its value in your backend code for manipulation of data.

Here’s an implementation outline of this pattern:

- Create two Datagrids on your form.
  
- For Master Datagrid (Customers), allow users to add new rows or delete existing rows.

- For the Detail Datagrid (Orders), bind it to the selected item of the Master Datagrid; allow users to add or delete rows here. 

- Also, hide the foreign key column ‘CustomerID’ in the detail datagrid.

- When updating the database, ensure that every operation in the UI reflects on the data. For instance, when a new row is added in the Master Datagrid (new customer is added), a new row should be inserted in the Customer Table in the database. Similarly, when a row is deleted from the Detail Datagrid (an Order of a customer is deleted), the corresponding row/entry in the Order Table should be deleted.

Remember to utilise events that most datagrid controls have, such as `RowEnter`, `RowLeave`, `RowValidated`, `CellValidating`, `RowDeleting`, etc. You can optimise updating your database in these event handlers.

Most importantly, ensure that the database transactions are atomic i.e., form a single unit of work. Even if one insertion or deletion fails, none of the changes proposed in the transaction should happen.

It is advisable to use a data access technology that supports LINQ (like Entity Framework) as that would make the code easier to manage.
<|end|>


