In addition, consider the numerical string to be "123456789ABCDEF...Za...z". Each number and character represents unique coding information which should be reflected visually in the pie chart. Each character should reflect a different color, and each digit should reflect different proportions of the pie chart accordingly. 

Furthermore, the erroneous HTML and JavaScript code below doesn't correctly generate the pie chart as expected. Identify the issue in this code, provide a comprehensive solution, and upgrade it to meet the specifications described above:

```html
<html>
<head>
    <script src="https://cdn.anychart.com/releases/8.1.0/js/anychart-bundle.min.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
    <div id="container"></div>
    <script>
       var chart = anychart.pie();
       var rawData = ['1', '2', '3', '4', '5', '6', '7', '8', '9']; 
       var data = [];
       for (var i = 0; i < rawData.length; i++) {
           data.push([rawData[i], parseInt(rawData[i])]);
       }
       chart.data(data);
       chart.title('Pie chart from numerical string');
       chart.container('container');
       chart.draw();
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The problem with the existing code is that it does not handle multiple parts of the chart or distinct sections; it also does not handle the color mapping based on the characters of your numerical string.

The instructions seem to indicate that you want to create distinct colors for each different character in your string, and proportion the segments in each section of the pie chart based on numerical values. If M is meant to signify the total number of sections in the pie chart while N represents the number of segments in each part, this will introduce complexity; the number of segments would need to be handled in a separate customization.

Assuming that the string represents proportional data rather than just colors, here is an updated version of the HTML and JavaScript code that creates a pie chart with 3 parts where each part has a different color and reflects a proportion of the total value. For simplicity, we are only using numbers to represent unique coding information and proportions:

```html
<html>
<head>
    <script src="https://cdn.anychart.com/releases/8.10.0/js/anychart-bundle.min.js"></script>
    <style>
        #container {
            width: 100%;
            height: 100%;
            overflow: hidden;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div id="container"></div>
    <script>
       var chart = anychart.pie();
       var rawData = '123456789'; // your numerical string

       // color mapping
       var colorMap = {
         '1': '#f44336', '2': '#e91e63', '3': '#9c27b0',
         '4': '#673ab7', '5': '#3f51b5', '6': '#2196f3',
         '7': '#03a9f4', '8': '#00bcd4', '9': '#009688'
       };

       var data = [];
       for (var i = 0; i < rawData.length; i++) {
           // push data proportional to each digit with related color
           data.push({value: parseInt(rawData[i]), 
                      name: rawData[i], 
                      normal: {fill: colorMap[rawData[i]]}
                  });
       }
       chart.data(data);
       chart.title('Pie chart from numerical string');
       chart.container('container');
       chart.draw();
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

The hexadecimal values in the colorMap object represent the RGB color codes for the part of the pie chart related to each numeric string character. The color mapping is a must to distinguish between the different parts of the pie chart in this case.
