Create a React Native application that stores a list of books. The application should have a list view that displays all of the books and a detail view for each book.
[
  {
    "title": "Lord of the Rings",
    "author": "J.R.R. Tolkien"
  },
  {
    "title": "Foundation",
    "author": "Isaac Asimov"
  }
]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, I will describe how to create a simple React Native app with these specifications. You'll need React Native environment properly setup. Please, note that this is a simplified example. Likely, you'd use some fetch request to fetch your data and have more complex book objects (with IDs, descriptions, cover image URLs etc). Also, for larger apps, you might need to consider better state management (like Redux, MobX, etc).  
  
Firstly, install a new project using either npm or yarn.

```npm install -g create-react-native-app```  
```create-react-native-app my-app```
  
Then create two components, one for the list of books and the other for showing the details of each book.
```jsx
// BookList.js

import React from 'react';
import { FlatList, View, Text, TouchableOpacity } from 'react-native';

class BookList extends React.Component{
    state = {
        books: [
            {
                "title": "Lord of the Rings",
                "author": "J.R.R. Tolkien"
            },
            {
                "title": "Foundation",
                "author": "Isaac Asimov"
            }
        ],
    }

    handlePress = (book) => {
        this.props.navigation.navigate('Details', {
            book: book
        });
    };

    render() {
        return (
            <View>
                <FlatList
                    data={this.state.books}
                    keyExtractor={(item, index) => index.toString()}
                    renderItem={({item}) => 
                        <TouchableOpacity onPress={()=> this.handlePress(item)}>
                            <Text>{item.title}</Text>
                        </TouchableOpacity>
                    }
                />
            </View>
        );
    }
}

export default BookList;
```
Then, 
```jsx
// BookDetails.js

import React from 'react';
import { View, Text } from 'react-native';

class BookDetails extends React.Component{
    render() {
        const {book} = this.props.route.params;

        return (
            <View>
                <Text>{book.title}</Text>
                <Text>{book.author}</Text>
            </View>  
        );
    }
}

export default BookDetails;
```
Finally, use react-navigation to navigate between these two screens
```jsx
// App.js

import { NavigationContainer } from '@react-navigation/native';
import { createStackNavigator } from '@react-navigation/stack';
import BookList from './BookList';
import BookDetails from './BookDetails';

const Stack = createStackNavigator();

function App() {
    return (
        <NavigationContainer>
            <Stack.Navigator>
                <Stack.Screen name="BookList" component={BookList} />
                <Stack.Screen name="Details" component={BookDetails} />
            </Stack.Navigator>
        </NavigationContainer>
    );
}

export default App;
```
To run this application, just type `npm run android` or `npm run ios`, depending on the platform you are targeting. The `BookList` screen would show a list of books, and once a book is clicked, the application would navigate to the `Details` screen, where more details about the book can be displayed.
<|end|>


