Develop a function in Python to locate and replace all URLs with the term "URL" in a given string utilizing regular expressions.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a Python function that uses the re (regular expressions) module to do exactly what you asked for.

```python
import re

def replace_urls_with_term(string: str) -> str:
    return re.sub(
        r'http[s]?://(?:[a-zA-Z]|[0-9]|[$-_@.&+]|[!*\\(\\),]|(?:%[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]))+',
        'URL', string)
```
This function will locate all URLs in a given string and replace them with the term "URL".

Please note the regular expression:

```python
'http[s]?://(?:[a-zA-Z]|[0-9]|[$-_@.&+]|[!*\\(\\),]|(?:%[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]))+'
```
This expression matches http URLs as well as https URLs.

The expression uses some special characters as well. For example, it uses the square brackets to specify a character range, the asterisk to match zero or more repetitions of the preceding character, the backslash to escape special characters, and so on.

Here's how you can use the function:

```python
string = 'This is a string with a URL: https://example.com.'
print(replace_urls_with_term(string))

# Output: This is a string with a URL: URL.
```
As you can see, the URL https://example.com was replaced with "URL".
