Develop a program using the C programming language that not only calculates the mean value of a series of positive integers contained within an array structure, but also identifies the median and mode values. Additionally, the program should be able to handle an array of arbitrary size that the user can input at runtime.

You need to account for edge cases: If the array contains an even number of values, calculate the median as the average of the two middle numbers. If multiple modes exist, return all. The use of external libraries is not allowed. Only rely on core C language features and standard libraries.

Sample array to be used for testing:
int nums[] = {2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 7, 8, 8, 8, 100};
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can utilize the following C program:

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#define MAX_SIZE 100

void sort(int a[], int n) {
    int i, j, min, temp;
    for (i = 0; i < n-1; i++) {
        min = i;
        for (j = i+1; j < n; j++)
            if (a[j] < a[min])
                min = j;
        temp = a[i];
        a[i] = a[min];
        a[min] = temp;
    }
}

int main() {
    int nums[MAX_SIZE], n, i, j, maxCount, mode[MAX_SIZE];
    float sum, median, mean;
    printf("Enter array size: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);

    printf("Enter numbers: ");
    for (i=0; i < n; i++) {
        scanf("%d", nums+i);
        sum += nums[i];
    }

    sort(nums, n);
    mean = sum/n;
  
    if(n%2 == 0)
        median = (nums[(n-1)/2] + nums[n/2])/2.0;
    else
        median = nums[n/2];

    maxCount = 0; j = 0;
    for(i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        int count = 1;
        while (i + 1 < n && nums[i] == nums[i+1]) {
            i++;
            count++;
        }

        if (count > maxCount) {
            maxCount = count;
            j = 0;
            mode[j] = nums[i];
            j++;
        }
        else if (count == maxCount) {
            mode[j] = nums[i];
            j++;
        }
    }
  
    printf("\nMean = %.2f", mean);
    printf("\nMedian = %.2f", median);

    printf("\nMode = ");
    for (i = 0; i < j; i++){
        printf("%d ",mode[i]);
    }
    return 0;
}
```
The program declares the following variables: `nums[]` stores the numbers; `n` stores the array's size; and `i`, `j`, `maxCount`, `mode[]`, `sum`, `median`, `mean` store the mode count, mean, and median of numbers, respectively. The function `sort()` is used to sort `nums[]` in ascending order. The programming logic inside the `main()` function calculates the mean, median and mode of `nums[]` following the rules defined in the problem. The `for` loop starting with `while (i + 1 < n && nums[i] == nums[i+1])` is for counting the frequency of each element in `nums[]`. If a number has a higher frequency than the current mode, it becomes the new mode. If the frequency is same, it gets added to the existing mode list.

Please remember that this code doesn't check for positive integers explicitly as it was stated (only positive integers). You might want to include error checking code for if the user inputs zero or negative numbers.
<|end|>


