Can someone please help me with the best Ramp up time for 20 users as well as 600 users.
I have given Users: 20, Rampup: 60 sec , which is leading to Reason for socket exception in Client-Server Application
I have given Users: 20, Rampup: 60 sec , which is leading to Reason for socket exception in Client-Server Application
Non HTTP response code: javax.net.ssl.SSLException/Non HTTP response message: Socket closed
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Ramp-up time in performance testing is the time taken to apply the complete load (full number of users) on the server from zero users. 

If you define a ramp-up period, JMeter will equally spread (linearly) the load out over that time. For example, if you have 20 users and a 60 second Ramp-Up period, then the delay between starting users would be 3 seconds (Ramp-Up period / Number of Users).

If you are seeing socket exceptions, this might indicate that your server is struggling to cope with the load. This could be due to a number of reasons such as database connections, memory issues, bandwidth limitations, etc.

To avoid this, you could consider increasing your ramp-up time, reducing the number of users, or addressing the server performance issues causing the socket exceptions.

There is no "best" ramp-up time as it depends on your specific use case, application architecture and performance goals.

However, a good practice in load testing is:
1. Start with a single user to check functionality. 
2. Once functionality is verified start with 20 users with a ramp up of 5 seconds per user (which is 100 seconds for 20 users).
3. For 600 users, you might consider starting with a higher ramp up, maybe 10 seconds per user (which is 6000 seconds/100 minutes for 600 users). 

Note: These are just starting points and may need to be adjusted based on testing results and further server and system monitoring.

Do remember, the aim of performance testing is not just about throwing maximum load on the server but also about increasing the load gradually and see how the server behaves and responds so you have a more realistic simulation.
<|end|>


