Has there been any historical evaluation of the quality of credit ratings provided by agencies such as Fitch, S&P, and Moody's? Are there any academic resources available on this topic? I have checked Google Scholar, but could not find much. I'm curious to learn more about this topic, including the methodologies that can be used to test the accuracy of these credit ratings.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, there have been several evaluations of the quality of credit ratings provided by agencies such as Fitch, S&P, and Moody's. Here are a few key resources:

1. "The Accuracy of Moody's Bond Ratings" by James H. Gellert: This study focuses on the measurement of the accuracy of Moody's bond ratings, using an extensive sample of rated and unrated firms.

2. "Rating the Raters: Are Some Credit Rating Agencies Better than Others?" by Prachi Deuskar (University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign), Timothy Johnson (MIT Sloan School of Management), and Zhihua Wei (University of Ulster). It examines the relative quality of credit ratings from different agencies.

3. "The Informational Role of Credit Ratings in Debt Pricing" by Doron Avramov (University of Maryland), Tarun Chordia (Emory University), Gergana Jostova (George Washington University), and Alexander Philipov (George Mason University).

Existing studies typically evaluate the accuracy of credit ratings by correlating them with subsequent default events or studying the effect of rating changes on asset prices. The methodology can vary depending on the focus of the research, but it often involves statistical and econometric techniques to hone in on the predictive power of ratings.

Some key points to note from these studies are the contingent nature of rating accuracy (some agencies are more accurate for certain types of debt or periods), the impact of potential conflicts of interest, and the extent to which rating changes are anticipatory or reactive.

The financial crisis of 2008 has also led to much scrutiny of credit rating agencies, as they were widely seen as failing to adequately assess the risk of various mortgage-backed securities that were central to the crisis. This event has sparked interest in evaluating and reformulating the methods by which these agencies assess credit risk.
<|end|>


