<|asst|>
You might want to look into AWS Batch. AWS Batch allows you to easily and efficiently run hundreds of thousands of batch computing jobs on AWS. 

If you need more than the 120GB of memory that is supported by the largest AWS Batch instance types, you can use multiple instances and spread out the work, or use Spot instances, which can provide significant cost savings for batch workloads.

AWS Batch supports Docker and allows for complex dependencies to be bundled with your jobs. You would need to run your calculations in Docker containers, which would then be managed by AWS Batch. 

In general, instances with large amounts of RAM (like r5.24xlarge, with 768 GB RAM) are available on EC2. The easiest way to administer such resources is often via Kubernetes (using the AWS managed Kubernetes service, EKS), but that might involve more management than you're interested in.

Keep in mind though, for instances with such high requirement, on demand prices can be quite high. Check if your workload can take advantage of spot instances. 

EMR (Elastic Map Reduce) is another service that allows larger instances sizes. This is a platform for data processing, offering powerful tools for big data processing, such as Apache Spark and Hadoop. While designed for distributed processing, if your task can't be broken up, there might be ways to use it for single-instance, high-memory tasks. 

Remember that additional charges will apply when using AWS services and the price may vary based on the compute resources you choose. Please refer to the AWS Pricing page for each service for more details.
<|end|>


