I am aware of [Capistrano](http://www.capify.org/), but it is a bit too heavyweight for me. Personally, I set up two [Mercurial](http://www.selenic.com/mercurial/wiki/) repositories, one on the production server and another on my local dev machine. Regularly, when a new feature is ready, I push changes from repository on my local machine to repository on the server, then update on the server. This is a pretty simple and quick way to keep files in sync on several computers, but does not help to update databases.
What is your solution to the problem?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
There are numerous methods for managing deployment, and many of them depend on what exactly your application stack is like. Here are a few potential solutions:

1. **Git & SSH**: Similar to your current setup with Mercurial, you can also use Git which has super flexible branching and good support for hooks. Create a post-receive hook on your production server to update the working copy whenever you push changes. Again, this only covers code deployment and not database modifications.

2. **Docker & Kubernetes**: If you're working with Docker-based applications, Kubernetes makes deployment a breeze. You can package both your application and its environment into a Docker image, and use Kubernetes to ensure that your application is always running in production.

3. **Database migrations**: For dealing with database changes, you could use a migrations system like those provided by Rails or Django. You write scripts that make the changes to your database and check them in alongside your code. When deploying, you run the migrations before the updated code.

4. **CI/CD tools**: Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment tools such as Jenkins, CircleCI or Travis CI automate your application building, testing and deployment process. These tools often have plugins or built-in features to manage database migrations as well.

5. **Deployment platforms**: Platforms such as Heroku, AWS CodeDeploy or Google App Engine handle a lot of the deployment procedure for you. Simply push your code to the platform and it takes care of building, deploying and scaling your application.

6. **Ansible**: If your application runs on a fleet of servers, Ansible can help manage configuration and deployment. You can describe the desired state of your servers using Ansible's declarative language, and then apply those descriptions to your servers. 

Just remember, deployment is a big topic and there is no one-size-fits-all approach. You need to choose a method that fits well with your application, your team's skillsets, and your organization's practices and needs.
<|end|>


