There are several options available for you, however, they may not all be straightforward as you would want them to be:

1. Use DirectShow: It is quite a low-level API but it should provide you with all the features you need. Check out this MSDN article for more information: https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/windows/desktop/dd375454(v=vs.85).aspx

2. Bass.Net API: A powerful, yet easy to use .NET version of the original multi-platform BASS Audio Library. It provides support for various codecs and has quite an easy to use API. More details and examples can be found on their official website: http://www.un4seen.com/

3. Use Windows Media Player: You can use the APIs provided by Windows Media Player itself. They are more complex, however, it is quite flexible. Here is a link to the API documentation: https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wmp/windows-media-player-sdk

4. CsCore Library: It is a .NET library for handling audio. It provides classes to read, write and manipulate audio data. It is a simple and user-friendly library. The source code is available on GitHub: https://github.com/filoe/cscore

5. Managed Media Helper Library: It is a wrapper class for the Windows API functions dealing with multimedia. It provides functionalities to play, pause, stop, and seek through audio files. More information can be found on author's blog: https://blogs.msdn.microsoft.com/mikehall/2006/02/13/managed-media-helpers/

6. NAudio Library: It is an open source .NET audio and MIDI library, providing high level API for managing and playing audio. GitHub Link: https://github.com/naudio/NAudio

Some of these options may put a bit of overhead on you, but unfortunately there is no straightforward API (at least not one that I know of) to perform all the audio playing functionalities on Windows Mobile. However, options like DirectShow, Bass.Net API, and Managed Media Helper should give you a good start.
