Is it possible to use Dynamic Time Warping (DTW) algorithm as a method to find the similarity between two time-series data that have different step/measurement sizes? For example, one is measured every hour and the other is measured every day.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, it is possible. The Dynamic Time Warping (DTW) algorithm is used to align and compare two time series which may vary in speed or length. It "warps" the time dimension of the two sequences (either compressing or expanding in time) to provide an optimal match. 

However, in your case, where one time-series is measured every hour and the other is measured every day, you may need to preprocess the data to make it comparable. For example, you could resample the hourly data down to the daily level or interpolate the daily data up to the hourly level. Once the data is in a comparable format, you can apply the DTW algorithm to find the similarity.

Remember, while DTW can handle different lengths and speeds of time series, it doesn't inherently deal with different granularities of data, hence the need for suitable preprocessing.
<|end|>


