Creating a full-featured web game in the context of this text-based AI is not possible. Here is a simple version of Hangman game using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Please note that adding an animated character will be quite complex and requires advanced CSS/JavaScript skills or using a graphics library.

HTML:
```HTML
<div id="gameArea">
  <div id="wordArea">

  </div>
  <button onclick="newGame()">New Game</button>
  <p id="message"></p>
</div>
```

CSS:
```CSS
body {
  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}

#wordArea {
  font-size: 2em;
}

button {
  margin-top: 1em;
  padding: 0.5em;
}
```

JavaScript:
```JavaScript
let wordList = ['apple', 'banana', 'cherry', 'date', 'elderberry'];
let secretWord = '';
let guessedWord = '';

function newGame(){
    secretWord = wordList[Math.floor(Math.random()*wordList.length)];
    guessedWord = '_'.repeat(secretWord.length);
    document.getElementById('wordArea').textContent = guessedWord.split('').join(' ');
    document.getElementById('message').textContent = 'Guess a letter';
}

newGame();

window.onkeypress = function(event) {
  let guess = event.key;
  let newGuess = '';
  for(let i=0; i<secretWord.length; i++){
    if(secretWord[i]===guess){
      newGuess += guess;
    }else{
      newGuess += guessedWord[i];
    }
  }
  guessedWord = newGuess;
  document.getElementById('wordArea').textContent = guessedWord.split('').join(' ');
  if(guessedWord===secretWord){
    document.getElementById('message').textContent = 'Congratulations! You won!';
  }
};
```
This code will produce a simple hangman game where you click "New Game" to start, then your keyboard input is used to guess letters. Each time you guess a letter correctly, that letter will be filled in for all instances in the word, and when you complete the word, a win message will be displayed. It is quite simple and doesn't include losing condition, multiple rounds, letter repetition check, or any sort of character animations. Those could be added to make it a more complete, competitive game. For an actual game, it would be better to implement this using a frontend framework, or at least to separate your HTML, CSS, and JavaScript into different files for clarity and maintainability.
