it is related to GitHUB.
I AM UNABLE TO MAKE CHANGES AND REVERT IN GITHUB
I want to undo a whole commit with all the changes. Instead of doing all this manually , I want to know more about  how to revert a commit
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To undo a whole commit with all changes, you can use the `git revert <commit>` command, where `<commit>` is the SHA-1 hash of the commit you want to revert.

Here are the steps:

1. First, you'll need to find the commit you want to revert. You can use `git log` command to see the commit history. This will list out all the commits in descending chronological order along with their SHA-1 hashes.

2. Once you have the SHA-1 hash of the commit you want to revert, you can just use the `git revert` command. For example, if the commit hash is `a1b2c3d4`, you'll use `git revert a1b2c3d4`.

This command will create a new commit that undoes all the changes that were made in the `a1b2c3d4` commit. It's like saying "I want to go back to the way things were before this commit was made."

You'll be prompted to enter a commit message for this new revert commit. Once you save and exit the text editor, the revert commit will be made.
  
Remember: The `git revert` command will not delete any commits. Instead, it creates a new commit that undoes the changes.

3. If you want to push these changes to the remote repository, you would then use `git push origin <your_branch_name>` where `<your_branch_name>` is the name of the branch.

Please note, you should be careful while reverting commits as it may affect the codebase and other collaborators, especially if you're working on a shared repository. It's a good practice to ensure that it's okay to revert the commits or to discuss it with your team.
<|end|>


