Most statistical computing packages have functions to sample out of a two-parameter Beta distribution, but not many offer functions for sampling out of a [four-parameter Beta distribution](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beta_distribution#Four_parameters). If I want to generate samples $y$ from a four-parameter Beta distribution with lower bound $a$ and upper bound $c$ so that
$$y \sim \text{Beta}(\alpha, \beta, c, a)$$
is it sufficient to generate samples $x$ from a two-parameter Beta distribution,
$$x \sim \text{Beta}(\alpha, \beta)$$
and then transform the samples using
$$y = x(c-a) + a$$
or is there some subtlety to the sampling algorithm that I'm not seeing which would result in the samples $y$ not having the desired Beta distribution?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
No, your approach is correct and there is no hidden subtlety or problem here. The four-parameter Beta distribution is just a two-parameter Beta distribution that has been re-scaled and shifted to have arbitrary lower and upper bounds instead of being bounded between 0 and 1. 

In other words, if X follows a Beta(α, β) distribution and Y=a+(c-a)X, then Y follows a Beta(α, β, a, c) distribution. Just make sure that a < c to prevent flipping the distribution. 

One thing to note here is that the transformation used doesn't alter the overall shape of the Beta distribution, but just changes its scale and location on the real line. This is a common technique of transforming random variables to achieve a required distribution. In practical terms, this would mean that $y$ values would indeed follow the shape of a Beta distribution but stretched to the range [a, c].
<|end|>


