In the Java programming language, construct a method to count and ascertain every irregular (odd) integer located within a preallocated set range.
int commencement = 1;
int termination = 10;
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, here is a method using Java that accomplishes this task:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int commencement = 1;
        int termination = 10;
        
        countOddNumbers(commencement, termination);
    }
    
    public static void countOddNumbers(int start, int end) {
        int count = 0;
        
        for (int i = start; i <= end; i++) {
            if (i % 2 != 0) {
                System.out.println("Odd number: " + i);
                count++;
            }
        }
        
        System.out.println("Count of odd numbers: " + count);
    }
}
```

This program first defines a range from 1 to 10. It then calls a function `countOddNumbers` with this range. This function iterates over the range, and uses the modulo operator (`%`) to determine if each number is odd. If the number is odd, it increases the count and prints out the number. At the end, it prints out the total count of odd numbers.

Every number divisible by 2 yields a remainder of 0 when divided by 2. Therefore, all odd numbers will have a remainder of 1 when divided by 2, which is why odd numbers are identified with `i % 2 != 0`.
