Write a code snippet to display a website's favicon using its URL.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can use requests and beautifulsoup packages in Python to do this. Here is an example:

```python
import requests
from bs4 import BeautifulSoup
import urllib

def get_favicon(url):
    html_text = requests.get(url).text
    soup = BeautifulSoup(html_text, 'html.parser')
    
    icon_link = soup.find("link", rel="shortcut icon")
    if icon_link is None:
        icon_link = soup.find("link", rel="icon")
    if icon_link is None:
        return "No favicon found"
    
    favicon = icon_link["href"]
    if favicon.startswith('http'):
        return favicon
    else:
        return urllib.parse.urljoin(url, favicon)

print(get_favicon('https://www.your_website.com'))
```

This script searches for `<link rel="shortcut icon">` and `<link rel="icon">` in the website's html. If found, it will return the URL of the icon, and if the favicon URL is relative it would make it absolute.
This script does not cover all cases (e.g., icons referenced in a `<meta>` tag or other unusual html structures) but it should work for most common website structures.

Remember to replace `'https://www.your_website.com'` with the actual URL of the website from which you wish to fetch the favicon. Run `pip install requests beautifulsoup4 urllib3` to install the necessary libraries if you haven't done it already.
