// Points to ponder:
// * The input sequence may consist of terms in either capital or small alphabetical characters.
// 
// For instance:
// * ReverseWords([]) => []
// * ReverseWords(["abc"]) => ["cba"]
// * ReverseWords(["cat", "dog", "elephant"]) => ["tac", "god", "tnahpele"]
// * ReverseWords(["HELLO", "WORLD"]) => ["OLLEH", "DLROW"]
func ReverseWords(words []string) []string {
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a Go code implementing the requirements specified:

```Go
func ReverseWords(words []string) []string {
    reversedWords := make([]string, len(words))

    for i, word := range words {
        reversed := ""
        for _, c := range word {
            reversed = string(c) + reversed
        }
        reversedWords[i] = reversed
    }

    return reversedWords
}

func main() {
    fmt.Println(ReverseWords([]string{})) // []
    fmt.Println(ReverseWords([]string{"abc"})) // ["cba"]
    fmt.Println(ReverseWords([]string{"cat", "dog", "elephant"})) // ["tac", "god", "tnahpele"]
    fmt.Println(ReverseWords([]string{"HELLO", "WORLD"})) // ["OLLEH", "DLROW"]
}
```
In this solution, we define a function `ReverseWords()`. For each word in the input slice, it uses a simple for loop to iterate over the runes(chars) in the word, concatenating each rune at the front of the reversed string. This results in the reversed word. This reversed word is then stored in the appropriate index of the `reversedWords` slice. The function finally returns this `reversedWords` slice. 

Please note that Go strings are UTF-8 encoded by default. The loop `for _, c := range word` ranges over the Unicode code points (runes) of the word, not its bytes. This makes the function work correctly even with words containing non-ASCII characters.
