<|asst|>
Here is a PHP conversion for your Java code. PHP uses associative arrays instead of classes for storing pairs of data, so the 'Pair' class was not included in this conversion.

```PHP
class Solution
{
    /**
    * Returns encoded string by shifting every character by a random integer between 1 and 5.
    */
    public function encodeShift($s)
    {
        $result = "";
        $shifts = array();
        for ($i = 0; $i < strlen($s); $i++) {
            $shift = rand(1, 5);
            $shifts[] = $shift;
            $c = chr(ord($s[$i]) + $shift);
            $result .= $c;
        }
        return array("result" => $result, "shifts" => $shifts);
    }

    /**
    * Takes as input string encoded with encodeShift function 
    * and returns decoded string.
    */
    public function decodeShift($s, $shifts)
    {
        $result = "";
        for ($i = 0; $i < strlen($s); $i++) {
            $c = chr(ord($s[$i]) - $shifts[$i]);
            $result .= $c;
        }
        return $result;
    }
}
```

Here's how you can use this `Solution` class in PHP:

```PHP
$solution = new Solution();
$encode_result = $solution->encodeShift("Hello");
$decode_result = $solution->decodeShift($encode_result["result"], $encode_result["shifts"]);

echo "Encoded result: " . $encode_result["result"] . "\n";
echo "Decoded result: " . $decode_result . "\n";
```

This will first encode the string "Hello" by shifting its characters, then decode this back to the original string.
