I'm having a hard time filtering by patterns in AgensGraph. According to the [Cypher Query Language Reference](https://s3.amazonaws.com/artifacts.opencypher.org/openCypher9.pdf#filter-on-patterns-using-not) I should be allowed to filter on patterns using `NOT`. However, this does not seem to work in AgensGraph.

CREATE (eric:person {name:'Eric'}), (amy:person {name:'Amy'}), (peter:person {name: 'Peter'})
MERGE (amy)-[:knows]->(peter);
                   
MATCH (persons:person), (peter:person {name: 'Peter'})
WHERE NOT (persons)-[]->(peter)
RETURN persons.name


I'd expect 'Eric' as a result, but I get an error saying `Graph object cannot be jsonb`.
How would I go about finding all people who don't know Peter using AgensGraph?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The error you're encountering appears to be related to a specific way AgensGraph processes properties of graph elements, causing `NOT` filtering of relationships to not work as expected−especially when you want to JSONify (`jsonb`) a graph path object.

Many graph database engines, including AgensGraph, do not yet fully support all features of Cypher language including filtering on patterns using `NOT`.

However, you can work around this by using a different approach to find people who are not connected to 'Peter'. An alternative approach might be using MINUS operator or a subquery.

```sql
MATCH (persons:person)
WITH collect(persons) as all_persons
MATCH (peter:person {name: 'Peter'})-[]->(receivers)
WITH all_persons, collect(receivers) as peter_knows
UNWIND all_persons as person
WHERE NOT person IN peter_knows
RETURN person.name
```

This query takes all persons, collects them as one set and then finds those who are on the receiving end of a 'knows' relation from 'Peter'. Then it unwinds the first set and checks if each person is not present in the 'Peter' set by applying `NOT IN` operator.

Please note that Cypher queries vary in compatibility across different graph databases due to different interpretations and implementations of Cypher standard provided by each database vendor. Always check your specific database vendor's Cypher documentation or guide for queries and their usage.
<|end|>


