<Modal>
    <Modal.Footer>
        // any custom content. User can completely control behavior of this content. 
    </Modal.Footer>
</Modal>

export const ModalFooter = ({children}) => {
     return <div className={'modal-footer'}>{children}</div>
}

.modal-footer {
    // css to make footer sticky or scrollable. 
}


The scenario is that the footer of the Modal is going to be an optional feature. Only if the person sends <Modal.Footer/>, will that appear. So I want to create <Modal.Footer> inside the modal component.
Can someone please tell me how to achieve that?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can create a compound component. A compound component gives more rendering control to the user of your component. By using the special React.Children.map, you can inject props into children or decide what to render. It's a common solution for complex components with a lot of optional parts.

```JavaScript
const Modal = ({ children }) => {
  let footer = null;

  const childrenWithFooter = React.Children.map(children, child => {
    if (child.type === Modal.Footer) {
      footer = child;
      return null;
    }

    return child;
  });

  return (
    <div className="modal">
      <div className="modal-content">{childrenWithFooter}</div>
      {footer && <FooterContent />}
    </div>
  );
};

Modal.Footer = ({children}) => {
  return <div className={'modal-footer'}>{children}</div>
};
```

The main idea is to loop through the children of the `Modal` component, check if the child is a `Modal.Footer` component or not. If it is, you store it aside and do not render it directly inside the modal's content. Instead, you put it into the footer area.

Now you can use it like this:

```JavaScript
<Modal>
  Any Custom content inside the modal.
  <Modal.Footer>Footer Content</Modal.Footer>
</Modal>
```
As result, "Footer Content" will be output inside the div with 'modal-footer' class, and "Any Custom content inside the modal." will be inside the 'modal-content'.
<|end|>


