Oliver is building a wooden fence around his garden. The remaining planks he needs to put up is 2/5 of the total planks he has already fixed. After fixing another 30 planks, the remaining planks are only 1/3 of the planks he has fixed. How many total planks does this fence have?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Let x be the total number of planks.
The number of remaining planks was 2x/5 after Oliver fixed x/5*3 planks.
So, 2x/5 - 30 = (x/5*3 + 30) / 3
By multiplying the equation by 15 we get 6x - 450 = 3x + 150
Then we get 3x = 600
So, the total number of planks is x = 600 / 3 = <<600/3=200>>200. Answer: \boxed{200}.
<|end|>


