Assuming the table name is `employees` and the column that stores emails is `email`, you can use the SUBSTRING_INDEX function to extract the domain part of the email. Here is a basic SQL query demonstrating this:

```sql
SELECT DISTINCT 
SUBSTRING_INDEX(email, '@', -1) as email_domain
FROM employees;
```

The function `SUBSTRING_INDEX` in MySQL splits a string at the specified delimiter (in this case, '@'), and returns the part after the delimiter when the index is `-1`. `DISTINCT` keyword ensures that we only list unique domains.
