Yes, WebStorm has a built-in feature for code live reload which can be used as an alternative to the VS Code Live Server extension. This built-in web server allows you to run your client-side JavaScript and Node.js apps right from the IDE.

Here's how you can use it:

1. Open the built-in web server settings by navigating to `File | Settings | Build, Execution, Deployment | Debugger`

2. In the `Built-in Server` section, you'll see the port number of the built-in server, and can decide whether it should be accessible externally.

3. Now open the HTML file which you want to auto-reload in the browser after changes. Right click on it and choose `Open in Browser`.

4. While saving changes in IDE, the browser tab will be automatically refreshed.

Also, WebStorm integrates with the `Live Edit` feature for instant updates of your HTML, CSS, and JavaScript in the browser without reloading, but this requires installing a JetBrains browser extension. While Live Edit is a bit more complex and sophisticated, it also provides an option similar to Live Server.

For more information, check the detailed guide provided in the WebStorm documentation.
