Construct a Python set that combines three dictionaries, maintaining their unique entries, and sort the resulting set alphabetically. The program should also manage case sensitivity, meaning it should be able from distinguishing between upper and lower case letters.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This problem can be solved in multiple ways in Python. We can utilize the update() method of Python sets to add elements of dictionaries and use the sorted() function to sort the final set alphabetically. Here I am presenting a Python 3 solution:

```python
# define the dictionaries
dict1 = {'a': 1, 'b': 2, 'c': 3}
dict2 = {'d': 4, 'e': 5, 'f': 6}
dict3 = {'g': 7, 'h': 8, 'i': 9}

# combine all dictionaries
items = set()

# get the keys from each dictionary and add to items
for d in [dict1, dict2, dict3]:
    for k in d.keys():
        items.add(k)

# sort alphabetically
sorted_items = sorted(items)

# print the sorted items
for item in sorted_items:
    print(item)
```
The above code will print the alphabetically sorted keys of all the three dictionaries. If any of the dictionaries share a key, it will be listed only once in the sorted_items list, thanks to the property of the set that eliminates duplicate items.

If you want to make this code work for case-sensitive keys, you need to ensure that keys in the dictionaries are saved in the proper case, as Python distinguishes case inherently.

For instance, 'a' and 'A' are treated as different keys in a Python dictionary due to case sensitivity. Assume dict1 is now `{'a': 1, 'A': 1}`, both 'a' and 'A' will be added to the set.
<|end|>


