Develop an encryption utility in the Java programming language, specifically applying the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) protocol. This method should process a text string input for encryption, ensuring encoded text integrity.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a Java implementation for your requirement. This program uses AES for text encryption and decryption.

```java
import javax.crypto.Cipher;
import javax.crypto.KeyGenerator;
import javax.crypto.SecretKey;
import javax.crypto.spec.SecretKeySpec;
import java.util.Base64;

public class AESUtility {
    private static final String ALGORITHM = "AES";

    public SecretKey generateKey() throws Exception {
        KeyGenerator keyGenerator = KeyGenerator.getInstance(ALGORITHM);
        keyGenerator.init(128); // AES uses 128 bit keys
        SecretKey secretKey = keyGenerator.generateKey();
        return secretKey;
    }

    public String encrypt(String plainText, SecretKey secretKey) throws Exception {
        byte[] plainTextByte = plainText.getBytes();
        Cipher cipher = Cipher.getInstance(ALGORITHM);
        cipher.init(Cipher.ENCRYPT_MODE, secretKey);
        byte[] encryptedByte = cipher.doFinal(plainTextByte);
        Base64.Encoder encoder = Base64.getEncoder();
        String encryptedText = encoder.encodeToString(encryptedByte);
        return encryptedText;
    }

    public String decrypt(String encryptedText, SecretKey secretKey) throws Exception {
        Base64.Decoder decoder = Base64.getDecoder();
        byte[] encryptedTextByte = decoder.decode(encryptedText);
        Cipher cipher = Cipher.getInstance(ALGORITHM);
        cipher.init(Cipher.DECRYPT_MODE, secretKey);
        byte[] decryptedByte = cipher.doFinal(encryptedTextByte);
        String decryptedText = new String(decryptedByte);
        return decryptedText;
    }

    // Using the utility
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        AESUtility aesUtility = new AESUtility();
        SecretKey secretKey = aesUtility.generateKey();
        String plainText = "HERE IS SOME TEXT TO BE ENCRYPTED";
        String encryptedText = aesUtility.encrypt(plainText, secretKey);
        String decryptedText = aesUtility.decrypt(encryptedText, secretKey);

        System.out.println("Original Text : " + plainText);
        System.out.println("Encrypted Text : " + encryptedText);
        System.out.println("Decrypted Text : " + decryptedText);
    }
}
```

Please keep in mind that this code only works in a "happy path" and doesn't do any sort of error correction or exception handling. This is an example of AES encryption. 

Please note, when distributing encryption utilities or using them in production, you should also take care to secure the key itself - ideally, keys should not be hard-coded but instead securely stored and managed, and you should only provide the minimal required access to them. 

Also, this code does not implement any sort of padding, so if your input data doesn't neatly fit into AES's block size, you might run into issues. 

You may consider using AES/CBC/PKCS5Padding instead of AES which would add proper padding to your data.
<|end|>


