Write a JavaScript code to organize not just one, but two arrays of student objects in alphabetical order based on their last names and then merge those arrays. Handle any duplicate student entries between the two arrays and make sure to remove them in your final array. Also, assume that there could be instances where 'lastName' and/or 'firstName' might not be provided or could be null.

Given two arrays of student objects such as below:

Array 1:
[{firstName: 'Emily', lastName: 'Smith'},
{firstName: 'Michael', lastName: 'Johnson'},
{firstName: 'Sophia', lastName: 'Williams'}]

Array 2:
[{firstName: 'Jacob', lastName: 'Johnson'},
{firstName: 'Mia', lastName: 'Taylor'},
{firstName: 'Michael', lastName: 'Johnson'},
{firstName: 'James'},
{firstName: '', lastName: 'Brown'}]

For your reference, here is a piece of erroneous code which fails to handle missing name and duplicate entries:
```javascript
function mergeAndSortStudents(arr1, arr2) {
  var mergedArray = [...arr1, ...arr2];
  mergedArray.sort((a, b) => a.lastName > b.lastName ? 1 : -1);
  return mergedArray;
}
```
Use this as a starting point, find out the issues and extend it to meet the updated requirements.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can use JavaScript's Set object along with JSON.stringify and JSON.parse methods to help with removing duplicate student entries. For handling missing name, you can provide a default value (like an empty string) to the sort function.

Here's how you could update the mergeAndSortStudents function:

```javascript
function mergeAndSortStudents(arr1, arr2) {
  var mergedArray = [...arr1, ...arr2];

  // removing duplicates
  mergedArray = Array.from(new Set(mergedArray.map(JSON.stringify))).map(JSON.parse);

  // sorting
  mergedArray.sort((a, b) => {
    let lastNameA = a.lastName || "";
    let lastNameB = b.lastName || "";

    return lastNameA > lastNameB ? 1 : -1;
  });

  return mergedArray;
}
```

Note: JSON.stringify and JSON.parse usage in this context -- we start by transforming each item of the array to a JSON string (JSON.stringify), create a Set from those strings (because in JavaScript a Set is a collection of distinct objects), and then transform each string back to an object (JSON.parse).

Remember, this only works when objects in the array are identical, so if you have two different students with the same name, they will both show up in the final array. If the objects contain more properties than just firstName and lastName, those properties will also be considered in the duplication check.

This approach also assumes that if 'lastName' is missing or null, you would sort it like an empty string.

This is arguably not a perfect solution, but it should work fine for simple scenarios.

You might want to come up with a custom solution for your specific requirements if they are more complex.
<|end|>


