Construct an object in JavaScript where the key is an integer from 1 to 20. There should be two values for each key: one is a boolean representing if it is an even number or not and another one is a string that returns "prime" if the number is prime and "not prime" if the number is not. Include error handling to account for potential misuse.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is the JavaScript code for the problem statement:

```javascript
function isPrime(num) {
    if(num < 2) return false;
    for(let i = 2; i < num; i++) 
        if(num % i === 0) return false;
    return true;
}

function constructObject(){
    let obj = {};
    for(let i = 1; i <= 20; i++){
        obj[i] = {
            isEven: i % 2 === 0,
            prime: isPrime(i) ? 'prime' : 'not prime'
        }
    }
    return obj;
}

try{
    console.log(constructObject());
} catch(error) {
    console.error("An error occurred: ", error);
}
```

This code first defines a helper function isPrime() to determine if a number is prime. Inside constructObject(), we loop from 1 to 20 creating a new object for each integer. We add two properties to the object: isEven and prime. If the number is divisible by 2, then isEven property is true. If the number is prime (as determined by the isPrime() function), then prime property is set to "prime", otherwise it is set to "not prime". The resulting object is then returned. We wrap the function call within a try-catch statement to handle any potential errors.

Note: This code may not correctly identify all prime numbers in the general case as isPrime() function checks divisibility from 2 to the given number which may result in unnecessary computations as it is sufficient to check divisibility until square root of the given number. However, given the problem constraints that the numbers are from 1 to 20, this should work correctly.
