<|asst|>
Here is a simple implementation in Python, Java, and C++.

Python does not have a native switch-case construct, so we can make use of if-elif-else.

Python:
```Python
def switch_case(x):
    if x > 0:
        print("x is a positive integer")
    elif x < 0:
        print("x is a negative integer")
    else:
        print("x is zero")


switch_case(10)  # Test with a positive integer
switch_case(-10)  # Test with a negative integer
switch_case(0)  # Test with zero
```

Java:
```Java
public class SwitchCase {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int x = 0;  // Change value of x as you wish

        if (x > 0) {
            System.out.println("x is a positive integer");
        } else if (x < 0) {
            System.out.println("x is a negative integer");
        } else {
            System.out.println("x is zero");
        }
    }
}
```

C++:
```C++
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int x = 0; // Change value of x as you wish

    if(x > 0) {
        cout << "x is a positive integer" << endl;
    } else if(x < 0) {
        cout << "x is a negative integer" << endl;
    } else {
        cout << "x is zero" << endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In the codes above, you can change the value of x to test the conditions. 

NOTE: Switch-case in traditional sense is used for discrete values matching (like int 1, int 2, ...) not for range matching(like int > 0). In order to use switch case for range matching, you will need to bin your data into discrete bins i.e (0-10 bin, 10-20 bin, ...) and use the bins as case statement. The provided solution is more elegant and correct for handling cases of positive, negative and zero.
<|end|>


