Yes, you can delete the HBITMAP immediately after creating the HBRUSH. The HBRUSH does indeed store its own copy of the HBITMAP, so even if you delete the original HBITMAP, the HBRUSH will still function as expected.

If you want to keep clean scope boundaries that match the lifetime requirements for each handle (HBITMAP or HBRUSH), you can do as following:

```cpp
HBRUSH CreatePatternBrushAndDeleteBitmap(HBITMAP hBitmap)
{
    HBRUSH hBrush = CreatePatternBrush(hBitmap);
    DeleteObject(hBitmap); // Safe to delete the bitmap after creating the brush
    return hBrush;
}
```

In this way, you are enforcing that the HBITMAP's scope (and thus its lifetime) is only within the function that creates the HBRUSH. Once you get the HBRUSH, the HBITMAP is already deleted, enforcing the rule that these two resources have different lifetimes.
