<|user|>
I frequently come across Windows programs that bundle in MSVCRT (or their more current equivalents) with the program executables.  On a typical PC, I would find many copies of the same .DLL's.   My understanding is that MSVCRT is the C runtime library, somewhat analogous to glibc/libc.so under *nix.
Why do Windows programs have to bring along their C libraries with them, instead of just sharing the system-wide libc?

---


Update: thanks to Shog9, I started to read about SxS, which has further opened up my eyes to the DLL linkage issues (DLL Hell) - [Link](https://web.archive.org/web/20160305220537/http://blogs.msdn.com/b/martynl/archive/2005/10/13/480880.aspx) is one useful intro to the issue...
<|end|>
<|asst|>
There are a few reasons why Windows programs often include their own copies of libraries, including MSVCRT:

1. **Binary Compatibility**: Unlike Linux distributions, which can recompile their entire repositories of software whenever libc changes, Windows needs to maintain binary compatibility across updates. Therefore, older programs need to have access to the old versions of libraries.

2. **Avoiding DLL Hell**: Including local copies of libraries prevents "DLL Hell," where different programs overwrite each other's shared libraries, leading to unpredictable bugs and crashes. It's a serious problem specifically with Windows since its library search order includes directories in the PATH before the system directories.

3. **Library Versioning**: The program knows exactly which version of the library it was compiled against and can confirm it's using that version by including it. 

4. **Isolation from System Problems**: It makes the program immune to certain types of system-wide issues - for instance, if the system-wide library becomes corrupted, the program will still work properly.

5. **Deployment Flexibility**: It allows the program to be used on systems that do not have the required version of the library installed. 

Overall, bundling libraries with the program is one way to ensure that the program runs the same way it did during testing, regardless of the specific configuration or state of the computer it's installed on.
<|end|>


