I will provide a basic solution with HTML form and JavaScript for client-side validation. Note that the design is simple and the form handling part has to be implemented on the server-side.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<style>
input.ng-invalid {
  border: 2px solid red;
}
</style>
<script>
function validateForm() {
  var form = document.forms["registerForm"];
  var username = form["username"].value;
  var password = form["password"].value;
  var email = form["email"].value;

  var valid = true;

  // Check Username is not empty
  if(username === "") {
    valid = false;
    form["username"].className = "ng-invalid";
  } else {
    form["username"].className = "";
  }

  // Check Password is not empty
  if(password === "" || password.length < 8) {
    valid = false;
    form["password"].className = "ng-invalid";
  } else {
    form["password"].className = "";
  }

  // Check Email is not empty
  var re = /^[\w-]+(\.[\w-]+)*@([\w-]+\.)+[a-zA-Z]{2,7}$/;
  if(email === "" || !re.test(email)) {
    valid = false;
    form["email"].className = "ng-invalid";
  } else {
    form["email"].className = "";
  }

  return valid;
}
</script>
</head>
<body>
<h2>User Registration</h2>
<form name="registerForm" action="/submit_registration" onsubmit="return validateForm()" method="post">
  <label for="username">Username:</label><br>
  <input type="text" id="username" name="username"><br>
  <label for="password">Password:</label><br>
  <input type="password" id="password" name="password" minlength="8" required><br>
  <label for="email">Email:</label><br>
  <input type="text" id="email" name="email"><br>
  <input type="submit" value="Register">
</form> 

</body>
</html>
```

This is a basic form with username, password, and email fields. We set a `onsubmit` event on the form that calls the `validateForm` function. This function checks each of the fields and adds a "ng-invalid" class to them if they do not meet the specific criteria. The style for "ng-invalid" is defined in the `<head>` and simply changes the form border to red. The validations performed are:

1. Username and password: Must not be empty. Password must be at least 8 characters.
2. Email: Must not be empty and must match regex for simple email validation.

To handle form submission and store user data appropriately, server-side programming is needed which depends on your back-end technology choice (like Node.js, PHP, .NET, Java, etc.).
<|end|>


