I have a Rails 2.0.2 application running with a postgresql db. The machine will receive data on a TCP port. I already have coded a working ruby multithreaded tcp server to receive the requests, but I need this code to run alongside my Rails app.

So I guess I need to know how to span a new process inside Rails, or how to create a worker thread that will run my threaded tcp server loop. My ruby tcp server could have access to ActiveRecord, but it's not necessary (I can always create an http request, posting the received data to the original Rails server)
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You should not run the threaded TCP server inside the same process as Rails. Rails isn't inherently designed to work well in a multi-threaded environment, especially if your Rails version is not recent. 

The best approach would be to keep the threaded TCP server in a separate process which can connect to the PostgreSQL DB or make HTTP requests to the Rails app. Here are some solutions:

1. **Running TCP server as a Daemon:** You can daemonize your already working Ruby TCP server. Once it's been turned into a daemon, you can run it on the server side by side your Rails application. For daemonizing you can use ruby gem daemonize. Take a look at the guide here: https://www.sitepoint.com/daemon-processes-in-ruby/

2. **Implementing a Message Queue:** Alternatively, you could use a message queue system to allow your Rails app and your TCP server app to talk to each other asynchronously. ActiveMQ, RabbitMQ, and ZeroMQ are all common choices for this in Ruby.

Remember, if your TCP server is going to handle heavy traffic or perform CPU-intensive tasks, it is better to run it on a separate machine than your web server, to avoid slowing down your Rails application.

If you want to access ActiveRecord from your TCP server, there are a few options:

1) **Extract out the model logic to a gem:** This is beneficial if there would be heavy reuse between the TCP server and the Rails app.

2) **Make HTTP calls:** As you suggested, the TCP server could make HTTP requests to your Rails server. This would be a more microservices style architecture.

3) **Shared Database Access:** Or you could just use ActiveRecord as normal within your TCP server and connect to the same database as your Rails app. Just beware of potential concurrency issues! Especially with older version of Rails.
  
Remember, to run and manage separate application processes such as your Rails application and the TCP server, it's useful to use a process monitor and control tool like systemd, runit, or foreman.
<|end|>


