Compose an SQL query, which allocates all registered clients who correspond to a particular designation of an electronic email address denoted as "test@example.com", and also incorporate in that query to order them by their last name in ascending order while excluding those who haven't made any transactions during this fiscal year.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, without concrete knowledge of your database structure, I will assume you have a clients table and a transactions table. Your clients table contains columns for clients' last names and email addresses, and the transactions table records all transactions made by the clients within this fiscal year. Here is a sample SQL query using MySQL:

```SQL
SELECT 
  clients.* 
FROM 
  clients 
INNER JOIN 
  transactions 
ON 
  clients.client_id = transactions.client_id 
WHERE 
  clients.email = 'test@example.com' 
AND 
  YEAR(transactions.date) = YEAR(CURDATE())
ORDER BY 
  clients.last_name ASC;
```

This SQL query works as follows:

- `SELECT clients.*` gets all the columns from the clients table.
- `FROM clients` specifies the clients table as the one we want to get data from.
- `INNER JOIN transactions ON clients.client_id = transactions.client_id` joins the clients and transactions tables where the client_id column matches. 
- `WHERE clients.email = 'test@example.com'` specifies that we only want clients who have an email of "test@example.com".
- `AND YEAR(transactions.date) = YEAR(CURDATE())` further narrows down our selection to only those clients who have made transactions this year.
- `ORDER BY clients.last_name ASC` finally orders the selected clients by their last name in ascending order.

Please replace 'client_id', 'email', 'last_name', 'date' with the actual column names in your database. Also, adjust the name of 'clients' and 'transactions' tables if they are named differently in your setup.
