<|asst|>
Below is a simple implementation of an HTML form with JavaScript validation.

```HTML
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
    <head>
        <title>User Details Form</title>
        <script type="text/javascript">
            function validateForm(){
                var firstName = document.forms["UserDetails"]["fname"].value;
                var lastName = document.forms["UserDetails"]["lname"].value;
                var dob = document.forms["UserDetails"]["dob"].value;
                var gender = document.forms["UserDetails"]["gend"].value;
                var email = document.forms["UserDetails"]["email"].value;
                var phone = document.forms["UserDetails"]["tel"].value;

                if(firstName == "" || lastName == "" || dob == "" || gender == "" || email == "" || phone == ""){
                    alert("All fields must be filled out");
                    return false;
                }
                else if(!/^(\d{3})[-. ]?(\d{3})[-. ]?(\d{4})$/.test(phone)){
                    alert("Invalid phone number");
                    return false;
                }
                else if(!/^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(email)){
                    alert("Invalid email address");
                    return false;
                }
            }
        </script>
    </head>
    <body>
        <form name="UserDetails" onsubmit="return validateForm()">
            First Name: <input type="text" name="fname"><br>
            Last Name: <input type="text" name="lname"><br>
            Date of Birth: <input type="date" name="dob"><br>
            Gender: <input type="text" name="gend"><br>
            Email: <input type="email" name="email"><br>
            Phone Number: <input type="tel" name="tel"><br>
            <input type="submit" value="Submit">
        </form>
    </body>
</html>
```

Here's a basic rundown of the JavaScript function `validateForm()`: 

- It starts by fetching the value of each field in the form.
- It then checks if any fields are empty. If they are, an alert is shown, and form submission is stopped (via `return false`). 
- The phone number field is validated using a regular expression to match commonly accepted phone number formats. If the phone number doesn't match this format, an alert is shown and form submission is stopped.
- The email field is validated by another regular expression. This checks if the email is in the format sometext@sometext.sometext, which is the standard email address format. If the email doesn't match the regular expression, an alert is shown and form submission is stopped.

Please note, in a real word application, client side validation is not enough, always implement server side validations to avoid security risks.
