Develop a JavaScript function to validate a phone number.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a simple JavaScript function to validate a US phone number. In this function, it's considering that valid phone numbers are in the format of (123) 456-7890.

```javascript
function validatePhoneNumber(phoneNumber) {
    let regex = /^\(?([0-9]{3})\)?[-. ]?([0-9]{3})[-. ]?([0-9]{4})$/;
    if (regex.test(phoneNumber)) {
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}

// Test the function
console.log(validatePhoneNumber("(123) 456-7890"));  // returns true
console.log(validatePhoneNumber("123 456 7890")); // returns true
console.log(validatePhoneNumber("123.456.7890")); // returns true
console.log(validatePhoneNumber("1234567890")); // returns true
console.log(validatePhoneNumber("123-456-7890")); // returns true
console.log(validatePhoneNumber("12345-67890"));  // returns false
console.log(validatePhoneNumber("phone number"));  // returns false
```
This function uses a regular expression (regex) to check the phone number's structure. In the regex:

- `^` asserts the start of a line
- `\(?([0-9]{3})\)?` checks for zero or one open parenthesis, three digits, and then zero or one close parenthesis
- `[-. ]?` checks for zero or one of either a hyphen, period, or space
- `([0-9]{3})` checks for three digits
- `[-. ]?` is the same as above
- `([0-9]{4})` checks for four digits
- `$` asserts the end of a line 

The function `test()` is then used to test the phone number input against the above regular expression, if it follows the pattern, it returns true else false.
