I want to construct a zero-curve from EUR OIS rates (IR swaps with ESTR as the floating rate).


For bootstrapping, some of the contracts I use have maturities of 15, 18 and 21 months (RIC: EUREST15M= etc.). How many payments do these contracts have, and when do they occur?


I found this similar thread, but can't find anything for the EUR market: [Is an 18 month OIS a bullet?](https://quant.stackexchange.com/questions/68942/is-an-18-month-ois-a-bullet)
<|end|>
<|asst|>
For the Euro (EUR) market, Overnight Index Swap (OIS) contracts typically have a maturity shorter than or equal to 12 months. These OIS contracts with less than or equal to 12 months maturity usually involve two payments: one at the start (floating rate payer pays fixed-rate payer) and one at the end (fixed-rate payer pays floating rate payer).

However, for longer maturities beyond 12 months, these contracts are structured differently. They involve multiple floating payments usually occurring every 12 months, while the fixed payment still only occurs at the end. This reflects the annual compounding frequency typical for the EUR OIS market.

So, for your OIS contracts with maturities of 15, 18 and 21 months:

- The 15-month contract would involve 2 floating rate payments: one at 12 months and one at the end (15 months).

- The 18-month contract would involve 2 floating rate payments: one at 12 months and one at the end (18 months).

- The 21-month contract would involve 3 floating rate payments: one at 12 months, one at 24 months (which would be interpolated), and one at the end (21 months).

Each contract would make a fixed payment at its respective end date. 

Remember that these are general conventions - the actual payment schedule can be subject to specific contractual terms and conditions, and could be tailored differently in bespoke contracts. I suggest confirming the schedule from the contract details if available. It is also essential to note these are primarily based on conventions in the interbank market; for non-interbank counterparties, they could be different.
<|end|>


