Craft a Python script, utilizing the complexities of beautifulsoup4 and requests modules, designed to scrape the content of an HTML page, delving further into extracting the hyperlinks embedded within.
url = "http://example.com"
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is a Python script using the requests and BeautifulSoup4 modules. The script sends a GET request to the specified URL and uses BeautifulSoup4 to parse the HTML content. The script then finds all 'a' tags (which denotes hyperlinks), and extracts the 'href' attribute, which is the actual URL of the link.

```python
import requests
from bs4 import BeautifulSoup

# The URL to scrape
url = 'http://example.com'

# Sending a GET request
res = requests.get(url)
# Parsing the HTML content
soup = BeautifulSoup(res.content, 'html.parser')

# Finding all 'a' tags and extracting the 'href' attribute
hyperlinks = [a['href'] for a in soup.find_all('a', href=True)]

# Printing out the hyperlinks
for link in hyperlinks:
    print(link)
```

Note: Before running the script, make sure to install the required modules using pip:
```bash
pip install requests beautifulsoup4
```
Also, this script may return relative urls if the website has relative urls. If required, you may need to join these relative urls with the base url to get absolute urls.
<|end|>


