Create a Java class known as Student with five private attributes: the forename, the surname, years of existence, grade point average (GPA), and a List of course objects that the student is enrolled into. It should also include a method to calculate the average grade of the student based on the List of courses. The class should include both the default and parameterized constructors, accessor and mutator methods for these identified attributes. Your class should implement the Comparable interface to compare the numerical ages and the GPAs of different Student instances.

Furthermore, supplement an erroneous code snippet utilizing this class, exemplifying at least three important mistakes that a prospective test taker must identify and correct. This code snippet should also require the test taker to implement exception handling for scenarios involving illegal access to array elements or attempting to calculate the average grade when no courses are assigned to the student. These mistakes may include, but are not limited to, object misdeclaration, inappropriate assigning of variable values, issues regarding data visibility, improper method operations, and violations of exception handling protocols.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's the Student class described above in Java:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Student implements Comparable<Student> {
    private String forename;
    private String surname;
    private int years;
    private float gpa;
    private List<Course> courses;

    public Student() {
        this.courses = new ArrayList<>();
    }

    public Student(String forename, String surname, int years, float gpa, List<Course> courses) {
        this.forename = forename;
        this.surname = surname;
        this.years = years;
        this.gpa = gpa;
        this.courses = courses;
    }

    // Accessor and Mutator Methods
    // getters and setters for forename, surname, years, gpa, courses

    public float calculateAverageGrade() {
        if (courses.isEmpty()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("No courses assigned to student");
        }
        float total = 0;
        for (Course c : courses) {
            total += c.getGrade();
        }
        return total / courses.size();
    }

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Student other) {
        int ageDifference = this.years - other.years;
        if (ageDifference != 0) {
            return ageDifference;
        }
        return Float.compare(this.gpa, other.gpa);
    }
}
```

Here's a code snippet utilizing this class with intentional mistakes:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Mistake 1: Initializing Student object with wrong parameters
        Student student = new Student("John", "Doe", 3.8, 20, null); 

        // Mistake 2: Trying to get average grade when no courses assigned
        System.out.println(student.calculateAverageGrade()); 

        // Mistake 3: Trying to access invisible attribute directly
        System.out.println(student.forename); 
    }
}
```

Here's the corrected code:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Corrected: Initializing Student object with correct parameters
        Student student = new Student("John", "Doe", 20, 3.8f, new ArrayList<Course>()); 

        try {
            // Corrected: Putting calculateAverageGrade method inside a try-catch block
            System.out.println(student.calculateAverageGrade()); 
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            System.out.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        // Corrected: Accessing attribute via getter method
        System.out.println(student.getForename()); 
    }
}
```
Your Course class must implement a getGrade method for this to work. 

