Write a function in JavaScript that not only selects all HTML elements with the "input" tag and type of "text" from an HTML document, but also needs to check for the existence of a specific attribute in each of these elements. Additionally, the function should also count the number of these specific input elements and return that count. You could also add event listeners to these elements to listen for an "onChange" event. Remember to take into account potential cross-browser compatibility issues when writing your code.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a JavaScript function which accomplishes that:

```javascript
function countTextInputElements(attributeName) {
    // Select all 'input' elements with type = 'text'
    let textInputs = document.querySelectorAll('input[type="text"]');
    
    let count = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < textInputs.length; i++) {
        // Check if the element has the specified attribute
        if (textInputs[i].hasAttribute(attributeName)) {
            count++;

            // Add an 'onChange' event listener to element
            if (textInputs[i].addEventListener) {  // Standard browsers
                textInputs[i].addEventListener('change', function() {
                    // Handle change event
                    console.log("Change event detected in element: " + i);
                });
            } else if (textInputs[i].attachEvent) {  // IE
                textInputs[i].attachEvent('onchange', function() {
                    // Handle change event
                    console.log("Change event detected in element: " + i);
                });
            }
        }
    }

    return count;
}
```

This function first selects all `input` elements of type `text` on the webpage. It loops through all these elements and checks for the existence of a specific attribute (specified by `attributeName`). If this attribute exists, it increments a counter and adds an `onChange` event listener to the element. The event listener logs a message to the console when a change event is detected. The function finally returns the count of input elements with the specific attribute. 

Cross-browser compatibility is ensured by checking for both `addEventListener` and `attachEvent` methods when adding the event listener. The `addEventListener` method is used by standard browsers, while the `attachEvent` method was used by older versions of Internet Explorer.
<|end|>


