I know for two mutually exclusive classes softmax is the best activation function in the output layer. However, somehow (2, softmax) and even (1,sigmoid) are giving average results and (2, sigmoid) as output layer is giving excellent results (in terms of validation and test set accuracy).
But, as per my understanding, using (2, sigmoid) in the output layer for mutually exclusive classes is not logically correct. I don't understand why in my case (2,sigmoid) is giving better results than (2, softmax). Have any one encountered similar results?
Also, an interesting observation is that my two mutually exclusive classes are very complex. i.e., although they are mutually exclusive but its very difficult to put them in any of the classes in the training data and there will be some instances where it could have been any of the two. Would (2,sigmoid) be justified given the complexity of the classes ?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
There may be several reasons why a sigmoid function works better than a softmax function in this case.

The main difference between the two is that softmax guarantees that the output probabilities will sum to 1, thus it is suitable for multiclass classification problems where the classes are mutually exclusive. Sigmoid, on the other hand, treats each output node independently, allowing for the possibility of multiple classes to be "active."

Even though your classes are mutually exclusive, your model might be finding some underlying structure in the data where the boundaries between classes are not that defined. In this case, a sigmoid function would allow the model to output a high probability for both classes, thus capturing that uncertainty.

Furthermore, the softmax function tends to make very confident predictions, because it amplifies differences between input values. This may make it more sensitive to noise or outliers in the data, leading to poorer performance.

Finally, it's also possible that the improvement you're seeing with the sigmoid function is due to random variation or the specific way you're training your model. It would be worth trying different configurations or tuning your model's hyperparameters to see if you can get better results with softmax.

Given the complexity of your classes and the uncertainty in class assignments, the use of sigmoid might be justified as it allows for the possibility of multiple activations thus capturing the complex structure better.
<|end|>


