Here's a typical way to do this:

First, install a HTML formatter like `tidy`. You can typically use your package manager to install it. For example, on Ubuntu you could run: `sudo apt-get install tidy`

Then, in your Emacs, select the region of HTML you want to format. Then run shell command on region (`M-|`), and input `tidy -q -indent -omit`. It will replace the selected region with the formatted output from `tidy`.

Here `-q` prevents `Tidy` from outputting non-error messages, `-indent` causes Tidy to pretty-print the output, and `-omit` tells Tidy to leave out optional end-tags.

Remember, the specific `tidy` options might vary depending on your requirements.

There also might be Emacs packages available for beautifying HTML, but using an external tool like `tidy` has the advantage of being able to use the same tool across different editors or even in scripts.
<|end|>


