In such a case, you will require a system-level approach with deep visibility into your system's operations. Here are some solutions you can employ:

1. Use System Call Monitoring Software - This type of software will allow you to record, analyze, and understand all system calls made within your system. The program intercepts any system calls, logs them, and then passes them on to the operating system. You can analyze the logs to identify any unusual patterns that might signify unauthorized activity.

2. Implement an Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR) platform - EDR platforms provide real-time monitoring and response to any potential threats to your system. They detect and investigate suspicious activities on a system's endpoints and can help stop any potential threats. 

3. Use Windows Sysinternals Suite - If you are using a Windows system, the Windows Sysinternals suite will be beneficial. It contains multiple utilities that will let you monitor and control processes running on your system.

4. Apply Kernel-level Security - This approach encompasses using the built-in security mechanisms found in the kernel of your operating system. This approach is more involving but very effective.

However, dealing with each Registry or Filesystem call individually can impact system performance. Ideally, you should have a well-designed heuristic algorithm that can decide which calls to delay or decline based on predefined criteria.

Please note that blocking these calls may lead to unwanted side effects like system instability or software malfunctions. 

Lastly, consider if the system is sandboxed or virtualized, and ensure any tools or methods account for that level of complexity.
