I'm a bit confused here.  Microsoft as far as I can tell claims that parametrization is the best way to protect your database from SQL injection attacks.  But I find two conflicting sources of information here:

[This page](http://support.microsoft.com/kb/200190) says to use the ADO command object.  But [this page](http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/ms679836(VS.85).aspx) says that the command object isn't safe for scripting.  I seem to recall reading somewhere that the command object shouldn't be used in VBScript or JScript because of security vulnerabilities, but I can't seem to find that article.

Am I missing something here, or do those two articles seem to contradict each other?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
These two articles are addressing different areas of development, and they do not necessarily contradict each other. 

The first article focuses on how to guard against SQL injection attacks when communicating with a database. It recommends using parameters with SQL queries and the ADO Command object to mitigate the risk of SQL injection. When you use parameters, the data provided by a user cannot be interpreted as SQL code, your application is more secure.

The second article focuses primarily on client-side scripting, specifically VBScript or JScript within a web page, which does not necessarily involve direct communication with a database. The main point here is to caution developers of the security risks of running scripts (like VBScript or JScript) within a web page, due to their potential for misuse by attackers.

It's also worth noting that even though the ADO Command object isn't safe for client-side scripting, it doesn't mean it's not safe for any scripting. In server-side scripts, where you have control over the environment and the safeguards in place, you can use the ADO Command object to avoid SQL injection attacks.

So, the key takeaways are: use parametrization to protect your database from SQL injection, and be careful when enabling scripting on your web pages to avoid other types of attacks.
<|end|>


