You can solve this problem using the SQL SELECT statement, WHERE clause, ORDER BY clause, and the LENGTH function. Here's how you can do it:

```sql
SELECT id, name, age, LENGTH(name) AS name_length
FROM employees
WHERE name LIKE '%John%' 
AND age > 30
ORDER BY age DESC;
```

This SQL query does the following: 

- `SELECT id, name, age, LENGTH(name) AS name_length`: This line selects all the rows of the columns id, name, and age, and creates a new column called "name_length" which contains the length of the names.

- `FROM employees`: This line specifies the table from where we want to select data.

- `WHERE name LIKE '%John%' AND age > 30`: This line filters all the employees whose name contain "John" and are older than 30 years.

- `ORDER BY age DESC`: This line orders all the selected rows by the "age" column in descending order.
