Conceive a function that acknowledges string input and substitutes each non-alphabetic unit with an ampersand symbol (&). This string requires to adhere to the following JavaScript format:
let inputStr = "Hello* World123!"
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can solve this problem with the help of JavaScript. Below is a function called `replaceNonAlphabets` that does exactly what we want:

```javascript
function replaceNonAlphabets(input) {
    let output = '';
    for(let i=0; i < input.length; i++) {
        if((input.charAt(i) >= 'a' && input.charAt(i) <= 'z') || (input.charAt(i) >= 'A' && input.charAt(i) <= 'Z')) {
            output += input.charAt(i);
        } else {
            output += '&';
        }
    }
    return output;
}

let inputStr = "Hello* World123!";
console.log(replaceNonAlphabets(inputStr));
```
In the above code, the function replaceNonAlphabets traverses each character in the string using a for loop. The charAt function is used to get the character at the current index. Using an if condition, we check whether the character is an alphabet (both lower and upper case). If it's a non-alphabetic character, an ampersand (&) is appended to the output string.
Finally the function returns the modified string. I also included an example input string and a console.log statement to test the function.

When you run this program, it will output `Hello&&&World&&&`.

Please note that this code does not replace non-alphabetic unicode characters. If your input string may contain complex unicode characters, you may need a more sophisticated solution.
<|end|>


