<|user|>
I have heard that it's best not to actually have any html in your helpers; my question is, Why not?  And furthermore, if you were trying to generate an html list or something like that, how can I avoid actual tags?

Thanks!

-fREW
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Having HTML in Rails helpers is often frowned upon because it mixes the responsibilities of logic and presentation, which goes against the principle of separation of concerns. The idea is that helpers should be responsible for facilitating data manipulation or business logic, not for building or managing HTML.

Placing HTML in helpers risks creating code that's hard to manage and modify. For example:

1. It mix up different languages (Ruby and HTML) in the same file, making the code harder to read.
2. It tightly couples your display logic and your data or business logic, making it more challenging to change one independently of the other.
3. If the HTML in a helper gets complex, understanding and modifying the code will be difficult since helpers are not the standard way of handling complex HTML in a Rails application.
4. Code reuse can become messy because HTML in one helper isn't accessible for use in another helper or view without duplication.

Instead, a better approach would be to use view templates and partials for HTML generation. In views, Rails provides many helper methods to generate HTML without writing raw HTML code, like `link_to`, `button_to`, `form_for`, etc. 

If your intent is to generate an HTML list, you can utilize Rails' `content_tag` method which will allow you to generate HTML in a more Ruby-friendly syntax. 

```ruby
<% @items.each do |item| %>
  <%= content_tag :li, item.name %>
<% end %>
```

It's important to note that these are guidelines, not hard rules. There will always be edge cases or specific situations. However, working toward the principle of separation of concerns will make your code base easier to manage in the long run.
<|end|>


