help me port this code over to perl:
from typing import List
def max_difference(nums: List[int]) -> int:
    if not nums:
        return 0
    min_num = nums[0]
    max_diff = nums[1] - nums[0]
    for i in range(1, len(nums)):
        if nums[i] - min_num > max_diff:
            max_diff = nums[i] - min_num
        if nums[i] < min_num:
            min_num = nums[i]
    return max_diff if max_diff > 0 else 0
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's how you can port your Python code to Perl:

```perl
sub max_difference {
    my @nums = @_;
    return 0 if (!@nums);
    my $min_num = $nums[0];
    my $max_diff = $nums[1] - $nums[0];
    for my $i (1..$#nums){
        if($nums[$i] - $min_num > $max_diff){
            $max_diff = $nums[$i] - $min_num;
        }
        if($nums[$i] < $min_num){
            $min_num = $nums[$i];
        }
    }
    return 0 if($max_diff < 0);
    return $max_diff;
}

my @nums = (10, 20, 30, 40, 50); 
print(max_difference(@nums));
```

This code performs the same operation, but in Perl. The `@nums` array stands for the list of integers which is passed to the function. The for loop `for my $i (1..$#nums){}` goes from 1 to the length of the array (`$#nums` returns the highest index in the array, which is array length - 1 in zero-based paradigm, thus matching the Python's `range(1, len(nums))` behavior). The `if` conditions and max difference updates are same as the Python version. The `my` keyword in Perl defines a lexically scoped variable, similar to what `var` does in Python. This script also includes an example usage of the `max_difference` function.
<|end|>


