Devise a blueprint for a mobile application interface, meticulously tailored for the upload of audio content. This interface should incorporate Speech-to-Text technology to precisely transcribe and distill keywords from the uploaded audio data, necessitating a multi-tiered analysis process that involves both the conversion of audio to text and the extraction of significant keywords.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
App Name: SoundScriber

Interface Overview:
SoundScriber's interface should be simple, yet functional - attuned to user-friendliness and optimized for ease of navigation. Users should instantly be able to decipher how to interact with each button, screen, and function.

Primary Functions:

1. Audio Upload
2. Speech-to-text Transcription
3. Keyword Extraction

I. Splash Screen:
An introductory screen with the application's logo and name. It should feature a catchy tagline that succinctly describes the app's main functions.

II. User Authentication:
Facilitate user registration and login. Provide users with multiple registration options (email, Google, Facebook).

III. Home Screen:
Once logged in, users will land on the home screen, which features:

1. A clear 'Upload Audio' button - This should stand out and lead to their phone's media browser directly.
2. Transcribed Files - List of previously transcribed files. Each file on the list should display key metadata such as the title, transcription date, and status (completed or in progress). Clicking on a file should take the user to a detailed Transcription page.
3. Help/FAQ Button - Connects users with useful resources for troubleshooting.

IV. Audio Upload:
Once an audio file is selected, the application should show a progress bar indicating the upload status. Post upload, there should be clear buttons to start transcription and another to cancel the operation if desired.

V. Transcription Screen:
This page will display the transcribed text in real time as the audio file plays. Functions should include play, pause, stop, rewind, and save options. There should be an option to toggle between processed and original text.

VI. Keyword Extraction:
After the transcription is complete, a 'Extract Keywords' button should appear. Once clicked, the app will display a list of significant keywords. Users should be able to select and deselect keywords, aiding in the fine-tuning of their results.

VII. File Management:
Users should be able to rename, delete, share, and download both the transcribed text document and the original audio file. This should all be accessible from the Transcription Screen.

VIII. Settings:
A screen to allow users to tweak app settings. This could include transcription accuracy preferences, language selection, keyword extraction sensitivity, and account settings.

IX. Push Notifications:
To keep users updated on transcription progress, successful uploads, and completed tasks. 

An algorithm for transcribing the audio, alongside another for keyword extraction, must underpin these interface designs to ensure accuracy and efficiency. Additionally, the level of customization and control offered to users will ascertain the app's functionality and usability.

This blueprint is a comprehensive depiction of an intuitive audio-to-text application, but revisions may be required based on user feedback and testing.
<|end|>


