Implement two entities encapsulating the concepts of a human being and an address in JavaScript. Label these entities as 'person' and 'address' respectively. Assign 'person' an attribute 'name' with a text value of 'John' and 'address' an attribute of 'country' with a default value of 'USA'. Make sure the 'person' object can reference its associated 'address' object.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is one way to implement such entities in JavaScript:

```JavaScript
// Define the Address entity
function Address(country) {
  this.country = country || 'USA'; // Defaults to 'USA' if no country is provided
}

// Define the Person entity
function Person(name, address) {
  this.name = name || 'John'; // Defaults to 'John' if no name is provided
  this.address = address; // This is how the person object references its associated address
}

// Create a new address object
var myAddress = new Address();

// Create a new person object, associated with the address object
var myPerson = new Person('John', myAddress);

console.log(myPerson.name); // Outputs: 'John'
console.log(myPerson.address.country); // Outputs: 'USA'
```

In the code above, we define two entities 'Person' and 'Address' as JavaScript functions (which can be used as constructors for creating new objects). 

The 'Person' entity has two attributes: 'name' and 'address'. The 'name' attribute defaults to 'John' if no name is provided when creating a new 'Person' object. The 'address' attribute is an object of type 'Address'.

The 'Address' entity has one attribute: 'country', which defaults to 'USA' if no country is provided when creating a new 'Address' object. 

Finally, we create a new address ('myAddress') and a new person ('myPerson'), and associate the person with the address. The association is achieved by setting the 'address' attribute of the 'Person' object to reference the 'Address' object.
<|end|>


