<|user|>
The central limit theorem says that $$
\frac{\bar{X}-\mu}{\frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}}} \stackrel{\mathcal{D}}{\rightarrow} N(0,1)
$$
What is the distribution of $\bar{X}$? I've seen it given as $\sum X \sim N\left((n)\left(\mu_x\right),(\sqrt{n})\left(\sigma_x\right)\right)$, but don't know how to prove this.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The Central Limit Theorem (CLT) as you stated above, indicates that the distribution of the sample mean $\bar{X}$ is approximately normal when the sample size $n$ is large.

Let
$$
Z=\frac{\bar{X}-\mu}{\sigma/\sqrt{n}}
$$
through manipulating this equation, we get
$$
\bar{X}=Z \cdot \frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}} +  \mu
$$
This indicates that $\bar{X}$ is a linear transformation of $Z$. We know that $Z$ follows standard normal distribution $Z\sim N(0,1)$ according to the CLT. 

A linear transformation of a normally distributed random variable is also normal. That means $\bar{X}$ is also normal but with parameters shifted and rescaled. 

Therefore,
$$
\bar{X}\sim N\left(\mu,\frac{\sigma^2}{n}\right)
$$
which means that the mean of $\bar{X}$ is $\mu$ and the variance is $\frac{\sigma^2}{n}$. The standard deviation would then be $\frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}}$.

It seems there might be a slight misunderstanding in the distribution you've mentioned. The sum of $n$ independent identically distributed (i.i.d.) normal random variables $X\sim N(\mu, \sigma^2)$, would indeed follow $N(n\mu, n\sigma^2)$:
$$
\sum_{{i=1}}^{n} X_i \sim N(n\mu, n\sigma^2)
$$
But for the sample mean $\bar{X}$, it should be divided by $n$ to get $N\left(\mu,\frac{\sigma^2}{n}\right)$.
<|end|>


