As mentioned [here](https://datascience.stackexchange.com/questions/22776/is-it-valuable-to-normalize-rescale-labels-in-neural-network-regression?rq=1), normalizing the target variable often helps a neural network converge faster.
Does it help in convergence, or is there otherwise a reason to use it, for any type of model other than neural nets?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, normalization can generally speed up the training process across a variety of machine learning models, not just neural networks. That's because it can help alleviate issues that arise when different features (or the target variable) are on different scales.

For example, in models that use gradient descent to optimize, having features (or a target) on a larger scale can significantly slow down convergence, as the optimizer has to go through more iterations to fine-tune the model's fit.

For models that rely on distance computations (like K-nearest neighbors or support vector machines), normalization can help ensure that all features contribute equally. Without normalization, features on larger scales can dominate the distance computations, leading the model to essentially ignore features on smaller scales.

Finally, in models that use regularization (like ridge or lasso regression), normalization ensures that the regularization applies equally to all features. Without normalization, the regularization term might apply too strongly to some features and not strongly enough to others, based on their scale.

All of these benefits can apply to the target variable too when you're performing regression. If the target variable is on a significantly different scale from the features, this can slow down training or cause the model to prioritize minimizing error on the features over the target.

However, whether normalizing the target variable actually improves the model's performance is a more complex question, and it can depend on the specifics of the model and the data. As always in machine learning, the best way to know for sure is to try it out and see how it affects your results.
<|end|>


