<|user|>
So it sounds like boosting techniques (eg. XGBoost) uses weak learners (stumps) to gradually learn sequentially. This is not in dispute I hope.
However, with bagging techniques (eg. Random Forest) I'm not sure if it grows weak learners (like stumps) in parallel, or fully grown trees in parallel? Since the definition of "Ensemble modeling" is using many weak learners to form 1 powerful model, I'm leaning towards Random Forests using stumps, but I can be swayed the other direction as well?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Random Forests actually use fully grown decision trees, not stumps. The weak vs. strong classification refers to the predictive power of the individual models used. In boosting techniques, weak learners are used and their performance is improved upon iteratively. In contrast, Random Forests use a set of strong learners (fully grown decision trees) which are created independently of each other. 

The concept of "weak learner" has more to do with the error rate being just better than random guessing, whereas "strong learner" has an error rate that is arbitrarily better than random. While Ensemble models can be built with lots of "weak learners", this is not always the case and Random Forest is an essential example of this. 

Bagging and Boosting are different approaches to ensemble learning though both techniques aim to reduce the variance of the prediction. Bagging, where Random Forest belongs, reduces variance by generating additional data for training from dataset using combinations with repetitions to produce multi-sets of the original data. Boosting, where XGBoost belongs, reduces variance by training the current predictor to correct its predecessor. The main distinction between these ensemble methods is that bagging produces full trees while boosting produces stumps.
<|end|>


