What is the best way of implementing a cache for a PHP site? Obviously, there are some things that shouldn't be cached (for example search queries), but I want to find a good solution that will make sure that I avoid the 'digg effect'.

I know there is WP-Cache for WordPress, but I'm writing a custom solution that isn't built on WP. I'm interested in either writing my own cache (if it's simple enough), or you could point me to a nice, light framework. I don't know much Apache though, so if it was a PHP framework then it would be a better fit.

Thanks.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
There are several approaches that you can use to implement caching in PHP:

1. **In-memory caching:** This is one of the most efficient ways to implement caching. It involves storing data in the server's memory. PHP provides several extensions for in-memory caching such as Memcached and APCu, which are fast because they store data in RAM. Memcached is a distributed cache system, meaning it allows for caching data across different servers, while APCu is a simpler cache system which caches data in the memory of the server it is installed on.

2. **Database query caching:** If your website is database driven, chances are that identical queries are being run multiple times. Most of the databases support internal caching, but you can also use PHP to cache the results of a query. You could save the result of a database query into a file or in-memory cache and the next time the same query is requested, you could serve up the cached file instead of querying the database again.

3. **Page caching:** If there are static pages not changing for a set time, it is probably best to cache the whole page. You can capture and store the output of the page in a file and serve up this file whenever the same page is requested. 

4. **HTTP caching:** Using HTTP headers, you can notify browsers whether they should cache the page or not. Etag and Cache-Control headers are used for this purpose. You would use this method to reduce the load of your server when clients (browsers) have a version of your page in their cache.

5. **Opcode caching:** PHP is an interpreted language, meaning that each time a PHP page is loaded, the server must read the file, process the code and then produce (or output) the resulting web page. OpCode cache improves PHP performance by storing precompiled script bytecode in shared memory, thereby removing the need for PHP to load and parse scripts on each request.

6. **CDN (Content Delivery Network):** This is not strictly a caching option, but a CDN can cache static files like CSS, JS, and images and deliver them from the closest server to the visitor. This decreases latency and increases the speed that the client can download your files.

One of the commonly used caching library is **Stash**, you might want to check it out.

Remember that implementing caching will definitely speed up your application, but added complexity of managing cache invalidation and updates should also be taken into account. So it's always a trade-off between speed and complexity.

If your PHP website is using a framework, you should check if the framework already provides a caching mechanism. Symfony and Laravel are two PHP frameworks that provide caching out of the box, so it might save you from handling it yet again in PHP.
<|end|>


