When I say DNN, I mean the simple usage of densely connected neurons (not CNN).
Say we are using Keras (with Tensorflow backend), the input_dim, using the Sequential API, can only take an integer or None value which restricts it to taking grayscale images right?
However since the functional API can accept dimensions that aren't just restricted to integers I would assume that it can take RGB images as opposed to only grayscale images?
If anyone can confirm or support, that would be great?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Both Keras Sequential and Functional API can process RGB images, the input dimension is not restricted to grayscale images only.

For input dimensions:

- Grayscale images are 2D arrays (Height x Width), so `input_shape` would be something like `(64, 64)`, considering each pixel as a feature.
- Color (RGB) images are 3D arrays (Height x Width x Channels), so `input_shape` would be something like `(64, 64, 3)`. Here 3 represents three color channels i.e. Red, Green, and Blue.

Hence, in case of RGB images, you would be dealing with three-dimensional input. Your input layer should match this shape. If you're using Keras, this can be done as follows for a Dense Layer:

```python
from keras.models import Sequential
from keras.layers import Dense
...
model = Sequential()
model.add(Dense(64, activation='relu', input_shape=(64, 64, 3)))
...
``` 
You flatten the input while feeding to the Dense Layer after Convolutional layers.

```python
from keras.models import Sequential
from keras.layers import Dense, Flatten
from keras.layers import Conv2D
...
model = Sequential()
model.add(Conv2D(32, kernel_size=(3, 3),activation='relu',input_shape=(64, 64, 3)))
model.add(Flatten())
model.add(Dense(64, activation='relu'))
...
```
This means you can work with grayscale or color images just as easily with either the Sequential or Functional API. The only difference would be how you structure your model.
<|end|>


