I feel that my shop has a hole because we don't have a solid process in place for versioning our database schema changes. We do a lot of backups so we're more or less covered, but it's bad practice to rely on your last line of defense in this way. 

Surprisingly, this seems to be a common thread. Many shops I have spoken to ignore this issue because their databases don't change often, and they basically just try to be meticulous.

However, I know how that story goes. It's only a matter of time before things line up just wrong and something goes missing. 

Are there any best practices for this? What are some strategies that have worked for you?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Database versioning is indeed a very important aspect of software development and deployment, and it is really good to see that you are looking for a solution to address this in your environment. Here are some recommended practices to help manage your database schema changes:

1. Source Control: Database Schema should be placed under a source control system like git, just like application source code. This includes all schema changes and reference data scripts.

2. Automated Migrations: Use a database migration tool to manage and automate your schema changes. Tools like Liquibase, Flyway, or Doctrine Migrations allow you to script database changes and run them in an orderly fashion.

3. Database Versioning: This refers to having a version number, or timestamp associated to each change on the database. This can be automatically managed by the migration tools mentioned above.

4. Include Schema Changes into CI/CD Pipeline: Automated deployment pipelines ensure that every change goes through the same process, avoiding mistakes that might be introduced because of human error during manual deployments. Use the migration tool within your CI/CD pipeline for automated deployment including DB changes.

5. Environments Parity: Keep Development, Staging, and Production environments as similar as possible. This helps limit the unpredictability of schema changes and catch potential issues early.

6. Backward Compatibility: Whenever possible, create schema changes that are backward compatible. This means the new schema version can run with the previous versions of the application.

7. Repeatable Process: Schema changes should be repeatable and idempotent. This means you can apply them several times without changing the result beyond the initial application.

8. Team Communication: Ensure that the development team communicates about and reviews any changes to the database schema to avoid any misunderstanding or miscommunication.

9. Documentation: Keep a well-documented history of all of the schema changes, why they were made, how they were implemented, and any important interactions they have with other parts of the system.

10. Testing: Rigorously test your schema changes to avoid breakages. DB schema changes should be tested locally before being pushed into the dev environment.

Following these practices will not only protect you against data loss or inconsistencies due to miscommunication but will also allow you to track changes over time and rollback to a known good state if and when required.
<|end|>


