Consider a scenario where an investor has a portfolio consisting of two categories of stock. Stock type one boasts an annual yield rate of 15% whereas stock type two delivers a relatively lower annual yield rate of 10%. Supposing that the said investor committed a cumulative amount of 1000 dollars toward these two categories of stocks and in one firm fiscal year, garnered total returns amounting to 130 dollars, can we decipher the proportion of investment allocated to each type of stock?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, it's possible to figure out the proportion of investment allocated to each type of stock.

Let's denote the amount of investment in the first type of stock as X and the amount of investment in the second type as Y. We know that X+Y=1000 dollars. 

We also know that the total return of 130 dollars is made from the returns of both types of stocks, the first yielding 15% and the second yielding 10%. Hence, we can set up the second equation as 0.15X+0.10Y=130.

To find the values of X and Y that satisfy both these equations, we can use the method of substitution or elimination in this system of linear equations. These values will represent the amount of investment made in each type of stock.
<|end|>


