Here is a simple C# program to calculate the factorial of a number:

```csharp
using System;

class Program {
    static void Main(string[] args) {
        Console.Write("Enter a number: ");
        int num = Convert.ToInt32(Console.ReadLine());
        long fact = Factorial(num);
        Console.WriteLine("Factorial of " + num + " is " + fact);
    }

    static long Factorial(int num) {
        if (num == 0)
            return 1;
        else
            return num * Factorial(num - 1);
    }
}
```

In this program, the main method prompts the user to enter a number. It calls the Factorial method by passing the user input. The Factorial method calculates the factorial of the given number using recursion. The result is then printed on the console.

Note: Factorial values grow extremely quickly, so this program will keep accuracy up to the largest number that can be stored in a `long` (factorial of 20 and below). Beyond that, the numbers are too big and you would need arbitrary precision arithmetic.

If user input is not required, you can just call the Factorial function with the hardcoded number, like this:

```csharp
long fact = Factorial(5);
```
