Design and implement a JavaScript function capable of visualizing an intricately structured three-dimensional array. Use the following 3D array as your test case: arr = [[[1, 2], [3, 4]], [[5, 6], [7, 8]]]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
JavaScript does not have native support for 3D visualization, but you can still represent the 3D array in a structured way like below:

```javascript
const arr = [[[1, 2], [3, 4]], [[5, 6], [7, 8]]];

let visualize3DArray = function (arr) {
  let result = "[";
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    result += "[";
    for (let j = 0; j < arr[i].length; j++) {
      result += "[" + arr[i][j].join(", ") + "]";
      if (j !== arr[i].length - 1) result += ", ";
    }
    result += "]";
    if (i !== arr.length - 1) result += ", ";
  }
  result += "]";
  return result;
};

console.log(visualize3DArray(arr));
```

This will output:

`[[[1, 2], [3, 4]], [[5, 6], [7, 8]]]`

This script works by iterating over the three dimensions of the array and joining the individual elements together into a string structure that matches the 3D array.

Please note that this strategy only works for visualizing the array in console or a web page where JavaScript is running. If you're looking for a way to visualize 3D array graphically, you'll need to use a library or a tool designed for that. Libraries that might help you include Three.js for general 3D, or for data specific visualizations maybe Plotly.js or similar.
