Here is the equivalent JavaScript function with inline comments:

```js
function sort_by_binary_ones_desc(arr) {
    // Comparator function for sort() method that compares two values based on the number of '1's in their binary representation.
    function sort_key(x, y) {
      // Find the binary representation of the numbers using toString() function and then find the count of '1's using match() function.
      // If there are no '1's, match() returns null, so the count is 0. 
      var xCount = (x.toString(2).match(/1/g) || []).length;
      var yCount = (y.toString(2).match(/1/g) || []).length;

      // Compare the number of '1's in the binary representation.
      if (xCount > yCount) {
        return -1;
      } else if (xCount < yCount) {
        return 1;
      } else {
        // If the number of '1's are same, then compare the values.
        // Negative value comparison is done by comparing y first because we want the array in descending order.
        if (y > x) {
          return -1;
        } else if (y < x) {
          return 1;
        } else {
          return 0;
        }
      }
    }

    // Use sort function with the comparator function we defined.
    arr.sort(sort_key);
    
    return arr;
}
```

Note: The sort() function in JavaScript performs an in-place sort while in Python, arr.sort() also modifies the list in-place. If you want a new sorted array without modifying the original array, you can replace `arr.sort(sort_key);` with `let sorted_arr = [...arr].sort(sort_key);` and return `sorted_arr;`
