Create a responsive CSS class that displays a square box. The box should have the following properties:

1. Box's border-radius should vary from 0 to 50px depending on the viewport size. In mobile viewports (width up to 480 pixels), the border-radius should be 0px; in tablet viewports (481-768 px), the border-radius should be 25px; in desktop viewports (greater than 768px), it should be 50px. 

2. The size of the box should be 10% of the viewport width, with a maximum size of 200px and a minimum size of 100px. 

Note: You are expected to use @media queries to create this responsive design. The initial CSS class is as follows:
```
.square {
  width: 100px;
  height: 100px;
}
```
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can achieve this by using the CSS `@media` queries to determine the viewport size and then set the properties of the box according to the specifics you listed above.

```
.square {
  width: 10vw;
  height: 10vw;
  max-width: 200px;
  min-width: 100px;
  max-height: 200px;
  min-height: 100px;
  border-radius:50px; /* Default property if viewport is greater than 768px */
}

@media (max-width: 768px) {
  .square {
    border-radius: 25px; /* If viewport is between 481px and 768px */
  }
}

@media (max-width: 480px) {
  .square {
    border-radius: 0px; /* If viewport is less than or equal to 480px */
  }
}
```

In the above CSS, the `.square` class initially sets the width and height of the box to be 10% of the viewport's width (vw). It also sets the border-radius to be 50px (for desktop viewports). Then, it utilizes `@media` queries to change the `border-radius` when the viewport's width decreases. When the viewport is less than or equal to 480px (mobile), the `border-radius` is set to 0px. If it's between 481px and 768px (tablet viewports), it becomes 25px. 

Also, to control the size of the box so that it never exceeds 200px or falls below 100px, `max-width`, `min-width`, `max-height`, and `min-height` properties are used.
<|end|>


