Construct a refined program in Java language that takes a provided string as an input and performs a validation to check whether it qualifies as an anagram. Consider the word pair "Listen" and "Silent".
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a simple Java program that takes two strings as inputs and checks if they are anagrams of each other. It does this by checking if both strings contain the same characters in the same or different order. Here's the solution:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class Anagram {

    // Method to sort a string alphabetically
    public static String sortString(String input) {
        // Convert input string to char array
        char[] tempArray = input.toCharArray();

        // Sort the tempArray
        Arrays.sort(tempArray);

        // Return new sorted string
        return new String(tempArray);
    }

    // Function to check whether two strings are anagram of each other
    public static boolean areAnagram(String str1, String str2) {
        
        str1 = str1.replaceAll("\\s", "").toLowerCase();
        str2 = str2.replaceAll("\\s", "").toLowerCase();
        
        // If string lengths are not same
        if (str1.length() != str2.length())
            return false;

        // See if sorted versions of strings are the same
        if (sortString(str1).equals(sortString(str2)))
            return true;
        else 
            return false;
    }


    public static void main(String args[]) {
       Anagram anagram = new Anagram();
       if(anagram.areAnagram("Listen", "Silent")) {
       	    System.out.println("The two strings are anagram of each other");
       } else {
            System.out.println("The two strings are not anagram of each other");
       }

    }
}

```

This program takes two strings, removes any whitespace they might contain, converts them to lowercase and sorts the characters in them alphabetically. If the sorted versions of the strings are the same ,then they are anagrams of each other; otherwise, they are not.
<|end|>


