Engineer a C++ class christened as "Vehicle" which should systematically derive its properties from an overarching superior class denoted as "Object".
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Before you begin, please note that C++ does not come with a predefined "Object" class (like Java, for example). It is common to create an Object class in C++, but it is not standard.

To create an "Object" class and a derived "Vehicle" class, we will pretend that there already is some "Object" parent class which "Vehicle" will derive from.

Here is a simple example of how an Object class and a Vehicle class may be structured:

```cpp
// Object parent class
class Object {
public:
    Object() {}

    virtual ~Object() {}

    // ... more methods and variables as necessary ...
};

// Vehicle child class
class Vehicle : public Object {
public:
    Vehicle() {}

    Vehicle(std::string brand, std::string model, int year) 
    : brand(brand), model(model), year(year) {}

    ~Vehicle() {}

    std::string getBrand() const {
        return brand;
    }

    std::string getModel() const {
        return model;
    }

    int getYear() const {
        return year;
    }

    // ... more methods and variables as necessary ...

private:
    std::string brand;
    std::string model;
    int year;
    // ... more variables as necessary ...
};
```

In this example, Vehicle is a child class of Object accomplished by the ": public Object" in the class declaration of Vehicle. Vehicle takes on and extends from the properties of Object. Vehicle also has it's own private member variables brand, model, and year; and public accessor functions to get the values of these variables. 

Also note that "~Object() {}" and "~Vehicle() {}" are destructor function declarations. These are used to clean up any resources the object may use when it is destroyed.

You would need to include `<string>` in order to use `std::string` in this example.
