Here's a function that accomplishes that task:

```javascript
var array = [3, 5, 2, 6, 8, 6];

function findLargestElementIndices(arr) {
    var largestValue = arr[0];
    var indices = [];

    //Find largest value in array
    for (var i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
        if (arr[i] > largestValue) {
            largestValue = arr[i];
        }
    }

    //Find indices where the largest value is found
    for (var i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
        if (arr[i] === largestValue) {
            indices.push(i);
        }
    }

    //Return either single index or array of indices
    if (indices.length === 1) {
        return indices[0];
    } else {
        return indices;
    }
}

console.log(findLargestElementIndices(array)); // Expected output: [3, 5]
```

This function works by first scanning the array to find the largest value. It then scans the array again to find the indices where this largest value is found and stores these indices in an array. It finally returns either a single index or an array of indices, depending on the number of occurrences of the largest value.
