Develop a for-loop algorithm that not only identifies but also exhibits the prime integers in the range of 20 to 40, displaying these numbers in an orderly and concise manner.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's an example of how you could do this using Python:

```python
# Initialize an empty list to hold our prime numbers
prime_nums = []

# We set the range from 20 to 40
for num in range(20, 41):
    # prime numbers are greater than 1
    if num > 1:
        for i in range(2, num):
            # check for factors
            if (num % i) == 0:
                # if factor is found, break out of loop and go to the next number
                break
        else:
            # this part of loop executes only if the for loop (not the if) completes
            # which means no factor was found and number is prime
            prime_nums.append(num)

# Print out the prime numbers
print("The prime numbers in the range of 20 to 40 are: ")
for prime in prime_nums:
    print(prime)
```

When you run this code, it will display:

```text
The prime numbers in the range of 20 to 40 are: 
23
29
31
37
```

Remember that a prime number is a positive integer that has exactly two positive integer factors, 1 and itself. To check if a number is prime, you should check if it has any factors other than 1 and itself. If it does, it's not prime. If it doesn't, it is prime.
<|end|>


