Design a Java class blueprint managing information about an individual customer for a specialized antiquarian literature emporium. This blueprint should include specific elements such as customer's name, phone number, email, address, and a list of purchases composed of unique books with attributes like title, author, year of publication, price, and condition.

In addition, create methods to handle the following more complex operations:

1. Calculating the total expenditure of a customer based on their purchases.
2. A method that tracks the most frequently purchased author by the customer.
3. A way to update the contact details of the customer in a thread-safe manner.
4. Arrange the books purchased by the customer according to the year of publication, both in ascending and descending order.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is a basic implementation of the above specification using Java:

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

class Book {
    String title;
    String author;
    int yearOfPublication;
    double price;
    String condition;

    // constructors, getters and setters removed for brevity
}

class Customer {
    private String name;
    private String phoneNumber;
    private String email;
    private String address;
    private List<Book> purchases;
    private ReentrantLock lock;

    public Customer(String name, String phoneNumber, String email, String address) {
        this.name = name;
        this.phoneNumber = phoneNumber;
        this.email = email;
        this.address = address;
        this.purchases = new ArrayList<>();
        this.lock = new ReentrantLock();
    }

    // thread-safe method to update contact details
    public void updateContactDetails(String name, String phoneNumber, String email, String address) {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            this.name = name;
            this.phoneNumber = phoneNumber;
            this.email = email;
            this.address = address;
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public void addBook(Book book) {
        purchases.add(book);
    }

    public double calculateTotalExpenditure() {
        return purchases.stream()
                .mapToDouble(Book::getPrice)
                .sum();
    }

    public String getMostFrequentlyPurchasedAuthor() {
        return purchases.stream()
                .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Book::getAuthor, Collectors.counting()))
                .entrySet()
                .stream()
                .max(Map.Entry.comparingByValue())
                .map(Map.Entry::getKey)
                .orElse(null);
    }

    // returns a new list of books sorted by year of publication
    public List<Book> getBooksSortedByYearAscending() {
        return purchases.stream()
                .sorted(Comparator.comparingInt(Book::getYearOfPublication))
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }

    public List<Book> getBooksSortedByYearDescending() {
        return purchases.stream()
                .sorted(Comparator.comparingInt(Book::getYearOfPublication).reversed())
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }

    // getters, setters and other methods removed for brevity
}
```

Please note: this is a simple version of solution, other considerations like properly overriding `equals()`, `hashCode()` methods, using `BigDecimal` for monetary calculations, and more comprehensive error/exception handling should be considered according to the actual requirements and use cases.
<|end|>


