<|asst|>
Sure, below is a simple example of a Node.js application that counts the frequency of each word in an input list.

```javascript
// Import required library
const readline = require('readline');

// Create readline interface
const rl = readline.createInterface({
    input: process.stdin,
    output: process.stdout
});

// Ask user for a list of words
rl.question('Enter a list of words: ', (answer) => {
    // Split input into an array of words
    let words = answer.toLowerCase().split(' ');

    // Create an empty object to hold word counts
    let wordCounts = {};

    // Loop through the array of words
    for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
        // If the word is already a key in the object, increment its count
        if (wordCounts.hasOwnProperty(words[i])) {
            wordCounts[words[i]]++;
        } 
        // If the word is not a key in the object, add it with a count of 1
        else {
            wordCounts[words[i]] = 1;
        }
    }

    // Print word counts
    console.log('Word frequencies:');
    for (let word in wordCounts) {
        console.log(`'${word}': ${wordCounts[word]}`);
    }

    // Close readline interface
    rl.close();
});
```

Save this as `wordFrequency.js` and run it with `node wordFrequency.js`. You enter a list of words when it prompts "Enter a list of words: ", and it will display the frequency of each word.

Note that this simple application doesn't do any complex text processing, so it won't correctly count words that are capitalized differently, and punctuation will interfere with word counts (e.g., "word" and "word," would be counted as different words). You might want to add text processing to handle these cases more correctly in a real application.
