Implement a Radius Neighbors Classifier in R using a custom non-Euclidean distance metric. Furthermore, your implementation should have an option to select k-nearest neighbors based on the calculated distances. The classifier should be able to handle multiclass classification problems efficiently. Additionally, provide an error handling logic when no points are within the given radius.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a basic implementation of Radius Neighbors Classifier in R programming language with custom non-Euclidean distance metric.

```R
# Loading necessary library
library(FNN)

# Radius Neighbors Classifier function
radiusNeighborsClassifier <- function(train_data, train_labels, test_data, radius=1, k=1){
  # Calculating distances using custom non-euclidean distance metric
  # Assume 'customDist' is your own distance function
  dists <- customDist(train_data, test_data)
  
  # Adding train labels as a column to distance matrix
  dists <- cbind(dists, train_labels)
  
  predicted_labels <- numeric(0)
  for(i in 1:nrow(dists)){
    # Selecting points within the radius
    neighbors <- dists[dists[,i] <= radius,]
    
    # If no points are within the radius
    if(nrow(neighbors) == 0){
      stop("No points are within the given radius.")
    }
    
    # Counting the occurrence of each label in the k nearest neighbors
    label_counts <- table(neighbors[order(neighbors[,i])[1:k], ncol(dists)])
    
    # Selecting the most common label among the nearest neighbors
    predicted_label <- as.numeric(names(label_counts)[which.max(label_counts)])
    
    # Storing the predicted label
    predicted_labels <- c(predicted_labels, predicted_label)
  }
  
  return(predicted_labels)
}

# Your custom distance function here
customDist <- function(x, y){
  # Replace this with your own logic
  return(dist(x, y, method = "euclidean"))
}

# Usage
# Suppose you have two matrices 'train_data' (m x n), 'test_data' (p x n) and a factor 'train_labels' (m x 1)
# (where 'm' is the number of training instances, 'n' is the number of features and 'p' is the number of testing instances).
# Here is how you would use it:
predicted_labels <- radiusNeighborsClassifier(train_data, train_labels, test_data, radius=1, k=3)
```

This script uses a custom distance function `customDist`, which you should replace with your non-Euclidean distance metric. The script also includes an optional parameter `k` that allows you to select the k-nearest neighbors based on the calculated distances. If no points are within the given radius, the script will raise an error. Note that this script assumes your data is numerical and your labels are factors. 

This classifier is efficient for multi-class problems as it uses a voting strategy among the k nearest neighbors within the given radius to determine the class of test points. 

One caveat of this Neighbors-based classification is that its complexity grows with the size of the training data, it could be slower compared to other algorithms when the training dataset is very large. So preprocessing, feature selection, or dimensionality reduction could be essential to improve its efficiency.
<|end|>


