Write a function in JavaScript that takes an array of character arrays. First, sort each of the smaller arrays in alphabetical order. Then, concatenate the characters in each array into a single string. After that, sort these strings in the order of the string length (if the string lengths are the same, sort them alphabetically). Finally, concatenate all of the strings together. Make sure to take care of edge cases such as empty arrays or arrays with only one character. Additionally, write test cases for your function to ensure its correctness.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's the JavaScript function that meets your criteria:

```javascript
function solve(arr) {
    if (arr.length === 0) return "";

    return arr
        .map(subArr => subArr.sort().join(''))
        .sort((a, b) => {
            if(a.length !== b.length) {
                return a.length - b.length;
            } else {
                return a < b ? -1 : 1;
            }
        })
        .join('');
}
```

This function first uses the `Array.prototype.map()` function to sort a sub-array and join it into a string. The `Array.prototype.sort()` function is then used again to sort these strings. A custom sort function is provided to achieve the desired sorting: if the strings have different lengths, they are sorted by length; if they have the same length, they are sorted alphabetically.

The function returns an empty string if the input array is empty.

Test cases:

```javascript
console.log(solve([]));                          // ""
console.log(solve([['a']]));                     // "a"
console.log(solve([['b', 'a']]));                // "ab"
console.log(solve([['b', 'a'], ['c']]));         // "abc"
console.log(solve([['b', 'a'], ['d', 'c']]));    // "abcd"
console.log(solve([['b', 'a'], ['d', 'c', 'e']]));   // "abdec"
```

These should cover most of the edge cases (Empty array, array with one string, array with multiple strings of different lengths) and demonstrate the function performing as expected under normal conditions.
<|end|>


