Generate a random string in C language of 10 characters that should contain only lower-case letters.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is an example in C for generating a 10 character long string containing lower case ASCII characters:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

int main() {
    srand(time(0)); // Seed the random number generator with the current time
    char string[11]; // Array to hold the string, extra space for the null terminating character
    for (int i=0; i<10; i++) {
        string[i] = 'a' + rand() % ('z' - 'a' + 1); // Generate a random character between 'a' and 'z'
    }
    string[10] = '\0'; // Null terminating character to make it a proper string
    printf("Random string: %s\n", string); // Print the generated string

    return 0;
}
```

Here, `rand()` is used to generate random numbers. By using `% ('z' - 'a' + 1)`, we ensure that the generated number is within the range of lower case letters in ASCII. `'a' + rand() % ('z' - 'a' + 1)` will always result in an ASCII value of a lower case letter. We do this in a loop 10 times to get a string of length 10. The string is then terminated with a null character (`'\0'`), as is required in C.
<|end|>


