Devise a Python code segment that performs the computation of the aggregate value of all unique constituents within a quadruple-dimensional array. This array should conform to the showcased structure below:

arr = [[[[2, 3],
         [4, 5]],
        [[6, 1],
         [7, 0]]],
       [[[8, 2],
         [3, 9]],
        [[5, 7],
         [1, 6]]]]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, the Python code segment for computing the aggregate value of all unique constituents would basically involve two steps:

1. Flatten the quadruple-dimensional array (4D array) using a nested list comprehension
2. Compute the aggregate value using the built-in sum() function.

Here's how it can be done:

```python
arr = [[[[2, 3], [4, 5]], [[6, 1], [7, 0]]], [[[8, 2], [3, 9]], [[5, 7], [1, 6]]]]

# Flatten the array and remove duplicates using set()
flat_arr = set(num for sublist1 in arr for sublist2 in sublist1 for sublist3 in sublist2 for num in sublist3)

# Compute the aggregate value
print(sum(flat_arr))
```

This will give you the sum of all the unique values in the array. If a value occurs more than once in the array, it will be added only once to the sum. Note that the set() method used to flatten the array also removes duplicates.
