Write a Haskell function that takes a list of integers and sorts them in ascending order.
[9, 3, 6, 4, 7]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a quicksort implementation in Haskell:

```haskell
quickSort :: [Int] -> [Int]
quickSort [] = []
quickSort (x:xs) = 
    let smallerSorted = quickSort [a | a <- xs, a <= x]
        biggerSorted = quickSort [a | a <- xs, a > x]
    in smallerSorted ++ [x] ++ biggerSorted

main = print (quickSort [9, 3, 6, 4, 7]) -- Prints: [3,4,6,7,9]
```

This quicksort function works by selecting a pivot (`x`), which is the first element of the list, and partitioning the rest of the list (`xs`) into two lists: one contains all the elements smaller or equal to the pivot, and the other contains all the elements larger than the pivot. It then recursively sorts these two lists and joins them together with the pivot in the middle. If the input list is empty, the function simply returns an empty list.

You can call this function with your list to get the sorted list. Note that this is not an in-place sort, so it will return a new sorted list without modifying the original one.
<|end|>


