I have 100K customers from a population with a KPI name "spent" which is lognormal distributed.
The customers were split into 2 groups -
1st group - customers with an id ending with 0 - control
2nd group - customers with an id ending with 1-9 - treatment
The treatment group got 20 marketing campaigns during 2022.
during 2021 no marketing campaign was made - for both groups.
When comparing the difference between the groups I can see 10% uplift in terms of total spent (after multiplying by 9 to overcome the size difference).
The uplift is already exist in the groups even before the campaigns.
This uplift is sourced to less then 0.1% of the customers - some of the customers spent a lot.
Does it make sense to compare the total spent in order to evaluate the effectiveness of the marketing campaigns? if not, why?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Comparing the total spent may not give an entirely accurate picture of the effectiveness of the marketing campaigns because it is sensitive to extreme data points or outliers i.e., a small group of customers spending a lot. It is possible that these high spenders are distorting the average and creating an apparent uplift that may not actually be representative of the majority of customers. 

There are couple of important aspects to consider:

1. Pre-existing Uplift: As you mentioned, the uplift already exists in the groups even before the campaigns. This could indicate inherent differences between the groups that are not related to the interventions (marketing campaigns). Therefore, attributing the total spent uplift solely to the campaigns might lead to misleading results.

2. Effect on majority: If the uplift is attributable to less than 0.1% of the customers, it raises the question whether the campaigns were really effective for the vast majority of customers (99.9%). Ideally, a successful campaign would encourage a wider range of customers to spend more, rather than just influencing a tiny fraction. 
  
You might want to consider other statistical measures that are less sensitive to outliers, such as median or interquartile ranges. Alternatively, you could perform an outlier treatment before the analysis to eliminate the extreme values. You could also conduct separate analyses: one for the bulk of your customers and another for the extreme high-spenders. 

You can look at other metrics like frequency of purchase, Net Promoter Score (NPS), Customer Lifetime Value (CLV) etc. Conducting A/B tests, Customer Segmentation and Cohort Analysis can also provide deeper insights about the effectiveness of the campaigns. 

Remember, the goal is to assess the impact of marketing campaigns so any inherent or pre-existing differences between the control and treatment group should be controlled for, and the resultant insights should ideally be representative for the general population and not just a specific group.
<|end|>


