I wanted to schedule the event to do some task (insert is dummy). The process should run daily at morning 8:00 AM and should be run every 5 min. till 8:00 PM. As per my script it ran for one day but next day it did not trigger.
Looking for the work around on this.
Event is :-

Drop event if exists test; 
CREATE EVENT test
  ON SCHEDULE EVERY 5 MINUTE
    STARTS (TIMESTAMP(CURRENT_DATE) + INTERVAL 28800 SECOND )
    ENDS (TIMESTAMP(CURRENT_DATE) + INTERVAL 72000 SECOND )
  DO
     insert into temp (name) values (concat('event_name ', sysdate()));


Note:-
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Assuming you're using MySQL, the issue could be that your event ends on the same day it starts and does not get recreated for the following day. If you start an event at 8:00 AM and end at 8:00 PM with interval every 5 minutes it means that next event will actually never starts. The event_schedule should be set for every 24 hours if you need the operation every day.

You must create a recurring event that loops every 24 hours.

Try something like the following:

```
DROP EVENT IF EXISTS test; 
CREATE EVENT test
  ON SCHEDULE EVERY 1 DAY
  STARTS (TIMESTAMP(CURRENT_DATE) + INTERVAL 8 HOUR )
  DO
    BEGIN
      DECLARE current_hour INT;
      my_loop: LOOP
        SET current_hour = HOUR(NOW());
        IF current_hour > 20 THEN 
          LEAVE my_loop;
        END IF;  

        INSERT INTO temp (name) VALUES (CONCAT('Event_Name ', NOW()));
        DO SLEEP(300);
      END LOOP;
    END;
```
This event gets created and runs every 24 hour, at start time it enters the loop, where it checks if current hour is less than 8PM. If it is, it does the insert and then sleeps for 300 seconds (5 minutes). If hour is more than 8PM, it exits the loop and finishes execution for the day.

Also, make sure that you have event_scheduler enabled. You can do this by executing this SQL:

```
SET GLOBAL event_scheduler = ON;
```

Remember that the event_scheduler variable reset off on every server reboot so you might want to set it as a global variable in MySQL configuration file (`my.cnf` or `my.ini` on Windows).

```
[mysqld]
event_scheduler=ON
```
<|end|>


