Craft a SQL command to retrieve the data of the worker possessing the smallest wage within the "employee" relational database where data is structured in table format. Additionally, return the worker with the highest wage per department. 
Employee Table

EmployeeName | Department  | Salary
-------------------------------
John         | IT          | 25000
Mary         | Finance     | 28000
Sam          | Marketing   | 23000
Randy        | HR          | 32000
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To solve this problem, you would use SQL queries. Here is how you would do it:

1) To retrieve the data of the worker possessing the smallest wage:
```SQL
SELECT * 
FROM Employee
ORDER BY Salary ASC
LIMIT 1;
```

2) To retrieve the worker with the highest wage per department, you would use a subquery to get the maximum salary from each department then join that result back to the Employee table to get the rest of the details for the employees:
```SQL
SELECT E.* 
FROM Employee E
JOIN (
    SELECT Department, MAX(Salary) AS max_salary
    FROM Employee
    GROUP BY Department
) AS D ON E.Department = D.Department AND E.Salary = D.max_salary;
```
This will return one row per department with the employee who has the highest wage within each department. If there are multiple employees in the same department with the same highest wage, all of them will be returned. If you want to return only one of them arbitrarily, you can use ROW_NUMBER() function when supported by your SQL system or similar approaches.
<|end|>


