<|user|>
I am creating an SQL view for a file that strips out the spaces in a particular field. My question is if there is a why to set a key on that new view so a person can still CHAIN the file. We are on V5R3.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
SQL views in DB2/400 (on OS V5R3 or any version of iSeries) do not support key specifications. A view essentially is a presented result of a query from one or more tables. It does not hold the data physically, hence the physical properties (like key, index etc) of a table cannot be applied on a view.

You can achieve the chaining behavior in AS400 RPG program by following these steps:

1. Create the SQL view with the space-stripped field.
2. Use this SQL view in your RPG program using an Open Access Handler (OAH), a feature of recent versions of RPG that lets RPG handle files using logic in an RPG program rather than the system's file I/O.

For CHAIN operation, you have to sequentially read the file/view until you find the record you want, since there is no key. 

Another probable workaround would be to create a new physical file based on the view (using CRTPF command) and add the key when creating the physical file; however, this will not automatically update when the base table(s) is/are updated.

In summary, you may need to revise why you need a key on a view and possibly reconsider the design.
<|end|>


