Construct an HTML document with JavaScript to display individual information extracted from a RESTful web service and format it for mobile viewing. JSON data includes additional fields for occupation and education.
{
 "name": "John",
 "age" : 25,
 "address" : "123 Main Street",
 "occupation" : "Developer",
 "education" : "Bachelor's Degree"
}
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Building an HTML document with JS can be done by combining HTML, CSS, and JavaScript as the following example demonstrates. We'll be using the `fetch` function to access the RESTful web service. Note that you will need to replace 'https://myapi.com' with the actual URL of your API.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
    <head>
        <title>Person Information</title>
        <style>
            /* Add some styling for better mobile viewing */
            body {
                font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
                margin: 0;
                padding: 0;
                background-color: #f0f0f0;
            }
            .info {
                box-sizing: border-box;
                width: 100%;
                padding: 10px;
                border: 1px solid #ddd;
                margin-bottom: 10px;
            }
            @media (max-width: 600px) {
                .info {
                    width: 100%;
                }
            }
        </style>
    </head>
    <body>
        <div id="info"></div>
        <script>
            const url = 'https://myapi.com';

            fetch(url)
                .then(response => response.json())
                .then(data => {
                    let div = document.getElementById('info');
                    div.innerHTML = `
                        <div class="info">
                            <h2>${data.name}</h2>
                            <p>Age: ${data.age}</p>
                            <p>Address: ${data.address}</p>
                            <p>Occupation: ${data.occupation}</p>
                            <p>Education: ${data.education}</p>
                        </div>
                    `;
                })
                .catch(err => console.error('Error:', err));
        </script>
    </body>
</html>
```

This code creates an HTML document with a single `<div>` element that is used to display the information retrieved. The JavaScript portion makes a `fetch` request to 'https://myapi.com' (must be replaced by real API endpoint), converts the response to JSON, then injects the received information into the `<div>`'s HTML using a template literal that includes the necessary HTML structure to display the information. The CSS makes the page responsive for mobile viewing by using a media query.

Please remember that you need to replace 'https://myapi.com' endpoint with the URL of your API. If this is not a localhost or a server controlled by you, make sure it has Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) enabled, or the code will fail because of the same-origin policy enforced by browsers.
