The concept of momentum in trading is a bit weird to me.
It seems to me to be measuring a first derivative or first difference of price, or something in that ballpark depending on the exact formulae used.  I would describe this as the velocity of a security's price.  I would expect momentum to require some concept of mass, and that momentum would be given by mass $*$ velocity.
Is there a notion of mass (perhaps related to trading volume) in finance?  If not, is trading momentum just the quantity that one would expect to be called velocity?  If so, why is it called momentum instead of velocity?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The term "momentum" in financial trading is indeed derived from the realm of physics, but the concepts don't exactly map one to one. In physics, momentum equals mass times velocity, as you said. But in trading, momentum often just refers to the speed and direction of the price movements, similar to velocity in physics.

The reason that momentum doesn't have a notion of "mass" as in physics is probably due to its definition and usage in finance. In the world of investing, momentum measures the rate of the rise or fall in stock prices or market indexes. Momentum is typically used as a trending indicator that can help traders identify potential price breakouts or reversals.

Using a physics analogy, you could say that in finance "momentum = velocity" (rate of price change), and "mass" could metaphorically correlate to the capital invested or perhaps volume of shares traded. But this isn't a universally accepted interpretation, it may vary between different markets, strategies or financial instruments.

To sum up, why is it called momentum instead of velocity? The term has probably been borrowed from physics due to the similar idea of observing a trend or movement force, and don't represent exactly the same concepts in both fields.
<|end|>


