Formulate an algorithm in C++ to find the maximum and minimum elements in an unsorted array. Additionally, find the second largest and second smallest elements in the array without sorting it. Keep the time complexity of your solution to O(n).

arr = {1, 7, 4, 2, 9, 6, 5}
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can use four variables to keep track of the maximum, second maximum, minimum and second minimum elements in your array. Here is a simple C++ code snippet that does it:

```C++
#include<iostream>
#include<climits>
using namespace std;

void findElements(int arr[], int n) {
   int max = INT_MIN, max2 = INT_MIN;
   int min = INT_MAX, min2 = INT_MAX;

   for(int i=0; i<n; i++) {
      // update max and max2
      if(arr[i] > max) {
         max2 = max;
         max = arr[i];
      } else if(arr[i] > max2 && arr[i] != max)
         max2 = arr[i];

      // update min and min2
      if(arr[i] < min) {
         min2 = min;
         min = arr[i];
      } else if(arr[i] < min2 && arr[i] != min)
         min2 = arr[i];
   }

   cout << "Max = " << max << ", Second Max = " << max2 << endl;
   cout << "Min = " << min << ", Second Min = " << min2 << endl;
}

int main() {
   int arr[] = {1, 7, 4, 2, 9, 6, 5};
   int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
   findElements(arr, n);
   return 0;
}
```

In the code above, we traverse the given array only once so the time complexity is O(n). We use two variables max and max2 to keep track of the maximum and the second maximum elements and two variables min and min2 to keep track of the minimum and the second minimum elements. For each element in the array, we update max, max2, min and min2 accordingly. At the end of the loop, we have our maximum, second maximum, minimum and second smallest elements.
<|end|>


