&lt;p&gt;In the extraterrestrial realm of J, a complete revolution around its star takes $D$ days, defining a year. The tradition of holiday declaration is governed by two distinct principles:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;&lt;li&gt;Upon the commencement of a new Emperor's rule, his birth anniversary is declared a holiday for all subsequent years.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;In the event that a day $d$ is sandwiched between two holidays, it is also designated as a holiday.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;&lt;p&gt;Initially, the calendar is devoid of any holidays. Let's denote $E(D)$ as the anticipated count of Emperors required to transform all the days of the year into holidays, under the assumption that their birthdates are independently and uniformly distributed over the $D$ days of the year.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Provided are the values $E(2)=1$, $E(5)=31/6$, $E(365)\approx 1174.3501$.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Your task is to compute $E(10000)$, ensuring that your answer is rounded to the nearest four decimal places.&lt;/p&gt;
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Assumptions are that the birthdates of the emperors are independently and uniformly distributed across the "D" days of the year. As a result, birthdays and the subsequent holidays are randomly distributed. 

Approach: 

One straightforward method is to model as a discrete Markov chain with D states denoting how many unique holidays have been formed and calculate the expected value of transitions. 

This would entail iterating through all status and calculating the probability of moving to the new status.

Let's denote E[D] as the anticipated count of Emperors required to transform all the days of the year into holidays. We have the recurrences:

E[D] = 1 + (D-1)/D * E[D]

E[D] = 1 + (D-1)/D * (1 + (D-2)/(D-1) * (E[D-1]))

E[D] = 1 + (D-1)/D * (1 + (D-2)/(D-1) * (1 + (D-3)/(D-2) * (E[D-2])))

and so on ...

Code Snippet in Python:

```python
def D_expected_emperors(day):
    E = 0
    for d in range(day, 0, -1):
        E = 1 + E * d / (d + 1)
    return E

day = 10000
print("%.4f" % D_expected_emperors(day))
```

This Python program calculates E[D] by using a loop to calculate from the back and ends up iterating 10,000 times which is feasible. The result will be displayed rounded to the nearest four decimal places.
<|end|>


