> 
  [Avoiding repeated constants in CSS](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/30170/avoiding-repeated-constants-in-css)  



We have some "theme colors" that are reused in our CSS sheet.

Is there a way to set a variable and then reuse it?

E.g.


.css
OurColor: Blue

H1 { 
 color:OurColor;
}
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, CSS has a feature called "Custom Properties", more commonly referred to as CSS variables. Here's how you could use them to resolve your issue:

```css
:root { /* Declare the variable in a universal scope */
  --our-color: blue;
}

h1 {
  color: var(--our-color);
}
```

The `:root` selector sets the CSS variable in a worldwide scope, which means you can use it anywhere in your CSS. The `var(--our-color)` function is how you then access the variable (you just replace `--our-color` with the name of your variable). 

You can also scope CSS variables to other selectors. In the following example, only `h1` elements inside `.class` would be blue:

```css
.class {
  --our-color: blue;
}

.class h1 {
  color: var(--our-color);
}
```

CSS variables are incredibly powerful and well-supported in modern browsers.
<|end|>


