<|user|>
I have some binary data inside a mongodb collection. I would like to clear a little bit the too heavy data. How can I sort the top 10 binary data (biggest size on disk)?
Thanks
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Unfortunately MongoDB does not provide a direct way to sort documents by their size on disk. The bson size of individual document can be measured, but this isn't directly associated to the size on disk due to factors like indexing, storage engine, compression, etc.

But if you want to sort the documents by the size of a single binary field, you can write a script to go through your collection, calculate the size (length) of this field for each document, store it in a new field in the same document and then query to sort based on that new field.

Here's a JavaScript script you can run in MongoDB shell for doing it:

```javascript
db.yourCollection.find().forEach(function(doc) {
    var binaryFieldSize = Object.bsonsize(doc.binaryData);
    db.yourCollection.updateOne({_id: doc._id}, {$set: {binaryFieldSize: binaryFieldSize}});
});

// Now you can use this new field for sorting:
db.yourCollection.find().sort({binaryFieldSize: -1}).limit(10)
```

In the script above you need to replace `yourCollection` with your actual collection name, and `binaryData` with actual binary field name. This script adds to each document in the collection a new field `binaryFieldSize`, measuring the size of binary data, and then uses that field to find 10 documents with largest binary field.

Remember that scripts like this could take a lot of time and resources to run on large collections. Always backup your data before performing operations like this.

Also, keep in mind that these size measurements are not directly related to the size of documents on disk as MongoDB applies compression and other optimizations to storage layer. To manage disk space utilization, check MongoDB's database commands like `compact`, `repairDatabase`, etc.

In addition, if you host MongoDB with Atlas, you can use its built-in metrics to monitor your disk usage. You can also use `$collStats` aggregation stage to get statistics about a collection, including its size on the disk.
<|end|>


