Create a web application for searching articles from Wikipedia.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Creating a web application for searching articles from Wikipedia involves a number of steps and requires knowledge of a number of different programming languages and tools, including HTML/CSS for the frontend, a server-side language such as Python or JavaScript (with Node.js), and the Wikipedia API to fetch articles from Wikipedia.

Here's a basic outline of how you could create this application:

1. **Design Your Frontend**

You'll need to create a user interface with search bar where users can enter the topic they want to search for. This could be a simple HTML/CSS form.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Wikipedia Search App</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Wikipedia Search App</h1>
    <form id="search-form">
        <input type="text" id="search-input" placeholder="Search Wikipedia" />
        <button type="submit">Search</button>
    </form>
    <div id="result"></div>
</body>
</html>
```

2. **Handle User Input**

Next, you need to use JavaScript to get the user's input when they submit the form and use that input to make a request to the Wikipedia API. 

```js
document.getElementById('search-form').addEventListener('submit', function(event) {
    event.preventDefault();
    var searchQuery = document.getElementById('search-input').value;
    getWikiData(searchQuery);
});
```

3. **Make Requests to the Wikipedia API**

You can make requests to the Wikipedia API using the `fetch` function in JavaScript. Here's a basic example of how you could implement the `getWikiData` function:

```js
function getWikiData(searchQuery) {
    var url = `https://en.wikipedia.org/w/api.php?action=query&list=search&srsearch=${searchQuery}&format=json`;
    fetch(url)
        .then((response) => {
            if (response.ok) {
                return response.json();
            } else {
                throw new Error('ERROR: ' + response.statusText);
            }
        })
        .then((responseData) => {
            displayData(responseData);
        })
        .catch((error) => {
            console.log(error);
        });
}
```

4. **Display the Result**

Finally, you need to display the result on your webpage. You can do this by changing the inner HTML of the result div using a function like this:

```js
function displayData(data) {
    var resultElement = document.getElementById('result');
    resultElement.innerHTML = '';
    var searchResults = data.query.search;
    for (var i = 0; i < searchResults.length; i++) {
        resultElement.innerHTML += `
            <h2>${searchResults[i].title}</h2>
            <p>${searchResults[i].snippet}</p>
            <button onclick="window.open('https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=${searchResults[i].pageid}', '_blank')">Read More</button>
        `;
    }
}
```

This is a very basic outline of how you can create a web application to search for articles from Wikipedia.

Remember to cross-check the CORS concept while fetching data from an external site like Wikipedia. To bypass the CORS policy in the development environment, you can use a CORS proxy server.
<|end|>


