I am aware that YOLO (v1-5) is a real-time object detection model with moderately good overall prediction performance. I know that UNet and variants are efficient semantic segmentation models that are also fast and have good prediction performance.
I cannot find any resources comparing the inference speed differences between these two approaches. It seems to me that semantic segmentation is clearly a more difficult problem, to classify each pixel in an image, than object detection, drawing bounding boxes around objects in the image.
Does anyone have good resources for this comparison? Or a very good explanation to why one is computationally more demanding that the other?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The computational demand of either approach depends upon several factors including but not limited to the implementation, model architecture details, input image size, etc. 

Object detection approaches like YOLO, despite being real-time, can consume more computation for higher accuracy. The computation in YOLO is more or less uniform throughout the image because it operates on full images (rather than patches or other kinds of regions). This might be more than what is necessary for some applications, especially when the objects of interest are sparse and/or small.

Semantic segmentation approaches like UNet, classify each pixel in an image, requiring a high level of detail. This often requires a high number of feature maps and therefore more computational resources. However, this approach scores in terms of delivering a more detailed output than object detection models like YOLO. Hence, depending on the application, the one with better performance may not be the one with lower computational demand.

Generally speaking, it is expected that semantic segmentation models would require more computation than object detection models. However, there are many exceptions to this, for instance, using semantic segmentation models on low-resolution images, or making architecture optimizations to reduce inference time, and so on.

Unfortunately, I couldn't find a direct comparison but you might find the following resources helpful for further research:

1. Papers With Code: Object Detection (https://paperswithcode.com/task/object-detection) and Semantic Segmentation (https://paperswithcode.com/task/semantic-segmentation) sections will provide you with a wide range of models, their relevant papers, and their performance benchmarks.

2. Google AI Blog's post on "High-Resolution Image Synthesis and Semantic Manipulation with Conditional GANs" (https://ai.googleblog.com/2017/10/high-resolution-image-synthesis-and.html) explains some of the computational implications.
<|end|>


