Develop a CSS rule to assign a responsive line height based on viewport size and adjustable letter spacing of 1-3px to all header elements on a webpage. This rule should also include a fall-back styling for browsers that do not support viewport units or have JavaScript disabled.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can achieve this by simply using CSS media queries, viewport units, and letter-spacing property. Here is a sample CSS rule to accomplish this:

```css
h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 {
    line-height: 1.5em; /* Fallback line-height */
    letter-spacing: 1px; /* fallback letter-spacing */
}

/* Small devices (landscape phones, 576px and up) */
@media (min-width: 576px) { 
  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 {
    line-height: 2vh; /* 2% of viewport height */
    letter-spacing: 1px;
  }
}

/* Medium devices (tablets, 768px and up) */
@media (min-width: 768px) {
  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 {
    line-height: 3vh; /* 3% of viewport height */
    letter-spacing: 2px;
  }
}

/* Large devices (desktops, 992px and up) */
@media (min-width: 992px) {
  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 {
    line-height: 4vh; /* 4% of viewport height */
    letter-spacing: 3px;
  }
}
```

In the above CSS, I have used `vh`(viewport height units) and `px`(pixel units) for line-height and letter-spacing respectively. Please adjust the values based on your requirements.

Browsers that do not support viewport units will simply ignore the properties and will use the fallback values that are in `px`. Note, this solution does not require JavaScript.
<|end|>


