Develop a C language program that is able to sort arrays of not only integers, but also strings, either in ascending or descending alphanumeric sequence. This should be based on the user's input preference. Additionally, the program must be robust enough to handle edge cases such as special characters, null values, and repetition of values in the array. Given an example array for testing the functionality: [2, 1, 4, 3, "abc", "def", null, "!@#", "abc"], and the chosen direction (ascending or descending).
<|end|>
<|asst|>
C is a statically typed language, which means you can't directly store different data types in a common array. But we can achieve the sorting through separate cases within different arrays.
This example shows how to sort integer and string arrays independently, not intermixed. Although C allows different data types to be stored in structures or unions, sorting such a mixed type could not be meaningfully interpreted and hence isn't usually done.

Here's an example of a C program with functionalities to sort an integer array and a string array separately. 

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

#define MAXLEN 100 /* Max word length */
#define NUMWORDS 5 /* Number of words */

int sort_direction; /* 0 for ascending, 1 for descending */

int cmpfunc (const void * a, const void * b) {
   if(sort_direction == 0) /* Ascending */
      return strcmp(*(char **)a, *(char **)b);
   else           /* Descending */
      return -strcmp(*(char**)a, *(char**)b);  
}

int icmpfunc(const void *a, const void *b) {
   int ia = *((int*)a);
   int ib = *((int*)b);
   if(sort_direction == 0) /* Ascending */
       return (ia - ib);
   else
       return -(ia - ib); /* Descending */
}

int main() {
   int iArr[] = {5, 2, 4, 3, 1};
   char *sArr[] = {"abc", "def", "ghi", "jkl", "mno"};

   int num, i;
   
   printf("Enter 0 for sort in Ascending, 1 for Descending: ");
   scanf("%d", &sort_direction);
   
   qsort(iArr, 5, sizeof(int), icmpfunc);
   printf("\nInteger array after sorting:\n");
   for( num = 0 ; num < 5; num++ ) {
      printf("%d ", iArr[num]);
   }
   
   qsort(sArr, 5, sizeof(char*), cmpfunc);
   printf("\nString array after sorting:\n");
   for( num = 0 ; num < 5; num++ ) {
      printf("%s ", sArr[num]);
   }

   return 0;
}
```
This C program uses `qsort` function from C standard library to sort an array of integers and an array of strings separately. `icmpfunc` and `cmpfunc` are comparator functions that compare two elements and decide their order during sorting. User is prompted to enter the sort direction.

Remember, handling "null" values or any special characters will depend on the context. If by "null" you mean a NULL pointer, your logic can check before printing or comparing. If by "null" you mean the string "null", then it shouldn't cause any issue. Special characters are also compared just like any other regular characters, using their ASCII values. 

For repetition of values, the same value will appear together after sorting.
<|end|>


