Construct an SQL command to extract detailed data on literary works archived within a bibliographical repository. Include the capability to perform complex join operations, data manipulation, and subqueries. Use the command to handle various types of data such as Strings, Integers, Dates and Blobs within a large database with at least a million records, and demonstrate efficient use of indexing to optimize query performance.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Assuming a hypothetical structure of tables that are typically seen in a bibliographical repository, I will write some SQL commands that carry out your requirements.

Tables:
- Books: ID (integer, primary key), Title (string), PublicationDate (date), PDF (blob)
- Authors: ID (integer, primary key), Name (string)
- BookAuthors: BookID (integer, foreign key), AuthorID (integer, foreign key)

Firstly, it's crucial to have indexes for performance improvement. If Books table contain millions of rows, proper indexing can dramatically increase the performance of data retrieval operations. 

```sql
-- An index on 'Title' of the Books table for faster search on title 
CREATE INDEX idx_title ON Books(Title); 

-- An index on 'Name' of the Authors table for faster search on author's name
CREATE INDEX idx_name ON Authors(Name); 

-- Indexes on foreign keys in BookAuthors for faster join operations
CREATE INDEX idx_book ON BookAuthors(BookID); 
CREATE INDEX idx_author ON BookAuthors(AuthorID);
```

SQL command to extract detailed information on literary works:

```sql
SELECT Books.ID, Books.Title, Books.PublicationDate, Authors.Name
FROM Books
INNER JOIN BookAuthors ON Books.ID = BookAuthors.BookID
INNER JOIN Authors ON BookAuthors.AuthorID = Authors.ID
WHERE Books.Title LIKE '%some title%'
ORDER BY Books.PublicationDate DESC;
```

This SQL command retrieves all the works that have 'some title' in their title. It's using a complex join operation to get author details for every book, and it's returning various types of data : Integer (ID), String (Name, Title), Date (PublicationDate).

To retrieve blob data (books' PDFs), you would append `, Books.PDF` to the select clause, but be very careful with retrieving such large data types. If you select millions of records, you're likely to consume a lot of memory.

Sub-queries can be used like this:

```sql
SELECT Books.ID, Books.Title, Books.PublicationDate, Authors.Name
FROM Books
INNER JOIN BookAuthors ON Books.ID = BookAuthors.BookID
INNER JOIN Authors ON BookAuthors.AuthorID = Authors.ID
WHERE Authors.ID IN (SELECT ID FROM Authors WHERE Name LIKE '%some author%')
ORDER BY Books.PublicationDate DESC;
```

This sub-query finds all books written by authors with 'some author' in their name. 

Hope this helps, let me know if you need help with more complex requirements. For further performance optimization, database-specific techniques like partitioning (if supported) can be used.
<|end|>


