In my ubuntu server I use fail2ban to block repeated failed ssh logins, when fail2ban detects this I want to execute a shell script, is this possible to do?
My ssh jail:

[ssh]
findtime = 10m
maxretry = 3


My shell script is in this location:

/home/MyUser/scripts/send-mail


Also, how can I get the banned IP? I want to send that information too if possible
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, it is possible to extend the default behavior of fail2ban using an action file. Follow the steps below to accomplish that;

1. Create a new action file.
   
   For example, you could create the file at this location: `/etc/fail2ban/action.d/sendemail.conf`

2. In this file, you should include the following - replace `/path/to/your/script.sh` with your actual script path:

   ```bash
   [Definition]
   actionstart =
   actionstop =
   actioncheck =
   actionban = /home/MyUser/scripts/send-mail <ip>
   actionunban =

   [Init]
   ``` 

3. Then, in your ssh jail configuration, you can link to the new action file:

   ```
   [ssh]
   enabled  = true
   port     = ssh
   filter   = sshd
   logpath  = /var/log/auth.log
   maxretry = 3
   findtime = 10m
   bantime  = 1w
   action   = sendemail
   ```

4. Reload fail2ban service

   Now reload the fail2ban service using the following command so that it takes the new configuration into account:

   ```bash
   sudo systemctl reload fail2ban 
   ```

Regarding the banned IP, the `<ip>` placeholder will be substituted with the actual IP address in the `actionban` command, so your script will receive it as a parameter.

Just make sure that your script is capable of accepting an argument. In your shell script, you can access this argument with the `$1` in the variable:

```bash
#!/bin/bash
BANNED_IP=$1
# Rest of your script
```
<|end|>


