        if freq_counter[unique_elements[left]] < freq_counter[unique_elements[right]]:
            if limit < freq_counter[unique_elements[left]]:
                break
            limit -= freq_counter[unique_elements[left]]
            changes += freq_counter[unique_elements[left]]
            right -= 1
        else:
            if limit < freq_counter[unique_elements[right]]:
                break
            limit -= freq_counter[unique_elements[right]]
            changes += freq_counter[unique_elements[right]]
            left += 1
    
    if left > right:
        return changes - limit // 2
    else:
        return changes
please include comments in the translated code to help me understand it.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a translation of your Python code into the C language. Python's dictionaries, list comprehension, and sorted function and other features come with built-in overhead, which is usually not an issue because Python is not usually used for time-critical operations. However, when translating to C, the resulting code can often be more complicated and verbose because C is a lower-level language without many of the data-handling abstractions provided by Python.

```C

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

// C does not support generics (like List[int] in Python),
// therefore to ensure that the function could handle various list sizes,
// one of the parameters is the length of the list.
int smallest_change (int arr[], int n, int limit) {
    int freq_counter[50] = { 0 };
    int unique_elements[50];
    int count = 0;
    
    // Calculating frequency of all elements
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        freq_counter[arr[i]]++;
    
    // Fetching unique elements
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        if (freq_counter[arr[i]] != 0) {
            unique_elements[count] = arr[i];
            count++;
        }
    
    // Sorting unique elements array
    int temp;
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)
        for (int j = 0; j < count; j++){
            if(unique_elements[i] < unique_elements[j]){
                temp = unique_elements[i];
                unique_elements[i] = unique_elements[j];
                unique_elements[j] = temp;
            }
        }
    
    int changes = 0;
    int left = 0, right = count - 1;
    
    while (left <= right) {
        if (freq_counter[unique_elements[left]] < freq_counter[unique_elements[right]]) {
            if (limit < freq_counter[unique_elements[left]])
                return changes;
            limit -= freq_counter[unique_elements[left]];
            changes += freq_counter[unique_elements[left]];
            right--;
        } else {
            if (limit < freq_counter[unique_elements[right]])
                return changes;
            limit -= freq_counter[unique_elements[right]];
            changes += freq_counter[unique_elements[right]];
            left++;
        }
    }
    
    if (left > right)
        return changes - limit / 2; 
    else 
        return changes;  
}
```
Please note that the this c code is not the equivalent of python version due to absence of dictionary type data structure in C and also the size of arrays is kept constant which in python can be len(arr). You'd have to add more code to count the unique elements and then allocate enough memory for the unique_elements array.
<|end|>


