I want to call a web service, but I won't know the url till runtime.

Whats the best way to get the web reference in, without actually committing to a url.

What about having 1 client hit the same web service on say 10 different domains?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To call a web service without knowing the URL until runtime, you need to create a dynamic web service client. This concept is also known as a proxy class that acts as a surrogate for the web service.

Here is a basic example of how you can achieve this in C#.

Firstly, you will need to add the web reference, without knowing the URL:

1. In Visual Studio, right-click on your project > Add > Service Reference.
2. In the window, click "Advanced..." to find "Add Web Reference."
3. Type in any URL, as it won't matter. We just need the wsdl / soap information.
4. After the service is added, then you'll use `System.Web.Services.Protocols.SoapHttpClientProtocol` class to call the dynamic URL.

Here is the dynamic URL setup example:

```csharp
string dynamicUrl = "http://runtime_url.com/service.asmx";
MyWebService myService = new MyWebService();
myService.Url = dynamicUrl;
```

If you want to hit the same web service on 10 different domains, loop through the domain list, update the URL dynamically, and call the web service:

```csharp
string[] domainList = ... // List of your domain urls
for (int i = 0; i < domainList.Length; i++)
{
    string dynamicUrl = domainList[i] + "/service.asmx";
    MyWebService myService = new MyWebService();
    myService.Url = dynamicUrl;
    // Call the web service methods
}
```
The important part to remember here is that the web service on each of the 10 domains must have the same WSDL (Web Service Description Language). This is due to the fact that your client was programmed to interface with a specific WSDL. If the WSDL doesn't match, your calls may not function as expected.
<|end|>


