Design a Python function that identifies the longest recurring non-overlapping substring in a given text string. The function should also return the starting and ending index positions of this recurring substring. If there are multiple substrings of the same length, the function should return all of them in the order they appear in the string. 

Additionally, the function should be able to handle large strings efficiently, and should be able to identify substrings that recur in different parts of the string, not just adjacent to each other. 

The function should also be able to handle special characters and numbers in the string. 

The function should also be able to handle and identify substrings in multiple languages, including languages that use non-Latin alphabets. 

Finally, write test cases to validate the functionality of your function.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a Python function that should do the trick. It does so by checking all substrings in a string, keeping track of the longest one, and building a dictionary to keep track of which substrings have been seen. For efficiency, it avoids checking overlapping substrings.

```python
def longest_recurring_substring(text):
    length = len(text)
    result = []
    max_length = 0
    substrings = {}

    for i in range(length):
        for j in range(i + max_length, length):
            substring = text[i:j + 1]
            if substring in substrings and substrings[substring] < i:
                result = [i, j]
                max_length = j - i + 1
            else:
                substrings[substring] = j

    longest_substrings = [text[result[0]:result[1] + 1]]

    for i in range(result[0] + 1, length - max_length + 1):
        if text[i:i + max_length] == longest_substrings[0]:
            longest_substrings.append(text[i:i + max_length])

    return longest_substrings, [(text.index(s), text.index(s) + len(s) - 1) for s in longest_substrings]
```

Here are some test cases to test out the function:

```python
assert longest_recurring_substring("abcabc") == (["abc"], [(0, 2), (3, 5)])
assert longest_recurring_substring("abcabcabc") == (["abc"], [(0, 2), (3, 5), (6, 8)])
assert longest_recurring_substring("abcabcabcabc") == (["abcabc"], [(0, 5), (6, 11)])
assert longest_recurring_substring("abcdabcdabcdabcd") == (["abcdabcd"], [(0, 7), (8, 15)])
assert longest_recurring_substring("a") == ([], [])
assert longest_recurring_substring("") == ([], [])
assert longest_recurring_substring("aa") == (["a"], [(0, 0), (1, 1)])
assert longest_recurring_substring("abcdefg") == ([], [])

# Test the function with non-english language and special characters
assert longest_recurring_substring("ТекстТекст") == (["Текст"], [(0, 4), (5, 9)])
assert longest_recurring_substring("中文中文") == (["中文"], [(0, 1), (2, 3)])
assert longest_recurring_substring("##$$##$$") == (["##$$"], [(0, 3), (4, 7)])
assert longest_recurring_substring("1234512345") == (["12345"], [(0, 4), (5, 9)]) 
```

This function should also run efficiently on large inputs because it uses a dictionary to remember which substrings it has seen, rather than repeatedly iterating through the input string. The time complexity is O(n^2), where n is the size of the input string.
<|end|>


