Given an array [2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128], write a function that locates the fifth element of the array. After locating the fifth element, the function should return an array containing every nth element starting from the fifth element until the end of the array, where n is the value of the fifth element.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a Python solution to your problem:

```Python
def array_slice(array):
    # get the fifth element
    fifth_elem = array[4]
    
    # get every nth element starting from the fifth element
    result_array = array[4::fifth_elem]
    
    return result_array

print(array_slice([2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128]))  # outputs: [32]
```

In this python function, "array[4]" is used to access the fifth element of the array. 

"array[4::fifth_elem]" is a slice of the array that starts at the fifth element (index 4) and steps forward by the value of the fifth element (skip `fifth_elem-1` elements between each included element).

"::" is the slicing operator, the number before "::" is the start index, the number after "::" is the step value.

However, the array has only three elements after the fifth element. So, the slice "array[4::fifth_elem]" only contains the fifth element (32). If you apply the function on an array with more elements, the function will return more than one element.
<|end|>


