<|user|>
Given a list of strings (e.g., myList = ["item1", "item2", "item3"]), devise a method to eliminate each string within it, ensuring the resulting list is devoid of any original elements.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In python, there is a built-in method called clear() which will remove all the items from a list.

Here is an example below.

```python
def clear_list(myList):
    myList.clear()
    return myList

myList = ["item1", "item2", "item3"]
print(clear_list(myList))  # Output: []
```

In the above python code, we defined a function named clear_list that takes a list as its parameter. All items in the list are removed using the clear() function. Finally, the modified list, which is now empty, is returned. 

When you run this code with a list ["item1", "item2", "item3"] as input, it will return an empty list [], as this list has been cleared of all its elements. 

Please note that changes made by clear() method are done in-place, meaning that it modifies the list it is called on. Though it returns None, the list passed to it will be empty after the call. If you want to keep the original list, you would need to make a copy before calling this method.

Finally, this method will not work if the list is immutable. For example, if the list is a tuple or a string, you will get an error because these types can't be modified after they are created.
<|end|>


