Develop a function in C++ to find the median of a given array after sorting it in ascending order.
arr = [9,3,2,6,1]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
```
#include <iostream>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

float findMedian(int arr[], int n) 
{ 
    sort(arr, arr+n); 
  
    if (n % 2 != 0) 
        return (float)arr[n/2]; 
  
    return (float)(arr[(n-1)/2] + arr[n/2])/2.0; 
} 
  
int main() 
{ 
    int arr[] = { 9, 3, 2, 6, 1 }; 
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]); 
    cout << "Median = " << findMedian(arr, n) << endl; 
    return 0; 
} 
```
The program first sorts the array using the C++ STL sort() function. If the number of elements n is odd, it returns the value at the middle index. If n is even, it returns the average of the values at the middle and the index before it. The result is typecasted as float to handle cases where the average can be a decimal number. 

In this case, after sorting the array becomes {1,2,3,6,9}, and since there are 5 elements, the median is 3. So, it outputs "Median = 3".
The time complexity of this approach is dominated by the sort() function, which is O(n log n), where n is the number of elements in the array.
<|end|>


