<|user|>
When a research design includes treatment and control group and the number of samples in the treatment group far exceeds that in the control group due to the nature of a data set, the treatment effect from a regression result becomes not significant.
I wanted to do a bit of experiment here by randomly sampling the treatment group side so that the number of treated samples reduces to a reasonable numbers that are comparable to the control group. After running the same regression, treatment effect becomes significant.
What would be the underlying reason that generates such a difference? Is this because the distribution of the treatment group became more randomly distributed?
I would try a matching algorithm such as Propensity Score Matching (PSM) instead, but there are not matching covariates between treatment and control group due to the nature of the limited data availability.
I wonder random sampling makes sense as the other option. Some experted knowledge would be very appreciated.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Random sampling from your treatment group to match the size of your control group may introduce a source of bias into your study design. Assuming that your treatment group is not a random sample to begin with (which is usually the reason why treatment groups are larger than control groups), conducting random sampling from your treatment group is equivalent to introducing a source of random error that will affect your results.

Random sampling from the treatment group can potentially change the group's characteristics, given that different subsets of this group can be significantly different from others. This is especially the case if the treatment group is large and varied, as different random samples could yield different subsets with different characteristics. This can result in your groups not being perfectly equivalent at baseline – the underlying principle of a controlled experiment.

If your treatment group is larger than your control group, it might also be that the study lacks statistical power – the number of available observations is insufficient for the statistical test to detect a significant difference. In this case, reducing the size of the treatment group might make the treatment effect appear more significant, but this effect is spurious. It's like picking and choosing your data to get the result you want which isn't good statistical practice. 

One potential solution might be to use statistical correction techniques designed to adjust for varying sample sizes and/or imbalances in the proportion of cases in each group. Another potential solution might be to adjust the regression model in a way that puts a lower weight on the treatment group so the overall balance is maintained. Or, if feasible, increase the sample size of the control group. 

Ultimately, the choice of an appropriate method should depend on a careful consideration of the specifics of your research design, the characteristics of your data, and the assumptions and limitations inherent to each methodology. Consulting with a statistician may be advisable.
<|end|>


