Use CSS to center a div inside a parent div, without using `flexbox` or `grid` properties. Additionally, ensure that the child div remains centered even when the window is resized.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In order to achieve that, we can use a combination of absolute positioning and transform methods. Here's how to accomplish this using CSS:

HTML:
```html
<div class="parent">
    <div class="child">
        <!-- Content goes here -->
    </div>
</div>
```

CSS:
```css
.parent {
    position: relative; /* Establish a coordinate system for child */
    width: 100%;  /* Can be any percentage or fixed value */
    height: 100%; /* Can be any percentage or fixed value */
}

.child {
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%); /* Centers the child element */
    /* Add the dimensions of the child element below */
    width: 50%;  /* Can be any percentage or fixed value */
    height: 50%; /* Can be any percentage or fixed value */
}
```

The `.child` div will be centered within `.parent` div both horizontally and vertically, and it will remain centered even when the browser window is resized. The `top` and `left` properties position the `.child` div's top-left corner at the center of the `.parent`. The `transform` property is used to shift it back by 50% of its own height and width, effectively centering the div within its parent. 

This solution is widely supported and do not require modern CSS layout techniques like Flexbox or Grid.
<|end|>


