Formulate a JavaScript function that can ascertain whether a particular string fulfills the criteria of a palindrome. Expand this concept to incorporate not only the basic definition of a palindrome, but also consider edge cases where capitalization, spaces, punctuation marks, and Unicode characters are involved.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a JavaScript function to check if a given text qualifies as a palindrome. This function first normalizes the text to lowercase, removes all the non-alphanumeric characters (including spaces and punctuation), and then checks if it satisfies the criteria of being a palindrome (i.e. it reads the same forwards and backwards):

```javascript
function isPalindrome(text) {
    // Normalize the text
    text = text.toLowerCase();
    text = text.normalize("NFD").replace(/[\u0300-\u036f]/g, ""); // Remove unicode characters
    text = text.replace(/[^a-z0-9]/g, ''); // Remove non-alphanumeric characters

    // Compare the text with its reverse
    let reversedText = text.split('').reverse().join('');
    return text === reversedText;
}
```
This function is very flexible - it can correctly handle all of the following inputs:
```
isPalindrome("Able was I, ere I saw Elba.") // Returns true
isPalindrome("A man, a plan, a canal, Panama!") // Returns true
isPalindrome("No 'x' in Nixon") // Returns true
isPalindrome("Not a palindrome") // Returns false
``` 

In the code, `normalize("NFD")` is used to decompose combined graphemes into the combination of simple ones. The `/[\u0300-\u036f]/g` is a regular expression that matches all the characters that are in the unicode range from 0300 to 036F, which are the combining diacritics. These two together remove accented characters.

The regular expression `/[^a-z0-9]/g` matches all non-alphanumeric characters, which are then removed from the text. The remainder of the function checks if the text and its reversed version are the same.
<|end|>


