Create a set of transformation rules to convert a complex, valid XML document with nested elements, attributes, and namespaces to a well-structured HTML5 document. Additionally, devise a methodology to handle XML entities and CDATA sections during the conversion. Validate the HTML5 document for compliance with the W3C standards.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This problem can be practically solved by creating a program using Python and some libraries that help handle and manipulate XML and HTML data like xml.etree.ElementTree, BeautifulSoup, and html5lib.

Here is a simple implementation. It has limitations including that it can't entirely handle namespaces, xml entities, and CDATA sections. For complex transformations and elaborate specifications, further coding is needed. This should be used as the foundation for a larger, more complex program.

```python
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET
from bs4 import BeautifulSoup

def prettify(html_soup):
    return html_soup.prettify()

def xml_to_html(xml_content):
    xml_tree = ET.ElementTree(ET.fromstring(xml_content))
    html_soup = BeautifulSoup(features="html.parser")
    html_tree = html_soup.new_tag('html')

    def iterate_xml(xml_elem, html_parent):
        html_elem = html_soup.new_tag(xml_elem.tag)
        html_elem.string = xml_elem.text
        for name, value in xml_elem.attrib.items():
            html_elem[name] = value
        html_parent.append(html_elem)
        for xml_child in xml_elem:
            iterate_xml(xml_child, html_elem)

    for xml_elem in xml_tree.iter():
        iterate_xml(xml_elem, html_tree)

    html_soup.append(html_tree)
    return prettify(html_soup)

# example usage
xml_data = """
    <note>
    <to>Tove</to>
    <from>Jani</from>
    <heading>Reminder</heading>
    <body>Don't forget me this weekend!</body>
    </note>
"""
print(xml_to_html(xml_data))
```

This program will create a BeautifulSoup HTML document. For each XML element in the input XML document, it will create a new HTML element with the same tag name, copying over children, attributes and text data. Nested elements will be handled recursively.

To handle XML entities and CDATA sections during the conversion, we can use dedicated xml libraries like lxml that provide support for these features. Below is CDATA section conversion sample.

```python
from lxml import etree
root = etree.fromstring("""<root><![CDATA[Some content]]></root>""")
cdata_text = root.text
```

As for the HTML5 document validation to ensure compliance with W3C standards, there are libraries such as html5lib and online services like W3C Markup Validation Service.

The Python code to validate HTML5 using html5lib is simply:

```python
import html5lib

def is_valid_html5(html_content):
    document = html5lib.parse(html_content)

# usage
html_content = "<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>Test</title></head><body><p>Test</p></body></html>"
print(is_valid_html5(html_content))  # True if no exception is raised
```

If the HTML document is not compliant with HTML5 standards, the html5lib will raise an exception.
<|end|>


