Conform the ensuing mutable markup language to accord with HTML5 norms, keeping in mind the proper layout of elements and the standardized structure.
<body>
    <div><head>My Website</head></div>
</body>
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The existing mutable markup language you provided is not following the HTML5 norms. The div is not allowed in the body tag before the head tag.

The head tag is also incorrectly placed and should be out of the body tag. In HTML5, the body tag typically follows the head tag, and elements including div, h1, etc. are then included within the body. Here's the corrected code:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>My Website</title>
</head>
<body>
    <div>
        <h1>My Website</h1>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
```

Please note, apart from the improper placement of tags, there was also the issue with the usage of the `head` tag. The `head` tag in HTML is used to contain metadata about the HTML document, including linked files, inline styles, and information about the document. The `title` tag is used within the `head` tag to set the title of the page (displayed on the browser tab). 

For specifying a web page's main title, we should use `<h1>`-`<h6>` header tags inside the `<body>`, not the `head` tag. 

Also, note the usage of the `<!DOCTYPE html>` declaration at the start of the document, which is recommended for HTML5 to make sure the browser renders the page in standards mode.
<|end|>


