Using JavaScript, write a program to update a nested key in a complex object without using any built-in methods. Consider the object:

object = { "person": { "name": "john", "address": { "city": "New York", "country": "USA" } } }
<|end|>
<|asst|>
We will solve this problem using recursion. Recursion function would be a best fit for this task as nested keys can be at any level. It will check for key in object if it exists it will update it, if not it will go to next level of keys.
We will now write a recursive function to takes key path and new value as input and update the key value in nested object.

let's assume we are trying to update "name" under"person" with "David", so key path would look like ["person", "name"]:

```javascript
function updateNestedKey(obj, keyPath, newValue, index = 0) {
    for (let prop in obj) {
        if (prop === keyPath[index]) {
            if (index === keyPath.length - 1) {
                // found the nested key, update the value
                obj[prop] = newValue;
            } else {
                // continue the search to next level of keys 
                updateNestedKey(obj[prop], keyPath, newValue, index + 1)
            }
        }
    }
}

let object = { "person": { "name": "john", "address": { "city": "New York", "country": "USA" } } }
updateNestedKey(object, ["person", "name"], 'David');
console.log(object);
```
In the updated object, you should now see that the person's name is updated to "David".
<|end|>


