<|user|>
I'm trying to understand the functionality difference between an extension and a plugin in PostgreSQL.
With my limited understanding I found that an Extension is built with the source code and loaded using a control file using `Create extension extension_name command`.

# extension_name
comment = 'extension purpose'
default_version = '1.0'
module_pathname = '$libdir/extension_name'
relocatable = true


Whereas plugin, we can build same as an Extension but load the library by modifying the `postgresql.conf` file as follows.

shared_preload_libraries = '$libdir/plugin_name'    # (change requires restart)


Please correct my above understanding if I got it wrong. I want to understand how do we decide that feature should be made as extension or a Plugin in PostgreSQL? I couldn't find much on internet regarding Plugins, Is there any reference I can use to understand it better?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Your understanding is pretty much correct!

In PostgreSQL, "extensions" and "plugins" are somewhat interchangeable terms in broad usage, though from the perspective of the PostgreSQL system itself they do refer to slightly different concepts.

A PostgreSQL extension is a collection of SQL and procedural functions that provide new functionality beyond what PostgreSQL by default offers, such as new data types, functions, operators, index types, and more. They are defined by a control file that states their dependencies and load order, and ensures that they integrate smoothly into the PostgreSQL system. You can manage extensions using SQL commands like `CREATE EXTENSION`, `DROP EXTENSION`, etc.

A PostgreSQL "plugin" is a shared library that can be loaded into the PostgreSQL process. It doesn't add new SQL-level functionality like an extension does, instead it changes the behavior of the PostgreSQL server at a lower level. Examples would be adding custom authentication methods, supporting custom storage models, or implementing complex middleware like clustering extensions. Because they operate at a lower level, plugins usually aren't managed directly using SQL commands, but rather they are directly loaded into the PostgreSQL process either via the `shared_preload_libraries` directive, as you found, or via other hooks and methods depending on the plugin itself.

What this all comes to is that when you decide between using an extension vs a plugin will depend greatly on what functionality you want to add. Do you want to add new SQL-level functions or data types? Use an extension. Do you want to change how the server works at a low level? Use a plugin. Some might also use the terms interchangeably, even to include full-featured middleware that runs entirely outside of PostgreSQL but is specifically built to interface with it.

For better understanding, you can refer to the PostgreSQL official documentation:
- [Chapter 36. Extending SQL](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/xextend.html)
- [Chapter 35. Server Programming Interface](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/spi.html)

Note that concrete documentation on "plugins" is scarce because it's a more nebulous and lower-level concept than extensions, so much of the info will be found indirectly within other parts of the documentation, like how to write C functions, internal server architecture, etc.
<|end|>


