Implement a JavaScript function that morphs an array of objects 
Array: [{name: "John", age: 21}, {name: "Mary", age: 18}, {name: "Steve", age: 24}], considering edge cases and time/space efficiency. Comprehend the nuances of mutation and array transformations in JavaScript.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
I'm assuming you want to apply some transformation to each object in the array. I'll write a function that increments each person's age by one:

```javascript
function morphArray(objects) {
    // map creates a new array with the results of calling a function for every array element
    for (let i = 0; i < objects.length; i++) {
        // check if objects[i] has property age and it's a number
        if (objects[i].hasOwnProperty('age') && typeof(objects[i].age) === 'number') {
            objects[i].age += 1;
        }
    }
    return objects;
}

let arr = [{name: "John", age: 21}, {name: "Mary", age: 18}, {name: "Steve", age: 24}];

let result = morphArray(arr);
console.log(result);
```

This solution already takes edge cases into account: if the 'age' property doesn't exist or isn't a number, it just skips it. It also only uses a small, constant amount of extra space (for the `i` variable), so it's space efficient. It looks through each object in the array once, so its time complexity is O(n), where `n` is the size of the array.

Note that this function modifies the objects in the original array. If you want a version of the function that creates a new array and leaves the original array unchanged, you can use the `map` function:

```javascript
function morphArray(objects) {
    return objects.map(obj => {
        // Create a shallow copy of the object, to avoid modifying the originals
        let newObj = {...obj};
        // check if newObj has property age and it's a number
        if (newObj.hasOwnProperty('age') && typeof(newObj.age) === 'number') {
            newObj.age += 1;
        }
        return newObj;
    })
}

let arr = [{name: "John", age: 21}, {name: "Mary", age: 18}, {name: "Steve", age: 24}];

let result = morphArray(arr);
console.log(result);
```

Here, map creates a new array without affecting the original. However, it uses more space, since it needs to store the new array.
<|end|>


