<|end|>
<|asst|>
```
function sortAlphabetically(arr){
    return arr.sort((a, b) => a.toLowerCase().localeCompare(b.toLowerCase(), 'en', { numeric: true, ignorePunctuation: true }));
}

var arr = ["![Tony]", "123Steve", "peter", "Bru^&ce"];

console.log(sortAlphabetically(arr));
```

This script will sort the array in alphabetical order, ignoring case and special characters. The function `localeCompare` is used here as it provides more accurate sorting based on language norms rather than charcode values. The argument `'en'` specifies English as the sorting language. The `numeric: true` option ensures that numeric parts of the string are sorted numerically rather than alphabetically, and `ignorePunctuation: true` will ignore special characters and punctuation during sorting. 

Running this script with your given array `["![Tony]", "123Steve", "peter", "Bru^&ce"]` should return `['123Ste^&ve', 'Bru^&ce', 'peter', '![Tony]']`.
