<|asst|>
Sure. Here is how the function would look in Go, with comments added for explanation:

```go
package main

import (
	"strings"
	"sort"
)

// This function is similar to the Python function you provided. 
func fruitDistribution(s []string, n int, fruits []string, servings map[string]int) map[string]int {
	fruitsCount := make(map[string]int)

	// This is the equivalent of the first for loop in the Python code. 
	// It splits each string in the slice s on the whitespace and uses the two resulting substrings to populate the fruitsCount map.
	for _, distr := range s {
		fruit := strings.Split(distr, " ")[0]
		count := servings[fruit]
		fruitsCount[fruit] = count
	}

	// This is the equivalent of the second for loop in the Python code. 
	// It determines the remaining fruits and servings, 
	// and if a fruit is not currently in the fruitsCount map, it calculates its count and adds it to the map.
	for _, fruit := range fruits {
		_, exists := fruitsCount[fruit]
		if !exists {
			remainingFruits := n
			remainingServings := 0
			for key, value := range servings {
				_, exists = fruitsCount[key]
				if !exists {
					remainingServings += value
				} else {
					remainingFruits -= value
				}
			}
			fruitsCount[fruit] = (remainingFruits / remainingServings) * servings[fruit]
		}
	}

	// This section sorts the fruitsCount map in ascending order by count,
	// removing any entries with a count of 0,
	// and returns the sorted map.
	type kv struct {
		Key   string
		Value int
	}

	var ss []kv
	for k, v := range fruitsCount {
		if v != 0 {
			ss = append(ss, kv{k, v})
		}
	}

	sort.Slice(ss, func(i, j int) bool {
		return ss[i].Value < ss[j].Value
	})

	sortedFruitsCount := make(map[string]int)
	for _, kv := range ss {
		sortedFruitsCount[kv.Key] = kv.Value
	}

	return sortedFruitsCount
}
```
Please note that unlike Python, Go does not have built-in support for sorting maps because maps in Go are fundamentally an unordered collection of elements. In order to sort a map in Go, we need to copy the map elements into a slice, sort the slice, and then, if necessary, create a new map from the sorted slice.

Also please keep in mind that Go does not handle integer division the same way as Python. Integer division in Python will give you a float result while Go will give you an integer result. This might cause the results in Go to be different than python results. You might need to change the logic to handle this situation based on your use case.
