Include a test case that includes punctuation and special symbols. Also, elaborate on how your function might behave in the presence of such characters.

This is your erroneous code for reference:

```javascript
let my_text = "coding is fun"
function leastFrequentCharacter(text) {
  let charCounts = {};
  for(let i = 0; i < text.length; i++) {
    if(charCounts.hasOwnProperty(text[i])) {
      charCounts[text[i]]++;
    } else {
      charCounts[text[i]] = 1;
    }
  }
  let minCount = Infinity;
  let minChar = null;
  for(let char in charCounts) {
    if(charCounts[char] < minCount) {
      minCount = charCounts[char];
      minChar = char;
    }
  }
  return minChar;
}

console.log(leastFrequentCharacter(my_text));
```

Increase the difficulty of this question by analyzing what's wrong with this piece of code and how it can be made more efficient or more robust.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The above code is incorrect because it only returns the first character it encounters with the least count. However, there can be many characters with the same minimum count in a string. So this solution won't correctly handle this situation. 

Also, it doesn't handle the cases of case sensitivity, empty string input, or non-string input.

Here is the corrected code.

```javascript
function leastFrequentCharacter(text) {
  if (typeof text !== 'string') {
    return 'Error: Input should be a string.';
  }
  if (text.length === 0) {
    return 'Error: Empty string is not allowed.';
  }

  let charCounts = {};
  for (let i = 0; i < text.length; i++) {
    if (charCounts.hasOwnProperty(text[i])) {
      charCounts[text[i]]++;
    } else {
      charCounts[text[i]] = 1;
    }
  }

  let minCount = Infinity;
  let minChars = {};
  for (let char in charCounts) {
    if (charCounts[char] < minCount) {
      minCount = charCounts[char];
      minChars = { [char]: minCount };
    } else if (charCounts[char] === minCount) {
      minChars[char] = minCount;
    }
  }

  return minChars;
}

console.log(leastFrequentCharacter("Coding is a lot of Fun")); // { C: 1, a: 1, l: 1, t: 1 ... }
console.log(leastFrequentCharacter("")); // Error: Empty string is not allowed.
console.log(leastFrequentCharacter(123)); // Error: Input should be a string.
```

And for testing it with a string including punctuation and special symbols:

```javascript
console.log(leastFrequentCharacter("Coding!! is a lot!! of Fun!")); // { C: 1, a: 1, l: 1, t: 1 ... }
```

This code will treat punctuation and special characters as distinct characters and count their frequencies.

The complexity of this function is O(n), where n is the length of the text. The code traverses the string twice: once to build the frequency table and once to find the least frequent character(s). This idea is to reduce the need for sorting, which could be costlier (O(n log n) or worse) for large inputs. As such, this is an efficient solution for the problem.
<|end|>


