Sometimes, such type of errors can be linked to a problem with cached data or cookies within Chrome. Here are a few steps you can try to rectify the issue:

1. Clearing Browser Cache and Cookies: Go to Chrome settings, then privacy and security, then clear browsing data. Check 'Cookies and other site data' and 'Cached images and files', and hit clear data. 

2. Try accessing in Incognito mode: This will bypass any cached info or extensions that could be affecting page loads. To enable incognito mode, press Ctrl + Shift + N. 

3. Disable Extensions: An extension may be causing the error. You can temporarily turn off all your extensions to see if that's the issue. If it is, you'll need to find out which extension is the problematic one by re-enabling them one by one.

4. Reset Chrome settings to default: Go to the Chrome settings, click on Advanced at the bottom, then Reset settings. 

If none of these steps help, then there could be an issue with the URL (as it seems quite long). You can access your Google Maps Timeline directly by navigating to [https://www.google.com/maps/timeline](https://www.google.com/maps/timeline)

Finally, confirm that you are logged into the correct Google Account and that Location History is enabled for your account. If it's not, Google Maps won't be able to provide you a timeline.

Bear in mind, as it goes with all troubleshooting methods, these steps might not guarantee to solve the problem. It's worth noting, however, that Google services do occasionally experience downtime, and there may be instances when this issue is out of your control. If issues persist, consider reaching out to Google or Chrome support.
