Draft a JavaScript function that not only meticulously controls and manipulates the spatial dimensions and visual perspective of any embedded images within specified containers, but also applies necessary transformations and animations to give them an optical illusion of three-dimensionality or depth. The function should also include custom animations and transitions for the image elements such as zooming, rotation, and panning with smooth transitions. Also provide event listeners and callback functions to control these transformations for user interaction such as mouse movement or touch gestures on mobile devices. The solution should maintain a high level of performance even when handling a large number of image elements.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a general example of how such a function may look. This particular example applies CSS transformations on mouse movement over an element to create a 3D effect. The exact specifics of the transformations can and should be adapted and modified to the specific use case:

```javascript
// This function would control an individual image element
function controlImageElement(element, event) {
    // Set up the transformations
    let transformProps = ['rotateY', 'rotateX'];
    let rotate = {
        rotateY: event.clientX / window.innerWidth * 360 - 180,
        rotateX: event.clientY / window.innerHeight * -360 + 180
    };
    
    // Apply transformations to element
    let style = window.getComputedStyle(element)
    let transform = style.getPropertyValue('transform')
    transformProps.forEach(function(prop) {
        let regExp = new RegExp(prop + '\\((-?[0-9.]*)(deg)?\\)', 'i');
        let matches = regExp.exec(transform);
        let angle = matches ? parseFloat(matches[1]) : 0;
        angle += (rotate[prop] - angle) * 0.1; // This controls the smoothness of the transition

        // Apply the transform to the style
        transform = transform.replace(regExp, prop + '(' + angle + 'deg)');
    });
    element.style.transform = transform;
}

// Attach this handler to the container 
function handleMouseMove(event) {
    let images = document.querySelectorAll('.container img');  // Get all images in the container
    images.forEach((img) => {
        // Control each image element on mouse move
        controlImageElement(img, event);
    });
}

let container = document.querySelector('.container');
container.addEventListener('mousemove', handleMouseMove);
```

This is a simplified example, it does not include touch gestures or any heavy optimizations for performance with large numbers of images. You can however increase performance by debouncing the mousemove event or using the requestAnimationFrame API to limit the number of times the transformations are calculated and applied.

Also, zooming, rotation, and panning would require additional code to handle those specific transformations, and potentially more complex event handling to respond to gestures like pinch-to-zoom. This would likely involve additional math to calculate the correct transformations.
