```go
// import `sort` and `strings` for string comparisons and sorting
import (
	"sort"
	"strings"
)

//define function Longest with arguments as slice of strings and a boolean order
func Longest(s []string, order bool) string {
	if len(s) == 0 { // check if the slice is empty
		return "" // return empty string
	}

	// use `sort` package's Slice function, which sorts the given slice in place
	// the less function compares strings' lengths and if equal it gives precedence to the one which needs to come first depending on order
	sort.Slice(s, func(i, j int) bool {
		if len(s[i]) == len(s[j]) {
			if order {
				return strings.Compare(s[i], s[j]) < 0
			}
			return strings.Compare(s[i], s[j]) > 0
		}
		return len(s[i]) > len(s[j])
	})

	return s[0] // return first string as after sorting it will be the longest as required
}
```

This Go function does the same thing as your JavaScript one: it takes a slice of strings and a boolean to check if strings of equal length should be sorted in order, then returns the longest string. It sorts the strings by their lengths, and if they are equal by order. At the end, the longest string will be at the beginning of the slice (s[0]). If the slice of strings is empty it returns an empty string.
