Devise a function within the Java programming language aiming to sum two integers that are mirrored and signified through a singly linked list data structure. Here, each specific digit (starting from the unit's place) is sequentially stored inside the list nodes.

//demonstrative linked list structure
Node { 
    int data; 
    Node adjacent; 
}

List A
|
V
2 -> 4 -> 3 -> NULL 

List B
|
V
5 -> 6 -> 4 -> NULL 

The computed aggregation must be 8 - 0 - 8 - NULL.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In this problem, you are required to create a Java function that adds two numbers represented by linked lists. In such linked lists, each digit of the number is stored in a separate node in reverse order.

Here's the Java function which creates a new linked list to return the result:

```Java
public Node addTwoLists(Node headA, Node headB) {
    Node dummyNode = new Node(0);
    Node current = dummyNode;
    int carry = 0;
    
    while(headA != null || headB != null){
        int x = (headA != null) ? headA.data : 0;
        int y = (headB != null) ? headB.data : 0;
        int sum = carry + x + y;
        carry = sum/10;

        current.adjacent = new Node(sum % 10);
        current = current.adjacent;
        
        if (headA != null) headA = headA.adjacent;
        if (headB != null) headB = headB.adjacent;
    }
    if(carry > 0){
        current.adjacent = new Node(carry);
    }

    return dummyNode.adjacent;
}

class Node {
    int data;
    Node adjacent;
    Node(int d) {
        data = d;
        adjacent = null;
    }
}
```

The addTwoLists method starts by creating a new "dummy" node and a "current" pointer pointing at the dummy node. Then, it enters a loop which continues until both of input lists have been completely traversed.

In each iteration of the loop, it adds the corresponding digits from each list along with the carry from the previous step. The sum value is then divided by 10 to obtain the new carry value and the remainder is used to create a new node which is then appended to the result list.

Finally, if there is still a carry left after the last iteration, it is added as a new node at the end of the result list. The method return the result list after skipping over the dummy node at the front.

Remember this code assumes addition with lowest digit first. It will start from the head of the linked lists, hence your example would be 342 + 465 = 807 (mirrored). To get the vanilla result, inputs should be reversed like so: 243+564 = 807.
