The CSS code is not provided on the task but let's assume that there is an issue with the CSS targeting the `#content` id. The first thing to check would be if your CSS selectors are written correctly and targeting the appropriate elements. 

Also, inline styles usually take precedence over external stylesheets or style tags unless you're using `!important` in your CSS. However, using `!important` is typically considered a bad practice because it makes debugging difficult by breaking the natural cascading in your stylesheets.

Here is the new CSS code that would apply `opacity: 0.5;` to the `#content` id on hover and transition that change over `0.3s`:

```css
#content {
  transition: opacity 0.3s ease-in-out;
}

#content:hover {
  opacity: 0.5;
}
```

In this case, the `transition` property is used to change the values of the `opacity` property gradually over a specified duration. The `ease-in-out` value means the beginning and end of the transition are slower than in the middle. 

The `:hover` pseudo-class is used to select an element when the user's pointer is over it. In this case, when the user hovers over the element with id 'content', `opacity: 0.5;` is applied.

Ensure this CSS code is either included in an external stylesheet linked in your HTML file or included inside a `<style>` tag in your HTML file. Ensure it's not being overridden by other styles. And make sure the CSS file or style tag is referenced *after* any other CSS files or style tags to ensure the styles cascade properly.
