Help me turn this code into C# syntax:
import nltk
from nltk.corpus import stopwords
from nltk.stem import WordNetLemmatizer
import spacy
from transformers import BertTokenizer
nltk.download('punkt')
nltk.download('wordnet')
nltk.download('stopwords')

# Lambdas for processing.
lemmatizer = WordNetLemmatizer()
stop_words = set(stopwords.words('english'))
tokenizer = BertTokenizer.from_pretrained('bert-base-uncased')

def text_preprocessing(text):
    # Tokenization
    tokens = nltk.word_tokenize(text)

    # Lower case conversion
    tokens = [token.lower() for token in tokens]

    # Remove stopwords
    tokens = [token for token in tokens if token not in stop_words]

    # Lemmatization
    tokens = [lemmatizer.lemmatize(token) for token in tokens]

    # Special tokens needs to be added at the beginning and end of each sentence for BERT to work properly
    tokens = ['[CLS]'] + tokenizer.tokenize(' '.join(tokens)) + ['[SEP]']

    return tokens
Could you also break down what's happening in the C# version?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Firstly, it's essential to note that we don't have a direct equivalent of the NLTK, spaCy, or transformers library in C#. However, in order to achieve similar functionality to deal with text data, we can use other libraries such as ML.NET for tokenization and lemmatization, and custom code for stop words removal and lower case conversion. 

Here's a simple C# example showcasing how this can be done:

Important Note: This example does not include BERT tokenizer functionality, because it's quite complex and is beyond the scope of the libraries in C#. You might need to use a different library that has same capability, or need to implement it from scratch.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;
using Microsoft.ML;
using Microsoft.ML.Transforms.Text;

public class TextProcessing
{
    private static HashSet<string> stopWords = new HashSet<string>
    {
        "a", "about", "above", "after", "again", "against", "all", "am", "an", "and", "any", "are", "aren't",
        // Continue defining the rest of the English stop words here.
    };

    public static string[] PreprocessText(string text)
    {
        // Tokenization
        var context = new MLContext();
        var emptyData = context.Data.LoadFromEnumerable(new List<TextData>());
        var textPipeline = context.Transforms.Text.TokenizeIntoWords("Words", "Text");
        var textTransformer = textPipeline.Fit(emptyData);
        var textData = new[] { new TextData { Text = text } };
        var predictionEngine = context.Model.CreatePredictionEngine<TextData, TextWords>(textTransformer);
        var prediction = predictionEngine.Predict(new TextData { Text = text });
        var tokens = prediction.Words;

        // Lower case conversion
        for (int i = 0; i < tokens.Length; i++)
        {
            tokens[i] = tokens[i].ToLower();
        }

        // Remove stopwords
        tokens = Array.FindAll(tokens, token => !stopWords.Contains(token));

        // Lemmatization - C# does not have a built-in lemmatization tool
        // You'll need external resources, third party libraries or API calls to perform lemmatization
        
        // Adding special tokens
        var output = new List<string> { "[CLS]" };
        output.AddRange(tokens);
        output.Add("[SEP]");

        return output.ToArray();
    }
}

public class TextData
{
    public string Text { get; set; }
}

public class TextWords
{
    public string[] Words { get; set; }
}
```

This C# code does the following:

1. Tokenization: Splits the input text into separate words (tokens) using TokenizeIntoWords method provided by ML.NET.

2. Lower case conversion: Converts each token to lower case for easier comparison and removing stop words.

3. Remove stopwords: Removes common English words such as "the", "a", "is", etc. Note, stop words have to be defined manually, as C# doesn't have an in-built library like NLTK.

4. Lemmatization: This part is missing. You'll need to use external resources, third-party libraries or APIs to perform lemmatization (converting words to their base or root form).

5. Adding special tokens: Add specific tokens (`[CLS]` and `[SEP]`) at the beginning and end of the input just like in your Python code.


Note: It is advisable to implement BERT related functionalities via Python, since it has readily available resources and support for the same. For C#, these functionalities have to be implemented manually and can be complex.
<|end|>


