I have a dataset in which the response variable is Sick(1) or not sick (2). As for the variables, there are a few numeric ones (2/14), all the others are variables by levels (example: 1-Abdominal pain, 2-Throat pain...).
I had two questions:
1-Can a multilayer perceptron classify a binary variable? Or can it only return numerical values?
2-Can binary or leveled variables be passed as training to the multilayer perceptron?
Thank you very much.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
1. Yes, a Multilayer Perceptron (MLP) can classify binary variables. It can work both for classification and regression problems and are therefore applicable for both numerical and categorical outputs. In your case, the binary output (sick or not) can be treated as two distinct classes and the MLP model can be trained to classify inputs into these classes. 

2. Yes, binary or leveled variables can be passed as input to the MLP. In fact, many machine learning algorithms, MLP included, perform better with numeric inputs. If the categorical variables are nominal, you can use techniques such as one-hot encoding to convert the categorical data into a numerical format that the MLP can process. If the categorical variables are ordinal, they can sometimes be directly converted to integers, preserving their order. Remember to normalize or scale your inputs when training an MLP to help the algorithm converge more quickly.

Before feeding to MLP, it might be necessary to preprocess your data. This might include encoding categorical variables, replacing missing values, scaling numeric variables etc. This would help to improve model’s performance.
<|end|>


