I need to build something that starts serving a H.264 encoded video to a flash player halfway through the file (to support skipping to a point in the video that has not been buffered yet). 

Currently, the videos are in the FLV container format, but transcoding is an option. I managed to re-write the file header and metadata information for a given byte offset. This works for older videos, but not for H.264 encoded files. I suspect this is because the video tags inside the file also have to be altered, which is not feasible (it would take too much processing power).

What is the "proper" way to do it?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The most efficient way to resolve this problem would be to use HTTP Live Streaming (HLS). HLS allows users to seek to any point in the stream, including points that haven't been buffered yet.

In HLS, the video and audio are divided into small chunks, typically around 10 seconds in duration. Each chunk is individually downloadable, which enables smooth streaming and quick skipping in the video. To implement HLS for H.264 videos, the video file has to be transcoded into TS (Transport Stream) format and divided into chunks.

You can use various tools to do this transcoding and chunking. FFmpeg is a popular tool which can handle this task efficiently.

If you cannot change your current infrastructure to support HLS, another alternative will be to use a media server that supports RTMP seeking, such as Adobe Media Server or Wowza Streaming Engine. These servers take care of the seeking complexity for you, so you just stream the file as it is and the server will handle the rest.

With RTMP seeking, the server will send the keyframe that is closest to the time the user wants to seek, then sends the remainder of the frames after that, ensuring smooth playback. Note that this method still requires the video to be properly encoded with keyframes at regular intervals.

One other possible option could be doing pseudo-streaming over HTTP, as many Flash Video Players like FlowPlayer, JWPlayer support this. In HTTP pseudo-streaming, a normal HTTP server is used to deliver the video, and seek operations are enabled by a server-side script, which accepts a GET parameter for the time, and then uses something like 'dd' to pipe the video from a certain offset. This also requires the video to have been properly encoded with keyframes.
<|end|>


