<|asst|>
Here is the equivalent Perl code.

```perl
use strict;
use warnings;

sub extract_advanced_data {
    my $error_prone_str = shift;
    $error_prone_str =~ s/[;,]/ /g;
    my @words_list = split " ", $error_prone_str;

    if (grep /^[[:upper:]]+$/, @words_list == @words_list) {
        my $index_count = () = $error_prone_str =~ /[02468]/g;
        return {'uppercase', $index_count};
    } else {
        my %character_categories = (
            'uppercase' => 0,
            'lowercase' => 0,
            'digits' => 0,
            'special_symbols' => 0,
            'whitespaces' => 0,
            'undefined' => 0
        );

        for my $c (split //, $error_prone_str) {
            if ($c =~ /[[:upper:]]/) {
                $character_categories{'uppercase'}++;
            } elsif ($c =~ /[[:lower:]]/) {
                $character_categories{'lowercase'}++;
            } elsif ($c =~ /\d/) {
                $character_categories{'digits'}++;
            } elsif ($c =~ /\s/) {
                $character_categories{'whitespaces'}++;
            } elsif ($c =~ /\p{Punct}/) {
                $character_categories{'special_symbols'}++;
            } else {
                $character_categories{'undefined'}++;
            }
        }

        return \%character_categories;
    }
}

my $error_prone_str = 'SOME EXAMPLE STRING';
my $data = extract_advanced_data($error_prone_str);

print "${_} => ${$data}{$_}\n" for keys %$data;
```

Note, however, that Perl does not have a way to count all Unicode punctuation characters as Python's `string.punctuation` do. The line with `\p{Punct}` only counts ASCII punctuation characters. If you need to count all Unicode punctuation characters, Perl's Unicode support is more intricate and complex.
<|end|>


