I'm starting off my undergraduate research on text classification even though I'm still considered new to this topic. I've collected more than 20K data from Twitter. I've been trying to label the data into 3 sentiments, positive, negative, and neutral. But, I oftentimes find it difficult to determine whether a tweet is categorized as positive, negative, or neutral due to my lack of knowledge in that field.
My supervisor has someone with a psychology background that can help me labeling the data instead of linguistics. I'm aware that sentiment analysis is a part of the natural language processing task which makes more sense if I ask for help from linguistics, but at the same time sentiment, in general, is also studied in psychology as I read more papers. Does anyone have any advice? Thank you in advance!
<|end|>
<|asst|>
If you look purely from the perspective of labeling data based on sentiments, both a linguist and a psychologist would provide different dimensions of analysis. 

A psychologist could be useful because he/she studies feelings, thoughts, and behaviors, so they would be able to provide a solid perspective about what emotional tone the text might carry. Psychologists have deep knowledge about human emotions and how they are expressed, which is a crucial aspect for sentiment analysis.

On the other hand, a linguist would focus more on language structure, meaning, and context. They would be able to understand the nuances of language that might escape the untrained eye. For instance, sarcasm is a very complex linguistic concept that could be incorrectly labeled as positive sentiment by a non-expert, while a linguist would understand that it is often used to express negative sentiment.

Another consideration is that the sentiment in text can be highly dependent on cultural context. Certain phrases or words may carry specific sentiments in one culture but not in another. Therefore, the sentiment might be misclassified if this is not taken into count.

My suggestion is a collaborative approach. Use both a linguist and a psychologist to create a more robust labeling system for your sentiment analysis. They can cross-check each other's work and provide feedback. This could help enrich your dataset and improve the accuracy of your sentiment classification. 

But remember that manual labeling can be cumbersome and error-prone due to subjective interpretation. So consider using some text classification or sentiment analysis algorithms as well, to automate and provide a benchmark for your task.
<|end|>


