Design a class hierarchy in C# to store employee details with different types of employees such as managers, engineers, and interns. Each type of employee should have unique attributes and methods relevant to their roles. Also, implement appropriate methods to calculate the pay for each employee type considering different factors such as overtime, bonuses, and deductions. Please ensure your design adheres to object-oriented programming principles such as inheritance and encapsulation.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a rudimentary implementation of the requested design:

``` C#
// Begin by creating a base class, Employee, from which all employee types will inherit
public abstract class Employee
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public string Id { get; set; }
    public double BaseSalary { get; set; }

    // All employees can have deductions so place the Deductions method in the base class
    public virtual double HandleDeductions(double deductionAmount)
    {
        double totalSalary = BaseSalary - deductionAmount;
        return totalSalary;
    }

    // Abstract method since each employee type may have different implementation
    public abstract double CalculatePay();
}

// The Manager class inherits from the Employee class
public class Manager : Employee
{
    public double Bonus { get; set; }

    // Overriding the base class's abstract CalculatePay method
    public override double CalculatePay()
    {
        return BaseSalary + Bonus;
    }
}

// The Engineer class also inherits from the Employee class
public class Engineer : Employee
{
    public double Overtime { get; set; }

    // Overriding the base class's abstract CalculatePay method
    public override double CalculatePay()
    {
        return BaseSalary + (Overtime * 1.5);
    }
}

// Finally, the Intern class inherits from the Employee class
public class Intern : Employee
{
    // The intern doesn't get overtime or bonuses, so we just return the base salary
    public override double CalculatePay()
    {
        return BaseSalary;
    }
}
```

In this example, each `Employee` has a `Name`, an `Id`, and a `BaseSalary`. The `CalculatePay` method is abstract and overridden in every subclass which ensures that each has their own unique implementation, as per the specifications. Lastly the `HandleDeductions` method is a virtual method that allows us to handle deductions. Classes that need to handle deductions in a different way can override this method. Pay special attention to the `Manager`'s bonus and `Engineer`'s overtime properties and how they affect the final salary calculation.
<|end|>


