Then in JavaScript/jQuery you can use the following script to perform the functionality:

```javascript
$('#newAccountForm').on('submit', function(e) {
    e.preventDefault();
    
    var username = $('#username').val();
    var email = $('#email').val();
    var password = $('#password').val();
    var confirmPassword = $('#confirmPassword').val();
    
    var userNameRegEx = /^[a-zA-Z0-9]+$/;
    if(!username.match(userNameRegEx)) {
        alert("User name should not contain special characters.");
        return false;
    }

    var emailRegEx = /\S+@\S+\.\S+/;
    if(!email.match(emailRegEx)) {
        alert("Please enter a valid email address");
        return false;
    }

    if(password !== confirmPassword) {
        alert("Password and Confirm Password do not match!");
        return false;
    }

    $('#result').html('<p>Username: ' + username + '</p><p>Email: ' + email + '</p>');

    return true;
});
```
The script prevents the form from its default submitting method. Instead, it takes the values of username, email, password, and confirmPassword fields into variables and then validates them. It checks if the username contains any special characters, if email is in correct format and if password and confirmPassword match. If any of these checks fail, it alerts the appropriate message to the user and aborts the submit by returning false. If all validations pass, it updates the `#result` div with the input data and then returns true. 

Please note that this validation is on the client side which is not secure because anyone can view and modify your code. To make it secure you should do the validation on the server side. 

Also, for real life scenarios never display password in the console or webpage. Our goal here was to display the entered values, that's why we are displaying.
