Part 1: Preeminence of SVG sprites over traditional raster image sprites

Scalable Vector Graphics (SVG) and raster graphics serve their unique purposes, but for web development, SVG provides several advantages:

Scalability: SVGs are resolution-independent, meaning they maintain their sharpness and detail at any scale. This is in stark contrast to raster images, which can become pixelated when scaled beyond their base resolution. Being vector-based, SVGs are perfect for responsive web design as they look crisp on all devices, regardless of resolution.

Performance: Loading a single SVG sprite that contains multiple icons can be significantly more efficient than loading many individual raster images. Less server requests results in better performance.

Adaptability: SVG code can be directly integrated into your HTML, manipulated with CSS or JavaScript, and can be animated, making them incredibly versatile. Raster images don't provide this level of adaptability.

Part 2: Making SVG sprite responsive and accessible

Responsiveness of SVGs is primarily managed through the viewBox attribute. The `viewBox` attribute allows you to specify that a given set of graphics stretch to fit a particular container element.

Accessibility with SVGs involves adding descriptive titles and/or descriptions to the SVG elements, and using ARIA attributes when necessary. For example:

```html
<svg role="img" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 32 32">
  <title>icon 1</title>
  <desc>A description of icon 1</desc>
  <path ... />
  <path ... />
</svg>
```

