Create a Javascript module to find and return the most frequently used words in a long text document.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
```javascript
function wordFrequency(text) {
    var words = text.replace(/[.]/g, '').split(/\s/);
    var freqMap = {};
    var maxFreq = 0;
    var mostFrequentWords = [];

    // create a frequency map
    for (var i in words) {
        var word = words[i].toLowerCase();
        if (word !== '') {
            var wordFrequency = freqMap[word] === undefined ? 1 : freqMap[word] + 1;
            freqMap[word] = wordFrequency

            //Update max frequency 
            if(wordFrequency > maxFreq) {
                maxFreq = wordFrequency;
                mostFrequentWords = [word];
            } else if(wordFrequency === maxFreq) {
                mostFrequentWords.push(word);
            }
        }
    }
    return mostFrequentWords;
}

module.exports = wordFrequency;
```
This function `wordFrequency` takes a `text` as an argument and finds the maximum frequency of words in that text. It first removes all full stops (periods) from the `text` and then splits the `text` into an array of words. For each word, it converts the word to lowercase and checks if it is already in the `freqMap`. 

If the word is not in the `freqMap`, it sets its frequency to `1`; otherwise, it increments the frequency by `1`. It then checks if the frequency of the current word is greater than the current maximum frequency. If so, it sets the maximum frequency to the current word's frequency and sets the most frequent word to the current word. If the current word's frequency is equal to the maximum frequency, it adds the current word to the list of most frequently used words.

Then it exports the `wordFrequency` function from this module so that it can be imported in other modules.

Please note that this implementation does not handle punctuation other than full stops (periods), nor does it handle different forms of the same word (like "running" and "run").
