Yes, there is a library called VueGL which is built for working with both Vue.js and WebGL (three.js). VueGL provides a bunch of Vue components which make it easier to build a 3d scene with Three.js. It's not as mature or as feature-heavy as React three fiber is for React, but it should at least get you up and running!

Github Link: https://github.com/vue-gl/vue-gl

NPM Link: https://www.npmjs.com/package/vue-gl

You can install it via NPM as follows:

```
npm install --save three vue-gl
```
To use VueGL, you have to import each component you want to use:

```javascript
import { VglNamespace, VglObject3d, VglScene, VglRenderer, VglCameraPerspective } from 'vue-gl';
```

Then, you can register them globally or locally as you would any other Vue component:

```javascript
new Vue({
  el: '#app',
  components: {
    VglNamespace,
    VglObject3d,
    VglScene,
    VglRenderer,
    VglCameraPerspective
  },
});
```
Then you can use these components in your Vue template as follows:

```html
<vgl-renderer antialias>
  <vgl-scene>
    <vgl-camera-perspective position="1 1 1"></vgl-camera-perspective>
    ...
  </vgl-scene>
</vgl-renderer>
```

This will create a WebGL rendering context, set up a scene, and add a perspective camera to it. More functionality can be added by using more specific components from the VueGL library.
