show me the c rendition of this code:
def will_it_fly(q, w):
    # check if q is the same forwards and backwards (palindrome) and if sum of q <= w
    return q == q[::-1] and sum(q) <= w
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The C equivalent of the code provided in Python attempts to create palindrome and sum checker. Python's built-in features such as slicing and sum function make the implementation quite straightforward. However, when it comes to C, there isn't a direct equivalent to Python's reverse slicing, so we would need to manually implement that functionality.

#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

// Function to check if a string is a palindrome 
int is_palindrome(char str[]) 
{ 
    int l = 0;
    int h = strlen(str) - 1;
   
    while (h > l) 
    { 
        if (str[l++] != str[h--]) 
        { 
            return 0; 
        } 
    } 
    return 1; 
}

// Function to calculate the sum of ASCII 
int sum_string(char *str) {
    int total = 0;
    while (*str!='\0') {
        total += (int) *str++;
    }
    return total;
}

int will_it_fly(char q[], int w) {
  return (is_palindrome(q) && sum_string(q) <= w) ? 1 : 0; 
}

int main() {
    printf("%d", will_it_fly("radar", 500));  // Returns 1
    return 0;
}

In this C code above, it checks if a C string is a palindrome and if it's ASCII sum is less than or equal to a given integer. This is as close as the C equivalent can get because arrays in C work differently than lists in Python.

Please note that the sum function in the original Python code adds list elements, whereas the C version sums ASCII values of characters in the string. They are fundamentally different because Python's list can hold multiple different types, whereas C's char array can only hold characters.

The main function is for testing purposes and uses radar as the input string and 500 as the comparison integer. Please replace "radar" and 500 with any test inputs of your choosing.
