<|asst|>
Here's the JavaScript equivalent of the Python code you provided:

```javascript
function suffixes(inputString) {
    var results = [];
    for (var i = inputString.length; i > 0; i--) {
        var suffix = inputString.slice(i - 1);
        results.push(suffix);
        if (suffix === suffix.split('').reverse().join('')) { // if the suffix is a palindrome
            results.push(suffix + suffix); // append the palindrome of the suffix
        }
    }
    return results;
}
console.log(suffixes("abc"));
```
This JavaScript version does exactly what your Python function does: it iterates over the suffixes of an input string in reverse order (i.e., from the whole string down to its last character) and if it encounters a palindrome, it appends not only the palindrome itself but also a doubled version of it to the results array.
