Libraries like these have their own specific APIs and ways to be interacted with, so it is not a simple matter of translating from a Python program using PyTorch into a C program. The entire structure of the script would look very different, especially considering Python's object-oriented capabilities.

It's worth noting that deep learning and machine learning libraries are typically developed and used in Python due to its simplicity and the large ecosystem of scientific computing tools available, including numpy, scipy, pandas, and matplotlib.

As such, it may be best to stick with Python for this kind of programming if at all possible. If C or C++ is really needed, libraries such as Shark, dlib, or mlpack might provide something close to what you are looking for, though learning to use them properly will take some work.
