I can't figure out why the responsive breakpoints of tailwind css are not working in my project.
Suppose, I'd like to add some padding in my parent div and want to minimise it on the md & lg screen size but it doesn't work.

<div class="text-grey-4 flex-1 lg:px-6 md:px-4 px-0 sm:text-left text-center self-center"><h2 class="h2"><b>Heading Text</b></h2><div> Lorum ipsum lorum ispum lorum ipsum Lorum ipsum lorum ispum lorum ipsum Lorum ipsum lorum ispum lorum ipsum</div></div>
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Tailwind CSS uses a mobile-first approach, which means that styles are applied from smallest to largest. So the styles that you define without any breakpoints are applied to all screen sizes (mobile and up), then from there you can override styles for larger screens as necessary.

Please, remember to add them in ascending order based on the screen sizes.

Try to switch your utility classes like this:

```html
<div class="text-grey-4 flex-1 px-0 sm:text-left text-center self-center md:px-4 lg:px-6"><h2 class="h2"><b>Heading Text</b></h2><div> Lorum ipsum lorum ispum lorum ipsum Lorum ipsum lorum ispum lorum ipsum Lorum ipsum lorum ispum lorum ipsum</div></div>
```

Also, the issue could be that your project setup isn't correctly configured to use Tailwind's responsive breakpoints. 

You should have a "tailwind.config.js" file in the root of your project where you can define your breakpoints (optional, only if you want to customize the default ones). 

If there isn't one, you can create it by running npx tailwindcss init at your project root, which will generate a minimal tailwind.config.js file.

Also, be sure to use your tailwind classes correctly, In your provided code, "text-grey-4" is not a valid tailwind css class, maybe you mean "text-gray-400".

A restart of your development server may also be needed after changing the tailwind.config.js.

If you are using PostCSS (most probably you are if you are using TailwindCSS) then you can set up your postcss.config.js file like this,

```js
module.exports = {
  plugins: [
    require('tailwindcss'),
    require('autoprefixer'),
  ]
}
```

Make sure you have installed these packages and imported the styles in your CSS.

```css
@import 'tailwindcss/base';
@import 'tailwindcss/components';
@import 'tailwindcss/utilities';
```

Be sure to check that your classes are correct and you've followed the setup instructions properly. 

If you're using a ready-made configuration (like a React, Vue, Angular, or Svelte template) you might want to check if there are unique conditions regarding the utility classes and the breakpoints.
<|end|>


