I'm looking for a tool like Atlassian's [FishEye](http://www.atlassian.com/software/fisheye/). The alternatives I've found so far (like [StatCVS](http://statcvs.sourceforge.net/), [ViewCVS](http://www.viewvc.org/) or [Bonsai](http://www.mozilla.org/projects/bonsai/)) are either lacking in features or are quite a pain to install and maintain. So before staying with one of these tools, I'd like to be sure I did not miss any other good, easy to install, open-source (prefereably java) version control-viewer which supports cvs as scm.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Some other alternatives for viewing and managing CVS repositories are:

- [CVS Monitor](http://cvsmonitor.sourceforge.net/): A web-based CVS monitoring tool which provides a simplified view of the CVS repository. It allows watching of modules/branches for changes and presents graphical timelines of project activity. It’s simple to set up and said to be easy to use. However, it might lack some of the features of FishEye.

- [CVSTrac](http://www.cvstrac.org/): CVSTrac includes features for bug tracking, version control, and wiki all in one. With CVSTrac, CVS change logs can be viewed in a tabular form, toggling of resolution and coloring of changes by author is allowed. It’s lightweight and reasonably easy to install and set up.

- [WebSVN](http://www.websvn.info/): Originally a SVN repository viewer, but it also supports CVS repositories. It's easy to set up and has a clear and easy to use web interface. It includes features such as file browsing, diffs, commit history, and blame. However, some users report that it’s less robust for managing larger codebases.

- [Trac](https://trac.edgewall.org/): Although Trac is primarily tailored for Subversion, it does provide basic support for other systems including CVS. It integrates with an SCM system, provides a wiki, an issue tracker and convenient report facilities. Trac allows wiki markup in issue descriptions and commit messages, creating links and references between bugs, tasks, changesets, files and wiki pages.

Please note that each of these tools has varying features and may not have all of the features that FishEye does. It would be wise to check each one out individually to see which fits your needs best.
<|end|>


