Design a function in Java that takes in a string and returns true if the string contains the letter "a" at least twice, and false otherwise. Additionally, the occurrences of "a" should be at least 3 characters apart in the string.

As an erroneous code example for reference, consider the following:

```java
public boolean containsA(String input) {
  int countA = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < input.length(); i++) {
    if (input.charAt(i) == 'a') {
      countA++;
    }
  }
  return countA > 1;
}
```
This code example does not take into account the constraint that the "a"s should be at least 3 characters apart.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's the correct Java code to solve this problem:

```java
public boolean containsA(String input) {
  int previousAIndex = -3; // Initialized to -3 to validate first valid "a" occurrence if it exists

  for (int i = 0; i < input.length(); i++) {
    if (input.charAt(i) == 'a') {
      if (i - previousAIndex > 3) {
        previousAIndex = i;
      } else {
        return false;
      }
    }
  }

  int countA = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < input.length(); i++) {
    if (input.charAt(i) == 'a') {
      countA++;
    }
  }

  return countA > 1;
}
```

This Java code has a function named `containsA` which checks the string on character by character basis and validates whether the characters 'a', if present, are more than 3 characters apart. If yes, then it records the index of 'a'. If not, it directly returns false since it violates the constraint. At last, it checks if there are two or more 'a's in the string. If yes, then it returns true. Content validation and count validation both are important for the problem statement's constraint satisfaction.
<|end|>


