My understanding of Bayesian optimization is that it is generally used in conjunction with Gaussian process (GP) as the surrogate model. Because GP inherently produces an uncertainty of estimate, the acquisition function of expected improvement (EI) can take that into account the uncertainity and strike a balance between exploration and exploitation.
If we were to use a (2nd order) polynomial regression model as the surrogate, as what is normally used in response surface methodology (RSM), is it possible to still use expected improvement as the acquisition function? If so, what is the method to obtain an uncertainty of estimate at any point from a 2nd order polynomial model in order to calculate the EI?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, Bayesian Optimization can be used with polynomial regression models. It’s not restricted to Gaussian Process models. Expected improvement (EI) can still be used as an acquisition function with a polynomial model. However, polynomial regression does not provide a natural measure of uncertainty like Gaussian Processes. Therefore, you will need to estimate the predictive interval (a measure of uncertainty) to compute EI, which can be quite challenging.

A method to get an estimation of the uncertainty from a polynomial model could be via resampling methods, such as bootstrapping. With bootstrapping, you repeatedly fit the polynomial to different subsamples of the data, yielding a range of models which can be used to estimate the distribution of the predictions -- hence, providing a measure of uncertainty. However, these methods can be computationally expensive.

In alternative, you might consider utilizing Bayesian linear regression models (which can be applied to polynomial models by extending the input features), as they naturally provide a posterior distribution of predictions and thus a measure of uncertainty. However, these models make additional assumptions and might be more complex to work with compared to Gaussian Processes.
<|end|>


