<|asst|>
Here's a simple Java program that uses a for-loop to reverse a string:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String input = "Hello World";
        String reversed = reverseString(input);
        System.out.println("Reversed String: " + reversed);
    }

    public static String reverseString(String str) {
        char[] inputArray = str.toCharArray();
        char[] reverseArray = new char[inputArray.length];
        for (int i = 0; i < inputArray.length; i++) {
            reverseArray[i] = inputArray[inputArray.length - 1 - i];
        }
        String reversed = new String(reverseArray);
        return reversed;
    }
}
```
This program works by creating a char array from the input string. Then it creates a new char array of the same length. It iterates over the original array, in the reverse order, appending each character to the new array. Finally it creates a new string from reversed char array.

Note: This solution still uses some inbuilt functions like .toCharArray() to convert string to array of characters and new string() to convert array of characters to string. 

If you want an implementation that does not use ANY in-built functions, you'd essentially have to create those functions yourself. This is usually not recommended because it's more error-prone and inefficient to recreate these rather than using the inbuilt ones.
