Refactor the subsequent JavaScript script to not only identify and display all prime numbers between 1 and 100 using the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm, but extend its functionality to also calculate and display these prime numbers' respective prime factors. Additionally, adjust the script to perform these operations for numbers up to 500. This task will necessitate the creation of an efficient prime factor function which will work optimally with larger number sets.

for (let i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
    console.log(i);
}
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can solve this using JavaScript in the following way. We first create a function to generate a list of prime numbers up to a given limit using the Sieve of Eratosthenes. Then, we create a separate function to factor a given number into its prime factors:

```javascript
function sieveOfEratosthenes(limit) {
    var primes = [];
    var sieve = Array(limit).fill(true);

    for (let i = 2; i <= limit; i++) {
        if (sieve[i]) {
            primes.push(i);
            for (let j = i * 2; j <= limit; j += i) {
                sieve[j] = false;
            }
        }
    }
    return primes;
}

function primeFactors(num, primes) {
    var factors = [];
    for (let prime of primes) {
        while (num % prime == 0) {
            factors.push(prime);
            num /= prime;
        }
        if (num == 1) {
           break;
        }
    }
    return factors;
}

var limit = 500;
var primes = sieveOfEratosthenes(limit);

console.log("Prime numbers up to " + limit + ":");
console.log(primes);

for (let i = 2; i <= limit; i++) {
    console.log("Prime factors of " + i + ":");
    console.log(primeFactors(i, primes));
}
```

In this script, `sieveOfEratosthenes` creates an array of boolean values indexed by numbers up to the given limit and initialized as `true`, which represent whether a number is prime. For every number that is marked as prime, it iterates through the multiples of that number and marks them as `false` (not prime). Finally, it returns the prime numbers as an array.

The `primeFactors` function iterates through the generated primes and repeatedly divides the number until it is fully factored, appending the extra prime factors to an array, and then returns that array. 

Finally, the script prints the prime numbers up to the limit, and for each number in this range, it prints its prime factors.
