#Fit model
model = ARIMA(series, order=(5,1,0))
model_fit = model.fit(disp=0)

#Forecast
forecast, stderr, conf_int = model_fit.forecast(steps=12)
print(f"Forecast: {forecast}")

#Plot
plt.figure(figsize=(12, 8))
plt.plot(series.index[-60:], series.values[-60:])
plt.plot(pd.date_range(series.index[-1], periods=12, freq='M'), forecast)
plt.show()
```

In this example, we load the data, then we handle missing data by using linear interpolation. We fit an ARIMA model to our data and make a forecast for the next 12 months. We then print the forecast and display a plot of our data along with the forecast.

The model order (p,d,q) is set as (5,1,0) for demonstration purposes only. In practice, you should use an approach like grid search to systematically try different combinations of p, d, and q values and select the one that minimizes your error metric like AIC or BIC.

Please note that ARIMA is a simple forecasting model which may not well capture the seasonal variation. Better forecasts may be achieved with a seasonal ARIMA (SARIMA) model.
