Some metrics are sample size-biased, i.e., they will give biased estimates in small sample sizes. Is there a formal proof that AUROC is either sample size-biased or unbiased? I.e., will the expectation over the AUROC computed over test sets of size 100 be the same as the expectation over the AUROC computed over test sets of size 10,000 drawn from the same distribution?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic (AUROC) is not biased by sample size per se, but the precision of this metric does depend on the sample size. Specifically, with small sample sizes, AUROC estimates can be more variable and potentially less reliable.

When computing AUROC, the relative ranks of the positive and negative observations are used. If the sample size is large, the influence of individual data points on the AUROC is small, resulting in a more stable estimator. In contrast, if the sample size is small, the influence of individual data points on the AUROC may be substantial, making the AUROC estimator less stable.

Thus, while the expectation of AUROC over an infinite number of test sets of size 100 is the same as the expectation over an infinite number of test sets of size 10,000 drawn from the same distribution, fewer samples will result in a less precise estimate due to random sampling variability.

There is no formal proof showing that AUROC is sample size-biased or unbiased, as its expectation depends more on the underlying probability distributions than the sample size itself. However, empirical studies and theoretical considerations show that its precision depends on sample size. Therefore, it's safe to say that the AUROC is not inherently a sample size-biased metric but that its precision depends heavily on the number of samples.
<|end|>


