<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, it is possible to estimate an approximate number for the minimum number of times you need to show the item to have a confident estimate of the Click-Through Rate (CTR). 

CTR, in this scenario, is a binomial proportion and its confidence intervals can be estimated using various methods. The \textit{Wald Method} is simplest yet not the most accurate, especially for extreme probabilities close to 0 or 1. Others like the \textit{Wilson Score Interval} or the \textit{Agresti-Coull Interval} are more accurate. 

The width of a 95% Wilson Score Interval, for example, is approximately `2*1.96*sqrt(p*(1-p)/n)`, where `p` is the true click-through rate and `n` is the number of trials (or impressions in this case). If we take the maximum value for width (which is at p = 0.5), and set it less than a certain pre-defined bound (say ε), we can solve for minimum `n`. 

`2*1.96*sqrt(0.5*(1-0.5)/n) < ε`

This gives us:

`n > (1.96/ε)^2 * (0.5*(1-0.5))`

So, whenever you have a new item, you could display it this many times as a start for getting a 95% CI to be less than ε in width. Adjust the value of ε to make the CI tighter or looser as per your needs. 

This doesn't really guarantee that the observed CTR will be within ε of the true CTR, but it ensures that if you repeat the experiment multiple times, the CI for the observed CTR will include the true CTR 95% of the time, and the width of the CI would be less than 2ε. 

Remember to adjust these calculations depending on the estimation method you use. Different methods might require different calculations.
<|end|>


