Write a Java program to print all the Armstrong numbers in the range provided by the user input. An Armstrong number is a number that is equal to the sum of its own digits each raised to the power of the total number of digits. 

Additionally, the program must check if the provided range exceeds the limits of the integer data type and alert the user if so. Also, implement functionality where the user can decide whether they want to include or exclude the upper and lower range bounds when searching for Armstrong numbers.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a simple example which might help you better understand how to solve this problem:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.println("Enter lower limit:");
        long lowerLimit = scanner.nextLong();

        System.out.println("Enter upper limit:");
        long upperLimit = scanner.nextLong();

        System.out.println("Include bounds? Y/N");
        char includeBounds = scanner.next().charAt(0);

        if(includeBounds =='Y' || includeBounds =='y') {
            if (lowerLimit > Integer.MAX_VALUE || upperLimit > Integer.MAX_VALUE) {
                System.out.println("Limits exceed Integer bounds. Please enter a valid range.");
            } else {
                for (long number = lowerLimit; number <= upperLimit; number++) {
                    if (isArmstrong(number)) {
                        System.out.println(number);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        else {
            if (lowerLimit >= Integer.MAX_VALUE || upperLimit > Integer.MAX_VALUE) {
                System.out.println("Limits exceed Integer bounds. Please enter a valid range.");
            } else {
                for (long number = lowerLimit+1; number < upperLimit; number++) {
                    if (isArmstrong(number)) {
                        System.out.println(number);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    public static boolean isArmstrong(long number) {
        long temp = number;
        int noOfDigits = String.valueOf(number).length();
        long sum = 0;

        while (temp > 0) {
            long digit = temp % 10;
            sum += Math.pow(digit, noOfDigits);
            temp /= 10;
        }

        if (number == sum) {
            return true;
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```

What this program does:

The program prompts the user to input the lower and upper limits of the range, as well as if they would like to include the bounds in the check for Armstrong numbers. 

It then checks if the chosen range exceeds the limit for the integer data type. 

If the range is valid, the program goes through each number in the range and checks if it's an Armstrong number by splitting it into its digits, then checking if the sum of each digit to the power of the number of digits equals the original number. If a number passes this test, it is printed out.
