Making an SVG look frozen with icicle-like shapes and icy textures can be done in different ways. You could add shapes in the style of icicles to your SVG file, and make use of gradient fills and filters to give the whole object a cold, frosty appearance. The most tedious part is adding the icicle shapes, but it's not too difficult. Below is a basic way to do it:

1. Icicle shapes are just elongated triangles. You can add them in SVG format using the <polygon> or <path> tags. A possible icicle could look like this in SVG: 

```html
<path d="M10 10 L15 30 L20 10" fill="lightblue" />
```
This is a path making a simple triangle, imagined in a 2D plane, starting at point (10,10), going to point (15,30), then to point (20,10), and back to the origin. You would use different points to position your icicles on the object where you want them.

2. To add frosty textures, we can use SVG filters, for example a feTurbulence filter can be used to add a random noise, combined with feDisplacementMap to give a sense of ice crystals:

```html
<filter id="frost">
  <feTurbulence type="fractalNoise" baseFrequency="0.01" numOctaves="2" result="turbulence"/>
  <feDisplacementMap in2="turbulence" in="SourceGraphic" scale="5" xChannelSelector="R" yChannelSelector="G"/>
</filter>
```
Then, you can apply this filter to your shapes with filter="url(#frost)".

3. In your SVG file, you currently have a gradient that uses warmer colors, you may want to replace it with a cool toned gradient to give a more "frozen" look, maybe in blue tones.

```html
<linearGradient id="iceGradient" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="1">
<stop offset="5%"  stop-color="#6dd5ed" />
<stop offset="95%" stop-color="#2193b0" />
</linearGradient>
```
Then, refer this gradient in your shapes: fill="url(#iceGradient)".

These changes would help in giving an ice-like appearance. If you're not very familiar with SVGs, there are also tools on the internet for creating SVGs (like Adobe Illustrator or Inkscape) where you could do the same more visually.
<|end|>


