Problem:
Consider a matrix X of dimensions M x N, where all elements are positive. Let's denote xi as the i-th column of X. An individual has constructed a 3-dimensional array Y of dimensions N x M x M, which is composed of M x M matrices resulting from the dot product of xi and its transpose xi.T. 
What would be the method to reconstruct the original matrix X, of dimensions M*N, utilizing numpy? 
A:
&lt;code&gt;
import numpy as np
Y = np.array([[[81, 63, 63],
        [63, 49, 49],
        [63, 49, 49]],

       [[ 4, 12,  8],
        [12, 36, 24],
        [ 8, 24, 16]],

       [[25, 35, 25],
        [35, 49, 35],
        [25, 35, 25]],

       [[25, 30, 10],
        [30, 36, 12],
        [10, 12,  4]]])
&lt;/code&gt;
X = ... # put solution in this variable
BEGIN SOLUTION
&lt;code&gt;
<|end|>
<|asst|>
X = np.zeros((M, N))

for i in range(N):
  # Find the eigenvalues of the i-th element of the 3D array
  val, vec = np.linalg.eig(Y[i])

  # The original column of the matrix is the eigenvector scaled by the positive eigenvalue
  X[:, i] = vec[:, np.argmax(val)] * np.sqrt(np.max(val)) if np.max(val) > 0 else vec[:, np.argmax(val)]
&lt;/code&gt;
END SOLUTION
This code will find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of each M x M matrix in Y. The original column of X that corresponds to each M x M matrix in Y is the eigenvector corresponding to the positive eigenvalue (if it exists), scaled by the square root of this eigenvalue.
This solution assumes all elements of X are positive, meaning that a positive eigenvalue will always exist. If this is not the case, some additional error handling might be needed.
If the max eigenvalue is negative or 0, then we just use the eigenvector corresponding to the max eigenvalue itself without scaling. In this case, negative elements of X would cause negative eigenvalues in Y, and zeros would cause zero eigenvalues.
<|end|>


