In order to add a reflection below the SVG, you need to create a copy of the SVG that is flipped vertically, and then apply an opacity gradient to it. This second SVG will serve as the reflection. Here's how you can do it:

1. Wrap the original SVG in a container:

```
<div class="svg-container">
  <svg>
    <!-- original svg code -->
  </svg>
  <svg class="svg-reflection">
    <!-- original svg code -->
  </svg>
</div>
```

2. Flip the second SVG and apply an opacity gradient in CSS:

```
.css-container {
  position: relative;
}

.svg-reflection {
  position: absolute;
  top: 100%;
  transform: scaleX(-1);
  opacity: 0.3;
}

.svg-reflection path {
  fill-opacity: 0.3;
}
```

Note that this may not create a perfectly realistic "reflection" effect depending on the complexity and color profile of the original SVG. This approach doesn't take into account any real world physics like vanishing points or light sources, it's just a simple "reflection".

Another option is you could create a separate SVG manually that represents the reflection and apply an opacity gradient to it. This method would give you more control over the look of the reflection. 

Keep in mind that dealing with complex SVGs may require more advanced techniques, like using SVG editing software or custom JavaScript.
