Create an Express middleware for redirecting requests from a given URL to another URL.
Base URL: "/about"
Redirect URL: "/about-us"
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can create the middleware function using the express.js `app.use` function. On any request to `/about`, the function will redirect the user to `/about-us`.

Here's a simple implementation of the middleware:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.use('/about', (req, res, next) => {
    res.redirect('/about-us');
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
    console.log('Server started on port: 3000');
});
```

This middleware intercepts all requests to '/about' and redirects them to '/about-us'. You could generalize this into a utility function to handle arbitrary path redirection like so:

```javascript
var redirect = (oldUrl, newUrl) => {
  app.use(oldUrl, (req, res, next) => {
    res.redirect(301, newUrl);
  });
};

redirect('/about', '/about-us');

app.listen(3000, () => {
    console.log('Server started on port: 3000');
});
```

Here, I made use of HTTP 301 Moved Permanently status code to tell the browser (or search engine) that the content has permanently moved to new location.
<|end|>


