This is essentially asking to extract all the tag names from an XML document. Python is well-suited for this task, so here's how you can solve this using Python's built-in xml.etree.ElementTree module:

```python
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET

def get_tags(xml_string):
    root = ET.fromstring(xml_string)
    tags = set()
    for elem in root.iter():
        tags.add(elem.tag)
    return tags

xml = """<note>
<to>Tove</to>
<from>Jani</from>
<heading>Reminder</heading>
<body>Don't forget me this weekend!</body>
</note>"""

tags = get_tags(xml)
print(tags)
```

In the example above, the function `get_tags()` takes a string representing an XML document (in the variable `xml`), parses it into an ElementTree (which is a hierarchical tree structure of all the tags and text in the XML), and adds all unique tag names to the set `tags`. After running this script, the output is a set of unique tag names in the XML document: `{'body', 'note', 'to', 'from', 'heading'}`.

Note: For an XML document stored in a file, use ET.parse() instead of ET.fromstring(). For example: `tree = ET.parse('path_to_your_file.xml')`, and then `root = tree.getroot()`.
<|end|>


