Devise a function in Java programming language which will, given an array of integers as an argument, output an array of the mathematical squares of the input numbers. In addition, your function should ensure that the result array is sorted in ascending order.

Make sure you handle edge cases appropriately and your program functions properly with large inputs.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is the implementation in Java:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class Main {
    
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {5, 3, -1, 7, 2};
        int[] squaredAndSorted = squareAndSort(nums);
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(squaredAndSorted)); // Output: [1, 4, 9, 25, 49]
    }

    public static int[] squareAndSort(int[] numbers) {
        if(numbers == null || numbers.length == 0) {
            return numbers; // return input as it is if null or empty
        }

        int n = numbers.length;
        int[] squared = new int[n];

        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            squared[i] = numbers[i] * numbers[i];
        }

        Arrays.sort(squared);
        return squared;
    }
}
```

This program will first square each element in the array, then it will sort the array in ascending order using the sort function from the `java.util.Arrays` package. The time complexity of this function is O(n log n) due to the sort operation, where n is the length of the input array. 

The edge case handled is the case when the input array is null or empty, where the input array is returned as it is. 

The Java sort function uses a modified mergesort algorithm which is capable of handling large inputs efficiently.

Please note that this implementation assumes that the integer square operation does not overflow. If the input array's elements can be very large, additional code should be added to handle potential integer overflow issues.
<|end|>


