I migrated a lambda script to fargate. The task is created and started via a scheduler. Once my code has finished its task which involves some queries to DynamoDB and writing files to S3 I would like the fargate task to stop.
I was under the assumption that this would just be the case following the flow above. Should I be expecting this or do I need something to explicitly stop the task from running?
I added some logging after I complete all my promises within the code and it looks like everything is good so I don't believe something is still waiting on that front.
Any suggestions are greatly appreciated.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
AWS Fargate tasks that are started by AWS ECS will automatically stop once the main process within the task's container is finished executing. This is usually the behavior you would see when running applications inside Docker containers.

If your code in the Fargate task is still running, it could mean there's something in your Node.js code that's keeping the process alive.

An important detail to mention regarding Node.js runtime (which is common runtime for lambda or fargate tasks) is that Node.js applications continue to run as long as there is anything in the event loop. If there’s a callback waiting for some I/O event, the process will keep running. This could be an open database connection, a timeout, an interval, a PROMISE that has not been fulfilled or rejected, or even an error in your asynchronous code.

Try to make sure you clean up everything after your promises are all dealt with. For example, close any database connection and clear any timeout or interval that your have set.

If your code looks fine and you are still having issues, you may have to add explicit code to exit your Node.js application by calling `process.exit()` after you're sure all of your asynchronous operations have completed.
  
Warning: Although `process.exit()` sounds good in theory, it's generally regarded as a dangerous function to call in most situations because it forces the process to abruptly stop, which means any remaining operations (like asynchronous writes to the database or S3) that haven't yet finished will be interrupted, causing possible data loss or corruption. 

It's better to let Node.js exit naturally by not leaving anything hanging in the event loop. Use `process.exit()` only when you're absolutely sure all your operations and callbacks have finished.
<|end|>


