I am using a bootstrap css theme on my website but in a certain sub section of it I want to only use the plain bootstrap css. Is this possible without resorting to using an iframe?
For clarity this is what I want:

<div> <!-- this is styled by both boostrap and theme css -->
  <div> <!-- this as well -->
    <div id="subsection"> <!-- this is styled only by bootstrap css -->
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<|end|>
<|asst|>
There's no out-of-box way to do this without removing the theme styling from the particular subsection manually. 

Bootstrap themes typically override bootstrap's base styles, and so will apply to all sub-elements you create within a styled parent. As a result, they remain applied unless specified otherwise on a case-by-case basis.

Here's what you can do:

1. Create a special CSS class with the name `no-bootstrap-theme` or similar. 

2. Apply this class to the `subsection` div (`<div id="subsection" class="no-bootstrap-theme">`)

3. Write new CSS rules for every typical bootstrap style you want to "reset" or revert under `no-bootstrap-theme` class.

This way, you will essentially override the bootstrap theme styles and only use the original bootstrap styles.
  
Note though that this could be quite a lot of work based on the complexity of your theme and the number of styles you'd want to overwrite.

An alternative is to use an iframe as you figured already, but this can bring its own sets of challenges and performance hits, so it's not ideal if not crucially needed.
<|end|>


