You can create a trailing or motion effect behind an SVG shape using various techniques, but I will demonstrate it using the CSS `animate` property.

The entire SVG code should be set inside a `<div>` for easier manipulation. You can apply a CSS class to this `<div>`, which includes properties to create the desired effect. 

Here's a simple CSS animation example for trails/motion effect:

```html
<head>
  <style>
    .motion-effect {
      position: relative;
      animation: trail 2s infinite;
    }

    @keyframes trail {
      0% { opacity: 1; }
      50% { opacity: 0.5; }
      100% {
        opacity: 1;
        transform: translateX(10px);
      }
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="motion-effect">
    <!-- Your SVG code here -->
  </div>
</body>
```

In this example, the SVG will fade to `50%` opacity and move `10px` to the right, creating a motion trail effect. The `2s` setting determines the duration of the animation, and `infinite` makes the animation loop forever.

Note: SVG animation is a large and complex topic. If you need a specific trailing/motion effect, you might need to use advanced techniques or libraries like GSAP, Anime.js, or specific SVG animation techniques. Depending on the effect you desire, you may also need to convert your SVG to a sequence of frames or use a drawing/animation software.
<|end|>


