I have an assembly, written in C++\CLI, which uses some of enumerations, provided by .Net. It has such kind of properties:   


property System::ServiceProcess::ServiceControllerStatus ^ Status  
{  
    ServiceControllerStatus ^ get()  
    {  
        return (ServiceControllerStatus)_status->dwCurrentState;   
    }  
}



it works fine, but when i use this assembly from my C# code, type of this property is  


System.Enum



and i have to make type-cast


if ((ServiceControllerStatus)currentService.Status == ServiceControllerStatus.Running)
     //do smth



The question is simple: why is it so, and how to fix it ?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Your problem arises from bridging the native C++ and the managed .NET worlds. When you compile your C++/CLI code, the CLR cannot correctly infer the type of the enum, so it defaults to `System.Enum`. 

This cast is necessary, but it can be improved by adding the correct type in the C++/CLI code.

Instead of using:

```
property System::ServiceProcess::ServiceControllerStatus ^ Status
```

You should use:

```
property System::ServiceProcess::ServiceControllerStatus Status
```

By not using a pointer `^` for the return type, you directly infer that `Status` is specifically of type `ServiceControllerStatus`, and not `System.Enum`. 

This way, you won't need to cast the `Status` in your C# code, and you can directly write:

```
if (currentService.Status == ServiceControllerStatus.Running)
     //do smth
```

Hopefully, that should eliminate the need for type-casting in C# code. C++/CLI can be slightly tricky with this kind of bridging between native and managed code.
<|end|>


