2. The images could be child elements in a parent element that has a specified width and height. The parent's size could be limiting the size of your images. Check the parent's CSS codes and make necessary adjustments.

3. Check to ensure the CSS selector you are using actually targets the images. Sometimes, spelling errors or wrong selectors could make you think the image is not resizing when, in fact, your resize codes are not getting to the images at all.

4. If the images are background images, ensure you set `background-size` property to `contain` or `cover`.

5. The HTML `img` element maintains the aspect ratio of the image by default. As a result, if you try to resize the image's width alone, the browser will automatically adjust the height to maintain the aspect ratio. The same applies if you try to adjust the height alone. To fully control the size of the image, you need to set both the width and the height.

