You can generate a random alphanumeric string with a given length in PHP using a custom function. Here is an example below:

```php
function generateRandomString($length = 10) {
    $characters = '0123456789abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ';
    $charactersLength = strlen($characters);
    $randomString = '';
    for ($i = 0; $i < $length; $i++) {
        $randomString .= $characters[rand(0, $charactersLength - 1)];
    }
    return $randomString;
}

echo generateRandomString();  // Outputs: H3vcW44T0j for example
```
In this function, we started by defining all possible characters we want to include in our string (numbers 0-9 and letters a-z (both lower and uppercase)). We then create a variable to store the length of this string.

In a for loop, a random character is chosen from the possible characters and concatenated to our final string. This is repeated until it reaches the designated length we wish our final string to have. 

Please note the result will be different each time you run our function because its output is (pseudo)random. 

The $length parameter can be changed to generate a string of different length.
