def rearrange_words(s, word_order):
    words = s.split(', ')
    return [words[i] for i in word_order]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is the equivalent C# code that reads a string, splits it into words, and rearranges according to a defined order:
```CSharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        string text = "How, to, Rearrange, Words, in, c#";
        int[] order = { 5, 3, 2, 0, 1, 4 };

        List<string> rearrangedWords = RearrangeWords(text, order);

        foreach (string word in rearrangedWords)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(word);
        }
    }

    public static List<string> RearrangeWords(string s, int[] word_order)
    {
        string[] words = s.Split(", ");
        List<string> result = new List<string>();

        for(int i = 0; i < word_order.Length; i++)
        {
            if (word_order[i] < words.Length)
            {
                result.Add(words[word_order[i]]);
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

You can run this code to take in a string `text`, split it into words separated by commas, and then rearrange the words according to the `order` array. Unless a word_order element doesn't have the corresponding index in words array to ensure array Index Out of Range exception isn't raised.

Note: The above code doesn't handle situations where the integers in word_order could correspond to a word index that is outside words array range.
<|end|>


