&lt;p&gt;
Consider a pair of positive integers $p$, $q$ ($p \lt q$) that we shall term as &lt;dfn&gt;reciprocal&lt;/dfn&gt;. This pair is defined as reciprocal if there exists a positive integer $r\lt p$ that is equivalent to both the inverse of $p$ modulo $q$ and the inverse of $q$ modulo $p$.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
As an illustration, the pair $(3,5)$ is a reciprocal pair with $r=2$.&lt;br&gt;
We define $F(N)$ as the cumulative sum of $p+q$ for all reciprocal pairs $(p,q)$ where $p \le N$.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
For instance, $F(5)=59$ is derived from these four reciprocal pairs $(3,5)$, $(4,11)$, $(5,7)$ and $(5,19)$.&lt;br&gt;
It is also provided that $F(10^2) = 697317$.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Your task is to compute the value of $F(2\cdot 10^6)$.&lt;/p&gt;
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This problem can be tackled using number theory. Given $p<q$ and positive integer $r<p$, finding an integer that satisfies the properties of a reciprocal pair involves solving two equations:

i) $r \equiv q^{-1} \mod p$

ii) $r \equiv p^{-1} \mod q$

The idea is to select each integer $p$ from 1 to $N$ and for each $p$ find its reciprocal pairs, and then store them in a dictionary. Then sum up the pairs (i.e. the keys in the dictionary) with values more than 1. 

We will use the Extended Euclidean algorithm, which calculates the inverse of a number modulo another. This algorithm would be necessary because Python does not have built-in functionality to handle modular multiplicative inverse for large numbers.

Here is a sample Python code that you can use:

```python
from collections import defaultdict

def extended_gcd(a, b):
    if a == 0:
        return b, 0, 1
    else:
        gcd, x, y = extended_gcd(b % a, a)
        return gcd, y - (b // a) * x, x

def mod_inverse(a, m):
    gcd, x, y = extended_gcd(a, m)
    return x % m

def reciprocal(N):
    pairs = defaultdict(int)
    result = 0
    for p in range(1, N+1):
        for q in range(2*p, N+1, p):
            temp = mod_inverse(q, p)
            if temp*p<q:
                pairs[temp] += 1
                if pairs[temp] == 2:
                    result += temp
    return result

print(reciprocal(2000000))
```

Please note that this implementation may be slow if the value of $N$ is considerably large (e.g., $10^6$ or above). This is because the time complexity of the problem is approximately $O(N^2)$. Hence, for larger values of $N$, you might want to make some additional optimizations, possibly involving more number theory, to the code.
<|end|>


