Full error: NotAllowedError: The request is not allowed by the user agent or the platform in the current context, possibly because the user denied permission.
Why I got first time, but not second when ?

const shareData = {
  files: [
    new File([buffer], `${imgId}.mp4`, {
      type: "video/mp4",
    }),
  ],
  title: "Megosztom",
  text: "Mentsd le a képekbe, és oszd meg storyban.",
};



navigator
  .share(shareData)
  .then(() => {
    log(
      {
        text: `'The share was successful 1 ` + getTempUserShortId(),
      },
      env
    );
  })
  .catch((error) => {
    log(
      {
        text: `There was an error sharing 1 ${error} ` + getTempUserShortId(),
      },
      env
    );
  });
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The `navigator.share` method requires user interaction to work. This error usually appears when the method is not triggered by direct user interaction. In your code, if `navigator.share` is not called on a user action (like a click), this may be the reason for the issue. 

The Web Share API has to be triggered by "user activation". User activation (also known as user engagement) is a transient state that an API can query, which signals that the user is actively interacting with the website (for example clicking, dragging selection or text, typing on a form, etc.).

In short: `navigator.share(shareData)` must be inside a user-triggered event like onClick, onKeyPress, etc. It can't run, for example, in the then() call after a fetch() Promise, even if that Promise was initiated by a user action.

So if the code you provided was not triggered by a user action, on the second try, it might have been triggered by a user action, and that's why it works. Without having a look at the full code or knowing complete context, it's hard to say why it wouldn't work the first time but works the second time. 

Also note, not all browsers have full support for the Web Share API. For example, Firefox and Internet Explorer do not support it at all. Always check if `navigator.share` is available before using it.
<|end|>


