Create a rectangular parallelepiped with dimensions specified as follows: length of 20cm, breadth of 10cm and altitude of 15cm. Upon expanding these dimensions--specifically, elongating the length by 2cm, augmenting the breadth by 3cm and incrementing the altitude by 1cm--determine the extent to which the volumetric capacity of the rectangular parallelepiped has escalated.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The volume of a rectangular parallelepiped is obtained by multiplying its dimensions--i.e. its length, breadth, and altitude.

So, the initial volume of the parallelepiped is 20cm * 10cm * 15cm = 3000 cubic cm.

After expanding the dimensions, the volume becomes (20cm + 2cm) * (10cm + 3cm) * (15cm + 1cm) = 22cm * 13cm * 16cm = 4576 cubic cm.

Therefore, the extent to which the volumetric capacity of the rectangular parallelepiped has escalated is 4576 cubic cm - 3000 cubic cm = 1576 cubic cm.
<|end|>


