<|asst|>
It appears that you haven't provided any specific code or framework you're using, so I'll provide a general approach to resolve the issue.

1. Set up a position tracker (like `x` and `y` coordinates) in your application.
2. Set up the directional movements (e.g., up will increase `y`, left will decrease `x`, and so on).
3. When a direction is chosen, update the `x` and `y` coordinates accordingly.
4. Check if the new coordinates exceed the boundaries you've set.
5. If they do exceed the boundaries, reset the `x` and `y` coordinates back to the center coordinates.

Here's a basic example in JavaScript:

```javascript
// Set up the initial x and y values
let x = 0;
let y = 0;

// Set up the boundary values
let maxX = 10;
let maxY = 10;

// Function to move in the desired direction
function move(direction) {
    switch (direction) {
        case 'up':
            y++;
            break;
        case 'down':
            y--;
            break;
        case 'left':
            x--;
            break;
        case 'right':
            x++;
            break;
        default:
            break;
    }

    // Reset to center if out of boundaries
    if (x > maxX || y > maxY || x < -maxX || y < -maxY) {
        x = 0;
        y = 0;
        console.log('Returning to center');
    }

    console.log('Current position:', x, y);
}

// Test the function
move('up');
move('down');
move('left');
move('right');
move('up');
```

You may need to adjust this logic to fit your specific needs and the particular framework or library you're using. If you provide more details about your setup, we may be able to give more specific advice.
