<|end|>
<|asst|>
To capture a high-quality, uncompressed fullscreen video, you would want to go for a high-performance screen recording software. I'm going to suggest you a few options below.

1. OBS Studio: OBS (Open Broadcaster Software) is a free and open-source software for video recording and live streaming which allows recording at high quality without compromising system performance. It allows for scaling up to 1920x1080.
   
2. Camtasia: This is a paid, professional software that enables high-quality screen recording and also includes additional features for video editing. It offers smooth and crisp video capturing up to 4K resolution.

3. Bandicam: It is a powerful screen recorder that can capture high-quality videos up to 4K Ultra HD resolution. With the built-in codec, Bandicam can produce high-quality results with small file sizes.

All these options should allow you to export your capture as an uncompressed AVI file. 

In some cases, choppy video can also result from inadequate system resources, such as low RAM or an underpowered graphics card. Consider upgrading these components if they continue to be a bottleneck. 

As for hardware solutions, Elgato offers a variety of game capture hardware that can record smooth, high quality video at up to 4K60 HDR10 quality. However, these are typically used for capturing gameplay from gaming consoles or other PCs, and may not be what you're looking for if you're trying to capture from the same PC you're working on.

Lastly, you might want to fine-tune the recording settings to avoid any potential choppiness. Always make sure your framerate is set correctly. The standard frame rate for video is 30fps (frames per second).

Please note that high quality, uncompressed video could result in very large file sizes, so ensure you have sufficient storage space.
