I want to convert a primitive to a string, and I tried:


myInt.toString();



This fails with the error:


int cannot be dereferenced



Now, I get that primitives are not reference types (ie, not an Object) and so cannot have methods.  However, Java 5 introduced autoboxing and unboxing (a la C#... which I never liked in C#, but that's beside the point).  So with autoboxing, I would expect the above to convert myInt to an Integer and then call toString() on that.

Furthermore, I believe C# allows such a call, unless I remember incorrectly.  Is this just an unfortunate shortcoming of Java's autoboxing/unboxing specification, or is there a good reason for this?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Java's autoboxing only works when you are assigning the value of a primitive to a variable of its respective wrapper class or when passing the value of a primitive to a method that expects an instance of the respective wrapper class.

It does not work when you try to call a method directly on a primitive.

What you can do instead is to either:

1. create a new instance of the wrapper class and call toString on it:

```java
Integer myInteger = new Integer(myInt);
String str = myInteger.toString();
```

1. use the static `toString()` method of the wrapper class:

```java
String str = Integer.toString(myInt);
```

The latter option is recommended, because it doesn't create an unnecessary wrapper object. Java's autoboxing and unboxing, while convenient, comes with an overhead and should be used judiciously.
<|end|>


