<|asst|>
There seem to be no libraries that directly transform HTML to Markdown in Java. However, there might be an indirect way of doing it using Jsoup and two libraries called Flexmark and ReverseMarkdown.

Firstly, Jsoup's purpose is to clean the HTML: Jsoup modifies the content beyond recognition, if it wants to.

Secondly, Flexmark's purpose is to convert the cleaned HTML to commonMark: the commonMark will then represent the structure of the original HTML. 

Finally, ReverseMarkdown's purpose is to convert the commonMark into markdown: the markdown will then represent the relevant parts of the original HTML.

Here's a usage example:

```java
import org.jsoup.Jsoup;
import com.vladsch.flexmark.html2md.converter.FlexmarkHtmlConverter;
import net.karlmoad.ReverseMarkdown;

...

public static String getMarkdown(String html) {

    // First phase: Cleaning HTML with Jsoup
    String cleanHtml = Jsoup.parse(html).html();

    // Second phase: Convert Cleaned HTML into commonMark with flexmark
    String commonMark = FlexmarkHtmlConverter.builder().build().convert(cleanHtml);

    // Third phase: Convert the commonMark to Markdown with ReverseMarkdown
    ReverseMarkdown reverseMarkdown = new ReverseMarkdown();
    String markdown = reverseMarkdown.convert(commonMark);

    return markdown;
}
```
Remember to include these libraries in your project:

