Compose an algorithm coded in the C programming language that meticulously scrutinizes and determines the primality of a given numerical input, with extensive attention to factors and divisibility, thereby confirming whether the number is prime or not.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a simple C program that determines if a given number is prime:

```C
#include <stdio.h>

int check_prime(int n) {
   int i;

   if (n <= 1) {
        return 0;  // zero and one are not prime
   }
   for (i=2; i*i<=n; i++) {
        if (n % i == 0) {
            return 0;
        }
   }
   return 1;
}

int main() {
   int num;
   
   printf("Enter a number: ");
   scanf("%d", &num);

   if (check_prime(num) == 1)
        printf("%d is a prime number\n", num);
   else
        printf("%d is not a prime number\n", num);

   return 0;
}
```

In this program, the `check_prime` function takes an integer as input and checks for primality. The function uses a for loop to check each integer up to the square root of the number to see if it is a factor. If it finds a factor, it returns 0, indicating that the number is not prime. If it doesn't find any factors other than 1 and itself, it returns 1, indicating that the number is prime.

The `main` function takes user input, passes it to `check_prime`, and then prints out whether the number is prime or not based on the result.
<|end|>


