Here is a simple C++ function to calculate the factorial of a positive number:

```cpp
#include<iostream>

using namespace std; 

long long factorial(int n){ 
    long long fact = 1; 
    for(int i = 1; i <=n; ++i) { 
        fact *= i; 
    } 
    return fact; 
} 

int main() { 
    int num; 
    cout << "Enter a positive integer: "; 
    cin >> num; 
    cout << "Factorial of " << num << " = " << factorial(num);    
    return 0; 
} 
```

In this C++ code, a positive integer is input by the user, which is then passed to the `factorial()` function. The function calculates the factorial by starting from 1 and multiplying each number up to the given number, and it returns the final result. 

Please note this script works for smaller numbers. Factorial increases very quickly and for large numbers (greater than ~20) it may go beyond the maximum limit of the `long long` type. If you need to calculate the factorial of very large numbers, you'll need to use some form of arbitrary-precision arithmetic or a language feature/library supporting big numbers.
