Create a function in Java that not only converts a given Unix timestamp into readable date and time in the format "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss", but also takes into consideration the user's current time zone. The function should then convert the timestamp into local time. The given timestamp will be in the following example format: 1586714115. The function should handle any edge cases such as daylight saving time changes.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a small Java function that converts the Unix timestamp into a received timezone's date and time format.

To highlight, this function uses java.time classes 'Instant', 'ZoneId' and 'ZonedDateTime', which should eliminate any issues with edge cases such as daylight saving time changes.

Java function:

```java
import java.time.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(convertUnixToTimeZone(1586714115, "Asia/Kolkata"));
    }

    public static String convertUnixToTimeZone(long timestamp, String zone) {
        Instant instant = Instant.ofEpochSecond(timestamp);
        ZoneId systemZone = ZoneId.of(zone); // making use of user's timezone
        ZonedDateTime zdt = ZonedDateTime.ofInstant(instant, systemZone);
        return DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss").format(zdt);
    }
}
```

In this program, a static method 'convertUnixToTimeZone' is created which takes a Unix timestamp and a string 'zone' that represents the ID of the user's current time zone. An Instant object is created from the timestamp, and a ZoneId object from the timezone. A ZonedDateTime object then uses these to construct a date-time value that represents the same point on the timeline as the Unix timestamp, but with the rules for date and time computations of the timezone applied. Finally, it formats this timestamp into the "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss" format.

You may change the "Asia/Kolkata" with your desired/id time zone to accomplish your need, and also notice the Unix time is using seconds not milliseconds.

Here is list of all available zone ids: https://garygregory.wordpress.com/2013/06/18/what-are-the-java-timezone-ids/
<|end|>


