<|asst|>
To superimpose multiple SVGs, you simply need to position them on top of each other. This can be achieved using CSS for styling. The 'position' property in CSS is used to handle the position of elements. You can use the 'absolute' value for this property to position all the SVGs on one spot.

Here are the steps to do this:

1) Import or create the SVG files that you want to superimpose.

2) Integrate the SVGs into your HTML file. Each SVG should be embedded in its own 'div' tag like this:

```html
<div class="svg-container">
  <div class="svg-item">
    <!-- Your first SVG code goes here -->
  </div>
  <div class="svg-item">
    <!-- Your second SVG code goes here -->
  </div>
  <!-- and so on for additional SVGs -->
</div>
```

3) Use CSS to position the SVGs on top of each other:

```css
.svg-container { 
  position: relative; // This makes the 'svg-container' div the reference point for the absolute positioning of children SVGs.
  width: 100px; // Or any other size you prefer
  height: 100px; // Same as the width for a perfect square
}

.svg-item {
  position: absolute; // This makes all SVGs pile on each other
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
}
```
Make sure all your SVGs have the same viewBox attributes to ensure they're the same size and align correctly. Using CSS and HTML together in this way allows you to overlap multiple SVGs to create a composite visual representation. Note that the SVG that appears on top will be the one that comes last in your HTML.
<|end|>


