Is there any easy way to create a class that uses  that writes out a user-friendly file-size?


public static string GetFileSizeString(string filePath)
{
    FileInfo info = new FileInfo(@"c:\windows\notepad.exe");
    long size = info.Length;
    string sizeString = size.ToString(FileSizeFormatProvider); // This is where the class does its magic...
}



It should result in strings formatted something like "", "", "" and so on.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can do it using extension methods. Below is an example:

```csharp
public static class FileSizeExtensions
{
    private static readonly string[] SizeSuffixes = {"bytes", "KB", "MB", "GB", "TB", "PB", "EB", "ZB", "YB"};

    public static string ToFileSizeString(this long value)
    {
        if (value < 0) { return "-" + ToFileSizeString(-value); }
        if (value == 0) { return "0.0 bytes"; }

        var mag = (int)Math.Log(value, 1024);
        var adjustedSize = (decimal)value / (1L << (mag * 10));

        return $"{adjustedSize:n1} {SizeSuffixes[mag]}";
    }

    public static string GetFileSizeString(this string filePath)
    {
        var info = new FileInfo(filePath);
        return info.Length.ToFileSizeString();
    }
}
```
Now you can use it like below:

```csharp
var sizeString = @"c:\windows\notepad.exe".GetFileSizeString();
```
This extension method starts from "bytes" and goes up to "YB" (yottabytes), which is way beyond current technology. It also formats the size to one decimal place. 

Please note that these are binary prefixes, indicating multiples of 1024, which is common for computer storage. The International System of Units (SI) and International Electrotechnical Commission (IEC) define these prefixes differently, and use the term "kibibyte" (KiB), "mebibyte" (MiB), etc. to avoid ambiguity.
<|end|>


