If I have a subclass that has yet to implement a function provided by the base class, I can override that function and have it throw a `NotSupportedException`.  Is there a way to generate a compile-time error for this to avoid only hitting this at runtime?

Update: I can't make the base class abstract.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
No, you can not generate a compile time error in this scenario in most of the languages, if the base class cannot be declared as abstract. Compile time errors can be generated by compilers based on rules specified by the language specification like type safety, function signatures, language syntax etc. Your requirement is a step outside the language/ compiler's ability to enforce function implementation in this scenario. 

However, you can reduce the chance of runtime error by following good programming practices, like:

1) Clear documentation: Clearly document that subclasses are meant to override specific methods, so that anyone who creates a subclass knows what they need to implement.

2) Unit Testing: Write Unit tests to make sure, each and every class and subclass functions as expected. It will reduce runtime errors.

If your purpose was to make sure subclasses implement a certain method, one workaround (In Object Oriented programming languages) could be to have a constructor in the base class that requires as a parameter a reference to a function that fits the signature of the function you want implemented. This doesn't enforce that a subclass implements the function directly, but it does enforce that anyone creating an instance of a subclass has to provide an implementation of that function. But this is not a neat and clean approach. 

Ultimately, this is a limitations that some object-oriented languages have when you can't make the base class abstract.
<|end|>


