From the start of using pycharm i am facing problems with working with libraries
I tried reinstalling python, pip, pycharm; adding and readding to path. I also tried using pipenv instead of virtenv.. and it worked once, but.. now again-
i use $pip install numpy |as example| in cmd window it says to be successfully installed. Go to pycharm, type 'import numpy'.. and nothing happens. I know i can download manually: go to settings and so on.. but it would be much beter if pip installed with cmd would be instantly displayed in Pycharm. Anybody, please, help.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This problem arises because PyCharm creates its own virtual environment and pip installs packages to the global Python environment. Therefore any module you install via the command line isn't available in the PyCharm project. 

1. If you would like to have the package available in PyCharm, you should install it via PyCharm. You can install packages in Pycharm as follows:
   - Go to `File > Settings > Project > Project Interpreter`
   - Click on the + button on the right side.
   - Search for the package you want (e.g., `numpy`), and install it.

2. Alternatively, you can change the Python interpreter of PyCharm to your global Python environment.
   - Go to `File > Settings > Project > Project Interpreter`
   - Click on the `Project Interpreter` dropdown and select `show all`
   - Click on the + button and navigate to the `python.exe` file of your global Python environment
   
3. If you want to use the command line, you need to use the pip that's inside PyCharm's virtualenv.
   - First, you need to find the location of the virtual environment used by PyCharm. You can do so by checking the `Project Interpreter` page as mentioned above.
   - Navigate to the `Scripts` folder inside that folder.
   - Use the `pip` present in that folder to install your package.
   E.g., `C:\Users\yourusername\.virtualenvs\yourvirtualenv\Scripts\pip install numpy`
   
Note: Always remember that PyCharm creates its own virtual environment, and the Python interpreter used in the command line may not be the same as the one used by PyCharm.
<|end|>


