I need to get a log of user access to our `SQL Server` so I can track  and . Is there a hidden table or something I'm missing that has this information for me? To my knowledge the application I'm looking at does not track this at the application level.

I'm currently working on `SQL Server 2000`, but will moving to `SQL Server 2005` shortly, so solutions for both are greatly appreciated.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
SQL Server 2000 doesn't have any built-in functionality to track login activity. But you can create a Login auditing by enabling login auditing to the Windows event log or SQL Server log.

To monitor the login activity, you need to enable login auditing at the SQL Server instance level as the following:

1. Open SQL Server Management Studio.
2. Connect to the target SQL Server instance.
3. Right-click on the SQL Server instance name, and select Properties.
4. In the SQL Server instance Properties dialog box, select the Security page.
5. In the Login auditing section, you can select None to disable the login auditing, select Failed logins only to log events for login failed attempts, select Successful logins only to log events for the successful logins only, and select Both failed and successful logins to log all login attempts. Here select "Both failed and successful logins".
6. Click on OK to apply the change.

These steps are similar for SQL Server 2005 as well. 

However, for more detailed logging, SQL Server auditing feature was introduced in SQL Server 2008 and that provides a simple and flexible way to track events that occur in SQL Server such as who is accessing what and when it was accessed.

For SQL Server 2000, you could manually create a logon trigger to track user logins, but this feature will not be available until SQL Server 2005. Triggers fire after the event has occurred, so they can't be used to prevent the event, they can only log it or perform other tasks in response to it.

For more advanced auditing, you might consider third-party options or upgrade to a newer version of SQL Server. SQL Server 2008 onwards, SQL Server introduces SQL Server Audit Action Groups (SQL Server Audit) which you can use to track database user logins.
<|end|>


