<|user|>
Develop a Java algorithm that not only calculates the average of a dynamic array of float type elements but also determines the median and mode of the array. Additionally, implement exception handling for potential errors such as an empty array.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a simple Java algorithm to solve the problem:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Statistics {

    private ArrayList<Float> list;

    public Statistics(ArrayList<Float> list) {
        if(list.size() <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("List cannot be empty");
        }
        this.list = list;
    }

    public float average() {
        float sum = 0f;
        for (Float aList: list) {
            sum += aList;
        }
        return sum / list.size();
    }

    public float median() {
        Collections.sort(list);
        float median;
        int totalElements = list.size();
        if (totalElements % 2 == 0) {
            float sumOfMiddleElements = list.get(totalElements / 2) + list.get(totalElements / 2 - 1);
            median = sumOfMiddleElements / 2;
        } else {
            median = list.get(totalElements / 2);
        }
        return median;
    }

    public ArrayList<Float> mode() {
        HashMap<Float, Integer> freqMap = new HashMap<>();
        for (Float aList: list) {
            if(freqMap.containsKey(aList)) {
                freqMap.put(aList, freqMap.get(aList) + 1);
            } else {
                freqMap.put(aList, 1);
            }
        }

        int maxVal = Collections.max(freqMap.values());
        ArrayList<Float> modes = new ArrayList<>();

        for(Map.Entry<Float, Integer> entry: freqMap.entrySet()) {
            if(entry.getValue() == maxVal) {
                modes.add(entry.getKey());
            }
        }

        return modes;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<Float> list = new ArrayList<>();
        list.add(1.0f);
        list.add(2.0f);
        list.add(2.0f);
        list.add(3.0f);
        list.add(4.0f);

        Statistics stats = new Statistics(list);

        System.out.println("Average: " + stats.average());
        System.out.println("Median: " + stats.median());
        System.out.println("Mode: " + stats.mode());

    }
}
```

This program first creates an ArrayList of Floats in the main method and then uses it to create a `Statistics` object. The `Statistics` object takes an ArrayList of Floats as an argument and assigns it to `list`. The `Statistics` class has methods to calculate the average (`average()`), median (`median()`) and mode (`mode()`). These methods can be called on the `Statistics` object as shown in the `main` method. 

Each of these methods (`average()`, `median()`, `mode()`) operate on `list`, which is a member of `Statistics`. Error handling is done in the constructor where if the provided list is empty, an `IllegalArgumentException` will be thrown.
<|end|>


