Create a SQL query to order the strings in the 'name' column alphabetically and then group the results based on the length of their names, also sorted in ascending order. Furthermore, calculate the average length of the names in each group.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's an example of how you can create such an SQL query:

```sql
WITH ordered_names AS (
    SELECT name
    FROM your_table
    ORDER BY name
),
grouped_names AS (
    SELECT name, LENGTH(name) AS name_length
    FROM ordered_names
)
SELECT name_length, GROUP_CONCAT(name) AS names, AVG(name_length) AS average_length
FROM grouped_names
GROUP BY name_length
ORDER BY name_length;
```

This query first orders the names alphabetically by using a (CTE) common table expression named `ordered_names`. Then, in the `grouped_names` CTE, it calculates the length of each name and orders the results based on the length of their names. Finally, in the main query, it groups the results by the name length, concatenates the names in each group, and calculates the average length of the names in each group. Make sure to replace `your_table` with the name of your actual table containing the 'name' column.
