<|user|>
I am going through the 2nd edition of Paul Wilmott on Quantitative Finance, and came across the following,
[](https://i.stack.imgur.com/A2Y7T.png)
Shouldn't the last equality be
$$
F(t;T) = y(t;T) + \color{red}{(T-t)}\frac{\partial y}{\partial T}
$$
or maybe I am missing something simple?
Also, is anyone aware is there is an erratum online?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The third equality appears to be derived from the chain rule for derivatives, specifically, the total derivative. 

If we write \(y(t;T) = y\), \(T = T\) and \(F(t;T) = F\), and we want to compute \(dF\), then the differential \(dF\) (the total derivative of \(F\)) can be written as follows,

\[ dF = \frac{\partial F}{\partial y} dy + \frac{\partial F}{\partial T} dT, \]

Substituting the given assumptions that \( \frac{\partial F}{\partial y} = 1 \) and \( \frac{\partial F}{\partial T} = T - t \), we get

\[ dF = dy + (T - t) dT. \]

In the limit where \(dT \rightarrow 0\), this simplifies to 
\[ dF = dy, \]
so,
\[ F(t;T) = y(t;T), \] 

which is basically saying that the Forward price \(F(t;T)\) is equal to the current yield \(y(t;T)\), i.e., future prices are unbiased estimates of future spot prices in an efficient market. 

About your second question, I couldn't find an official erratum for Paul Wilmott on Quantitative Finance 2nd edition online.
<|end|>


