I'm looking to compare two sets of time series data via T-test (let's call them X and Y) to see if there's a statistically significant difference in means. I understand (I think) the need to test for auto-correlation to ensure there's no correlation between residuals of the regression. What I'm not understanding is what residuals are being referred to here.
Would this be the residuals from the regression of X vs Y? I'm unclear on what's considered the "predictor" here as I'm not trying to fit a model to the time series data.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In time series analysis, residuals are the differences between the observed and predicted values of data. Generally, this will relate to the regression model for a given variable - so when considering auto-correlation in the context of a T-Test between two time series, X and Y, you'd likely need to test for it in both separately.

In other words, examine the residuals in time series X (the differences between the observed and a predicted values in X) and do the same for Y. We do this because the residuals should ideally be random noise and not show any pattern or correlation over time - if they do, it breaches the assumption in a T-Test that the observations are independent. This could bias our results, leading to a Type I or Type II statistical error.

However, you are correct about not needing a predictor variable in a T-test. It is simply used to compare the means of two groups to see if they are significantly different from each other. In your case, you are comparing time series X and Y. But residuals and their auto-correlation come into play when checking underlying assumptions of a T-Test in the context of time series data.
<|end|>


