<|user|>
When connecting to remote hosts via ssh, I frequently want to bring a file on that system to the local system for viewing or processing.  Is there a way to copy the file over without (a) opening a new terminal/pausing the ssh session (b) authenticating again to either the local or remote hosts which works (c) even when one or both of the hosts is behind a NAT router?

The goal is to take advantage of as much of the current state as possible: that there is a connection between the two machines, that I'm authenticated on both, that I'm in the working directory of the file---so I don't have to open another terminal and copy and paste the remote host and path in, which is what I do now. The best solution also wouldn't require any setup before the session began, but if the setup was a one-time or able to be automated, than that's perfectly acceptable.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, you can use the `scp` (secure copy) command, which uses the ssh protocol to copy files between a local host and a remote host or between two remote hosts.

You need to use the `scp` command along with the file path at the source host and the destination host. Here is the basic syntax to copy a file from a remote host to your local host:

```
scp username@your-remote-host:/remote/path/to/file /local/path/to/destination
```

This command will copy the file at the specified location on the remote host to the specified location on your local machine.

If you want to copy a file from your local host to a remote host, you would simply reverse the order of the paths:

```
scp /local/path/to/file username@your-remote-host:/remote/path/to/destination
```

For the NAT issue, as long as you can ssh to the host then you should be able to scp to it as well.

You can use this command in the same terminal in which you are working, and you don't need to pause your ssh session. Also, it won't require you to authenticate again if you have set up ssh keys.

It's also possible to use an `rsync` command, which is a fast and versatile file-copying tool, perfect for synchronizing files and directories between locations with minimal data transfer:

```
rsync -avz -e ssh username@your-remote-host:/path/to/your/files /local/destination/path/
```

The `rsync` command is quite powerful and can give you more options than `scp`, such as progress reporting, preserving file permissions and ownership, and updating only files that have changed.

Both `scp` and `rsync` would need to be installed on the machines you are copying from and to, but they are present on most Unix-like systems by default. If you are using a Windows machine, you could use a tool like PuTTY, which includes a Secure Copy client.
<|end|>


