Develop a Python function that not only filters elements from a list of integers which are greater than the average value, but also takes into account a new, secondary list of white-listed integers that should always be returned, regardless of whether they are greater than or equal to the average value or not. Furthermore, ensure that the final returned list does not contain any duplicates by considering the elements from the second list as well. Additionally, provide the implementation assuming this filter function is a piece of a larger software which interacts with different components, and therefore the function should raise an exception when the input is not a list. 

Also, debug the erroneous code given below and utilize it as your reference:

```python
def above_avg_filter(in_list, whitelist=[]):
  if not isinstance(in_list, list):
    raise ValueError("Input should be a list")
  average = sum(in_list)/len(in_list)
  return [i for i in in_list if i > average or i in whitelist]

print(above_avg_filter("1234"))
```

Example Lists: Primary List - [12, 45, 23, 56, 89, 18], Secondary List - [10, 12, 15, 22]
