Given the code snippet below which attempts to solve a Sudoku problem using backtracking, explain why it fails to work correctly and correct the code. Also, explain the term "backtracking" and outline an optimized backtracking algorithm.

JavaScript Code Snippet:
```
function sudoku(board) {
  for (let i = 0; i < board.length; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < board[i].length; j++) {
      if (board[i][j] === '.') {
        for (let k = 1; k <= 9; k++) {
          if (valid(board, i, j, k)) {
            board[i][j] = `${k}`;
            if (sudoku(board)) {
              return true;
            } else {
              board[i][j] = '.';
            }
          }
        }
        return false;
      }
    }
  }
  return true;
}

function valid(board, row, col, num) {
  for (let i = 0; i < 9; i++) {
    const m = 3 * Math.floor(row / 3) + Math.floor(i / 3);
    const n = 3 * Math.floor(col / 3) + i % 3;
    if (board[row][i] === num || board[i][col] === num || board[m][n] === num) {
      return false;
    }
  }
  return true;
}
```
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This code is supposed to solve a Sudoku puzzle using backtracking. The backtracking is a "trial and error" algorithm where it tries to fill the Sudoku cells with valid numbers (1-9) and if it fails, it reverses or "backtracks" its steps to try again with different numbers.

The code's failure to operate correctly can be attributed to a type error in the `valid` function. This function checks the validity of the input `num` by comparing it with existing numbers on the Sudoku board. However, in JavaScript, you should make sure you are comparing the same types. In the `valid` function, `num` is a number, but `board[row][i]`, `board[i][col]`, and `board[m][n]` values are strings.

This can be easily corrected by converting `num` to a string when it's being compared:

```JavaScript
function valid(board, row, col, num) {
  for (let i = 0; i < 9; i++) {
    const m = 3 * Math.floor(row / 3) + Math.floor(i / 3);
    const n = 3 * Math.floor(col / 3) + i % 3;
    if (board[row][i] == num || board[i][col] == num || board[m][n] == num) {
      return false;
    }
  }
  return true;
}
```

Backtracking, as a concept, refers to the programming approach of solving problems recursively by trying to build a solution incrementally, one piece at a time. If a solution that we have built so far does not look promising, we discard or "backtrack" on parts of it and try other options. This is especially useful in problems which involve searching a solution space for a valid solution.

An optimized backtracking algorithm for Sudoku would incorporate "forward checking", where we keep track of the remaining valid numbers for each cell. This way, when one number is placed, we can immediately eliminate it as a valid number from the cells in the same row, column, and block. This avoids wasting time checking invalid numbers. This reduces the size of the search space and speeds up the solution finding process.

Also, "least constraining value" heuristic could be applied. This allows us to choose the number that imposes the least restriction on the remaining cells. The aim is to minimize the impact of a decision on future steps and avoid or delay encountering failures.
<|end|>


