I've been thinking about unifying some notions related to [ranking](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ranking), [order theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Order_theory), [ordinal data](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ordinal_data), and [graded posets](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graded_poset). While the notion of a grade in order theory is quite general, in some sense the way we talk about ranks is more general. I would like to bring rank-based statistics and order theory together in my theory development and analysis of data.
I would really like to know if there are others (especially but not exclusively in the literature) that have attempted to define ranks in a way that generalizes or interoperates with concepts in order theory.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
There isn't a noticeable abundance of research or literature that directly focuses on unifying concepts of rank-based statistics and order theory. However, some research has involved an investigation or application of these concepts simultaneously, implying their interoperability and potential for a unified framework.

1. A notable example is the study of partially ordered sets (posets) in the theoretical development of ordinal data analysis. For instance, Agresti (2010) in his book "Analysis of Ordinal Categorical Data" discusses methods for analyzing ordinal data using order-restricted statistical inference.

2. Researches in social choice theory and voting systems also bring together order theory and ranking. For instance, the study of ranking methods like the Borda count and Condorcet methods involve a consideration of the rankings as posets (Farina et al., 2008).

3. A variety of research in multivariate analysis, specifically in the study of multidimensional scaling, has involved the use of rank-based statistical techniques applied to dissimilarity data, which inherently involves an ordering or grading according to said dissimilarities (Borg & Groenen, 2005).

4. In the study of networks, concepts of ranking and order can be combined, especially when studying network centrality measures, with techniques often involving posets or graded posets.

5. The mathematical and algorithmic study of tournament rankings also involves an interplay between ranking processes and order theory.

Despite the implication of interoperability in these examples, a defined approach combining these two notions seems largely unexplored. It might be valuable to further sharpen these ideas into a formal structure, explore its properties and applicabilities in theoretical and empirical research.

References:

- Agresti, A. (2010). Analysis of ordinal categorical data. John Wiley & Sons.
- Farina, F., Grimalda, G., Schmidtchen, D., & Truger, A. (2008). Social Welfare. New Palgrave Dictionary of Economics, Second Edition. Edited by Steven, 1.
- Borg, I., & Groenen, P. J. (2005). Modern multidimensional scaling: Theory and applications. Springer Science & Business Media.
