<|asst|>
Sure, here is the equivalent Java code:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {

    private static int minSubArraySum(int[] nums) {
        int totalSum = 0;
        int minNum = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

        for (int num : nums) {
            totalSum += Math.abs(num);
            if (num < minNum) {
                minNum = num;
            }
        }

        boolean[] dp = new boolean[totalSum + 1];
        dp[0] = true;

        for (int num : nums) {
            for (int i = totalSum; i >= Math.abs(num); i--) {
                dp[i] = dp[i] || dp[i - Math.abs(num)];
            }
        }

        int result = totalSum;
        for (int i = 0; i <= totalSum / 2; i++) {
            if (dp[i]) {
                result = Math.min(result, totalSum - 2 * i);
            }
        }

        if (result == totalSum && minNum > 0) {
            return minNum;
        }
        return -result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(minSubArraySum(new int[]{2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 4}));  // Outputs 1
        System.out.println(minSubArraySum(new int[]{-1, -2, -3}));  // Outputs -6
    }
}
```

Please note that this code does the same as your Golang program: it calculates the minimum subarray sum for given array of integers.
