What makes differences in each head in the multiheaded attention in transformer?
As they are fed and trained in the exact same way, except the initialization of weights are different for each head to produce different sets of (Q,K,V) in each head. Such multi-headed design to me seems no difference than ensembling multiple models that are initialized differently.
Many sources claim that the multi-head attention 'can help capture meaning in different contextual subspace' without further substantiation or supporting proofs. Honestly I've been quite fed up with all those vague descriptions in the data science world that they make claim without mathematical rigor. I think I'm looking for more rigorous explanation as to why "multi-head attention 'can help capture meaning in different contextual subspace'" when they are simply an ensemble of identical models but weights randomly initialized?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Think of each "head" as looking at a different feature or aspect of the input data, which could provide complementary or disparate information to the other heads. These features are often not explicitly defined or dedicated (for example, one head particularly focuses on syntax, another on semantics), but learnt during training.

The concept doesn't necessarily differ from a naïve interpretation of why deeper networks (more stacked layers) can work better than shallow ones, or why having more neurons in a layer can improve performance. A network with more capacity can learn more complex tasks, given that you have enough data to appropriately train it.

Remember, different heads do not remain identical due to the backpropagation process. Based on the outcome of the loss function, the weights of different heads will be updated differently, meaning that while they may start off identical (save the random initialization), they diverge as the model is trained.

Each individual head after being trained often focus on different kinds of relations. For example, in the case of machine translation, you may find one focusing more on agreement in number, another on a distant syntactic dependency, a third one on a closer semantic relation, etc.

In a certain sense, it can also be considered as an ensemble of different models, where each "model" is looking at different features of the data. The difference is that unlike typical ensemble methods where each model is trained individually, here the training of all these "models" (heads) are coupled together. This means that during training, these heads affect each other and therefore may provide a better overall performance than naive ensembling. 

In sum, the multi-headed design allows different parts of the model to focus on different types of information, which can increase the model’s overall ability to recognise complex patterns or nuanced relationships.
<|end|>


