To create a reflection effect in SVG, you may create a linear gradient that goes from opaque to transparent, and apply this as a mask to a flipped version of your SVG shape. 

To achieve the reflection effect with your specific SVG code you may follow these steps.

Wrap the SVG with a group`<g>` tag for it to transform and reflect the group together.

Create a linear gradient inside the `<defs>` that can be applied for the image reflection.

Apply a reflection transformation to the second group element.

Here is your SVG adjusted to include reflection:

```html
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 64 64" >
    <defs>
        <linearGradient id="linear-gradient" y1="1" x2="0.5" y2="0" spreadMethod="pad">
            <stop offset="0%" stop-color="#000" stop-opacity="0.8"/>
            <stop offset="100%" stop-color="#000" stop-opacity="0"/>
        </linearGradient>
        <mask id="Mask">
            <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#linear-gradient)"/>
        </mask>
    </defs>
    <g>
      <path fill-rule="evenodd" clip-rule="evenodd" d="M11.6248 7.66474C11.6249 7.66498..."/>
    </g>
    <g transform="scale(1,-1)" mask="url(#Mask)" transform="translate(0, -34)">
        <path fill-rule="evenodd" clip-rule="evenodd" d="M11.6248 7.66474C11.6249 7.66498..."/>
    </g>
</svg>
```
You might need to adjust the translation according to your SVG's size to get the reflection to the right place. In this case, I used translate(0, -34) for demonstration purposes. The mask id on the reflection group is used to add the fade-out effect from the gradient we defined earlier.
<|end|>


