<|user|>
Many times I've seen links like these in HTML pages:


<a href='#' onclick='someFunc(3.1415926); return false;'>Click here !</a>



What's the effect of the `return false` in there?

Also, I don't usually see that in buttons.

Is this specified anywhere? In some spec in w3.org?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In HTML elements with event handlers, `return false;` is used to prevent the default action associated with the event from occurring. It's a way that JavaScript communicates to the browser not to perform the default action it would normally perform in response to the event.

In the specific context of your anchor (`<a>`) tag, returning false in the onclick event handler prevents the browser from navigating to the URL specified in the `href` attribute after the JavaScript code has run. It also stops the event bubbling up to parent elements.

In your case, the `#` in the `href` attribute is a placeholder that usually makes the browser jump to the top of the page. So when the anchor is clicked, `someFunc(3.1415926);` is executed and then `return false;` tells the browser not to "follow" the link, thus it doesn't jump to the top of the page or anywhere.

Regarding buttons, you don't typically see `return false;` because the default action of a button (submitting a form, for example) would be often what you want to occur. However, if you wanted to perform some JavaScript when the button is pressed but didn't want to continue with the default action, you could utilize `return false;`.

This behavior isn't covered specifically in the w3.org specs, but it's covered broadly under JavaScript events guidelines and resources.
<|end|>


