I am using SDL to render graphics like lines and pixels using C#. I have to display some text on screen as well. I haven't found any resource on this topic on the internet. Most of the code that I wrote is translated from C++. Now I need to render text and I am not able to translate its C++ implementation into C# since the classes used are not seem to be available in C#.
I have found this great C++ implementation for rendering text: [https://stackoverflow.com/a/22889483/574917](https://stackoverflow.com/a/22889483/574917)
I need to know how to do this in C#.
Code sample is highly appreciated.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In order to display text on the SDL window with C#, you will need an additional library called SDL_ttf for handling true type fonts. You can use it via the SDL2-CS C# Wrapper.

Here's a complete example of how to write text on the SDL window:

```csharp
using SDL2;
using System;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;

namespace SDL2Test
{
    class Program
    {
        const int SCREEN_WIDTH = 640;
        const int SCREEN_HEIGHT = 480;

        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            if (SDL.SDL_Init(SDL.SDL_INIT_VIDEO) < 0)
            {
                Console.WriteLine("SDL could not initialize! SDL_Error: {0}", SDL.SDL_GetError());
            }
            else
            {
                IntPtr window = SDL.SDL_CreateWindow(
                    "SDL Tutorial",
                    SDL.SDL_WINDOWPOS_UNDEFINED,
                    SDL.SDL_WINDOWPOS_UNDEFINED,
                    SCREEN_WIDTH,
                    SCREEN_HEIGHT,
                    SDL.SDL_WindowFlags.SDL_WINDOW_SHOWN);

                if (window == IntPtr.Zero)
                {
                    Console.WriteLine("Window could not be created! SDL_Error: {0}", SDL.SDL_GetError());
                }
                else
                {
                    IntPtr renderer = SDL.SDL_CreateRenderer(window, -1, SDL.SDL_RendererFlags.SDL_RENDERER_ACCELERATED);
                    
                    if (SDL_ttf.TTF_Init() == -1)
                    {
                        Console.WriteLine("SDL_ttf could not initialize! SDL_ttf Error: {0}", SDL.SDL_GetError());
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        IntPtr font = SDL_ttf.TTF_OpenFont("arial.ttf", 28);
                        if (font == IntPtr.Zero)
                        {
                            Console.WriteLine("Failed to load font! SDL_ttf Error: {0}", SDL.SDL_GetError());
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            SDL.SDL_Color textColor = new SDL.SDL_Color { r = 0xFF, g = 0xFF, b = 0xFF };

                            IntPtr textSurface = SDL_ttf.TTF_RenderText_Solid(font, "Hello, World!", textColor);
                            if (textSurface == IntPtr.Zero)
                            {
                                Console.WriteLine("Unable to render text surface! SDL_ttf Error: {0}", SDL.SDL_GetError());
                            }
                            else
                            {
                                IntPtr texture = SDL.SDL_CreateTextureFromSurface(renderer, textSurface);

                                SDL.SDL_Rect textRect = new SDL.SDL_Rect { x = 50, y = 50, w = 300, h = 300 };
                                SDL.SDL_RenderCopy(renderer, texture, IntPtr.Zero, ref textRect);

                                SDL.SDL_FreeSurface(textSurface);
                                SDL.SDL_DestroyTexture(texture);
                            }

                            SDL_ttf.TTF_CloseFont(font);
                        }
                    }

                    SDL.SDL_DestroyRenderer(renderer);
                    SDL.SDL_DestroyWindow(window);
                }
            }

            SDL_ttf.TTF_Quit();
            SDL.SDL_Quit();
        }
    }
}
```

In the above code, modify the "arial.ttf" with the path to the actual TTF font you are using. The "Hello, World!" is the text that you want to display and `(255, 255, 255)` is the white color of the text. The `(50, 50, 300, 300)` are the rectangle's location and dimension where your text will be displayed.

Make sure you add references to `SDL2.dll` and `SDL2_ttf.dll` in your project and also, those dll files should be present in your output directory. You can download these SDL libraries from [here](https://www.libsdl.org/download-2.0.php) and [here](https://www.libsdl.org/projects/SDL_ttf/).
<|end|>


