Design and create a complex React component that not just displays a table structure, but is also able to sort, filter, and paginate the data using state and props, obtained from an array of objects, each object representing a unique record.

For sorting functionality, the component should be able to sort the entries alphabetically by name, and numerically by age and id. For filtering, it should be able to filter entries on the basis of text input from the user. For pagination, it should show only 5 records by default but handle larger numbers of records by adding additional pages.

Use the following object array for your data:
const records = [
    { id: 1, name: 'Adam', age: 25 },
    { id: 2, name: 'James', age: 30 },
    { id: 3, name: 'Nick', age: 28 },
    { id: 4, name: 'John', age: 29 },
    // insert more records here
];

Bonus Question:
Extend the component to support dynamic addition and removal of records. Create a form with input fields for 'age' and 'name' and a submit button for adding new records. Each record in the table should also have a delete button for its removal. Handle the updates appropriately to keep the data consistent.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a basic functional component in React that will get the job done.

```jsx
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

const DataTable = ({ data }) => {
  const pageSize = 5;
  const [records, setRecords] = useState(data);
  const [displayedRecords, setDisplayedRecords] = useState([]);
  const [pageNum, setPageNum] = useState(0);
  const [filter, setFilter] = useState('');

  const handleSort = (field, numeric = false) => {
    setRecords(records.sort((a, b) => numeric ? a[field] - b[field] : a[field].localeCompare(b[field])));
  };

  const handleFilter = e => {
    setFilter(e.target.value);
  };

  const handlePreviousPage = () => {
    if (pageNum > 0) {
      setPageNum(pageNum - 1);
    }
  };

  const handleNextPage = () => {
    if (pageNum < Math.ceil(records.length / pageSize) - 1) {
      setPageNum(pageNum + 1);
    }
  };

  useEffect(() => {
    let filteredRecords = records.slice();
    if (filter) {
      filteredRecords = records.filter(record => record.name.toLowerCase().includes(filter.toLowerCase()));
    }
    setDisplayedRecords(filteredRecords.slice(pageNum * pageSize, (pageNum + 1) * pageSize));
  }, [records, pageNum, filter]);

  return (
    <div>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th onClick={() => handleSort('id', true)}>ID</th>
            <th onClick={() => handleSort('name')}>Name</th>
            <th onClick={() => handleSort('age', true)}>Age</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {displayedRecords.map(record => (
            <tr key={record.id}>
              <td>{record.id}</td>
              <td>{record.name}</td>
              <td>{record.age}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <button onClick={handlePreviousPage}>Previous page</button>
      <button onClick={handleNextPage}>Next page</button>
      <input type="text" onChange={handleFilter} value={filter} placeholder="Filter by name..." />
      <p>Total records: {records.length}</p>
      <p>Page: {pageNum + 1} of {Math.ceil(records.length / pageSize)}</p>
    </div>
  );
}

export default DataTable;
```

Please note, this is a basic implementation, it sorts the records in ascending order, paginates the records and has filters. It does not handle the case of dynamically adding and removing records. The sort buttons are simple text in table headers, could be replaced with better UI. The Page num and size are hardcoded and not dynamic.

A more robust solution would handle all these edge cases and improve the User Interface as well. For example, you could add a ascending/descending functionality to the sort and a more dynamic pagination system.

This code should be placed into a .js or .jsx file and imported into your main App.js to display it. It will need the 'data' passed in as a prop. You can import it like so in App.js

```jsx
import DataTable from "./DataTable";

function App() {
    const records = [
        { id: 1, name: 'Adam', age: 25 },
        { id: 2, name: 'James', age: 30 },
        { id: 3, name: 'Nick', age: 28 },
        { id: 4, name: 'John', age: 29 },
    ];
    return (
        <div>
            <DataTable data={records} />
        </div>
    );
}
export default App;
```
<|end|>


