Create a function using the Ruby programming language, which not only removes all forms of whitespace characters from a given string, but also handles complex whitespace cases such as tabs and newlines.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a simple method in Ruby that uses the `gsub` (global substitution) function to replace all whitespace characters with nothing:

```ruby
def remove_whitespace(string)
  string.gsub(/\s+/, '')
end
```

In this function, the `\s+` is a regular expression (regex) that matches any whitespace character: spaces, tabs, newlines, etc. The `+` means "one or more". `gsub` then replaces each match with nothing (`''`), effectively removing them.

You can call this function with a string as follows:

```ruby
puts remove_whitespace("This string\t has \nwhitespace.")
```

It will output `Thisstringhaswhitespace.`, which is the original string with all whitespace characters removed. 

You can modify this function as needed to suit more specific requirements. For example, you might only want to remove certain types of whitespace, or remove them in certain situations. Simply modify the regex to match what you want to replace.
<|end|>


