Craft a multistep C++ function that first validates the type of the inputs as integers, and then determines the smallest among the three, ensuring they all are in fact integers.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is how you can solve this problem:
```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <limits>

bool isValid(int input) {
    // A simple function to check if the given input is of integer type
    if(input <= std::numeric_limits<int>::max() && input >= std::numeric_limits<int>::min())
        return true;
    return false;
}

int smallest(int a, int b, int c) {
    // If they are not all integers, return a special value (-1)
    if(!isValid(a) || !isValid(b) || !isValid(c)) {
        return -1;
    }
    
    // Otherwise, find and return the smallest among them
    return std::min({a, b, c});
}

int main()
{
    int a = 5;
    int b = 10;
    int c = 8;

    int smallestNum = smallest(a, b, c);

    if(smallestNum == -1) {
        std::cout << "Inputs are not all integers." << std::endl;
    } else {
        std::cout << "Smallest integer is: " << smallestNum << std::endl;
    }
    
    return 0;
}
```
This program first checks if all the inputs are integers by calling `isValid()` function. If all are integers, it finds the smallest among them using `std::min()` function provided in the Standard Template Library (STL). If any of the inputs is not an integer, it returns -1.

Please note, function `isValid()` is not necessary in this case because of strong type checking in C++. This function is included just to fulfil requirement of the problem, in real application variables `a`, `b` and `c` are declared as integers so they are guaranteed to be integer.
<|end|>


