In your capacity as an artificial intelligence model, utilize the digits dataset accessible through sklearn.datasets to facilitate the training process of a gradient-boosting classifier. Subsequently, ascertain the precision and overall performance accuracy of the classifier and present these metrics.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To carry out the task, we first need to import all the relevant Python libraries. Then, we will load the digits data, create the gradient boosting classifier, train it and generate the classification report. Here is how it can be done:

```python
from sklearn import datasets
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split
from sklearn.ensemble import GradientBoostingClassifier
from sklearn.metrics import classification_report, accuracy_score

# Load the digits dataset
digits = datasets.load_digits()

# Prepare the training and test sets
X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(digits.data, digits.target, test_size=0.3, random_state=42)

# Create a gradient boosting classifier and train it
gb = GradientBoostingClassifier(n_estimators=100, learning_rate=1.0, max_depth=1, random_state=42)
gb.fit(X_train, y_train)

# Make predictions
predictions = gb.predict(X_test)

# Print the accuracy
print("Accuracy: ", accuracy_score(y_test, predictions))

# Print the classification report
print(classification_report(y_test, predictions))
```

In the output, you will see metrics such as accuracy, precision, recall, f1-score for each digit class (0 to 9) as well as the macro average and weighted average.

Keep in mind that the results might be slightly different each time you run it, because of the random nature of train_test_split and GradientBoostingClassifier methods. To get the same results each time, you can fix the random state as shown above. The parameters of GradientBoostingClassifier such as n_estimators, learning_rate and max_depth can be tuned for better results.
<|end|>


