<|user|>
ReSharper Code cleanup feature (with "[reorder members](http://www.jetbrains.com/resharper/features/code_formatting.html#Reordering_Type_Members_C#_only)" and "[reformat code](http://www.jetbrains.com/resharper/features/code_formatting.html#Code_Style_Configuration_and_Sharing)" enabled) is really great. You define a layout template using XML, then a simple key combination reorganizes your whole source file (or folder/project/solution) according to the rules you set in the template.

Anyway, do you think that could be a problem regarding VCS like subversion, cvs, git, etc. ? Is there a chance that it causes many undesired conflicts ?

Thank you.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
It depends on your team's usage of Version Control Systems (VCS) and other practices like code reviews, how frequently you merge or rebase, etc.

1. Many Changes in a Single Commit: One of the key rules of using a VCS is to make each commit as small as possible and relevant to a single change/unit of work. Using ReSharper's code cleanup feature could result in large changes across multiple files in a single commit. This makes it harder for your team members to review your changes and understand what exactly has changed. So, it's better to run ReSharper's code cleanup before you start making changes to a file.

2. Merge Conflicts: If multiple team members are working on the same set of files and one of them uses ReSharper's code cleanup feature, the others are likely to face a lot of merge conflicts. To mitigate this, you could run ReSharper's code cleanup feature once for the entire codebase and commit these changes. This would ensure that all future changes are according to the defined coding standard and would reduce merge conflicts.

3. Blame/History: Using ReSharper's code cleanup feature would change the lines of code and therefore, the 'blame' or 'history' information would point to the clean up commit. This might make it harder to find out why a line of code was changed.

4. Automated Tests: Depending upon the complexity of your project, large scale changes from ReSharper's code cleanup may trigger unforeseen consequences and break existing functionality. So, you should have a good suite of automated tests that you can run after doing a cleanup.

In conclusion, yes, ReSharper's code cleanup feature could potentially create problems in a team environment using a VCS, but with good practices and communication, these issues can be mitigated.
<|end|>


