Debug and write a reusable function to dynamically rearrange the order of the elements in the arrays below alphabetically:

1. Array_1 = ["Grapefruit", "Apricot", "Fig", "Peach", "Pineapple"]
2. Array_2 = ["Apple", "Grapefruit", "Apricot", "Fig", "Banana"]

You are given the following erroneous code:

```javascript
function sortArray(arr) {
  const newArr = arr.sort();
  return newArr;
}

sortArray(["Grapefruit", "Apricot", "Fig", "Peach", "Pineapple"]);
sortArray(["Apple", "Grapefruit", "Apricot", "Fig", "Banana"]);
```
You should notice something wrong with the output. The reason for the error lies in how the JavaScript's .sort() method is dealing with upper and lower case strings. Adjust the function accordingly. 

Furthermore, given that the expected output should always start with "Apple", modify the function so that regardless of the alphabetical order, "Apple" would always be the first element of the sorted array, if available. If "Apple" is not in the array, it should sort it as it should alphabetically.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's the revised code:

```javascript
function sortArray(arr) {
  // First normalize case so that sort puts capitals and lowercase in correct order
  const newArr = arr.sort((a, b) => a.toLowerCase().localeCompare(b.toLowerCase()));
  
  // If we have an 'Apple' in the array, find it and move it to the start
  const appleIndex = newArr.indexOf('Apple');
  if (appleIndex > -1) {
      newArr.splice(appleIndex, 1);
      newArr.unshift('Apple');
  }
    
  return newArr;
}

console.log(sortArray(["Grapefruit", "Apricot", "Fig", "Peach", "Pineapple"]));
console.log(sortArray(["Apple", "Grapefruit", "Apricot", "Fig", "Banana"]));
```

The JavaScript `sort()` with the `localeCompare()` function will order the string irrespective of its case. After sorting, the function searches for "Apple" in the sorted array, removes it if it exists, and then adds it to the beginning of the array.
<|end|>


