The exciting world of post-quantum cryptography hinges on the development of robust security protocols capable of withstanding the formidable computational power of quantum computers. Nowhere is this pursuit more vigorous than in Lattice-based cryptographic systems – systems which have rapidly gained prominence due to their unique resilience against quantum attacks.

Lattice-based cryptography constructs its cryptographic schemes based on the complexity inherent in lattice problems, therefore forming the crux of secure data transmission over insecure channels. But unlike most other cryptographic systems, lattice-based cryptography is resistant to attacks from Shor’s algorithm, a potent quantum algorithm capable of breaking RSA and ECC-based encryption. Hence, the quest for absolute security is, in essence, the quest for perfecting lattice-based cryptographic systems.

The progression and practical application of these systems have been challenging yet rewarding. Early security protocols were rudimentary, but the evolution of these protocols has hinged on the identification and resolution of weaknesses, coupled with the introduction of enhanced analytical tools allowing for greater scrutiny of the system architecture. This systematic methodology aids in pinpointing vulnerabilities, leading to innovative solutions designed to fortify these nascent security frameworks.

Computational complexity theory plays a crucial role in assessing the security of these systems. The inherent difficulty associated with lattice problems is what affords lattice-based cryptography its resilience. Understanding the relation between problem complexity and cryptographic security enables the development of highly secure and efficient encryption methods. 

Cryptographic efficiency and data integrity remain critical areas that are continually being refined. The operational speed of encryption and decryption methods directly impact the functionality of the systems they protect. In this respect, understanding exact lattice problems and incorporating advanced algorithms, have smoothed the path for increased efficiency. 

The complexity of continuous integration and deployment is another area necessitating rigorous analysis. By seamlessly merging multiple code changes into a single software version, continuous integration prevents the late discovery of errors and incompatibilities. Parallelly, continuous deployment allows for the systematic application of upgrades or patches that can circumvent potential security breaches, that are recognized only post-deployment.

Despite the rigorous scrutiny and progressive enhancements made in lattice-based systems, roadblocks loom. These challenges emerge both from within the rapidly evolving field of quantum computing, and from factors external to it- for instance, National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) standardization.

However, possible solutions exist – with continued development of more sophisticated cryptographic techniques, coupled with widespread education and awareness of quantum threats, might offer a more secure future. Rigorous and continual analyses provide us the tools to disarm potential intrusions. Additionally, collaboration between academia, industry, and government bodies could shape policies and practices that accelerate the adoption and progress of post-quantum cryptographic systems.

Thus, the trajectory of lattice-based cryptographic systems, though ripe with challenges, is powered by consistent ingenuity. The promise of absolute security hangs tantalizingly within reach, and the steady progression of this field confirms our unwavering commitment to ensuring that promise becomes reality.
<|end|>


