      * The sentence only contains letters
  */
const wordsInSentence = (sentence) => {
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is the adjusted code to solve this problem using JavaScript:

```javascript
const wordsInSentence = (sentence) => {
    if (sentence.length < 1 || sentence.length > 100) {
        throw new Error('Invalid input');
    }
    
    const words = sentence.split(" ");
    const result = [];
    for (let word of words) {
        const uniqueCharacters = [...new Set(word.split(''))];
        if(uniqueCharacters.length % 2 === 0) {
            result.push(word);
        }
    }
    return result.join(" ");
};
```

In this code, we first split the input sentence into words and start iterating over each word. For each word, we create a Set from the characters in the word. A Set in JavaScript is a collection of unique elements, so it helps us to count the number of unique characters in a word. If the count of unique characters in a word is even, we push the word to the result array. In the end, we join all the words in the result array into a single string with spaces between the words and return it.
