Write a JavaScript function to convert the given XML data into a JSON object.
<user>
  <name>John</name>
  <age>35</age>
  <location>New York</location>
</user>
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You will first need to parse the XML data into a usable format for JavaScript before converting it into JSON.

You can do this with the DOMParser API. Once you have a usable JavaScript object, you can then stringify it with `JSON.stringify`. 

Here is an example function which achieves this:

```javascript
function xmlToJson(xmlStr) {
    // Parse the XML into a DOM object
    var domParser = new DOMParser();
    var xmlDoc = domParser.parseFromString(xmlStr, "text/xml");
    
    // Construct JS object
    var jsonObj = {
        name: xmlDoc.getElementsByTagName("name")[0].childNodes[0].nodeValue,
        age: xmlDoc.getElementsByTagName("age")[0].childNodes[0].nodeValue,
        location: xmlDoc.getElementsByTagName("location")[0].childNodes[0].nodeValue,
    };
    
    // Convert it to JSON and return
    return JSON.stringify(jsonObj);
}

var xml = `<user>
               <name>John</name>
               <age>35</age>
               <location>New York</location>
           </user>`;
console.log(xmlToJson(xml));
```
This function creates a new `DOMParser`, which turns the XML string into a Document object.  

It then creates a JavaScript object (`jsonObj`) using the 'nodeValue' of the XML tags for 'name', 'age', and 'location'. This JS object structure matches the structure of the XML data provided. 

Finally, it turns the JS object into a JSON string using `JSON.stringify()`, and returns this JSON string.

Just bear in mind that this code only works as intended with this specific XML structure. For more complex structures, you will need to adjust the methods and properties you use to navigate and extract the data from the Document object.
