Write a C++ code function that identifies arrays with single-character strings in C++ and replaces all with a specified non-null value.
arr = {"", "a", "b", ""}
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is a simple implementation of the problem statement written in C++. This code replaces all single-character strings in an array of strings with a specified non-null value.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

std::vector<std::string> ReplaceSingleCharStrings(std::vector<std::string> arr, std::string newVal) {
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {
        if (arr[i].size() == 1) {
            arr[i] = newVal;
        }
    }
    return arr;
}

int main () {
    std::vector<std::string> arr = {"", "a", "b", ""};
    std::string newVal = "replaced";
    arr = ReplaceSingleCharStrings(arr, newVal);
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {
        std::cout << arr[i] << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}
```

In the code above,
1. We create a `ReplaceSingleCharStrings` function that takes in a vector of strings and a non-null string.
2. It iterates through each string in the vector.
3. If the string length is 1, it replaces it with the non-null string.
4. It returns the modified vector.
5. In the `main` function, we call `ReplaceSingleCharStrings` to replace all single-character strings in `arr` with `"replaced"`.
6. Finally, we print all the strings in the modified vector.
<|end|>


