Design a rudimentary deep learning neural network topology geared towards efficaciously distinguishing between classifications of Felis catus (domestic cats) and Canis lupus familiaris (domestic dogs).
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In order to design a rudimentary deep learning neural network topology to perform the classification task for distinguishing between cats and dogs, we would need a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN). CNNs are most commonly used in analyzing visual imagery.

CNN's architecture is composed of an input layer, many hidden layers (convolutional layers, pooling layers and fully connected layers) and an output layer.
Here's a very basic example of using a CNN for image classification using Keras, a high level neural networks API built on tensorflow.

```python
from keras.models import Sequential
from keras.layers import Conv2D, MaxPooling2D
from keras.layers import Flatten, Dense, Dropout

model = Sequential()

# Block 1
model.add(Conv2D(32, (3, 3), activation='relu', input_shape=(64, 64, 3)))
model.add(MaxPooling2D(pool_size=(2, 2)))
model.add(Dropout(0.2))

# Block 2
model.add(Conv2D(64, (3, 3), activation='relu'))
model.add(MaxPooling2D(pool_size=(2, 2)))
model.add(Dropout(0.2))

# Block 3
model.add(Conv2D(128, (3, 3), activation='relu'))
model.add(MaxPooling2D(pool_size=(2, 2)))
model.add(Dropout(0.2))

# FC Block
model.add(Flatten())
model.add(Dense(128, activation='relu'))
model.add(Dropout(0.5))
model.add(Dense(1, activation='sigmoid'))

model.compile(optimizer = 'adam', loss = 'binary_crossentropy', metrics = ['accuracy'])
```
This model uses several convolutional layers each followed by max-pooling layers. At the end, a fully connected layer is added for classification. The dropout layer is also used after each block to prevent overfitting.

Note that the Conv2D layer requires input shape which should match the shape of your input images. The binary_crossentropy loss function is used because it's a binary classification (cats or dogs).

This is just a very basic topology for the task. It will require tweaking and optimization, including potentially more hidden layers, different numbers of neurons, different drop out rates, etc. Remember that a more complex network may not always give better results and can make training slower. So the complexity of model will be decided based on the available dataset complexity and your computational resources. 

Finally, keep in mind that you can augment the input data(images) to avoid overfitting and increase the diversity of data available for training models, without actually collecting new data. This includes tasks like rotations, shifts, flips, and more.

This model does not include the process of preparing your images for the training & testing, fitting the model to the data, evaluating model etc, which are also important steps that need to be implemented.
<|end|>


