<|user|>
Develop a multithreaded Java program that prints out two separate statements alternatively every 5 seconds in a synchronized manner. Moreover, ensure that the program execution can be stopped externally. You also need to handle potential concurrency and deadlock scenarios. The application performance must not degrade over time. Bonus points for code comments explaining design choices or complex segments.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a simple example of a Java program that prints out two statements alternately every 5 seconds in a multithreaded and synchronized manner:

```java
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicBoolean;

class PrintThread extends Thread {
    private final AtomicBoolean running = new AtomicBoolean(false);
    private String message;
    private static String lastPrintedMessage;

    PrintThread(String message) {
        this.message = message;
    }

    public void run() {
        running.set(true);
        while (running.get()) {
            synchronized (PrintThread.class) {
                if (lastPrintedMessage != message) {
                    System.out.println(message);
                    lastPrintedMessage = message;
                    PrintThread.class.notifyAll();
                }
                try {
                    PrintThread.class.wait(5000);
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                    System.out.println("Thread was interrupted");
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // Call this method to stop the thread
    public void stopThread() {
        running.set(false);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PrintThread thread1 = new PrintThread("Statement 1");
        PrintThread thread2 = new PrintThread("Statement 2");

        thread1.start();
        thread2.start();

        try {
            // Let the threads run for 30 seconds
            Thread.sleep(30000);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        // Stop the threads
        thread1.stopThread();
        thread2.stopThread();
    }
}
```

This program creates two threads, each of which prints a distinct message. It is ensured that the messages are printed alternately by using a static variable `lastPrintedMessage` and by printing the message only when `lastPrintedMessage` is not the same as the current thread's message.

To allow the program execution to be stopped externally, a `AtomicBoolean` is used to control the execution of the thread's main loop. If `running.get()` returns `false`, the thread will exit the loop.

Deadlocks are prevented by the `wait()` and `notifyAll()` methods. After a thread prints its message, it calls `wait()`, releasing the lock and allowing the other thread to print its message. When the other thread has printed its message, it calls `notifyAll()`, waking up the first thread. To avoid interruptions to the application's performance overtime, the `wait()` method is called with a timeout value.

The `InterruptedException` is caught and processed correctly to clean up the thread's state. This is important because when a thread is interrupted, it doesn’t stop its execution immediately. The interruption is just a status flag on the thread, indicating it has been requested to stop.
<|end|>


