Given the task below, compose a JavaScript code to segregate names of fruits and names of animals into separate arrays from a mixed array. Further, sort each array in alphabetical order and output the content of the two arrays. Try to also implement error handling in case of unrecognized names in the list. Here's a piece of JavaScript code as a start (Please note that this code contains some errors that should be fixed in the process):

```javascript
var mixedArray = ["Kangaroo", "Apple", "Lion", "Rabbit", "Banana", "Fox", "Orange", "Giraffe"]
var fruitArray = [];
var animalArray = [];

mixedArray.onEach(item => {
    if (item === "Apple" || item === "Banana" || item === "Orange") {
        animalArray.push(item);
    } else if (item === "Kangaroo" || item === "Lion" || item === "Rabbit" || item === "Fox" || item === "Giraffe") {
        fruitArray.push(item);
    } 
})

console.log(fruitArray.sort());
console.log(animalArray.sort());
```

As an added complexity, you may want to check if any of the words in the array are not either a fruit or animal, and print those out as "unrecognized" in a new array. Also try to implement it in a way that adding new names would require minimal code adjustment.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The provided JavaScript code contains several issues:

- The method `onEach` doesn't exist in JavaScript. It should be `forEach`.
- Fruits are being pushed into the `animalArray` and animals into the `fruitArray`. The arrays' names used should correspond with their intended content.

Here's how you could fix these issues, sort the arrays, and handle unrecognized items in the mixed array:

```javascript
let mixedArray = ["Kangaroo", "Apple", "Lion", "Rabbit", "Banana", "Fox", "Orange", "Giraffe", "Rectangle"]
let fruitArray = [];
let animalArray = [];
let unrecognizedArray = [];

let fruits = ["Apple", "Banana", "Orange"];
let animals = ["Kangaroo", "Lion", "Rabbit", "Fox", "Giraffe"];

mixedArray.forEach(item => {
    if(fruits.includes(item)) {
        fruitArray.push(item);
    } else if(animals.includes(item)) {
        animalArray.push(item);
    } else {
        unrecognizedArray.push(item);
    }
});

fruitArray.sort();
animalArray.sort();

console.log("Fruits: " + fruitArray);
console.log("Animals: " + animalArray);
console.log("Unrecognized: " + unrecognizedArray);
```
In this code, I maintained two separate arrays for fruits and animals so adding new names would be a straightforward task. Any name not recognized as a fruit or animal is considered unrecognized and added to the `unrecognizedArray` for reporting.

Please note that sorting is done in place and doesn't need to be assigned back to our arrays (i.e., we used `fruitArray.sort()` and `animalArray.sort()` not `fruitArray = fruitArray.sort()`). 

This code also assumes a case sensitive comparison. If you want to handle item names case insensitively, convert item to lowercase or uppercase before checking its inclusion in the fruit or animal array.
<|end|>


