<|asst|>
First, to debug the provided erroneous code: In the second `for` loop where `string2` is being iterated through, we need to correct the index being used from `i` to `j`.

In handling exceptions, ensure safety by using `try-catch` blocks.

The corrected program:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        String string1 = "ProgrammingAssignment";
        String string2 = "ProgrammingTest";

        try {
            int[] freq1 = new int[255];
            int[] freq2 = new int[255];

            for (int i = 0; i < string1.length(); i++) {
                char character = string1.charAt(i);
                freq1[character]++;
            }

            for (int j = 0; j < string2.length(); j++) {
                char character = string2.charAt(j);
                freq2[character]++;
            }

            for (int i = 0; i < 255; i++) {
                if (freq1[i] != freq2[i]) {
                    System.out.println(
                        (char) i + " has frequency " + freq1[i] + " in string1 and " + freq2[i] + " in string"
                    );
                }
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("An error occurred.");
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

This program accomplish the required task by:

1. Initializing two integer arrays `freq1` and `freq2` with size 255 (Maximum ASCII value + 1).
2. Iterating through each character in `string1` and `string2`, and incrementing the corresponding ASCII value index in its frequency array.
3. Comparing the frequencies of each ASCII value in `freq1` and `freq2` and printing the characters and their frequencies in `string1` and `string2` when they are not equal. 

