I have about 150 000 rows of data written to a database everyday. These row represent outgoing articles for example. Now I need to [SSRS](http://www.microsoft.com/technet/prodtechnol/sql/2005/2005ssrs.mspx). .

The idea is to have a aggregated view on all our transactions and have something that can indicate that something is wrong (that we for example send out 20% less articles than the average).

My idea is to have yesterdays data moved into [SSAS](http://www.microsoft.com/sql/technologies/analysis/default.mspx) every night and there store the aggregated value of number of transactions and the actual number of transaction from yesterdays data. Using SSAS would hopefully speed up the reports.

[SSAS](http://www.microsoft.com/sql/technologies/analysis/default.mspx)
<|end|>
<|asst|>
would indeed be a good fit for the purpose you described. By creating a OLAP cube in SSAS, you can pre-aggregate your data thereby making report generation faster. The cube will allow you to slice and dice your data, that is, to view data from different perspectives and levels of detail.

In terms of aggregating your daily output and comparing it to averages, you can set up calculated measures in your cube. For example, you could set up a measure that calculates the sum of outgoing articles for each day. You could then set up another measure to calculate the average daily output over a certain period (e.g. the past 30 days). You could then put these measures on your SSRS report to highlight any days where the number of transactions is notably below average.

Further, SSAS contains data mining functionality that might be of interest. You could, for example, use it to identify patterns or trends in your data, or to forecast future outputs based on past data.

The process flow would look like this:

1. SSIS (SS Integration Services) Package: This can be scheduled to extract data from your database every night and load it into your SSAS Cube.

2. SSAS (SS Analysis Services) Cube: The cube will store your data in an aggregated, multi-dimensional format. You would set up your measures (e.g. daily output, moving average) here.

3. SSRS (SS Reporting Services) Report: The report would provide the daily view of your data and highlight any anomalies.

4. Users: They can view the SSRS reports as needed and drill through the data if they need more detail.

Remember, this kind of setup would require a good understanding of the Microsoft BI Stack (SSIS, SSAS, SSRS) as well as data warehousing concepts. If you're not already familiar with these, you might need some time to get up to speed. But once set up, this could be a very efficient and powerful system for monitoring your daily operations.
<|end|>


