&lt;p&gt;A contiguous sub matrix is referred to as a window within a matrix.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine a $16\times n$ matrix where each element is either a 0 or a 1. Let's define $B(k,n)$ as the cumulative count of such matrices where the sum of the elements in every $2\times k$ window equals $k$.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is provided that $B(2,4) = 65550$ and $B(3,9) \equiv 87273560 \pmod{1\,000\,000\,007}$.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your task is to compute $B(10^5,10^{16})$. Please provide your answer modulo $1\,000\,000\,007$.&lt;/p&gt;
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This problem is quite challenging to calculate directly due to the massive matrix size, and we need to apply techniques to reduce computational load. Here a dynamic programming based approach can be very useful as it will calculate smaller problems first and use those solved sub-problems to construct the solutions of larger one.

However, to solve this problem within a reasonable time for such a huge size of the matrix, an additional mathematical trick is necessary. We can notice that the calculation of the cumulate count will eventually loop into a cycle. In other words, when the length `n` is large enough, adding extra columns to the matrix won't change the mod result. Thus the task can be separated into two parts, one for finding the period of this looping cycle and another for finding the starting $n$ that the cycling begins.

Unfortunately, despite this optimization, solving this problem with Python may still be very computational expensive and time consuming, possibly exceeding the standard resources of common hardware and the time limit of Python execution. As such, this problem is typically solved using a more efficient programming language like C++ or Java for practical purposes, or even more specific tools designed for solving large scale mathematical problems.

