Develop a C# function capable of producing every distinct combination of a specified length, n, derived from an input array, while ensuring this task necessitates multiple-stage reasoning from AI systems.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Answering this question will involve a recursive function, since producing combinations all stem from base case of combination of one, two until you reach to n. Plus, maintaining distinct combinations further complicated because the function should not count similar combinations twice.

Here is a possible solution in C#:

```C#
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        char[] set = { 'a', 'b', 'c', 'd' };
        int k = 3;

        GetCombinations(set, k);
    }

    // Recursive function to print combinations
    static void CombinationRecursive(char[] set, char[] data, int start, int end, int index, int k)
    {
        if (index == k)
        {
            // Combination is ready to be printed
            for (int i = 0; i < k; i++)
                Console.Write(data[i]);

            Console.WriteLine("");
            return;
        }

        for (int i = start; i <= end && end - i + 1 >= k - index; i++)
        {
            data[index] = set[i];
            CombinationRecursive(set, data, i + 1, end, index + 1, k);
        }
    }

    // Helper function that build combinations
    static void GetCombinations(char[] set, int k)
    {
        int n = set.Length;
        // Array to store individual combination
        char[] data = new char[k];

        // Distinct Combinations are stored in HashSet
        HashSet<string> distinctCombinations = new HashSet<string>();

        // Calculate all possible combinations
        CombinationRecursive(set, data, 0, n - 1, 0, k);

        // Print all unique combinations
        foreach (var combination in distinctCombinations)
            Console.WriteLine(combination);
    }
}
```

Note that the concept of combination is defined as "order does not matter." As such, the combinations of ['a', 'b'] and ['b', 'a'] would be considered duplicates. If order does matter (i.e., 'ab' != 'ba'), you should be computing permutations, not combinations.
<|end|>


