I need to store some sensitive data by encrypting it with atleast 128 bit key. I investigated into javax.crypto package and found that there are certain Cipher names, like 
PBEWithMD5AndDES or PBEWithSHA1AndDESede which provides encryption upto 56 bit and 80 bit ([http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DESede](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DESede)).

I referred other guys posts but those are mainly using RSA and in my understanding RSA is generally suitable for encrypting the communication data (with private-public key pair). My need is different, I just want to store the data and retrieve it back by decrypting it. Therefore I don't need any private-public key pairs.

Please let me know if you have any idea about this.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You may use Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) algorithm which is widely used for encryption. It provides encryption and decryption with 128 bits key size.

Follow this step-by-step guide:

First, you need to add the necessary imports.

```java
import javax.crypto.Cipher;
import javax.crypto.spec.SecretKeySpec;
import java.security.Key;
import java.util.Base64;
```

Second, define the Key and initialization vector (IV).

```java
// 128 bit key
String key = "Bar12345Bar12345"; 

Key aesKey = new SecretKeySpec(key.getBytes(), "AES");
```

Then, you can encrypt data like so:

```java
// Create an AES cipher instance
Cipher cipher = Cipher.getInstance("AES");

// Initialize the cipher for encryption
cipher.init(Cipher.ENCRYPT_MODE, aesKey);

// Our cleartext
String cleartext = "This is just an example";

// Encrypt the cleartext
byte[] encrypted = cipher.doFinal(cleartext.getBytes());

// Prints encrypted text
System.err.println(new String(Base64.getEncoder().encode(encrypted)));
```

and then perform decryption using the code below:

```java
// Initialize the same cipher for decryption
cipher.init(Cipher.DECRYPT_MODE, aesKey);

// Decrypt the ciphertext
byte[] decrypted = cipher.doFinal(encrypted);

System.err.println(new String(decrypted));
```

This way, the data will be stored and decrypted using the same symmetric key. It's simple, efficient, and provides strong encryption. But remember to handle the key carefully. If someone obtains it, they will be able to decrypt your data.
