I don't find any information about how are the special case NULL DACL and empty DACL treated in Windows.
I understand what they are but don't know how they are treated..
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In the context of Microsoft Windows, Discretionary Access Control List (DACL) is a component that identifies the users who are granted or denied access permissions to a securable object. 

1. A NULL DACL: A null DACL is an absent DACL - it does not exist at all. A secureable object with a null DACL is accessible to all users because the system cannot restrict access. Thus, when a DACL is missing from a security descriptor, the system does not deny any access rights to the object.

2. An empty DACL: An empty DACL is a DACL that exists but contains no ACEs (Access Control Entries). A secureable object with an empty DACL explicitly denies all access attempts to the object because there are no permissions specifying who can access the object. 

So to summarize, a null DACL allows all user access, while an empty DACL denies all user access.
<|end|>


