Python solution with Pandas library would look like:

```python
import pandas as pd

data1 = [{"name": "john", "age": 20, "location": "New York"},
         {"name": "sarah", "age": 30, "location": "Los Angeles"},
         {"name": "rita", "age": 25, "location": "Los Angeles"},
         {"name": "michael", "age": 40, "location": "New York"}]

data2 = [{"name": "lily", "age": 18, "location": "Boston"},
         {"name": "richard", "age": 55, "location": "San Francisco"},
         {"name": "emma", "age": 38, "location": "San Francisco"},
         {"name": "jacob", "age": 45, "location": "Boston"}]

# Convert dictionaries to dataframes
df1 = pd.DataFrame(data1)
df2 = pd.DataFrame(data2)

# Concatenate the two dataframes
df = pd.concat([df1, df2])

# Check for any missing values
if df.isnull().sum().sum() > 0:
    print ("There are missing values in the dataframe")
else:
    print ("There are no missing values in the dataframe")

# Group by location and compute the average age
average_age = df.groupby("location")["age"].mean()
print(average_age)

# Other statistics you may compute depends on what you are interested in. 
# Here we compute the minimum and maximum age at each location
min_age = df.groupby("location")["age"].min()
max_age = df.groupby("location")["age"].max()

print (min_age)
print (max_age)
```

This script will first print whether there are any missing values in the DataFrame (none for this case). Then it will print the average age for each location followed by the minimum and maximum ages for each location. 

For dealing with missing data, we have done nothing since there are no missing values in the provided data. If there were, we could use either `df.fillna(value)` to fill missing values with a particular value, `df.dropna()` to drop rows with missing values, or use other imputation techniques such as mean imputation or regression imputation depending on the nature of the data.
