I have a PHP script that runs as a CGI program and the HTTP `Authenticate` header gets eaten and spit out. So I would like to implement some kind of FORM based authentication. As an added constraint, there is no database so no session data can be stored.

I am very open to having a master username and password. I just need to protect the application from an intruder who doesn't know these credentials.

So how would you implement this?

Cookies?

I could present the form and if it validates, I can send back a cookie that is a hash of the IP address come secret code. Then I can prevent pages from rendering unless the thing decrypts correctly. But I have no idea how to implement that in PHP.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a simple example of a PHP login script that implements form-based authentication with cookies.

```php
<?php
define('USERNAME', 'master');
define('PASSWORD', 'password');
define('SECRET', 'SHHHH_SO_SECRET');

if (isset($_POST['username']) && isset($_POST['password'])) {
    // User is trying to log in
    if ($_POST['username'] === USERNAME && $_POST['password'] === PASSWORD) {
        // Correct login. Set cookie and redirect
        $token = hashSecret($_POST['username'], $_POST['password']);
        setcookie('auth', $token, time()+(3600*24*7), ''); // cookie expires in 7 days
        header('Location: welcome.php'); // redirect to your main page
    } else {
        // Incorrect login. Display error message
        echo '<p>Incorrect username or password.</p>';
    }
}

// Check if user is authenticated
$token = $_COOKIE['auth'] ?? '';
$authenticated = checkAuth($token);

function hashSecret($username, $password) {
    $str = $username . $password . SECRET;
    return hash('sha1', $str);
}

function checkAuth($token) {
    $hash = hashSecret(USERNAME, PASSWORD);
    return $hash === $token;
}

if (!$authenticated) {
    // User is not logged in. Display login form.
    echo <<<HTML
        <form method="POST">
            <input type="text" name="username" placeholder="Username">
            <input type="password" name="password" placeholder="Password">
            <input type="submit" value="Login">
        </form>
HTML;
}
else {
    // User is logged in. Display content.
    echo 'Welcome, master!';
}
?>
```

Note that this is an oversimplified script, and not recommended for a real-world application due to a few inherent security vulnerabilities. For example, it uses a fixed username/password which should never be done in production. In a real-world situation, you would want to salt your hashes and use a slow hashing function like bcrypt.

Furthermore, holding sensitive information in cookies, even if encrypted, can be risky as it may be possible for attackers to steal them (for instance, with a cross-site scripting attack). Another problem is IP address-based authentication is not secure, because IP addresses can be spoofed, and users often change IP addresses (for example, by switching networks).

Use this script for your basic understanding and learning purpose only. In production, you should always use a well-tested and maintained authentication library or service instead of rolling your own.
<|end|>


