Unfortunately, SVG code does not inherently support the radiant fire effect that you're looking for. SVG is a static image format and can't natively simulate dynamic effects like glowing or fire.

However, there are workarounds! You can use CSS animations or JavaScript to achieve the effect. Please note that this might need to be added to an HTML file to work properly.

If you want to add a simple glowing effect, you can use CSS like this:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <style>
        .glow path{
            stroke: #ffffff;
            stroke-width: 1px;
            fill: #CDC4D6;
            animation: glow 2s ease-in-out infinite alternate;
        }

        @keyframes glow {
            from {
                stroke-width: 2px;
                fill: #CDC4D6;
            }
            to {
                stroke-width: 8px;
                fill: #ffe47a;
            }
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    // Paste your SVG here and add the 'glow' class to the svg element
    // For example: <svg width="32" height="32" class="glow" ...>
</body>
</html>
```
In the above code, a glowing effect is given by animating the `stroke-width` and `fill` style properties repeatedly.

It's important to remember that CSS animations will not work in all browsers, and the SVG will not glow when opened outside of a web page (like opened directly in an image viewer).
Please note for more complex animations or effects, you may need to use JavaScript libraries such as GreenSock or use a more complex animation tool and export as an animated SVG.
<|end|>


