<|user|>
I read to alleviate the problem of Out of Vocabulary (OOV), there are two techniques:

- BPE

- Copy mechanism


It appears to me they are two orthogonal approaches.
Can we combine the two, i.e.,  we use both the copy mechanism and BPE? Are there any work out there that combines the two? I cant find any.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, BPE (Byte Pair Encoding) and Copy Mechanism are two separate approaches that address the out-of-vocabulary problem in different ways. 

1. BPE algorithm splits words into sub-words. These sub-words are then used to represent the original words, It helps to handle the OOV words because even if a word is not in the vocabulary, it would be possible to break it into sub-words that are in the vocabulary.

2. Copy Mechanism enables the model to copy words from the source text that might not be in the generated vocabulary during sequence prediction tasks. This is particularly useful in tasks like text summarization, translation where you want to preserve names, places, etc.

Since they solve the problem in different ways, it is possible to combine both methods in a hybrid approach. However, it might need significant modification to your existing model's architecture. Combining these methods has been explored in several works:

- Gu et al. in their paper "Incorporating Copying Mechanism in Sequence-to-Sequence Learning" introduce a model that combines copying mechanism with a sequence-to-sequence architecture, which can be made to work with BPE tokenization.

- Another approach was introduced by Bhoopchand et al. in their paper "Learning Python Code Suggestion with a Sparse Pointer Network". They propose a model that combines BPE with a sparse pointer mechanism (a type of copy mechanism), and show that this model can significantly outperform a model using BPE alone.

Detailed exploit of both methods might be more task-specific, though this approach is not very common in general-purpose language models.
<|end|>


