Develop a class script in Java that incorporates three methods - algorithmically adding, subtracting and multiplying a pair of floating point numbers. Simultaneously, these methods must also conduct various error checks to ensure the numbers are not NaN (Not a Number) or infinite.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a simple Java class which does what you requested. It provides three methods: add, subtract, and multiply. Before performing these operations, it checks whether the provided numbers are NaN or Infinite and throws an IllegalArgumentException if they are.

```java
public class ArithmeticOperations {

    public static void verifyNumbers(double num1, double num2){
        if(Double.isNaN(num1) || Double.isNaN(num2)){
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("One of the numbers is NaN");
        }
        if(Double.isInfinite(num1) || Double.isInfinite(num2)){
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("One of the numbers is Infinite");
        }
    }    
    
    public static double add(double num1, double num2) {
        verifyNumbers(num1, num2);
        return num1 + num2;
    }

    public static double subtract(double num1, double num2) {
        verifyNumbers(num1, num2);
        return num1 - num2;
    }

    public static double multiply(double num1, double num2) {
        verifyNumbers(num1, num2);
        return num1 * num2;
    }
}
```

You can use these methods like this:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
    double num1 = 5.5;
    double num2 = 6.6;
    double sum = ArithmeticOperations.add(num1, num2);
    double difference = ArithmeticOperations.subtract(num1, num2);
    double product = ArithmeticOperations.multiply(num1, num2);
    
    System.out.println("Sum: " + sum);
    System.out.println("Difference: " + difference);
    System.out.println("Product: " + product);
}
```

Please note that this ArithmeticOperations class uses static methods, which means you don't need to create an instance of the class to use them. Just call the methods directly on the class itself. Also, these methods throw an IllegalArgumentException if the provided numbers are NaN or Infinite. You should be prepared to catch this exception in any code which calls these methods.
<|end|>


