<|user|>
Singletons are a hotly debated design pattern, so I am interested in what the Stack Overflow community thought about them.

Please provide reasons for your opinions, not just "Singletons are for lazy programmers!"

Here is a fairly good article on the issue, although it is against the use of Singletons:
[scientificninja.com: performant-singletons](http://web.archive.org/web/20090303174418/http://scientificninja.com/advice/performant-singletons).

Does anyone have any other good articles on them? Maybe in support of Singletons?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Singletons design pattern is controversial as some developers believe it's an anti-pattern, mainly due to its inherent problems, while other developers find value in this design pattern due to its unique characteristics. 

Pros of Singleton pattern:
1. Controlled access to a single instance: Singleton restricts object creation by limiting them to a single instance.
2. Global point of Access: Singleton classes can provide a global point of access to the resource, which is both easy and controlled.
3. Lazy Initialization: With Singleton pattern, we can initiate the resources only when needed.

Cons of Singleton pattern:
1. Global State: The global state created by Singletons can lead to unforeseen side effects and make system-wide changes harder to implement. 
2. Hidden dependencies: Singletons can serve as hidden dependencies in your code, making understanding & maintaining the codebase difficult.
3. Difficulty in testing: Singletons are notoriously difficult to test as they promote tight coupling in a system and testing in parallel may be difficult.

There are numerous articles and discussions on the pros and cons of singletons. Here are few of them for further reading:
1. [Wiki.c2.com: Singleton's are pathological liars](http://wiki.c2.com/?SingletonsArePathologicalLiars)
2. [Google Testing Blog - Singleton](https://testing.googleblog.com/2008/08/by-miko-hevery-so-you-join-new-project.html)
3. [Dzone.com - Singleton Pattern](https://dzone.com/articles/singleton-pattern-positive-and-negative-aspects)

At the end, the use of singleton pattern greatly depends on the specific needs & context of your application.
<|end|>


