<|user|>
Given that emergency room visits in a large hospital are roughly normally distributed with an average duration of 167 minutes and a standard deviation of 76 minutes, within which time bracket, rounded to the nearest minute, would the central 80% of visit durations fall? Also, consider that the distribution is asymmetric due to the possibility of extremely long visits. Would the probabilities change if this were the case? A)15 minutes to 319 minutes B) 53 minutes to 281 minutes C) 70 minutes to 264 minutes D) 91 minutes to 243 minutes.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The central 80% of a normally distributed data set falls within approximately 1.28 standard deviations of the mean. This is because for a normally distributed data, approximately 80% of the data lies within +-1.28 standard deviations of the means. So we can calculate the upper and lower bounds of the visit durations as follows:

Lower_bound = mean - 1.28 * standard_deviation
Upper_bound = mean + 1.28 * standard_deviation

Python code would be:

```python
import math

mean = 167
std_dev = 76
z_score = 1.28

lower_bound = mean - z_score * std_dev
upper_bound = mean + z_score * std_dev

lower_bound = round(lower_bound)
upper_bound = round(upper_bound)

print(f"The central 80% of visit durations would fall between {lower_bound} minutes and {upper_bound} minutes")
```

Regarding the asymmetric nature of the distribution due to the possibility of extremely long visits, this would distort the normal distribution and possibly create a right skew. In such a case, the mean would be greater than the median and the standard deviation would be larger. However, the probabilities would not necessarily change as these are based on the areas under the curve which would still integrate to 1. 

Considering the options given, the calculated output from the Python code would reveal the correct answer.
<|end|>


