```xml
<person>
  <name>Jane Smith</name>
  <age>28</age>
  <city>Los Angeles</city>
  <job>
    <title>Software Engineer</title>
    <company>ABC Corporation</company>
    <duration>2</duration>
  </job>
</person>
```
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's an example in Python using built-in modules for handling XML data. 

This script create a Person class to represent the data in object-oriented style. It also implements a `from_xml` class method to create an instance of the Person class from an XML string. During XML parsing, if there are any structural discrepancies, it catches the Exception and handles the error. 

```python
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET

class Job:
    def __init__(self, title, company, duration):
        self.title = title
        self.company = company
        self.duration = duration

    @classmethod
    def from_node(cls, node):
        return cls(node.find('title').text,
                   node.find('company').text,
                   node.find('duration').text)

class Person:
    def __init__(self, name, age, city, job):
        self.name = name
        self.age = age
        self.city = city
        self.job = job

    @classmethod
    def from_xml(cls, xml_str):
        try:
            root = ET.fromstring(xml_str)
            name = root.find('name').text
            age = root.find('age').text
            city = root.find('city').text
            job_node = root.find('job')
            job = Job.from_node(job_node)
            return cls(name, age, city, job)
        except ET.ParseError as e:
            print(f'Error parsing XML: {e}')
            return None

    def display_info(self):
        print(f'Name: {self.name}')
        print(f'Age: {self.age}')
        print(f'City: {self.city}')
        print(f'Job: {self.job.title} at {self.job.company} for {self.job.duration} years')

xml_str = '''
<person>
  <name>Jane Smith</name>
  <age>28</age>
  <city>Los Angeles</city>
  <job>
    <title>Software Engineer</title>
    <company>ABC Corporation</company>
    <duration>2</duration>
  </job>
</person>
'''
p = Person.from_xml(xml_str)
if p is not None:
    p.display_info()
```

This code will parse the XML string and then display the information about the person. If the XML string is not structured correctly, it will print an error message and exit.
