Determine the output and possible errors in the following JavaScript code: 

Consider variable 'y' is not defined previously and x is an array of integers i.e., x = [5, -7, 2, 0, 11]. The code needs to process every element in 'x' and print whether it is positive, negative, or zero.

```javascript
x = [5, -7, 2, 0, 11]
for (let y in x){
    if (y < 0) {
        console.log('y is negative');
    } else if (y > 0) {
        console.log('y is positive');
    } else {
        console.log('y is zero');
    }
}
```
Can you identify the logical error in the code snippet, correct it and also provide the correct output for each element in the array x?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, the code does contain a logical error. In JavaScript, the `for...in` statement iterates over the enumerable properties of an object. In the case of an array, the index of each element is treated as a property. So, the variable `y` is getting assigned the indices of the array elements (i.e., 0, 1, 2, etc.) instead of their actual values.

As a result, all numbers will be treated as positive, except for the first one, which will be treated as zero due to JavaScript treating 0 as false in a boolean context.

Here is the corrected JavaScript code:

```javascript
x = [5, -7, 2, 0, 11]
for (let i in x){
    let y = x[i];
    if (y < 0) {
        console.log('y is negative');
    } else if (y > 0) {
        console.log('y is positive');
    } else {
        console.log('y is zero');
    }
}
```

And here is the output:

```
y is positive
y is negative
y is positive
y is zero
y is positive
```
<|end|>


