Embark on an in-depth expedition into the intricate universe of lattice-based cryptography, with a particular focus on its cardinal role in the sphere of post-quantum cryptography. Could you weave a detailed narrative that meticulously traces the evolution and practical implementation of security protocols anchored in lattice-based cryptographic systems, leveraging their inherent robustness? This narrative should guarantee unassailable security and ward off potential cryptographic infringements, emphasizing systematic methodologies for identifying and rectifying vulnerabilities. Furthermore, it should incorporate a sophisticated, structured analytical framework for overcoming obstacles, demanding a wide range of analytical prowess and adaptable problem-solving skills. It should also highlight potential impediments and their solutions, intertwined with a profound understanding of algebraic structures and their influence on cryptographic security. In addition, it should delve into the intricacies of cryptographic efficiency and data integrity, paired with the most potent strategies for continuous integration and deployment in the lattice-based cryptography domain. In conclusion, it should provide a meticulous examination of potential security threats and the most effective strategies to ensure the seamless progression and widespread acceptance of lattice-based cryptographic systems in the post-quantum cryptography epoch. Could you also elucidate the role of lattice-based cryptography in the framework of quantum-resistant algorithms, and how it can be utilized to build a secure and resilient cryptographic infrastructure in the face of quantum computing threats?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The origins of lattice-based cryptography can be traced back to the work of Ajtai in the late 20th century, when he first introduced the 'worst-case to average-case' reduction concept. This foundational work established a strong theoretical base that built upon lattice theory and algebra and set the stage for the development of other cryptographic systems in the years to follow.

Lattice-based cryptographic systems provide a robust and effective approach. It involves defining a mathematical lattice, an algebraic structure over a finite field. Consequently, solving the closest vector problem (CVP) or the shortest vector problem (SVP), which lie at the heart of these systems, becomes harder as the dimension of the lattice increases.

These protocols have proven themselves resilient to many cryptanalytical advancements and quantum mechanical computing abilities. Quantum computers cannot efficiently solve the SVP and CVP in polynomial time; hence lattice-based systems remain secure. This robustness firmly secures the critical role of lattice-based cryptography systems in the realm of post-quantum cryptography.

However, while it guarantees a high degree of security against quantum attacks, several challenges impede the widespread implementation of lattice-based cryptographic systems. The computational cost and management of large key sizes tend to offset the security advantages. Overcoming these obstacles necessitates not only in-depth knowledge of the algebraic structures that form the foundations of the key designs but also requires an innovative approach to the problem of effectively dealing with these large data quantities.

Determining lattice parameters is also of paramount importance in balancing the twin goals of security and efficiency. A meticulous study is thus required to evaluate the strength of the lattice parameters to ward off potential cryptographic infringements. Strategies for continuous integration and deployment focus on minimizing the impact of these system shortcomings, employing various techniques such as compressing the data to reduce the key size and working to enhance the speed and efficiency of computations.

Given the potential threat quantum computing poses to current cryptographic algorithms, the role of lattice-based cryptography as a quantum-resistant algorithm becomes even more relevant. It can create a formidable front of defense by inculcating an added layer of security, intriguingly merging with the existing orthodox cryptographic ecosystem. 

Not only does lattice-based cryptography act as a robust quantum-resistant algorithm on on its own, but it also provides foundational bases for the creation and development of other post-quantum cryptographic techniques - such as homomorphic encryptions and cryptographic obfuscations. These techniques work collectively with the sole purpose of ensuring the creation of a secure and resilient cryptography infrastructure undeterred by the advent of quantum computing. 

Therefore, a profound understanding of lattice-based cryptography, continuously improving its efficiency and resiliency by identifying and rectifying vulnerabilities, can effectively prepare us for the post-quantum cryptographic era, securing our cryptographic infrastructure against the potential disruption by quantum computing.
<|end|>


