it's possible that somethink is setting google analitics _ga and _gid cookies without script in code in my app ?
I am starting work in old project Angular + .Net and this cookies are randomly set but I can't found any code to do this. I have not working with google analitics yet.
The only think that I found it was Content Security Policy allow to :
[https://www.googletagmanager.com](https://www.googletagmanager.com)[https://api.someDomain.com](https://api.someDomain.com)[https://analytics.google.com](https://analytics.google.com)
It's possible that this is a source of this cookies ? I know that others app under the same domain are using google analitics.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, it is possible that these cookies are being set without a direct script in your code. The host websites in your Content Security Policy (CSP), Google Tag Manager and Google Analytics, use cookies such as _ga and _gid for tracking users' activities. Specifically:

1. _ga: This is a Google Analytics cookie used to distinguish unique users by assigning a randomly generated number as a client identifier. It is included in each page request in a site and used to calculate visitor, session and campaign data for the site's analytics reports.

2. _gid: This Google Analytics cookie is used to store and update a unique value for each page visited.

Even if you don't explicitly add Google Analytics script in your code, if your website is allowing scripts or pixels to be fired from these host websites through CSP or by being embedded through other means (like a server side language or an iframe), they can set cookies. This would be especially true if other apps under the same domain are using Google Analytics, as the cookies will be set for the domain, not just the individual app. 

Consider going through all components/modules and server-side code of your project to see if Google Analytics or Google Tag Manager is being imported/included anywhere. It might not be directly in your Angular code, it could be on the .Net server side, or perhaps even in a separate JavaScript or tracking file.

In summary, yes, your CSP host addresses are quite likely the source setting these Google Analytics cookies. As you mentioned, other apps on the same domain use Google Analytics, which is generally implemented site-wide (including across sub-domains) and not limited to specific apps/pages.
<|end|>


