Here is a Javascript solution for the problem:

```javascript
var myObj = {
    "fullName": "Bob Smith",
    "yearsLived": 20,
    "currentLocation": "New York City",
    "lifeEvents": ["Graduated College", "Got a Job", "Moved to New York City"],

    // Print out each unique value associated with each key
    print: function() {
        for(var key in this) {
            if(typeof this[key] != 'function'){
                console.log(`${key}: ${this[key]}`);
            }
        }
    },

    // Implement a method to change the value of `yearsLived`
    changeYearsLived: function(years) {
        if(typeof years != 'number'){
            throw new Error("Input years is not a number.");
        }
        this.yearsLived = years;
        this.print();
    },

    // Implement a method to add a new life event
    addLifeEvent: function(event) {
        if(typeof event != 'string'){
            throw new Error("Input event is not a string.");
        }
        this.lifeEvents.push(event);
        this.print();
    }
};

myObj.print();

try{
    myObj.changeYearsLived(21);
}catch(error){
    console.error(error);
}

try{
    myObj.addLifeEvent("Got Married");
}catch(error){
    console.error(error);
}
```
In this program, `myObj` is an object with properties and methods. The `print()` method loops through each property in `myObj` except its own methods and logs its key and value. The `changeYearsLived()` and `addLifeEvent()` methods update the `yearsLived` property and add a new life event to the `lifeEvents` array respectively only if the type of the input parameter matches (throws an error otherwise). After updating, the `print()` method is called to log the updated object. The `try-catch` statements are used to handle any possible errors caused by invalid inputs.
