import java.util.*;
import java.lang.*;

class Solution {
    /**
    Given two positive integers N and K, return the k-th digit in binary form of the sum of all digits of N.

    Example
        For N = 1000, K = 2, the sum of digits will be 1. There is no k-th digit, the output should be "ERROR".
        For N = 150, K = 3, the sum of digits will be 6 and k-th digit is 1, the output should be "1".
        For N = 147, K = 1, the sum of digits will be 12 and k-th digit is 0, the output should be "0".

    Variables:
        @N integer
        @K integer  (Kth position digit to return)
             Constraints: 0 <= N <= 10000, 1 <= K <= Total digits in binary form.
    Output:
         a string of k-th digit in binary number.
     */

    public String solve(int N, int K) {
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is the enhanced code. This code first calculates the sum of digits in N, converts it to binary string, checks if K is out of bounds and finally gets the Kth binary digit.

```Java
import java.util.*;
import java.lang.*;

class Solution {
    public String solve(int N, int K) {
        
        // convert N to String and calculate the sum of digits
        String s = Integer.toString(N);
        int sum = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            sum += s.charAt(i) - '0';
        }
        
        // convert the sum to binary
        String binary = Integer.toBinaryString(sum);
        
        // check if K is out of bounds
        if (K <= 0 || K > binary.length()) {
            return "ERROR";
        }
        
        // get the Kth digit from the end of the binary string
        // because the 'rightmost' bit is considered the 1st index
        char kthDigit = binary.charAt(binary.length() - K);
        
        return Character.toString(kthDigit);
    }
}
```
If `K` is considered from the left (or higher order of magnitude), then the line to get the Kth digit should be:
```Java
char kthDigit = binary.charAt(K - 1);
```
