Can you rewrite this function in Java?
from typing import Tuple

def custom_mix_strings(s1: str, s2: str) -> str:
    """ 
    Blend two strings by interchanging their alphabets before reverting the final combined output
    
    >>> custom_mix_strings("", "")
    ""
    >>> custom_mix_strings("a", "b")
    "ba"
    >>> custom_mix_strings("abc", "def")
    "fedacb"
    """
    length_s1 = len(s1)
    length_s2 = len(s2)
    shortest_length = min(length_s1, length_s2)

    combined_string = ""

    # Swapping the alphabets of the two strings
    for i in range(shortest_length):
        combined_string += s1[i]
        combined_string += s2[i]

    # Adding remaining alphabets if the strings are of unequal length
    if length_s1 > shortest_length:
        combined_string += s1[shortest_length:]
    elif length_s2 > shortest_length:
        combined_string += s2[shortest_length:]

    # Reverting the final combined output
    return combined_string[::-1]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is the above function rewritten in Java:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(customMixStrings("", ""));
        System.out.println(customMixStrings("a", "b"));
        System.out.println(customMixStrings("abc", "def"));
    }

    public static String customMixStrings(String s1, String s2) {
        int lengthS1 = s1.length();
        int lengthS2 = s2.length();
        int shortestLength = Math.min(lengthS1, lengthS2);

        StringBuilder combinedString = new StringBuilder("");

        // Swapping the alphabets of the two strings
        for(int i = 0; i < shortestLength; i++) {
            combinedString.append(s1.charAt(i)).append(s2.charAt(i));
        }

        // Adding remaining alphabets if the strings are of unequal length
        if(lengthS1 > shortestLength) {
            combinedString.append(s1.substring(shortestLength));
        }
        else if(lengthS2 > shortestLength) {
            combinedString.append(s2.substring(shortestLength));
        }

        // Reverting the final combined output
        return combinedString.reverse().toString();
    }
}
```
This Java version works in the same way as the original Python function. It also checks if the strings are of unequal length and adds the remaining characters of the longer string to the result. Finally, the combined string is reversed using the `StringBuilder`'s `reverse()` method.
<|end|>


