Formulate a CSS customization that dynamically influences a designated paragraph and its incorporated elements: modulate the font size using relative units that adaptively responds to both the viewport size and the user's zoom level, morph the text and page background hues as a function of the day's temporal sector (for instance, a light hue such as beige for the text during high sun hours paired with a contrasting darker backdrop, and a darker shade such as crimson for text post-sunset with a contrasting lighter environment), and integrate 'Montserrat' font family from Google Fonts. Guarantee compatibility across multiple web browsers and optimal time for page loading, with attention to the font rendering and page loading duration, taking into account compromised network conditions.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is a solution using JavaScript and CSS to fulfill the requirements:

First of all, you need to import 'Montserrat' font from Google Fonts. Add this code within the <head> section in your HTML:

```html
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css?family=Montserrat&display=swap" />
```

The CSS could be handled as follows:

```css
#myParagraph {
    font-family: 'Montserrat', sans-serif;
    transition: 300ms;
    /* Define default as 16px, it will be updated by JavaScript */
    font-size: 16px;
    /* Define default as black, it will be updated by JavaScript */
    color: black;
}

body {
    /* Define default as white, it will be updated by JavaScript */
    background-color: white;
    transition: 300ms;
}
```

Here is your JavaScript:

```javascript
function dynamicStyles() {
    // Identify paragraph
    var p = document.getElementById('myParagraph');

    // Change font size based on viewport width
    var fontSize = Math.max(Math.min(window.innerWidth / 50, 26), 18);
    p.style.fontSize = fontSize + 'px';

    // Change color based on time of the day
    var hour = new Date().getHours();
    if (hour > 6 && hour < 20) {
        // Daytime
        p.style.color = 'beige'; // text color
        document.body.style.backgroundColor = 'darkslategray'; // background color
    } else {
        // Nighttime
        p.style.color = 'crimson'; // text color
        document.body.style.backgroundColor = 'snow'; // background color
    }
}

// Initialize when document is ready
document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", function() {
    dynamicStyles();
});

// Update when window is being resized
window.onresize = function() {
    dynamicStyles();
}
```

Remember to replace 'myParagraph' with the id of the specific paragraph you intend to adjust. If you want to apply these changes to other paragraphs, be sure to replace 'myParagraph' with the correct id. 

This approach uses vanilla JavaScript and basic CSS. It allows for the paragraph to change contextually with the viewport and the hour of the day. It does so by adjusting the paragraph's style properties within a function that's called when the page loads (DOMContentLoaded event) and as the window is resized. The 'transition' property in the CSS ensures that the changes happen smoothly over the course of 300 milliseconds. 

Compatibility and page loading optimization was considered by making use of standard and efficient JavaScript and CSS techniques, beneficial for users with slow connections or old devices. The transition property also smoothes out the changes which could otherwise appear abrupt and jarring, especially on slower devices.
<|end|>


