<|asst|>
Here's how you could translate your Python code into PHP:

```php
function statistics($array) {
    // Check if input is an array
    if (!is_array($array)) {
        return 'Invalid input';
    } else {
        // Check if elements of array are all integers
        foreach ($array as $element) {
            if (!is_int($element)) {
                return "List must only contain integers";
            }
        }
        // Calculate the list length, sum and mean
        $size = count($array);
        $list_sum = array_sum($array);
        $mean = $list_sum / $size;
        
        // Sort the array
        sort($array);
        
        // Check if length of list is even
        if ($size % 2 == 0) {
            $mid1 = $array[$size/2];
            $mid2 = $array[$size/2 - 1];
            // Calculate median for even-sized array
            $median = ($mid1 + $mid2) / 2;
        } else {
            // Calculate median for odd-sized array
            $median = $array[($size - 1) / 2];
        }
        
        // Return the results (rounded)
        return array(round($median, 4), round($mean, 4));
    }
}
```

Keep in mind that in PHP, arrays are zero-indexed, so $array[$size/2] in PHP has the same effect as l[size//2] in Python (taking the floor of the division), even when the size is odd. The key difference is in the case of an odd size, where the Python approach does not need to subtract 1 to get the correct index, but PHP does.
<|end|>


