<|asst|>
In C++, we can create a Struct to hold the information for each employee and then use a vector to make a list of employees. 

Here's an example of how you might do this. 

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

struct Employee 
{
  int id;
  std::string email;
};

int main() 
{
  // Creating two employees
  Employee emp1;
  emp1.id = 1;
  emp1.email = "emp1@example.com";
  
  Employee emp2;
  emp2.id = 2;
  emp2.email = "emp2@example.com";
  
  // Creating a vector of employees
  std::vector<Employee> employees;
  employees.push_back(emp1);
  employees.push_back(emp2);

  // Printing out the employee data
  for (const auto& emp : employees) 
  {
    std::cout << "ID: " << emp.id << ", Email: " << emp.email << std::endl;
  }

  return 0;
}
```

In this code: 

- We first define a struct `Employee` that will hold two fields: `id`, and `email`.
- In main we define two employees `emp1` and `emp2`.
- We then define a `std::vector` of `Employee` objects, and add `emp1` and `emp2` to it with `push_back()`.
- Lastly, we loop through all the `Employee` objects in the vector and print out their `id` and `email`.
