If you want to find all the HTML elements with the attribute `data-featured` carrying a Boolean truth value using Zepto.js, you would need to iterate over all the elements in the DOM and check for each element whether it has this attribute and if the attribute is a truthy value. Here is a simple script that does this:

```javascript
// Finding all HTML elements with the attribute "data-featured" carrying a Boolean truth value
var featuredElements = [];

Zepto('*').each(function() {
  var el = Zepto(this);
  var attrValue = el.attr('data-featured');

  // Checking if the attribute exists and if it is a truthy value 
  if (typeof attrValue !== typeof undefined && attrValue !== false) {
    if (attrValue.toLowerCase() === 'true') {
      featuredElements.push(this);
    }
  }
});

// Printing found elements
console.log(featuredElements);
```

Run this script in the browser's console on the page you need to check.

In this script, `Zepto('*')` selects all elements in the DOM. The `each` function then iterates over all the selected elements. Inside the callback function, we first select the current element with `Zepto(this)`. We then check if this element has the attribute `data-featured` and if the attribute is a truthy value (`'true'`). If it is, we add the element to the `featuredElements` array.

Please note that this script considers only elements that have the `data-featured` attribute set exactly to the string `'true'`.

In case there's the possibility that `data-featured` could carry a truthy value in a numerical or other format like '1', or 'yes', you would have to adjust the comparison accordingly.

Please also note that, dependent on the number and complexity of your DOM elements, this code could consume significant resources. It is always advised to use more specific selectors if possible.
