Could you devise a C++ code to execute the multiplication of two 2-dimensional matrices with specific integer elements? 

Please create the code to also handle both square and rectangular matrices. Also, the program should be able to display an error message if the multiplication of the given matrices is not possible.

Matrix1 = [[2, 3],
           [4, 5],
           [6, 7]]

Matrix2 = [[6, 7],
           [8, 9]]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, here is a simple C++ program that can multiply two 2-dimensional matrices of integers.

This code does check if the number of columns in the first matrix equals the number of rows in the second matrix, which is the rule under which matrix multiplication is possible.

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
  std::vector<std::vector<int> > a = {{2, 3}, {4, 5}, {6, 7}};
  std::vector<std::vector<int> > b = {{6, 7}, {8, 9}};
  std::vector<std::vector<int> > c (a.size(), std::vector<int>(b[0].size()));

  if(a[0].size() != b.size()) {
    std::cout << "Error: matrices cannot be multiplied!\n";
    return 0;
  }

  for(std::size_t i = 0; i < a.size(); ++i) {
      for(std::size_t j = 0; j < b[0].size(); ++j) {
          for(std::size_t k = 0; k < a[0].size(); ++k) {
              c[i][j] += a[i][k] * b[k][j];
          }
      }
  }

  std::cout << "The result of the multiplication is:\n";
  for(std::size_t i = 0; i < a.size(); ++i) {
      for(std::size_t j = 0; j < b[0].size(); ++j) {
          std::cout << c[i][j] << ' ';
      }
      std::cout << '\n';
  }

  return 0;
}
```

When you run this code with the given input matrices, the result is:

```bash
The result of the multiplication is:
36 41 
64 73 
92 105 
```
