<|asst|>
Layering multiple SVGs to create a composite image can be done in several steps:

1. Start with a basic HTML structure, specifying the HTML, head, and body tags.
2. Inside the body tags, paste the SVG code you want to use as your base. This will be your first and biggest base layer.
3. For every following SVG layer you want to add, paste the SVG code immediately after the previous SVG's closing `</svg>` tag.
4. Ensure that each SVG layer is assigned appropriate `z-index` CSS properties. The `z-index` property determines the stack order of the elements. An element with a higher `z-index` will be displayed on top of an element with a lower `z-index`. Use CSS to position your SVGs, varying the `z-index` to dictate the layering order.

Consider the following example where two SVGs are layered where one SVG is on top of other:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<style>
/* Position SVGs using CSS */
.svg1 {
  position: absolute;
  z-index: 1;
}
.svg2 {
  position: absolute;
  z-index: 2;
}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<svg class="svg1" width="100" height="100">
  <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="40" stroke="black" stroke-width="3" fill="red" />
</svg>

<svg class="svg2" width="100" height="100">
  <circle cx="70" cy="70" r="40" stroke="black" stroke-width="3" fill="blue" />
</svg> 

</body>
</html>
```

Note that this will place the SVG elements at same position on top of each other. You may want to adjust their `position` properties (`left`, `top`, `right` and `bottom`) to move them around the container (body or relative parent) as needed.
<|end|>


