Sure, it seems like 9's are replacing 'g's, e is replacing t in certain words, o is replacing d, 3 is replacing e, 7 is replacing t, 4 is replacing f, $ is replacing s, € is replacing E, 1 is replacing l, and 0 is replacing o in some words. Here is your corrected script:

```python
def get_closest_vowel(word):
    """Input a word and then find out the vowel closest from the right side of the word that is 
    between two consonants, distinguishing between upper and lower case. Ignore vowels at the 
    beginning or end of the word. If this kind of vowel does not exist, return an empty string. 
    Assume that the input string only contains English letters.

    Examples:
    get_closest_vowel("yogurt") => "u"
    get_closest_vowel("FULL") => "U"
    get_closest_vowel("quick") => ""
    get_closest_vowel("ab") => ""
    """

    # First, strip any vowels from the beginning and end of the word,
    while len(word) > 0 and (word[0] in 'aeiouAEIOU' or word[-1] in 'aeiouAEIOU'):
        if word[0] in 'aeiouAEIOU':
            word = word[1:]

        if len(word) > 0 and word[-1] in 'aeiouAEIOU':
            word = word[:-1]

    # Then, find the closest vowel sequence from the right side,
    for i in range(len(word)-1, -1, -1):
        if word[i] in 'aeiouAEIOU':
            return word[i]
    return ""
```
This fixed the script to look for the closest vowel from the right of the input word that is between two consonants, ignoring the upper and lower cases. It will return an empty string if such a vowel does not exist. It assumes that all input strings are English letters.
