Use jQuery to traverse the DOM, find all elements with the given CSS class, and then hide them with a smooth transition effect. Additionally, count the number of elements with the given class and display the count in an alert message to the user. The given CSS class is "hidden".
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can use the jQuery functions ".addClass()", ".hide()", and ".length" to solve this problem. Here is an example solution:

```javascript
// Wrap your code in a $(document).ready function to ensure it runs after the document is fully loaded
$(document).ready(function() {
  // Use the jQuery function $ to select all elements with the class "hidden"
  var hiddenElements = $('.hidden');

  // Use the jQuery function hide to hide these elements with a smooth transition effect
  hiddenElements.hide('slow');

  // Use the length property of a jQuery object to get the number of elements with the class "hidden"
  var count = hiddenElements.length;

  // Use the JavaScript function alert to display this count to the user
  alert('There are ' + count + ' elements with the class "hidden"');
});
```
In this script, `$('.hidden')` selects all elements with the class "hidden". The `.hide('slow')` function hides these elements with a smooth transition effect. The `.length` property of a jQuery object gives the number of selected elements, and `alert()` displays a message box with the given string to the user. The string concatenation operator '+' is used to construct a string containing the count.
