<|user|>
I am working on understanding recursion. I wrote a function that returns the sum of an array.
If the if statement for the base case looks like this if (arr.length === 0), the function works. If it looks like this if (arr === []), I get a stack overflow. Why is that? Here is the full program.

const getSum = function (arr){

    //the if statement in question
    if (arr.length === 0){
        return 0;
    }
    else{
        console.log(arr)
        return arr[0] + getSum(arr.slice(1));
    };

};
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The issue is with the way JavaScript compares arrays. In JavaScript, arrays are reference types and are not the same even if they contain the same elements.

When you compare `arr === []`, JavaScript isn't checking if `arr` is an empty array, it's checking if `arr` references the same array as `[]`. However, `[]` is a new, empty array, so `arr` will never reference the exact same array, and the comparison will always return `false` regardless of the contents of `arr`.

In contrast, checking `arr.length === 0` is checking whether the length property of `arr` is zero, which is a correct way to check if an array is empty. So, when `arr` becomes an empty array, the base case gets triggered and the recursive function stops calling itself.

So, the main thing to consider here is that while `[] === []` is `false`, `[].length === [].length` is `true`. That’s why you got a stack overflow error because the recursion never hits the base case and so, never stops.
<|end|>


