<|user|>
Given a faulty piece of code in C++, debug and modify it so that the final code correctly prints the elements of a 2D array. The rows and columns of the array should be given as input parameters. The function will take the 2D array, the number of rows, and the number of columns as arguments. Use smart pointers to allocate the array in memory.

The original faulty implementation is provided below:
```C++
void print2DArray(int** arr, int m, int n)
{
    for (int i = 0; i <= m; ++i) {
        for (int j = 0; j <= n; ++j) {
            cout << arr[i][j] << " ";
        }
        cout << endl;
    }
}
```
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The original implementation has a couple of issues. Firstly, it is using raw pointers to handle memory instead of smart pointers which is considered bad practice in modern C++ due to possible memory leaks. Secondly, it is accessing one index out of bound in both of the for loop conditions.

In C++, arrays indices run from 0 to n-1, where n is the length of the dimension. Thus, the code is trying to access an element that are not part of the array in the top and rightmost part of the 2D array.

We will use std::vector as our 2D array and std::shared_ptr to handle memory. Here is the corrected code:

```C++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <memory>

using namespace std;

void print2DArray(shared_ptr<vector<vector<int>>> arr, int m, int n)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
            cout << (*arr)[i][j] << " ";
        }
        cout << endl;
    }
}

int main() {
    auto array_ptr = make_shared<vector<vector<int>>>(2, vector<int>(3, 0));  // Example 2 x 3 array
    (*array_ptr)[0][0] = 1;  // Just to show it works, populating it with some values
    (*array_ptr)[0][1] = 2;
    (*array_ptr)[0][2] = 3;
    (*array_ptr)[1][0] = 4;
    (*array_ptr)[1][1] = 5;
    (*array_ptr)[1][2] = 6;
    print2DArray(array_ptr, 2, 3);
    return 0;
}
```

In the corrected code, std::shared_ptr is used to automatically manage the memory of the vector. When we pass the shared_ptr to print2DArray, the array itself is actually passed by reference (i.e., no copy is made), and we simply increment the reference counter in the smart pointer.

Also notice that we directly pass the dimensions (row and column count) in the vector creation (make_shared). This creates a dynamic 2-dimensional array with the specified number of rows and columns.

Furthermore, the actual indexing should be from 0 to m-1 and 0 to n-1. This ensures that we don't go out of bounds and possibly accessing illegal memory, causing segmentation fault or undefined behavior.
<|end|>


