Engineer a function in C++ that systematically arranges an array constituted of integer entries in an ascendant sequence based on their explicit quantitative values without using in-built sorting functions. Alongside, create an error handling method for any possible invalid types or missing values that might break the function. Your solution should still function even when the array is randomly ordered or contains both positive and negative numbers. Here is a current piece of code that does not work:

```C++
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

void sort(int unsorted_arr[]){

  int size = sizeof(unsorted_arr)/sizeof(unsorted_arr[0]);

  for(int i=0;i<size;i++)
  {
     for(int j=i+1;j<size;j++)
     {
        if(unsorted_arr[i] > unsorted_arr[j]){
            int temp = unsorted_arr[i];
            unsorted_arr[i] = unsorted_arr[j];
            unsorted_arr[j] = temp;
        }
     }
  }
}
```
Given array is unsorted_arr[] = {45, 21, 12, 56, 7, 47, 34, -23, 0, 89, -54}.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The array size in your function is not correctly determined. In C++, if you want to determine the size of an array, you need to pass it as an argument to your function because the `sizeof` operator does not work as you expect it to when dealing with array arguments (actually the 'array' that you're passing is merely a pointer in the context of the function). Here is the correct code:

```C++
#include<iostream>

void sort(int *unsorted_arr, int size){
    for(int i = 0; i < size; i++){
        for(int j = i+1; j < size; j++){
            if(unsorted_arr[i] > unsorted_arr[j]){
                int temp = unsorted_arr[i];
                unsorted_arr[i] = unsorted_arr[j];
                unsorted_arr[j] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}

int main(){
    int unsorted_arr[] = {45, 21, 12, 56, 7, 47, 34, -23, 0, 89, -54};
    int size = sizeof(unsorted_arr)/sizeof(unsorted_arr[0]); 
    sort(unsorted_arr,size); 
    for(int i = 0; i < size; i++)
        std::cout << unsorted_arr[i] << " ";
    return 0;
}
```
In this code, the size of array is computed in the `main` function and passed to the sorting function. After executing the function, sorted array is printed to the standard output.

Unfortunately, C++ does not provide direct mechanisms for error handling in cases of invalid types or missing values (null or uninitialized), because arrays in C++ cannot contain null values, and the type of the array elements is always known at compile time. Using vectors or other container classes instead of array would provide a safer and more flexible solution. Always make sure that the data you input is valid and use the correct datatype while declaring the array.
<|end|>


