Write a JavaScript script to reverse a string. Additionally, the reversal should preserve the capitalization of the original string (the reversed string should have the same letters capitalized as in the original). For example, text = “Hello world” should return “dlroW olleH”.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here you go:

```javascript
function reverseString(str) {
    let reversedStr = str.split('').reverse().join('');

    // preserve original capitalization
    let finalStr = '';
    for (let i in reversedStr) {
        if (str[i] === str[i].toUpperCase()) {
            finalStr += reversedStr[i].toUpperCase();
        } else {
            finalStr += reversedStr[i].toLowerCase();
        }
    }

    return finalStr;
}

console.log(reverseString("Hello world"));
```

This reverseString function first reverses the input string using the split, reverse, and join methods. It then iterates over the characters in the reversed string, checking the casing of the correspondingly positioned character in the original string. If the original character is uppercase, the script converts the character in the reversed string to uppercase; if not, it converts it to lowercase. The final output is the reversed string with preserved capitalization.
