We are using `JdbcOperations.queryForStream()` to fetch 30k+ rows from database as per the inputs from [Spring Integration Jdbc OutboundGateway returning 1 record ONLY even with MaxRows(0)](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/74729365/spring-integration-jdbc-outboundgateway-returning-1-record-only-even-with-maxrow), however `split()` and `aggregate()` on the stream is not working. We need the aggregation to work as to know when all the stream records are consumed to perform a final operation.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Spring Integration's `split()` and `aggregate()` methods do not support Java Streams directly. This is because the Java Stream API is fundamentally different from the mechanism that Spring Integration uses to handle message flows. 

You have a couple of different options:

1. **Convert the Stream to a List/Collection.**
You can collect all the items in the Stream into a List or other Collection, then use the `split()` method like you're used to.

```java
Stream<YourObject> yourObjectStream = jdbcTemplate.queryForStream(yourQuery, yourRowMapper);
List<YourObject> yourObjectList = yourObjectStream.collect(Collectors.toList());
```

Keep in mind that this approach may not be feasible if you have a large number of rows, because it will load all of the rows into memory at once.

2. **Use `foreach`, not `split()`, for processing.** 
If you process the stream item-by-item in a `foreach` loop (so that each item is handled individually and can be garbage-collected when no longer needed) can be more memory-efficient. 

However, keep in mind that you can't use the `aggregate()` method directly with this approach as well. You'll need to design your own aggregation logic which suits your particular circumstances.

Additionally, using `JdbcOperations.queryForStream()` effectively fetches all the rows into memory which might also cause issues if you're dealing with a large number of rows. You may want to look into other options to handle large results, such as paging or cursor-based interactions. 

If you need the `aggregate()` function for some reason, then separating your logic and properly implementing the Aggregator pattern yourself might be another solution worth considering.
<|end|>


