Implement a function in JavaScript that not only checks if a string ends with a given substring but also takes into account a specified number of occurrences of that substring. Utilize the concept of Regular Expressions in this particular task. Furthermore, also consider the case in which the string or substring could contain special characters and handle these cases effectively.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a JavaScript function that fulfills the requirements. This function creates a dynamic regular expression which matches the expected number of occurrences.

```javascript
function stringEndsWithSubstr(input, substr, occurrences) {
    // Escape special characters
    var escapedSubstr = substr.replace(/[-\/\\^$*+?.()|[\]{}]/g, '\\$&');

    // Build up the regex pattern
    var pattern = "(" + escapedSubstr + "){" + occurrences + "}$";
    var regex = new RegExp(pattern);

    // Check if the input matches the regex
    return regex.test(input);
}
```

Special characters in substrings are escaped with the help of the string replace method. Mystery symbols are replaced with their escaped counterparts. In regular expressions, these characters include backslashes `\`, forward slashes `/`, caret symbols `^`, dollar signs `$`, asterisks `*`, plus signs `+`, question marks `?`, dots `.` and vertical bars `|`.

For instance, if you want to check if the string `Hello, World!...` ends with three periods, you can call the function like this:

```javascript
console.log(stringEndsWithSubstr('Hello, World!...', '.', 3));  // Outputs: true
```

This outputs `true` as the string `Hello, World!...` indeed ends with three periods.

Keep in mind that the function is case-sensitive. If you want to make it case-insensitive, you can add `i` when creating the regex:

```javascript
var regex = new RegExp(pattern, "i");
```

But this will only make sense if your substr contains alphabet characters.
<|end|>


