Construct a PHP script that utilizes recursion instead of a for loop to produce the integers from 0 to 20, also include an option to specify a different start and end point. An extra complexity could be an option to produce the sequence in reversed order.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, here's the PHP recursive function `generate_sequence()` which solves the problem:

```php
function generate_sequence($start = 0, $end = 20, $reverse = false) {
    echo $start . "<br>";
    
    if ($reverse) {
        if ($start < $end) {
            generate_sequence($start + 1, $end, $reverse);
        } 
    } else {
        if ($start > 0) {
            generate_sequence($start - 1, $end, $reverse);
        } 
    }
}

generate_sequence(0, 20, false); // prints 0 to 20
generate_sequence(20, 0, true);  // prints 20 to 0
generate_sequence();             // prints default sequence 0 to 20
```

In this script, the `generate_sequence()` function takes in three arguments:

- `$start` for the starting number of the sequence
- `$end` for the ending number of the sequence
- `$reverse` for the order of the sequence, be it normal (from smaller to larger number; `false`) or reversed (from larger to smaller number; `true`)

Given these inputs, the function will print a sequence of integers from `$start` to `$end`. It uses recursion to do this by calling itself with the next integer in the sequence until it reaches `$end`. The `$reverse` flag determines whether it prints the sequence in ascending or descending order.
