We have a PHP project that we would like to version control. Right now there are three of us working on a development version of the project which resides in an external folder to which all of our Eclipse IDEs are linked, and thus no version control.

What is the right way and the best way to version control this?

We have an SVN set up, but we just need to find a good way to check in and out that allows us to test on the development server. Any ideas?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a simple and effective workflow you can use with Git, assuming you have all the necessary permissions:

1. Move your development project to a new folder and initialize it as a Git repository:
    ```
    git init
    ```
2. Add all your existing project files to the Git repository and commit:
    ```
    git add .
    git commit -m "Initial commit"
    ```
3. Link your local repository to your SVN repository. You may need to convert it to a Git repository first if you want to use Git on your local machine. Git-SVN is a good tool that allows you to use Git locally but still keep SVN as your remote server.
    ```
    git svn clone [SVN repo URL]
    ```
4. Each developer can now clone the repository onto their individual machines and work from there:
    ```
    git clone [repo URL]
    ```

Now everyone can work on their own local copy of the project. They can commit their changes locally, then push them to the remote repository when they're ready:

```git
    git commit -m "Made changes"
    git push origin master
```

Others can then pull these changes into their own copies:

```git
    git pull origin master
```

This system allows each developer to work independently, without affecting the main codebase until they're ready. This preserves a clean, coherent version of the project that can be tested on your development server at any time.

While this is a simplified workflow, I would recommend learning more about Git and its strong capabilities such as branching and merging to more effectively manage the development process. Tools like Atlassian SourceTree offer a visual representation of your commits, which can be helpful to understand and manage.

Also consider solutions like Bitbucket or GitHub for hosting your repositories, they also could offer additional functionalities like pull requests and code reviews that can be beneficial for a team development process.
<|end|>


