func EvenOddPalindrome(n int) [][]int {
    // Initialize result variable
    var result = make([][]int,n)

    evenPalindromeCounter, oddPalindromeCounter := 0, 0

    for i := 1; i <= n; i++ {

        // check each number for palindrome
        for j := 1; j <= i; j++ {
            if isPalindrome(j) {
                if j % 2 == 0 {
                    // even palindrome
                    evenPalindromeCounter++
                } else {
                    // odd palindrome
                    oddPalindromeCounter++
                }
            }
        }
        result[i-1] = []int{i,evenPalindromeCounter, oddPalindromeCounter}
    }

    return result
}

// Helper Function to check if a number is palindrome
func isPalindrome(n int) bool {
    str := strconv.Itoa(n)
    for i := 0; i < len(str)/2; i++ {
        if str[i] != str[len(str)-i-1] {
            return false
        }
    }
    return true
}
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is the Java version:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 10;
        List<List<Integer>> result = evenOddPalindrome(n);

        for(List<Integer> list: result){
            System.out.println(Arrays.toString(list.toArray()));
        }
    }

    static List<List<Integer>> evenOddPalindrome(int n) {
        // Initialize result variable
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();

        int evenPalindromeCounter = 0, oddPalindromeCounter = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {

            // check each number for palindrome
            for (int j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
                if (isPalindrome(j)) {
                    if (j % 2 == 0) {
                        // even palindrome
                        evenPalindromeCounter++;
                    } else {
                        // odd palindrome
                        oddPalindromeCounter++;
                    }
                }
            }
            result.add(Arrays.asList(i, evenPalindromeCounter, oddPalindromeCounter));
        }

        return result;
    }

    // Helper Function to check if a number is palindrome
    private static boolean isPalindrome(int n) {
        String str = Integer.toString(n);
        for (int i = 0; i < str.length() / 2; i++) {
            if (str.charAt(i) != str.charAt(str.length() - i - 1)) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```  
In Java, we often use the List interface and classes that implement it (like ArrayList) when we want resizable arrays. Also, we use Arrays.asList to create lists and Arrays.toString when we want to convert arrays to strings.
Note that the (int) n passed to the evenOddPalindrome function is the upper limit of the for loop which inspects every number from 1 to n. The output will be a List of lists of integers. Each inner list will contain a number i, the count of even palindromes, and the count of odd palindromes for all the numbers from 1 to i.  

Our main method tests our code, creating a list of lists of the first 10 positive numbers. This list includes each number and its even and odd palindrome counts from the first number up to the current one. After that, we print the result.
<|end|>


