I am dealing with a 2-dimensional numpy array as illustrated below:
a = np.array([[1,5,9,13],
              [2,6,10,14],
              [3,7,11,15],
              [4,8,12,16]])
My objective is to dissect this array into non-overlapping patches of 2 by 2 dimensions. The resulting patches should maintain the original order of elements as shown below:
[[[1,5],
 [2,6]],   
 [[3,7],
 [4,8]],
 [[9,13],
 [10,14]],
 [[11,15],
 [12,16]]]
What is the most efficient way to achieve this? In my actual problem, the array 'a' has dimensions of (36, 72). Manual extraction is not feasible, hence I am seeking a programmatic solution.
A:
&lt;code&gt;
import numpy as np
a = np.array([[1,5,9,13],
              [2,6,10,14],
              [3,7,11,15],
              [4,8,12,16]])
&lt;/code&gt;
result = ... # insert solution in this variable
BEGIN SOLUTION
&lt;code&gt;
<|end|>
<|asst|>
def extract_patches(arr, patch_shape):
    arr_shape = np.array(arr.shape)
    patch_shape = np.array(patch_shape)
    patch_strides = arr.strides

    sub_shape = tuple(arr_shape - patch_shape + 1)
    sub_strides = tuple(patch_strides * patch_shape)

    view_shape = tuple(np.concatenate((sub_shape, patch_shape)))
    view_strides = tuple(np.concatenate((sub_strides, patch_strides)))

    patches = np.lib.stride_tricks.as_strided(arr, view_shape, view_strides)
    return patches

result = extract_patches(a, (2,2))
result = result.reshape(-1, 2, 2)
&lt;/code&gt;
<|end|>


