I run two compilations together: server-side and client-side. I wish to let webpack  import files into server bundle when it is referenced (imported) by somefile in the client bundle and when it pass some check, but not vice-versa. I'm looking for some sort of plugin (probably custom) which would control which files should be imported and adds them to the server bundle. It should also co-operate with hot module replacement. And when file is no longer imported by the client compilation, it should be also removed from server bundle. Or when the automatically imported file is changed, it should notice the change and recompile it.
I tried creating custom plugin but I am not able to get the file content into resulting bundle. I am also using babel, so maybe that could help somehow.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can use webpack's NormalModuleReplacementPlugin to control what files are included in each of your server and client bundles, as well as conditions for which files to include.

Here is an example of how you could use this plugin to control file inclusion based on a condition:

```javascript
new webpack.NormalModuleReplacementPlugin(
  // You can change this regex to match the files you want to include or exclude
  // This current regex will match any .js files
  /(.*)\.js$/,
  function(resource) {
    // You can put any conditions here to determine what files to include
    // For example, check if the file is being imported by another file on the client
    if (resource.context.indexOf('client') !== -1) {
      resource.request = resource.request + '.server';
    }
  },
),
```

The code above changes the file extension of all imported JavaScript files to .server.js in the server bundle. Note that this is a rather brute-force method which change all your JavaScript import statements in the server to import .server.js files meaning these files need to exist too.

Unfortunately, webpack does not provide a direct way to remove files automatically from the server bundle when they are no longer required in the client bundle. An alternative approach is to enhance the plugin by storing the file name and path in a database and check that database whenever a build is triggered. If the file does not exist, remove the entry from the database. However, this might be overkill depending on your application size and structure.

It's important to note that module replacement might also end up causing HMR issues, since HMR is based on module id's and replacement might end up changing these id's.

Also, a custom plugin would need to tap into the necessary events emitted by webpack during the compilation process, in order to respond to changes to imports & changes/reloads in any required modules.

Hope this helps you to get some idea.
<|end|>


