You can use JavaScript's built-in Math.random() function to generate random numbers. Here's a JavaScript object that creates an ordered array of numbers between 20 and 50:

```
let NumberCompilation = {
    data: [],
    populateData: function(size) {
        for(let i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            let randomNumeral = Math.floor(Math.random() * 31) + 20;
            this.data.push(randomNumeral);
        }
        this.data.sort((a, b) => a - b);
    }
}

// Use the object
NumberCompilation.populateData(10);

// Print the ordered array of numbers
console.log(NumberCompilation.data);
```
In the above code, populateData method fills the data array with size amount of random numbers between 20 and 50, and then sorts the data. Math.random() generates a random number between 0 and 1, we multiply that by 31 to get a number in range 0 to 30, then add 20 to shift that range to 20 to 50. We use Math.floor() to convert the resulting number to an integer.

Please observe that the same number could be added multiple times since the numbers are chosen randomly. If unique numbers are required, use a while loop to keep creating a new number until a unique one if found. Lastly, The numbers are sorted in ascending order using array's sort() method.
