Design a console application in C++ that computes the factorial of a user-input number using recursion.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, here is a simple C++ console application that calculates the factorial of a number using recursion.

```C++
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

// Function to compute factorial
long long Factorial(int number) {
    if (number >= 1)
        return number*Factorial(number - 1);
    else
        return 1;
}

int main() {
    int number;
    cout << "Enter a positive integer: ";
    cin >> number;
    // Check if the number is negative
    if (number < 0) {
        cout << "Error! Factorial of negative number doesn't exist.";
    } else {
        cout << "Factorial of " << number << " = " << Factorial(number) << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
In this program, we first define a recursive function `Factorial()`. This function calls itself with a lower value in each iteration until it reaches the base case (number = 0). Then the computed values are returned back up the call stack to compute and return the final factorial value.

In the `main()` function, we get an input number from the user, then we pass this number to the `Factorial()` function and print out the result. Note that if the user enters a negative number, we display an error message since the factorial of negative numbers is undefined.

Remember to always take care of the termination condition while dealing with recursive functions. The termination condition in this case is when the number equals 0. Without a proper termination condition, a recursive function can end up calling itself indefinitely.

Also, factorials can get very large very quickly, so this program uses `long long` to store the result. Even so, for inputs above 20, the result will still be too large to represent accurately.
<|end|>


