Devise an HTML script integrated with CSS and JavaScript, in such a way that it furnishes a page design with an interactive transition from your usual theme to a "night mode". The "night mode" should be characterised by an ebony-hued background and an overlay of alabaster written content. The script should remember the user preference, so that on subsequent visits, the page presents in last chosen mode. Additionally, scripts should be efficiently cross-browser compatible.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a simple HTML, CSS, and JavaScript code that accomplishes exactly what you asked.

The HTML file:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Night Mode Website</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
</head>
<body>
    <div id="content">
        <h1>Night Mode Example</h1>
        <button id="toggleBtn">Toggle Night Mode</button>
    </div>
    <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```
The CSS file (styles.css):

```css
body {
    transition: background-color 1s ease;
    color: black;
    background-color: white;
}

body.night {
    color: white;
    background-color: black;
}

button {
    margin-top: 50px;
}
```
The JavaScript file (script.js):

```javascript
window.onload = function() {
    var toggleBtn = document.getElementById('toggleBtn');
    var preference = localStorage.getItem('mode_preference');

    if (preference === 'night') {
        document.body.classList.add('night');
        toggleBtn.textContent = "Toggle Day Mode";
    } else {
        document.body.classList.remove('night');
        toggleBtn.textContent = "Toggle Night Mode";
    }

    toggleBtn.onclick = function() {
        if (document.body.classList.contains('night')) {
            document.body.classList.remove('night');
            localStorage.setItem('mode_preference', 'day');
            toggleBtn.textContent = "Toggle Night Mode";
        } else {
            document.body.classList.add('night');
            localStorage.setItem('mode_preference', 'night');
            toggleBtn.textContent = "Toggle Day Mode";
        }
    }
}
```

This code gives users a pretty basic "night mode" feature. When the button is pressed, the color of the page is toggled between black (for "night mode") and white (for the default mode). Also, the user's preference is stored using the LocalStorage API in the user's web browser, so when they revisit the page, their last theme choice is remembered. The color transition is smooth, thanks to the CSS transition property on the body tag.

This code should work in modern web browsers like Chrome, Firefox, Safari, and Edge. If you need to support Internet Explorer, you may need to adjust the JavaScript code to replace the `classlist.add`, `classlist.remove` and `localStorage` functionalities as they are not fully supported in older versions of Internet Explorer.
<|end|>


