Write a PHP script to compare two text files of varying lengths (file1.txt and file2.txt). Your script should consider case sensitivity in comparison and return:

1. The number of unique words that are common in both files.
2. The frequency of each common word.
3. The number of unique characters that are common in both files.
4. The frequency of each common character.

Provide solutions for both space and non-space complexity. Test your script with various sizes of text files.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is the code:

```php
<?php

// Read file 
function readFileContent($file) {
    $content = file_get_contents($file);
    return strtolower($content);
}

// Get Words
function getWords($text) {
    $text = preg_replace('/\s+/', ' ', $text);
    $words = explode(' ', $text);
    return $words;
}

// Get Character
function getCharacters($text) {
    return str_split(preg_replace('/\s+/', '', $text));
}

// Frequency Count
function frequencyCount($arr) {
    $frequency = array_count_values($arr);
    return $frequency;
}

// File readings
$file1Content = readFileContent('file1.txt');
$file2Content = readFileContent('file2.txt');

// Get words and Character from File content
$file1Words = getWords($file1Content);
$file2Words = getWords($file2Content);
$file1Characters = getCharacters($file1Content);
$file2Characters = getCharacters($file2Content);

// Get Common Words and Characters
$commonWords = array_intersect($file1Words, $file2Words);
$commonCharacters = array_intersect($file1Characters, $file2Characters);

// Frequency of Common Words and Characters
$commonWordsFrequency = frequencyCount($commonWords);
$commonCharactersFrequency = frequencyCount($commonCharacters);

// Output
echo "Number of Unique Common Words: " . count($commonWords) . "\n";
echo "Frequency of Each Common Word: \n";
print_r($commonWordsFrequency);
echo "Number of Unique Common Characters: " . count($commonCharacters) . "\n";
echo "Frequency of Each Common Character: \n";
print_r($commonCharactersFrequency);

?>
```

The time complexity of the script is O(n), where n is the number of words/characters in the larger file. The space complexity is also O(n) for the same reason, as we are storing the words/characters in arrays.

It is important that the file size does not surpass the available memory, as in this implementation, the whole file content is loaded into memory. In a real-world situation, where you might need to handle very large files, you'd have to consider a solution that reads the files piece by piece instead.

For testing, you could use text files of various sizes and content, just be sure to replace 'file1.txt' and 'file2.txt' with the appropriate file paths on your server. For example, you could test with small text files of a few kilobytes, then work upwards towards larger files of a few megabytes to stress test and understand how your script handles larger files.
<|end|>


