The task is to eliminate any rows that are considered overlapping. Overlapping is defined as any row that falls within X days of another row. For instance, if X = 365, the expected output would be:
ID  date       close
1   09/15/07   123.45
3   10/25/08   132.01
5   11/07/09   145.99
7   07/03/11   171.10

If X = 50, the output should be:
ID  date       close
1   09/15/07   123.45
2   06/01/08   130.13
3   10/25/08   132.01
4   05/13/09   118.34
5   11/07/09   145.99
7   07/03/11   171.10

The current code in use is inefficient and fails to deliver the correct results when X is large (e.g., X = 365), as it eliminates all dates except the original one. The code is as follows:
filter_dates = []
for index, row in df.iterrows():
     if observation_time == &#39;D&#39;:
        for i in range(1, observation_period):
            filter_dates.append((index.date() + timedelta(days=i)))
df = df[~df.index.isin(filter_dates)]

The solution should consider every row, not just the first one. Any guidance or suggestions would be greatly appreciated!

A:
&lt;code&gt;
import pandas as pd

df = pd.DataFrame({&#39;ID&#39;: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8],
                   &#39;date&#39;: [&#39;09/15/07&#39;, &#39;06/01/08&#39;, &#39;10/25/08&#39;, &#39;1/14/9&#39;, &#39;05/13/09&#39;, &#39;11/07/09&#39;, &#39;11/15/09&#39;, &#39;07/03/11&#39;],
