I'm trying to parse the coordinates of a matrix from a vector to retrieve an element.
Data:

m <- matrix(1:25, ncol = 5)
v <- c(2,3)


I'm basically trying to get the element`m[2,3]`, in that case the value 12, by parsing the vector as coordinates:
`m[v]`
but all I get is `NA`. I have tried `m[paste(v, collapse="\",\""]`, which also did not work.
I know I could use
`m[paste(v[1]), paste(v[2])]`
but I'm trying to find a more elegant solution.
Any idea how to get this to work?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can use the `do.call` function to pass the coordinates from a vector to the subset operator like so:

```r
do.call(`[`, c(list(m), as.list(v)))
```

This will return `12`, which is the element at the position specified by the vector `v` in `m`.

What this does is it uses `do.call` to call the function `[` (the subset operator) with the arguments given as a list, extracting the values from the vector `v` into separate list elements with `as.list`, so you don't need to manually specify each index. This can be particularly useful if you want to extract elements from a matrix with more dimensions, as you would just need a longer vector and don't need to change your subsetting code.
<|end|>


