In order to show a gif from a local directory in Flutter, you can use the Image.file widget along with the dart:io library's File class.  
Here is an example that shows you how to do it:

```dart
import 'dart:io';
import 'flutter/material.dart';

void main() {
  runApp(MyApp());
}

class MyApp extends StatelessWidget {
  // This widget is the root of your application.
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return MaterialApp(
      home: Scaffold(
        appBar: AppBar(
          title: Text('Display Local GIF'),
        ),
        body: Center(
          child: Image.file(
            File('/your_directory_path/your_file.gif'),
            height: 150.0,
            width: 100.0
            ),
        ),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```
Please replace `'/your_directory_path/your_file.gif'` with your real file path.
This example assumes the file is accessible and the path is correct and points to a .gif file.

If it doesn't do the animation, You can also use the Photoview library and CachedNetworkImageProvider in order to read GIF files from the device storage.

Remember that to read files, you will need the read/write permission according to your platform (iOS/Android).
