How would you create a CSS style for alternating the background color of table rows, ensuring the colors maintain a minimum contrast ratio of 4.5:1 for accessibility purposes?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
First, you will need to define your colors ensuring that the color contrast ratio is at least 4.5:1 for accessibility purposes. This usually involves a light color and a darker color. For our example, let's use light gray (#f5f5f5) and darker gray (#707070). 

Once you've decided on your color scheme, we can use the :nth-child selector in CSS to alternate between the two colors for table rows. 

Here is an example of how this could be implemented:

```css
/* Define basic table style */
table {
  width: 100%;
  table-layout: auto;
  border-collapse: collapse;
}

/* Define color for all table rows */
tr {
  background-color: #f5f5f5;  /* light gray */
  color: #000000;  /* black */
}

/* Define color for alternate table rows */
tr:nth-child(even) {
  background-color: #707070;  /* darker gray */
  color: #ffffff;  /* white */
}
```

This will style all rows with a light gray background by default, and then will override this for every even numbered row to have a darker gray background. It also ensures that the text will be black for light rows and white for dark rows, to provide contrast. 

You can check the contrast ratio using online tools like "WebAIM's contrast checker". Note that it's not just about color contrast, but also about size and font weight when it comes to text legibility for accessibility purposes. 

Remember, alternative rows start from 0, rendering the first row as an "even" row. If you want the first line to be light and the second line to be dark, you should swap the colors in the snippets above. 

It's always best practice to check your website with accessibility tools and/or people with diverse abilities to ensure your site can be used by everyone.
<|end|>


