I am doing survival analysis with KM estimates and one of my curve displays a survival drop to zero:
[](https://i.stack.imgur.com/fqjUO.png)
As I was explained [here](https://stats.stackexchange.com/a/262456/378355) the drop is here because the last time is an observed event (death) and not a censor. In my case it is not a database error because I don't have a constant censoring time. In this case, how should I interpret the drop? What is counterintuitive is that I probably don't have zero survival at this time point...
Here is the data:

time event
0.1 0
0.1 0
0.6 1
1.3 0
1.8 0
1.9 0
3.2 0
3.5 0
4.3 0
4.5 1
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The drop to zero on the KM curve represents that there were no individuals left at risk in the study beyond that time point. Since the last time point (4.5) is an observed event (death) and all the remaining participants at that time point had the event, the curve drops to zero.

In terms of interpretation, it doesn't literally mean that no one could survive past that time point. It's simply an artifact of your data - no one in your study was observed beyond that time point without having the event. 

If you had additional data (either people who were censored after 4.5 or people who died after 4.5), the curve would not drop to zero at that point. It's important to clarify that the basis of the Kaplan-Meier estimator is the data that's available to you, and it makes certain assumptions about censoring being unrelated to the probability of the event. If those assumptions are violated, the KM curve can be misleading. 

In scenarios where it's crucial to avoid this kind of drop to zero, you might consider alternative methods, such as parametric survival analysis models, that are able to extrapolate survival probabilities beyond the observed data. However, such methods come with their own assumptions and complexities.
<|end|>


