<|asst|>
Embarking on an adventure into the world of quantum teleportation evokes incredible images of realms beyond the familiar, where particles separated by unfathomable distances can communicate instantaneously, and information can be transported across space without traversing the intermediate distance.

The genesis of quantum teleportation emerged from the mind-boggling intricacies of quantum mechanics, namely the iconic principle of quantum entanglement. Quantum entanglement, first scrutinized by Einstein, Podolsky, and Rosen (EPR) in 1935, gained a novel interpretation in the early 1990s when six physicists, including Charles Bennett, proposed the notion of quantum teleportation. By intertwining two particles in a state of quantum entanglement, it became theoretically feasible to teleport quantum information from one particle to its counterpoint, irrespective of the distance separating them.

The journey of quantum teleportation, however, has not been entirely straightforward, necessitating multiple refinements and continual improvements. One of the prime obstacles in this expedition has been error correction—addressing inaccuracies in quantum states and information loss induced by environmental disturbances.

Addressing these issues required the conceptual finesse of quantum error correction theories, which strive to protect quantum information from corruption during storage and transmission. These theories depend on redundancy to detect and correct errors, similar to classical error-correcting codes but with one crucial distinction: quantum codes preserve coherence — the crucial factor underpinning quantum superposition and entanglement — between quantum bits (qubits).

The robust monitoring and maintenance of quantum teleportation systems are undoubtedly critical, but perhaps more so are potential theoretical risks. Solving these risks require innovative problem-solving tactics such as decoherence-free subspaces, quantum error correction codes, and fault-tolerant quantum computing strategies. 

Astonishingly, techniques like these have made the construction of quantum teleportation systems conceivable. Quantum teleportation can now serve as a cornerstone in building complex systems for quantum communication, boosting communication’s security and efficiency. Quantum teleportation could thus construct a secure communication infrastructure that could encode information in quantum states and teleport it across vast distances instantaneously.

Also, quantum teleportation has the potential to revolutionize the entire vista of quantum physics, steering it to new directions. By overcoming classical information boundaries, quantum teleportation can change the paradigms of secure data transmission, pushing us closer to quantum supremacy and an era of unparalleled computational power and communication speed.

The potential influence, implications, and repercussions of quantum teleportation are colossal, reshaping not just quantum communication but significantly heralding a new frontier in our understanding of the universe. With constant research and innovation, one can only imagine the boundaries that quantum teleportation will break to redefine the future.
<|end|>


