Develop a Java function which executes the Linerar-Time Longest Common Subsequence algorithm. The function should take two distinct alphanumeric character sequences as input. If there exists a common subsequence, the function should return that subsequence. If more than one common subsequence exists having the same length, output all of them. Besides, the function should also return the sequence which has a greater length among the initial two strings. In case of the length of both strings being equal, return both.

Here are the two distinct character sequences for the task:

String str1 = "stackoverflow";
String str2 = "statisticsflow";
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In Java, you can solve this problem using dynamic programming. Below is the Java function that executes the Linerar-Time Longest Common Subsequence algorithm.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    String str1 = "stackoverflow";
    String str2 = "statisticsflow";

    List<String> result = LCS(str1, str2);
    System.out.println("LCS: " + result);

    if (str1.length() == str2.length()) {
        System.out.println(str1 + "\n" + str2);
    } else {
        System.out.println(str1.length() > str2.length() ? str1 : str2);
    }
  }

  public static List<String> LCS(String X,String Y) {

    int m = X.length();
    int n = Y.length();

    int[][] dp = new int[m+1][n+1];
     
    for (int i=0; i<=m; i++) {
      for (int j=0; j<=n; j++) {
        if (i == 0 || j == 0) dp[i][j] = 0;
        else if (X.charAt(i-1) == Y.charAt(j-1)) dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1;
        else dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]);
      }
    }

    int index = dp[m][n];
    char[] lcs = new char[index+1];
    lcs[index] = '\u0000';

    int i = m, j = n;
    while (i > 0 && j > 0){
        if (X.charAt(i-1) == Y.charAt(j-1)){
            lcs[index-1] = X.charAt(i-1);
            i--; j--; index--;
        } else if (dp[i-1][j] > dp[i][j-1]) i--;
        else j--;
    }

    List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
    result.add(String.valueOf(lcs));
    return result;
  }
}
``` 

You will note that this program will find only one Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) when there are multiple LCSs of the same length. That's because it captures only one LCS in the way it navigates the dynamic programming grid. To find all possible LCSs, we would need a far more complex algorithm that can navigate all possible paths through this grid, not just one.
<|end|>


