[https://go.dev/play/p/BeDOUZ9QhaG](https://go.dev/play/p/BeDOUZ9QhaG)
Output -

map[something:map[ACM:34.12 age:12 dune:dune]]


How does changing values in t variable affect in x?

package main

import "fmt"

    func main() {
        x: = make(map[string] interface {}, 10)
        x["something"] = map[string] interface {} {
            "dune": "dune", "age": 12
        }
    
        t: = x["something"].(map[string] interface {})
        t["ACM"] = 34.12
       

 fmt.Println(x)
}
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The `x` variable in this code is a map where each value can be of any type (specified by `interface{}`). One item's value is another map with string keys and values of any type.

When you do this line: `t := x["something"].(map[string]interface{})`, you are getting the value of "something" key from the `x` map and asserting that the value is of type `map[string]interface{}` (which it indeed is). It means that `t` is not a new copy of the map, but it's just a point towards the original inner map stored in `x["something"]`.

In the next line, `t["ACM"] = 34.12`, you are inserting a new key-value pair into the `t` map. But because `t` points to the original map inside `x["something"]`, that change also updates the inner map stored as the "something" key's value in `x`.

Think of `map` as a pointer type. Any changes to the map that a map variable points to will affect the map itself and are visible by other variables pointing at the same map. In your example, `t` and `x["something"]` both point to the same map instance, so changes through `t` are seen by `x["something"]`.
<|end|>


