I am writing a number of actions (IBM Cloud functions,) which share a couple of the same parameters, e.g. the IAMAuthenticator or the database path. Is there a way to set these as some kind of environmental variables common to several functions?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
IBM Cloud Functions doesn't support environment variables in the same way as Cloud Foundry or a Kubernetes cluster does. IBM Cloud Functions are stateless and reusable pieces of codes that can be triggered by various events and can return a result after execution.

However, there are several ways you can share a set of parameters across multiple functions:

1. Package Binding: You can create a package binding, which is a new package that references an existing package and includes a set of default parameters. These default parameters are passed to the actions within the package when they are invoked.

2. Coding Approach: Using your programming code, you can create a function or class that includes the common parameters and use this function or class in your actions.

3. Parameter Files: You can create parameter files in JSON format and pass them to your code when you invoke or update your actions.

4. Not Ideal but Workaround: As a less direct option, you can set the common parameters in an external configuration service or a database, and have your actions retrieve and use them as needed. 

Remember that Cloud Functions is based on Apache OpenWhisk. Some of the above methods are suggested in the OpenWhisk docs under the "Parameter Passing" section.
<|end|>


