&lt;p&gt;Dave is engaged in his academic tasks on the balcony, specifically crafting a presentation on the topic of Pythagorean triangles. He has just fashioned a triangle from cardboard with side lengths of 30cm, 40cm, and 50cm, when an unexpected gust of wind propels the triangle into the garden.&lt;br /&gt;
Subsequently, another gust transports a tiny ant directly onto this triangle. The disoriented ant, in an attempt to return to the grass, commences crawling in a random direction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Assuming that all potential positions of the ant within the triangle and all possible trajectories of movement are equally likely, can you determine the probability that the ant departs the triangle along its longest side?&lt;br /&gt;
Please provide your answer with a precision of 10 decimal places.&lt;/p&gt;
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The ant can depart the triangle from any of its three sides. But if we consider a perpendicular dropped from the opposite vertex on the base (the longest side) forming two right triangles, we can derive the answer from here.
Let's denote the shortest side as a, the middle side as b, and the longest side as c.
Now, if the ant starts from either of the two smaller triangles, it will depart through a or b. It will depart through c only if it originally resides in the larger triangle. The probability, therefore, is the ratio of the area of the largest right triangle to the total area of the triangle. The area of a right triangle = 0.5*a*b which divides the original triangle into two equal parts. Therefore, the required probability is 0.5.

The Python code for this can be:

```python
prob = 0.5
print(format(prob, '.10f'))
```

This will print the probability to 10 decimal places.
<|end|>


