I would like to authenticate using the OAuth 2.0 Authorization Flow in an application context. It is my understanding that it is not possible to use the http_request_post plugin to send a request to the OAuth URI oauth2/token to retrieve a bearer token, for the following reason: The Content-Type attribute in the Content parameter for the plugin is hardcoded as "application/json" while the OAuth endpoint is expecting it to be "application/x-www-form-urlencoded". [Background](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/security/defender-endpoint/exposed-apis-create-app-webapp?view=o365-worldwide#get-an-access-token)
Is my understanding correct and is there a workaround?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, your understanding is correct. OAuth 2.0 specification indicates that clients must send form-urlencoded parameters when making token request to OAuth server. So if your plugin only supports "application/json" and doesn't allow you to change Content-Type to "application/x-www-form-urlencoded", you wouldn't be able to use it to get a bearer token.

However, there are various workarounds you could try:

1. Use an HTTP client library: There are many libraries out there (like Axios in JavaScript, HttpClient in .NET, etc.) that allow you to send HTTP requests with any Content-Type.

2. Update the plugin's source code: If you have access to the plugin source and it's open-source, you could potentially modify it to support "application/x-www-form-urlencoded" Content-Type.

3. Use a different plugin or API: If the plugin you're using doesn't support this functionality, there might be others that do. For example, depending on the platform and language, you could consider using libraries such as OkHttp in Java, Requests in Python, or Guzzle in PHP.

Remember, regardless of the method you opt for, the client secret should always be kept securely, and not exposed to the end user.
<|end|>


