Write a JavaScript code segment that creates a multiple option survey question. Each option should be represented by a radio button followed by a label explaining the option. This should include at least five different options with a range of responses such as "Strongly Disagree", "Disagree", "Neutral", "Agree", "Strongly Agree". Once an option is selected, the color of a descriptive text paragraph underneath the question should change accordingly. The color change should be such that it reflects the sentiment of the selected option. Furthermore, design an elegant way of presenting error messages when the user tries to proceed without selecting an option. 

Also, implement a way to store and display the results of multiple users' responses. Write a function that calculates the percentage of each response category and updates every time a new response is entered. The results should be displayed dynamically on the webpage and be persistant across multiple sessions. 

Consider using Local Storage in JavaScript to implement data persistence.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To solve this problem we can use JavaScript, HTML and CSS. Local storage is a way to persist data across multiple user sessions.

Here is the JavaScript code, which uses jQuery for simplicity. It creates a radio button list representing a survey question, and the color of the paragraph changes according to the chosen option. If a user tries to go ahead without selecting an option, an alert is shown. The results of the survey are stored in local storage, and a function calculates and shows the percentage of each option.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<style>
  .red { color: red; }
  .yellow { color: yellow; }
  .green { color: green; }
</style>
  <script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.5.1.min.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
  <form id="surveyForm">
    <input type="radio" name="survey" value="Strongly Disagree"> Strongly Disagree<br>
    <input type="radio" name="survey" value="Disagree"> Disagree<br>
    <input type="radio" name="survey" value="Neutral"> Neutral<br>
    <input type="radio" name="survey" value="Agree"> Agree<br>
    <input type="radio" name="survey" value="Strongly Agree"> Strongly Agree<br>
    <button type="submit">Submit</button>
  </form>
  <h2>Survey Results</h2>
  <div id="results"></div>
  <script>
    let results = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('results')) || { 'Strongly Disagree': 0, 'Disagree': 0, 'Neutral': 0, 'Agree': 0, 'Strongly Agree': 0 };
    updateResults();
    $('#surveyForm').on('submit', function(e) {
      e.preventDefault();
      let selectedOption = $('input[name="survey"]:checked').val();
      if (!selectedOption) {
        alert('You must select an option to proceed.');
        return;
      }
      results[selectedOption]++;
      localStorage.setItem('results', JSON.stringify(results));
      updateResults();
      alert('Your response has been recorded!');
    });
    function updateResults() {
      $('#results').empty();
      let total = Object.values(results).reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
      for (let option in results) {
        let percentage = total !== 0 ? (results[option] / total * 100).toFixed(2) : 0;
        $('#results').append(`<p>${option}: ${results[option]} (${percentage}%)</p>`);
      }
    }
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This is a basic implementation. For a production environment, we would need to improve error handling and data validation, and probably use a more robust way of storing data, such as a server-side database rather than local storage. Also consider accessibility issues, like providing text alternatives for color changes.
<|end|>


