import itertools as it

def is_prime(n):
    if n == 1 or n == 0:
        return False
    else:
        for i in range(2, int(n**0.5) + 1):
            if n % i == 0:
                return False
        return True

def has_odd_digits(n):
    num_digits = len(str(n))
    return num_digits % 2 != 0

def prime_sum(n):
    sum_val = 0
    lower_limit = 10 ** (n - 1)
    upper_limit = 10 ** n
    for num in range(lower_limit, upper_limit):
        if is_prime(num):
            digits_sum = sum(map(int, str(num)))
            if is_prime(digits_sum) and (digits_sum % 5 != 0) and has_odd_digits(num):
                sum_val += num
    return sum_val

print(prime_sum(2))
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's the C# version of your Python code. Please note that in C# the index-based loops and conditions for mathematics operations are much more specific and verbose than in Python.

```csharp
using System;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(PrimeSum(2));
    }

    public static bool IsPrime(int n)
    {
        if (n == 1 || n == 0)
        {
            return false;
        }
        else
        {
            for (int i = 2; i <= Math.Sqrt(n); i++)
            {
                if (n % i == 0)
                {
                    return false;
                }
            }
            return true;
        }
    }

    public static bool HasOddDigits(int n)
    {
        int numDigits = n.ToString().Length;
        return numDigits % 2 != 0;
    }

    public static int PrimeSum(int n)
    {
        int sumVal = 0;
        int lowerLimit = (int)Math.Pow(10, n - 1);
        int upperLimit = (int)Math.Pow(10, n);

        for (int num = lowerLimit; num < upperLimit; num++)
        {
            if (IsPrime(num))
            {
                int digitsSum = SumDigits(num);
                if (IsPrime(digitsSum) && (digitsSum % 5 != 0) && HasOddDigits(num))
                {
                    sumVal += num;
                }
            }
        }
        return sumVal;
    }

    public static int SumDigits(int number)
    {
        int sum = 0;
        while (number != 0)
        {
            sum += number % 10;
            number /= 10;
        }
        return sum;
    }
}
```

This program includes a `Program` class with a `Main` method to run your `PrimeSum` method with argument `2`. In C#, each class that defines a method needs to have a static keyword if it belongs to the class rather than an instance of the class. The `IsPrime`, `HasOddDigits`, `PrimeSum`, and `SumDigits` helper functions are also included.
You can run this code in your terminal or command line using the `csc` compiler tool that comes with the .NET framework.
