Finalize the below faulty code block to return an array of prime numbers less than the provided integer `n` without using any built-in math library.
Note: A prime number is a natural number greater than 1 that has no positive divisors other than 1 and itself.
```javascript
function primesLessThan(n) {
    let primes = [];
    for(let i = 2; i < n; i++){
        let j = 2;
        while(j < i){
            if(i % j === 0)
                break;
            j++;
        }
        if(j == i)
            primes.push(i);
    }
    return primes;
}
console.log(primesLessThan(10));   /* Expected output [2, 3, 5, 7] */
console.log(primesLessThan(20));   /* Expected output [2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19] */
console.log(primesLessThan(2));    /* Expected output [] */
```
Something in the code isn’t letting it return the correct values. Identify and fix the error.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The error in the given code block is that the loop is not breaking correctly when a number is not prime. It should stop the current loop and continue the outer loop instead of just breaking one iteration. This can be done by using a flag variable. We would use this flag to see if any factors were found or not.

Here is the repaired JavaScript code:

```javascript
function primesLessThan(n) {
    let primes = [];
    for(let i = 2; i < n; i++){
        let isPrime = true;
        for(let j = 2; j * j <= i; j++){
            if(i % j === 0){
                isPrime = false;
                break;
            }
        }
        if(isPrime) {
            primes.push(i);
        }
    }
    return primes;
}
console.log(primesLessThan(10));   /* Expected output [2, 3, 5, 7] */
console.log(primesLessThan(20));   /* Expected output [2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19] */
console.log(primesLessThan(2));    /* Expected output [] */
```

We are using a separate variable "isPrime" to keep track of whether a number is prime or not. When we find that a number is divisible, we set the variable "isPrime" to false and break the loop. If after the loop the "isPrime" variable is still true, we add the number to the list of prime numbers. 

Also, the inner loop condition is optimized to `j*j <= i` instead of `j < i` because a larger factor of a number n would be a multiple of smaller factor that has already been checked. So it won't go beyond the square root of n in a number field.
<|end|>


