Fragment and reverse a designated colloquial expression into an assortment of individual words and characters. Additionally, detect any possible palindromes within the given assortment of words based on their reverse order. Implement this functionality using the C++ programming language.
Expression: Today is a sunny day.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a C++ code solution to solve the problem.

The code will use vectors and strings as well as the built-in library functions such as find_first_of, find_last_of, substr, reverse and erase.
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

using namespace std;

vector<string> splitAndReverse(string str)
{
    vector<string> words;
    size_t     start = 0, end = 0;

    while (end != string::npos)
    {
        end = str.find_first_of(" .", start);

        string word = str.substr(start, end - start);

        // Skip empty words
        if (!word.empty())
        {
            reverse(word.begin(), word.end());
            words.push_back(word);
        }

        start = end != string::npos ? end + 1 : string::npos;
    }
  
    return words;
}

bool isPalindrome(string word)
{
    string origWord = word;

    reverse(word.begin(), word.end());

    return word == origWord;
}

int main()
{
    string str = "Today is a sunny day.";
    vector<string> words = splitAndReverse(str);

    cout << "Reversed words: ";
    for (string &word : words)
        cout << word << " ";

    cout << "\n\nPalindromes: ";
    for (string &word : words)
        if (isPalindrome(word))
            cout << word << " ";

    return 0;
}
```
This solution first splits the sentence into words and then reverses each word. In the second part of the solution, it determines if any of the words are palindromes.

As the provided sentence doesn’t contain any palindromes, the output of the program would simply be the reversed words:
```
Reversed words: yadoT si a ynnus .yad
```

Adjust the input string as needed to test with different sentences/expressions.
