<|asst|>
Sure, here's the translated code in Rust:

```rust
fn round_away_from_zero(value: &str) -> Result<f64, &'static str> {
    // Parse the string input to a floating-point number
    let float_value = value.parse::<f64>();

    // Matching on the result of the parse operation
    match float_value {
        // If the parse is successful
        Ok(float) => {
            // Check if the parsed number is NaN
            if float.is_nan() {
                Err("Invalid number")
            }
            else {
                // If the number is positive, use the ceiling function
                if float > 0.0 {
                    Ok(float.ceil())
                } 
                // If the number is negative or zero, use the floor function
                else {
                    Ok(float.floor())
                }
            }
        },
        // If the parse is not successful, return an error
        Err(_) => Err("Invalid number"),
    }
}
```

Please note that the function `round_away_from_zero` now has a return type of `Result<f64, &'static str>`. This means that it will return either a `f64` number on success or a static string slice (`&'static str`) containing an error message on failure. Unlike JavaScript, Rust does not throw exceptions, but rather encourages the use of the `Result` type for error handling. The `Result` enum has two variants: `Ok(T)`, which indicates success and includes a value of type `T`, and `Err(E)`, which signals failure and includes a value of type `E`.
<|end|>


