Write CSS code to style a list of items with color green, font-size 14px, and font-weight bold. Additionally, the list's background should become red when hovered, and the list items should gradually transition to this state over 0.5s.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a CSS code snippet that achieves exactly what you've described:

```css
ul {
    color: green;
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: bold;
    transition: background-color 0.5s ease;
}

ul:hover {
    background-color: red;
}

ul li {
    transition: background-color 0.5s ease;
}

ul li:hover {
    background-color: red;
}
```

The `ul` selector targets all unordered list elements in your HTML document, while the `ul li` selector focuses on the individual list items. The `:hover` pseudo-class activates styling when your mouse is over the targeted element.

The `transition` property achieves the gradual shift to the hover state. The value `background-color 0.5s ease` means that the 'background-color' property will transition over half a second (0.5s) following an 'ease' timing function, which starts slowly, speeds up in the middle, and then slows again towards the end of the transition period. 

It's important to note that the chosen colors (green text on a red background) might pose accessibility issues, as not all color combos provide enough contrast for people with color blindness or other vision disabilities. Always utilize tools that can help you ensure your web pages are accessible to everyone.
