Develop a JS function that not only makes an API request, but also incorporates error handling and parsing of the received data.

Your function should meet the following requirements:
- Make an API request to a given URL (use https://example.com/api for testing).
- Utilize Promises with async/await for handling asynchronous operations.
- The function should incorporate error handling for the case when the request fails (e.g., network issues or the server is down). If such a case happens, log an appropriate error message.
- On a successful request, assume the server returns a JSON response. Your function needs to parse this JSON data and log the parsed data.

Here, using Promises and async/await not only increases the difficulty level, but they are also more aligned with how real-world applications work. Single API call can be extended to chained API calls where output of one API is used as input for the next API call. Asynchronous handling ensures smooth user experience and error handling is always of utmost importance in production-grade code. Web developers should be familiar handling JSON data as it is frequently used data format.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, here is the JavaScript code using the axios library that meets all the requirements:

```javascript
const axios = require('axios');

async function fetchAPI() {
    try {
        let response = await axios.get('https://example.com/api');
        
        let data = response.data;

        // Perform JSON parsing and log the parsed data.
        let parsedData = JSON.parse(data);
        console.log(parsedData);

    } catch (error) {
        // Log the error message in case of API request failure.
        console.error('An error occured', error);
    }
}

fetchAPI();
```

In this function, I am using axios library to perform a GET request. This library automatically takes care of transforming JSON data.

Make sure to install axios using npm (`npm install axios`) if not installed already.

`fetchAPI` is an asynchronous function (denoted by `async`) that allows us to use the `await` keyword inside it. “await” makes JavaScript wait until the promise returns a result.

In case of any error (e.g., the API server is down), the catch block gets executed and the error message is printed with `console.error`.

When we receive a successful response, JSON parsing occurs, and the parsed data is logged to the console.
