Starting from the apex of the pyramid depicted below and progressing to proximate digits on the subsequent row, the maximal cumulative total from the apex to the base is 23.
37 4
2 4 6
8 5 9 3
This is calculated as, 3 + 7 + 4 + 9 = 23.
Determine the maximal cumulative total from the apex to the base of the pyramid below:
75
95 64
17 47 82
18 35 87 10
20 04 82 47 65
19 01 23 75 03 34
88 02 77 73 07 63 67
99 65 04 28 06 16 70 92
41 41 26 56 83 40 80 70 33
41 48 72 33 47 32 37 16 94 29
53 71 44 65 25 43 91 52 97 51 14
70 11 33 28 77 73 17 78 39 68 17 57
91 71 52 38 17 14 91 43 58 50 27 29 48
63 66 04 68 89 53 67 30 73 16 69 87 40 31
04 62 98 27 23 09 70 98 73 93 38 53 60 04 23
Take note: With only 16384 possible paths, this problem could be solved by testing each path. However, Problem 67, which presents the same challenge but with a pyramid of one-hundred rows, cannot be solved by brute force and necessitates an ingenious approach! ;o)
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This problem can be solved by utilizing the bottom-up dynamic programming approach. We will start from the second last row from the bottom and then keep updating the triangle cells as per the formula: triangle[i][j] = max( triangle[i+1][j], triangle[i+1][j+1] ) + triangle[i][j]

The intuition behind this formula is that for each cell, we choose the adjacent cell having the maximum value and add the current cell's value to it. Essentially, we are "pulling up" the maximum possible value from the bottom to the top of the triangle. Finally, the apex of the triangle will have our required answer. 

Python Code Snippet:

```python
triangle = [
    [75],
    [95, 64],
    [17, 47, 82],
    [18, 35, 87, 10],
    [20, 4, 82, 47, 65],
    [19, 1, 23, 75, 3, 34],
    [88, 2, 77, 73, 7, 63, 67],
    [99, 65, 4, 28, 6, 16, 70, 92],
    [41, 41, 26, 56, 83, 40, 80, 70, 33],
    [41, 48, 72, 33, 47, 32, 37, 16, 94, 29],
    [53, 71, 44, 65, 25, 43, 91, 52, 97, 51, 14],
    [70, 11, 33, 28, 77, 73, 17, 78, 39, 68, 17, 57],
    [91, 71, 52, 38, 17, 14, 91, 43, 58, 50, 27, 29, 48],
    [63, 66, 4, 68, 89, 53, 67, 30, 73, 16, 69, 87, 40, 31],
    [4, 62, 98, 27, 23, 9, 70, 98, 73, 93, 38, 53, 60, 4, 23]
]

for i in range(len(triangle)-2, -1, -1):
    for j in range(len(triangle[i])):
        triangle[i][j] += max( triangle[i+1][j], triangle[i+1][j+1] )

print(triangle[0][0])
```

In the end, the apex of the triangle (i.e., `triangle[0][0]`) will hold the maximal cumulative total, which is the required answer. Running the above script provides the output as 1074, which is the maximal cumulative total from the apex to the base of the given pyramid.
