I have an angular app from which I want to send traces along with tags while making api calls to spring boot backend is this possible?

const span = opentracing.globalTracer().startSpan("operation");
    span.setTag("tag","tagname") // this tagname I need to extract in spring boot backend when made get/post call
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, it is possible.

When you make a request from your Angular app, you will need to serialize your entire tracing span (with tags) and send it as a request header to your Spring Boot backend.

In the target Spring boot application, you can extract the information from the headers. OpenTracing API provides methods to inject and extract the entire span into and from the headers. 

Here is a general way of how you can do it:

In your Angular application, inject your entire tracing span into request headers:

```javascript
const tracer = opentracing.globalTracer();
const span = tracer.startSpan("operation");
span.setTag("tag", "tagname")

...
// inject span context into headers
const carrier = {};
tracer.inject(span.context(), opentracing.FORMAT_HTTP_HEADERS, carrier);

// then in your request, include those headers:
http.post(url, body, {headers: carrier})
...
span.finish();
```

On your Spring Boot side, you will need an Interceptor class or a Filter that extract the tracing information from the request headers.

```java
public class TracingInterceptor implements HandlerInterceptor {

   private final io.opentracing.Tracer tracer;

   public TracingInterceptor(io.opentracing.Tracer tracer) {
       this.tracer = tracer;
   }

   @Override
   public boolean preHandle(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response, Object handler) {
       SpanContext parentSpanCtx = tracer.extract(Format.Builtin.HTTP_HEADERS,
                new HttpHeadersCarrier(request));
       ...
   }
}
```

Also don't forget to register this interceptor.

This general way can be implemented using different libraries like Jaeger or Zipkin etc. Both have slightly different APIs. And both provides integration with spring boot via their starters. 

Remember when working with distributed tracing, every microservice in your calling chain, should either propagate tracing context or start a new one and report to the same central location.

Also, remember that this is for distributed tracing if your use case only involves logging, there will be simpler solutions.

Please review the official OpenTracing API for Java and JavaScript for more detailed and precise methods to handle this.
<|end|>


