Create a Rails controller to generate a chart displaying the amount spent on each category in the past year.
[
  { 'category': 'food', 'amountSpent': 200 },
  { 'category': 'travel', 'amountSpent': 800 },
  { 'category': 'entertainment', 'amountSpent': 400 }
]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a simple example of how to create a Rails controller for chart generating purposes. Assume the model name is 'Expense'.

```ruby
class ChartsController < ApplicationController
  def get_data
    # Assuming there is a date column in the Expense model named 'date'
    start_time = 1.year.ago

    # Group by category and sum all expenses in each category within the past year
    expenses = Expense
      .where("date > :start_time", start_time: start_time)
      .group(:category)
      .sum(:amount_spent)

    # Transform the Hash into an Array of Hashes
    chart_data = expenses.map do |category, sum|
      { 'category' => category, 'amountSpent' => sum }
    end

    render json: chart_data
  end
end
```

Next step, you need to create route for the `ChartsController` and `get_data` action. Add this line to your `config/routes.rb`.

```ruby
resources :charts, only: [] do
  get 'get_data', on: :collection
end
```

Afterwards, you can access this data from `charts/get_data` URL.

Remember that this example only generates the JSON data for the chart. Displaying the chart itself (JSON parsing, setting up the chart library etc.) needs to be done on the front-end side, using JavaScript and a library like Chart.js.

Don't forget to adjust the model and attribute names to suit your application.
