<|end|>
<|asst|>
The statement in the FRTB's documentation does seem confusing but here is the general process of how it works:

1. A bank must demonstrate that its model is sound, accurate, and reliable based on P&L attribution test and backtesting. These tests involve the use of risk factors in the model.

2. Once the bank's model passes the above, the bank gets the approval to use the internal model (IMA) for the purpose of determining its minimum capital requirements.

3. After the approval, all risk factors used in the model are then reviewed for their eligibility in the internal expected shortfall (ES) model. Not all risk factors from the approved IMA would necessarily be eligible for the ES model, because the latter has more stringent requirements. Risk factors that are considered illiquid or hard-to-model will not be allowed in the ES model and the capital requirement for these risk factors will be calculated using the standardized approach (SA).

Therefore, risk factor eligibility does not refer to whether or not the risk factor can be used in the internal model per se, but if it is eligible for use in the internal ES model for regulatory capital requirements.
<|end|>


