<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a simple SQL query that would satisfy the conditions:

```
SELECT DISTINCT *
FROM (SELECT *
      FROM products
      LIMIT 5) AS subquery
```

This query will select the distinct rows from the initial five rows of the "products" table. Please note that the DISTINCT keyword operates on the entire row, so if you have 2 rows with exactly the same data in all columns, one will be eliminated.

Remember that the * character selects all columns. If you want to fetch the distinct values from a specific column, you can replace * with your column name. Also, some databases might not support the use of LIMIT in a subquery. It’s always a good practice to test out your queries first.

SQL has different flavors, the query might differ a little depending upon the database you are using. The above query is drafted as per standard SQL.
