In the language of C++, formulate a code that organizes a two-dimensional matrix of string objects in alphabetical sequence. Your code should also handle potential special characters and numerals within the strings, considering their ASCII values.
Be aware that the dimension of the matrix, inputs and their size can change and ensure your code can accommodate these changes efficiently.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can use the std::sort function to sort the two-dimensional matrix in lexicographical (i.e., dictionary/alphabetical) order. Here is an example solution:

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <iostream>

// Function to print the matrix
void printMatrix(const std::vector<std::vector<std::string>>& matrix) {
    for (const auto& rows : matrix) {
        for (const auto& elem : rows) {
            std::cout << elem <<  " ";
        }
        std::cout << std::endl;
    }
}

// Function to sort the matrix
void sortMatrix(std::vector<std::vector<std::string>>& matrix) {
    for (auto& rows : matrix) {
        std::sort(rows.begin(), rows.end());
    }
    std::sort(matrix.begin(), matrix.end());
}

int main() {
    std::vector<std::vector<std::string>> matrix = {{"beta", "alpha"}, {"delta", "gamma"}, {"epsilon","zeta"}};

    std::cout << "Matrix before sorting: " << std::endl;
    printMatrix(matrix);

    sortMatrix(matrix);

    std::cout << "Matrix after sorting: " << std::endl;
    printMatrix(matrix);

    return 0;
}
```

In this code, the `sortMatrix` function sorts each row and then sorts the rows. Note that lexicographical order means that special characters and numerals might come before or after letters depending on their ASCII values. Here is a simplified ASCII reference:
- Special characters: `!` to `/` (ASCII 33-47)
- Numbers: `0` to `9` (ASCII 48-57)
- Uppercase letters: `A` to `Z` (ASCII 65-90)
- Lowercase letters: `a` to `z` (ASCII 97-122)

So, for example, '4' comes before 'A', and 'A' comes before 'a'. However, please note that this code assumes all strings in the matrix are of the same length. If they are of different length, you might need to modify the comparison operator.
<|end|>


