Create a JavaScript program that generates and displays prime numbers up to a given limit. Besides that, implement a feature to display only odd or even primes based on user input. Calculate and display the time complexity of your created program. Ensure to create a UI interface where users can input the number limit and choose the type of primes to display. Remember to validate your inputs before processing.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's how you would go ahead with the problem.

Firstly, let's create a function to calculate prime numbers knowing the fact that a prime number can only be divided by 1 and itself:

```javascript
function generatePrimes(n) {
    var primes = [];
    for (var i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
        if (isPrime(i)) {
            primes.push(i);
        }
    }
    return primes;
}

function isPrime(n) {
    for (var i = 2; i * i <= n; i++) {
        if (n % i === 0) return false;
    }
    return n > 1;
}
```

Next, let's create two additional functions to filter even and odd primes:

```javascript
function filterEvenPrimes(primes) {
    return primes.filter(function(prime) {
        return prime % 2 === 0;
    });
}

function filterOddPrimes(primes) {
    return primes.filter(function(prime) {
        return prime % 2 !== 0;
    });
}
```

For the UI, you can use HTML and JavaScript.
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<body>

<h1>Prime Number Generator</h1>
<p>Click the button to display prime numbers up to the given limit:</p>
<label for="limit">Enter limit:</label>
<input type="number" id="limit" name="limit">
<br>
<input type="radio" id="even" name="primetype" value="even">
<label for="even">Even Primes</label><br>
<input type="radio" id="odd" name="primetype" value="odd">
<label for="odd">Odd Primes</label><br>

<button onclick="displayPrimes()">Display</button>

<p id="demo"></p>

<script>
... // paste the JavaScript functions here
function displayPrimes() {
  var limit = document.getElementById('limit').value;
  if (limit < 2) {
    alert("Please enter a number greater than 1");
    return;
  }
  var primes = generatePrimes(limit);
  if (document.getElementById('even').checked) {
    primes = filterEvenPrimes(primes);
  } else if (document.getElementById('odd').checked) {
    primes = filterOddPrimes(primes);
  }
  document.getElementById("demo").innerHTML = primes.join(", ");
}
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

As for time complexity,

- The `isPrime` function has a time complexity of O(sqrt(n)) because in the worst case, we're only iterating up to the square root of n.
- The `generatePrimes` function calls `isPrime` for each number up to n, so its complexity would be O(n sqrt(n)).
- Both `filterEvenPrimes` and `filterOddPrimes` functions have a time complexity of O(n) as they filter through the entire list of primes.
- So, the overall time complexity of the program would be O(n sqrt(n)).

This time complexity assumes that the number of primes is proportional to the input size n, which is a reasonable assumption for larger n.
