Develop a Java method which determines if a provided String input represents a palindromic sequence of characters. Your method should not only ignore case sensitivity and special characters, but must also be able to handle multi-byte characters, such as emoji or characters from non-Latin alphabets. 

Enhance the difficulty by experimenting with space and time complexity. Try to implement a version with a space complexity of O(1) and another one with time complexity of O(n), where n is the length of the string. 

Finally, provide a unit test suite which extensively tests your method against diverse scenarios, such as empty string, single characters, numeric characters, special characters and multi-byte characters. 

Note that a palindromic string is the one which reads same backwards as forwards, ignoring spaces, punctuation and letter casing.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is the Java solution for the above problem.

```java
import java.text.Normalizer;

public class PalindromeChecker {

    public static boolean isPalindrome(String str) {

        String normalizedStr = Normalizer
                               .normalize(str.toLowerCase(), Normalizer.Form.NFD)
                               .replaceAll("[\\p{InCombiningDiacriticalMarks}\\p{IsM}]", "")       // remove diacritics
                               .replaceAll("\\P{L}", "");       // remove everything except Unicode letters

        int left = 0;
        int right = normalizedStr.length() - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            if (normalizedStr.charAt(left) != normalizedStr.charAt(right)) {
                return false;
            }
            left++;
            right--;
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

Here are the JUnit tests to cover all the cases.

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

class PalindromeCheckerTest {

    @Test
    public void testIsPalindrome() {
        assertTrue(PalindromeChecker.isPalindrome(""));
        assertTrue(PalindromeChecker.isPalindrome("a"));
        assertTrue(PalindromeChecker.isPalindrome("A"));
        assertTrue(PalindromeChecker.isPalindrome("aA"));
        assertTrue(PalindromeChecker.isPalindrome("áA"));
        assertTrue(PalindromeChecker.isPalindrome("áéíóúAÉÍÓÚ"));
        assertTrue(PalindromeChecker.isPalindrome("a man a plan a canal panama"));
        assertTrue(PalindromeChecker.isPalindrome("amó lápiz azul, él ápoda má."));
        assertFalse(PalindromeChecker.isPalindrome("emoji😀"));
        assertTrue(PalindromeChecker.isPalindrome("нетetn"));
    }
}
```

Furthermore, we also need to observe that even though removing non-alphabetic symbols (using the replaceAll method) does not involve creating additional variables, some additional memory will be used internally by the JVM while performing this operation, so a truly O(1) space complexity might not be achievable depending on how strictly you define "space usage". 

This solution also ignores diacritics (accents, umlauts, etc.) and determines palindrome status based purely on the basic forms of the letters. If diacritic insensitivity is not desired, the Normalizer operation and first `replaceAll` call can be omitted. 

Also, while the overall time complexity is linear (O(n)), the specific constant factor will depend on the exact string content and could be larger if the string contains many complex Unicode characters due to overhead of their processing.
<|end|>


