I have recently read in a paper that the 25 day SMA and 36 day EWMA are equivalent for comparison in tests due to their half lives. The half life of the 25 day SMA is obviously 12.5 days and I understand the basic form of the equation to calculate the EWMA. I have been struggling trying to back out the half life from this form of the equation though. Would anyone be able to put me out of my misery and show me how to do it?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, here's how you can calculate it.

The half life of an Exponential-Weighted Moving Average (EWMA) can be calculated using a formula that takes into account the decay factor (λ) of the EWMA as follows:

Half Life = [log(0.5)] / [log(λ)]

This formula is derived from the fact that the EWMA uses a decay factor for weighting its values, which means that values become "half as relevant" after a certain number of periods which is the half-life.

The EWMA incorporates a decay factor which gives less weight to older observations. This decay factor, lambda (λ), can be calculated from a given half-life (h) with the following formula:

λ = exp(log(0.5)/h)

To know how the 25-day SMA and the 36-day EWMA are equivalent through their half lives, this implies the decay factor for the 36-day EWMA should be approximately equal to 2/25 = 0.08. Substitute this into the formula to solve for half-life, you would get roughly 12.5 days.

Therefore, in your context, the 36-day EWMA decay factor is designed to provide the equivalent half life as the 25-day SMA.
<|end|>


