Based on the supplied Python code fragment, decipher the method to eradicate all recurring values from the array, whilst preserving the inherent sequence of components.
mylist = [3, 1, 2, 2, 7, 8, 1, 9, 3, 7, 8]
# TODO: Author Python code to eliminate repetitious items from mylist, sustaining the primary arrangement of components #
<|end|>
<|asst|>
We can use list comprehension and 'not in' clause to solve this problem.

Here is the Python code:

```python
mylist = [3, 1, 2, 2, 7, 8, 1, 9, 3, 7, 8]
mylist = [v for i, v in enumerate(mylist) if mylist.index(v) == i]
print(mylist)
```

Explanation: 
This code scans each component in the list (mylist) via the enumerate() function. If the current element's index is not the same as the first occurrence's index, it means that it's a repeative element and will not be added to the new list. The 'mylist.index(v)' function gives the index of the first occurrence of the value 'v'. Hence it makes sure only the first occurrence is preserved in the sequence and all repetitious items are eliminated. 

After running the code, mylist will be [3, 1, 2, 7, 8, 9], which preserves the order and removes the recurring values.

Note: This method is not very efficient for large lists, as it has quadratic time complexity. For larger lists, you might want to consider using an OrderedDict from the collections module, which keeps the items in order while eliminating duplicates. It is available from Python 2.7 onwards.

Here is the Python code in such case:

```python
from collections import OrderedDict
mylist = [3, 1, 2, 2, 7, 8, 1, 9, 3, 7, 8]
mylist = list(OrderedDict.fromkeys(mylist))
print(mylist)
```

Running this code would give you the same output [3, 1, 2, 7, 8, 9] and it has linear time complexity.
<|end|>


