The Mongo query to calculate the average of all fields may vary according to your document structure. Assuming we have a collection named 'scores' with fields like 'math', 'english', 'science' and 'history' in it, and you want to calculate the average of all these fields, here is how you can do it.

```javascript
db.scores.aggregate([
    {
        $project: {
            average: {
                $avg: ['$math', '$english', '$science', '$history']
            }
        }
    }
]);
```

In this query, the `$project` operator is used to reshape each document in the stream, i.e., add new fields or remove existing fields. It also allows you to create computed values and insert them in the new documents. The `$avg` operator returns the average of the specified values. It ignores non-numeric values.

Keep in mind, this will give the average for each individual document in your collection, and not the grand average of all documents. To calculate a grand average, you may need to add another stage to your pipeline with `$group` and `$avg` operators.
