Given an unsorted array of n integers, sort it using the quick sort algorithm. Implement a version of quick sort algorithm that has space complexity O(log n). [5, 4, 8, 7, 1, 3, 10, 9]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a python version of quick sort algorithm using in-place partition method which provides us a space complexity of O(log n):

```python
def quick_sort(arr, low, high):
    if low < high:
        # pi is partitioning index, arr[p] is now at right place
        pi = partition(arr,low,high)
        
        # Separately sort elements before and after partition
        quick_sort(arr, low, pi-1)
        quick_sort(arr, pi+1, high)
        
def partition(arr, low, high):
    # choose the rightmost element as pivot
    pivot = arr[high]
    
    # pointer for greater element
    i = low - 1
    
    # traverse through all elements
    # compare each element with pivot
    for j in range(low, high):
        if arr[j] <= pivot:
            # swap elements at i and j
            i += 1
            arr[i], arr[j] = arr[j], arr[i]
    
    # swap the pivot element with the greater element specified by i
    arr[i + 1], arr[high] = arr[high], arr[i + 1]
    
    # return the position from where partition is done
    return i + 1    
        
def main():
    data = [5, 4, 8, 7, 1, 3, 10, 9]
    size = len(data)
    quick_sort(data, 0, size - 1)
    print('Sorted array is: ')
    print(data)
        
if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
```

When you run this program, it'll output:
```
Sorted array is: 
[1, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10]
```

In this program, we didn't use any additional arrays except the input array itself, so the space complexity is O(log n) due to the recursive stack space.
<|end|>


