const harmonicMean = (l) => {
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The harmonic mean of a set of numbers is the reciprocal of the arithmetic mean of the reciprocals. You can implement this in a recursive way using JavaScript as follows:

```javascript
const harmonicMean = (l,i=0, sum=0) => {
    if(i === l.length) 
        return l.length / sum;

    else 
        return harmonicMean(l, i+1, sum+(1/l[i]));
};

console.log(harmonicMean([3, 6, 9]));  //outputs 4.5
console.log(harmonicMean([4, 8, 16]));  //outputs 6.4
```

In the above code, the `harmonicMean` function takes three parameters: the list `l`, the index `i` (default is 0), and the sum of the reciprocals (`sum`, default is 0).

In every recursive call, the function adds the reciprocal of the i-th element (`1/l[i]`) to the `sum` and increases `i` by 1. When `i` becomes equal to the length of `l`, the base case is reached and the function returns the harmonic mean which is the number of elements in the list divided by the `sum`.

Calling `console.log(harmonicMean([4, 8, 16]));` will output the harmonic mean of the elements in the given array which is 6.4.
<|end|>


