We are building a web app that shows users content which they rate on a 1-5 ratings scale. Users typically spend 5 to 10 seconds per item shown before rating.
We would like to display content that is more relevant to them. There seem to be plenty of implementations of Recommender Systems, however most of them use a batch-processing, whereas we would need something that can react after every user rating.
Are there any open-source real-time recommenders that we can use or adapt to our use-case? What's the easiest way to get started with one? Any kind of advice is more than welcome.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
There are indeed various open source recommender systems that you can use for real-time recommendations. Implementing it would require some level of technical knowledge. Here are some options:

1. **LightFM**: It's a Python library for hybrid recommender systems, meaning it uses both collaborative filtering (based on user-item interaction) and content-based features (item metadata like genre, summary etc.). You can learn more about it here: https://github.com/lyst/lightfm

2. **Lenskit**: A toolkit for experimenting with recommender algorithms. It provides support for user-user and item-item collaborative filtering, as well as hybrid methods and baseline algorithms. Here is their GitHub: https://github.com/lenskit/lenskit

3. **Surprise** is a Python scikit for building and analyzing recommender systems. It provides ready-to-use state-of-the-art recommender algorithms, and tools to evaluate, analyze and compare the performance of a recommender. Find out more at: https://surprise.readthedocs.io/en/latest/index.html

4. **Implicit**: This is a Fast Python Collaborative Filtering for Implicit Datasets. This project seeks to provide fast collaborative filtering models utilizing Cython, and the power of making use of implicit feedback datasets. Visit: https://github.com/benfred/implicit

Please note none of the above python libraries is a drag and drop kind of solution. You will have to deal with data science concepts, build, and train models and implement the logic conditionally to show the recommendation in real-time.

Finally, recommendation systems are a rich field in machine learning, and many of the state-of-the-art techniques are based on deep learning. There could be factors in real-time recommendations such as sequence of user actions. This suggests the use of recurrent neural network or transformers. If you're interested, using a deep learning library (like TensorFlow or PyTorch) might be the right avenue instead.
<|end|>


