Does anyone know a method to programmatically close the CD tray on Windows 2000 or higher?
Open CD tray exists, but I can't seem to make it close especially under W2k. 

I am especially looking for a method to do this from a batch file, if possible, but API calls would be OK.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The method I am familiar with involves API calls in a small C program. However, it can't directly be done from a batch file. Here's a small C program which does the job:

```c
#include <windows.h>
#include <winioctl.h>

int WINAPI WinMain(HINSTANCE hInstance, HINSTANCE hPrevInstance, PSTR lpCmdLine, INT nCmdShow)
{
    DWORD dummy;
    HANDLE hCD;

    hCD = CreateFile("\\\\.\\e:", GENERIC_READ, FILE_SHARE_WRITE, NULL, OPEN_EXISTING, 0, NULL);
    if (hCD == INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE) {
        return 1;
    }
    if (!DeviceIoControl(hCD, IOCTL_STORAGE_LOAD_MEDIA, NULL, 0, NULL, 0, &dummy, NULL)) {
        CloseHandle(hCD);
        return 1;
    }
    CloseHandle(hCD);
    return 0;
}
```

In this code, "\\\\.\\e:" specifies the CD drive with E: letter. If your CD drive letter is different, you'll have to modify it. And remember to link with `kernel32.lib`.

Then, you can call the executable from your batch file:

```batch
@echo off
start /wait closecd.exe
```

This will start the closecd.exe program, which sends a command to the OS to close the CD tray, and the batch file will wait until closecd.exe finishes before it continues.
  
Here is another way to do it in VBScript:

Create a new Text Document, then rename it with a `.vbs` extension. For example `CloseCD.vbs`.

Then paste this code into the file and save it:

```vbs
Set oWMP = CreateObject("WMPlayer.OCX.7" ) 
Set colCDROMs = oWMP.cdromCollection 
if colCDROMs.Count >= 1 then 
For i = 0 to colCDROMs.Count - 1 
colCDROMs.Item(i).Eject 
Next ' cdrom 
End If
```

This will close the CD tray, you can call it from your batch file as you would a regular executable:

```batch
@echo off
cscript CloseCD.vbs
```
<|end|>


