If we look at the yield spreads on Bloomberg of some TIPS bonds, we see they have credit spread < 0 (i.e. their yield is even lower than their benchmark treasury bonds)
Why is that the case. Shouldn't treasury bonds typically have the lowest yield? How does TIPS work to have lower yield than treasury?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
TIPS, which stands for Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities, have a key distinguishing feature from typical treasury bonds: they offer protection against inflation. The principal of a TIPS bond increases with inflation.

When the yield spreads on TIPS are negative compared to Treasury bonds, it indicates that an investor is willing to accept a lower yield in exchange for the inflation protection that TIPS offer. This is particularly the case when there's a high expectation of future inflation. Investors are willing to pay a premium (thereby accepting a lower yield), for the assurance that their investment will keep pace with inflation.

On the flip side, in a low inflation environment, regular Treasuries may offer more attractive yields than TIPS, as the inflation adjustment is less meaningful.

It is also noteworthy that TIPS and regular treasuries are both backed by the U.S. government, considered to be risk-free in terms of credit risk. Therefore, the observed spread difference is not due to differing credit qualities, but rather, differing inflation expectations.
<|end|>


