Write a script in JavaScript to perform a bank transaction involving multiple accounts and currencies. The script should also include error checking for insufficient funds and prevent potential double-spending. Advanced concepts such as concurrency control and transaction isolation should be implemented to handle multiple transactions happening at the same time.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The task requires an advanced knowledge of JavaScript, object-oriented programming, concurrency and transaction processing. Below is a simplified implementation of a banking system with multiple accounts and currencies handled. Be aware that real-world banking systems contain lots of additional features and safety measures.

Since JavaScript doesn't support multithreading or concurrency out of the box, we'd use promises to simulate the asynchronous nature of multiple transactions.

```javascript
class Account {
  constructor(id, balance, currency) {
    this.id = id;
    this.balance = balance;
    this.currency = currency;
    this.pendingTransactions = [];
  }

  debit(amount, currency) {
    if (this.currency !== currency) {
      throw new Error('Currency mismatch');
    }
  
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      setTimeout(() => {
        if (amount <= this.balance) {
          this.balance -= amount;
          this.pendingTransactions = this.pendingTransactions.filter(transaction => transaction !== amount);
          resolve(true);
        } else {
          this.pendingTransactions = this.pendingTransactions.filter(transaction => transaction !== amount);
          reject(new Error('Insufficient funds'));
        }
      }, 1000);
    });
  }

  credit(amount, currency) {
    if (this.currency !== currency) {
      throw new Error('Currency mismatch');
    }
  
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      setTimeout(() => {
        this.balance += amount;
        resolve(true);
      }, 1000);
    });
  }
}

class Bank {
  constructor() {
    this.accounts = []
  }

  addAccount(account) {
    this.accounts.push(account)
  }

  transferMoney(senderId, receiverId, amount, currency) {
    const senderAccount = this.accounts.find(account => account.id === senderId)
    const receiverAccount = this.accounts.find(account => account.id === receiverId)

    if (senderAccount.pendingTransactions.includes(amount)) {
      throw new Error('Double-spending attempt detected');
    }

    senderAccount.pendingTransactions.push(amount);

    return senderAccount.debit(amount, currency).then(() => {
      return receiverAccount.credit(amount, currency);
    });
  }
}

// Usage
const bank = new Bank()

const account1 = new Account(1, 1000, 'USD')
const account2 = new Account(2, 2000, 'USD')

bank.addAccount(account1)
bank.addAccount(account2)

bank.transferMoney(1, 2, 200, 'USD')
  .then(() => console.log('Transaction successful'))
  .catch(err => console.error(err.message))
```

This solution doesn't handle exchange rates between different currencies. For a proper implementation of currency exchanges, you'd need some sort of conversion strategy. The solution is a simplistic model, and not meant for production use. It assumes that the debiting process is always slower than the crediting process, which isn't always true in the real-world. Proper handling would involve atomic transactions.
<|end|>


