<|user|>
I want to install a gem on all my application servers, but gem install requires sudo access - how can I enable sudo only for running this capistrano command? 

In other words, I don't wish to use sudo for all my deployment recipes, just when I invoke this command on the command line.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You could use something like this in your Capistrano task:

```
task :install_my_gem do
  on roles(:app) do |host|
    execute "sudo gem install mygem"
  end
end
```

This way, you only use `sudo` for the gem installation and not for the rest of your recipes.

Remember, the user you use to deploy should have necessary permissions to run `sudo` commands. This can be set by editing `/etc/sudoers` file usually with `visudo` command by adding something like `deployuser  ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL` (replace `deployuser` with your user). Be aware this will give full `sudo` powers to the user i.e., he could execute any command as `sudo`.

If you want to restrict your deploy user to only run gem install as `sudo`, you would write something like this in `/etc/sudoers` file with `visudo` command:

```bash
deployuser  ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/gem install *
```

You should replace `deployuser` with your deployment username and `/usr/bin/gem` with the actual `gem` binary path which you can get by running `which gem` in your terminal.
<|end|>


