Increase the complexity of the given programming task.

Complete the program so that it filters out strings that start with a specific prefix in an input vector, in addition to the strings that end with another specific suffix. Make sure your function uses an efficient algorithm, preferably with a time complexity less than O(n^2). 

So, for example: 
>>> filter_by_prefix_suffix({}, "a", "c")
{}
>>> filter_by_prefix_suffix({"abc", "bcd", "cde", "vector"}, "a", "c")
{"abc"}
```C++
#include<stdio.h>
#include<vector>
#include<string>
using namespace std;
vector<string> filter_by_prefix_suffix(vector<string> strings, string prefix, string suffix){
```
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's how you can modify your function to achieve the desired task in C++. This function goes through all the strings in the input vector, checks if they starts with the prefix and ends with the suffix. To achieve this, std::starts_with and std::ends_with methods are used, which are implemented as of C++20.

```C++
#include<vector>
#include<string>
#include<algorithm>
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

vector<string> filter_by_prefix_suffix(vector<string> strings, string prefix, string suffix){
	vector<string> filtered_names;

    for (auto &str : strings) {
        if (str.starts_with(prefix) && str.ends_with(suffix)) {
            filtered_names.push_back(str);
        }
    }

	return filtered_names;
}

int main() {
    vector<string> strings = {"abc", "bcd", "cde", "vector"};
    string prefix = "a";
    string suffix = "c";
    vector<string> res = filter_by_prefix_suffix(strings, prefix, suffix);
    for(auto &s: res)
        cout << s << "\n";
    return 0;
}
```

Here, complexity of checking if string starts with prefix and ends with suffix is O(m+n) where m and n are lengths of prefix and suffix respectively. Since m and n are usually much smaller than the size of array, the overall complexity becomes O(N),  where N is the size of the input vector.
<|end|>


