The erroneous code was not completely posted, but based on the problem definition, I can provide a correct implementation in C++.

```C++
#include<iostream>
#include<string>
using namespace std;

bool has_suffix(string str, string suffix) {
  if(str.size() < suffix.size())
      return false;

  string str_suffix = str.substr(str.size() - suffix.size());

  return str_suffix == suffix;
}

int main() {
  cout<<has_suffix("activity","ity")<<endl;
  cout<<has_suffix("helloityity","ity")<<endl;
  cout<<has_suffix("heaven","ity")<<endl;

  return 0;
}
```

In this modified code, the `has_suffix` function first checks if the string's length is less than the suffix's length. If it is, then it automatically returns `false`. 

Then it extracts the substring from the string by starting from the position where the suffix string should start (which is calculated by subtracting the size of the suffix from the size of the string) till the end of the string. This gives the end substring of the string. It then compares this to suffix and if they are equal, it returns `true`, else returns `false`. This function checks if the string ends with the specified suffix string.

The `main` function demonstrates several test cases. It is expected to print:
```
1
1
0
```
The `1`s and `0` represent `true` and `false` respectively in C++. It implies that "activity" and "helloityity" end with "ity" while "heaven" does not end with "ity".
