Is there something like [Python's getattr()](http://effbot.org/zone/python-getattr.htm) in C#? I would like to create a window by reading a list which contains the names of controls to put on the window.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, in C#, you can use reflection to accomplish something similar. The class `System.Reflection.PropertyInfo` can fetch property details and then `GetValue` can be used to read a property value. Here's an example:

```csharp
System.Reflection.PropertyInfo pi = yourObject.GetType().GetProperty("propertyName");
object value = pi.GetValue(yourObject, null);
```

This is roughly the equivalent of Python's `getattr()` function.

Creating a window by reading a list of controls could be accomplished, but it is a more complicated task. If you have a dictionary that maps strings to control-creating functions, you could iterate over your list of control names and call the corresponding functions to create each control. Here's a skeletal example:

```csharp
var controlsMapping = new Dictionary<string, Func<Control>> {
    ["TextBox"] = () => new TextBox(),
    ["Button"] = () => new Button(),
    // Add more mapping here...
};

foreach (string controlName in controlsList) {
    if (controlsMapping.TryGetValue(controlName, out var controlCreator)) {
        Control ctl = controlCreator();
        window.Controls.Add(ctl);
    } else {
        // Handle case where controlName is not recognized
    }
}
```

Remember to replace "`window`" with your concrete window object. This approach requires an initial setup of the mapping, but gives a lot of flexibility. If you instead wanted to use reflection to look up a type from its name and instantiate it, this is possible too, but requires that your control types have parameterless constructors.
