When you send an email using C# and the System.Net.Mail namespace, you can set the "From" and "Sender" properties on the MailMessage object, but neither of these allows you to make the MAIL FROM and the from address that goes into the DATA section different from each other.  MAIL FROM gets set to the "From" property value, and if you set "Sender" it only adds another header field in the DATA section.  This results in "From X@Y.COM on behalf of A@B.COM", which is not what you want.  Am I missing something?

The use case is controlling the NDR destination for newsletters, etc., that are sent on behalf of someone else.

I am currently using [aspNetEmail](http://www.aspnetemail.com/) instead of System.Net.Mail, since it allows me to do this properly (like most other SMTP libraries).  With aspNetEmail, this is accomplished using the EmailMessage.ReversePath property.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
No, you're not missing anything. Unfortunately, the ability to set the MAIL FROM independently from the DATA FROM ('From' field in the email header) is not possible with the `System.Net.Mail` namespace in C#. 

The `MailMessage.Sender` property you mentioned does not change the 'MAIL FROM' value in the SMTP conversation as you've noticed, it merely adds a 'Sender' field to the email header which is not what you want.

There is no built-in way in .NET's `System.Net.Mail` to control envelope information independently from header information. For this reason, many developers opt to use third-party libraries, like aspNetEmail or others, which provide fuller SMTP control.

Another option could be to send the emails via raw SMTP commands using `TcpClient` or `Sockets` but it's not advised due to the complexity, low-level, and error-prone approach for such a task.

Those who have control over the SMTP server sometimes set up a 'return path' or 'bounce address' on the server side so any NDRs are sent there regardless of the 'MAIL FROM' value but these options are not always available. 

Alternatively, you could use a more comprehensive, third-party email library which supports this feature, much like aspNetEmail. These libraries typically offer more advanced SMTP features not present in `System.Net.Mail`. For example, MailKit and MimeKit libraries offer various advanced features for controlling emails sending behaviour like what you want, DLL to work with return path (bounce address), or MAIL FROM in SMTP.
<|end|>


