Here is your Go code translated into C#:

```csharp
using System;

public class Program
{
    public static (bool, int) MoveAndCountShifts(int[] arr)
    {
        int count = 0, shift = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.Length-1; i++)
        {
            if (arr[i] > arr[i+1])
            {
                count++;
                shift = i + 1;
            }
        }
        if (count > 1)
        {
            return (false, 0);
        }
        else if (count == 0)
        {
            return (true, 0);
        }
        else
        {
            return (true, shift);
        }
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        var arr = new int[]{3, 4, 5, 1, 2};
        var (result, ops) = MoveAndCountShifts(arr);
        Console.WriteLine($"({result}, {ops})");
        arr = new int[]{3, 5, 4, 1, 2};
        (result, ops) = MoveAndCountShifts(arr);
        Console.WriteLine($"({result}, {ops})");
    }
}
```

Compared to Go, C# has some differences:

- The use of `var` as a general-purpose variable declaration.
- The use of `new` for creating new instances of objects (in this case, arrays).
- Array members are accessed with a different syntax (`array[index]`).
- Tuple syntax for multiple return values. You can use a tuple to return two values from the `MoveAndCountShifts` function.
