Do any C++ GNU standalone classes exist which handle paths cross platform?  My applications build on Windows and LInux.  Our configuration files refer to another file in a seperate directory.  I'd like to be able to read the path for the other configuration file into a class which would work on both Linux or Windows.

Which class would offer the smallest footprint to translate paths to use on either system?  Thanks
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, the C++17 filesystem library that's part of the standard library is cross-platform and can be used to handle file paths on both Linux and Windows. It also provides utilities for performing operations on files and directories.

Here's an example:

```cpp
#include <filesystem>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::filesystem::path p{"C:\\Path\\To\\File.txt"};

    std::cout << p << '\n';                                   // Prints: "C:\Path\To\File.txt"
    std::cout << p.parent_path() << '\n';                     // Prints: "C:\Path\To"
    std::cout << p.filename() << '\n';                        // Prints: "File.txt"
    std::cout << p.stem() << '\n';                            // Prints: "File"
    std::cout << p.extension() << '\n';                       // Prints: ".txt"

    return 0;
}
```

In the above example, the program works with a given file path and prints out different parts of the path using the filesystem library's functions.

Using this library greatly simplifies file and directory operations in your code and saves you from having to write your own cross-platform file path handling functions.

If you are using a version of C++ older than C++17, you can use the boost::filesystem library which provides similar functionality.
<|end|>


