```python
import pandas as pd
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split
from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier
from sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score, classification_report
from sklearn.feature_selection import SelectKBest, chi2

# Load data
url = "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/akanz1/weather-data/main/weather.csv"
df = pd.read_csv(url)

# Inspecting data and preprocessing
print(df.head())
print(df.shape)
print(df.info())

# Handle missing data:
# Here, simply dropping missing values for demonstration
df = df.dropna()

# Convert categorical variables into dummy/indicator variables
df_dummies = pd.get_dummies(df)

# Feature Selection: Here we use univariate selection
bestfeatures = SelectKBest(score_func=chi2, k=10)
fit = bestfeatures.fit(df_dummies.iloc[:,:-3],df_dummies.iloc[:,-3:])
dfscores = pd.DataFrame(fit.scores_)
dfcolumns = pd.DataFrame(df_dummies.columns)
#concat two dataframes for better visualization 
featureScores = pd.concat([dfcolumns,dfscores],axis=1)
featureScores.columns = ['Specs','Score']  #naming the dataframe columns
print(featureScores.nlargest(10,'Score'))  #print 10 best features

#split dataset into training set and test set
X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(df_dummies.iloc[:,:-3], df_dummies.iloc[:,-3:], test_size=0.2, random_state=1) 

#Create a RandomForest Classifier
clf = RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=100)

#Train the model using the training sets y_pred=clf.predict(X_test)
clf.fit(X_train,y_train)

# prediction on test set
y_pred = clf.predict(X_test)

# Model Accuracy
print("Accuracy:", accuracy_score(y_test, y_pred))
``` 

This provides a general approach to handling a classification problem in machine learning. From handling missing data, to converting categorical variables, feature selection, splitting the dataset, model training and testing, to finally evaluating the model's performance. Each step is essential to ensure the trained model's efficient prediction. The code also demonstrates the common practice of printing or understanding the loaded dataset before performing any operations on it.
