I have a dataset with binary values. I want to find out the least frequent value in each row. This dataset has several million records. What would be the most efficient way to do it? Here is a sample of the dataset.
import pandas as pd
data = pd.read_csv('myData.csv', sep = ',')
data.head()
bit1    bit2    bit2    bit4    bit5    least_frequent    least_freq_count
0       0       0       1       1       0           1
1       1       1       0       0       1           2
1       0       1       1       1       1           1


I want to create least_frequent as well as least_freq_count columns like the sample above. These are not part of the original dataset and will be created after examining all rows.


A:
<code>
import pandas as pd

df = pd.DataFrame({'bit1': [0, 1, 1],
                   'bit2': [0, 1, 0],
                   'bit3': [1, 0, 1],
                   'bit4': [1, 0, 1],
                   'bit5': [0, 1, 1]})
</code>
df = ... # put solution in this variable
BEGIN SOLUTION
<code>
<|end|>
<|asst|>
df['least_frequent'] = df.apply(lambda row: 0 if row.sum()>len(row)/2 else 1, axis=1)
df['least_freq_count'] = df.apply(lambda row: len(row)-row.sum() if row.sum()>len(row)/2 else row.sum(), axis=1)
</code>
END SOLUTION
df

The solution works by first determining if the sum of all binary values in a row is greater than half of the row length, which would mean that ones are more frequent, so the least frequent value is 0. If this isn't the case, the least frequent value is 1. This result is put in the 'least_frequent' column.

For the 'least_freq_count', it counts the total number of least frequent values by subtracting the sum of the row from the total length, or returning the sum directly, depending on whether the least frequent value is 0 or 1 respectively.

This solution assumes that your dataset only contains binary values and no null values. Also, it doesn't handle possible ties, in which case you may want to add specific rules, such as prioritizing 0 or 1 when counts are equal.  You also might assume that sum of zeros and ones won't be equal cause it has a very low chance in large datasets.
