You can achieve this with jQuery easily. Here is a way to do it:

```html
<html>
<head>
    <script src="https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/jquery/3.5.1/jquery.min.js"></script>
    <style>
        .red-text { color: red; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
<div>Count of color change: <span id="counter">0</span></div>
<button id="btn">Click me</button>
<p>Hello, World!</p>
<p>Another paragraph</p>

<script>
$(document).ready(function() {
    var counter = 0;
    
    $("#btn").click(function() {
        $("p").addClass("red-text");
        counter++;
        $("#counter").text(counter);
    });
});
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

In the script tag, when the page loads $(document).ready() is executed. Inside this function, an event handler is assigned to the button to respond to click events. When the button is clicked, all paragraphs are assigned a class of "red-text" that changes the color of the text to red. 

In addition, we increment the counter by 1 for each click event and update the #counter element with the new value.

This all happens without using the jQuery css() function. The .addClass() function is used to change the color of the p tags.
