Any ideas how to display a PDF file in a WPF Windows Application? 


---



I am using the following code to run the browser but the `Browser.Navigate` method does not do anything!


WebBrowser browser = new WebBrowser();
browser.Navigate("http://www.google.com");
this.AddChild(browser); // this is the System.Windows.Window
<|end|>
<|asst|>
WPF's WebBrowser control is tricky. In order to work properly, it needs to be added to the visual tree of your control hierarchy in your app. Essentially, WPF needs to know where to draw the browser. 

In addition, you need to be aware that the WebBrowser will only navigate to the page once it has been loaded using its `.Load()` method. 

So, to have it work, you need to: 

1. Add the WebBrowser control to your XAML.
2. Wait until the control is loaded. 
3. Call the `.Navigate()` method.

Here's an example of how you can do it:

In your XAML file:

```xml
<Window x:Class="WpfApplication1.MainWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        Title="MainWindow" Height="350" Width="525">
    <Grid>
        <WebBrowser Name="browser" />
    </Grid>
</Window>
```

In the code-behind:

```csharp
public MainWindow()
{
    InitializeComponent();
    this.Loaded += MainWindow_Loaded;
}

private void MainWindow_Loaded(object sender, RoutedEventArgs e)
{
    browser.Navigate("http://www.google.com");
}
```

However, WebBrowser is not the best way to display a PDF file in a WPF application because it has some limitations. Some other options for displaying PDF files in a WPF application include:

1. Using a third-party .NET PDF library, like `PdfiumViewer`, `Spire.PDF` for WPF or `pdftron`.

2. Converting PDF pages to images and displaying them in an `Image` control. This might not be feasible for large PDF files, though, due to memory limitations.

3. Embedding the Adobe PDF Reader COM Component. This requires that the Adobe Reader is installed on the client machine, which is not always the case and the integration is also not very seamless.

Remember, each approach has its own pros and cons that need to be considered in relation to the specific needs of your application.
<|end|>


