2. Visual Studio: From Visual Studio 2017 and onward, you can download documentation for offline use directly from the IDE. Go to Help -> Add and Remove Help Content -> then select the technology stack you want to have on your USB key.

3. .NET API Browser: For the .NET API Browser version, there is also a project called ".NET API browser Offline version" that provides a convenient package of the documentation in html format that can be easily transferred to the USB key.

4. Commercial third-party software like Zeal or Dash provide an extensive documentation library - including MSDN - that can be downloaded for offline use.

Remember to check for updated offline documentation periodically, as Microsoft releases software updates on a regular basis. 

Note: This instructions assumes you have enough storage space on your USB drive and that your USB key is configured for hosting files i.e. properly formatted etc. Also be aware of any potential security risks involved with downloading and transferring data to a USB device.
