How do I save a jpg image to database and then load it in Delphi using FIBplus and TImage?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a general guide you can use to save a jpg image to the database and load it using FIBplus and TImage in Delphi.

Part 1: Save a jpg image to the database

1. First, you need to have a BLOB field in your table where you will save the image. 

2. Now create an instance of TJPEGImage and TStream objects.

```delphi
var
  jpg: TJPEGImage;
  imgStream: TMemoryStream;
begin
  jpg := TJPEGImage.Create;
  imgStream := TMemoryStream.Create;
```

3. Load the jpeg image into the jpg object and save it into the stream.

```delphi
try
  jpg.LoadFromFile('c:\my_image.jpg');  //Load an image from a jpg file
  jpg.SaveToStream(imgStream);  //Save the image to the stream
```

4. Write the stream into your BLOB field.

```delphi
  imgStream.Position := 0;  //Set stream position to 0
  YourQuery.ParamByName('BlobParam').LoadFromStream(imgStream, ftBlob); //Write the stream to the BLOB field
finally
  jpg.Free; //Free the jpeg and stream objects
  imgStream.Free;
end;
```

5. Finally, post the changes to the database.

```delphi
YourQuery.Post;
```

Part 2: Load a jpg image from the database

1. First, create an instance of TJPEGImage and TStream objects.

```delphi
var
  jpg: TJPEGImage;
  imgStream: TStream;
begin
  jpg := TJPEGImage.Create;
  imgStream := YourQuery.CreateBlobStream(YourQuery.FieldByName('BlobField'), bmRead);
```

2. Load the image from the stream into the jpeg object and load it into a TImage.

```delphi
try
  jpg.LoadFromStream(imgStream);   //Load the image from the stream
  Image1.Picture.Graphic := jpg;   //Load the jpeg image into a TImage
finally
  jpg.Free;    //Free the jpeg and stream objects
  imgStream.Free;
end;
```

Always remember to replace `'BlobParam'` and `YourQuery` with the actual name of your blob field and query respectively.  Similarly, replace `Image1` with the actual name of your `TImage` object you're using. Make sure to include the `JPEG` unit in your uses clause.

Also note that the method of saving images to a database as BLOB data is not always the best method since it can strain the database when the image files are large. An alternative way which is commonly used is to save the image file paths into the database and only load the image when it's needed in the application.
<|end|>


