Write a SQL statement to select the names, IDs, and order histories of customers from the 'customers' and 'orders' tables that live in California and have placed more than 10 orders in the past year.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Assuming the 'customers' table has fields 'customer_id', 'name', 'state', and the 'orders' table has fields 'customer_id', 'order_id', 'order_date'. Here is the SQL statement:

```sql
SELECT c.name, c.customer_id, o.order_id 
FROM customers c
JOIN orders o 
ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id
WHERE c.state = 'California' AND o.order_date > DATE_SUB(CURDATE(), INTERVAL 1 YEAR)
GROUP BY c.customer_id
HAVING COUNT(o.order_id) > 10;
```

This SQL statement first performs an inner join to combine rows from the 'customers' and 'orders' tables that have the same 'customer_id'. It then restricts the results to only customers who live in California and have placed orders within the past year. Finally, it groups the results by 'customer_id' and selects only the groups (i.e., customers) that have placed more than 10 orders.
<|end|>


