Develop an HTML document encompassing several hyperlinks, each directing to distinct search platforms (for instance, Google, Bing, Yahoo, etc.). Make sure all these hyperlinks launch in a fresh browser window upon interaction. Moreover, incorporate a tactic that randomizes the sequence of these hyperlinks with each reload of the webpage.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Creating an HTML document as requested involves knowledge in HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Here is a simple solution that outputs the hyperlinks:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Search Platforms</title>
</head>
<body>

<div id="platforms"></div>

<script>

var platforms = [
    {'name': 'Bing', 'url': 'https://www.bing.com/'},
    {'name': 'Google', 'url': 'https://www.google.com/'},
    {'name': 'Yahoo', 'url': 'https://www.yahoo.com/'},
    // Add more platforms here
]

// Function to shuffle array (Fisher-Yates Shuffle Algorithm)
function shuffleArray(array) {
    for(let i = array.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
        let j = Math.floor(Math.random() * (i + 1));
        let temp = array[i];
        array[i] = array[j];
        array[j] = temp;
    }
    return array;
}

platforms = shuffleArray(platforms); // Shuffles the array

var platformsDiv = document.getElementById('platforms');

// Iterate over platforms and create an `<a>` element for each one
for(let i = 0; i < platforms.length; i++) {
    var a = document.createElement('a');
    var linkText = document.createTextNode(platforms[i].name);
    a.appendChild(linkText);
    a.title = platforms[i].name;
    a.href = platforms[i].url;
    a.target = '_blank';    // Makes it open in a new tab
    platformsDiv.appendChild(a);

    // Add a line break after each link
    platformsDiv.appendChild(document.createElement('br'));
}

</script>

</body>
</html>
```

This code creates a simple HTML page that, when loaded, will randomly organize the search platform links. The script uses JavaScript to randomize the placement of the links. The links open in new tabs due to the “_blank” target in the anchor (‘<a>’) tag. Note that adding more search platforms is as easy as adding more objects in the `platforms` array, following the structure {'name': 'Platform Name', 'url': 'Platform URL'}.
