Elucidate the distinction between a superficially duplicated object, often referred to as a shallow copy, and an utterly replicated object, sometimes known as a deep copy. Along with your explanation, describe and code an approach to implementing both methods in Python and the specific situations where one might be more preferable than the other.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
A shallow copy refers to creating a new object and then copying the references from the original object to the new object. This means that changes to the original object will reflect in the shallow copied object, specifically when the object contains non-primitive sub-objects like lists or dictionaries, because only the memory addresses of those sub-objects were copied, not the actual values.

On the other hand, a deep copy refers to creating a new object and then recursively adding copies of the child entities found in the original. Any changes to the original object will not reflect in the deep copied object, because everything (original value and all the values of non-primitive sub-objects) was fully duplicated.

Here is how you can implement both in Python:

```python
import copy

# Original List
list1 = ['a','b',['c','d'],[1,2]]

# Creating Shallow Copy
list2 = copy.copy(list1)

# Creating Deep Copy
list3 = copy.deepcopy(list1)

# Changing the original list
list1[2][0] = 'x'
list1[3][0] = 7

print("Original List: ", list1)
print("Shallow Copy: ", list2)
print("Deep Copy: ", list3)
```

The output will be:
```
Original List:  ['a', 'b', ['x', 'd'], [7, 2]]
Shallow Copy:  ['a', 'b', ['x', 'd'], [7, 2]]
Deep Copy:  ['a', 'b', ['c', 'd'], [1, 2]]
```

As seen above, modification of the original list changed the shallow copy but not the deep copy.

When to use which:

Shallow copy can be used when you want to create a new object and any changes to the original object should reflect in the copied object. If your object does not contain any non-primitive sub-objects, using shallow copy is faster both performance and memory wise because only references are copied.

Deep copy should be used when the object contains non-primitive sub-objects and you want the copied object to not be affected by changes to the original object. Deep copy takes more memory as it duplicates everything.
<|end|>


