What is the the best way to write a form to submit some data in ASP.NET MVC? Is it as [Scott Guthrie](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scott_Guthrie) demonstrates here? Are there better approaches? Perhaps with less using of strings?

![alt text](https://i.stack.imgur.com/yehPg.png)
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The form depicted by Scott Guthrie is a solid way of setting up a form in ASP.NET MVC, especially if you're just starting. This technique uses Html Helpers to render the form and its elements, which eases the process of binding form values to model properties.

However, there is a more modern approach that uses less strings and diminished likelihood of human error by adopting "strongly typed" Html helpers. This approach also offers better support for IntelliSense and compile-time checking.

Here is an example of creating a form using strongly typed helpers:

```html
@using (Html.BeginForm("Create", "Home", FormMethod.Post))
{
    <div>
        @Html.LabelFor(model => model.Name)
        @Html.TextBoxFor(model => model.Name)
    </div>
    <div>
        @Html.LabelFor(model => model.Email)
        @Html.TextBoxFor(model => model.Email)
    </div>
    <input type="submit" value="Submit" />
}
```

The "strongly typed" helpers, such as TextBoxFor or LabelFor, take a Lambda expression which points to the property to bind from the Model. Thus, if you refactor the model, these expressions are updated simultaneously, which compiles and delivers error if you remove or rename the property in your model.

Moreover, these helpers will automatically produce validation error messages for your fields if the Model is annotated with DataAnnotations. 

On the other hand, always look into Tag Helpers if you're employing ASP.NET Core - they provide an even more HTML-centric way of creating your forms.
<|end|>


