Adapt this snippet to Javascript syntax:
def sort_by_binary_ones_zeros_desc(arr):
    arr.sort(key=lambda x: (bin(x).count('1'), bin(x).count('0'), -x))
    return arr
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In JavaScript, you can create a sorting function by using the `sort()` method. However, unlike Python, JavaScript does not have a simple way of assigning a sort key. Instead, you can pass a sorting function into the `sort()` method, which will compare elements in an array by your custom sorting rule. This function can return either a positive number, negative number, or zero, dictating which element has precedence. 

In your case, you want to sort by the amount of ones in the binary representation, followed by the amount of zeroes, then by their original value. Here's a JavaScript adaptation of the Python code:

```javascript
function sort_by_binary_ones_zeros_desc(arr){
    arr.sort((a, b) => {
        const binA = a.toString(2);
        const binB = b.toString(2);
        const countOnesA = (binA.match(/1/g) || []).length;
        const countOnesB = (binB.match(/1/g) || []).length;
        const countZerosA = (binA.match(/0/g) || []).length;
        const countZerosB = (binB.match(/0/g) || []).length;
        
        if (countOnesA !== countOnesB) {
          return countOnesB - countOnesA;
        } 
        if (countZerosA !== countZerosB) {
          return countZerosB - countZerosA;
        } 
       return b - a; 
    });
    return arr;
}
```
Here, `(a, b)` are elements in your array. `a.toString(2)` and `b.toString(2)` are binary representations of `a` and `b`, respectively. `(binA.match(/1/g) || []).length` and `(binB.match(/1/g) || []).length` are the counts of ones in `binA` and `binB`. The same is done for the zeros. 

The function will sort by the count of ones, then the count of zeros, and lastly by their original values.
