You can define a function arrSum to do the task. The function would take an array arr and the start and end indices of the range. Here is the entire snippet which includes the function definition and a test.

```R
arrSum <- function(arr, start, end) {
  if (start < 1 || end > length(arr)) 
      stop("Invalid range values")
  subset <- arr[start:end]
  if (any(!is.numeric(subset))) 
      stop("Non-numeric value in range")
  
  return(sum(subset))
}

# test the function
arr = c(2, 3, -6, 1, 9, 'a', 7, 8)

# this should compute the sum of 2, 3, -6, 1
print(arrSum(arr, 1, 4)) # should print 0

# this should return an error, noting that the range includes a character 'a'
print(arrSum(arr, 1, 6)) # should throw an error
```

In this script, `arrSum` first checks if the range values are invalid (i.e., smaller than 1 or larger than the array size). If the range values are valid, it extracts the subarray. Then, the function checks if all elements in the subset are numeric. If they are, it calculates the sum and returns it; otherwise, it stops and throws an error.
