Is there a command in classic ASP I can use to tell the browser not to pull the page from it's cache, or, to not cache, or clear the cache of my page?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, you can tell the browser not to cache your page by setting several HTTP headers in your ASP script. These headers basically tell the browser to retrieve a new version of your page every time, instead of using a cached one. 

Here is an example of how you can accomplish this in classic ASP:

```asp
Response.CacheControl = "no-cache"
Response.AddHeader "Pragma", "no-cache"
Response.Expires = -1
```

This command tells the browser not to store a cached copy of the page:

- The "Cache-Control" header is set to "no-cache" to let the browser know it shouldn't cache the page.
- The "Pragma" header is similar, and is used for backward compatibility with HTTP/1.0 (where "Cache-Control" was not defined).
- The "Expires" header is set to -1, which in HTTP terms means "the past", i.e., the page has already expired, so the browser should get a fresh copy.

You should place this code at the top of your ASP page to make sure there is not any form of caching going on in the browser. This can be very useful if you have dynamic content that's changing frequently.
<|end|>


