Construct an advanced quicksort methodology in Python, that is adaptive and makes a deciding choice for the pivot during runtime depending upon the incoming data so as to procure the best possible performance. Examine the efficiency and output of your code by subjecting it to the following array, namely, arr = [1, 9, 4, 6, 5, 8, 2].
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This can be achieved using the QuickSort algorithm, specifically the "Median of three" method (choosing the pivot as the median between the first, middle, and last elements) for pivot selection. It helps to reduce the worst-case scenario. Here is the implemented code:

```python
def quicksort(arr):
    if len(arr) <= 1:
        return arr
    pivot = select_pivot(arr)
    less = [i for i in arr[1:] if i <= pivot]
    greater = [i for i in arr[1:] if i > pivot]
    return quicksort(less) + [pivot] + quicksort(greater)

def select_pivot(arr):
    mid = len(arr) // 2
    candidates = [arr[0], arr[mid], arr[-1]]
    candidates.sort()
    if candidates[1] == arr[0]:
        arr[0], arr[-1] = arr[-1], arr[0]
    elif candidates[1] == arr[mid]:
        arr[mid], arr[-1] = arr[-1], arr[mid]
    return arr[-1]

arrays = [1, 9, 4, 6, 5, 8, 2]
print(quicksort(arrays))
```

In the quicksort function, we split our input list into three parts: elements less than pivot, the pivot itself, and elements greater than pivot, and then recursively sort the 'less than' and 'greater than' sublists.

The select_pivot function chooses the pivot as the median of the first, middle, and last elements of the list. This technique can provide a significant performance improvement over always using the first or last element, particularly for partially sorted data. If the selected pivot is not the last element of the list, we swap it with the last element (because our partition function assumes that the pivot is always the last element).

The output of this script will be a sorted version of the input array: [1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9]. As for efficiency, pivot selection strategies have a major impact on QuickSort's efficiency, and using a good strategy can reduce the time complexity from O(n^2) to O(n log n) in real-world scenarios. However, keep in mind that QuickSort's worst-case time complexity will always be O(n^2), no matter what pivot selection strategy you use.
<|end|>


