Here is a simple C code snippet that does what you asked for. This implementation uses the `printf` function to output the string and the `%*s` and `%-*s` format specifiers for right and left alignment of the string. For simplicity, the alignment instruction is passed as an integer where `1` indicates right alignment and `0` (or any other value) indicates left alignment as default. Note that C does not have a Boolean data type, but it does treat any non-zero value as true and zero as false.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void printAlignedString(const char* str, int alignment) {
    if (alignment == 1) {  // Right alignment
        printf("%*s\n", 20, str);
    } 
    else {  // Left alignment
        printf("%-*s\n", 20, str);
    }
}

int main() {
    printAlignedString("Hello, world!", 1);  // Right alignment
    printAlignedString("Hello, world!", 0);  // Left alignment
    return 0;
}
```

