Using TortoiseSVN against VisualSVN I delete a source file that I should not have deleted. Now this isn't a train smash because I can get the file back from the daily backup. However I would like to undelete it from SVN (VisualSVN) so that I can get the history back. However I can't work out how to do that.

Anybody know how to undelete a file from VisualSVN either using the VisualSVN interface or the latest version of TortoiseSVN?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can retrieve the deleted file along with its history from Subversion using the following steps:

1. Using TortoiseSVN, go to the directory where the file was deleted. 

2. Right-click on the directory and select "Show log" from the TortoiseSVN context menu. 

3. In the log dialog, you can see all the changes made to that folder (including all deleted files).

4. Find the revision where the file got deleted. 

5. Select the revision immediately before the file was deleted.

6. In this older revision, find the deleted file. It should be present in this older commit.

7. Right click on the file and select "Save revision to...". Store it in some temporary location.

8. Close the log dialog.

9. Now, go back to the directory where the file was deleted, right click on the directory and select "Add" in the TortoiseSVN context menu. 

10. Select the file in the temporary location then Click "OK".

11. Now commit the changes. The file will be added back to the repository, retaining its history.

Remember: In Subversion, nothing is really deleted, it keeps the history of all your actions. So no worry if you delete something by accident.
<|end|>


