A client has a number of disparate systems that they are planning to link together and have asked for a set of system interface specifications that would document the data and protocols used to interface the different parts.

The interfaces are between processes not between users. 

Any recommendations for a template that we could use to document the system interfaces?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Depending on the complexity of your interfaces and the degree of formality required by your client, you may want to include the following sections in your system interface specification template:

1. Introduction: Outline the purpose and scope of the document i.e., documenting all the distinct system interfaces, processes and services.

2. System Overview: Provide a high level picture of the various systems and how they interact. This could include a diagram showing the systems and their interconnections.

3. Interface Description: For each interface, provide a detailed description covering things like the interface's purpose, the systems it connects, the data exchanged, and the protocols used. 

4. Data Dictionary: This section should provide a detailed description of all the data exchanged across the interfaces. This should include data types, sizes, and any protocol-specific information.

5. Protocol Specification: This should include detailed specifications of the protocols used in the interfaces. This could including message formats, communication modes, frequency and timing information, operation sequences, error handling and recovery procedures.

6. Use Cases/Scenarios: This section should illustrate concrete examples of how the interfaces are used in real-world scenarios. This helps to promote understanding and acceptance tests.

7. Non-functional Requirements: Discuss the operational, performance, security, and compliance requirements pertinent to the interfaces.

8. Validation Criteria/Methods: Outline how the interfaces will be tested and validated. This section can be used to define specific test cases to confirm that all requirements have been met.

9. Approval and Change Management: This section should specify who has the authority to approve changes, what the procedures are for changing the document, and how changes are tracked.

Remember to update your document as changes or enhancements are made to the interfaces over time. A good place to maintain this document would be in a shared location where all stakeholders can easily access it.
<|end|>


