Write a SQL stored procedure to find all students enrolled in a specific course and the course start date. In addition to the existing tables, utilize the "course_dates" table.
Table: courses 
Columns: Id (int), Name (string)
Table: students 
Columns: Id (int), Name (string), CourseId (int)
Table: course_dates
Columns: CourseId (int), StartDate (date)
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This problem can be solved using SQL join queries in the stored procedure. The procedure will take course name as an input parameter and return all students enrolled in that particular course along with the course start date. Below is the SQL stored procedure:

```sql
DELIMITER $$
CREATE PROCEDURE GetStudentsAndCourseStart(@course_name nvarchar(50))
BEGIN
    SELECT s.Name as StudentName, cd.StartDate as CourseStartDate
    FROM students s
    INNER JOIN courses c ON s.CourseId = c.Id
    INNER JOIN course_dates cd ON c.Id = cd.CourseId
    WHERE c.Name = @course_name;
END $$
DELIMITER ;
```

This procedure joins the students, courses, and course_dates tables based on the CourseId. It selects the student name and course start date and filters based on the course name given as input.

To call the stored procedure for a specific course:

```sql
CALL GetStudentsAndCourseStart('Course Name');
```

NOTE: Change 'Course Name' to the course you desire to see results for.

This will return all the students who are enrolled in the "Course Name" course and the start date of the course. Please replace the 'Course Name' with the actual course name.

Please note that the SQL syntax might slightly differ depends on the SQL database you are using. The above syntax is compatible with MySQL. Make sure to replace nvarchar with the correct string data type that your database uses if it's not compatible.
<|end|>


