<|user|>
Elucidate the distinctions between the two JavaScript comparison operators "==" and "===", while indicating their unique functionalities in the context of type coercion and strictness.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In JavaScript, the "==" and "===" operators are both comparison operators. However, they differ in how they compare values, particularly with regard to type coercion and strictness.

"==" (double equals) is a loose equality operator. It compares two values for equality, after performing any necessary type conversions. This feature of the "==" operator is known as type coercion.

For example:

```javascript
console.log(1 == '1'); // true
console.log(true == 1); // true
console.log(null == undefined); // true
```

"===" (triple equals) is a strict equality operator. It compares two values for equality, without performing type conversions. If the types of two variables are different, "===" assumes the values are unequal.

For example:

```javascript
console.log(1 === '1'); // false
console.log(true === 1); // false
console.log(null === undefined); // false
```

Thus, "==" and "===" differentiate themselves by the way they handle type coercion. The "==" operator utilizes type coercion and doesn't enforce type matching, whereas the "===" operator doesn't coerce types and enforces type matching. Amateurs tend to use "==" while professionals prefer "===" because it avoids odd side effects that can cause bugs. 

In conclusion, if you want to avoid any unexpected results due to type coercion, it is recommended to stick with the strict equality operator "===" in your comparison operations.
<|end|>


