Creating an embossed or 3D effect in SVG involves multiple steps including adjusting the color gradients, adding highlights and shadows, and adjusting layering of the SVG elements. Given this SVG code is quite complex, it would require extensive knowledge and manipulation of the SVG code to create a properly embossed or 3D effect. If you're not familiar with SVG editing, you might want to consider using graphic software like Adobe Illustrator which offers much more user-friendly tools to create these types of effects.

However, if you'd like to attempt adding a 3D effect with code, you can use the 'filter' SVG element to add some depth to your image. Here's an example of an embossing filter:

```html
<filter id="emboss">
  <feGaussianBlur in="SourceAlpha" stdDeviation="2" result="blurout"/>
  <feSpecularLighting in="blurout" surfaceScale="6" specularConstant="0.5" specularExponent="30" lighting-color="white" result="specOut">
    <fePointLight x="-5000" y="-10000" z="20000"/>
  </feSpecularLighting>
  <feComposite in="specOut" in2="SourceAlpha" operator="in" result="specOut"/>
  <feComposite in="SourceGraphic" in2="specOut" operator="arithmetic" k1="0" k2="1" k3="1" k4="0"/>
</filter>
```

To apply this filter to your shapes, include a `filter="url(#emboss)"` attribute in your `<path>` elements. However, it may not give the desired results depending on the shape and color gradient of the SVG.

Adding 3D effects through SVG manually is quite complex and this might not give exactly the effect you're looking for. If you're not familiar with SVG filters and effects, consider using advanced graphic design tools for more controlled and precise effects.
<|end|>


