Based on your criteria, here are a few database solutions that might serve your needs:

1. Apache Hadoop: Hadoop is a reliable, scalable, distributed computing system. It uses HDFS for storing large data sets and MapReduce for processing this data. It is open source and can run seamlessly on commodity hardware.

2. PostgreSQL: This is an advanced relational database management system that emphasizes extensibility and SQL compliance. It is open source and known for its robustness, strong ACID compliance, and full compatibility with different platforms including Windows.

3. SQLite: SQLite is a powerful embedded database engine. It is reliable, fast and self-contained. It handles large datasets well and has a highly efficient disk I/O. SQLite might be best suitable for single-user applications and embedded systems as it lacks inherent network capabilities.

4. MariaDB: A fork of MySQL developed by the original developers, MariaDB is fully open source and is designed for speed and robustness. It supports a wide range of storage engines and offers scalability and speed.

Please note that while these databases offer extensive features and many of them will run well on a Windows x64 system, they may not have the capacity to provide efficient read/write access to very large datasets on a single machine. For such use cases, it is advised to leverage distributed storage and computing architectures.  

Lastly, it's important to conduct performance testing to see whether the chosen solution meets your specific requirements.
<|end|>


