Identify the greatest cumulative total of four consecutive integers within a numerical sequence, considering there could be both negative and positive values. Solve with time complexity below O(n^2).
[2, -7, -4, 15, 25, 18, -12]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a Python solution using the sliding window technique to solve the problem in O(n) time complexity.

```Python
def max_sequence_sum(numbers, k):
    # Calculate the initial sum
    sum_k_numbers = sum(numbers[:k])
    max_sum = sum_k_numbers
    for i in range(k, len(numbers)):
        # Subtract the left most number and add the new rightmost number
        sum_k_numbers = sum_k_numbers - numbers[i - k] + numbers[i]
        max_sum = max(max_sum, sum_k_numbers)
    return max_sum

numbers = [2, -7, -4, 15, 25, 18, -12]
print(max_sequence_sum(numbers, 4))
```
The program first calculates the sum of the first four numbers. This sum is then compared with the sum of the next four numbers, and so on. The maximum sum found is returned as the result.

This algorithm works well because it only calculates the sum of four consecutive numbers once and then just adds and subtracts numbers to slide the window. Therefore its time complexity is O(n).

Once you run this script, it will output 58 as the greatest cumulative total of four consecutive integers in the given sequence.
<|end|>


