I've got this dependency in my pom.xml

<dependency>
        <groupId>com.itextpdf</groupId>
        <artifactId>itext7-core</artifactId>
        <version>7.1.18</version>
        <type>pom</type>
    </dependency>


And in my module-info.java file I have the following

requires transitive kernel;


which produces the following warning, "Name of automatic module 'kernel' is unstable, it is derived from the module's file name."
What can I do to get rid of this warning?
I tried changing it to

requires transitive com.itextpdf.kernel;


but then I get the following error, "com.itextpdf.kernel cannot be resolved to a module"
I tried a bunch of other similar lines of code but nothing worked. What can I change the code to in order to remove the error/warning?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The warning message you're seeing is due to the use of an automatic module. In Java 9 and higher, JAR files that aren’t explicitly designed as modules (i.e., they don’t contain a module-info.java file) can still be used as automatic modules.

The warning message is advising that the name of the automatic module, which is derived from the file name of the JAR, might change if the file name of the JAR changes. That's because the name of an automatic module isn't stable.

To fix this warning, the owner of that library (in this case, iText) should offer a proper Java module, by adding a module-info.java file into their JAR, with a provides clause. It also needs to make sure that classes and resources in the JAR file, which are meant for public use, are exported in order to be accessible to code in other modules.

Layers, iText isn't modularized yet, they're still using the automatic-modules.

So, there aren't much solutions for this issue from your side, unless you suppress the warning or ignore it until, iText gets modularized.

If you, however, have the authority to change the library, consider making it fully modularized.
To fix this from library side or if you have the access to change the library code, you can do this:
1. Add `module-info.java` into `com.itextpdf.kernel` sources, for example:
```java
module com.itextpdf.kernel {
    exports com.itextpdf.kernel;
}
```
2. Make sure, the JAR of `com.itextpdf.kernel` contains `module-info.class` (usually in the top level directory inside the JAR).
So, the structure of the JAR should be something like this (at least the top level files):
```
com.itextpdf.kernel.jar
│ module-info.class
│
└─com/itextpdf/kernel/
    └─ /* all classes and resources of the package */
```
When `module-info.class` is packed in proper place inside the JAR, Java treats it as proper Java module and should not give any more warnings.
Please note, that the module name `com.itextpdf.kernel` is only an example. It is up to the author of the library to decide what module name they want to use.
This should remove the warning about automatic module name instability.
<|end|>


