I have a React context that manages authentication and provides an Axios client.  However that Axios client needs to be reconfigured everytime there's a new token.
I'm also using expo-sqlite which has issues with database locking when the application restarts and sometimes when the object gets reloaded.
So I was wondering if there's a way for a plain module to access the React Context to get an item from the context in React-Native.
I was thinking of Portals but that's talking about DOM
<|end|>
<|asst|>
No, a plain module can't directly access a React context value - context is a feature of React components, not ECMAScript modules.

However, that doesn't prevent you from designing your module with an API that requires or accepts the relevant information from the context. For instance, you could create a function in your module that accepts the token as an argument. Any component that calls this function would need to have access to the current token, and could acquire it dynamically through the context.

```
// in your module
export const createClient = token => {
  return axios.create({
    // ... your axios config here
    authorization: `Bearer ${token}`
  });
}

// in your component
const token = useContext(AuthContext).token;
const client = createClient(token);
```

Another way of doing this could be by using an Auth Provider and letting each function/component receive the token through the provider instead of context.

Final way could be by re-configuring the module every time a new token is received. You also have the possibility to use global object but this is considered as a hack and could encounter issues in the future.
<|end|>


