Revise the following Ruby script in a manner that can calculate and display the duration, in days, an office staff member has been engaged in various different project assignments. The script should consider the holidays and weekends when calculating the duration. Furthermore, introduce error handling for incorrect or invalid date formats.

start_project_dates = ["10 June 2021", "15 July 2021", ...]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is an example of how you might edit the Ruby script to include error handling and account for weekends and holidays:

```ruby
require 'date'
require 'business_time'

start_project_dates = ["10 June 2021", "15 July 2021"] # add more dates
end_project_dates = ["20 June 2021", "30 July 2021"] # each date corresponds to start_project_dates

holidays = [Date.parse('2021-7-4'), Date.parse('2021-9-6')]
BusinessTime::Config.holidays << holidays

def calculate_working_days(start_date, end_date)
  start_date = Date.parse(start_date)
  end_date = Date.parse(end_date)
  working_days = 0

  while start_date <= end_date
    unless start_date.weekend?
      working_days += 1 unless BusinessTime::Config.holidays.include?(start_date)
    end

    start_date = start_date + 1
  end

  working_days
rescue ArgumentError
  puts "Please provide valid date format (i.e., DD MMM YYYY)"
end

def calculate_project_durations(start_dates, end_dates)
  durations = []

  start_dates.each_with_index do |start_date, index|
    durations << calculate_working_days(start_date, end_dates[index])
  end

  durations
end

project_durations = calculate_project_durations(start_project_dates, end_project_dates)
puts "Project durations in working days: #{project_durations}"
```

With this script, you have an array of start project dates (start_project_dates) and corresponding end project dates (end_project_dates). All dates must be in string format with the pattern "DD MMM YYYY". The calculated durations of the project assignments are then stored in the array project_durations.

The key function here is calculate_working_days, which iterates from the start date to the end date, incrementing the count of working days if the day is not a weekend or a holiday (as defined in the BusinessTime::Config.holidays array).

This script uses the business_time gem, which provides utility functions for calculating business time aware math. If you don't have the gem installed, you can install it with gem install business_time.
<|end|>


