<|asst|>
To create a simple ToDo list application on your Android device, you need to have knowledge and skills in Android development and use Android Studio, the official Integrated Development Environment (IDE) for Android application development. Below is a simplified step by-step-guide to assist you in designing, developing, and testing your application. This guide assumes that you have a basic understanding of the Java programming language and Android Studio IDE.

Step 1: Install Android Studio
Download and install Android Studio, which can be obtained from the Android Developer's website.

Step 2: Develop a New Project
Create a new project. Go to File -> New -> New Project

Step 3: Name the Project
Name the project "ToDoListApp", choose an Empty Activity template and press "Finish"

Step 4: Design Interface
The first step in developing the ToDo list app is to design its interface. We will need a RecyclerView for displaying the task list, a FloatingActionButton for adding new tasks, and ActionBar menu for deleting all tasks. The EditText, DatePicker, and TimePicker dialogs are used for input, and AlertDialog is used for editing and deleting tasks.

Step 5: Update the dependencies
Open the build.gradle (Module: app) and add the following dependencies to include the RecyclerView and CardView libraries:
```Java
dependencies {
implementation 'androidx.recyclerview:recyclerview:1.1.0'
implementation 'androidx.recyclerview:recyclerview-selection:1.1.0-rc03'
}
```
After adding these dependencies, sync the gradle files.

Step 6: Create the Model Class
Create a new class named "Task" that will contain properties such as TaskTitle, TaskDetail, Date, and Time.

Step 7: Database Operations
We will use SQLite as the database where the tasks will be stored.
Create a new class named "DatabaseHelper". This class will help us to open the database, create tables, and perform CRUD operations.

Step 8: Adapters and ViewHolders
RecyclerView requires an adapter and view holder. Create a new class named "TasksAdapter".

Step 9: MainActivity
This is the core of the application. In this file, add instances of the DatabaseHelper class and TasksAdapter class. Add OnClickListener to the FloatingActionButton to start creating tasks. Configure the RecyclerView with the TasksAdapter. Then apply the OnClickListener to the RecyclerView items for updating and deleting tasks.

Step 10: Testing and Troubleshooting
Once you have finished the development process, test out the app on an Android emulator or an Android physical device. Be keen to debug and check for any errors that may arise.

Finally, be noted that the above guide provides a simplified schema and many improvements can be made based on your design skills and your familiarity with Android Studio. For example, you could add additional features like notifications and categories, or you could implement the Archiving tasks feature.
<|end|>


