Design a CSS stylesheet for a responsive webpage that dynamically changes layout for different screen sizes, vertically aligns the text, sets the background color to light blue, and changes to dark blue during nighttime (8PM to 6AM).
<|end|>
<|asst|>
While writing full CSS is a bit complex, I have mentioned main ideas and some snippets that you can easily put together to achieve your desired behavior.

This problem requires a mix of CSS for styling, HTML for page structure, and JavaScript for dynamic time-based changes. As you'll find out, CSS alone doesn't have time functions and cannot determine what "nighttime" is.

Firstly, you can use media queries in CSS to make the page responsive:

```css
@media screen and (max-width: 480px){
  /* style for screens less than 480px here */
}
```

The above code will apply the style declared within it only when the screen size is lower than 480 pixels in width. You can use as many media queries as needed for different screen sizes.

To vertically align text in CSS you can use flex, below is an example for a paragraph:

```css
.container {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
  height: 100vh;
}
```

You can set the background color to light blue with simple css rule:

```css
body {
  background-color: lightblue;
}
```

As for the nighttime change, you can use JavaScript to get the current hour and change the class of your `body` tag accordingly. Below is a very simple implementation:

```javascript
var date = new Date();
var hour = date.getHours();
var body = document.body;

if(hour >= 20 || hour <= 6) {
  body.classList.add('nighttime');
} else {
  body.classList.add('daytime');
}
```

With the corresponding CSS looking something like:

```css
body {
  transition: background-color 1s;
}

body.daytime {
  background-color: lightblue;
}

body.nighttime {
  background-color: darkblue;
}
```

Keep in mind that the JavaScript will only run once when the page loads, it doesn't continually check the time. If you need the background color change to happen exactly at 8PM or 6AM, you'd need to implement some kind of looping check. Also, please note that this approach doesn't consider users' local timezone and it's better to use timezone-aware methods considering your users' demographics.
<|end|>


