<|asst|>
Here is a Python solution using `xml.etree.ElementTree` library. This script extracts 'student' elements and checks for certain attributes, printing special messages when they're found:

```python
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET

data = '''<root>
    <student name="John" roll_no="123" course="Science" grade="B" />
    <student name="Jane" roll_no="456" course="Mathematics" grade="A" />
</root>'''

root = ET.fromstring(data)

for student in root.iter('student'):
    name = student.get('name')
    roll_no = student.get('roll_no')
    course = student.get('course')
    grade = student.get('grade')

    print(f'Found student: {name}, roll no: {roll_no}, course: {course}, grade: {grade}')

    if course == 'Mathematics' and grade == 'A':
        print('Eureka! Found a student who excelled in Mathematics!')

```

In this script, `ET.fromstring(data)` builds an ElementTree instance from the XML data. `root.iter('student')` gets us all 'student' elements in the tree, including nested ones. We check the 'course' and 'grade' attributes to see if they match our special case. If they do, a special message is printed. Replace `data` with your actual XML data.

Note: This script will not work if your XML data in a file or URL, in that case you perform file I/O operation then parse using `ET.parse(FILE_NAME_OR_PATH)` or fetch data over network (using `requests` or similar library).
