I know that Dash provides checkboxes / dropdowns (via `dcc`, Dash Core Components, see [https://dash.plotly.com/dash-core-components](https://dash.plotly.com/dash-core-components)).
But how to create a checkbox in the context of a pure Plotly.JS plot, without dash?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Plotly.JS doesn't provide out of the box components like checkboxes or dropdowns similar to Dash. However, you can definitely create a checkbox using plain HTML/Javascript and then listen to its change event to update the Plotly.JS chart accordingly.

Here's an example with a simple checkbox that toggles a trace on a plot:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <script src="https://cdn.plot.ly/plotly-latest.min.js"></script>
</head>
<body>

    <div id="myDiv"><!-- Plotly chart will be drawn inside this DIV --></div>
    <input type="checkbox" id="showtrace" checked> Show Trace

    <script>
    var trace1 = {
      x: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5],
      y: [1, 6, 3, 6, 1],
      mode: 'scatter',
      type: 'scatter'
    };

    var data = [ trace1 ];

    var layout = {
      title:'Data',
      showlegend: false
    };

    Plotly.newPlot('myDiv', data, layout);

    // listen to change event on checkbox
    document.getElementById('showtrace').addEventListener('change', function(e) {
      if(e.target.checked) {
        // if checked, show trace
        Plotly.restyle('myDiv', 'visible', true);
      } else {
        // if unchecked, hide trace
        Plotly.restyle('myDiv', 'visible', 'legendonly');
      }
    });
    </script>

</body>
</html>
```

In this code:

- `Plotly.newPlot('myDiv', data, layout)` creates a new scatter chart inside the `myDiv` element, with the specified data and layout.
- The `addEventListener` function listens to the `change` event on the checkbox element. When this event occurs, it calls the specified function.
- If the checkbox is checked, the `Plotly.restyle` function is called with parameters `'myDiv', 'visible', true`, which makes the trace visible.
- If the checkbox is unchecked, the `Plotly.restyle` function is called with parameters `'myDiv', 'visible', 'legendonly'`, which hides the trace.
<|end|>


