Devise an instance-restricted Singleton class in Java boasting a thread-safe synchronized technique.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a thread-safe Singleton class in Java using the "instance-restricted" and "synchronized" techniques:

```java
public class Singleton {

    private static Singleton singletonInstance = null; // Singleton instance

    private Singleton() {
        // Private constructor to restrict new instances
    }

    // Note that the synchronized keyword is used in this static method
    // to make our Singleton implementation thread-safe.
    public static synchronized Singleton getInstance() {
        if (singletonInstance == null) {
            singletonInstance = new Singleton();
        }
        return singletonInstance;
    }

    // Add business logic methods below
}
```

In this code:

- The Singleton constructor is private to prevent creating a new instance of Singleton via the `new` keyword.
- getInstance() method is static so that it can be accessed directly through the class, Singleton. This static method creates a new Singleton object when it is null, and returns the Singleton object.
- `synchronized` keyword on the getInstance() method: This is used to ensure that only one thread at a time can execute getInstance(). When one thread is executing a synchronized method for an object, all other threads that invoke synchronized methods for the same object block (suspend execution) until the first thread is done with the object.
- Singleton instance (singletonInstance) is a class-level variable and it is the only instance of the Singleton class in this approach.

This code may have performance issues because every time a thread wants to access the single instance, it has to wait its turn. All threads are blocked. For low traffic multi-threaded applications this should be okay, but there's a better way in high traffic multi-threaded applications called "Double Checked Locking". 

Here's the code using Double Checked Locking:

```java
public class Singleton {
 
    private static volatile Singleton singletonInstance = null; // singleton instance
 
    private Singleton() {
        // private constructor to prevent others from instantiating
    }
  
    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        if (singletonInstance == null) { // first check
            synchronized (Singleton.class) {
                if (singletonInstance == null) { // double check
                    singletonInstance = new Singleton();
                }
            }
        }
        return singletonInstance;
    }

    // Business logic methods below
}
```

In this code, the keyword `volatile` is added to the `singletonInstance` declaration to ensure that multiple threads offer the `singletonInstance` variable initialized Unique Singleton instance.
<|end|>


