<|asst|>
The issue you're experiencing is due to the way redirection (the ">" sign) handles encoding in the Windows PowerShell environment. It seems to create files in Unicode encoding by default.

Luckily, you can bypass this problem by using Ruby's built-in File class to handle file writing, which will make sure that the file is written in UTF-8. Here's an example:

# Write to the file
```
ruby -E UTF-8 -e "File.open('test.txt', 'w') { |file| file.write 'どうぞよろしくお願いします,Mr Jason' }"
```

# Read from the file
```
ruby -E UTF-8 -e "puts File.read('test.txt')"
```

In this particular example, I'm using File.open with the 'w' option, which tells Ruby that we want to write a file (and create it if it doesn't already exist). Then, I'm using the write method to put your text into the file.

Remember that this will overwrite 'test.txt' each time you run the command. If you want to append text to the file instead of overwriting it, use 'a' instead of 'w' as the File.open option.
