Formulate a succinct JavaScript code segment that will dynamically adjust the opacity of an image, identified by the class "background_image", as the user scrolls down the page. Additionally, the opacity should reset to full when the user scrolls back up to the top, and implement a debounce function to optimize it.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can do this using JavaScript's scroll event listener, along with a debounce function to prevent the function running too frequently and causing performance issues. Below is an example of how this could be achieved:

```javascript
function debounce(func, wait = 15, immediate = true) {
  var timeout;
  return function () {
    var context = this, args = arguments;
    var later = function () {
      timeout = null;
      if (!immediate) func.apply(context, args);
    };
    var callNow = immediate && !timeout;
    clearTimeout(timeout);
    if (callNow) func.apply(context, args);
    else timeout = setTimeout(later, wait);
  };
};

function adjustOpacity() {
  const image = document.querySelector('.background_image');
  const scrollHeight = window.innerHeight;
  const scrollPos = window.scrollY + scrollHeight;
  const imageHeight = image.offsetHeight;
  const imageMiddle = imageHeight / 2;
  
  if (scrollPos < imageHeight) {
    image.style.opacity = "1";
  } else if (scrollPos > imageMiddle + imageHeight) {
    image.style.opacity = "0";
  } else {
    image.style.opacity = 1 - (scrollPos - imageHeight) / (scrollHeight - imageMiddle);
  }
};

window.addEventListener('scroll', debounce(adjustOpacity));
```

This JavaScript code listens for the page to be scrolled, and when it is, it triggers the `adjustOpacity` function. This function checks where the user has scrolled to, and adjusts the opacity of the image accordingly. 

The debounce function is used to ensure that the `adjustOpacity` function is not called too often, which could lead to performance issues. The debounce function will ensure that `adjustOpacity` is not called more than once every 15 milliseconds, which is fast enough to be smooth for the user, but not so fast that it causes performance issues.
<|end|>


