Using the JavaScript library Underscore.js, write a script to filter all objects in an array that have the property "isPromoted" set to false. Alongside this, each object in the array has another property "expirationDate", expressed as a string in JavaScript Date format. Your script should also filter out objects whose "expirationDate" is prior to the current date. The script should return the updated array sorted in ascending order by "expirationDate". Write your script to ensure it can handle any errors arising from date format issues or other potential object abnormalities.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is the JavaScript code using Underscore.js according to your requirements:

```javascript
let _ = require('underscore')

const filterObjects = (arr) => {
  try {
    return _.chain(arr)
        .filter(obj => obj.isPromoted !== false)
        .filter(obj => {
            if (obj.expirationDate) {
                let expDate = new Date(obj.expirationDate);
                if (!isNaN(expDate.getTime())) {
                    return expDate >= new Date();
                }
            }
            return false;
        })
        .sortBy(obj => new Date(obj.expirationDate))
        .value();
  } 
  catch (error) {
        console.error('An error occurred:', error);
  }
};

let data = [
  { isPromoted: true, expirationDate: '2022-12-31' },
  { isPromoted: false, expirationDate: '2024-12-31' },
  { isPromoted: true, expirationDate: '2023-11-30' },
  // Add more objects if necessary
];

console.log(filterObjects(data));
```
This script filters all objects in the input array that have the `isPromoted` property set to `false` and whose `expirationDate` is in the past relative to the current date. It also sorts the remaining objects in ascending order by `expirationDate`. A try-catch error handling block is used to deal with any potential errors related to the JavaScript Date object or other potential issues.
<|end|>


