While I don't have first-hand experience using BQNT vs Desktop API, I can provide some suggestions based on online data and user experiences.

First, it's important to note that Bloomberg BQNT and Bloomberg API provide two different approaches to handling Bloomberg data. 

Bloomberg's BQNT is a cloud-based solution that replaces the need for having a physical Bloomberg terminal. It’s indeed part of the Bloomberg Anywhere service and is designed to allow users to perform complex analysis and visualize data, all in a web browser. BQNT might be a good solution if you wish to run complex scripts and computations without being tethered to a physical Bloomberg terminal. 

The Bloomberg API, on the other hand, allows third-party systems to consume Bloomberg's broad range of data, including market data, reference data, historical data etc. If you frequently face data limits with Bloomberg Desktop API, it might be worth investigating if there is a Bloomberg Server API that allows for higher data limits.

Many users have reported that the Bloomberg API is generally more task-specific, requiring more manual programming but offering more flexibility in return. BQNT, however, is an all-in-one solution, but might not offer the same degree of customization.

In terms of data limits, it seems that BQNT would indeed give you broader data access than the Desktop API for the fact that the programs run in the Bloomberg cloud. But it's still advised to check with Bloomberg representative to confirm.

Also, keep in mind that transitioning from the Desktop API to BQNT will probably require some rewrite of your scripts and analysis setup, because these two environments use different coding paradigms.

Lastly, another factor to consider is the cost. The pricing model for BQNT may differ from the Desktop API, it's essential to understand the difference and weigh the increased accessibility against the potentially higher cost.
