I want to complete few tasks parallel using reactive Reactor Core streams. So if there are the long timed task - the next emitted objects must be processed without delay.
So, I have two object, the first took a lot of time to complete. I expect no exact order, but parallel execution. What's wrong?

Flux.just(obj1, obj2)
        .subscribeOn(Schedulers.newParallel("parallel", 5))
        .flatMap(Transformator::process)


Program is stopped by first element if there are an infinite loop in it's processing.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The problem you're experiencing has to do with the `flatMap` operator. Even though you're using the `subscribeOn` operator to use a parallel scheduler, `flatMap` operator by default runs on the initial thread. As a result, `flatMap` is not able to execute tasks in parallel.

`flatMap` operator can run tasks in parallel, but you need to tell it to do so by introducing inner Publishers that use distinct schedulers from `subscribeOn`.

Here is how you can do it:

```java
Flux.just(obj1, obj2)
    .flatMap(obj -> Mono.just(obj)
        .subscribeOn(Schedulers.newParallel("parallel", 5))
        .map(Transformator::process))
    .subscribe();
```

Here, for each source element, a new Mono is created and subscribed on a new thread from the pool (thanks to `Mono.just(obj).subscribeOn(Schedulers.newParallel("parallel", 5))`). Hence, each Transformator::process transformation will run in a different thread concurrently.

Please note that `.subscribeOn` has no effect on operators following it and only affect the source emission. Whereas `.publishOn` switch the execution context for the following operators.

So, it's more about where you put your `subscribeOn` not about `flatMap`.
<|end|>


