<|asst|>
The first common problem with CSS not loading on GitHub Pages is the path specified in the HTML file. Ensure that your CSS file path is correct. 

Remember, GitHub is case-sensitive. For example, if you have a CSS file at `css/styles.css` and you refer to it in your HTML like this:

```html
<link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="CSS/styles.css">
```

It will not be found because "CSS" does not match "css". Generally, I would recommend always using lower case for file names and directories.

The second common problem is related to the file path. If your file structure is like this:

```
| - index.html
| - css
    | - style.css
```

And in your `index.html` file you refer to the CSS file like this 

```html
<link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="style.css">
```

It will not find the CSS file because the css directory is not specified. Instead it should be:

```html
<link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="css/style.css">
```

If you're definitely sure you're doing the above correctly, check the browser's developer console to see if there are any error messages. These could give you clues as to why your CSS is not loading.

Last but not the least, it's also a good idea to check if you've properly set up the repo to be a GitHub Pages repo:

1. Go to the GitHub page for the repo.
2. Click on 'Settings'.
3. Scroll down to the 'GitHub Pages' section.
4. Make sure the 'Source' is set to the branch you want to use (usually main or master).
5. You can also set a theme here, but that's not necessary if you have your own CSS.

I hope one of these solutions works for you. If none of them do, I suggest cloning the repo to your local machine and trying to serve the site from there to see if it displays correctly. If it does, then it's most likely a GitHub Pages issue. If it doesn't, then it's probably a problem with the code itself.
<|end|>


