Create an initial setup using the basic components of a blockchain: blocks that include IoT data packages and timestamps, and a consistent hashing function for data encryption. 

Step 2: Stratified Hidden Markov Model implementation

The next step is to work on adding a stratified Hidden Markov Model (HMM) to the framework. 

The first layer of this stratified Hidden Markov Model will handle the data inflow – the transactions and the data received from IoT devices. This will handle not just the data conveyance but also perform an initial data validation task.

The second layer of the stratified HMM should focus on data encryption and storage. Using the native Go or Scala cryptography libraries, encrypted data should then be added to a block and the block added to the chain, thus effectively completing the data preservation phase.

Step 3: Distributed Ledger Technologies (DLT) integration

After successfully encrypting and storing the data in a block, the next step is to update this data across all the nodes and hence maintain the transparency and integrity of DLTs. This step should also be proficient enough to handle high transaction rates and manage large data networks.

Step 4: Self-learning mechanism

Adding the third layer to our stratified HMM is the self-learning mechanism. This can be implemented using machine learning models that would learn from the previous data patterns and adapt to new ones, thus preventing any data redundancy and latency.

Step 5: Quantum computer methodologies consideration

Post constructing the entire blockchain framework, attention must be reoriented on how possible future integration with quantum computer methodologies would look like in terms of improving data security and processing speed.

This is a high-level pipeline, but it contains all the necessary components to build a hands-on implementation. It's a robust project that requires a team of professionals in specific domains and can result in worthwhile technology for IoT.
