The challenge you are to tackle is named "Random Selection Excluding Blacklisted Elements". The problem description is as follows: You are provided with a blacklist `B` that encompasses unique integers within the range `[0, N)`. Your mission is to devise a function that will yield a uniformly random integer from the identical range `[0, N)` that is not included in `B`. The function should be engineered to reduce the calls to the system's `Math.random()` function to the minimum.

Please adhere to the following constraints:
`1 &amp;lt;= N &amp;lt;= 1000000000`
`0 &amp;lt;= B.length &amp;lt; min(100000, N)`
The range `[0, N)` excludes N, as per interval notation.

For example, consider the following scenarios:

Example 1:
Input: 
[&amp;#34;Solution&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;pick&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;pick&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;pick&amp;#34;]
[[1,[]],[],[],[]]
Output: [null,0,0,0]

Example 2:
Input: 
[&amp;#34;Solution&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;pick&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;pick&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;pick&amp;#34;]
[[2,[]],[],[],[]]
Output: [null,1,1,1]

Example 3:
Input: 
[&amp;#34;Solution&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;pick&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;pick&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;pick&amp;#34;]
[[3,[1]],[],[],[]]
Output: [null,0,0,2]

Example 4:
Input: 
[&amp;#34;Solution&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;pick&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;pick&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;pick&amp;#34;]
[[4,[2]],[],[],[]]
Output: [null,1,3,1]

To elucidate the input syntax: The input comprises two lists - the invoked subroutines and their corresponding arguments. The `Solution` constructor has two parameters, `N` and the blacklist `B`. The `pick` function does not possess any parameters. Parameters are always encapsulated within a list, even if there are none.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This problem can be solved using hash map and array. Here is the JavaScript solution:

```javascript
class Solution {
    constructor(N, blacklist) {
        this.map = new Map();
        this.M = N - blacklist.length;
        const last = new Set(Array.from({length: N-this.M}, (_, i) => i + this.M));
        blacklist.forEach(x => last.delete(x));
        let iter = last.values();
        blacklist.forEach(x => {
           if (x < this.M) { 
               this.map.set(x, iter.next().value);
           }
        });
    }

    pick() {
        let k = Math.floor(Math.random() * this.M);
        return this.map.has(k)? this.map.get(k) : k;
    }
}
```

The main idea is to make a map `map` from black numbers in `[0, M)` to white numbers in `[M, N)` with `M = N−blacklist.length` and randomly pick a number in `[0, M)`. If the number is black, return the corresponding white number; else return the number itself.

In more detail, the gaps between consecutive white numbers in `[M, N)` are filled in by black numbers in `[0, M)`. When initializing, use a `Set` `last` to store all white numbers in `[M, N)`, and remove black numbers from `last`. Then assign the remaining numbers in `last` to black numbers in `[0, M)` one by one.

In `pick`, get a random `k` in `[0, M)`. If `k` is a black number, return `map.get(k)`; else return `k`.
<|end|>


