Convert the ensuing HTML configuration, with its HTML-specific tags, to an equivalent, valid XML schema.

<html> 
   <body> 
     <div>Hello World!</div> 
   </body> 
</html>
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is a simple Python approach using the xml.etree.ElementTree library to convert the HTML configuration to a valid XML schema:

```Python
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET

# Create the 'html' element
html = ET.Element('html')

# Create the 'body' sub-element
body = ET.SubElement(html, 'body')

# Create the 'div' sub-element within the 'body' element
div = ET.SubElement(body, 'div')
div.text = "Hello World!"

# Convert the elements to a string format
xml_str = ET.tostring(html, encoding='unicode')

# Print the XML schema string
print(xml_str)
```

The above Python code results in the following XML:
```XML
<html>
  <body>
    <div>Hello World!</div>
  </body>
</html>
```
This is a very simple case example. For more complex HTML configurations, other tools like BeautifulSoup in Python would be more suitable.
  
Remember that XML and HTML share similar syntax, but they are used for different purposes:  
HTML is designed to display data and focuses on how data looks, while XML is designed to carry data and focuses on what data is. Also, XML offers a more strict validation of the data structure than HTML.
<|end|>


