In Python, classes are defined using the `class` keyword followed by the name of the class (Rectangle in this case). Inside the class, the `__init__` method is defined, which is the initializer (or constructor) that gets called whenever a new instance of the class is created. This method takes self (which represents the instance of the class) as well as any other arguments that the class requires.

In this case, your Rectangle class needs two arguments: length and breadth. These are defined as attributes of the class in the `__init__` method by saying `self.length = length` and `self.breadth = breadth`, respectively. This code assigns the values passed to the `__init__` method to the current instance of the class.

After defining this class, you could create a Rectangle object with a length of 5 and a breadth of 3 like so:

