<|user|>
In [Spring retry's README](https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-retry#stateful-retry), they mention how a "stateless retry" is not enough to rollback a transaction, but a stateful retry can by using a Map ?
I don't really understand what they mean.
If I want a transactional method to rollback the transaction and retry with a new one, how is a stateful retry different than a stateless retry ?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In the context of Spring Retry, a retry is considered stateless if it does not keep track of any context between invocations. So if a method fails and the framework decides to retry, it just calls the method again as if it is called for the first time. This is typically what you would want if the failure is transient and unrelated to the state of the current transaction, such as a temporary network glitch when the method is trying to retrieve data from another service.

Stateful retry, on the other hand, is used when the outcome of a method call might depend on the outcome of previous invocations. In this case, the framework keeps track of the context between retries in a map. This is useful in a situation where you try to perform an operation, if it fails because the state it requires is not yet available, you would want to rollback, change something (like updating a record which is required to be present/absent for the next retry) in the current transaction and then retry the entire process. The retry template will execute in a new transaction context each time so that changes made prior retries are persisted even if the retry fails again.

For example, let's say you have a method that retrieves data from a database and then calls another service to process the data. If the call to the second service fails, you might want to roll back the transaction so the data stays in the database, update something in the database to mark the data has not been processed yet, and then retry the method. In this case, a stateful retry would be more appropriate than a stateless retry, as the method needs to know that it is a retry invocation and it might need to behave differently than the original invocation. 

This is the main difference between the two types of retries. Depending on the specific requirements of your application, you might need to use either one or even both types of retries.
<|end|>


