Develop a Java function capable of receiving three different input parameters. Subsequently, it should seamlessly transition their corresponding values in a cyclical fashion, aligning more with the aspect of circular rotation.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In Java, we cannot directly swap values of variables by creating a function as Java is strictly pass-by-value, meaning it copies the values in the variables, not the variables themselves.

However, we can accomplish the task you're asking using an array or a class. Here is a method using an array:

```java
public class Main {
  public static void rotateValues(int[] values) {
    if(values == null || values.length != 3) {
      System.out.println("Invalid input");
      return;
    }

    int temp = values[2]; // save last value

    // shift each value by one position to the right
    for(int i = 2; i > 0; i--) {
      values[i] = values[i - 1];
    }

    values[0] = temp; // put last saved value in the first position

    // print new array values
    for(int value : values) {
      System.out.println(value);
    }
  }

  public static void main(String[] args) {
    int[] values = new int[]{1, 2, 3};
    rotateValues(values);
  }
}
```

This code represents a function called `rotateValues` that accepts an array of integers with exactly three elements. It will rotate the values within the array (modify the array in-place), moving each value to the next index position. The last value of the array will become the first. After rotation, it will print out all the elements in the array.

If you run `rotateValues` with `[1, 2, 3]`, you'll get `[3, 1, 2]`. Running again on the result will give `[2, 3, 1]`, and a third time will return you to `[1, 2, 3]`.
<|end|>


