What's the "right" way to do the following as a boolean expression?


for i in `ls $1/resources`; do
    if [ $i != "database.db" ]
    then
        if [ $i != "tiles" ]
        then
            if [ $i != "map.pdf" ]
            then
                if [ $i != "map.png" ]
                then
                    svn export -q $1/resources/$i ../MyProject/Resources/$i
...
<|end|>
<|asst|>
A "right" way of doing this would be to simplify the structure using logical AND in a single if statement, which checks for all conditions at once:

```bash
for i in `ls $1/resources`; do
    if [[ $i != "database.db" && $i != "tiles" && $i != "map.pdf" && $i != "map.png" ]]; then
        svn export -q $1/resources/$i ../MyProject/Resources/$i
    fi
done
```

This script loops over all files/directories in the `$1/resources` directory and checks if the current file/directory is not `database.db`, `tiles`, `map.pdf` or `map.png`. If the current item is not any of these four, it performs the `svn export` command.  

Adding `[[` and `]]` instead of `[` and `]` allows for additional functionality, such as the ability to use logical operators like `&&` and `||`.
<|end|>


