Design an intricate algorithmic procedure tasked with carrying out the encryption of textual data via a cipher mechanism. The algorithm should be sufficiently complex to effectively scramble data, yet maintain the feasibility of decryption when necessary.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This is a Python implementation for a cipher-based encryption-decryption system known as the "Caesar cipher". The Caesar cipher works by shifting the positions of characters by a determined amount. This encryption method is not highly complex, but is simple and demonstrates the fundamentals of encryption and decryption:

```python
def caesar_encrypt(text, shift):
    result = ""

    for i in range(len(text)):
        char = text[i]

        if (char.isupper()):  # For uppercase characters
            result += chr((ord(char) + shift - 65) % 26 + 65)
        else: # For lowercase characters
            result += chr((ord(char) + shift - 97) % 26 + 97)

    return result

def caesar_decrypt(encrypted_text, shift):
    result = ""

    for i in range(len(encrypted_text)):
        char = encrypted_text[i]

        if (char.isupper()):  # For uppercase characters
            result += chr((ord(char) - shift - 65) % 26 + 65)
        else: # For lowercase characters
            result += chr((ord(char) - shift - 97) % 26 + 97)

    return result

# Sample text to encrypt and shift of 4
text = "hello"
shift = 4

encrypted_text = caesar_encrypt(text,shift)
print(f"Encrypted text: {encrypted_text}")

decrypted_text = caesar_decrypt(encrypted_text,shift)
print(f"Decrypted text: {decrypted_text}")
```

This algorithm will shift the characters in the input text by the amount defined by 'shift'. The decryption process simply reverses the operation. This codes works for both lowercase and uppercase letters, with other characters remaining unchanged. 

Do note: In real-life scenarios, this would be an insecure method of encryption and would not be recommended for secure systems. Modern systems use way more advanced methods such as symmetric-key algorithms (AES) or public-key algorithms (RSA).
<|end|>


