Use the NearMiss method to better handle the imbalanced classes problem found in the Customer Churn Prediction dataset obtainable at https://www.kaggle.com/blastchar/telco-customer-churn
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The NearMiss method is a type of under-sampling technique. The NearMiss algorithm selects the majority class samples whose average distances to the k nearest minority class samples are the smallest. It has three versions:

- NearMiss-1 selects samples from the majority class for which the average distances to the nearest 3 samples of the minority class is the smallest.
- NearMiss-2 selects the samples from the majority class for which the average distances to the farthest 3 samples of the minority class are the smallest.
- NearMiss-3 is a 2-level algorithm in which, for each minority sample, their m nearest-neighbors will be kept. Then, the majority samples selected are the on for which the average distance to the nearest N samples of the previously kept samples is the smallest.

We are going to use Python's imbalanced-learn module to perform the NearMiss method.

Here's your Python solution using NearMiss-1:
```python
# Import necessary libraries
import pandas as pd
from imblearn.under_sampling import NearMiss
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split

# Load the dataset
data = pd.read_csv('WA_Fn-UseC_-Telco-Customer-Churn.csv')

# Assume 'Churn' is the target variable
X = data.drop('Churn', axis=1)
y = data['Churn']

# Split the data into train and test sets
X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.3, random_state=0)

# Initialize NearMiss-1
nm = NearMiss(version=1)

# Fit Train Data
X_res, y_res = nm.fit_resample(X_train, y_train)
```
Here, 'X_res' and 'y_res' are the resultant balanced training data. You can now proceed with training your classification model on this balanced data. 

Remember that undersampling techniques like NearMiss should only be applied on the training data and not the test data so as to avoid information leakage. Also, NearMiss can sometimes lead to loss of potentially useful data, as it removes some instances from the majority class.
<|end|>


