<|asst|>
Here is the equivalent code in C#:

```csharp
using System;

public class InvalidModification : Exception
{
    public InvalidModification(string message) : base(message)
    {
    }
}

public class Program
{
    public int SmallestChange(int[] arr, int[] limit)
    {
        int i = 0;
        int j = arr.Length - 1;
        int modification = 0;

        while (i < j)
        {
            if (arr[i] != arr[j])
            {
                if (Math.Min(arr[i], arr[j]) < limit[0] || Math.Max(arr[i], arr[j]) > limit[1])
                {
                    throw new InvalidModification("Modification not possible within the given limit");
                }
                else if (arr[i] < arr[j])
                {
                    arr[i] = arr[j];
                }
                else
                {
                    arr[j] = arr[i];
                }
                modification++;
            }
            i++;
            j--;
        }
        return modification;
    }
}
```
A few notes about the translation:
1. In C#, arrays use the `[]` syntax instead of `arr`.
2. In C#, built-in minimum (`min`) and maximum (`max`) functions are part of the `Math` class in the `System` namespace.
3. C#'s equivalent to Python's `raise` syntax to throw exceptions is the `throw` keyword.
4. I implemented the exception and method as part of a class `Program`, to comply with object-oriented principles in C#. 
5. Unlike Python, C# requires formal declaration of all variables with their datatype.
6. C# uses braces `{}` to signify blocks of code (e.g. method bodies, conditional statements) instead of indentation.
