<|asst|>
You can use `echo -e` with the `sed` command to decode these HTML entities:

```bash
title=$(curl -s $theurl | grep -o "<title>[^<]*" | tail -c+8)
title_decoded=$(echo -e "$(echo $title | sed 's/&#0*//g; s/;//g')")
touch "$title_decoded.txt"
```

However, this technique only converts numeric entities.

A more universal solution would be to use Perl to handle all HTML entities:

```bash
title=$(curl -s $theurl | grep -o "<title>[^<]*" | tail -c+8)
title_decoded=$(echo $title | perl -MHTML::Entities -pe 'decode_entities($_);')
touch "$title_decoded.txt"
```

The Perl method relies on the CPAN module HTML::Entities, so make sure it's installed in your environment. You need to run `sudo cpan HTML::Entities` to install it if it isn't already.

NOTE: The title may still contain characters that are not valid in a filename, so you may want to further clean the title programatically before using it as a filename.
