Render the following in C:
def within_range(lst: list, lower_limit: int, upper_limit: int) -> bool:
    return all(lower_limit <= item <= upper_limit for item in lst)
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's how you could write this function in C. The function will return a boolean value and it takes in an array of integers, the size of the array, and the lower and upper limit.

```c
#include <stdbool.h>

bool within_range(int lst[], int listSize, int lower_limit, int upper_limit) {
    for (int i = 0; i < listSize; i++) {
        if (lst[i] < lower_limit || lst[i] > upper_limit) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}
```
Keep in mind that unlike Python, C does not inherently understand the size of its arrays, so you have to explicitly pass the size of the input array as an argument in the function. This function uses a loop to iterate over all elements in the array. If it finds a number outside the range, it returns false. If it never finds such a number, it returns true.
