Develop an object-oriented JavaScript class architecture dedicated to a Human being which not only archives a moniker and a surname, but also handles complex actions like creating unique id for each human object and updates their age every year automatically. The human class should also contain methods to simulate actions such as eating, sleeping and working, which alter internal states of the object. Moreover, create subclasses such as Worker and Student with additional attributes and methods, to see polymorphism in action.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, here's a basic example of how you might implement such an architecture in Javascript:

```javascript
class Human {
  constructor(name, surname, birthYear) {
    this.id = Math.random().toString(36).substr(2, 9);
    this.name = name;
    this.surname = surname;
    this.birthYear = birthYear;
    this.age = this.calculateAge();
  }
  
  calculateAge() {
    const currentYear = new Date().getFullYear();
    return currentYear - this.birthYear;
  }

  eat() {
    console.log(`${this.name} is eating.`);
  }

  sleep() {
    console.log(`${this.name} is sleeping.`);
  }

  work() {
    console.log(`${this.name} is working.`);
  }
}

class Worker extends Human {
  constructor(name, surname, birthYear, job) {
    super(name, surname, birthYear);
    this.job = job;
  }

  work() {
    console.log(`${this.name} is working as a ${this.job}.`);
  }
}

class Student extends Human {
  constructor(name, surname, birthYear, grade) {
    super(name, surname, birthYear);
    this.grade = grade;
  }

  study() {
    console.log(`${this.name} is studying for grade ${this.grade}.`);
  }
}

// create worker and student
const worker = new Worker('John', 'Doe', 1990, 'developer');
const student = new Student('Jane', 'Doe', 2000, '10th');

// check the polymorpism with work method
worker.work(); // John is working as a developer.
student.work(); // Jane is working.

// use the study method
student.study(); // Jane is studying for grade 10th.
```
In this code, the `Human` class has methods like `eat()`, `sleep()`, and `work()` , and subclasses `Worker` and `Student` inherit these methods. The `work()` method in `Worker` class is overridden to provide a more specific action for a worker.

The unique id for each human object is created in the constructor of Human class by generating a random string. And the age is calculated automatically based on the birth year.

Subclasses extend the Human class but add their own unique methods and properties too. The `Worker` class has the `job` property and a overridden `work()` method while the `Student` class has `grade` property and an additional `study()` method, demonstrating the principle of polymorphism whereby methods in a subclass can have a different implementation than the same methods in the superclass.

Note: This code assumes that it's being run in current year i.e after the birthYear of the human. In case birthYear can be future year, you may want to add validation for birthYear.

Also, the calculateAge() will give the age of human at the start of this year, not exact current age. You may want to change this as per your requirements.
