Here's the context:
I want to code a corona runner, basically a chrome dino imitation in pygame.
For that I want the player to have a circular hitbox.
The image I'm using is square-shaped, but has a transparent background.
So there are 2 possible solutions, that I don't know how to code though:

1. Create a circle, fill the circle with the image, remove the transparent background
2. Create a square, remove the transparent background and make only the non-transparent part of the image be able to collide.


Besides not knowing how to code any of these three, I have the problem that every time I insert the image with the transparent background, the transparent background turns black instead of non-visible.
I tried to add the image and hoped, that the transparent background would disappear automatically, which it didn't.
I tried to turn any of the 2 possible solutions mentioned above into code, but I didn't know how to.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
1. For creating a circular hitbox with a transparent background that is filled with an image, it is almost impossible to do in Pygame. A common way in Pygame to resolve the issue of transparency is to define the transparent areas in an image by using the alpha channel. However, Pygame's collision functions are mostly rectilinear.

2. You could use the `colliderect` and `collidepoint` methods for Pygame Surface objects on images with transparent backgrounds. However, these won't work in a pixel-perfect manner. 

Take a look at the with `pygame.mask.Mask` that offers pixel perfect collision detection. This means you can define your image in a way in which only the visible parts of it cause a collision:

```python
player_image = pygame.image.load('player.png').convert_alpha()
player_mask = pygame.mask.from_surface(player_image)
```

With the above code, we are giving your player a mask based on its alpha channel, meaning that only the non-transparent parts of the image are considered part of the mask and thus only those parts are considered for collision.

Now you load the obstacle image and create a mask in the same way:

```python
obstacle_image = pygame.image.load('obstacle.png').convert_alpha()
obstacle_mask = pygame.mask.from_surface(obstacle_image)
```

Now, to check collision between the player and the obstacle, you can do the following:

```python
offset_x = obstacle_rect.x - player_rect.x
offset_y = obstacle_rect.y - player_rect.y
collision_point = player_mask.overlap(obstacle_mask, (offset_x, offset_y))

if collision_point:
    print("Collision!")
```

Regarding the issue with the transparent background turning black, when you load your image with `pygame.image.load()` make sure you call `convert_alpha()` as well. The alpha channel is responsible for transparency. So, if the image file has an alpha channel, `convert_alpha()` will maintain it.

```python
player_image = pygame.image.load('player.png').convert_alpha()
```

I hope this helps! Happy coding!
