Construct an HTML table that presents the contents of a JSON file as rows and columns. Assume the JSON file has the following format:
{
  "employees": [
    { "designation": "software engineer", "location": "New York", "experience": "5 years" },
    { "designation": "web developer", "location": "San Francisco", "experience": "3 years" }
  ]
}

For simplicity, you may consider the JSON file is named as "data.json", and is available in the same directory as your code file. 

Additionally, you need to add "sort" button headers to each column that, when clicked, sorts the data in the table by that column in ascending order. If the button is clicked again, it should sort the data in descending order. Furthermore, develop a function to filter the table rows based on the experience in years. For instance, when '3' is entered, the table should only display rows where the experience is 3 years or less.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Given the requirements, a simple JavaScript/HTML solution would look something like this:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<body>

<h2> Employee Table</h2>
<input type="text" id="year_input" onkeyup="filterYear()" placeholder="Enter year to filter..">
<table id="employee_table">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th onclick="sortBy('designation')">Designation &#x1F5D8;</th>
      <th onclick="sortBy('location')">Location &#x1F5D8;</th>
      <th onclick="sortBy('experience')">Experience &#x1F5D8;</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody id="table_body">
  </tbody>
</table>

<script>

var jsonData = {
  "employees": [
    { "designation": "software engineer", "location": "New York", "experience": "5 years" },
    { "designation": "web developer", "location": "San Francisco", "experience": "3 years" }
  ]
};

var sortDirection = {
  "designation": true,
  "location": true,
  "experience": true
};

window.onload = function() {
  drawTable(jsonData.employees);
};

function drawTable(data) {
  var tableBody = document.getElementById("table_body");
  tableBody.innerHTML = "";
  for (let i of data) {
    let row = document.createElement('tr');
    row.innerHTML += `<td>${i.designation}</td>`;
    row.innerHTML += `<td>${i.location}</td>`;
    row.innerHTML += `<td>${i.experience}</td>`;
    tableBody.appendChild(row);
  }
}

function sortBy(key) {
  jsonData.employees.sort((a,b) => {
    if (sortDirection[key]){
      return a[key].localeCompare(b[key]);
    } else {
      return b[key].localeCompare(a[key]);
    }
  });
  sortDirection[key] = !sortDirection[key];
  drawTable(jsonData.employees);
}

function filterYear() {
  var inputValue = document.getElementById("year_input").value;
  var filteredData = jsonData.employees.filter(v => Number(v.experience.split(" ")[0]) <= inputValue);
  drawTable(filteredData);
}

</script>

</body>
</html>
```

In this solution, the JSON data is used to populate a table in the HTML document. Clicking on any column header invokes the `sortBy` function that sorts the data based on the key (column name) and redraws the table.

The filter function retrieves the value from the input field, filters the JSON data where experience is equal to or less than the input number, draws the filtered data to the table. The number of years is extracted from the JSON data using String's `split` function and converted to a number using Number constructor.
<|end|>


