Develop a JavaScript object that performs multiple distinctive operations based on a series of complex provided conditional circumstances. The object should efficiently manage memory and should incorporate error handling for unforeseen input parameters. This must be done without the use of JavaScript's built-in eval function or any third-party libraries.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Given your requirements, here is a simplified example of a JavaScript object that can perform multiple operations based on provided conditions. This object efficiently manages memory and includes error handling for unanticipated input parameters:

```javascript
var customOperations = {
  add: function(a, b) {
    this._validateNumbers(a, b);
    return a + b;
  },
  subtract: function(a, b) {
    this._validateNumbers(a, b);
    return a - b;
  },
  multiply: function(a, b) {
    this._validateNumbers(a, b);
    return a * b;
  },
  divide: function(a, b) {
    this._validateNumbers(a, b);
    if (b === 0) throw new Error("Cannot divide by zero");
    return a / b;
  },
  _validateNumbers: function() {
    for (var i = 0; i < arguments.length; i++) {
      if (typeof arguments[i] !== 'number') {
        throw new Error('All input values must be numbers');
      }
    }
  }
};
```

You would use it like this:

```javascript
console.log(customOperations.add(1, 2)); // 3
console.log(customOperations.subtract(10, 5)); // 5
console.log(customOperations.multiply(3, 3)); // 9
console.log(customOperations.divide(10, 2)); // 5
```

If you provide an incorrect input parameter, an error is thrown:

```javascript
console.log(customOperations.add('one', 2)); // Error: All input values must be numbers
console.log(customOperations.divide(10, 0)); // Error: Cannot divide by zero
```

This object efficiently manages memory because it does not create unnecessary objects (with the exception of error objects in the case of invalid input). It also uses a private function `_validateNumbers` (denoted by the underscore prefix) to avoid repeating the same validation logic in every method.
