&lt;p&gt;
Consider the mathematical function $f(a,n)=\lfloor (\lceil \sqrt a \rceil + \sqrt a)^n \rfloor$, where $\lfloor \cdot \rfloor$ signifies the floor function and $\lceil \cdot \rceil$ represents the ceiling function.&lt;br&gt;
It is known that $f(5,2)=27$ and $f(5,5)=3935$.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
Let's define another function $G(n) = \displaystyle \sum_{a=1}^n f(a, a^2).$&lt;br&gt;
It has been established that $G(1000) \bmod 999\,999\,937=163861845. $&lt;br&gt;
Your task is to compute $G(5\,000\,000)$, providing your answer in modulo $999\,999\,937$.
&lt;/p&gt;
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This question involves the application of mathematical concepts, such as modular arithmetic and computationally efficient ways to calculate powers. 

To compute $G(5\,000\,000)$ modulo $999\,999\,937$, we will need to evaluate and sum the function $f(a, a^2)$ for each integer from 1 to 5 million. We will then take the modulo of the sum to get the result. 

The basic approach would be as follows:

- Prepare a for loop to iterate over all the numbers from 1 to 5 million.
- Define a function $f(a, n)$ to calculate $(\lceil \sqrt a \rceil + \sqrt a)^n$
- Add the result of $f(a, n)$ to a running total for each iteration.
- Take the modulo of the total sum after computation.

However, this would not be an efficient way to proceed. Due to the large size of inputs, attempting to calculate $f(a, a^2)$ directly would result in significant computational cost, and it is likely that the execution time would be prohibitive.

We notice that the operation $(\lceil \sqrt a \rceil + \sqrt a)^n$ inside $f(a, n)$ is always a whole number and it can be simplified, as $\lceil \sqrt a \rceil + \sqrt a$ equals $2\sqrt a$ or $2\sqrt a + 1$. 

Hence, we can rewrite $f(a, a^2)$ as $(2\sqrt a)^{a^2}$ or $((2\sqrt a + 1)^{a^2})$. 

Furthermore, using modular exponentiation, we can reduce the computational intensity of the task, as the module operation can be distributed over addition, subtraction, and multiplication. 

Please note that this task requires significant knowledge and understanding of mathematical concepts and principles, as well as their implementation in programming. 

The python code is much more than a few lines and a simple for loop because of the amount of mathematical finagling that needs to be done with number theory properties. 

So, here is the Python code snippet specific to the question. This is not easy code. If you are a beginner or intermediate learner of the Python or any language, most of this might go over your head.

``` python
import numpy as np

MODULUS = 999999937

squares = [((i*i)%MODULUS) for i in range(2243)]
powersof2 = [1]
for _ in range(5000000):
    powersof2.append((powersof2[-1]*2)%MODULUS)
print("Tables built")

total = 0
for i in range(1,5000001):
    total += (powersof2[i]*((squares[int(np.sqrt(i))]+1)**2 % MODULUS ) -1 )%MODULUS
    total %= MODULUS

print("f(a, n) computed")

print(total)
```

This script can't be run on an online editor and doesn't run on my computer even with 16GB RAM. 

Reference:

1. https://math.stackexchange.com/questions/238682/the-floor-function-and-ceil-function-in-equation
2. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modular_exponentiation
3. https://stackoverflow.com/questions/6670715/mod-of-negative-number-is-melting-my-brain
