A sequence of numbers is generated by perpetually summing the squares of the digits in a number to yield a new number, until a previously encountered number reappears. For instance,
\begin{align}
&amp;44 \to 32 \to 13 \to 10 \to \mathbf 1 \to \mathbf 1\\
&amp;85 \to \mathbf{89} \to 145 \to 42 \to 20 \to 4 \to 16 \to 37 \to 58 \to \mathbf{89}
\end{align}
Consequently, any sequence that reaches $1$ or $89$ will be trapped in an infinite cycle. The most astonishing fact is that ALL initial numbers will ultimately lead to $1$ or $89$. Can you determine the quantity of initial numbers less than ten million that will culminate at $89$?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To compute this, we realize that only the digits of the number are important, not the order they're written in, so all anagrams of a number end up in the same place. So we only need to analyze the numbers from 0 to 9,999,999, which have ten million possible anagrams. Since we have to handle each number only once and not every one of its anagrams separately, this drastically reduces the required computation.

The Python code for this would then be:

```python
COUNTS = [0]*10 + [1]*80  # Stores previously computed results
for i in range(10, 568):   # Only checking for numbers from 10 to 567, because the sum of squares of a 7-digit number barely exceeds 567.
    x = i
    while x != 89 and x != 1:
        x = sum(int(i)**2 for i in str(x)) # Generate the next number
    COUNTS.append(COUNTS[x])
    
from math import factorial
from collections import Counter

output = sum(COUNTS[sum(int(i)**2 for i in str(i))] * factorial(7) // factorial(7 - len(str(i))) // 
            np.product([factorial(i) for i in Counter(str(i)).values()]) for i in range(1, 10000000))
print(output)
```
This program first fills the COUNTS array: for each i from 0 to 567, if it's less than 10 it's 0, if it's greater it's computed as described above. Then it tests every number from 1 to 1e7, counting the ones that end up in 89, and prints the total count.
The code also makes use of Counter to count the occurrences of each digit in the number. This is necessary to correctly calculate the number of distinct anagrams of the number.

This approach ensures that the overall time complexity of the program is O(n), which lets it handle large inputs efficiently. The space complexity of the program is also O(n), since we need to store the check results for all numbers up to n.
<|end|>


