> 
  [How can I develop for iPhone using a Windows development machine?](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/22358/how-can-i-develop-for-iphone-using-a-windows-development-machine)  



Is there a way to develop iPhone (iOS) applications on Windows?  I really don't want to get yet another machine.  

There is a project on [http://code.google.com/p/winchain/wiki/HowToUse](http://code.google.com/p/winchain/wiki/HowToUse) that seemed to work with iPhone 1.0, but had limited success with iPhone 2.0, plus it requires all the [Cygwin](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cygwin) insanity.  

Is there anything else, or do I have to buy a Mac?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The official way to develop iOS applications requires using Xcode, which only runs on a Mac. However, there are some alternative ways you can use to develop iOS applications on Windows. 

1. Cross-Platform Tools: Cross-platform software development tools can be used to compile the same code into multiple platforms, like iOS, Android, Windows Mobile, etc. Some examples include Xamarin (uses C#), PhoneGap (uses JavaScript), Appcelerator Titanium (uses JavaScript).

2. Use Virtual Machine: Using VirtualBox or similar software, you can run the macOS on your Windows machine. In this virtual Mac environment, you can install Xcode and develop your app. Be aware that this might require a quite powerful machine and it's not officially supported by Apple.

3. Use a Mac remotely: If you have access to a Mac but don't want to use it as your main machine, consider using a service like MacStadium, which rents Macs in the cloud.

4. React Native: It is also possible to write apps with React Native, which allows you to code in Javascript and compile down to native code.

5. Flutter: Similar to React Native, Flutter, developed by Google, also allows you to write apps in a single language (Dart in this case) and compile down to native code for multiple platforms.

6. Use an online code editor: Online IDEs like Repl.it, Appery.io, or CodeAnywhere enable you to write code from a browser.

Remember, while the methods listed above enable you to develop an app without a Mac, you still need a Mac to submit your app to the Apple App Store.
<|end|>


