Complete the following code:
import java.util.*;
import java.lang.*;

class Solution {
    /**
    Return the greatest common divisor of two integers a and b.
    Additionally, the function should handle multiple inputs, not just two - i.e. it should work for any number of integers (greater than two), returning the greatest common divisor of all of them.
    Ensure that your function is optimized for efficiency.
    >>> greatestCommonDivisor(3, 5, 9)
    1
    >>> greatestCommonDivisor(25, 15, 45)
    5
     */
    public int greatestCommonDivisor(int ...a) {
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is the completion of your code:

```java
import java.lang.*;

class Solution {
    public int greatestCommonDivisor(int ...a) {
        int result = a[0];
        
        for (int i = 1; i<a.length; i++) {
            result = gcd(result, a[i]);
        }

        return result;
    }
    
    // Helper method: usual method of finding GCD using Euclidean algorithm.
    private int gcd(int a, int b) {
        if (b == 0) {
            return a;
        }
        return gcd(b, a % b);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Solution obj = new Solution();
        System.out.println(obj.greatestCommonDivisor(3, 5, 9)); // Output: 1
        System.out.println(obj.greatestCommonDivisor(25, 15, 45)); // Output: 5
    }
}
```

This function works by first setting the result to the first number in the array. Then, it iteratively calculates the GCD of the current result and the next number, storing the result back into `result`. Once it has run through all the numbers in the array, it returns the remaining value in `result` which is the GCD of all input numbers. It finds the GCD of two numbers using the Euclidean algorithm, which is a very efficient algorithm for finding the GCD. Thus, this function should run efficiently, even for fairly large input arrays.
<|end|>


