In studying skew I've been advised to focus on understanding on components that affect it.  One such component that's been recommended to me is the relationship btwn RV-IV and realized skew.  Allegedly, there should be a strong correlation btwn the 2 (skew steepens when RV exceeds implied), but when I regress one on the other, I find no such relationship.  Am I wasting my time?
I also cannot think of some fundamental theory that might explain the relationship.  What would be the theoretical underpinning for why this happens?  Hedgers buying tails?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The relationship between Realized Volatility (RV), Implied Volatility (IV) and skew can be complex to understand at the beginning. The supposed correlation suggested that when the Realized Volatility is higher than the Implied Volatility (RV > IV), then there is an increase in skewness, which means the distribution of returns is moving away from the mean or normal distribution.

In theory, a difference between RV and IV might signal something about the future path of the skew. If RV is higher than IV, it could suggest that the underlying is moving more than what the options market has priced in. This could be caused by hedgers buying out-of-the-money options as an insurance policy against big moves in the underlying, which in turn would steepen the skew. 

However, in practice, the relationship may not be straightforward due to several market factors such as liquidity, market microstructure, demand and supply imbalances, market sentiment, etc. Empirically proving this relationship could require a long time series of high-quality data and a well-specified econometric model.

It’s also worth considering that options skew itself is a complex metric and can be influenced by many other factors such as the level of volatility, market direction, time to expiry, etc. 

So while understanding these concepts are crucial for those trading and risk managing options, there may not be a direct and simple relationship among them. It is not necessarily a waste of time to study these components but it would important to recognize the limitations and complexities involved in their interactions.

It may be beneficial to seek further academic resources or consult with practitioners in the field for more insights into these concepts and their practical applications.
<|end|>


