Create a React Native application for tracking diet goals. The application should include a screen with a form for adding new diet goals and a list of existing diet goals.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Setting up a diet tracking app requires a fundamental understanding of JavaScript and React Native. Below is an overview of the app structure:

1. Install the necessary packages:

```
npm install @react-navigation/native @react-navigation/stack react-native-reanimated react-native-gesture-handler react-native-screens react-native-safe-area-context @react-native-community/masked-view
```

2. Set up two main files: App.js (which acts as the app navigator) and screens/DietGoals.js (which represents the diet goals' screen):

**App.js**

```js
import 'react-native-gesture-handler';
import React from 'react';
import {NavigationContainer} from '@react-navigation/native';
import {createStackNavigator} from '@react-navigation/stack';
import DietGoalsScreen from './screens/DietGoals';

const Stack = createStackNavigator();

function App() {
  return (
    <NavigationContainer>
      <Stack.Navigator initialRouteName="DietGoals">
        <Stack.Screen name="DietGoals" component={DietGoalsScreen} />
      </Stack.Navigator>
    </NavigationContainer>
  );
}

export default App;
```

**screens/DietGoals.js**

```js
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import { Button, StyleSheet, Text, View, TextInput, FlatList } from 'react-native';

const DietGoalsScreen = () => {
  const [goals, setGoals] = useState([]);
  const [newGoal, setNewGoal] = useState('');

  const goalInputHandler = (text) => {
    setNewGoal(text);
  };

  const addGoalHandler = () => {
    setGoals(currentGoals => [...currentGoals, newGoal]);
    setNewGoal('');
  };

  return (
    <View style={styles.screen}>
      <View style={styles.inputContainer}>
        <TextInput 
          placeholder="New Diet Goal..."
          style={styles.inputField}
          onChangeText={goalInputHandler}
          value={newGoal}
        />
        <Button title="Add" onPress={addGoalHandler} />
      </View>
      <FlatList 
        data={goals} 
        keyExtractor={(item, index) => index.toString()} 
        renderItem={itemData => <Text style={styles.goalItem}>{itemData.item}</Text>} 
      />
    </View>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  screen: {
    padding: 50
  },

  inputContainer: {
    flexDirection: 'row', 
    justifyContent: 'space-between', 
    alignItems: 'center'
  },

  inputField: {
    width: '80%', 
    borderBottomColor: 'black', 
    borderWidth: 1, 
    padding: 10
  },

  goalItem: {
    padding: 10,
    backgroundColor: '#ccc',
    borderColor: 'black',
    borderWidth: 1,
    marginTop: 10,
  }
});

export default DietGoalsScreen;
```

These are basic components, and there are more options you could consider adding to your app. Always remember to run `npm install` in your project directory to ensure all used packages are installed.

Also, you need to be aware that the above are just basic screens. To make the app advanced and interactive, you would need to integrate Redux for state management, Formik/Yup for handling form submission/validations and animations for user interaction.

Additionally, you could also integrate your app with a database to store the diet plans instead of storing them in a state. You could choose Firebase Firestore or any other NoSQL database for this purpose. Your choice highly depends on your needs and how robust you want your app to be.
<|end|>


