As for the offline authentication process, it's quite a broad topic and could be its own separate discussion, and greatly depends on your specific use case, your user authentication model, and client-server model. One possible solution might be storing hashed passwords on the client side, then comparing the hash of the entered password with the stored one.

Additionally, you will also need a service worker for your Progressive Web Application to function properly. You would use this to cache the static files required for your website, as well as to provide offline functionality.

Indeed, all these examples are only proofs of concept. Always, when dealing with real user data, it’s essential to apply all the necessary measures to secure your user’s data.

Remember, to fully implement this feature, you will need to do some complex server-side coding which includes setup of databases and connection between the front end and the back end of the application. Typically, a back end language like PHP, Node.js, or Python would be used.
