How do I get Perl to read the contents of a given directory into an array?

[Backticks](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grave_accent#Use_in_programming) can do it, but is there some method using 'scandir' or a similar term?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In Perl, you often use `opendir`, `readdir` and `closedir` to get the contents of a directory into an array. This method doesn't require executing shell commands like `scandir` and it can work across different operating systems, such as Unix, Linux and Windows. Here's a simple perl script to read a directory's contents into an array:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl
use strict;
use warnings;

my $dir = '/path/to/your/directory';  # specify directory here
opendir(my $dh, $dir) or die "Could not open '$dir' for reading: $!\n";
my @files = readdir $dh;
closedir $dh;

print "$_\n" foreach @files;
```

This will loop through all the files and directories in the specified directory and print each one on a separate line. The files are just strings in the `@files` array. If you want to do something more complicated with them apart from printing, you can modify the loop block.

Please replace `'/path/to/your/directory'` with your actual directory. This script includes `.` and `..`, which are aliases for the current directory and the parent directory. If you want to exclude them, you can use a grep function like so:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl
use strict;
use warnings;

my $dir = '/path/to/your/directory';  # specify directory here
opendir(my $dh, $dir) or die "Could not open '$dir' for reading: $!\n";
my @files = grep {!/^\.{1,2}$/} readdir $dh;
closedir $dh;

print "$_\n" foreach @files;
```

This version of the script will not include `.` and `..` in the list of files.
<|end|>


