Write a C++ program to generate Fibonacci numbers up to 100.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a simple C++ program to generate Fibonacci numbers up to 100:
```cpp
#include<iostream>

void generateFibNumbers(int limit) {
   int a = 0;
   int b = 1;
   int fib = 0;
   
   if(limit == 0){
     std::cout<< a<<std::endl; 
   }
   else{
     std::cout<< a << " "<< b << " "; // printing 0 and 1
     while (1) {
       fib = a + b;
       a = b;
       b = fib;
       if(fib > limit)
         break;
       std::cout << fib << " ";
     }
     std::cout << std::endl;
   }     
}

int main() {
   int limit = 100;
   std::cout << "Fibonacci numbers up to " << limit << " are: \n";
   generateFibNumbers(limit);
   return 0;
}
```
This program begins with the first two Fibonacci numbers 0 and 1. It continuously adds the last two numbers and updates the Fibonacci number. The process stops as soon as the Fibonacci number becomes greater than the limit (100, in this case). If the limit is 0, it directly prints 0, as that's the only Fibonacci number in the range. For any other number, it begins by printing 0 and 1, since they are always the two starting numbers in the Fibonacci sequence.
<|end|>


