change this code to its c++ equivalent:
from functools import lru_cache

@lru_cache(maxsize=None)
def fibfib(n: int):
    """
    The FibFib sequence mirrors the Fibonacci sequence, as defined below:
    fibfib(0) == 0
    fibfib(1) == 0
    fibfib(2) == 1
    fibfib(n) == fibfib(n-1) + fibfib(n-2) + fibfib(n-3) for n > 2
    fibfib(n) == fibfib(n+3) - fibfib(n+2) - fibfib(n+1) for n < 0

    Implement dynamic programming and memoization for function optimization.
    """
    if n == 0:
        return 0
    elif n == 1:
        return 0
    elif n == 2:
        return 1
    elif n > 2:
        return fibfib(n-1) + fibfib(n-2) + fibfib(n-3)
    elif n < 0:
        return fibfib(n+3) - fibfib(n+2) - fibfib(n+1)
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is the C++ code equivalent for your Python function:

```c++
#include <unordered_map>

std::unordered_map<int,long long> m;

long long fibfib(int n) {
    /**
     The FibFib sequence mirrors the Fibonacci sequence, as defined below:
     fibfib(0) == 0
     fibfib(1) == 0
     fibfib(2) == 1
     fibfib(n) == fibfib(n-1) + fibfib(n-2) + fibfib(n-3) for n > 2
     fibfib(n) == fibfib(n+3) - fibfib(n+2) - fibfib(n+1) for n < 0

     Implement dynamic programming and memoization for function optimization.
     */
    if(m.count(n))
        return m[n];
    if (n == 0)
        return m[n] = 0;
    else if (n == 1)
        return m[n] = 0;
    else if (n == 2)
        return m[n] = 1;
    else if (n > 2)
        return m[n] = fibfib(n-1) + fibfib(n-2) + fibfib(n-3);
    else if (n < 0)
        return m[n] = fibfib(n+3) - fibfib(n+2) - fibfib(n+1);
    return 0;
}
```

The C++ function also calculates the values of fibfib sequence. It leverage dynamic programming, but instead of using lru_cache decorator like Python, it uses the built in standard template library (unordered_map) for memoization to optimize the calculation. This hash map (unordered_map) stores the already calculated values of the fibfib function, which helps in avoiding the repetitive calculations and thus optimizes the function. If a fibfib value for a particular number 'n' is already calculated and stored in the hash map, the function just returns the stored value instead of recalculating it.
<|end|>


