Devise a nested decrementing loop in Python that begins at integer 20, with the inner loop starting at the current count of the outer loop and both ending at integer 1.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a simple Python script to accomplish this:

```python
for i in range(20, 0, -1):
    for j in range(i, 0, -1):
        print(j)
```

Here, `range(20, 0, -1)` initiates a loop that starts at 20 and decrements down to 1 (--thereby including 1). This represents the outer loop.

Within this loop, for each iteration of `i`, we also iterate over the same range with `j`, this time starting at the current value of `i`, not always at 20. 

For example, on the first pass, `i` begins at 20, so `j` will decrement from 20 to 1. On the next pass, `i` will be 19, so `j` will decrement from 19 to 1. This continues down until `i` and `j` are both 1. 

The `print(j)` statement simply outputs the current value of `j`, creating a visible count.
<|end|>


