Here is a simple program in Java with BufferedReader and InputStreamReader that reads input, validates it for non-empty input and handles possible errors. However, console input is limited and does not fully support advanced inputs such as emojis and non-Roman characters as well. These are usually handled well in GUI-based applications. If you're using advanced development frameworks, you might be able to find libraries that assist with this:

```Java
import java.io.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in, "UTF-8"));
            
            System.out.println("Please enter your data: ");
            String input = reader.readLine();

            if (input == null || input.trim().isEmpty()) {
                System.out.println("Input is not valid");
            } else {
                System.out.printf("You entered: %s\n", input);
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.out.println("An error occurred while reading your input. Please try again.");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("An unexpected error occurred.");
        }
    }
}
```
This is a simple demonstration and might not cover all possible cases of input handling. In a real application setting, you would also want to sanitize input to prevent injection attacks, handle multi-line input, handle end of input (ctrl+d, ctrl+z), etc. Depending upon specific requirements, regular expressions might be used for input validation. In advanced cases where UI is involved, input handling would be more sophisticated.

Moreover, the program assumes that console supports UTF-8 encoding. If encoding is not supported, emojis or non-roman characters might not be displayed/processed correctly. 

Also, the program does not check for special inputs like emojis and non-Roman characters specifically, as the check would heavily depend on your specific definition of what inputs are 'legitimate'.
