Construct an HTML code snippet that not only forms a responsive navigational interface, but does so by encompassing three separate menu choices specifically identified as 'Home', 'About', and 'Contact', assuring that it's adaptable to varying viewport and screen sizes. To add another layer of complexity, ensure this navigation system incorporates appropriate semantic HTML5 elements for enhanced usability and accessibility.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Solution:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <style>
        body {
            margin: 0;
            font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
        }

        .header {
            overflow: hidden;
            background-color: #f1f1f1;
            padding: 20px 10px;
        }

        .header a {
            float: left;
            color: black;
            text-align: center;
            padding: 12px;
            text-decoration: none;
            font-size: 18px; 
            line-height: 25px;
            border-radius: 4px;
        }

        .header a.logo {
            font-size: 25px;
            font-weight: bold;
        }

        .header a:hover {
            background-color: #ddd;
            color: black;
        }

        .header a.active {
            background-color: dodgerblue;
            color: white;
        }

        @media screen and (max-width: 500px) {
            .header a {
                float: none;
                display: block;
                text-align: left;
            }
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="header" role="navigation">
    <a href="#" class="logo">Logo</a>
    <nav>
        <a href="#home" class="active">Home</a>
        <a href="#about">About</a>
        <a href="#contact">Contact</a>
    </nav>
</div>

</body>
</html>
```

This code snippet creates a responsive navigation bar with three links: 'Home', 'About', and 'Contact'. When the screen's width is less than 500 pixels, the links will stack on top of each other instead of side by side. The 'nav' tag is used here as it is a semantic HTML5 element that enhances usability and accessibility by indicating that its content is a navigation section.
<|end|>


