I am faced with the following challenge: Construct a Single-Tab Browser History
The details are as follows: You are given a single-tab browser where you commence on the `homepage` and you have the ability to navigate to another `url`, retreat in the history by a certain number of `steps`, or advance in the history by a certain number of `steps`.

You are to construct the `BrowserHistory` class:
`BrowserHistory(string homepage)` This initializes the object with the `homepage` of the browser.

`void visit(string url)` This function allows you to navigate to `url` from the current page. It erases all the forward history.

`string back(int steps)` This function allows you to retreat `steps` back in history. If you can only return `x` steps in the history and `steps &gt; x`, you will return only `x` steps. It returns the current `url` after retreating in history at most `steps`.

`string forward(int steps)` This function allows you to advance `steps` forward in history. If you can only advance `x` steps in the history and `steps &gt; x`, you will advance only `x` steps. It returns the current `url` after advancing in history at most `steps`.

Example:
Input:
[&#34;BrowserHistory&#34;,&#34;visit&#34;,&#34;visit&#34;,&#34;visit&#34;,&#34;back&#34;,&#34;back&#34;,&#34;forward&#34;,&#34;visit&#34;,&#34;forward&#34;,&#34;back&#34;,&#34;back&#34;]
[[&#34;leetcode.com&#34;],[&#34;google.com&#34;],[&#34;facebook.com&#34;],[&#34;youtube.com&#34;],[1],[1],[1],[&#34;linkedin.com&#34;],[2],[2],[7]]
Output:
[null,null,null,null,&#34;facebook.com&#34;,&#34;google.com&#34;,&#34;facebook.com&#34;,null,&#34;linkedin.com&#34;,&#34;google.com&#34;,&#34;leetcode.com&#34;]
Explanation:
BrowserHistory browserHistory = new BrowserHistory(&#34;leetcode.com&#34;);
browserHistory.visit(&#34;google.com&#34;);       // You are in &#34;leetcode.com&#34;. Navigate to &#34;google.com&#34;
browserHistory.visit(&#34;facebook.com&#34;);     // You are in &#34;google.com&#34;. Navigate to &#34;facebook.com&#34;
browserHistory.visit(&#34;youtube.com&#34;);      // You are in &#34;facebook.com&#34;. Navigate to &#34;youtube.com&#34;
browserHistory.back(1);                   // You are in &#34;youtube.com&#34;, retreat to &#34;facebook.com&#34; return &#34;facebook.com&#34;
browserHistory.back(1);                   // You are in &#34;facebook.com&#34;, retreat to &#34;google.com&#34; return &#34;google.com&#34;
browserHistory.forward(1);                // You are in &#34;google.com&#34;, advance to &#34;facebook.com&#34; return &#34;facebook.com&#34;
browserHistory.visit(&#34;linkedin.com&#34;);     // You are in &#34;facebook.com&#34;. Navigate to &#34;linkedin.com&#34;
browserHistory.forward(2);                // You are in &#34;linkedin.com&#34;, you cannot advance any steps.

browserHistory.back(2);                   // You are in &#34;linkedin.com&#34;, retreat two steps to &#34;facebook.com&#34; then to &#34;google.com&#34;. return &#34;google.com&#34;
browserHistory.back(7);                   // You are in &#34;google.com&#34;, you can retreat only one step to &#34;leetcode.com&#34;. return &#34;leetcode.com&#34;

Constraints:
`1 &lt;= homepage.length &lt;= 20`
`1 &lt;= url.length &lt;= 20`
`1 &lt;= steps &lt;= 100`
`homepage` and `url` are composed of  &#39;.&#39; or lower case English letters.

A maximum of `5000` calls will be made to `visit`, `back`, and `forward`.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a Python solution for your task, it uses a list to keep track of the history and two pointers to mark the current page and the forward-most page in the history.

```Python
class BrowserHistory:
    def __init__(self, homepage: str):
        self.history = [homepage]
        self.curr_page = 0  # pointer to denote the current page
        self.max_page = 0   # pointer to denote the maximum forward page possible
       
    def visit(self, url: str) -> None:
        self.curr_page += 1
        if self.curr_page < len(self.history):
            self.history[self.curr_page] = url
        else:
            self.history.append(url)
        self.max_page = self.curr_page
        
    def back(self, steps: int) -> str:
        self.curr_page = max(self.curr_page - steps, 0)
        return self.history[self.curr_page]
        
    def forward(self, steps: int) -> str:
        self.curr_page = min(self.curr_page + steps, self.max_page)
        return self.history[self.curr_page]
```

In this class creation, the __init__ method initializes the status of the browser history. Then the `visit` method updates the current page and erases all the forward history. `Back` and `forward` methods move the current page pointer within the range of history list and return the current URL.

Each of the browser commands operates in O(1) time, given a reasonable maximum constraint on the history size.
<|end|>


