Write a JavaScript program to create a class 'Shape' that can contain different types of shapes like 'circle', 'rectangle', and 'triangle'. Each shape should have a method to calculate its area. Instantiate objects from each class and use their methods to display the area. Implement error handling for cases when incorrect values are provided for dimensions like negative values or non-numeric inputs.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In this JavaScript solution, a parent `Shape` class is created which contains common characteristics of all shapes. Then, separate classes for `Circle`, `Rectangle`, and `Triangle` are created which extend the `Shape` class. 

The `calculateArea()` method is overridden in each class to provide the correct calculation for each shape. The error handling has also been implemented in the constructor of each class to validate inputs.

```javascript
class Shape {
  constructor(name) {
    if (this.constructor === Shape) {
      throw new Error("Abstract class Shape cannot be instantiated directly.");
    }
    this.name = name;
  }

  calculateArea() {
    throw new Error("Method 'calculateArea()' must be implemented.");
  }
}

class Circle extends Shape {
  constructor(radius) {
    super("Circle");
    if (isNaN(radius) || radius <= 0) {
      throw new Error("Invalid input for radius.");
    }
    this.radius = radius;
  }

  calculateArea() {
    return Math.PI * this.radius * this.radius;
  }
}

class Rectangle extends Shape {
  constructor(length, width) {
    super("Rectangle");
    if (isNaN(length) || length <= 0 || isNaN(width) || width <= 0) {
      throw new Error("Invalid input for length or width.");
    }
    this.length = length;
    this.width = width;
  }

  calculateArea() {
    return this.length * this.width;
  }
}

class Triangle extends Shape {
  constructor(base, height) {
    super("Triangle");
    if (isNaN(base) || base <= 0 || isNaN(height) || height <= 0) {
      throw new Error("Invalid input for base or height.");
    }
    this.base = base;
    this.height = height;
  }

  calculateArea() {
    return 0.5 * this.base * this.height;
  }
}

// instantiation
try {
  let circle = new Circle(5);
  console.log(circle.calculateArea());

  let rectangle = new Rectangle(4, 5);
  console.log(rectangle.calculateArea());

  let triangle = new Triangle(6, 7);
  console.log(triangle.calculateArea());
} catch (error) {
  console.error(error.message);
}
```
In this code, instantiation and method calls are wrapped in a try-catch block. If any error gets thrown, it will be caught in the `catch` block and the error message will be displayed at the console.
<|end|>


