a = np.ones((41,12))
Could you guide me on how to zero pad this specific array to align with the shape of (93,13)? Furthermore, how can I scale this process to accommodate thousands of rows? I am particularly interested in padding the array equally on the left and right, as well as the top and bottom. If an equal distribution is not possible, I would prefer to allocate the remaining row/column to the bottom/right. For instance, transforming [[1]] into [[0,0,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,0]].
A:
&lt;code&gt;
import numpy as np
a = np.ones((41, 12))
shape = (93, 13)
&lt;/code&gt;
result = ... # insert solution here
BEGIN SOLUTION
&lt;code&gt;
<|end|>
<|asst|>
# Calculate the padding required on each side
pad_top = (shape[0] - a.shape[0]) // 2
pad_bottom = shape[0] - a.shape[0] - pad_top
pad_left = (shape[1] - a.shape[1]) // 2
pad_right = shape[1] - a.shape[1] - pad_left

# Apply padding
result = np.pad(a, ((pad_top, pad_bottom), (pad_left, pad_right)))
&lt;/code&gt;
To scale this up for thousands of arrays, loop through each array and apply the padding process accordingly. This can be accomplished in parallel due to the independent nature of the task.
