<|asst|>
Creating such an application using ReactJS requires expertise in Node.js, ReactJS, and possibly a database technology such as MongoDB. Here's a basic outline of how you might make the app:

First, make sure that you have Node.js and npm installed in your system. If you don't, you may download it from here https://nodejs.org/. After installing, you can verify it by using commands `node -v` & `npm -v` in your terminal.

1. Install the create-react-app package using npm (This package helps in setting up a new ReactJS project).

```shell
npx create-react-app expense-tracker
```

2. Go inside your project directory.

```shell
cd expense-tracker
```

3. Now, create a new component `ExpenseItem.js` inside src directory and this component will display the individual expense item.

```jsx
import React from 'react';

const ExpenseItem = (props) => {
  return (
    <div>
      <p>{props.date}</p>
      <p>{props.category}</p>
      <p>{props.amount}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default ExpenseItem;
```

4. Now, create a new component `ExpenseList.js` that will render the list of expense items.

```jsx
import React from 'react';
import ExpenseItem from './ExpenseItem';

const ExpenseList = (props) => {
  return (
    <div>
      {props.items.map(item => 
        <ExpenseItem 
          key={item.id}
          date={item.date}
          category={item.category}
          amount={item.amount} 
        />
      )}
    </div>
  );
};

export default ExpenseList;
```

5. Now, inside `App.js`, we will use our `ExpenseList` component. For simplicity, we are storing expenses in a state variable.

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import ExpenseList from './ExpenseList';

function App() {
  const [expenses, setExpenses] = useState([
    { id: 'e1', date: '2021-07-01', category: 'Food', amount: '$50' },
    // ... more data
  ]);

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Expense Tracker</h2>
      <ExpenseList items={expenses} />
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

6. Now, if the app needs to handle adding new expenses, filtering expenses by date or category etc, you’d likely want to add more components and state hooks. Consider using something like Redux or Context API for state management.

7. For persistent data storage, you might want to use a database. This could be either a traditional SQL database like PostgreSQL, or a NoSQL database like MongoDB. 

8. For user authentication, consider using something like JWT or OAuth.

This is a very basic outline of what your app might look like. Creating a full-featured expense tracking app with ReactJS would be a significant undertaking and would require careful design and planning.
