Sure! Here's how you could do it in PHP:

```php
<?php
function choose_num($x, $y, $z, $w, $m) {
    // Create an array to store the found numbers
    $numbers_found = array();

    // Iterate over the range, from y down to x (inclusive), in steps of -1
    for ($num = $y; $num >= $x; $num--) {
        // Check whether the number is even, and whether it is divisible by z and w
        if ($num % 2 == 0 && $num % $z == 0 && $num % $w == 0) {
            // If so, add the number to our array
            array_push($numbers_found, $num);

            // If we have found m numbers, return the last one found
            if (count($numbers_found) == $m) {
                return $numbers_found[count($numbers_found) - 1];
            }
        }
    }

    // If no number has been found or m is larger than the 
    // count of such numbers, return -1
    return -1;
}
?>
```

The PHP version of the function operates in the exact same way as the Python version. The only differences are the changes in syntax between the two languages. The "for" loop syntax in PHP is a bit more traditional, and arrays are created and handled differently than in Python. 

The "array_push()" function is used to append elements on an existing array and "count()" function is used to find the number of elements in an array. The "-1" indexing that python does to get the last element of an array can be emulated in PHP using "count($someArray) - 1". In PHP, comparisons are made using '==' or '===', and logical operators are words ("and", "or") rather than symbols ("&&", "||").
<|end|>


