I try to externalize all strings (and other constants) used in any application I write, for many reasons that are probably second-nature to most stack-overflowers, but one thing I would like to have is the ability to automate spell checking of any user-visible strings.  This poses a couple problems:

- - - 

How do you incorporate something like this into your build procedures/test suites?  It is not feasible to have someone manually spell check all the strings in an application each time they are changed -- and there is  that they will all be spelled correctly the first time.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Integrating automated spell check into your application workflow in an automated way can be achieved using a variety of tools and techniques. Here are some general steps on how you can do this:

1. Extract all user-facing strings: If you have all your user-visible strings externalized in a single location such as properties file or JSON file, this step is already done for you. If not, you might want to consider doing so.

2. Spell Check the Extracted Strings: There are several software tools you can use to run spell checks on your extracted strings:

   - For command-line tools, you can use codespell, Aspell, Hunspell etc. These tools can be integrated into your version control system (like git pre-commit hook) or Continuous Integration server (like Jenkins, Bamboo or Travis CI).
   
   - For IDE based checks, plugins such as Spelling in IntelliJ IDEA or spellchecker for Visual Studio Code can come in handy.
   
   - For online API option, you can consider Grammarly's API (it's not free, though).
   
   - For programming language-specific libraries (like Python’s `pyspellchecker` or Java’s `Jazzy`) can be used.

3. Build Process Integration: Integration into your build process will depend upon what build system you use. For instance, if you use Jenkins, you can add spell checking as a step in your Jenkinsfile. If you use Maven or Gradle, you can write a custom plugin or task, respectively. The specific commands will depend on what tool you decided to use in step 2.

4. Automated Test Integration: Alternatively, you can add spell checking as part of your automated tests. Write a test that reads all of your externalized strings, runs them through a spell checking tool, and asserts that no spelling errors were found. This way, if a spelling error is introduced, your tests will fail. You can use testing frameworks like Junit (for Java) or Pytest (for Python).

Remember, false positives can be common in spell checks especially for technical words and company-specific terminology. It might be useful to maintain a custom dictionary file to avoid these.
<|end|>


