Develop a function to validate if the provided array exemplifies a minimum heap structure or not. In case it doesn't, your function should be capable of transforming the array into a minimum heap. The function should be equipped to manage duplicate values within the array, as well as negative integers and zero. 

Consider the following flawed Python code:

```python
def isMinHeap(arr, i, n):
    if i &gt; (n - 2) / 2:
        return True
    if arr[i] &lt;= arr[2 * i + 1] and arr[i] &lt;= arr[2 * i + 2] and isMinHeap(arr, 2 * i + 1, n) and isMinHeap(arr, 2 * i + 2, n):
        return True
    return False
```

This code neglects to verify the existence of the node's right child prior to comparing the node's value with the right child's value. 

Your assignment is to rectify this mistake and devise a method to transform the array into a minimum heap if it doesn't already embody one. Furnish a comprehensive explanation of your strategy and the time complexity of your solution. 

For further reference, you may consult this link: https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/how-to-check-if-a-given-array-represents-a-binary-heap/
<|end|>
<|asst|>
We need to write a Python function that validates if an array satisfies the conditions of a minimum heap and if not, transform it into one.

A minimum heap is a specialized tree-based data structure that satisfies the property: 

    The parent node is less than or equal to its child node(s).

Here is the corrected Python code:

```python
def isMinHeap(arr, i, n):
    # if the parent has exceeded the last parent node return True (base case)
    if i > (n - 2) // 2:
        return True

    # validate heap property at root; continue to check for the left and right subtrees.
    if arr[i] <= arr[2 * i + 1] and (2 * i + 2 == n or arr[i] <= arr[2 * i + 2]) and isMinHeap(arr, 2 * i + 1, n) and isMinHeap(arr, 2 * i + 2, n):
        return True
    return False

import heapq
def minHeapify(arr):
    heapq.heapify(arr)

# Test the code:
arr = [10,20,15,17,25,30]
print(isMinHeap(arr, 0, len(arr)))
minHeapify(arr)
print(isMinHeap(arr, 0, len(arr)))
```

The issue with the initial implementation lies in the comparison of the node value with the right child's value. Because every node need not have a right child, our implementation may break down if we attempt to compare the node's value with a non-existent right child’s value. 

The function minHeapify() is used to transform the given array into a minimum heap if the array doesn’t already embody a heap. This function uses a library function 'heapify' from the heapq module which modifies the array in-place and constructs a valid heap in linear time.

Regarding time complexity, the isMinHeap() function operates in O(n) time because in the worst-case scenario the function will visit all nodes in the array to check whether they follow the min-heap property or not. In case of minHeapify(), the time complexity is O(n), the function traverses the given list only once and builds a min heap with given elements.

The place complexity of these functions is O(1) because they operate in-place and do not use any auxiliary memory.
<|end|>


