(Swift/SwiftUI - iOS) My app allows authentication via firebase auth with phone number. Users can optionally add their email address in the preferences page of the app (to receive marketing emails, not for authentication).
Does anyone know if I can use firebase a cloud function to verify these user emails but NOT use them for auth? I can already save the user email to firestore, I just want to know if there is a way for firebase to email the user to confirm the email, then e.g. write a verified boolean value to firebase.
I know I can verify emails with firebase auth but these users did not sign up with email and have no password. I also don't want to overwrite their phone number as primary signin method for firebase auth as I am not supporting login with username/password.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Unfortunately, Firebase Authentication only works on the authentication step and is made specifically to verify emails for USER REGISTRATION, but for your situation, that is verify emails for MARKETING PURPOSES, it does not fit. 

One solution for your case can be using a third-party service like Mailgun, Sendgrid, etc. to send a verification email to the user with a URL that has to be clicked to confirm. The URL would link to an http trigger Cloud Function that would then update Firestore to mark the account as email-verified.

This is a high level view of the process:

1. Create a unique token (could be a hash of the email or any other form) and save this to Firestore along with the email and a 'verified' boolean field set to false.
2. Use third-party service to send a link like 'https://us-central1-yourproject.cloudfunctions.net/confirmEmail?token=xyz123' to the email
3. Write a cloud function for the URL that retrieves the token param, looks up the relevant Firestore document by token, and if it exists, sets the 'verified' value to true.

Here is a simple example using Sendgrid and an HTTPS Cloud Function:

First, create SendGrid account and get an API Key. You can use Node mailer in the backend to send the verification email:

```javascript
const nodemailer = require('nodemailer');
const sgTransport = require('nodemailer-sendgrid-transport');

const options = {
  auth: {
    api_key: YOUR_SENDGRID_API_KEY
  }
}

const mailer = nodemailer.createTransport(sgTransport(options));


exports.sendVerificationEmail = functions.firestore
    .document('users/{userId}')
    .onCreate((snap, context) => {
      const newValue = snap.data();

      const email = {
    to: newValue.email,
    from: 'your-email@example.com',
    subject: 'Confirm your email',
    text: `Hello, please confirm your email by opening the following link: https://us-central1-yourproject.cloudfunctions.net/confirmEmail?token=${newValue.token}`
  };

return mailer.sendMail(email);
});
```
Then, in the Cloud Function, you can confirm the email:

```javascript
exports.confirmEmail = functions.https.onRequest((req, res) => {
  const token = req.query.token;

  return admin.firestore().collection('users').where('token', '==', token).get()
    .then((querySnapshot) => {
      querySnapshot.forEach((doc) => {
        doc.ref.update({ verified: true });
      });
      res.send('Email confirmed');
    })
    .catch((error) => {
      console.log("Error getting documents: ", error);
      res.send('Error confirming email');
    });
});
```
Keep in mind that neither the email sending function nor the verification function does any user authentication. So, make sure your token is unique and hard to guess. This way, even if someone got a hold of the token, they can't verify the email.
<|end|>


