In my problem I have to account for an imbalance between the samples and I also made a custom loss function and so I was wondering if I should take those weights into account in the loss function or was it automatic?
I use `flow_from_dataframe` to pass the data with the parameter `weight_col`.
So I have to do something like this?

def my_loss(y_true,y_pred):
    mae_func = tf.keras.losses.MeanAbsoluteError()
    mae = mae_func(y_true,y_pred)
    return mae


Or like this?

def my_loss(y_true,y_pred,weight):
    mae_func = tf.keras.losses.MeanAbsoluteError()
    # Do some operations with weights
    mae = mae_func(y_true,y_pred)
    return mae
<|end|>
<|asst|>
It would be best to incorporate the weights into the loss function manually, because Keras does not automatically adjust for sample weights. 

The sample weights are typically used to inform the model that some data points are more important than others when computing the loss, so it may penalize the model more if it makes mistakes on certain data points. 

You can use `sample_weight` argument when calling the `fit` or `train_on_batch` methods of your model:

```
weights = ...  # Compute the weights based on your imbalance
model.train_on_batch(x, y, sample_weight=weights)
```

Your loss function might look something like this:

```python
def my_loss(y_true,y_pred,sample_weight):
    mae_func = tf.keras.losses.MeanAbsoluteError()
    mae = mae_func(y_true, y_pred)
    return mae * sample_weight
```

Keep in mind that this way weighted loss will be more than unweighted (in the case where weights are more than 1), but it doesn't mean that model performs worse. With weighting, you just assign more importance to underrepresented classes in your multiclass classification problem.
  
Note that `sample_weight` has to have the same shape as `y_pred`.
<|end|>


