<|asst|>
Sure, here is your function ready for javascript:

```javascript
function median(l, cmp_func) {
    if (l.length === 0) {
        throw new Error("List is empty");
    }

    for (let i = 0; i < l.length; i++) {
        if (typeof l[i] !== 'number') {
            throw new Error("Non-numeric value in list");
        }
    }

    try {
        l.sort(cmp_func);
        let n = l.length;
        let median;
        if (n % 2 === 0) {
            let median1 = l[Math.floor(n/2)];
            let median2 = l[Math.floor(n/2) - 1];
            median = (median1 + median2) / 2;
        } else {
            median = l[Math.floor(n/2)];
        }
        return median;
    } catch (e) {
        throw new Error("An error occurred in calculating the median");
    }
}
```
Please remember that in JavaScript all values are objects, so the function check for non-numeric values is implemented with the `typeof` operator. The division operator `/` in JavaScript converts its integer result into a float if necessary, so no specific floor division operator `//` is required as in Python, instead `Math.floor()` is used to get the whole part of the division. In case of error, a standard `Error` object is thrown.
