In the (otherwise) excellent book [C++ Coding Standards](http://www.gotw.ca/publications/c++cs.htm), Item 44, titled , Sutter and Alexandrescu recommend that only functions that really need access to the members of a class be themselves members of that class. All other operations which can be written by using only member functions should not be part of the class. They should be nonmembers and nonfriends. The arguments are that:

- - - 

Although I see the value in these argument, I see a huge drawback:  Whenever I have an object of some kind, and I want to see what operations are available on it, I can't just type "`pMysteriousObject->`" and get a list of member functions anymore. 

Keeping a clean design is in the end about making your programming life easier. But this would actually make mine much harder.

So I'm wondering if it's really worth the trouble.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Whether this type of design is worth the trouble can largely depend on the situation and the needs of the specific project. Here are a few factors that could influence this decision:

1. **Size of the Project**: In larger projects, this type of design can improve modularity and code reusability, which can make code easier to manage, test, and debug. Therefore, the inconvenience of not being able to list member functions might be outweighed by these benefits. In smaller projects, on the other hand, where these benefits are less significant, it might make more sense to prioritize convenience and keep more functions as members of the class.

2. **Nature of the Work**: If you are frequently using objects in an exploratory manner, trying out different methods to see what they do, then the convenience of being able to list member functions could be very important. On the other hand, if you are mainly writing new code, with a clear idea of what each method should do, the organization benefits of the design recommended by Sutter and Alexandrescu might be more relevant.

3. **Team Preferences**: If you are part of a team, the preferences and habits of your colleagues can also play a role in this decision. If everyone on the team is used to listing member functions and finds this very convenient, it might not be worth upsetting this workflow to achieve a cleaner design.

4. **Tooling**: Many IDEs and code editors can list free functions and show their documentation in a similar way they do for members. Good documentation can alleviate many of the concerns about discoverability.

In the end, both designs have their pros and cons, and it's up to you to decide which is more important for your particular use case. It's also always possible to follow the principle for the most part, but make exceptions in cases where convenience outweighs the benefits of stricter encapsulation. Programming is as much art as science - sometimes you'd have to balance 'pure' design principles against practical considerations.
<|end|>


