I am building a next JS application with a firebase backend. I use context for logging in, logging out and auth state changes. I would like to check if the Firebase user object exists in the middleware file and then redirect the visitor appropriately. My middleware is like below.

export default function middleware(req){
const { user 
} = UserAuth;


this however throws an error:

Cannot read properties of null (reading 'useContext')


The app works and I can get the user object from other pages when i comment it on middleware
Help
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The issue here is that you are trying to use React's `useContext` hook in a non-react component (your middleware), and that's why it's throwing the error. React hooks are for usage in React function components or custom hooks.

`useContext` hook is supposed to only be called from the render method of a React Function Component (or custom Hook). 

In Next.js, all API routes/middleware don't run inside the context of your React app, they are just regular Node.js server-side scripts and can't use React Hooks because they don't have a React component tree. 

If you want to check if the user is authenticated, you can use firebase-admin in your API routes to verify the Firebase Authentication user token. Below is an example on how to do this:

```javascript
// Assuming you have already initialized your firebase admin
import admin from 'firebase-admin';

export default async function(req, res) {
  const token = req.headers.authorization?.split('Bearer ')[1];

  if (!token) {
    return res.status(401).send({ error: 'You must be logged in to view this resource.' });
  }

  try {
    const user = await admin.auth().verifyIdToken(token);
    req.currentUser = user;
    
    // Add the following line if you're wanting to pass control to your Next.js page:
    // return NextResponse.next();
  } catch (error) {
    return res.status(401).send({ error: 'Token is invalid.' });
  }
}
```

This middleware checks if a user is authenticated by reading the `Authorization` header, expecting a Bearer token, then verifying the token using the Firebase Admin SDK. With this solution, you don't need to use React's context in your Next.js middleware.
<|end|>


