I want to execute script using nrpe in selected compute nodes and output the result
I want to run scripts on compute nodes. I can select the compute nodes and input command to execute. Executed result will be given as output. I want to provide the compute nodes where the scripts are to be executed.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The NRPE (Nagios Remote Plugin Executor) allows you to execute scripts and commands on remote Linux/Unix machines and get the result back to the host server.

The following is an example of how you can execute a script using NRPE on selected compute notes:

Step 1: Install and Configure NRPE 

On the compute nodes (also called 'remote' or 'client' nodes), you need to install NRPE and its associated plugins. The steps usually differs according to the Linux distribution.

For example, on Ubuntu/Debian, you can simply use apt-get:

```
sudo apt-get install nagios-nrpe-server nagios-plugins
```

Next, you need to edit the NRPE configuration file (usually located at `/etc/nagios/nrpe.cfg`) to add the command that you wish to execute.

```
command[check_disk]=/usr/lib/nagios/plugins/check_disk -w 20% -c 10% -p /dev/sda1
```

The command named 'check_disk' will execute the script 'check_disk' located in the Nagios plugins directory. The script is followed by parameters of the command.

Remember to replace '/dev/sda1', '20%' and '10%' with your own values according to your requirements.

Step 2: Restart NRPE service 

After editing the configuration file, you need to restart your NRPE service:

```
sudo systemctl restart nagios-nrpe-server
```

Step 3: Execute the script using NRPE from Nagios server

Finally, on your Nagios server (also called 'host' or 'master'), you will be able to execute your command using the check_nrpe plugin:

```
/usr/lib/nagios/plugins/check_nrpe -H [remote_server_IP] -c check_disk
```

Replace '[remote_server_IP]' with the IP address (or hostname) of your compute node. Your script will execute and you should receive the output on your Nagios server.

Remember, the NRPE needs to be able to access the remote/client nodes. If there are any firewalls in place, you need to allow NRPE traffic. The default port used by NRPE is 5666. So, please check and confirm whether the NRPE traffic is allowed or not.

The above-given process is a very basic setup with no encryption or security. Please ensure you have necessary securities and restrictions in place.
<|end|>


