/*Given a string s and a number n. Move each character at an odd index n steps in the alphabet. Move right if n is positive and left if negative. Keep non-alphabetical characters unchanged. Overflow from one end to the other. The updated function should return the resulting string and also catalogue the frequency of all the alphabets in the resulting string.
For example, 
moveChars("abc",1) = "bbc", frequency = {b:2, c:1}
moveChars("a1c",2) = "c1e", frequency = {c:1, e:1}
moveChars("AB2C",-1) = "ZB2B", frequency = {Z:1, B:2}
*/ 
const moveChars = (s, n) => {
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, here is the enhanced function in JavaScript considering all the requirements:

```js
const moveChars = (s, n) => {
    let frequency = {};
    let result = [];
    for(let i=0; i<s.length; i++) {
        let c = s[i];
        if(i % 2 === 1 && c.toLowerCase() != c.toUpperCase()) {
            let code = c.charCodeAt(0);
            let newChar = '';
            if(code >= 65 && code <= 90) 
                newChar = String.fromCharCode((code - 65 + n + 26) % 26 + 65); // For uppercase letters
            else if(code >= 97 && code <= 122) 
                newChar = String.fromCharCode((code - 97 + n + 26) % 26 + 97); // For lowercase letters    

            result.push(newChar);
            frequency[newChar] = (frequency[newChar] || 0) + 1;
        } else {
            result.push(c);
            if(c.toLowerCase() != c.toUpperCase())
                frequency[c] = (frequency[c] || 0) + 1;
        }
    }

    return {
        result: result.join(''),
        frequency: frequency
    }
}
```

This code creates a new array `result` to store the resulting string and an object `frequency` to store the frequency of all the alphabets in the resulting string. It iterates through the given string and checks if the index is odd and the character is an alphabet letter. If yes, the corresponding character in the alphabet is calculated depending on if n is positive or negative. The frequency object is then updated accordingly. The updated function then returns the resulting string and the frequency object.

You can check the output by using console.log like this:
```js
console.log(moveChars("abc",1));
console.log(moveChars("a1c",2));
console.log(moveChars("AB2C",-1));
```

Notes:
1. The frequency object holds only frequencies of alphabetical characters (irrespective of their case), it doesn't consider non-alphabetical characters.
2. This function maintains the original case of the letters (upper or lower). If you want all lowercase or uppercase in the frequency object, an update should be made in the function.
