I know that `JTable` can sort by a single column. But is it possible to allow for multiple column sort or do I need to write the code myself?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, it is possible to perform multi-column sorts in JTable. Beginning from Java SE 6, you can do so by using `setSortKeys()` method of `TableRowSorter` class.

Here's an example:

```java
TableRowSorter<TableModel> sorter = new TableRowSorter<>(yourTable.getModel());
yourTable.setRowSorter(sorter);
List<RowSorter.SortKey> sortKeys = new ArrayList<>();

int columnIndexForFirstSortKey = 1;
boolean sortKeyAscendingOrderForFirstKey = true; 
sortKeys.add(new RowSorter.SortKey(columnIndexForFirstSortKey, sortKeyAscendingOrderForFirstKey ? SortOrder.ASCENDING : SortOrder.DESCENDING));

int columnIndexForSecondSortKey = 0;
boolean sortKeyAscendingOrderForSecondKey = false; 
sortKeys.add(new RowSorter.SortKey(columnIndexForSecondSortKey, sortKeyAscendingOrderForSecondKey ? SortOrder.ASCENDING : SortOrder.DESCENDING));

sorter.setSortKeys(sortKeys);
```

As per your requirement to sort by different columns, you can modify your `RowSorter.SortKey` to contain the column index of the columns you wish to sort first, in which order you want to sort them and then you pass this list to `setSortKeys()` method.

Remember, the first element of `SortKey` list will be primary key for sorting, the second one will be the secondary key, and so on. So, the sort keys will determine the order of sorting across multiple columns.

You can also allow users to do multi-column sorting by enabling it like this:

```java
yourTable.setAutoCreateRowSorter(true);
yourTable.getRowSorter().toggleSortOrder(columnIndexForFirstSortKey);
yourTable.getRowSorter().toggleSortOrder(columnIndexForSecondSortKey);
```

This way users can toggle (click) on the headers for multiple columns they want to sort by. The first click sorts in ascending order, the second click sorts in descending order, and the third click restores the original order.
<|end|>


