<|asst|>
Here's how you can recreate the same functionality using PHP:

```php
<?php

function interpolate($x, $y, $x_new) {
    // find two points (x1, y1) and (x2, y2) such that x1 <= x_new <= x2
    for ($i = 0; $i < count($x) - 1; $i++) {
        if ($x[$i] <= $x_new && $x_new <= $x[$i+1]) {
            $slope = ($y[$i+1] - $y[$i]) / ($x[$i+1] - $x[$i]);
            return $y[$i] + $slope * ($x_new - $x[$i]);
        }
    }

    // if the function gets here, that means that x_new was not in the interval [x[0], x[-1]]. 
    echo ("x_new not in range\n");
    return null;
}

// load the dataset
$file = fopen("filepath.csv", "r");
$x = [];
$y = [];
while (($data = fgetcsv($file)) !== false) {
    $x[] = $data[0];
    $y[] = $data[1];
}
fclose($file);

// Some data cleaning
// Replace all occurrences of NaN
foreach ($y as $i => $val) {
    if (is_nan($val)) {
        $y[$i] = interpolate($x, $y, $x[$i]);
    }
}

// Check for outliers using Z-Scores 
$mean_x = array_sum($x) / count($x);
$std_dev = sqrt(array_sum(array_map(function($val) use ($mean_x) { return ($val - $mean_x) ** 2; }, $x)) / count($x));
$z_scores = array_map(function($val) use ($mean_x, $std_dev) { return ($val - $mean_x) / $std_dev; }, $x);
$outliers = [];
foreach ($z_scores as $i => $score) {
    if (abs($score) >= 3) {
        $outliers[] = "X: " . $x[$i] . ", Y: " . $y[$i] . ", Z_Score: " . $score;
    }
}

echo "Outliers: \n";
foreach ($outliers as $outlier) {
    echo $outlier . "\n";
}
?>
```

Some things to consider:
- The PHP version operates on arrays directly, while the Python example is using a pandas DataFrame. This results in some differences and limitations in the PHP version.
- PHP doesn't have a direct equivalent to numpy's or pandas' NaN handling capabilities, so we're looking for NAN values using is_nan() function.
- The CSV data is assumed to be 2-dimensional (X and Y columns only). If your dataset is more complex, additional code would be required.
- Z-score calculation and outlier detection are manually implemented, as PHP does not have built-in utilities for these operations like scipy in Python.
<|end|>


