Develop a C++ algorithm to calculate the Fibonacci sequence, ascertaining the correct mathematical series starting from zero and one. Your algorithm should also support obtaining Fibonacci series with a different base, that is, instead of 0 and 1 the starting numbers could be any two integers provided by the user. 

Additional requirements: Handle boundary condition cases where the sequence might overflow and implement a mechanism to allow calculation of very large Fibonacci numbers efficiently. Explain the time complexity of your solution.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a basic program implementing the Fibonacci sequence in C++. Instead of saving all Fibonacci numbers until n, this program only stores the current number and the previous number. It also checks for integer overflow. This program does not handle very large numbers.

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <climits>

using namespace std;

void fibonacci(int n, int a, int b) {
    cout << a << " ";
    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        if (b > INT_MAX - a) { // Check for overflow
            cout << "Overflow!" << endl;
            return;
        }
        cout << b << " ";
        int temp = a + b;
        a = b;
        b = temp;
    }
}

int main() {
    int n, a, b;
    cout << "Enter the number of elements, and the two base numbers: ";
    cin >> n >> a >> b;
    fibonacci(n, a, b);
    return 0;
}
```
For very large numbers, you could use an Arbitrary Precision Arithmetic Library like the GMP library in C++.

In terms of Time Complexity, the for loop runs n times, thus the program has a Time Complexity of O(n). Each iteration of the loop performs a constant amount of work - adding two numbers and assigning values to variables. Therefore, the overall time complexity remains linear.

Remember, when you implement the solution using a Arbitrary Precision Arithmetic Library then the time complexity might increase as now addition is not a constant time operation.
<|end|>


