I am using textmask module in angular and I was wondering is there any way to use text mask with the following regex and not specifying length?

public regexMask: any = [/^([1-9]+|[ -,.()]+|[\u10D0-\u10F0]+)$/];


Following example above limits my input to 1 length, is there a way to make sure that input text is validated by regex and not by length
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Text-mask library in Angular is more designed for scenarios where you have clear structure and format for an input. It works great for inputs like phone numbers, SSNs, dates, etc, but unfortunately does not support variable-length regular expression validation.

However, Angular out-of-the-box already has really powerful form validation API that allows you to use arbitrary regex to validate form inputs.

Below is an example of how to use regex in form validation in Angular:

```
import { FormGroup, FormBuilder, Validators } from '@angular/forms';


export class MyFormComponent implements OnInit {
  myForm: FormGroup;

  //this is your regex
  public regexMask: any = /^([1-9]+|[ -,.()]+|[\u10D0-\u10F0]+)$/;
  
  constructor(private _fb: FormBuilder) {}
  
  ngOnInit() {
    this.myForm = this._fb.group({
      myInput: ['', [Validators.pattern(this.regexMask)]]
    });
  }
}
```

Then in your template 

```
  <form [formGroup]="myForm">
    <input formControlName="myInput">
    <button [disabled]="myForm.invalid">Submit</button>
    <div *ngIf="myForm.controls.myInput.hasError('pattern')">Not valid input</div>
  </form>
```

This way, by using Angular's built-in Form API, you can apply any kind of complex validation logic to your inputs.
<|end|>


