Here is a Python solution demonstrating how you might incorporate all these features in your code. This program uses a class TestResult with method evaluate which will take in a single parameter, evaluate it, and then print either "Passed" or "Failed" based on the type and value of the input. Please also check error handling built-in for invalid inputs:

```python
class TestResult:
    def evaluate(self, input_val):
        try:
            if type(input_val) == bool:
                if input_val == True:
                    print("Passed")
                else:
                    print("Failed")
            elif type(input_val) == str:
                if ord(input_val) >= 80:
                    print("Passed")
                else:
                    print("Failed")
            elif isinstance(input_val, (int, float)):
                if input_val >= 80:
                    print("Passed")
                else:
                    print("Failed")
            else:
                print("Error! Invalid input {} of type {}".format(input_val, type(input_val)))
        except TypeError:
            print("Error! TypeError for input {}".format(input_val))
        except:
            print("Error! Unknown error for input {}".format(input_val))

# Testing the program
result = TestResult()
result.evaluate(80) # Should print "Passed"
result.evaluate(79.9) # Should print "Failed"
result.evaluate('P') # Should print "Passed"
result.evaluate(True) # Should print "Passed"
result.evaluate(False) # Should print "Failed"
result.evaluate('invalid') # Should print "Failed"
result.evaluate(None) # Should print "Error! TypeError for input None"
result.evaluate([80]) # Should print "Error! Invalid input [80] of type <class 'list'>"
```

This is a simple toy example of how you can handle various types of data input. In a more comprehensive program, you would likely want to add additional features, such as handling a broader range of data types or incorporating more complex logic in the evaluation process.
