In the context of an experimental design where a reading specialist in a large public school system surmises that the more the students read, the better their academic performance becomes, she decides to conduct an experiment among middle school students. She assigns an SRS of 30 eighth graders with four additional hours of reading per week, another SRS of 30 seventh graders with two additional hours of reading per week while an SRS of 30 sixth graders are left with no extra assigned reading serving as the control group. After a full academic year, she plans to compare the mean GPAs from each group. In your opinion, is this an effective experimental design bearing in mind the importance of blinding, potential for placebo effect, lurking variables such as grade level, and the establishment of a cause-and-effect relationship between reading hours and GPA?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In a scholarly perspective, while this design does establish a control group and varying levels of the independent variable (additional reading hours), it has some significant flaws.

1. Potential Confound(s): The biggest flaw is the introduction of a potential confound into the study: all the eighth graders are assigned four additional hours, all the seventh graders two hours, and all the sixth graders zero hours. This makes it impossible to distinguish whether any observed difference in GPAs is due to the different amounts of reading or the different grade levels. It would be preferable to randomize the assignment of additional reading hours within each grade level.

2. Placebo effect: In regards to the placebo effect, while not a traditional placebo scenario (as found in clinical trials), it might be considered that knowing they are part of an experiment to improve their learning could indeed motivate students to work harder in all subjects.

3. Lack of Blinding: It is not clear whether this experiment is performed in a blind or double-blind manner. If the students and/or the teachers know who is in which group, this could introduce another bias.

4. Cause-and-effect relationship: Although this design could potentially reveal correlation, it may not satisfactorily establish a cause-and-effect relationship because of the aforementioned confounds.

5. Statistical Power: Lastly, with groups of only 30 students, the sample size is relatively small, which could limit the statistical power of the study, making it harder to detect a significant effect if one exists.

An improved version of the experiment would randomly assign students within each grade level to different amounts of additional reading, thereby controlling for grade level as a lurking variable. It would also be advantageous to design the experiment in a way to include blinding and avoid potential placebo effects, such as ensuring teachers and students are not aware of the group assignments. Lastly, increasing the sample size would help achieve better statistical power.
<|end|>


