Employ the Flask Python framework to implement a routing procedure that facilitates the delivery of a dynamically assembled HTML web page.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To achieve this, we should first set up a Flask application and then create a route that serves our dynamic HTML. The content of the dynamic HTML can be made up using Jinja2 templates supported by Flask.

First, install Flask if you have not done so. 
```
pip install flask
```
Here is an example code for the requirement:

```python
from flask import Flask, render_template

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/')
def home():
    # Let's say we want to pass some data to our HTML and display it
    data = "Hello, World!"
    return render_template('index.html', data=data)

if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.run(debug=True)
```

This basic Flask application has one route ("/") which responds to the HTTP GET request. When we navigate to the root URL ("/") of our application, whatever is returned by the `home()` function is what we're going to see in the browser.

`render_template` is a Flask function that searches for the specified file in the templates folder in the same directory as your Flask application and returns an HTML string, that can be automatically convert to HTTP response.

Now, in the templates folder, create an HTML file `index.html`.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Dynamic Page</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>{{data}}</h1>
</body>
</html>
```

In this template, anything within {{ }} is a variable which will be substituted by its real value. In this example, "data" will be replaced with the actual data passed to the template. As such, if everything is set up correctly, when you run your Flask app and access the root domain, you should see a webpage displaying "Hello, World!".


Note: Flask uses a templating language called Jinja2 that allows you to send data from your Python script to your HTML file. It's an extremely powerful feature that makes Flask applications very flexible and efficient.
<|end|>


