Elucidate the distinction between a superficially duplicated object, often referred to as a shallow copy, and an utterly replicated object, sometimes known as a deep copy. Further, describe the effects on performance when using these two techniques. Additionally, provide a real-world programming example outlining the potential issues that could arise when using either method.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
A shallow copy of an object creates a new object which is a copy of the original object. It simply references the instance variables of the original object. Any changes made to the new object's instance variables will also reflect on the original object's instance variables because they are pointing to the same memory location. In essence, a shallow copy is creating a new reference to the same memory location.

In contrast, a deep copy generates a new object and recursively adds the copies of the child objects found in the original object. Simply put, a deep copy doesn't share the same memory location as the original object. Hence, changes to the original object will have no effect on the deep copied object and vice versa.

Performance Impact:
Since shallow copy just references the same memory location, the creation of copy is always fast. However, the risk of unintended side-effects from modifying data in-place can be high. Deep copy, on the other hand, can potentially take up a lot of memory and time, especially for complex objects. Yet, it is safer in terms of data integrity as it doesn't affect the original object.

Example:
Consider a list of lists in Python. Shallow copy behaviour can be problematic:

`import copy
list1 = [[1, 1], [2, 2]]
list2 = copy.copy(list1)
list2[1][1] = 'example'`

In the above code, 'example' will appear in both list1 and list2 because list2 is a shallow copy.

If we use deep copy, this issue will not arise:

`import copy
list1 = [[1, 1], [2, 2]]
list2 = copy.deepcopy(list1)
list2[1][1] = 'example'`

In this case, 'example' will only appear in list2. It will not affect list1 because list2 is a deep copy.
<|end|>


