Design a multifaceted web application using JavaScript or a similar foundational scripting language. The application should have the central feature of delivering the phrase "Greetings, Universe!" while ensuring user interactivity and engagement. 

Develop a GUI that has this phrase manifest into various languages based on the user's selection from a dropdown menu, employing an API to provide accurate translations. Additionally, add supportive features such as accessibility options, including a text-to-speech feature for the translated phrase. 

Also, consider optimizing the load time and responsiveness of your application and demonstrate the use of best coding practices for maintainability and scalability. Include thorough documentation of your code for ease of understanding by other developers. 

The application should be able to handle multiple user requests concurrently and should have error handling mechanisms to cope with translation errors or failures. Once the project is completed, write a set of test cases to ensure everything works as expected.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This problem is essentially asking for the design of a full stack project with several components like a backend API and a frontend interface, and requires knowledge of several complex programming concepts. A complete solution would require much more than a text response, but we can definitely sketch out a rough blueprint.

Given that JavaScript is mentioned as the scripting language, we'll use Node.js as the backend scripting language because it allows us to write JavaScript on the server-side. Here's a step-by-step overview of what we could do.

In our `server.js` file, we could set up a simple express server:

```javascript
const express = require("express");
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

// More code will go here

const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3001;
app.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`Server running on port ${PORT}`));
```

Next, we'll need to set up a route to interact with the translation API. We'll use axios to make HTTP requests, and for now we'll pretend we're using the Google Translation API (though we'd normally need to sign up and obtain a key).

```javascript
const axios = require("axios");

app.post('/translate', (req, res) => {
  // The text to translate will be sent in a JSON object
  const { text, targetLanguage } = req.body;

  axios.post('https://translation.googleapis.com/language/translate/v2:translate', {
    q: text,
    target: targetLanguage
  })
  .then(response => {
    res.send(response.data);
  })
  .catch(error => {
    console.log(error);
    res.status(500).send("An error occurred while trying to translate the text.");
  });
});
```

On the frontend, we'd have a React app with a dropdown for selecting the language and a button for triggering the translation.

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import axios from 'axios';

export default class TranslationAppComponent extends React.Component {
  // More code will go here
}
```

Inside the component, we would have a function for requesting a translation and setting the state with the result, as well as JSX for rendering the app. Note that we would also need to build the dropdown menu and feed it options for the languages.

```javascript
export default class TranslationAppComponent extends React.Component {
  state = {
    languages: [], // This would be populated with the available languages
    selectedLanguage: null,
    translatedText: ''
  };

  translateText = () => {
    axios.post('/translate', {
      text: "Greetings, Universe!",
      targetLanguage: this.state.selectedLanguage
    })
    .then(response => {
      this.setState({ translatedText: response.data.translations[0].translatedText });
    })
    .catch(error => console.log(error));
  };

  render() {
    return (
      <>
        <h1>Translation App</h1>
        {/* We would also have a dropdown menu for selecting the language, and a button for triggering the translation */}
        <button onClick={this.translateText}>Translate</button>

        {this.state.translatedText && 
          <h2>{this.state.translatedText}</h2>
        }
      </>
    );
  }
}
```

There are many details left out here that you would need to fill in, such as fetching the available languages from the translation API, adding the dropdown menu, adding the text-to-speech functionality, improving the UI, adding tests, setting up error handling, and more. But hopefully this gives you a rough idea of how you could get started with this project. We strongly recommend training and experimentation with the technologies mentioned (Express.js, axios, React) before undertaking this project.
<|end|>


