To address this problem, we'll build a simple responsive website with a navigation bar that changes when viewed on smaller viewports (mobile devices).

HTML structure (index.html):
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
    <script src="app.js"></script>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  </head>
  <body>
    <nav id="navbar">
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#home">Home</a></li>
        <li><a href="#about">About</a></li>
        <li><a href="#contact">Contact</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </body>
</html>
```

CSS styling (styles.css):
```css
/* Styles for all devices */
* {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}

body {
  margin: 0;
  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}

#navbar ul {
  list-style-type: none;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  background-color: #333;
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-around;
}

#navbar ul li a {
  display: block;
  color: white;
  text-align: center;
  padding: 14px 16px;
  text-decoration: none;
}

#navbar ul li a:hover {
  background-color: #111;
}

/* Styles for screens less than 600px wide */
@media screen and (max-width: 600px) {
  #navbar ul {
    flex-direction: column;
  }
}
```

JavaScript for user interaction (app.js):
```JavaScript
// When a link in the navbar is clicked, display an alert
document.getElementById('navbar').addEventListener('click', function(e) {
  if (e.target.tagName === 'A') {
    alert('You clicked ' + e.target.innerText);
  }
});
```

This is a very simple example and does a few things:
-Defines a navigation bar with three links (Home, About, Contact).
-Styles the navigation bar, making it responsive with flexbox. By default, the links are spread evenly across the width of the screen. 
