Design a JavaScript function that ascertains if every individual integer within a provided array is a prime number. You need to handle miscellaneous cases as well such as the array might include non-integer numbers or even non-numeric values for which the function needs to throw a specific error message. Additionally, we'll provide a piece of erroneous code below and you'd be required to identify the mistakes in it and refactor it correctly.

```
function checkPrime(nums) {
 for(var i=0; i<nums.length; i++) {
    if(nums[i] === 2) {
        return true;
    } else if(nums[i] > 1){
        for (var j = 2; j < nums[i]; j++) {
            if(nums[i] % j !== 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
    } else {
        return false;
    }
  }
}
```
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The provided function `checkPrime(nums)` has a few issues. 

1. It does not handle non-integer or non-numeric values in the given array.
2. It returns `true` or `false` immediately after checking the first number in the array, instead of checking all the numbers.
3. The condition inside the second loop is wrong. It should be `if(nums[i] % j === 0)` instead of `if(nums[i] % j !== 0)`. The reason being, if `nums[i]` can be divided evenly by `j`, it means `nums[i]` is not a prime number. 

Here is the refactored code:

```javascript
function checkPrime(nums) {
  function isPrime(n) {
    if (typeof n !== 'number' || !Number.isInteger(n)) {
      throw new Error('All array values must be integers.');
    } else if(n < 2) {
      return false;
    } else if(n === 2) {
      return true;
    } else {
      for(var j = 2; j*j <= n; j++) {
        if(n % j === 0) {
          return false;
        }
      }
      return true;
    }
  }

  for(var i=0; i<nums.length; i++) {
    if(!isPrime(nums[i])) {
      return false;
    }
  }
  
  return true;
}
```

In this refactored code, I added an inner function `isPrime(n)` which checks if a single number is prime or not. It throws an error if `n` is not a numeric integer. In the main function, I call `isPrime(nums[i])` for all elements in the array. If any of them are not prime, the main function returns `false`. If all elements are prime, the main function returns `true`. This ensures that all numbers in the array are checked. Also, the loop condition in the `isPrime(n)` function has been optimized to run until `j*j <= n` to reduce unnecessary iterations.
<|end|>


