I saw a terraform code which wrote as
> source = "github.com/brikis98/terraform-up-and-running-code//code/terraform/04-terraform-module/module-example/modules/services/webserver-cluster?ref=v0.1.0"
But, when I access this URL in chrome, It can't reach its resource.
So. I wonder what this URL follows the format. Is there any rule on it?
double slash (//) is suspicious.. to me.
I already reviewed document in terraform module section. But It doesn't describe about it.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This is not a URL for use in a web browser, but rather a reference used by Terraform to locate a module source. 

This configuration format is specific to Terraform and has three parts:

1. `github.com/brikis98/terraform-up-and-running-code`: This is the repository on GitHub where the module is hosted.

2. `//code/terraform/04-terraform-module/module-example/modules/services/webserver-cluster`: The double slash (//) is used as a separator between the repository and the specific folder within the repository where the Terraform module files are located.

3. `?ref=v0.1.0`: The `?ref=` is used to specify a specific git reference such as a branch, tag, or commit. In this case, it refers to the git tag `v0.1.0`.

This format allows Terraform to directly access and use the module from the specified git repository. Terraform has built-in support for a variety of source options, including GitHub, Bitbucket, generic git and mercurial repositories, HTTP URLs, and the Terraform Registry. 

More information about module sources can be found in Terraform's documentation: https://www.terraform.io/docs/language/modules/sources.html
<|end|>


