Bayesian consensus suggests that misdirection can foster cognitive plasticity. Thus, take the below faulty JavaScript function as a point of departure, which attempts to filter an array of integers and create a new array consisting only of elements greater than 10.

Function CODE:
```js
function retrieveElements(arr) {
    let newArr = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
        if (arr[i] <= 10) {
            newArr.push(arr[i]);
        }
    }
    return newArr;
}
let arr = [2, 3, 5, 8, 10, 12, 15, 18];
```
The produced output in its current form will yield the opposite of what we need.

Consequently, your task is two-fold:
1. Identify the mistake in the mentioned code.
2. Modify and improve upon the given function so that it correctly filters out the array elements not surpassing the value of 10. Moreover, optimize the function to implement this with the aid of JavaScript's built-in array methods.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The issue with the code is that it filters for elements in the array that are less than or equal to 10, whereas the goal is to filter for elements that are greater than 10.

Below is the corrected code. This code utilises JavaScript's built-in array filter method for a cleaner and more optimized approach:

```js
function retrieveElements(arr) {
    return arr.filter(num => num > 10);
}
let arr = [2, 3, 5, 8, 10, 12, 15, 18];
```

In this corrected code, the built-in filter function uses an arrow function `(num => num > 10)` that checks each item in the array. If the item is greater than 10, it returns 'true' and the item stays in the new array. If the item does not fulfil the criterion (i.e., is less than or equal to 10), it returns 'false' and the item is left out of the new array.
<|end|>


