<|user|>
I recently asked a question about Oracle Encryption.  Along the way to finding a solution for myself I decided to move the encryption (well, obfuscation) to the application side for certain tasks.  

My problem is that the database is already encrypting data a certain way and I need Java code to duplicate that functionality, so that text encrypted by one system can be decrypted by the other and vice versa.

I want the encryption to be compatible with what the DB was already doing but couldn't find the documentation that describes exactly what Oracle is doing.  How do I replicate this in Java?


dbms_obfuscation_toolkit.DESEncrypt(
  input_string => v_string,
  key_string => key_string,
  encrypted_string => encrypted_string );
RETURN UTL_RAW.CAST_TO_RAW(encrypted_string);



No matter what I try, it seems as if the Java DES encryption is different than Oracle's.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Oracle DBMS obfuscation toolkit uses DES encryption in CBC mode without any padding and with an IV (initialization vector) of all-zero bytes.

Here is a Java code that uses DES encryption in CBC mode with no padding and with an all-zero bytes IV:

```java
import javax.crypto.Cipher;
import javax.crypto.spec.IvParameterSpec;
import javax.crypto.spec.SecretKeySpec;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.util.Base64;

public class OracleDESEncrypt {

    private static final String ALGORITHM = "DES";
    private static final String TRANSFORMATION = ALGORITHM + "/CBC/NoPadding";
    private static final IvParameterSpec ZERO_IV = new IvParameterSpec(new byte[8]);

    public static byte[] encrypt(byte[] data, byte[] key) throws Exception {
        return transform(Cipher.ENCRYPT_MODE, data, key);
    }
    
    public static byte[] decrypt(byte[] data, byte[] key) throws Exception {
        return transform(Cipher.DECRYPT_MODE, data, key);
    }
    
    private static byte[] transform(int mode, byte[] data, byte[] key) throws Exception {
        MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
        byte[] thedigest = md.digest(key);
        SecretKeySpec skey = new SecretKeySpec(thedigest, "DES");
        Cipher cipher = Cipher.getInstance(TRANSFORMATION);
        cipher.init(mode, skey, ZERO_IV);
        return cipher.doFinal(data);
    }
}
```
Use this class for encryption and decryption. Convert encrypted string to Base64 before storing them to Oracle and decode them after retrieving.

```java
byte[] keyBytes = "yourKey".getBytes();
byte[] dataBytes = "stringToEncrypt".getBytes();
byte[] resultBytes = OracleDESEncrypt.encrypt(dataBytes, keyBytes);
String result = Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString(resultBytes);

//use result to store to Oracle

//load from Oracle to string 'loadData'
byte[] loadDataBytes = Base64.getDecoder().decode(loadData);
byte[] decryptedBytes = OracleDESEncrypt.decrypt(loadDataBytes, keyBytes);
String decryptedData = new String(decryptedBytes);
```
Only catch in here is  the padding, if your strings are not divisible by 8 you should add padding in the end until it becomes divisible by 8. Oracle uses "Space" for character data and 0x00 for RAW or BLOB data. The `NoPadding` option is used, then it is assuming that you've already performed necessary padding in the input data.
<|end|>


