I want to filter a `java.util.Collection` based on a predicate.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can achieve this by using the `stream()` method in conjunction with `filter()` and `collect()` methods provided by the Java 8 Stream API. Here is a skeleton of how you can do it:

```java
import java.util.Collection;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

Collection<T> collection = // your collection;
Predicate<T> predicate = // your predicate;

Collection<T> filteredCollection = collection.stream()
                                    .filter(predicate)
                                    .collect(Collectors.toList());
```
In this code:

- T is the type of the elements in your collection.
- `stream()` method converts the collection into a Stream.
- `filter(predicate)` is a stateless intermediate operation that takes a Predicate and filters out elements that do not match the predicate.
- `collect(Collectors.toList())` is a terminal operation that converts the result back into a new `List`.

As an example, if you have a `Collection<String>` and you want to keep only the strings that start with `"A"`, you can do it in this way:

```java
Collection<String> collection = Arrays.asList("Apple", "Banana", "Avocado", "Cherry", "Apricot");
Predicate<String> predicate = s -> s.startsWith("A");

Collection<String> filteredCollection = collection.stream()
                                    .filter(predicate)
                                    .collect(Collectors.toList());
System.out.println(filteredCollection); // prints [Apple, Avocado, Apricot]
```
<|end|>


