<|user|>
Review and debug the given C++ code where multiple factors might be causing the program to not function as intended. 

Besides, you should also consider the efficiency of your solution. Try to solve it with a time complexity better than O(n).

```cpp
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

void print_numbers_to_10(int &x){
while (x < 10)
  x = x + 1
cout<<x<<endl;
}

int main()
{
int x = 0;
print_numbers_to_10(x);

if (x==10)
    return 0;
else
    return 1;
}
```
In this code, the developer aimed to call the print_numbers_to_10() function from the main function to increment 'x' until it reaches 10, then print its value. 
If 'x' indeed becomes 10, the main function should return 0, otherwise, it should return 1.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
There are several issues with this code:

1. The function print_numbers_to_10() doesn't actually print the incremented values of 'x' as it increases; instead, it only prints the final value of 'x'. If we want to print each increment of 'x', the printing statement should be inside the loop.

2. There is a missing semicolon (;) after `x = x + 1`, which will cause a syntax error.

3. The while loop will only stop when 'x' equals 10. But this will never happen since the condition for the loop to continue is 'x < 10'. Therefore, 'x' will always be less than 10, and the loop will not stop.

4. The time complexity of this function is O(n) because we iterate over 'n' steps in the worst-case scenario (when 'n' is close to 10). But since 'n' in this problem is always 10, we can regard this as constant time O(1), as it doesn't scale with 'n'.

Here is the corrected and efficient version of the code:

```cpp
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

void print_numbers_to_10(int &x){
while (x < 10){
    x = x + 1;
    cout<<x<<endl;
    }
}

int main(){
    int x = 0;
    print_numbers_to_10(x);

    if (x==10)
        return 0;
    else
        return 1;
}
```

This code will print the numbers 1 through 10 sequentially. After 'x' reaches 10, the program will stop and return 0, indicating successful execution. If 'x' did not reach 10 due to any reason, the program would return 1, indicating an error.
<|end|>


