<|user|>
Develop a strategy for implementing an automated authentication procedure for a user login system.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To solve this problem via programming, it's important to choose an appropriate programming language and technology stack. I'll assume that we will be using Node.js and Express framework for server-side logic, MongoDB for the database layer, and JSON Web Token (JWT) for the authentication mechanism.

1. **Database Schema:**
The MongoDB schema for a user would look like this:

```javascript 
const mongoose = require('mongoose');
const Schema = mongoose.Schema;

const UserSchema = new Schema({
  username: {type: String, required: true, unique: true},
  password: {type: String, required: true},
});

module.exports = mongoose.model('User', UserSchema);
```

2. **Password Hashing:**
Never save plain text password in a database. Instead, hash them using a library like bcrypt.

```javascript
const bcrypt = require('bcryptjs');
const saltRounds = 10;

UserSchema.pre('save', function(next) {
    if (this.isNew || this.isModified('password')) {
        const document = this;
        bcrypt.hash(document.password, saltRounds, function(err, hashedPassword) {
            if (err) {
                next(err);
            }
            else {
                document.password = hashedPassword;
                next();
            }
        });
    } else {
        next();
    }
});
```

3. **User Registration:**
Register a new user in the system by accepting a unique username and a password.

```javascript
app.post('/register', (req, res) => {
    const newUser = new User({
        username: req.body.username,
        password: req.body.password
    });

    newUser.save((err, user) =>{
        if(err) return res.status(500).send(err);
        
        return res.status(200).send("Registration successful.");
    });
});
```

4. **User Authentication:**
To authenticate the user, the application will receive a username and password. It will then use bcrypt to hash the provided password and compare it to the hashed password held in the database.

```javascript
app.post('/auth', (req, res) => {
    User.findOne({username: req.body.username}, (err, user) =>{
        if(err) return res.status(500).send(err);
        
        if(!user) return res.status(403).send('User not found.');
        
        user.isCorrectPassword(req.body.password, (err, same)=>{
            if(err) return res.status(500).send(err);

            if(!same) return res.status(403).send('Wrong password.');

            // Create a JWT and return it
            const token = jwt.sign({username: user.username}, secret, { expiresIn: '1h' });
            return res.status(200).json({ token: token });
        });
    });
});
```

5. **Authorization:**
To make sure a user is authorized to access an endpoint create a middleware.

```javascript
function verifyToken(req, res, next) {
    const token = req.headers['authorization'];

    if (!token) return res.status(403).send('No token provided.');
    
    jwt.verify(token, secret, function(err, decoded) {
        if (err) return res.status(500).send("Failed to authenticate token.");
        
        // If everything good, save to request for use in other routes
        req.username = decoded.username;
        next();
    });
}
```

6. **Usage:**
Use it on a route.

```javascript
app.get('/dashboard', verifyToken, (req, res)=>{
    return res.send(`Hello ${req.username}`);
});
```

This provides a secure way of registering and authenticating a user. Use this functionality in a web application, mobile application or anything similar. Do note, for production-ready applications - additional checks and measures should also be implemented.
<|end|>


