I'm starting a new web project and I am considering two presentation frameworks. I am thinking either about ASP.NET MVC or Silverlight. I would tend toward Silverlight since I'm quite experienced .NET developer while I have just a basic knowledge of ASP.NET controls.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
If you're a seasoned .NET developer and have basic knowledge of ASP.NET controls, both options are viable for you. However, each framework has its pros and cons which depend on your needs and goals. 

ASP.NET MVC is a great option if you wish to use a more traditional HTML/CSS/JavaScript-based web application and want more control over your HTML. ASP.NET is widely used, has a large community, and supports a variety of third-party libraries. This framework is good if you aim to create a site with a lot of server-side functionality and want the site to be highly testable and modular. MVC pattern helps to manage complexity by dividing an application into the model (data), the view (user interface), and the controller (processes that handle input).

However, if you want a rich internet application with a desktop-like user interface, and have a target audience with high-speed internet connections, then Silverlight may be a better option. But you should consider that Microsoft officially stopped supporting Silverlight in October 2021 and recommended to developers that they should move to other platforms such as HTML5. 

Remember that building a web application entirely on Silverlight can limit its accessibility because it requires users to have a Silverlight plug-in installed. Thus, it is often best used for developing parts of a web application (like complex UI components), rather than a complete website.

So, in short, if you want a maintainable, scalable, and SEO-friendly traditional web application, go for ASP.NET MVC. If your project requires rich, complex UI controls and animations, and you don't have to worry about SEO or support from non-Windows platforms, you might want to consider Silverlight knowing the lack of support from Microsoft. However, with the formal discontinuation of Silverlight, it might be better to lean toward ASP.NET MVC or look at more current technologies like Blazor for a more strategic web development approach.
<|end|>


