Formulate Kubernetes deployment and service YAML files to facilitate the deployment of a multitude of services on Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS) and establish inter-service communication protocols.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Creating Kubernetes deployment and service YAML files requires a clear understanding of the application's infrastructure needs. Below, we will create a simple example for a hypothetical HTTP server application.

Deployment YAML:

```yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: http-server-deployment
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: my-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: my-app
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: http-server
        image: my-http-server:1.0.0
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
```

This file named `deployment.yaml` creates a Deployment that runs 3 replicas of an HTTP server application. The image `my-http-server:1.0.0` is a placeholder, replace it with your actual image. The `app: my-app` is a label to help identify the pods in this deployment.

Service YAML:

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: http-server-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: my-app
  ports:
  - protocol: TCP
    port: 80
    targetPort: 80
  type: LoadBalancer
```

This file named `service.yaml` creates a LoadBalancer service that exposes your HTTP server application to the internet on port 80. The `selector` is used to determine which pods to route traffic to. In this case, it matches the label set in the Deployment.

Please be sure to replace the placeholders with your actual image, application name, port numbers etc. and take care that the volumes, CPU, and memory are designed to suit the needs of your application. 

For EKS, you would then apply these configs with `kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml` and `kubectl apply -f service.yaml`.

For inter-service communication protocols, you would typically define endpoints in your applications which other services will call, possibly with a service discovery mechanism. It depends mostly on how your services are designed to interact. If they are HTTP-based apps, they can just make HTTP calls to the other service's URL.
<|end|>


