Here is the Java version of your code:

```java

import java.util.*;

public class Main {

    public static Object[] reverseCaseSpecial(String string) {

        StringBuilder modifiedString = new StringBuilder();
        Map<Character, Integer> charCount = new HashMap<>();
        
        for (char c : string.toCharArray()) {
            // Convert uppercase to lowercase and vice versa
            if (Character.isLetter(c)) {
                char modifiedChar = Character.isUpperCase(c) ? Character.toLowerCase(c) : Character.toUpperCase(c);
                modifiedString.append(modifiedChar);
                
                //Tally the instances of each modified character
                charCount.put(modifiedChar, charCount.getOrDefault(modifiedChar, 0) + 1);
            }
            // Convert integers to their closest even counterpart
            else if (Character.isDigit(c)) {
                int num = Character.getNumericValue(c);
                char modifiedChar = (char)((num % 2 == 0) ? (num + '0') : ((num + 1) + '0'));
                modifiedString.append(modifiedChar);
                
                //Tally the instances of each modified character
                charCount.put(modifiedChar, charCount.getOrDefault(modifiedChar, 0) + 1);
            }
            // Double special symbols
            else {
                modifiedString.append(c);
                modifiedString.append(c);
                
                //Tally the instances of each modified character
                charCount.put(c, charCount.getOrDefault(c, 0) + 2);
            }
        }

        Object[] result = {modifiedString.toString(), charCount};
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
      String test = "ABC$123";
      Object[] result = reverseCaseSpecial(test);
      System.out.println("Modified String: " + result[0]);
      System.out.println("Character count: " + result[1].toString());
    }
}
```

Note: This Java version returns an `Object[]` because a method cant return more than one data type or object. You can typecast `result[0]` to `String` and `result[1]` to `Map<Character, Integer>` to access their content. Feel free to adjust the code based on how you want to structure your java project.
