<|asst|>
Here is a simple sorting algorithm known as "Bubble Sort". This algorithm repeatedly steps through the array, compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they are in the wrong order. The pass through the array is repeated until the list is sorted. Bubble Sort is not the most efficient algorithm, but it is simple to understand.

```C++
#include <iostream>

using namespace std;

// Function to print an array
void printArray(int arr[], int size)
{
    int i;
    for (i = 0; i < size; i++)
        cout << arr[i] << " ";
    cout << endl;
}

// Function to implement bubble sort
void bubbleSort(int arr[], int size)
{
    int i, j;
    for (i = 0; i < size-1; i++)

    // Last i elements are already in place   
    for (j = 0; j < size-i-1; j++)
        if (arr[j] > arr[j+1])
            swap(arr[j], arr[j+1]);
}

int main()
{
    int arr[] = {9, 4, 7, 3, 1};
    int n = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);
    bubbleSort(arr, n);
    cout<<"Sorted array: \n";
    printArray(arr, n);
    return 0;
}
```

This code starts by declaring a function to print arrays, then a function to perform the bubble sort. In the main function, an array is declared and the size determined. The bubbleSort function is invoked on the array, and then the sorted array is printed using the printArray function. 

The bubbleSort function itself uses two loops - one to traverse through the entire array, and another to compare each element with the next one. If the next element is smaller, the elements are swapped.

Bubble sort has a worst-case and average time complexity of O(n²), where n is the number of items being sorted.  This makes the bubble sort well-suited to small or mostly sorted datasets.
<|end|>


