Create a basic website that uses responsive web design principles.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Creating a website with responsive design requires HTML, CSS and sometimes Javascript knowledge. Given the limitation of this platform, here's a simple example of how a responsive web design could be achieved using HTML and CSS.

HTML:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title>Responsive Website</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="main.css">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  </head>
  <body>
    <header>
      <h1>Welcome to My Responsive Website!</h1>
    </header>

    <main>
      <section>
        <h2>About</h2>
        <p>This is a simple responsive website.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>Contact us through this email: example@example.com</p>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer>
      <p>Copyright 2021. All rights reserved.</p>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
```

main.css:
```css
body {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}

header, main, footer {
    padding: 20px;
}

h1, h2 {
    color: #333;
}

footer {
    background-color: #333;
    color: #fff;
}

/* Responsive styles */
@media screen and (max-width: 600px) {
    body {
        font-size: 18px;
    }

    h1, h2 {
        font-size: 24px;
    }
}
```

This layout is very simple, but the core structure of a responsive website is displayed. You have a `<header>`,`<main>`,`<footer>` and each of them have some content within them. The `<meta>` viewport tag in the `<head>` ensures the site will scale properly on all devices.

The CSS makes the site responsive. It styles the body text and header colors. The "@media screen..." part says that when the screen is 600px wide or less, changes the body and header font sizes. This makes it easier to read on smaller devices.

Remember, this is a basic example of a responsive website. Normally, you would have navigation menus, images, multiple pages, etc. Applying responsive design principles to these elements involves more complex CSS and possibly JavaScript, but the basic principle — using media queries to adjust your layout for different screen sizes — remains the same.
<|end|>


