Here's a Python function that creates a set from the input list to remove duplicates, converts this set back to a list, and then picks a random word:

```python
import random

def get_random_word(words_list):
  words_set = set(words_list) # convert list to set to remove duplicates
  words_list_unique = list(words_set) # convert set back to list
  random_word = random.choice(words_list_unique) # pick a random word
  print(random_word)

words_list = ['cat', 'dog', 'ball', 'toy', 'cat', 'ball']
get_random_word(words_list)
```

Every time you will run this function, it will print one of the words 'cat', 'dog', 'ball', or 'toy' - chosen randomly. Set does not allow duplicates, so each word is guaranteed to print out only once no matter how many times it appears in the original list.
