<|user|>
Assuming undirected graph of a-b-c. One knows the joint $p(a,b,c)=\frac{1}{Z}\phi(a,b)\phi(b,c)$ What is the conditional probability $p(a|c)$?
So far I know that $p(a|c)=\frac{p(a,c)}{p(c)}=\frac{\sum_b p(a,b,c)}{\sum_a \sum_b p(a,b,c)}=\frac{\frac{1}{z}\sum_b\phi(a,b)\phi(b,c)}{\frac{1}{z}\sum_a \sum_b\phi(a,b)\phi(b,c)}=\frac{\mu_a(b)\mu_b(c)}{\mu_b (c)}=\mu_a(b)$
Is this correct?
The reason Im unsure is because the message is clearly supposed to be passing from c to b to a, while my numerator before simplicfication contains messages from a to b then b to c.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To calculate the conditional probability $p(a|c)$, you would need to marginalize over the variable $b$. The correct way of calculating $p(a|c)$ is given by:

$p(a|c) = \frac{\sum_b p(a,b,c)}{\sum_{a,b}p(a,b,c)}$

Substitute for $p(a,b,c)$:

$p(a|c) = \frac{\sum_b \phi(a,b)\phi(b,c)}{\sum_{a,b} \phi(a,b)\phi(b,c)}$

Take note that to marginalize over $b$, you are summing over all possible values of $b$. Hence, the final result is not simply equal to $\mu_a(b)$, where $\mu_a(b)$ is the marginal of $a$ over $b$. 

In message passing algorithms, there indeed will be messages passed from $c$ to $b$ and from $b$ to $a$, however, these messages interact in a way that is not simply just passing the messages along. In the marginalization process, the contribution from each message is combined together for each possible state of the variable being marginalized over.

Finally, the conditional probability $p(a|c)$ does not depend on the variable $b$. It is a distribution over the variable $a$ given the variable $c$. Hence, the notation of $\mu_a(b)$ is incorrect, since the right hand side should not contain $b$.
<|end|>


