Designing a data conduit system linking a web-based application to Redshift would entail various facets, including the ingestion, parsing, and analysis of logs.

Step 1: Configuration of the Web-Based Application 

The web-based application should be configured to record all relevant user activities and events. The logs should display clear, understandable indicators of user interactions, which can be extracted and analyzed later. 

Step 2: Set Up of Amazon Redshift 

Amazon Redshift is a cloud-based data warehousing service that requires configuration. It includes setting up the clustering environment, schema design, creating databases, tables, and more importantly, configuring the necessary security settings.

Step 3: Data Ingestion 

The next step would be setting up a data pipeline to transport data from the web application to Redshift. AWS offers different services like Amazon Kinesis, AWS Glue, and AWS Data Pipeline which can help to transfer logs. This process should recognize, inquire, and process the logs, transforming data into a format that can be handled by Redshift.

Step 4: Parsing of Logs 

Once the data is on Redshift, it would be parsed. Parsing converts data into a format that could be analyzed more readily. This process might require different tools or software, such AWS Glue or custom scripts.

Step 5: Analysis of Data 

With data now in Redshift and parsed, now comes the analysis part. SQL queries would be developed to extract and interpret the patterns and behaviors of users. Depending upon requirement one can use various analytical tools which have integration with Amazon Redshift like Quicksight, Tableau, Looker etc.

Step 6: Results Interpretation and Decision Making

Once the queries have been executed and data has been analysed it can be reported or visualised using different software tools and this data can be used for making business decisions.

This entire pipeline should be designed considering performance, cost-effectiveness, and security. Also, regular reviews and improvements should be carried out to ensure that the system remains efficient and accurate. 

