Sure, I'll give a basic SQL query that meets the requirements. Note that this assumes the "RentalDate" column is a Date or DateTime, that your SQL server is SQL Server, and that the current date/time is subtracted by 7 days to get the "last week".

```
SELECT SUM(RentalFee) as TotalRentalCost
FROM Rentals
WHERE RentalDate >= DATEADD(day, -7, GETDATE())
```

This SQL query will sum up all the "RentalFee" for the records in the "Rentals" table where the "RentalDate" is within the previous week.

However, to make this query performant on large tables, consider the index on the "RentalDate" column. Without an index, the database must scan the entire table to find relevant rows, which can be slow for large tables. With an index, the database can quickly narrow down the potential rows to a much smaller subset.

Including an index in your design mostly improves the performance by minimising the number of disk I/O operations. 

Here is how you can add an index on the RentalDate column:

```
CREATE INDEX idx_Rentals_RentalDate ON Rentals (RentalDate)
```

With this index, your SQL Server will be able to execute the above query much faster because it can find the relevant rows without scanning the entire table.
<|end|>


