How does the following CSS style the first letter of a paragraph within a div container with a specific class attribute?
```css
div.container p::first-letter {
    font-weight: bold;
    font-size: 1.5em;
}
```
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This CSS code applies styles to the first letter of a paragraph inside a `div` having the class `container`. 

- `div.container` is a selector which targets all `div` elements with a class attribute value of `container`.
- `p` is another selector which refers to paragraph elements inside the `div.container`.
- `::first-letter` is a pseudo-element that selects the first letter of a block-level element (in this case the first letter of each paragraph `p`).

The styles applied are:
1. `font-weight: bold;` makes the first letter of each paragraph bold.
2. `font-size: 1.5em;` makes the first letter of each paragraph 1.5 times larger than the default font size of the paragraph.

So, if there are multiple paragraphs inside a `div` with the class `container`, the first letter of each of those paragraphs will be bold and 1.5 times larger than the rest of the text in the paragraph.

Example:
If we apply this CSS to the following HTML:

```html
<div class="container">
  <p>This is a paragraph.</p>
  <p>Another paragraph.</p>
</div>
```
The result will be:

The first letter "T" in "This is a paragraph." and "A" in "Another paragraph." will be bold and 1.5 times bigger than the other letters.
<|end|>


