This piece of code indeed has several errors. All parts of the code contain wrong variable names, syntax errors and gibberish words. Here is the sanitised version:

```python
import re
import ast

def relocate_numbers(input_string):
    # Split the string into numbers and non-numbers
    split_string = re.findall(r"[+-]?[0-9]+(?:\.\d*)?(?:[eE][+-]?[0-9]+)?|[-zA-Z,!?\\-]+|0[xX][0-9a-fA-F]+|0[oO][0-7]+|0[bB][01]+", input_string)

    distinct_numbers = set()
    sum_of_all_numbers = 0
    non_numeric_strings = []
    numeric_strings = []

    for s in split_string:
        try:
            # Try to convert to number
            number = ast.literal_eval(s)
            distinct_numbers.add(number)
            sum_of_all_numbers += number
            numeric_strings.append(s)
        except (ValueError, SyntaxError):
            # If it is not a number, add to non-numeric strings
            non_numeric_strings.append(s)

    # Join all strings back together, moving numbers to the end
    result_string = ''.join(non_numeric_strings + numeric_strings)

    return result_string, len(distinct_numbers), sum_of_all_numbers

relocated_string, count_of_distinct_numbers, sum_of_all_numbers = relocate_numbers(input_string)

print('Relocated string:', relocated_string)
print('Count of distinct numbers:', count_of_distinct_numbers)
print('Sum of all numbers:', sum_of_all_numbers)
```
Please note that script requires a correctly formulated `input_string` variable on the last call for `relocate_numbers`.
