<|user|>
If you call javascript window.open and pass a url to a .xls file it open on some machines in the browser window.  How can you force it into Excel?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To force JavaScript to open the .xls file in Excel and not in the browser, you have to modify the HTTP headers of the file. You have to set the Content-Type to the MIME type of Excel, which is `application/vnd.ms-excel` or `application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet` for the newer .xlsx files. Also, you have to set the Content-Disposition to `attachment; filename="<file name>"` to trigger the download.

Because JavaScript that runs on the browser doesn't have the power to set these HTTP headers, this must be done on the server side. Here is a basic example in PHP:

```php
<?php
$file = 'path_to_file.xls';

header('Content-Type: application/vnd.ms-excel');
header('Content-Disposition: attachment; filename=' . urlencode(basename($file)));

readfile($file);
```

In the example above, PHP sets the HTTP headers and then reads the file content to the output buffer, effectively sending the file as a download to the client.

If you need to do this on a different server-side technology, you will have to find the equivalent ways of modifying HTTP headers and reading file content.

After setting this up on your server side, you must also have to change the URL you're giving to `window.open` in JavaScript to the URL of this server-side script.

But, another important thing is that it still requires that the user's machine has Excel installed to be able to understand and open the file type.
<|end|>


