I created an nested array with empty array for each item. I want to input the data to each index with push method. But instead of inserting to each item the data is filled to all item. Could someone help me explain this behavior? Thanks

let input = new Array(4).fill([]);
let data = [[0,1],[3,2],[2,1]];
for(let i = 0; i < data.length; i++){
   const key = data[i][1]
   const value = data[i][0]
   input[key].push(value)
}
console.log(input)
<|end|>
<|asst|>
When using `let input = new Array(4).fill([]);`, you're creating an array with four elements, but because you're using `fill([])`, you're filling all four slots with reference to the _same_ empty array. So when you add an element to one "slot", it's seen as adding to the same array, and thus appears in all four slots.

In JavaScript, objects and arrays are reference types. When you pass them around, you're passing around a reference to the actual object, not a fresh copy.

So when you use `fill([])`, you're creating a new empty array once, and then filling the four slots in your new array with references to that one array.

When you want to create an array of arrays, you'll need to create a new array for each slot. Here's how you do it:

```
let input = Array.from({length: 4}, () => []);
let data = [[0,1],[3,2],[2,1]];
for(let i = 0; i < data.length; i++){
   const key = data[i][1]
   const value = data[i][0]
   input[key].push(value)
}
console.log(input)
```

In this version, each slot in input array is filled with a new, unique array.
<|end|>


