<|user|>
Write JavaScript code to change the color, add shadow, and underline a <h1> tag when the mouse pointer hovers over it.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can achieve that via JavaScript by adding event listeners to the `mouseover` and `mouseout` events on the `<h1>` tag. But, it is more advisable and much cleaner to handle these kind of effects and styling changes using CSS.

Here's a simple example of how you can do this using JavaScript:

```html
<html>
<body>
  <h1 id="myH1">Hello World</h1>
  <script>
    var h1tag = document.getElementById('myH1');

    h1tag.addEventListener('mouseover', function() {
      h1tag.style.color = 'red';
      h1tag.style.textShadow = '2px 2px 8px #000000';
      h1tag.style.textDecoration = 'underline';
    });

    h1tag.addEventListener('mouseout', function() {
      h1tag.style.color = 'black';
      h1tag.style.textShadow = 'none';
      h1tag.style.textDecoration = 'none';
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This JavaScript code targets the `<h1>` tag with the ID `myH1`. When you hover over that tag, the JavaScript code changes the text color to red, adds a text shadow, and underlines the text. When you move your cursor off the tag, the JavaScript changes those styles back to their initial state.

But, a much cleaner, efficient and suitable way of achieving this would be using CSS pseudo selectors like `:hover`.

Here's how to do it using CSS:

```html
<html>
<style>
  h1:hover {
    color: red;
    text-shadow: 2px 2px 8px #000000;
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
</style>
<body>
  <h1>Hello World</h1>
</body>
</html>
```

In this example, CSS targets the `<h1>` tag when hovered over and it applies the same styles as the JavaScript example. When the mouse pointer is moved off the tag, the styles are automatically reverted back by the browser.
<|end|>


