I have a data set with 29 features 8 if them have missing values.
I've tried Sklearn simple imputer and all it's strategies
KNN imputer and several Number of K
Iterative imputer and all combinations of imputation order , estimators, number of iterations.
My question is how to evaluate the imputation techniques and choose the better one for my Data.
I can't run a base line model and evaluate it's performance because I'm not familiar with balancing the data and tunning the parameters and all models are giving poor scores.
There's why I'm searching if there's another way to evaluate the data imputation techniques
Something like evaluation of distribution or something like that.
I'm very newbie btw so pardon my stupidity
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Evaluating an imputation method is directly linked with the performance of the final model. But there are also few steps you can consider to get a sense of how each imputation method is working:

1) **Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA):** Look at the distributions of values for each feature before and after imputation. Does the imputed data roughly follow the same distribution as the original data or does it distort it significantly? If the distribution is too distorted, that might be a sign that the imputation was not appropriate.

2) **Univariate Statistical Measures:** Calculate mean, median, variance for each feature before and after imputation. See if the imputation technique significantly changes these. Also remember, it might be okay for there to be some change because the imputation technique is adding information to the dataset, but dramatic changes could be a red flag.

3) **Missingness Patterns:** Consider the nature of the missing data in your dataset. Is it missing completely at random (MCAR), missing at random (MAR), or missing not at random (MNAR). Different methods of imputation handle these differently. For example, simple imputation (replacing with mean or median) usually works well with MCAR type of missingness, but could produce highly biased result in MNAR or even MAR cases. On the other hand, iterative imputation or K-NN might be a better method for MAR and MNAR type of missingness.

4) **Simulation Studies:** If feasible, a rigorous way could be to conduct a "leave-one-out" simulation study. Here's how to do it: randomly choose some values to set as missing and then impute them. Compare the imputed values with the true values that you left out. Repeat this for many iterations and combinations of features/values. This can give you an overall sense of how well the used imputation method is working.

Remember, in the end the best method would depend on your final model's performance and specific details about the variables/features you are working with. Different imputation methods might work better/worse for different features depending on their distribution, amount of missing data and relationship with other features.
<|end|>


