<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is how you can construct an HTML form with all the requirements:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<body>

<form action="/submit_here" method="post" id="myForm">
  <label for="username">Username:</label><br>
  <input type="text" id="username" name="username"><br>
  <input type="checkbox" id="remember" name="remember">
  <label for="remember"> Remember me</label><br>
  <input type="submit" value="Submit">
</form> 

</body>
</html>
```

Here's a brief explanation of the components and attributes used in the HTML structure:

- `<form action="/submit_here" method="post" id="myForm">`: This is the form tag with an `action` attribute which states where to send the form-data when a form is submitted. We also specify a method attribute which defines how to send form-data (the form-data is sent to the page specified in the action attribute). `id` is for uniquely identifying the form.
  
- `<input type="text" id="username" name="username">`: This is a text input field where the user can input their username. `id` and `name` are both attributes which can be used to uniquely identify this text field.
  
- `<input type="checkbox" id="remember" name="remember">`: This is a checkbox input field which user can check/uncheck. The label "Remember me" is given in the following line using `<label>` tag. Again `id` and `name` are used for identifying this checkbox.

- `<input type="submit" value="Submit">`: This is the submit button for the form. Once clicked, the form-data will be sent to the page specified in the `action` attribute of the `<form>`element.
