<|asst|>
The view of the cosmos as merely a three-dimensional space is a classical notion at odds with the intricacies revealed by modern theoretical physics. Exploring profound questions about the architecture of the cosmos urge us to consider dimensions beyond the traditional length, width, and depth. The possibility of higher dimensions is not a facetious speculation, but a serious proposition under robust mathematical and conceptual frameworks such as M-theory.

In contrast to classical mechanics, quantum mechanics demands a different interpretation of the cosmos – one linked to potential realities in higher dimensions. As a natural evolution of previous string theories, M-theory uniquely proposes the existence of eleven dimensions - a revelation that might fundamentally redefine our understanding of space-time.

M-theory saves string theory by facilitating the five different versions of string theories to coexist, incorporating dimensions and strings, ultimately unifying quantum mechanics with Einstein's General Relativity, thus unifying the two prime yet incompatible pillars of theoretical physics. It does so through the propagation of these strings vibrating in an eleven-dimensional space.

The ramifications on our understanding of celestial phenomena are profound, starting with black holes. In the context of higher dimensions, black holes aren't mere points but higher dimensional analogs called 'black branes'. These 'black branes' in the M-theory could potentially emit gravitational waves, akin to the ripples caused by a stone thrown into a pond. 

Moreover, additional dimensions also introduce an intriguing concept of 'Cosmic Strings' - one-dimensional defects in space-time. These strings, existing in higher dimensions, could have potentially played a crucial part in the structure formation of the universe and can also provide subtle hints towards the possibility of parallel universes.

The concept of higher dimensions and their impact fundamentally redefines the Big Bang theory and the formation of the cosmic structure. The higher-dimensional Big Bang could be considered as a collision between two ‘Branes’ in eleven-dimensional space-time. These Branes, proposed in M-theory, are objects that exist in several dimensions. Brane collisions would have initiated a vast amount of heat and light, subsequently giving birth to our universe.

Beyond our current cosmos’s structural formation, the understanding of these higher dimensions promises radical alterations in comprehending the universe's evolution. Hypotheses like 'Braneworlds' propose that our universe exists on a 4-dimensional Brane, floating in the higher-dimensional space (Bulk). This allows room for parallel universes’ possibilities, given each Brane could act as a separate universe.

Our understanding of the fundamental essence of reality also evolves via the higher dimensions concept. The existence of wormholes, bridges between different areas of space-time, finds mathematically more feasible existences in higher dimensions. Moreover, it brings clarity to the bizarre yet extraordinary concept of quantum entanglement, often described as 'spooky action at a distance'. Higher dimensions could provide the spatial 'shortcut' for entangled particles to interact instantaneously.

The dawn of M-Theory and its requisite dimensions could be a gateway to a more profound understanding of the reality we inhabit. It offers not just notions of new cosmic structures, but fundamentally different explanations for well-established celestial phenomena. Although currently we cannot directly perceive these extra dimensions, we continue our quest to reveal the cosmos's hidden dimensions, with the hope of finding the ultimate theory of everything. The confirmation of these incredibly bold theories could indeed be the most profound scientific revelation in human history, radically altering our perception of the universe's origin, its future path, and its multi-dimensional reality.
<|end|>


