I have created a database model in Visio Professional (2003). I know that the Enterprise version has the ability to create a DB in SQL Server based on the data in Visio. I do not have the option to install Enterprise. Aside from going through the entire thing one table and relationship at a time and creating the whole database from scratch, by hand, can anyone recommend any tool/utility/method for converting the visio database model into a SQL Script that can be used to create a new DB in SQL Server?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a way to convert a Visio database model to SQL Script:

1. First, export your model from Visio to an XML file (File > Save As > XML file).
2. Next, you can import your XML file into Database Workbench (or another database design tool that supports importing from XML). These tools often support generating a SQL create script from the design.
3. Once you've imported the XML into the tool, you can generate a SQL script (often under an option like "Export to SQL" or "Generate SQL script").
4. From there, you can run this SQL script in your SQL Server environment to create your database.

This general process should work for most database design tools that support importing from Visio's XML format.

Another alternate tool can be `DeZign for Databases`:

1. You import your Visio database model into DeZign for Databases.
2. Once imported, you can generate an SQL script within DeZign for Databases via the Generate menu > SQL or Generate SQL option.

Another way is to hire a programmer to write a custom program for translating the Visio XML file to the SQL Create script.

Please note that some functionality or relationships may not be imported accurately depending on the complexity of your model. Always verify your SQL script and database structure after creating it from a design tool (whether Visio or otherwise). 

And lastly - please do bear in mind that Visio 2003, being nearly 20 years old now, may not support more modern database features or structures - depending on the complexity of your database, you may find it worthwhile to look into more recent versions of Visio, or more specialized database design tools.
<|end|>


