You can dynamically add columns to a ListView by creating a `GridView` and then adding columns to that `GridView` in code. Here is a sample snippet that will give you an idea about this:

```csharp
void CreateColumns(DataTable dataTable)
{
    ListView listView = new ListView();
    GridView gridView = new GridView();
    listView.View = gridView;

    // add columns
    foreach (DataColumn column in dataTable.Columns)
    {
        GridViewColumn gridViewColumn = new GridViewColumn();
        gridViewColumn.DisplayMemberBinding = new Binding(column.ColumnName);
        gridViewColumn.Header = column.ColumnName;
        gridView.Columns.Add(gridViewColumn);
    }

    listView.ItemsSource = dataTable.DefaultView;

    // set the ListView to the MainWindow's content
    this.Content = listView;
}

DataTable CreateSampleData()
{
    DataTable dataTable = new DataTable();
    dataTable.Columns.Add("Name", typeof(string));
    dataTable.Columns.Add("Age", typeof(int));
    dataTable.Columns.Add("Email", typeof(string));

    dataTable.Rows.Add("John", 25, "john@gmail.com");
    dataTable.Rows.Add("Alex", 27, "alex@yahoo.com");
    dataTable.Rows.Add("Mike", 30, "mike@hotmail.com");
    return dataTable;
}

// Usage
private void MainWindow_Loaded(object sender, RoutedEventArgs e)
{
    DataTable myDataset = CreateSampleData();
    CreateColumns(myDataset);
}
```

In the CreateColumns method, we are creating new GridViewColumns for each DataColumn in our DataTable. We bind the display by setting the DisplayMemberBinding property to a new Binding that uses the column's ColumnName. After all the columns are added, we set the ItemsSource of the ListView to the DefaultView of the DataTable. This will automatically generate the rows.
<|end|>


