Anyone doing any work using "offline" forms?  We have an application that requires inputting data from outside our company.  I was thinking about sending a form out via email, allowing the form to be filled out then sent back.  Obviously a web application would be the best solution, but management doesn't seem ready to build the infrastructure and security to support that.  I've read a little about PDF forms is that a good solution or are there other solutions?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, offline forms can be a good solution if you are not ready to invest in a web application yet. It gives people flexibility to fill out the information anytime without the need of an internet connection. 

The most common way to do this is to use PDF forms, as you have mentioned. They are easy to create and can be filled out using free software (like Adobe Reader) which most people already have installed on their computers. They also provide a relatively clean and professional appearance and are easy to print, if necessary. 

Another option is to use Word document forms, which can also be filled out offline, but they might not look as professional as PDFs since anyone can easily edit the layout. Also, with Word, you'll need to instruct your recipients how to enable the 'Developer' tab to access form controls, which can be a bit tricky for non-technical people.

Excel spreadsheet is great if you need to collect numeric data or answers to multiple-choice questions (it can automate calculations and create graphs), but it's not great for long, text-based responses. 

Google Forms too allows the functionality where it syncs the collected data once the user is back online. But this requires the user to open the form when they are online and then keep the form open until they reconnect to the internet.

Remember, whichever tool you choose, the most important thing is to make your forms as clear and simple as possible. 

Also, keep in mind that the collecting and integrating data from offline solutions might require more manual work (like data entry), unless you use a tool that allows you to easily export and import the data into your database. 

Finally, while offline forms eliminate the need for constant internet access, they still involve sending sensitive information over the internet when the form is returned, so make sure to use a secure method (like encrypted email) to protect your data.
<|end|>


