Write a concise piece of JavaScript code that conducts a comprehensive validation process for a phone number using regular expressions.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In JavaScript, you can use regular expressions (regex) to match patterns in strings. Here's a simple piece of JavaScript code that validates a phone number format conforming to these conditions:

- The phone number can start with an optional '+' sign indicating the international dialing code.
- Followed by any number of spaces, dashes or parenthesis.
- After that comes the main number part which is exactly 10 digits (not including the optional '+' sign).
- These 10 digits can have spaces, dashes or periods in between them.

Please note, this is a very standard/basic phone number validation and might not cover all the variations existent worldwide.

```javascript
function validatePhoneNumber(phoneInput) {
  /* The regular expression for phone number validation:
  - ^\+? allows optional '+' at the start
  - [\d\s-.()]*$ allows digits, spaces, dashes, periods, and parentheses in the input, 
    and it tests the entire number input from start (^) to finish ($).
  */
  var phoneRegex = /^\+?[\d\s-.()]*$/;
  
  // Test the input against the regex
  var isValid = phoneRegex.test(phoneInput);

  // Remove any non-digit characters for final length check
  phoneInput = phoneInput.replace(/\D/g, '');

  // Check if the phone number has exactly 10 digits
  isValid = isValid && (phoneInput.length === 10);

  return isValid;
}
```
Usage:

```javascript
console.log(validatePhoneNumber("+1 (123) 456-7890")); // Outputs: true
console.log(validatePhoneNumber("123.456.7890")); // Outputs: true
console.log(validatePhoneNumber("1234567890")); // Outputs: true
console.log(validatePhoneNumber("123456789")); // Outputs: false
console.log(validatePhoneNumber("12345678901")); // Outputs: false
console.log(validatePhoneNumber("123-456-78900")); // Outputs: false
console.log(validatePhoneNumber("abc1234567")); // Outputs: false
```
<|end|>


