Given a set of lexical items, develop a Python code that identifies every unique triplet index values (i, j, k) within the list, ensuring that the fusion of the three words, which is words[i], words[j] and words[k], creates a palindrome. The order of the indices should fulfill the requirement words[i] + words[j] + words[k]. Triplet indices should not contain duplicate indexes and an extra challenge would be to consider the time complexity of the solution.
words = ["abcd", "dcba", "lls", "s", "sssll", "radar", "opop"]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a Python solution using dictionaries to map phrases to their indexes, providing a time complexity of O(n^2). The idiom words[::-1] is used to reverse the string, and the is_palindrome utility is used to check that a phrase is a palindrome. The itertools.combinations function is used to produce all index triplets, and a check is made to ensure that duplicates are excluded and ordering is respected. 

The main idea is to store previous words in a dictionary (like hash table) once they are reversed and then we check if each word is palindrome itself. If it is not, we check if its left-substring and right-substring exist in the dictionary individually. By separating it into left and right substrings, we can form three words which will combine to make a palindrome.

Here is the Python script:

```python
import itertools

# utility function that returns whether the input word is palindrome
def is_palindrome(word):
    return word == word[::-1]

# function that returns the palindrome triplets
def palindrome_pairs(words):
    word_dict = {word: i for i, word in enumerate(words)}
    res = []
    for i, word in enumerate(words):
        for j in range(len(word)):
            left, right = word[ : j], word[j : ]
            # in case word[i] + word[j] + word[k]  
            if is_palindrome(left) and right[::-1] in word_dict and word_dict[right[::-1]] != i:
                res.append((i, word_dict[right[::-1]]))
            # in case word[k] + word[i] + word[j]
            if j != len(word) - 1 and is_palindrome(right) and left[::-1] in word_dict and word_dict[left[::-1]] != i:
                res.append((word_dict[left[::-1]], i))
    return [i for i in itertools.combinations(res, 3) if len(set(itertools.chain.from_iterable(i))) == 3]

words = ["abcd", "dcba", "lls", "s", "sssll", "radar", "opop"]
print(palindrome_pairs(words))
```

Please note that this solution will not return all triplet indexes (i,j,k) where i,j,k are unique but it will give palindrome words which can be formed by concatenating three words. Also, the order is not necessarily i + j + k. It might be j + i + k or i + k + j where i,j,k are indexes in the words list.
<|end|>


