I'm currently designing a program that will involve some physics (nothing too fancy, a few balls crashing to each other)

What's the most exact datatype I can use to represent position (without a feeling of discrete jumps) in c#?

Also, what's the smallest ammount of time I can get between t and t+1? One tick?

EDIT: Clarifying: What is the smallest unit of time in C#? `[TimeSpan].Tick`?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The most precise numerical data type you can use for the positions in C# is the `double` datatype. It offers about 15-16 digits of precision over a large range of magnitudes. If you want to use integers, you might consider using the `long` datatype.

As per your question regarding time, yes, the smallest unit of time in C# is indeed `TimeSpan.Tick`. One tick represents 100 nanoseconds or one ten-millionth of a second. 

However, it's worth noting that just because a `Tick` is the smallest unit of time, it does not mean that C# can actually measure time increments as small as a single tick accurately. The actual precision will depend on your computer's hardware and the Windows scheduler. The actual accuracy for Stopwatch, for example, can be found in Stopwatch.Frequency.

Also, remember that in a physics simulation, the timestep you choose for your simulation can significantly affect the accuracy and stability of the simulation - it's a common requirement in simulations to have a fixed timestep to ensure stable and reproducible results.

Be aware of round-off errors. In numerical calculations, the difference between the calculated result and the exact result may accumulate over time, which can lead to visible inaccuracies in your physics simulation. Keeping track of the time using `DateTime.Now` for each frame and then calculating the time difference might introduce too many rounding errors. A better solution to get the elapsed time would be to use a `Stopwatch` instance.
<|end|>


