I've followed this otherwise [excellent tutorial](http://howtoforge.com/ubuntu-8.04-server-install-xen-from-ubuntu-repositories) on getting Xen working with Ubuntu but am not able to get a console into my virtual machine (domU). I've got the `extra = '2 console=xvc0'` line in my /etc/xen/.cfg file like they say, but am not able to get a console on it.

If I statically assign an IP to the VM I can SSH to it, but for now I need to be able to use DHCP to give it an address (and since that's what I'm trying to debug, there's the problem). I know I've got a free DHCP address (although I'm getting more at the moment), so I don't think that's the problem. I've looked on Google and the Xen forums to no avail as well.

Any ideas?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can try this method:

1. First tail the log file during vm startup. Type:

    tail -f /var/log/xen/qemu-dm-vmName.log

Replace `vmName` with the actual name.

You should see something like:

    Started domain vmName console=1 vnc=1 vncunused=1

This tells you that your VM has started successfully. Now to access the console:

2. On your terminal type:

    xm console vmName

Replace `vmName` with the actual name. If it works you should be able to see the boot process of your VM.

If you can't see the process, make sure you have configured your firewall to allow connection to the VNC port assigned, and also VNC is enabled and configured properly within your VM.

You can also make sure you are running your session as a root user. The Xen tools usually need root access to manage the domains.

It's also worth noting that typically, xvc0 is used in place of hvc0 as the qemu-dm log highlights a console parameter.
Often, changing the `console=xvc0` part of the `extra` option to `console=hvc0` in your VM's config file located at /etc/xen/vm or /etc/xen/auto can resolve this issue. 

Final config file should be:

    kernel  = '/boot/vmlinuz-...'
    ramdisk = '/boot/initrd.img-...'
    extra   = '2 console=hvc0'

Now, restart your VM using `xm create -c vmName` again.
If this works, you will be able to see the boot process of the VM on your terminal.
<|end|>


