I would like to see this in Perl form:
def check_anagram_partitions(text: str, partitions: int) -> bool:
    length = len(text)
    if length % partitions != 0:
        # If the length isn't evenly divisible by the number of partitions, return False
        return False

    partition_length = length // partitions
    sorted_text = sorted(text)

    for i in range(0, length, partition_length):
        partition = sorted_text[i:i + partition_length]
        if partition != sorted_text[0:partition_length]:
            # If any partition isn't an anagram of the first, return False
            return False

    return True
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is the Python function translated to Perl:

```perl
sub check_anagram_partitions {
    my ($text, $partitions) = @_;
    my $length = length($text);
    
    return 0 if $length % $partitions;
    
    my $partition_length = $length / $partitions;
    my @sorted_text = sort split '', $text;

    for my $i (0 .. $partition_length .. $length - 1) {
        my @partition = @sorted_text[$i .. $i + $partition_length - 1];
        return 0 if "@partition" ne "@sorted_text[0 .. $partition_length - 1]";
    }

    return 1;
}
```
You can use this function in Perl script like this:

```perl
if(check_anagram_partitions('eatate', 3)) {
    print "True\n";
} else {
    print "False\n";
}
```

Please note that Perl does not have native support for boolean types, so it uses `1` for `true` and `0` for `false`. And array `@partition` is being stringified using `join` to compare partition slices properly.
<|end|>


