<|end|>
<|asst|>
To compute the standard deviation of a data set in Python, we'd use the scipy library like so:

```python
from scipy import stats

data = [4, 12, 6, 10, 16]
standard_deviation = stats.tstd(data)

print("The standard deviation is ", standard_deviation)
```

Running this code computes the standard deviation of the provided data set, and will print out the result.

The standard deviation is a measure of the amount of variation or dispersion of a set of values. A low standard deviation indicates that the values tend to be close to the mean of the set, while a high standard deviation indicates that the values are spread out over a wider range. 

To be more precise in the context of your data - if the computed standard deviation is small, it means that most of your numbers are close to the average (mean) of your data set. In contrast, if the standard deviation is big, this means that your numbers are more spread out and deviate more from the average. Thus, standard deviation can tell us if the data points in our set are generally consistent, or if they widely vary.
<|end|>


