JavaScript to handle form validation:
```javascript
document.getElementById('credit-card-form').addEventListener('submit', function(event) {
  event.preventDefault(); // Prevent form from submitting to server
  
  // Validate credit card number. Implementation would depend on your requirements.
  if (!validateCreditCardNumber(document.getElementById('cardNumber').value)) {
    alert('Invalid credit card number.');
    return;
  }

  // Validate expiry date. Implementation would depend on your requirements.
  if (!validateExpiryDate(document.getElementById('expiryDate').value)) {
    alert('Invalid expiry date.');
    return;
  }

  // Validate CVV. Implementation would depend on your requirements.
  if (!validateCVV(document.getElementById('cvv').value)) {
    alert('Invalid CVV.');
    return;
  }

  // Validate captcha code. You may have to send a request to the server with entered captcha code
  if (!validateCaptchaCode(document.getElementById('captcha-code').value)) {
    alert('Invalid Captcha.');
    return;
  }
  // If everything is validated, submit the form
  this.submit();
});
```

This is just a frontend part. You'll need a back-end in place to validate the CAPTCHA and process the form. Also, remember that sensitive information such as credit card details should be transmitted over a secured (HTTPS) connection and store in a secured manner. 

Moreover, to ensure the best security, you should generally leave handling credit card details to PCI-compliant payment providers such as PayPal, Stripe, etc.
