I'm looking at building a simple web app that will expose an API that lets third-party (well, written by me, but that's not the point) apps query for and modify user-specific data stored on the site.

Obviously I don't want to allow apps to be able to get user-specific information without that users consent. I would want some kind of application authentication where users allow an application they run to use the web API to access their information.

Is there a standard way to achieve this or does every app (i.e. [rememberthemilk](http://rememberthemilk.com)) just hack up a bespoke solution specifically for them?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
OAuth is the most standardized way to achieve what you're describing. It's specifically designed to allow users to authorize third-party applications to access their data on a website without giving the application their username/password. Twitter, Google, and many others use OAuth. 

The way it usually works from the user's perspective is:

1. A third-party application asks the user to authorize it to access their data on a website. 
2. The user is redirected to the website's login page (assuming they're not already logged in).
3. After successfully logging in, the user receives a prompt asking if they'd like to authorize the third-party application to access their data.
4. If the user clicks "Yes," then the website redirects the user back to the third-party application with an authorization code.
5. The third-party application can then exchange the authorization code for an access token, which allows it to query the user's data on the website. 

OAuth 2.0 protocol provides various "flows" (ways to get a token), depending on the type of the application (web, desktop, mobile, server-to-server) so you can choose the most appropriate one for yours.
<|end|>


