Here is the corrected version of the function. Now it correctly calculates the mean of a list of numbers. Also, it checks for invalid input like an empty list.

```python
def mean(numbers):
    if not numbers or not isinstance(numbers, list) or not all(isinstance(num, (int, float)) for num in numbers):
        raise ValueError("Invalid input! Please provide a non-empty list of numbers.")
    
    return sum(numbers) / len(numbers)

print(mean([10, 20, 30, 40]))
```

This version of the function uses the sum() function to add up all the numbers in the list, and then divides that sum by the length of the list (which is the count of numbers), achieving the proper formula for calculating the mean. It also checks if the input is a properly formatted list of numbers, and raises a ValueError with an appropriate error message if not.
