<|user|>
On long Visual C++ builds, it would be really helpful to hear some sort of (optional) sounds for such build/compile results as:

- - - - 

Does anyone know how to enable sounds for these kinds of build occurrences in Visual C++ (especially Visual C++ 2008 on Vista)?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
While Visual Studio doesn't have an in-built function to play sounds for build results, you can utilize Windows's ability to play sounds in response to system events.

Here's how you can do it:

1. First, you need to make sure that you have a .wav file for the sound that you want to play. 

2. Then, you can create a simple .NET program that can play a .wav file.

An example in C# would be:

    using System.Media;
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            using (var soundPlayer = new SoundPlayer(@"c:\windows\Media\chord.wav"))
            {
                soundPlayer.PlaySync();
            }
        }
    }

This program will play the file `c:\windows\Media\chord.wav`.

3. After you have the .exe for this .NET program, you can add a new entry in the `Tools`-> `External Tools` menu for this program.

4. Then, you can add this .exe to the `Build Events`-> `Post-Build Event`->`Command Line` in the project properties.

Here, you can use the `$(SolutionDir)` macro to refer to the directory of the solution file.

The command should be something like this:

    "$(SolutionDir)PlaySound.exe"

This way, the sound will play every time a build is completed.

To play different sounds depending on whether the build succeeded or failed, you need to handle build errors and then play an appropriate sound. The try-catch block in the .NET program can help you in this.

Remember, this solution is a workaround because Visual Studio by itself doesn't have built-in support for playing sounds on build events. You should adapt it according to your needs.
