<|user|>
We develop Win32 application that access to SQL 2005 database through Linq to SQL. The issue is when 2 users access to same record (View and Edit)… User 1 update record (DataContext.SubmitChanges()), User 2 will continue to see old information until he restart application. So, we would like to update context of user 2… The solution that appears right now is to call DataContext.Refresh to sync object with SQL table… We wondering if other solution exist ?

Thank you
<|end|>
<|asst|>
There are a few ways that you can handle this, depending on how you want to handle the situation when two users are editing the same record:

1. Pessimistic Concurrency: This involves locking the record when User 1 starts editing it, so that User 2 cannot start editing until User 1 is done. This would prevent User 2 from having to restart their application, but it also means that they have to wait for User 1 to finish.

2. Optimistic Concurrency: This involves letting both users edit the record, but having a mechanism in place to handle conflicts when they save their changes. The DataContext.Refresh method you mentioned is part of this approach, but you'll also want to call it with the RefreshMode.KeepChanges parameter to ensure that User 2's changes are not overwritten.

3. Manual Updates: Rather than using Linq to SQL's built-in mechanisms for handling concurrent edits, you could write your own logic to handle this. For example, you could have a field on the record that indicates when it was last updated, and then check this field each time a user tries to save their changes.

Overall, there's no one-size-fits-all solution to this problem, because different applications may have different requirements. However, hopefully these pointers will help you find a solution that's right for your application.
<|end|>


