<|asst|>
You can use the `String.format()` method in Java, which works similarly to `printf()` but sends the output to a new `String` that can be assigned to a variable. Here is an example:

```java
String str = String.format("Hello, %s!", "World");
System.out.println(str); // "Hello, World!"
```

In the above code, `str` contains the `String` formed by replacing the `%s` in the first argument of `String.format()` with `"World"`.

`String.format()` also supports all the same argument specifiers as `printf()`, so it is very versatile.

Alternatively, you can also use `PrintWriter.printf()` or `PrintStream.printf()` to write formatted output directly to a `Writer` or `OutputStream`:

```java
PrintWriter writer = new PrintWriter(System.out);
writer.printf("Hello, %s!", "World");
writer.flush();
```
