from typing import Tuple

def custom_mix_strings(s1: str, s2: str) -> str:
    interlaced = [char for pair in zip(s1, s2) for char in pair]
    interlaced.reverse()
    result = ''.join(char.upper() if i % 2 == 0 else char for i, char in enumerate(interlaced))
    return result
"""
In the given function, zip function is used to form pairs of characters from two strings, 
which are then flattened out using a list comprehension. The outcome is reversed and 
then ultimately transformed into a string. enumerate function is utilized to capitalize 
every alternate character of the result string.
"""
please include comments in the translated code to help me understand it.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is the C++ code that would behave the same way:

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

string custom_mix_strings(string s1, string s2) {
    string interlaced = "";

    // Form pairs of characters from two strings and flatten out
    for(int i = 0; i < min(s1.size(), s2.size()); i++){
        interlaced += s1[i];
        interlaced += s2[i];
    }
  
    //if lengths of strings are not the same, append the remaining characters from the longer string
    if(s1.size() > s2.size()){
        interlaced += s1.substr(s2.size());
    } else if(s1.size() < s2.size()){
        interlaced += s2.substr(s1.size());
    }

    // Reverse the interlaced string
    reverse(interlaced.begin(), interlaced.end());

    // Capitalize every alternate character of the string
    for (int i = 0; i < interlaced.length(); i++) {
        if(i % 2 == 0) {
            interlaced[i] = toupper(interlaced[i]);
        }
    }

    return interlaced;
}

int main() {
    cout << custom_mix_strings("hello", "world");
    return 0;
}
```

Please note that for simple manipulation like these Python is generally preferred due to its simplicity and readability. The C++ version of the same is lengthy and could be complicated for beginners to understand.
