Sure, I can help with this. Here's the Python function translated to Swift:

```swift
import Foundation 

// Here we define a function in Swift named customSortUnique that accepts an array of integers and 
// returns an array of integers as well. Note that we do not define the return type in Python 
// because Python is dynamically typed.
func customSortUnique(numbers: [Int]) -> [Int] {
    
    // We first convert numbers (an array) to a Set, because a Set inherently does not allow duplicate values. 
    // Thus, any duplicate numbers will be removed in this step.
    let uniqueNumbers = Set(numbers)

    // Next, we convert the Set back to an Array. We need to do this because, unlike a Python set, 
    // a Swift Set does not have a sorted() function. 
    let uniqueNumbersArray = Array(uniqueNumbers)
    
    // Finally, we sort the array of unique numbers. This is similar to Python's sorted() function.
    let sortedUniqueNumbers = uniqueNumbersArray.sorted()

    // After sorting, we return the array. 
    return sortedUniqueNumbers
}
```

I hope that helps! I tried to include all the information you would need to understand this function, but if you have any other questions, feel free to ask.
<|end|>


