I'm sick of remembering all the passwords for different logins. Lately I found the interesting tool [password composer](http://www.xs4all.nl/~jlpoutre/BoT/Javascript/PasswordComposer/) which lets you generate passwords base on the  and a secret . But I don't want to use a website or installing software to generate my passwords.

So I'm looking for a simple one way hashing alogorithm which I can execute without computer aid to generate my passwords. Something in the spirit of the [solitare cipher](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solitaire_(cipher)) without the need for cards.

Using a PW store is not an option.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a simple paper-based hashing-like method you could use:

1. Write out a "base phrase" that you use for all of your passwords - it could be anything, but make sure it's something you can remember easily.

2. For each character in the website/service name you're creating a password for, go to the corresponding position in your base phrase. If the character isn't a letter, skip it and go to the next letter. If you get to the end of the base phrase, loop back to the beginning. Write down these letters.

3. Use a simple Caesar cipher to shift each letter by a certain number of positions. You could base the shift on the number of letters in the website/service name, or choose a fixed number for all sites.

4. If you want an extra bit of complexity, you can add some kind of symbol or number to your password. For instance, you could draw a simple symbol on a piece of paper, such as a plus sign, and write a number next to each quadrant (top left, top right, bottom left, bottom right). Then you could decide that your symbol and numbers get added to your password based on the first four letters of the website/service name (or the first four letters of your encrypted password). For instance, if your symbol is "+", and the numbers are "1" on the top left, "2" on the top right, "3" on the bottom left, and "4" on the bottom right, and the first four letters of your encrypted password are "abcd", you would add "+1+4+3+2" to your password.

With this method, you really only need to remember your base phrase and the Caesar cipher shift, your symbol, and the numbers. Even if someone knows that you're using this method, without either the base phrase, the shift, the symbol, and the numbers, they wouldn't be able to figure out your passwords. And since the method is somewhat flexible, you can adjust it to meet different password requirements (for instance, by using different symbols or including digits).
You could modify it as needed to include numbers and special characters for more complex passwords.

Now, you don't have to remember multiple passwords, and you have a solid method of creating a different password for every website.

Of course, this isn't perfect. It's still a mental load to remember the key and execute the cipher correctly. It's also possible someone who knows what you're doing and get access to your encryption key (i.e. your base phrase and shift) could crack your passwords cheaper and faster by far than any brute force attack. But it's a compromise between security and convenience. As with most things in security, you have to find the balance that works best for you. 

Also no hashing algorithm is proof against someone who has access to both the hash and the original plaintext. If they know you're using an operation as simple as Caesar cipher, they can compare the transformed and un-transformed versions to figure out what you did quite easily. Real security with hashed passwords comes from preventing anyone else having access to the hash in the first place.
<|end|>


