Develop a code segment that not only verifies the existence of a specific element within a predetermined numerical array, but also returns its index in the array if it exists. If the element occurs more than once in the array, the program should return all indices where it is found. Additionally, the algorithm should be optimized to minimize search time - avoid duplicate traversals and unnecessary checking.

Array provided: [1,2,5,7,2,1,5,7]
Target number: 5
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a Python solution using list comprehension which is efficient and avoids duplicate traversals:

```python
def find_index(target, array):
    indices = [i for i, x in enumerate(array) if x == target]
    if indices:
        return indices
    else:
        return "Element not found."

array = [1,2,5,7,2,1,5,7]
target = 5
print(find_index(target, array))
```

This code will print: `[2, 6]`. The function `find_index` traverses the list `array` once, checking at each index whether the element is equal to the target number. If it is, it adds that index to a list. If the list is empty at the end of the traversal (meaning the target wasn't found), it returns a string indicating that the target isn't in the list.

This is a time-optimized algorithm because it minimizes the number of traversals over the input (it only requires a single pass through the list) and doesn't do unnecessary comparisons once it has found the target. This is an O(n) time-complexity algorithm, where n is the size of the list.

If the target occurs more than once in the array, this code will find all instances because it goes through every element in the array without stopping when it finds the first instance of the target.
<|end|>


