Invent a new sorting algorithm that is O(n) and works with modern computers.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Inventing an O(n) sorting algorithm for implementation with modern computers is theoretically impossible. All known sorting algorithms that depend on comparing elements have a lower bound of O(n log n) based on the decision-tree model.

However, there are linear time sorting algorithms used for specific scenarios. For instance, 'Counting Sort' sorts integers in linear time but uses an amount of memory exponential to the number of bits in the integers.

Additionally, 'Bucket Sort' or 'Radix Sort' (with time complexity O(nk) for n keys which are integers of word size k) take advantage of specifics of the input distribution or the input datatype to perform sorting in linear time.

That being said, presenting a concept for a completely new linear time sorting algorithm without using the above-mentioned approaches while also working with any possible input would be a major breakthrough in computer science. However, it would go against currently held theoretical beliefs about sorting problems.

Keep in mind, while the problem with creating a linear time sorting algorithm might not be computational resources, it is more related to information theory - getting the complete order of n items simply needs more than n actions using traditional comparison-based sorting methods. 

However, if large-scale quantum computers become prevalent, a prime field where they could potentially make a significant difference is in sorting algorithms, where quantum behavior might allow for checking multiple possibilities simultaneously, in theory leading to substantial speedups beyond what is currently believed possible.
<|end|>


