What libraries exist for other programming languages to provide an Erlang-style concurrency model (processes, mailboxes, pattern-matching receive, etc.)?

Note: I am specifically interested in things that are intended to be similar to Erlang, not just any threading or queueing library.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
1. Akka (Scala, Java): Akka is a toolkit and runtime for building highly concurrent, distributed, and resilient message-driven applications. It provides Erlang style actors that can be distributed across a computer cluster.

2. Pony (Ponylang): Pony is an object-oriented, actor-model, capabilities-secure, high performance programming language. It offers an Erlang-style concurrency model with actors and a garbage collector.

3. Elixir (Elixir): Elixir is a functional, concurrent, general-purpose programming language that runs on the Erlang virtual machine (BEAM). It essentially brings a Ruby like syntax to the Erlang concurrency model.

4. Rust (RustActors, Actix): Rust language has several libraries that provide actor-based concurrency model. RustActors is built on top of Rust's standard library. Actix is a Rust actor system and web framework which makes it easier to write concurrent code.

5. Rayon (Rust): Rayon is a data-parallelism library for Rust. It uses a work-stealing scheduler to efficiently run tasks on a multi-core CPU, thus modernizing the traditional Erlang style of concurrency.

6. Cloud Haskell (Haskell): Cloud Haskell provides a message-passing style of distributed concurrency, very similar to Erlang's.

7. CAF (C++): The C++ Actor Framework (CAF) is an open source C++ actor model implementation featuring lightweight & fast actor implementations, pattern matching for messages, network transparent messaging, and more.

8. Scala (Scala): Scala is a functional and object-oriented language that runs on the JVM. Scala also provides actors through the standard library which is similar to that of Erlang. 

9. Celluloid (Ruby): Celluloid provides a simple and natural way to build fault-tolerant concurrent programs in Ruby, much like an Erlang-style actor system for Ruby.

10. Orleans (C#): Microsoft's Orleans is a framework that provides a straightforward approach to building distributed high-scale computing applications, without the need to learn and apply complex concurrency or other scaling patterns. It was created by Microsoft Research and was used in Halo 4.
<|end|>


