I'm using C++ and accessing a UNC path across the network.  This path is slightly greater than MAX_PATH. So I cannot obtain a file handle.

But if I run the program on the computer in question, the path is not greater than MAX_PATH.  So I can get a file handle.  If I rename the file to have less characters (minus length of computer name) I can access the file. 

Can this file be accessed across the network even know the computer name in the UNC path puts it over the MAX_PATH limit?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, you can access files across the network that exceed the MAX_PATH limit by using the extended-length pathname with the "\\?\" prefix. When you use this prefix, the maximum file path size can be up to 32,767 characters. You need to use the fully qualified path. Here is an example of a local file path along with a UNC path:

Local file path: "\\?\C:\folder\file.txt"

UNC path: "\\?\UNC\server\share\folder\file.txt"

Notice that for UNC paths, the prefix is "\\?\UNC\" instead of "\\?\C:". This will work across the network even when subtracting the computer name length exceeds the MAX_PATH limit.

However, please note that not all the APIs work with long file paths even with this prefix. You must ensure that the APIs you are using support long file paths.

Here is the code showing how you can access a file over a network which exceeds MAX_PATH limit.

```cpp
std::wstring filePath(L"\\\\?\\UNC\\server\\share\\folder\\ReallyReallyLongPath\\file.txt");
HANDLE hFile = ::CreateFileW(filePath.c_str(),
                             GENERIC_READ,
                             0,
                             NULL,
                             OPEN_EXISTING,
                             FILE_ATTRIBUTE_NORMAL,
                             NULL);
if (hFile == INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE)
{
    // Handle the error case. For instance, call GetLastError() for more details.
} 
else
{
    // Success, don't forget to call CloseHandle() when you're done with the handle.
}
```

This code snippet will open a file using a UNC path and handle the file. If the return value is INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE, you can check the error by calling GetLastError().
<|end|>


