I have a container app with 4 different Vue apps. They all are using almost same third party packages. I want to share the packages in runtime (i.e., to cache them somewhere), so that any of my apps can use the packages without multiple time downloading and use them from that cache. Basically how can I share dependencies across multiple Vue apps in runtime?
I tried on it but I don't want to use MonoRepo, as that is more to do with build time reduction. I want to share the dependencies in runtime, as whenever an app runs do not have to download them several times
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Webpack 5 Module Federation is a great feature for this kind of task. It allows a JavaScript application to dynamically run code from another bundle/build, on both client and server. One advantage is to use packages from a remote server in runtime.

Here is an example that might help:

In your Webpack configuration :

```javascript
plugins: [
  new ModuleFederationPlugin({
    name: "app_name",
    filename: "remoteEntry.js",
    exposes: {
    './Button':'./src/components/Button'
    },
    shared: { vue: { singleton: true }}
  })
]
```

Then you can use it in another project like:

```javascript
const Button = React.lazy(() => import("app1/Button"));
```

The above configuration exposes the button from the "app1" and you can use it anywhere as if it is locally present in your app. Also, it keeps the Vue as a singleton shared instance between all of your federated modules.

Unfortunately, Module Federation is not supported in Vue CLI yet, but you can use Vite - it has a plugin for that: https://github.com/originjs/vite-plugin-federation

Remember to secure the communication if you handle sensitive data, and be aware that all applications need to be online as they will be communicating at runtime. That could introduce another point of failure to your system but offers great advantages in terms of bundle size reduction and version upgrade.
<|end|>


