I am faced with the following challenge: Constructing a HashMap without the aid of any pre-existing hash table libraries.

The task requires the creation of the `MyHashMap` class with the following methods:

`MyHashMap()` which initializes an empty map object.

`void put(int key, int value)` which introduces a `(key, value)` pair into the HashMap. If the `key` is already present in the map, the corresponding `value` should be updated.

`int get(int key)` which retrieves the `value` associated with the specified `key`, or returns `-1` if the map does not contain a mapping for the `key`.

`void remove(key)` which eliminates the `key` and its associated `value` if the map contains the mapping for the `key`.

Here is an example for better understanding:

Input
[&#34;MyHashMap&#34;, &#34;put&#34;, &#34;put&#34;, &#34;get&#34;, &#34;get&#34;, &#34;put&#34;, &#34;get&#34;, &#34;remove&#34;, &#34;get&#34;]
[[], [1, 1], [2, 2], [1], [3], [2, 1], [2], [2], [2]]
Output
[null, null, null, 1, -1, null, 1, null, -1]

This is explained as follows:
MyHashMap myHashMap = new MyHashMap(); // The map is now empty
myHashMap.put(1, 1); // The map now contains [[1,1]]
myHashMap.put(2, 2); // The map now contains [[1,1], [2,2]]
myHashMap.get(1);    // returns 1, The map remains [[1,1], [2,2]]
myHashMap.get(3);    // returns -1 (i.e., not found), The map remains [[1,1], [2,2]]
myHashMap.put(2, 1); // The map now contains [[1,1], [2,1]] (i.e., the existing value is updated)
myHashMap.get(2);    // returns 1, The map remains [[1,1], [2,1]]
myHashMap.remove(2); // the mapping for 2 is removed, The map now contains [[1,1]]
myHashMap.get(2);    // returns -1 (i.e., not found), The map remains [[1,1]]

The constraints are as follows:
`0 &lt;= key, value &lt;= 106`
A maximum of `104` calls will be made to `put`, `get`, and `remove`.

Additional requirement: Refrain from using the built-in HashMap library.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is a simple implementation of HashMap in Java using array and linked list.

```java
public class MyHashMap {

    class Node {
        int key;
        int value;
        Node next;
        Node(int key, int value) {
            this.key = key;
            this.value = value;
        }
    }

    Node[] nodes;

    /** Initialize your data structure here. */
    public MyHashMap() {
        nodes = new Node[10000];
    }

    /** value will always be non-negative. */
    public void put(int key, int value) {
        int idx = getIndex(key);
        Node prev = findElement(idx, key);
        if (prev.next == null)
            prev.next = new Node(key, value);
        else 
            prev.next.value = value;
    }

    /** Returns the value to which the specified key is mapped, or -1 if this map contains no mapping for the key */
    public int get(int key) {
        int idx = getIndex(key);
        Node node = findElement(idx, key);
        return node.next == null ? -1 : node.next.value;
    }

    /** Removes the mapping of the specified value key if this map contains a mapping for the key */
    public void remove(int key) {
        int idx = getIndex(key);
        Node prev = findElement(idx, key);
        if (prev.next != null) 
            prev.next = prev.next.next;
    }

    private int getIndex(int key) {
        // I used a simple hash function here, in reality one would use a much more complex function
        return Integer.hashCode(key) % nodes.length;
    }

    private Node findElement(int idx, int key) {
        // If first time, we initialize an empty placeholder
        if (nodes[idx] == null)
            return nodes[idx] = new Node(-1, -1);

        Node prev = nodes[idx];
        while (prev.next != null && prev.next.key != key) {
            prev = prev.next;
        }
        return prev;
    }
}
```
This is a basic HashMap that can perform the required operations: insert, remove, and get in expected constant time complexity, but in worst case these operations would perform in linear time complexity. You can replace LinkedList with balanced binary search trees to improve the worst case time complexity to log N.

Please note that the hash function used here is very simple. In a production quality code, you would use a more sophisticated hash function to ensure a better distribution of keys and avoid too many collisions.
<|end|>


