<|user|>
I know that tables are for tabular data, but it's so tempting to use them for layout. I can handle DIV's to get a three column layout, but when you got 4 nested DIV's, it get tricky. 

Is there a tutorial/reference out there to persuade me to use DIV's for layout?

I want to use DIV's, but I refuse to spend an hour to position my DIV/SPAN where I want it.

@GaryF: [Blueprint CSS](http://www.blueprintcss.org/) has to be the CSS's best kept secret.

Great tool - [Blueprint Grid CSS Generator](http://kematzy.com/blueprint-generator/).
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here are a few reasons why using DIVs over tables for layout may be beneficial:

1. **Separation of content and design**: DIV tags are for shaping the layout and tables are for representing data. Keeping them separate allows for easier reading and managing of the code.

2. **Flexibility and reusability**: DIVs are more flexible and can be reused throughout the page or site. This can help reduce the amount of code and keep your site more consistent.

3. **Load time**: Since DIVs use less HTML than tables, pages can load faster.

4. **Responsive design**: DIV allows for responsive designs. With mobile internet use on the rise, having a website that adjusts to different screen sizes is increasingly important.

5. **Accessibility and SEO**: Screen readers have a difficult time understanding tables that are used for layout. Divs are easier to understand and this can also help with Search Engine Optimization (SEO).

If you're having trouble with nested DIVs, CSS frameworks like Bootstrap or Foundation can help make managing layouts easier. There are also plenty of tutorials online to help you understand how to use DIVs effectively for layout.
 
An hour might seem like a lot for positioning a DIV, but when you consider the long-term benefits (reusability, accessibility, better search engine indexing, etc), it might be a worthwhile investment.

Here are a few resources that you can refer to learn more about DIVs:

1. [Learn CSS Layout](http://learnlayout.com/)
2. [CSS-Tricks Guide](https://css-tricks.com/snippets/css/a-guide-to-flexbox/)
3. [Mozilla MDN](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Element/div)

The Blueprint CSS you mentioned is indeed a great tool for creating custom grid systems. Remember, the right approach depends a lot on the specific project, your personal comfort level with different methods, and long-term maintainability.
<|end|>


