<|asst|>
The essence of elliptic curve cryptography (ECC) was birthed in the mind of mathematician Neal Koblitz. It was the late '80s when he proposed the use of elliptic curves in cryptography. Born were cryptographic systems that were paradoxically complex in design, yet elegantly simple in utility. These became the building blocks of some of the world's most secure, digital infrastructures.

As computing power loomed ever larger in the late 20th century, the conventional encryption methods founded on factoring large numbers were put at risk. Increasing computational power risked turning what were once considered secure cryptographic systems into childs play. However, the intricate geometry of elliptic curves offered a solution. ECC uses the algebraic structure of elliptic curves over finite fields, providing the same level of security with shorter keys, making it more efficient and faster than other types of encryption.

The heart of blockchain technology is the unfailing security it provides against duplicate transactions and fraud. Once any transaction is conducted, it's almost impossible to erase or alter it. This record-keeping technology establishes trust by decentralizing data across a network of computers, but it demands robust encryption algorithms to ensure security. ECC is one of the cryptographic primitive functions that this technology hinges upon.

The mathematical premise of ECC lies in the difficulty of reverse-engineering the discrete logarithm problem—an operation that’s significantly more arduous when applied to the points on an elliptic curve compared to its traditional form. This feature makes ECC a powerful shield against potential cryptographic transgressions in blockchain technology.

As the blockchain technology epoch unfolds, potential roadblocks have arisen. Quantum computing, for instance, signifies a looming threat as it could decrypt the keys that protect blockchains. Here, ECC provides a bulwark against this impending hazard, as long as keys are frequently updated and cryptography is sensitively managed.

Even now, cryptographers are developing post-quantum ECC (PQ-ECC) to boost the cryptographic robustness in the face of quantum computing threats. In conjunction, continuous integration and deployment of ECC into blockchain networks, along with the vigilant detection and rectification of vulnerabilities, should be prioritized for unassailable security.

The use of ECC in blockchain-resistant algorithms further enhances the security film. It provides a robust platform for developing cryptographic infrastructures that can withstand blockchain computing threats. The complexity it introduces makes transgressions incredibly difficult as elliptic curves operate in different forms and on a variety of platforms.

Transmuting the complexities of ECC to ensure data authenticity, security, and cryptographic efficiency is essential for the smooth advancement of blockchain technology. The roadmap to achieving this will require meticulous application of intricate techniques, while keeping a watchful eye over potential threats to maintain an iron-clad infrastructure for the future of blockchain technology.
<|end|>


