```Python
import unittest

class TestSortRemoveDuplicates(unittest.TestCase): 

    def test_sort_remove_duplicates(self): 
        
        self.assertEqual(sort_remove_duplicates([None, 1, 2, None, 2]), [0, 1, 2])      
        self.assertEqual(sort_remove_duplicates([4, 2, 9, 2, 5]), [2, 4, 5, 9])
        self.assertEqual(sort_remove_duplicates([4, None, 9, 2, 5]), [0, 2, 4, 5, 9])
        self.assertEqual(sort_remove_duplicates([None, None, None, None]), [0])
        self.assertEqual(sort_remove_duplicates([]), [])

if __name__ == '__main__': 
    unittest.main()
```

This Python function converts the input values to a set to remove duplicates (since sets cannot contain duplicates), converts the set back into a list, and finally sorts the list. It also uses a list comprehension to replace any None values with zeros.

For the complexity analysis, except the sorting algorithm which complexity is O(n log n), everything else is either O(n) or less. Python's sort function is a Timsort, so it is optimized and very efficient. However, this function only works with numerical values, as specified, but cannot handle other types of values.
