Compose a program in R to detect outliers from a given dataset.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a simple R code that can be used to detect outliers in a given dataset. The code basically measures the Z-score, which is the number of standard deviations data point is from the mean. Data points with z score greater than 3 are usually considered outliers.

```R
# Function to detect outliers
detect_outliers <- function(data) {
  # Calculate the mean of the data
  mean <- mean(data)
  
  # Calculate the standard deviation of the data
  standard_deviation <- sd(data)
  
  # Calculate the z-scores of the data
  z_scores <- abs((data - mean) / standard_deviation)
  
  # Determine the outliers
  outliers <- data[z_scores > 3]
  
  # Return the outliers
  return(outliers)
}
# Example usage:
# Suppose 'dataset' is the name of your dataset and 'column' is the name of the column for which you want to detect outliers
outliers <- detect_outliers(dataset$column)
print(outliers)
```
This function will return any values in the dataset that are more than 3 standard deviations from the mean as potential outliers. The cutoff of 3 is arbitrary and you may need to adjust it depending on your specific situation. Similarly, this simple method may not suit all datasets, especially those with non-normal distributions.
<|end|>


