I’m trying to build a regression model that estimates the amount of sales of a beer product on a given day based on the prices of the product and competitors, the weather, the season and the day of week of that specific day
My question is how to split the data into train and test
Because I pretend to use the model to make a prediction for a future day, I think I could split the data so that the test set is composed of those observations with recent dates and the train test with past dates
This make sense or I should split at random?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Splitting time series data randomly can cause a look-ahead bias. It's common practice to split time series sequentially with the training set consisting of the earliest dates and the testing set the later dates. For instance, you may take first 80% of your time series as training data and the rest 20% as testing data. It is because the sequence of data matters in the time domain. You will be using this model to forecast future sales which will only depend upon the past data. So, considering this, you should split sequentially and not randomly.
<|end|>


