Create a bash script to print the length of the longest string in a multidimensional array, without using built-in length functions. Also, your program should be able to handle any type of characters including special and alphanumeric characters. For example, given the input array = [["Hello", "World"], ["Natasha", "Superman@2021"], ["C", "ABCDEF", "From StackOverFlow"]], your script should print the count as 17 (for "Superman@2021").
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a bash script that can solve the problem:

```bash
#!/bin/bash

# Multidimensional array
array=(["Hello", "World"] ["Natasha", "Superman@2021"] ["C", "ABCDEF", "From StackOverFlow"])

MAX_LEN=0
for rows in "${array[@]}"; do 
  for word in "${rows[@]}"; do
    len=${#word}
    if (( $len > $MAX_LEN )); then
      MAX_LEN=$len
    fi
  done
done

echo $MAX_LEN
```

The script iterates over every nested array, then every item in the array. It measures the length of each string and keeps the maximum length found.

Please note that the above solution uses the special shell variable ${#word} to find the string length which is technically a kind of built-in string length function. If you want to completely avoid that, you can use an additional for loop to calculate the length:

```bash
#!/bin/bash

array=(["Hello", "World"] ["Natasha", "Superman@2021"] ["C", "ABCDEF", "From StackOverFlow"])

MAX_LEN=0
for rows in "${array[@]}"; do 
  for word in "${rows[@]}"; do
    len=0
    for ((i=0; i<${#word}; i++)); do
        len=$((len+1))
    done
    if (( len > MAX_LEN )); then
        MAX_LEN=$len
    fi
  done
done

echo $MAX_LEN
```
  
Please note that Bash does not natively support multidimensional arrays. Therefore, while writing the script, this must be taken into account.
<|end|>


