Revise this code for Javascript:
import java.util.*;
import java.lang.*;

class Solution {
    public List<String> filterBySubstring(List<String> strings, String regexPattern) {
        List<String> filteredStrings = new ArrayList<String>();
        for(String str : strings) {
            if(str.matches(regexPattern)) {
                filteredStrings.add(str);
            }
        }
        return filteredStrings;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Solution solution = new Solution();
        
        System.out.println(solution.filterBySubstring(List.of(), "a")); // []
        System.out.println(solution.filterBySubstring(Arrays.asList("abc", "bacd", "cde", "array"), "a.*")); // ["abc", "bacd", "array"]
    }
}
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is the JavaScript version of your code:

```javascript
class Solution {
    filterBySubstring(strings, regexPattern) {
        let filteredStrings = strings.filter(str => new RegExp(regexPattern).test(str));
        return filteredStrings;
    }
}

let solution = new Solution();

console.log(solution.filterBySubstring([], 'a')); // []
console.log(solution.filterBySubstring(["abc", "bacd", "cde", "array"], "a.*")); // ["abc", "array"]
```

Note that JavaScript's `filter()` method creates a new array with all elements that pass the test implemented by the provided function. In this case, the test is whether the string matches the regular expression.

Also, please note that in the example "Arrays.asList("abc", "bacd", "cde", "array"), "a.*"), the resulting array in JavaScript version includes only ["abc", "array"] because JavaScript's RegExp.test() method is not equivalent to Java's String.matches(). The test() method checks if there's at least one occurrence of the pattern in a string, just like in Java's matches(). But in Java's String.matches(), the entire string must be matched to return true, for partial matches it returns false.
