I need to detect when a session has expired in my Visuial Basic web application.  This is what I'm using...


Protected Sub Page_Load(ByVal sender As Object, ByVal e As System.EventArgs) Handles Me.Load

    If CurrentSession.IsNew AndAlso (Not Page.Request.Headers("Cookie") Is Nothing) AndAlso (Page.Request.Headers("Cookie").IndexOf("ASP.NET_SessionId") >= 0) Then
        Response.Redirect("TimeOut.aspx")
    End If

    ...do something...

End Sub



 `CurrentSession.IsNew` returns `HttpContext.Current.Session.IsNewSession`

This seems to work well for , but seems to fail with .
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Your approach looks correct for detecting an expired session or a new session being created, but the issue might be with some browsers. You're checking whether the session is new and still showing the "ASP.NET_SessionId" cookie (indicating a previous session), but different browsers may handle cookies and sessions in different ways.

Here are few alternatives for checking session expiration:

1. `Session` object check: You can check whether a specific Session object is `Nothing`. You should set this object when the session starts and when the session ends it will be `Nothing`.

Example:

```vb
If Session("MySessionObject") Is Nothing Then
    Response.Redirect("TimeOut.aspx")
End If
```

2. Detect session expiration via AJAX: When a Session times out, a Session_End event is fired in the Global.asax file.  You can fire an AJAX request at regular intervals, and if session has timed out, it will trigger `Session_Start` event in which you can set a flag indicating that session is started because of timeout, which you can use as a check.

3. Use JavaScript to detect idle time and manually end the session.

Remember not to rely fully on the client side for session timeout detection as it can be easily manipulated. Always have a server-side check.
<|end|>


