In [SOFR Discount Curve Construction in Nov 2021](https://quant.stackexchange.com/questions/68814/sofr-discount-curve-construction-in-nov-2021?), @dm63 does an excellent job describing the way to build the SOFR discount curve.  A few questions:

- What is the market convention for building the front-end of the curve?


- Do folks use the SOFR fixings or SOFR futures more commonly?


- What are the pros and cons of each method in question 2?


- When using SOFR futures to build the curve, is it common to use SOFR futures expiries past 2 years (i.e. similar to when folks would use a strip of 4 or 5 years of Eurodollar futures to build the LIBOR discount curve, then use swaps beyond that point)?



Thanks!
<|end|>
<|asst|>
1. The market convention for building the front end of the SOFR curve involves using the overnight SOFR fixings for the shortest maturities, followed by SOFR futures for the intermediate maturities, and finally SOFR swaps for the longer maturities.

2. Both methods are used, and their application depends on the purpose. If you want to have a higher degree of precision for the shortest maturities, you would use the SOFR fixings. If you want to capture market forward-looking expectations, you would use the SOFR futures.

3. The advantage of using SOFR fixings is that they represent actual market transactions, and thus provide the most precise and reliable measure of the overnight SOFR rate. However, they cover only overnight maturities, and hence their usage is limited when it comes to the construction of an entire yield curve.  The advantage of using SOFR futures is that they provide a market forecast of the future overnight SOFR rates for a range of maturities. However, they are less reliable than the actual fixings because they represent market expectations, which can be subject to various factors such as market sentiment, macroeconomic forecasts, and trading activities.

4. It is not uncommon to use SOFR futures for maturities beyond two years. Similar to how Eurodollar futures are used to build a LIBOR curve, a strip of SOFR futures can be used to capture the market expectation of the future SOFR rates for a range of maturities, and provide necessary information for building a SOFR discount curve. Using SOFR futures beyond two years can provide additional information for the curve in this range. However, the liquidity and trading volume of SOFR futures decrease significantly for maturities beyond two years, which may increase the cost and risk of using these contracts for curve construction.
    
   By the way, you need to be aware that the SOFR discount curve must be built under risk-neutral valuation, i.e., the forward rates are not expected rates but rates that make no-arbitrage between futures prices and forward prices from the constructed curve.
<|end|>


