```

This is a simple architecture where a `User` class is responsible for processing the biometric data, while a `BiometricDatabase` class is responsible for connecting to database, storing user data and verifying data.

The user biometric data is hashed before storing, to add further security. When verifying, the comparison is done with the hashed data.

Please note, the above case scenario does not encrypt the biometric data or the hash of it. We just store the hash of the biometric data to compare it later. Therefore, it is not the most secure solution and should be used for educational purposes only.

For a production environment, you should use an industry-accepted encryption technique on top of the hashing methods and secure the keys used for the encryption, because hashed biometric data can still fall victim to dictionary/rainbow table attacks if not encrypted. 

It's also important to consider following GDPR or other geographically relevant data protection regulations. It is always recommended to seek professional advice when collecting and storing sensitive information. 

And also, using hashing functions on biometrics data has its own disadvantages where – once some biometric data was stolen and it was known that it is hashed with some function, the attacker can try to generate all possible biometric information, hash it and then compare it with the leaked hashes, breaking the security. Biometrics should ideally not be stored but instead, be used to generate some secrets, like private keys of asymmetric key pairs. This way, the biometric data can be discarded after the key generation is done.
