First, you need to consider what type of form element you want to incorporate. For this example, let's add an email input field. The HTML markup for that would look like this:

```html
<html>
<head>
    <title>My Form</title>
</head>
<body>
    <form id="myForm">
        <label for="name">Name</label>
        <input type="text" id="name" required>
        
        <label for="email">Email</label>
        <input type="email" id="email" required>
        
        <input type="submit" value="Submit">
    </form>
<script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```
We added a new email input field with the id "email". Also, we made both the name and email fields required.

Then, we add an external JavaScript file named "script.js". Now we have to write JavaScript code for client-side data validation.

Here is an example how we can do that:

```javascript
document.getElementById('myForm').addEventListener('submit', function(event) {
    var name = document.getElementById('name');
    var email = document.getElementById('email'); 

    if(name.value === '') {
        alert('Please enter your name');
        event.preventDefault();
    } else if(email.value === '') {
        alert('Please enter your email');
        event.preventDefault();
    } else if(!validateEmail(email.value)) {
        alert('Please enter a valid email');
        event.preventDefault();
    }
})

function validateEmail(email) {
    var re = /^(([^<>()[\]\\.,;:\s@"]+(\.[^<>()[\]\\.,;:\s@"]+)*)|(".+"))@((\[[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}])|(([a-zA-Z\-0-9]+\.)+[a-zA-Z]{2,}))$/;
    return re.test(String(email).toLowerCase());
}
```

In the code above, we are checking whether the name and email fields are empty and if the email entered is valid. If any of these checks fail, an alert is shown to the user and the form is not submitted. The validateEmail function is used to check if the email is valid. It returns true if the email is valid, else it returns false.
