<|asst|>
The reason this code doesn't update the 3D viewport immediately because Blender's UI only updates once scripts have finished running. Instead of using `time.sleep()`, the proper way to do this would be through the use of an animation or a loop within the Blender's main event loop (bpy.app.handlers).

Here's an example of creating an animation:

```python
import bpy

# get the reference to the object
obj = bpy.context.active_object

# set start frame
bpy.context.scene.frame_start = 0
bpy.context.scene.frame_end = 10

# insert scale keyframes
for frame in range(bpy.context.scene.frame_start, bpy.context.scene.frame_end+1):
    bpy.context.scene.frame_set(frame)
    obj.scale[0] += 0.1
    obj.keyframe_insert(data_path="scale", index=0)
```
This code will create an animation of the object scaling up continuously over 10 frames around the X axis.

If you want to use bpy.app.handlers, here's how you do it:

```python
import bpy

# get the reference to the object
obj = bpy.context.active_object
obj.scale[0]+=0.1

def my_handler(dummy):
    obj.scale[0] += 0.1
    print(obj.scale[0])
    if obj.scale[0] >= 2:  # if scale value is enough, remove the handler
        bpy.app.handlers.frame_change_post.remove(my_handler)

bpy.app.handlers.frame_change_post.append(my_handler)
```
This script will increase the X scale of object by 0.1 for each frame change until it reaches 2.0.

Remember that bpy.app.handlers cause the function to be called every frame change, so use them carefully.
<|end|>


