I seem to be seeing more 'for' loops over iterators in questions & answers here than I do for_each(), transform(), and the like.  Scott Meyers suggests that [stl algorithms are preferred](http://www.ddj.com/cpp/184401446), or at least he did in 2001.  Of course, using them often means moving the loop body into a function or function object.  Some may feel this is an unacceptable complication, while others may feel it better breaks down the problem.

So... should STL algorithms be preferred over hand-rolled loops?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
As with many aspects of programming, it depends on the context. That said, using STL algorithms often results in cleaner, more reusable code, so they should generally be preferred when possible. 

Remember, STL algorithms abstract away the details of how we traverse a collection. This is beneficial as it helps to prevent off-by-one errors and other similar bugs. 

It's important to also note that STL algorithms are often more descriptive. They tell a reader what is being accomplished rather than how it is being done. This makes code easier to read and understand. 

However, understandability decreases if you have to write a complex function or functor to pass to the algorithm. In such cases, a hand-rolled loop might be easier to understand. 

Performance-wise, modern compilers can heavily optimize STL algorithms, so in many contexts they could perform just as well as, if not better than, hand-rolled loops.

In summary, try to prefer STL algorithms wherever it makes sense, but don't be dogmatic about it. There will be cases where hand-rolled loops are a more suitable choice.
<|end|>


