<|asst|>
Yes, it is possible to know which groups are different from each other using a post-hoc test after conducting an ANOVA test. ANOVA tells us if there is a difference among the groups, but to know which specific groups are different from each other, a post-hoc test is necessary.

Common post-hoc tests include Tukey's HSD (Honest Significant Difference), Bonferroni, and LSD (Least Significant Difference), etc. These tests perform multiple pair-wise comparisons and will provide you with the differences between all pairs of groups.

To implement this in Python, you can use the pairwise_tukeyhsd function from the statsmodels library is usually used. For example:

```python 
from statsmodels.stats.multicomp import pairwise_tukeyhsd

# Assuming 'values' is your column of measurements and 'countries' is your group column
tukey = pairwise_tukeyhsd(endog=df['values'],     # Data
                          groups=df['countries'],   # Groups
                          alpha=0.05)          # Significance level

tukey.plot_simultaneous()    # Plot group confidence intervals
plt.vlines(x=49.57,ymin=-0.5,ymax=4.5, color="red")

tukey.summary()              # See test summary
```

The results will tell you all the pairwise comparisons, the difference between group means, and whether or not each difference is statistically significant.
<|end|>


