Develop a React component that implements an engaging animated loading screen, ensuring smooth transitions and optimum performance.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Creating an engaging animated loading screen with React involves using CSS for styling and animations. For better performance, use React hooks to manage the component state and lifecycle. Here is a basic sample code:

```jsx
// Our LoadingComponent.js file
import React from 'react';
import './LoadingComponent.css';

const LoadingComponent = () => (
    <div className="spinner-container">
      <div className="spinner-circle"></div>
    </div>
);

export default LoadingComponent;
```

We can create a CSS file to style our loading component:

```css
/* Our LoadingComponent.css file */
.spinner-container {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
  height: 100vh;
}

.spinner-circle {
  width: 50px;
  height: 50px;
  border: 3px solid #ccc;
  border-top-color: #1abc9c;
  border-radius: 50%;
  animation: spin 1s linear infinite;
}

@keyframes spin {
  0% {
    transform: rotate(0deg);
  }
  100% {
    transform: rotate(360deg);
  }
}
```

In your main App component, you can use state to determine when to show the loading screen:

```jsx
// App.js
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import LoadingComponent from './LoadingComponent';

const App = () => {
  const [isLoading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  
  // Simulate a loading delay
  useEffect(() => {
    setTimeout(() => {
      setLoading(false);
    }, 5000);
  }, []);

  return (
    <div className="App">
      {isLoading ? <LoadingComponent /> : <h1>Welcome to our site!</h1>}
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

In this example, the loading screen displays for 5 seconds before "Welcome to our site!" appears. In a real-world application, you might replace this set timer with a data-fetching operation. When the data loads completely, setLoading(false) can be used to remove the loading screen.
