With the new app dir, i dont know how render a private route.
If i have a login page and a home page i want to show the login page if the user isnt logged.
I found methods with 12 version of next, but with the 13 version i found nothing.
I've tried this: [https://figueroanicolas-a.medium.com/crear-rutas-privadas-en-nextjs-2021-f%C3%A1cil-1425f4e9aef3](https://figueroanicolas-a.medium.com/crear-rutas-privadas-en-nextjs-2021-f%C3%A1cil-1425f4e9aef3).
But it doesnt works.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In Next.js 13, you can use either middleware or React Components or a combination of the two to achieve the same effect of private routes.

Using the `_middleware.js` file which runs on every page, you can check if a user is authenticated and then redirect them to the Login page if not.

Let's take a look at one approach using React Components.

Here's a simple example of a React component named `PrivateRoute`:

```jsx
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { useRouter } from 'next/router';
import { isAuthenticated } from '../utils/auth'; // This is your method to check if user is authenticated

const PrivateRoute = ({ children }) => {
  const router = useRouter();
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

  useEffect(() => {
    const checkAuth = async () => {
      if (!(await isAuthenticated())) {
        router.push('/login');
      } else {
        setLoading(false);
      }
    };

    checkAuth();
  }, [router]);

  if (loading) {
    return <div>Loading...</div>; // Or your own loading state
  }

  return children;
};
export default PrivateRoute;
```
This `PrivateRoute` component will check if the user is authenticated when first rendered. If the user is authenticated, it will render its children, else it will redirect to /login.

Then your pages will look something like this:

```jsx
import PrivateRoute from '../components/PrivateRoute';

const HomePage = () => {
  return (
    <PrivateRoute>
      <div>Welcome to the Home Page.</div>
    </PrivateRoute>
  );
};

export default HomePage;
```
Do note that "isAuthenticated" function is not implemented here. Its implementation will depend on your own auth system (like checking auth cookies or tokens in localStorage).

Using middleware would be quite similar, you'd check if a user is authenticated and if not, you'd redirect them to the login page. However, middleware are better suited for session-based authentication like using `express-session` or `cookie-session`, because they allow you to access the Next.js `req` object which holds the session data.
<|end|>


