Construct a computer program, written in C programming language, which is designed to solicit from the user an integrative value while also undertaking the task of arithmetic aggregation of the unique digit characters encapsulated within this integer, such as when encountering an integer such as n = 473.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a simple C program to solve the problem:

```C
#include<stdio.h>

int main(){
    int num, digit, sum = 0;
    printf("Enter a number: ");
    scanf("%d",&num);

    while(num > 0) {
        digit = num % 10;
        sum += digit;
        num /= 10;
    }

    printf("Sum of digits: %d\n", sum);

    return 0;
}
```
Explanation:

This C program accepts an integrative value from the user and then calculates the sum of all unique digit characters in the integer. The work is handled in the while loop. In each iteration, it extracts the last digit of the number (using the modulus operator), adds it to the sum, and then removes the last digit from the number (using integer division by 10). This process repeats until all digits are extracted, and finally prints the sum. 

For example, if the input number is 473, the program fetches the last digit (3), adds it to the sum, then the number becomes 47. Again the program does its task with the last digit (7) and the number becomes 4. At the end, the final digit (4) is added to the sum. So the output will be the sum of these digits (3+7+4), which is 14.
<|end|>


