Here's an optimized version of the function `is_perfect_square(x)`. The core functionality remains the same: 

```python
import math
def is_perfect_square(x):
    """Returns true for perfect square integers, false for non-square integers.
    >>> is_perfect_square(9)
    True
    >>> is_perfect_square(144)
    True
    >>> is_perfect_square(25)
    True
    >>> is_perfect_square(169)
    True
    >>> is_perfect_square(2)
    False
    >>> is_perfect_square(0)
    True
    >>> is_perfect_square(1)
    True
    """
    if x < 0:
        return False
    
    sqrt = math.isqrt(x)
    return sqrt*sqrt == x
```

This new version of function uses the built-in Python function `math.isqrt(x)` to get the integer square root of `x`. Then it checks if the square of this value `sqrt` equals `x`, which would mean that `x` is a perfect square. 

Also, please note that perfect squares can be 0 as well. So you might want to return `True` for `is_perfect_square(0)`.
