Develop an array utilizing Java containing N double-precision floating point numbers. Compute the mean, variance, and standard deviation of these numbers without using any built-in functions or libraries. Ensure your program can handle large values of N.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a sample program for solving your problem in Java. This program calculates the mean, variance and standard deviation of N double numbers stored in an array.

```Java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int n = 5;  // Size of the array
        double[] array = new double[n]; // Creating an array

        // Inserting random values in array for demonstration
        array[0] = 10.5;
        array[1] = 20.6;
        array[2] = 30.1;
        array[3] = 40.7;
        array[4] = 50.2;

        double sum = 0;  // To store the sum of elements in the array
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            sum = sum + array[i];

        double mean = sum/n;   
        System.out.println("Mean: " + mean);

        double varianceSum = 0;  
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            varianceSum = varianceSum + Math.pow((array[i] - mean), 2);

        double variance = varianceSum/n;
        System.out.println("Variance: " + variance);

        double std_deviation = Math.sqrt(variance);
        System.out.println("Standard Deviation: " + std_deviation);
    }
}
```

This program works by first taking the sum of all elements in the array. Then the mean is computed by dividing this sum by the number of the elements in the array. Then it calculates the sum of square difference between each element from the mean which is used to calculate variance by dividing it by the number of elements. Lastly, standard deviation is calculated using the square root of variance.
Remember to replace the array size and values with actual dynamic input in real world scenarios.
