Many frameworks seek to abstract away from HTML (custom tags, JSFs component system) in an effort to make dealing with that particular kettle of fish easier.

Is there anything you folks have used that has a similar concept applied to CSS? Something that does a bunch of cross-browser magic for you, supports like variables (why do I have to type #3c5c8d every time I want that colour), supports calculated fields (which are 'compiled' into CSS and JS), etc.

Alternatively, am I even thinking about this correctly? Am I trying to push a very square block through a very round hole?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
There are indeed tools that help abstract away CSS (Cascade Style Sheets) to provide functionality such as variables, calculations, nesting, and more. These tools are called CSS preprocessors, and the most popular ones are Less and Sass (Syntactically Awesome Style Sheets).

Both Less and Sass allow you to write CSS in a more programmer-friendly way, with variables, functions, mixins (similar to functions), and more. You write in the Less or Sass syntax, and then "compile" your Less or Sass code into regular CSS that can be understood by web browsers.

For instance, in both Less and Sass, you can define a color as a variable at the top of your file:

```
@myColor: #3c5c8d;  // Less
$myColor: #3c5c8d;  // Sass
```

Then you can use that variable throughout your code:

```
body {
  background-color: @myColor;  // Less
  background-color: $myColor;  // Sass
}
```

When you compile this code, it generates:

```
body {
  background-color: #3c5c8d;
}
```

You can set up Less or Sass to watch your files and automatically recompile them every time you save, so you don't have to manually run the compile command each time you make a change. There are also various build tools, such as Gulp and Grunt, that you can use to automate this process.

So no, you're not trying to push a square block through a round hole. CSS preprocessors are widely used in the web development industry, and they can certainly make your life easier and your code cleaner and more maintainable.
<|end|>


