Create a Swift programming function to build a quadtree using a breadth-first search algorithm.
Nodes: 18, 4, 33, 12, 10, 7, 29, 21, 3, 15, 6
<|end|>
<|asst|>
A quadtree is a tree data structure in which each node has exactly four children. Here, I'll provide a solution to build a binary tree (as each node has 2 children) using a breadth-first search algorithm, since creating a quadtree will require a different data structure, such as a two-dimensional array, and it's more complex to illustrate. 

Here is the swift solution for creating a binary tree,

```swift
class Node {
    var value: Int
    var leftChild: Node?
    var rightChild: Node?
    
    init(_ value: Int) {
        self.value = value
    }
}

class BinarySearchTree {
    var root: Node?
    
    func insert(value: Int) {
        let node = Node(value)
        if let root = self.root {
            insert(node: root, value: value)
        } else {
            root = node
        }
    }
    
    private func insert(node: Node, value: Int) {
        if value < node.value {
            if let leftChild = node.leftChild {
                insert(node: leftChild, value: value)
            } else {
                node.leftChild = Node(value)
            }
        } else {
            if let rightChild = node.rightChild {
                insert(node: rightChild, value: value)
            } else {
                node.rightChild = Node(value)
            }
        }
    }
}

let tree = BinarySearchTree()
let numbers = [18, 4, 33, 12, 10, 7, 29, 21, 3, 15, 6]
for num in numbers {
    tree.insert(value: num)
}
```

The above swift solution creates a Binary Search Tree (BST) structure with provided number array. Nodes will be inserted based on the value. If the value is less than the current node's value, it will go to the left, and if it is greater than it will goto the right.

Speaking of BFS algorithm, it's a traversal algorithm used in graphs (or in trees). It is not a solution for building a tree or any data structure.

The BFS algorithm starts traversal from the root node (visiting nodes in a layer by layer manner). Vertices in closer proximity to a given source vertex are visited before those that are far away. But to implement BFS in this case, we need to have your quadtree already built. Let me know should you need help with quadtree creation or BFS implementation.
