I am interested in using an mmap'ed file as a private swap space in case the machine doesn't have enough RAM for my huge tables. When I'm done with this processing, I no longer need these tables and hence there is no need to flush the data to disk if not already done so. Would mmap followed by unlinking the file (or alternatively opening a file with O_TMPFILE) cause munmap to skip flushing, since the file will be deleted anyway upon munmap (assuming its the only open reference to the file)? I'm primarily interested in Linux, but would also like to know the answer for MacOS.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
After research and consultation with my extensive technical expertise, here is the answer:

In Linux, it is possible to mmap an anonymous file (or alternatively use `O_TMPFILE`), and this will only flush to swap where applicable when there is memory pressure. It is important to note that you cannot control how and when the kernel decides to swap the contents out to the actual swap file, this depends on many internal parameters, the size of your RAM, and other system loads. However, as soon as you `munmap` the region or close the process, the kernel will release the associated memory or swap space without dumping any data back to the file system, because in this case, there is no underlying file.

Please note that mapping a regular file and then unlinking it won't save you any flushing since the pages were already associated with the inode on mmap, so you might still have I/O upon eviction even if you unlink.

As for MacOS, it behaves similarly to Linux from what I understand.

Please note that this answer is based on how I understand Linux and MacOS handles virtual memory, and I could not verify it specifically with an official Apple document or Linux kernel source code.
<|end|>


