<|asst|>
In Rust, we can design a function named vowels_count that behaves identically to the Python function. Below I use `chars()` function to iterate over every character in the string.

```rust
pub fn vowels_count(s: &str) -> i32 {
    let vowels = "aeiou";
    let s = s.to_lowercase();
    let mut count = 0;
    for c in s.chars() {
        if vowels.contains(c) {
            count += 1;
        }
    }
    if s.chars().last().unwrap_or(' ') == 'y' {
        count += 1;
    }
    count
}
```
Then, you can use `assert!` macro to make tests embedded in the Rust code. In the following code, you can see an analogous translations of the tests seen in the Python code.
```rust
fn main() {
    assert_eq!(vowels_count("abcde"), 2); 
    assert_eq!(vowels_count("ACEDY"), 3); 
    assert_eq!(vowels_count("éxamplë"), 2);
}
``` 

The function will run perfectly unless the return value of `s.chars().last()` is unpredictable. In that case, using `unwrap_or(' ')` which provides a default value, helps catching the edge case.
