If I am trying to price a strategy, say for example a call spread where we are long a call, strike L and short a call strike M, would the pricing formula simply be the Black-Sholes price for the Call at Strike L subtract the Black Scholes price for a call at Strike M?
Is it really that simple or am I missing something?
Furthermore, the Delta would be 0 when the price is < l or > m, but in between l and m, would it just be the average of each leg?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, to price a call spread using the Black-Scholes model, you simply need to subtract the price of the call option you are shorting (with strike M) from the price of the call option you are buying (with strike L). This is because the cost of setting up the call spread strategy is the difference between the premium you paid to buy the call with strike L and the premium you received from writing the call with strike M.

Concerning the Delta, it wouldn’t be an average of each leg. Delta is the rate of change of the option price with respect to the underlying security price. For a call spread, you can compute the Delta by taking the Delta of the long call and subtracting the Delta of the short call. 

The Delta will indeed be near zero when the underlying security price is less than L or greater than M because a call spread's value doesn't change much outside this price range. But within this range, it will fluctuate between those values based on the underlying security price and the delta of each call option leg.
<|end|>


