In the event that you're grappling with an overfitting issue in your computational learning algorithm, which of the following strategies would be deemed inappropriate or counterproductive in your pursuit to mitigate the overfitting phenomenon? A) Augmenting the quantity of training data, B) Refining the error reduction function of the employed optimization algorithm, C) Reducing the intricacy of the predictive model, D) Decreasing the noise level inherent in the training data.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
All of the listed strategies (A, B, C, and D) are typically helpful in combating overfitting in computational learning algorithms. None of them is considered inappropriate or counterproductive. Overfitting typically results from a model that is too complex, or from insufficient or noisy data, so strategies that simplify the model, increase the quantity of data, improve the error function, or decrease noise can all potentially help.
<|end|>


