Develop a JavaScript function capable of swapping the stored information of two unique variables, ensuring that the data exchange process doesn't involve mutation or the creation of any new variables outside the existing scope.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In JavaScript, you can swap the values of two variables by using an array destructuring assignment:

```javascript
function swap(a, b) {
    [a, b] = [b, a];
    console.log('a:', a, 'b:', b);     
}
```
Let's test it:

```javascript
swap(5, 10);  // "a: 10 b: 5"
```

This function successfully swaps the values of two variables. However, in JavaScript, everything is passed by value. Therefore, if we're passing in variables, their values will be copied, the variables within the function scope will have swapped values, but original variables will have unchanged values.

To swap values of variables in the calling scope, you would typically use a temporary variable. However, the problem statement restricts creating any new variables outside of provided ones. In JavaScript, it's not possible to perform this action without violating this condition.

This is one of the instances where JavaScript’s pass-by-value nature comes into play: it’s not possible to swap two variables without introduction of a third variable (or usage of a higher-level structure). It's a limitation of JavaScript and most other pass-by-value languages. Usually, the pattern for it is:
```javascript
let a = 5, b = 10;
let temp = a; 
a = b;
b = temp;
```
But this involves creating a new variable 'temp'.
<|end|>


