I have the following code and I would like to avoid typing the conditional statement every single time after every instruction. The only improvement I can think of to improve my code quality is using a function. Could there be anything similar to a `try catch` in go that can make this better ?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Go discourages the use of exceptions for normal error handling in favor of return values. There is no 'try-catch' concept but you can create a helper function for error handling to make your code cleaner:
	
```go
func handleError(err error) {
	if err != nil {
		// Handle error here...
		log.Fatal(err) // replace this with whatever necessary
	}
}

func main() {

	a, err := A()
	handleError(err)
 
	b, err := B()
	handleError(err)
 
	c, err := C()
	handleError(err)

	// ...
}
``` 

Alternatively, consider using the "github.com/pkg/errors" package, you can wrap errors with more context:

```go
a, err := A()
if err != nil {
	return nil, errors.Wrap(err, "Failed to perform A")
}
b, err := B()
if err != nil {
	return nil, errors.Wrap(err, "Failed to perform B")
}
c, err := C()
if err != nil {
	return nil, errors.Wrap(err, "Failed to perform C")
}
```

These will allow the caller to use `errors.Cause(err)` to get the original error, and lots of other helpful methods for inspecting the error.

Another concept you could use the "[must](https://golang.org/pkg/regexp/#MustCompile)" concept in standard library. Although using panics aren't conventionally used as error handling , they could be used for instance configuration errors which will halt the system anyways.

```go
func MustA() AType {
	a, err := A()
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
	return a
}

func MustB() BType {
	b, err := B()
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
	return b
}

func MustC() CType {
	c, err := C()
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
	return c
}
```

And then use it like:

```go
a := MustA()
b := MustB()
c := MustC()
```
<|end|>


