I have a new 1 TB drive coming in tomorrow. What is the best way to divide this space for a development workstation?

The biggest problem I think I'm going to have is that some partitions (probably /usr) will become to small after a bit of use. Other partitions are probably to huge. The swap drive for example is currently 2GB (2x 1GB RAM), but it is almost never used (only once that I know of).
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This is quite subjective because it really depends on how you intend to use your system. But as a starting point, you could consider the following allocation:

1. "/boot" : 1 GB 

Many recommend they keep it small, around a few hundred MB, but I leave a bit more for flexibility. 

2. "/" (root): 50 -100 GB 

This will include "/usr". Unless you're installing a huge amount of applications, this should be sufficient.

3. "/home": Rest of the space 

This is where most of your personal files will go. Depending on your usage, it could fill up quite a bit, especially if you keep big files (like images, videos, etc.). So, it could be beneficial to keep the majority of your space here (around 800-900 GB).

4. Swap: 2-4 GB 

With 1GB of RAM, having the same or a little more should be fine. However, if you're planning to use hibernation, you should increase your swap space to match your RAM size.

5. "/var": 10-15 GB 

Var holds variable data such as log files. The size will depend on how much and what type of services you run on your system.

6. "/tmp": 5-10 GB 

Temporary files are stored here. 

These are estimates that should offer a good starting point for a typical developer workstation. Adjust according to your needs and workflow. Remember, it's easier to allocate more space from the beginning than to reallocate later. 

Some might argue to use LVM (Logical Volume Manager) as it gives flexibility to resize your partitions easily later, especially when you are not sure of how space will be used. However, it adds layer of complexity. 

Also keep in mind, regularly backing up important data is crucial as well, not just managing disk space. You can have the best partition layout in the world, but it won't protect your important files from accidental deletions, hardware failures, etc.
<|end|>


