I am currently learning OLAP & MDX after many years of relational database development.  

Any tips on getting started in MDX? What are the best books and resources to learn MDX?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Transitioning from relational database development to OLAP and MDX can indeed be quite challenging. Here are some tips, books, and resources to help you get started with using MDX:

1. Tips:
   - Think multi-dimensional: Unlike SQL which is set-based and two-dimensional, MDX queries data from a cube (which is multi-dimensional). Every data point can be addressed by the intersection of dimensions.
   - Know the basic syntax and functions: Learn the basic structure of a MDX query - Measures on columns, Dimensions on rows. And be familiar with important functions such as .Members, .Children, .CurrentMember, .Parent, etc.

2. Books:
   - "MDX Solutions: With Microsoft SQL Server Analysis Services" by George Spofford and Sivakumar Harinath: Offers a real-world introduction to MDX, providing a comprehensive guide with examples.
   - "MDX with SSAS 2012 Cookbook" by Tomislav Piasevoli: Provides more than 80 hands-on recipes to help you solve common problems with MDX and SSAS.
   - "Professional Microsoft SQL Server 2012 Analysis Services with MDX and DAX" by Sivakumar Harinath and Ronald Pihlgren: Covers the multiple dimensions of data processing with SSAS and MDX.

3. Resources:
   - MSBISkills.com: Offers tutorials on basic and advanced MDX concepts.
   - MDX Studio: A tool that helps to learn and use MDX. It provides performance analysis of MDX queries.
   - SQL Server Analysis Services (SSAS) Tutorial: Offers step by step guide to learn SSAS.
   
4. Web Resources & Blogs:
   - MDSN MDX Reference (https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/mdx/mdx-statement-reference-mdx?view=sql-server-ver15): Comprehensive guide and examples on MDX from Microsoft. 
   - Chris Webb's BI Blog (https://blog.crossjoin.co.uk/): Covers a range of topics on MDX.
   
Apart from above, practice is really important. The more you practice, the better you will get at formulating queries and models.
<|end|>


