Given the Ethereum USD pricing data available at this link: https://github.com/ethereum/csv/raw/master/ethereum.csv, your task is to analyze this data and display it through visual components. 

Please perform the following:

1. Load and clean the data set, handling any missing or error-prone data.
2. Create an algorithmically calculated, weighted moving average of the prices over a specific period. You should be able to change the weighting system to allow for a greater focus on more recent prices if required.
3. Provide a weekly chart of the moving averages.
4. Calculate the standard deviation of the prices for the same period, and use this to generate upper and lower Bollinger Bands.
5. Plot these Bollinger Bands on the weekly chart.
6. Finally, formulate a simple trading strategy based on the moving averages and Bollinger Bands and backtest it on this same data.

Note: You are allowed to use any statistical approach to confirm the assumptions you use in your trading strategy. Write this code in Python, and use Matplotlib, NumPy and Pandas for data manipulation and visualization. Provide inline comments for your code for clarification of your logic and approach.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's how to approach this task:

```python
# First we start with importing the necessary libraries
import pandas as pd
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from datetime import datetime
from matplotlib import style
style.use('ggplot')

# Load the data
url = "https://github.com/ethereum/csv/raw/master/ethereum.csv"
data = pd.read_csv(url)

# Clean the data
data.dropna(inplace=True)  # drop missing values
data['Date'] = pd.to_datetime(data.Date, format="%Y-%m-%d")

# Reset index to date
data.set_index('Date', inplace=True)

# Calculate weighted moving average
wma_window_len = 10  # period over which WMA will be calculated
weights = np.arange(1, wma_window_len + 1)
data['WMA'] = data['Value'].rolling(wma_window_len).apply(lambda prices: np.dot(prices, weights) / weights.sum(), raw=True)

# Calculate standard deviation
std_dev = data['Value'].rolling(window=wma_window_len).std()

# Calculate upper and lower Bollinger Bands
data['Upper BB'] = data['WMA'] + 2*std_dev
data['Lower BB'] = data['WMA'] - 2*std_dev

#Plotting the Bollinger Bands and moving averages
plt.figure(figsize=(12,6))
plt.plot(data['Value'], label='Ethereum prices')
plt.plot(data['WMA'], label='Weighted Moving Average')
plt.plot(data['Upper BB'], label='Upper Bollinger Band')
plt.plot(data['Lower BB'], label='Lower Bollinger Band')
plt.fill_between(data.index, data['Lower BB'], data['Upper BB'], color='gray', alpha=0.3)
plt.title('Bollinger Bands for Ethereum')
plt.ylabel('Price (USD)')
plt.xlabel('Date')
plt.legend(loc='best')
plt.show()

# Define the simple trading strategy: Buy when the price hits the lower Bollinger band and sell when it hits the upper Bollinger band
# Additionally you can improve performance by selling only when hitting the upper band AND the moving average is falling
# We define a dataframe to keep track of the events
trades = pd.DataFrame(index=data.index)
trades['Buy'] = np.where((data['Value'] <= data['Lower BB']), 1, 0)  # Buy signal
trades['Sell'] = np.where((data['Value'] >= data['Upper BB']) & (data['WMA'] > data['WMA'].shift()), 1, 0)  # Sell signal

# Expected profit of the strategy
buy_price = data['Value'][trades['Buy'] > 0]
sell_price = data['Value'][trades['Sell'] > 0]
profits = sell_price.values - buy_price.values
expected_profit = np.mean(profits)
print("Expected profit:", expected_profit)
```
The above code imports the necessary libraries and loads the data we need, cleans the data, calculates the moving average and standard deviation, calculates and plots the Bollinger Bands, and finally applies a simple trading strategy. The trading strategy buys when the price hits the lower Bollinger band and sells when the price hits the upper Bollinger band and the moving average is increasing, implying an upwards trend. The strategy then calculates the average expected profit of each trade.
