In C#, what is the difference (if any) between these two lines of code?


tmrMain.Elapsed += new ElapsedEventHandler(tmrMain_Tick);



and


tmrMain.Elapsed += tmrMain_Tick;



Both appear to work exactly the same. Does C# just assume you mean the former when you type the latter?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, both lines of code achieve the same outcome of attaching an event handler to the Elapsed event of a Timer object. However, they use different syntaxes.

- `tmrMain.Elapsed += new ElapsedEventHandler(tmrMain_Tick);`
  
  This line uses the older, explicit syntax where the delegate `ElapsedEventHandler` is being explicitly instantiated. This was the syntax used in C# 1.0, before the introduction of anonymous methods and lambda expressions.

- `tmrMain.Elapsed += tmrMain_Tick;`
  
  This line uses the newer, implicit syntax. Starting from C# 2.0 and onwards, the compiler allows for implicit conversion from methods to delegate types, and is capable of inferring the needed delegate creation. This makes the code cleaner and easier to read.

So, while both perform the same function, the latter is preferred due to being more modern and readable. Some integrated development environments (IDEs) like Visual Studio will even recommend converting the former to the latter.
<|end|>


