Implement a C++ function intended to determine the relative primality status of a pair of numerical inputs, expanding on principles of mathematical number theory.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In number theory, two integers a and b are said to be relatively prime, mutually prime, or coprime if the only positive integer that divides both of them is 1. That is, the only common positive factor of the two numbers is 1. 

This function can determine whether two integers are relatively prime. To this end, we can make use of the Euclidean algorithm principle, considering two numbers to be relatively prime if their greatest common divisor (gcd) equals 1.

Here is the C++ function:

```c++
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

// Function to find gcd of a and b
int gcd(int a, int b) {
    if (b == 0)
        return a;
    return gcd(b, a % b);
}

// Function to check if two numbers are relatively prime
bool areRelativelyPrime(int a, int b) {
    return (gcd(a, b) == 1);
}

int main() {
    int num1 = 34, num2 = 17;
  
    if (areRelativelyPrime(num1, num2))
        cout << num1 << " and " << num2 << " are relatively prime." << endl;
    else
        cout << num1 << " and " << num2 << " are not relatively prime." << endl;
    
    return 0;
}
```
You can replace `num1` and `num2` with the pair of numbers you want to check. The `areRelativelyPrime` function will return true if they are relatively prime and false if they are not.

The `gcd` function is utilized within the `areRelativelyPrime` function. It uses recursion to calculate the greatest common divisor between two numbers, relying on the fact that the gcd of a and b is the same as the gcd of b and a%b.
<|end|>


