<|user|>
Develop a Singleton design pattern class utilizing Java programming language, incorporating a synchronized method to bolster thread safety, and ensure that only one instance of the class can exist at any given time.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, here is a basic Singleton class example in Java with a synchronized method for thread safety:

```java
public class Singleton {
    private static Singleton instance = null;

    // Private constructor prevents instantiation from other classes
    private Singleton() {}

    public static synchronized Singleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new Singleton();
        }
        return instance;
    }

    // Example of a method within the class
    public void showMessage(){
           System.out.println("Hello from Singleton!");
    }
}
```

In this code:

- `Singleton` class contains a static field `instance` that will hold the one and only instance of `Singleton` when one is created.
- `Singleton()` is private, which assures the class cannot be instantiated other than inside this class.
- `getInstance()` method is synchronized to prevent two threads from concurrently entering the `if` statement and thereby creating two instances of the Singleton. If `instance` is `null`, it creates a new `Singleton` instance and if not, it returns the existing `instance`.

To use it, get the one-and-only instance with the `getInstance()` method:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Get the only object available
        Singleton singleton = Singleton.getInstance();

        // Show the message
        singleton.showMessage();
    }
}
```

This style of singleton is thread-safe and also lazy-loaded. The instance is not created until the `getInstance()` method is called the first time. This can be useful if the Singleton class is large and consumes significant resources.
<|end|>


