Using reflection, how can I get all types that implement an interface with C# 3.0/.NET 3.5 with the least code, and minimizing iterations?

This is what I want to re-write:


foreach (Type t in this.GetType().Assembly.GetTypes())
    if (t is IMyInterface)
        ; //do stuff
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Note: The code was checking if the Type `t` is IMyInterface which will never be true, it should be checking if the Type `t` implements the interface IMyInterface.

REPLACE THAT CODE WITH THIS:

```
var types = AppDomain.CurrentDomain.GetAssemblies()
    .SelectMany(s => s.GetTypes())
    .Where(p => typeof(IMyInterface).IsAssignableFrom(p));
```

This will get all types from all assemblies that implement `IMyInterface`. If you only want to check a specific assembly, you can use the original `this.GetType().Assembly.GetTypes()` instead of `AppDomain.CurrentDomain.GetAssemblies().SelectMany(s => s.GetTypes())`.

Also, if you have sub interfaces of `IMyInterface` and you don't want types implementing them to be returned, replace `IsAssignableFrom` by `==`:

```
var types = AppDomain.CurrentDomain.GetAssemblies()
    .SelectMany(s => s.GetTypes())
    .Where(p => p.GetInterfaces().Any(i=> i == typeof(IMyInterface)));
```

Remember that this kind of approach might lead to slow application startups or higher memory usage because you are loading all assemblies and enumerating all types. It is also important to know IMyInterface cannot be a private or protected interface; any code outside the assembly where IMyInterface is declared won't be able to see or use the interface at all.
<|end|>


