If an experimental study is implemented and the ensuing effect is being measured by the average improvement in participants' performance in long-jump, a two-sample t-test will be conducted utilizing a 5% level of significance. Keeping all other variables constant, which statement correctly portrays a potential procedural amendment?
A) would the power of the test be augmented if we transition from equal size treatment groups to significantly diverse size treatment groups? 
B) would the potency of the examination be enhanced if we alter from a 5% significance threshold to a 1% significance level?
C) would meticulous measurement techniques that minimize response variability improve the strength of the test?
D) would a boost in sample size decrease the risk of committing a Type I error?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
C) Would meticulous measurement techniques that minimize response variability improve the strength of the test?

The statistical power of a given test refers to the probability that the test will detect a statistically significant effect when one is actually present. Among the factors that can influence statistical power is variability within the sample data.

Variability refers to how spread out or closely grouped a set of data is. High variability can make it more difficult to detect a pattern or an effect, thus decreasing statistical power, whereas low variability can have the opposite effect, increasing the ability to detect a true effect and thereby enhancing statistical power.

Given this, implementing a meticulous measurement technique could potentially reduce response variability (i.e., variability internal to each participant’s performance), which in turn could increase the power of the test. This implies that the use of such techniques might be advisable if enhancing the power of the test is a key goal.
<|end|>


