Debug and complete the below provided code. Optimize the code if possible.

The code must return the greatest common divisor of two integers a and b.

Here is the incorrectly implemented code:
```javascript
const greatestCommonDivisor = (a, b) => {
  while (b != 0) {
    temp = b;
    a = b;
    b = a % temp;
  }
  return a;
}
```
Example:
```javascript
greatestCommonDivisor(3, 5) // Expected output: 1
greatestCommonDivisor(25, 15) // Expected output: 5
```
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The code provided contains an error. In the while loop, after `temp = b;`, `a` is reassigned to `b`'s value. However, when computing `b = a % temp;` the new value of `a` is used instead of the previous one, thus resulting in a wrong computation.

In addition to the error, we can avoid using an unnecessary extra variable `temp`.

The fixed and optimized version of this implementation in JavaScript is:

```javascript
const greatestCommonDivisor = (a, b) => {
  while (b !== 0) {
    let temp = a % b; 
    a = b; 
    b = temp;
  }
  return a;
}
```
This implementation uses Euclid's algorithm, reducing the problem of finding the GCD of two numbers to the problem of finding the GCD of smaller numbers, and will function correctly for any pair of integers. In the while loop, `b` will eventually become `0`, so `a` will be the GCD.
<|end|>


