Create a JavaScript function to merge multiple objects into a single object. The objects may have duplicate keys. The function should be able to handle these duplicates by prioritizing the values in the latter objects. The function also needs to perform deep merging, where nested objects are merged instead of being replaced.

Example:

var a = {name: 'John', age: 21, details: {eyes: 'blue', hair: 'black'}};
var b = {surname: 'Doe', gender: 'male', details: {hair: 'brown', height: '6ft'}};

The resulting merged object should be:

{
  name: 'John',
  age: 21,
  surname: 'Doe',
  gender: 'male',
  details: {eyes: 'blue', hair: 'brown', height: '6ft'}
}
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a function in JavaScript that should perform the necessary merging action on your objects:

```javascript
function isObject(item) {
  return (item && typeof item === 'object' && !Array.isArray(item));
}

function mergeDeep(target, ...sources) {
  if (!sources.length) return target;
  const source = sources.shift();

  if (isObject(target) && isObject(source)) {
    for (const key in source) {
      if (isObject(source[key])) {
        if (!target[key]) Object.assign(target, { [key]: {} });
        mergeDeep(target[key], source[key]);
      } else {
        Object.assign(target, { [key]: source[key] });
      }
    }
  }

  return mergeDeep(target, ...sources);
}

// use the function
var a = {name: 'John', age: 21, details: {eyes: 'blue', hair: 'black'}};
var b = {surname: 'Doe', gender: 'male', details: {hair: 'brown', height: '6ft'}};

var result = mergeDeep({}, a, b);
console.log(result);
```

In this solution, we first define a helper function `isObject` that checks whether a given item is an object or not. We need this function because we want to make a deep copy of nested objects, rather than just copying their references.

In the `mergeDeep` function, we're merging all properties from each source object into the target object. If the property value we are trying to merge is an object itself, the function will call itself recursively in order to handle this nested object. Referenced objects will be entirely replaced if the new value isn't also an object, but when it is an object, a new object is generated with properties from both old object and new object (with the properties from the new object overwriting the old ones).
<|end|>


