I know what `yield` does, and I've seen a few examples, but I can't think of real life applications, have you used it to solve some specific problem?

(Ideally some problem that cannot be solved some other way)
<|end|>
<|asst|>
`yield` in Python enables function to remember its state and allows it to produce a series of results over time, rather than computing them all at once and returning them in a huge list, for instance. It's a concept of iterators or generator functions.

It is useful when the produced sequence is huge or infinite, so computing it fully in memory is not an option. Another real-life use case is when each item in the sequence is expensive to compute and you want to do it on the go.

One such example was when I was working with Natural Language Processing to generate all possible permutations of a sentence based on synonyms of each word in the sentence.

Say, I have a sentence - "Quick brown fox" and I want to generate all combinations using synonyms of 'quick', 'brown' and 'fox'. If we try to store all permutations in memory at once, it becomes pretty big. But with `yield`, we can generate each permutation one by one.

Another real-life example is when I was working on a web scraping project. The aim was to scrape a huge website. If we try to scrape all the data at once and then return it as a huge list, it will consume a lot of memory. Instead, I used a generator with `yield` that returned one scraped page at a time, significantly saving memory usage. 

These examples can technically be solved in other ways, but `yield` makes it very memory efficient.

In general, `yield` is great for working with large data streams or files as it allows you to create a pipeline of functions that can process parts of data one after another, rather than having to process it all at once.
<|end|>


