Generate a recursive function in Java for printing Fibonacci series up to number N. Ensure to handle potential error cases, such as when N is less than 0. Discuss the space and time complexity of your solution.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a recursive function in Java for printing the Fibonacci series up to a given number N.

```java
public class FibonacciSeries {
    public static void printFibonacci(int n) {
        if (n < 0) {
            System.out.println("Input cannot be negative!");
            return;
        } else if (n == 0) {
            System.out.println("0");
            return;
        } 

        int a = 0;
        int b = 1;

        System.out.print("Fibonacci Series: " + a + " " + b);

        // print the rest of Fibonacci numbers
        printFibonacciUtil(n, a, b);
    }

    private static void printFibonacciUtil(int n, int a, int b) {
        if (n <= 1)
            return;
        
        int c = a + b;
        System.out.print(" " + c);
        printFibonacciUtil(n - 1, b, c);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        printFibonacci(10);
    }
}
```
In this program, `printFibonacci` function checks for error conditions, and if no error is found, it prints the first two numbers of the Fibonacci series, and then calls the `printFibonacciUtil` function to print the rest of the numbers. `printFibonacciUtil` function reduces the problem size by 1 with each recursive call, and stops when n becomes 1.

Space Complexity:
The space complexity of this solution is O(N), representing the maximum depth of recursion that would be saved in the stack memory. For a large number N, this could result in a `StackOverflowException`.

Time Complexity:
The time complexity of this solution is O(N), as we only solve each subproblem once and the time to solve a subproblem is directly proportional to the size of the subproblem. However, due to the usage of recursion, this function may suffer from function call overhead for large N. This makes iterative solution more efficient for larger values of N (also avoiding the potential `StackOverflowException`).
<|end|>


