My automated script for starting and stopping VMWare Server virtual machines has stopped working. vmware-cmd has started raising the error: 

> The ordinal 3288 could not be located in the dynamic link library LIBEAY32.dll.

I am not aware of any specific change or update when this started happening.

I have found a bunch of other people reporting this problem (or very similar) but no solution.

Do you know what caused this error, and/or how to fix this?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This error usually indicates an issue with the OpenSSL library, which is specific to the DLL file mentioned. LIBEAY32.dll is part of the OpenSSL project's implementation of the SSL and TLS cryptographic protocols. 

A common reason for seeing this error is an incompatibility or conflict between different versions of this DLL. A newer or older version might have been installed by another application, or perhaps the current DLL has been corrupted or inadvertently deleted.

To fix this problem, you could try the following steps:

1. Backup the LIBEAY32.dll file: Navigate to the directory which contains this DLL, which may be in a location such as: C:\Program Files\VMware\VMware Server. Create a backup copy of this DLL, in case you need to revert to it later.

2. Update or replace DLL: Try replacing your existing LIBEAY32.dll with a different version. You could try to download a new version of LIBEAY32.dll from a trusted source. There are many sites that provide various versions of DLL files for download. Make sure to choose a reputable source to avoid any security risks.

3. Reinstall VMWare Server: If updating or replacing the DLL file does not work, consider reinstalling the VMWare Server software. Uninstall it completely first, and then install it again. During the reinstallation, the necessary DLL file should be installed.
   
4. Update OpenSSL: If none of the above steps work, consider updating your OpenSSL installation. It is possible that the VMware Server software you are using is not compatible with the version of OpenSSL installed on your machine.

5. Check for system-wide issues: Scan your computer for malware and make sure your system is updated to the latest version. Sometimes, DLL errors can be caused by malware or due to a bug in Windows.

Remember to always be cautious when downloading and installing DLL files from the internet; they can often be a source of malware. Reinstalling the related software is usually the safest way to fix these types of errors.
