<|user|>
Rewrite the below code in Rust syntax:
def reverse_string(s):
    i, j = 0, len(s) - 1

    # Check whether the string is empty or contains only one character.
    if len(s) == 0 or len(s) == 1:
        return s

    # Convert the string to a list of characters
    s = list(s)

    while i < j:
        # Ignore special characters
        if not s[i].isalnum():
            i += 1
        elif not s[j].isalnum():
            j -= 1

        # Swap the characters
        else:
            s[i], s[j] = s[j], s[i]
            i += 1
            j -= 1

    # Convert the list back to a string
    s = ''.join(s)

    return s
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is the equivalent code in Rust:

```rust
fn reverse_string(s: &str) -> String {
    let mut s = s.chars().collect::<Vec<char>>();
    let mut i = 0;
    let mut j = s.len() - 1;

    // Check whether the string is empty or contains only one character
    if s.len() == 0 || s.len() == 1 {
        return s.into_iter().collect::<String>();
    }

    while i < j {
        // Ignore special characters
        if !s[i].is_alphanumeric() {
            i += 1;
        } else if !s[j].is_alphanumeric() {
            j -= 1;
        }
        // Swap the characters
        else {
            s.swap(i, j);
            i += 1;
            j -= 1;
        }
    }
    
    s.into_iter().collect::<String>()
}
```
Note that the Rust program's behavior is a bit different from the Python program's, especially around characters that are not considered alphanumeric in Rust's `char::is_alphanumeric`, while Python's `str.isalnum`considers them as alphanumeric.
<|end|>


