<|asst|>
Sure, here is the Perl version for your function:

```perl
use strict;
use warnings;

# Script to find intersection
sub intersection{
    my @pairOfIntegers1 = @{$_[0]};
    my @pairOfIntegers2 = @{$_[1]};

    # Calculating common start and end numbers in the pairs
    my $commonStart = ($pairOfIntegers1[0] > $pairOfIntegers2[0]) ? $pairOfIntegers1[0] : $pairOfIntegers2[0];
    my $commonEnd = ($pairOfIntegers1[1] < $pairOfIntegers2[1]) ? $pairOfIntegers1[1] : $pairOfIntegers2[1];

    # Checking if there is at least 1 common integer
    if ($commonEnd - $commonStart < 1){
        return "NO";  # No common integer
    }

    my $intersectionLength = $commonEnd - $commonStart + 1;

    # Checking if intersection length is divisible by i or not
    # If the length is divisible, return "NO", as it means there are other factors 
    for (my $i = 2; $i <= sqrt($intersectionLength); $i++){
         if ($intersectionLength % $i == 0) {
             return "NO";  # Found a factor of intersectionLength
         }
     }

    return "YES";  # If no factors found in previous loop
}
```

The logic of this script is to find out whether there is an intersection (at least 1 common integer) between two pairs of integers, and if there is, we check whether the length of this intersection is a prime number or not. If the length factors other than 1 and itself, then it's not a prime number, and we return "NO". Otherwise, it's a prime number, and we return "YES".
