Construct a jQuery expression to dynamically hide and display different HTML elements on a webpage. Your program should:

- Conceal every HTML element carrying the class attribute "closed" from visibility on the webpage. 
- Also, it should reveal the elements with the class attribute "open".
- The change should occur whenever a user clicks a button with the id "toggle". Also, make sure the first click hides the "closed" elements and reveals the "open" elements, but a second click reverses this, and so on.

Test your solution thoroughly with different HTML pages to ensure its proper functioning. Please provide a clear explanation of your logic and reasoning for the approach you took to solve this problem.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is a script that will solve the problem at hand using jQuery. 

```javascript
$(document).ready(function(){
    var toggle = true;
    $("#toggle").click(function(){
        if(toggle){
            $(".closed").hide();
            $(".open").show();
            toggle = false;
        } else {
            $(".closed").show();
            $(".open").hide();
            toggle = true;
        }
    });
});
```

This script works as follows:

- The $(document).ready(function(){}) wrapper ensures that the script only runs once all page elements are loaded.
- A boolean variable `toggle` is set to `true` to keep track of whether the elements with class "open" or "closed" are displayed.
- The function bound to the click event of the button with id `toggle` checks the `toggle` status.
- If `toggle` is `true`, it hides all the elements with a class of "closed" (using the jQuery `hide()` function) and displays all the elements with a class of "open" (using the jQuery `show()` function). Subsequently, it sets `toggle` to `false`.
- Otherwise, if `toggle` is `false`, it hides "open" elements and shows "closed" elements, and changes `toggle` back to `true`. This way, the action reverses at each click, hence the toggle functionality.

You can include this script in your HTML pages, and test by creating a button with id `toggle` and other elements with class `open` or `closed`.

Please note that you need to include the jQuery library in your HTML pages for this script to run. You can include it by using the following tag in the HTML head section:

```html
<script src="https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/jquery/3.5.1/jquery.min.js"></script>
```
