Here is a step-by-step guide to get you started:

1. Open VS Code and make sure you have your workspace loaded (i.e., the directory which contains the projects you're working on).

2. In the menu bar, navigate to Terminal -> Configure Tasks. If you’ve never set a task in this workspace, you should be seeing options like "No task to configure". Click on "Create tasks.json file from template", and then select "Others". This will create a new `tasks.json` file in the `.vscode` directory at your workspace root.

3. In `tasks.json`, define a new task under `"tasks"`. As you're using `gcc` to build your programs, the `"command"` property should be set to `"gcc"`. Then, in `"args"`, list all the options, flags and arguments that you want to pass to `gcc`. Here is a sample task definition based on the command you provided:

```json
{
    // Other task properties...
    "tasks": [
        {
            "label": "build OpenGL Base",
            "type": "shell",
            "command": "gcc",
            "args": [
                "${workspaceFolder}/OpenGL Base/main.c",
                "-o",
                "${workspaceFolder}/OpenGL Base/main.exe",
                "-lglu32",
                "-lglut32",
                "-lopengl32",
                "-std=c99",
                "-I\"${workspaceFolder}/MinGW/include\"",
                "-L\"${workspaceFolder}/MinGW/lib\"",
                "-static-libstdc++",
                "-static-libgcc"
            ],
            "problemMatcher": [ "$gcc" ]
        }
    ]
}
```

Note that `${workspaceFolder}` is a variable automatically replaced by VS Code with the path to your workspace root directory.

With this task set up, you can run it from Terminal -> Run Task and then select the task you've just created.

If you have multiple projects in your workspace and want to compile them all with the same task, you may need to use more advanced configurations such as utilizing scripts or makefiles.
