i am using the IC.test formula to compare models but don't know how to interpret it

dqAIC df
DM7  0.0  20
DM1 65.7  17
DM2 65.7  17
DM3 65.7  17
DM4 67.8  7 
DM5 88.2  11
DM6   NA  23


This is the formula i used

testIC.D <- ICtab(DM1, DM2, DM3, DM4, DM5, DM6, DM7, type="qAIC")
testIC.D
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The results of IC.test (or ICtab) provide information on models selection based on information criteria such as AIC, BIC or QAIC. 

In your case, you have opted for "qAIC" (also known as 'quasi-AIC'), often used when dealing with over-dispersed count data (like in ecological studies with Poisson or negative binomial distributions).

Your output shows two columns (dqAIC and df) for each of your models (DM1, DM2, etc.).  

1. 'dqAIC': This represents the difference in qAIC between each model and the 'best' model (the one with the lowest qAIC). Lower values indicate better fit. In your output, DM7 has 0 indicating it's the best fitting model.

2. 'df': Degrees of Freedom for each model. The complexity of the model increases with the increase in the number of parameters. Thus, a model with higher df is more complex.

In general, the preferred model is the one providing the best balance between goodness-of-fit (lower IC values) and complexity (lower df values). A commonly used rule of thumb is that a difference of 2 or more in IC values represents a relevant difference in model fit.

In your case, DM7 appears to be the most favorable model. However, it's also the most complex one (df=20). If simpler models with slightly higher dqAIC (e.g., within 2 units of difference) were there, those might be considered better choices due to their simplicity.

Please note, model selection based on IC is just one criterion, and the choice of a model also depends on biological interpretability, the purpose of the model, and its predictive performance.

In addition, when working with QAIC, it's important to account for overdispersion in count data. Check that too while making final decision.
<|end|>


