JSX is a syntax extension for JavaScript primarily used with React, a JavaScript library for building user interfaces. Here's how you would write the "Hello, World!" greeting in JSX:

```jsx
import React from 'react';

class HelloWorld extends React.Component {
    render() {
        return (
            <div>
                Hello, World!
            </div>
        );
    }
}

export default HelloWorld;
```

You would then be able to include this HelloWorld component elsewhere in your React application to display the greeting "Hello, World!". Note that you can only run this code in a React environment.

Another, simpler version:

```jsx
import React from 'react';

function HelloWorld() {
    return <h1>Hello, World!</h1>;
}

export default HelloWorld;
```

This is a functional component in React, which is easier to understand, maintain, and debug than class components. However, they are limited in what they can do and when you have more advanced use cases you might need to use class components.

Also, you would need to render the component to the DOM:

```
import React from 'react';
import ReactDOM from 'react-dom';
import HelloWorld from './HelloWorld'; // Assuming HelloWorld component is in 'HelloWorld.js'

