I want to ask why Label Encoding before train test split is considered data leakage?
From my point of view, it is not. Because, for example, you encode "good" to 2, "neutral" to 1 and "bad" to 0. It will be same for both train and test sets.
So, why do we have to split first and then do label encoding?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Although in your example, that might be the case, in a general scenario it mightn't always be true. For instance, if you have some label values that are only present in the test set but not in the train set, when you label encode before splitting the data, you have information from the test set that's leaking into the training set. 

Consider a scenario where there is a categorical feature with different labels in the train and test set. Now, if you apply Label Encoding before splitting, it might create a misleading numerical relationship, assigning similar numerical labels to the different categorical labels in the train and test set or vice-versa. Such leakage might cause an unrealistic optimistic performance evaluation.

Therefore, the usual practice is to split the data first into train and test, and then apply any preprocessing steps (like scaling, encoding etc.) separately on these sets to avoid such data leakage.
<|end|>


