I am using pipes to transfer information between two vxWorks tasks.

Here is a code sample:


Init()
{
   fd = open("/pipe/mydev", O_RDWR, 0777);
...
}

taskRx()
{
   ...
   len = read(fd, rxbuf, MAX_RX_LEN);
   ...
}
taskTx()
{
   ...
   len = write(fd, txbuf, txLen);
   ...
}



If we send a message that is longer than MAX_RX_LEN, (ie txLen > MAX_RX_LEN) we do 2 reads to get the remainder of the message.

What we noticed is that the 2nd read didn't receive any data!

Why is that?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This behavior could be due to multiple factors such as:

1. Buffering: When reading from or writing to a pipe, buffering may be involved either at the OS level or specific to your application. If the data isn't being flushed properly in the writer task before it's read by the reader task, your reader task may not get all the data.
   
2. Pipe is Blocking or Non-blocking: If the pipe is opened in non-blocking mode, read() returns immediately without waiting for data. Make sure the pipe is open in blocking mode if you want to always read once data is written.
   
3. Timing Issues: If your writer task is not giving enough time for the reader task to process the data, it may overwrite the data before the reader task gets a chance to read it.

Please check the flags you're using to open the file, the flush mechanism if any, and the sync between your reader and writer tasks to troubleshoot further. Be sure to take into account buffer sizes and the possibility of split or chunked reads/writes when working with IO in vxWorks, as it's not guaranteed a write() will be atomic.
<|end|>


