Can the point estimate be equal to one of the boundaries of a 95% confidence interval? Or, can the under limit of a 95% confidence interval be equal to the point estimate?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
No, typically the point estimate, whether it is a sample mean or a proportion, lies in the center of the confidence interval. The confidence interval provides a range of plausible values for the population parameter. The lower and upper boundaries of the confidence interval are calculated by adding and subtracting the margin of error from the point estimate. So in most situations, the point estimate won't be exactly equal to one of the boundaries of the 95% confidence interval. It's conceived to fall in the middle of the interval. Similarly, the under limit or lower boundary of the 95% confidence interval can't be equal to the point estimate as it falls below the point estimate.
<|end|>


