<|asst|>
The mistake in your code is that, you are accessing array index more than its limit. 
Also, an array's index starts from 0, not 1. So to correct this code, you need to start the loop from length-1 and iterate until you reach 0 index. By using this, we print the array in a reversed order without going out of the boundary.

Corrected C code is:

```c
#include<stdio.h>

int main(){

    int arr[] = {1,2,3,4,5};
    int length = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);

    if(length == 0) {
        printf("Array is Empty\n");
        return 0;
    }

    for(int i=length-1; i>=0; i--){
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Here, an error handling condition is also added to check if the array is empty or not. If the array is empty, it will print "Array is Empty" message and the program will end returning 0. If array has elements, it will print those elements in reverse order.
