I am using Vim for windows installed in Unix mode. Thanks to this site I now use the `gf` command to go to a file under the cursor.

I'm looking for a command to either:


1. return to the previous file (similar to Ctrl+T for ctags), or
2. remap gf to automatically launch the new file in a new window.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
1. To return to the previous file, you can use the following command: `:bp` or `:b#`. The former stands for buffer previous which switches to the previous buffer in the buffer list. The latter (`:b#`) switches to the alternate file - meaning the last file you edited.

2. To remap `gf` to open the file under the cursor in a new window:

Add the following to your .vimrc file:

```
nnoremap gf :split <C-R><C-F><CR>
```

Every time you press `gf`, it will open the file under the cursor in a new window split. 

`<C-R><C-F>` will take the filename under the cursor into the command line, and `:split` will open the file in a new window.

NOTE: If you prefer to open the file in a vertical split, use `:vsplit` instead of `:split`.
<|end|>


