How can I set up my crontab to execute X script at 11:59PM every day without emailing me or creating any logs?

Right now my crontab looks something like this


@daily /path/to/script.sh
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To set up your crontab script to run daily at 11:59PM without sending any emails or generating logs, you'll need to redirect the standard output (STDOUT) and the standard error output (STDERR) to `/dev/null`.

Here's how you do it:

1. Open your crontab file in edit mode by typing the following command:

    ```bash
    crontab -e
    ```
    
2. Modify your current cron job to look like this:

    ```bash
    59 23 * * * /path/to/script.sh > /dev/null 2>&1
    ```
    
The line `59 23 * * *` sets the time to 11:59PM every day (`*` refers to every day of the month, every month and every day of the week).

The `>` operator is used to redirect STDOUT to `/dev/null`, effectively discarding it. Similarly, `2>&1` redirects STDERR to STDOUT, which is already being discarded. 

So `> /dev/null 2>&1` is essentially telling the system to discard both standard and error output.

3. Save the file and exit.

Your crontab should now run the script daily at the specified time, and you won't receive any emails or see any log files created as a result of its execution.

Remember that completely discarding output like this means that you won't have any way to debug your script if something goes wrong. So only use this method if you're absolutely sure your script is working perfectly!
<|end|>


