I heard a remarkable claim at work last week
> 
Fixed effects in logistic regression of panel data introduces bias, so we would want to do a linear probability model.


I find this remarkable for two reasons.

- The usual maximum likelihood estimator for a logistic regssion is biased, so if you are intolerant of biased estimators, the usual logistic regression was never for you.


- The whole point of GLMs is that they do so much similar to linear models, just with a nonlinear link function, and the proposed alternative of a linear probability model is exactly such a model.



What's the deal with including fixed effects in a logistic regression on panel data? (Why) Does such inclusion cause problems in a logistic regression but not in a linear regression?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The primary issue is not the inclusion of fixed effects per se, but rather the incidental parameters problem, which can occur when conducting a fixed effects logistic regression with panel data. This problem arises in non-linear models where the number of parameters grows with the size of the panel.

The incidental parameters problem can introduce bias in small sample sizes, however when the number of time periods is large the bias tend to be small. This potential bias has led some to suggest using a linear probability model instead. 

The linear probability model is more straightforward and does not suffer from incidental parameters problem. But it has other potential issues, for instance predicted probabilities can fall outside the 0-1 range. 

In conclusion, both models have their potential drawbacks and the choice between them will depend largely on the specifics of the use case, including considerations such as out-of-range predictions, bias, interpretability and the number of time periods in the panel data.
<|end|>


