We need to replace the menu system in our main ASP.NET application. So naturally we're looking at the ASP.NET `SiteMapProvider` and `Menu` controls. However we also need enough security to prevent users from directly entering URLs that they shouldn't have access to. We can do this by putting `<location>` entries in  and securing them individually but that's going to be a PITA to manage across multiple web servers.

Is there a `Provider` that can be used to, well, provide the equivalent of the `<location>` entries? I haven't been able to find one, and it's slightly frustrating given the existence of the [ConfigurationLocation](http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/system.configuration.configurationlocation.aspx) class.

Alternatively is there a configuration option we're missing in SiteMapProvider that will restrict users from getting to URLs they shouldn't?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, ASP.NET SiteMapProvider combined with security trimming can achieve this. Look at the `securityTrimmingEnabled` attribute. When `securityTrimmingEnabled` is true, the site map provider automatically trims site map nodes from the site map based on the currently authenticated user's roles.

Here's an example:
```xml
<siteMap defaultProvider="YourProvider" enabled="true">
    <providers>
        <add name="YourProvider" type="System.Web.XmlSiteMapProvider" siteMapFile="Web.sitemap" securityTrimmingEnabled="true" />
    </providers>
</siteMap>
```

In your `Web.sitemap` file, you can specify the roles allowed to access a particular node. 

```xml
<siteMapNode url="~/Home.aspx" title="Home"  description="Home Page" roles="*">
    <siteMapNode url="~/Admin.aspx" title="Admin"  description="Admin Page" roles="Admin" />
</siteMapNode>
```

In above example, `Admin.aspx` page is only accessible to users in "Admin" role, if someone directly enters the URL and he isn't part of Admin role, then they won't have access.

To use this feature properly, you need the role Manager feature in ASP.NET, which allows you to manage user roles easily. And please note that the `<authorization>` sections still need to be set in `Web.config` for those pages you want to secure.

In terms of the SiteMap and Menu controls, they will respect these settings. The menu will not display links users are not authorized to see.

You may have to implement custom SiteMapProvider if your authorization needs are complex. For example, if your roles are stored in a database or you perform authorization checks which are not role-based.
<|end|>


