I am trying to find a statistical test that will compare the medians of two columns. When I try have searched around I have found the wilcox.test function in R, however, I have read mixed interpretations on whether this is for the median or the mean. Could someone confirm whether or not this would be appropriate to compare median wages over time? If not, what tests would you recommend?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The Wilcoxon rank sum test (also known as the Mann-Whitney U test) is a nonparametric statistical test that is used to compare two independent samples to see if they likely come from the same distribution. Importantly, it compares the entire distributions of values, not just the means or medians. 

However, under certain (basic) conditions, it can be used to test whether the medians of the two groups are equal. In particular, if you know (or are willing to assume) that the shapes of the two distributions are the same, then differences in the distributions can be interpreted as differences in the medians. 

So depending on the data and your assumptions, the Wilcoxon rank sum test could be used to compare median wages over time. 

Also, there is another nonparametric test which is used to compare the medians of two or more groups, the Kruskal-Wallis test. It extends the Wilcoxon rank sum test to more than two groups.

However, it is important to keep in mind that these tests are more sensitive to differences in the shape of the distributions than the t-test (used for comparing means).
<|end|>


