With the Fed raising interest rates so fast and so drastically, classical interest rate models such as the Black Karasinski (BK), Hull-White (HW), etc., may have trouble calibrating to current rate levels and at the same time passing backtesting due to resent negative rates,
For example, you can shift BK to capture the past negative rates, but then you fail to capture high-interest rates (even when reasonably increasing the volatility terms)...
I wonder what is being done in practice under the current market environment in industry.
Any suggestions?  I appreciate any available references.
Thank you.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The rapid changes in interest rates can indeed present a challenge when using classical models, such as Black Karasinski (BK) and Hull-White (HW). Given the limitation of these models to adequately capture the current drastic negative, zero, and high-interest rates scenarios, some changes are being implemented in practices in the financial industry.

1. Use of Hybrid Models: These models take the traditional models, like those of Black-Karasinski and Hull-White, and add features that better model current observed market factors. For example, Hybrid Vasicek-GARCH model which is a mixture of Vasicek and GARCH models. It can cover the shortcomings and retain the strengths of both models, thus having a better ability to capture drastic changes in interest rates.

2. Deep Learning Techniques: Financial institutions and hedge funds have started to employ machine learning algorithms to capture the nonlinear relations in these drastic shifts more dynamically.

3. Adjusting Models: The traditional models are being adjusted with floor methodology integration to account for these low or negative rates.

4. Other Models: The industry might also resort to the use of stochastic volatility models which are capable of coping with large movements of the interest rates.

5. Multiple Curve Approach: As for derivatives pricing, a multiple curve approach is being used which distinguishes between the forecasting curve and the discounting curve creating more robustness against fast changes. 

6. Hedge accordingly: In trading, you could add a tail hedge that profits from movements in interest rates that are not captured by the calibrated model. 

There are not a lot of publications on this subject. For more theoretical aspects, you might consider the book "Interest Rate Models – Theory and Practice" by Damiano Brigo and Fabio Mercurio. For the practical side of things, most of the information would not be freely disclosed, as these adjustments would constitute a competitive edge in trading strategies. 

Remember that each model will have its own strengths and weaknesses, and the choice of model will often reflect the specific needs and circumstances of the financial institution. It is also crucial to remember that models are just approximations of reality and will never be able to completely capture all aspects of market behavior. Models are tools to help us understand and make educated guesses about the future, but they are not crystal balls.
<|end|>


