I want to access the call stack at runtime in a Native C++ application. I am not using the IDE. How do I display the call stack?

Update: I have a function which is called from many points all over the application. It crashes on rare occasions. I was looking for a way to get name of the caller and log it.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Getting the call stack at runtime in native C++ can be a complex task that requires platform-dependent code. This is because the call stack information isn't inherently part of the portable (ISO) C++ language or its standard libraries, instead, it is managed by the operating system and can be retrieved via platform-specific APIs.

Here is an example code using CaptureStackBackTrace function, part of the Windows API. 

You should also include "Dbghelp.h" to your project. Note that this will work only on Windows.

```cpp
#include <windows.h>
#include <dbghelp.h>
#include <string>
#include <iostream>


std::string GetCallStack(int skipFrames = 0) 
{
    unsigned int   i;
    void         * stack[100];
    unsigned short frames;
    SYMBOL_INFO  * symbol;
    HANDLE         process;

    process = GetCurrentProcess();

    SymInitialize(process, NULL, TRUE);

    frames = CaptureStackBackTrace(skipFrames + 1, 100, stack, NULL);
    symbol = (SYMBOL_INFO*)calloc(sizeof(SYMBOL_INFO) + 256 * sizeof(char), 1);
    symbol->MaxNameLen = 255;
    symbol->SizeOfStruct = sizeof(SYMBOL_INFO);

    std::string callStack;

    for(i = 0; i < frames; i++)
    {
        SymFromAddr(process, (DWORD64)(stack[i]), 0, symbol);
        callStack += std::to_string(frames - i - 1) + ": " + symbol->Name + "\n";
    }

    return callStack;

    free(symbol);
}

void test() 
{
    std::cout << GetCallStack(1);
}

void anotherTest()
{
    test();
}

int main()
{
    anotherTest();
    return 0;
}
```

This code will print the function call trace when invoked. Note that actual function name resolution only works if your function names have been exported (for DLLs) or if you're running in debug and have PDB files available.

If the situation you're dealing with is a crash, your best option may be to use a post-mortem debugger or a crash reporting library, which can automatically capture and present the call stack at the time of the crash, among other information. It could be more effective than writing your own stack trace function.

Remember to link your project with "Dbghelp.lib" if you are using Microsoft's linker.

Also, always be aware of the license terms of any code you use (this code is based on an example from MSDN and as such, use of the code is subject to your agreement with Microsoft).
<|end|>


