[SHA Hash functions](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SHA-1)
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The Secure Hash Algorithms (SHA) are a family of cryptographic hash functions published by the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) as a U.S. Federal Information Processing Standard. The most commonly used functions in this family are SHA-1, SHA-256, and SHA-3.

SHA-1 is the most widely used of the existing SHA hash functions and is employed in several widely used security applications and protocols, including TLS and SSL, PGP, SSH, and IPsec. However, SHA-1's weakness in collision resistance has been exploited in practice, and it is now considered to be broken for most purposes.

SHA-256 is part of the SHA-2 family and is now commonly used in recent security protocols and systems. It provides a stronger security than SHA-1 and is not yet compromised in any practical way.

SHA-3, released in 2015, is the latest member of the Secure Hash Algorithm family and is considered to be even more secure than the previous hash functions. It can be used when the highest level of security is required.

In general, the goal of any hash function is to take an input (or 'message') and return a fixed-size string of bytes that is typically a 'digest' that is unique to each unique input. It is designed such that any change to the input, even a minute one, should produce such a drastic change in the output that the new hash value appears uncorrelated with the old hash value. This randomness makes them suitable for cryptography and for integrity checks.
<|end|>


