Designing an AI model to detect sophisticated cyber-attacks on electronic health record (EHR) systems would require a mix of various skills and tools, including artificial intelligence, machine learning, cybersecurity, and potentially natural language processing. A general way to approach this problem would be a three-step process: preprocessing and feature extraction, model selection, and training and evaluation.

This is a complex task which involves handling sensitive data, so it's crucial to ensure that all the data handling processes comply with all the various privacy laws and standards. This solution involves using machine learning (more specifically deep learning) to detect anomalies that might indicate a cyber attack.

Here's a simplified Python-esque pseudo code that would work with a tool like Keras or TensorFlow. For the sake of answer length and complexity the code displays one way of implementation and doesn't cover everything (like data preprocessing, feature extraction).

Note that the actual implementation would be much more complex. It will include significant data pre-processing, feature engineering/extraction, hyperparameter tuning, and would require large amounts of labeled data (both normal and abnormal traffic).

```python
from keras.models import Sequential
from keras.layers import Dense
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split

# Assuming that X is feature matrix and y are targets
X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, test_size = 0.2)

# define the keras model
model = Sequential()
model.add(Dense(128, input_dim=X_train.shape[1], activation='relu'))
model.add(Dense(64, activation='relu'))
model.add(Dense(32, activation='relu'))
model.add(Dense(16, activation='relu'))
model.add(Dense(1, activation='sigmoid'))

# compile the keras model
model.compile(loss='binary_crossentropy', optimizer='adam', metrics=['accuracy'])

# fit the keras model on the dataset
model.fit(X_train, y_train, epochs=100, batch_size=10)

# evaluate the model
accuracy = model.evaluate(X_test, y_test)
```

This will create a basic feed-forward neural network (Dense layers) and optimize it using the 'adam' optimizer and 'binary_crossentropy' loss function (because I am assuming we have a binary classification task: attack vs no attack).

It's also possible to use different types of layers like Convolution layers, dropout layers, etc. depending on the format of your data and the specific problem you are trying to solve.

Remember, you'll need a labeled dataset (examples of both normal traffic and malicious traffic) to train this network. Without a high-quality dataset, the performance of this network will likely be poor. If there's a lack of labels, you may also explore semi-supervised or unsupervised approaches.
