I am working on upgrades to a screen scraping application. We are using an ActiveX control to scrape screens out of an IBM mainframe. The mainframe program often hangs and crashes the ActiveX control causing our application to crash. We don't have access to the mainframe or the ActiveX source code. We are not going to write our own active x control.

What is the bast way to encapsulate an ActiveX control to detect application hangs with the control so we can kill the process and restart with code?

Should I create 2 separate applications? One as a controller that checks on the other and kills/restarts the process when it hangs? 

Would they have to be on separate app domains? Is it possible have two programs communicate with each other even if they are on separate app domains?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The best approach in this scenario would be to run your ActiveX control in a separate process rather than a separate application, and then monitor that process from your main application. In essence, this is similar to creating a supervisor that checks the worker and restarts it when it breaks.

By running the ActiveX control in a separate process, you isolate it from your main application, meaning that when it crashes it doesn't interrupt the operation of the primary application.

Communicating between two different processes can be done using Inter-Process Communication (IPC) mechanisms such as WCF, named pipes, sockets, message queues, or even shared memory. 

Alternatively, you can just run regular checks from your supervisor 'watchdog' to see if the ActiveX control process has become unresponsive or has exited. If it has, you can then kill the process and spawn a new one.

You don't need to operate these on separate app domains - indeed, by running them as separate processes, they will be isolated from one another and run on different threads.

Remember, the key is to run the potentially unstable ActiveX control in a separate process that is monitored, and can be killed and restarted when needed, by the main application.

Also, using the Windows Job Objects may also give you more control over the child processes, as you can use it to impose limits on the child processes, or to ensure that all child processes exit when the parent (controller) process exits.
<|end|>


