I am interested in training different algorithms on a data set and observing performance metrics.
Currently, my approach is to train different algorithms on train data, and then evaluate performance on a test set. Then, I use a `GridSearch` with cross-validation to find the optimal hyper-parameters for the best-performing model and test again using those.
I am a bit confused about cross-validation - Would this be the right approach or is there a way I could/should do cross-validation for all models?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Your approach is correct in general, but it's always good to apply cross-validation on your models before choosing the best one. This will give you a better understanding of how well your model will perform on unseen data.

Splitting data into training and testing set and then training algorithms on train data and evaluating performance on test set is a good practice.

The confusion arises often in how, when and why to use cross-validation. The key point here is that cross-validation is used during the model selection process. That is, when you have several different models (for example, Decision Trees, Random Forest, SVM, etc.) or several configurations of a single model (which is the case of hyperparameters in GridSearch) and you want to choose the "best" one. "Best" here typically means the model or configuration that has the best compromise between bias and variance, also known colloquially as the model that "generalizes" the best.

So, the complete process would be (approximately):

1. Split your data into a "training+validation" set and a "test" set. Never touch your test set unless you're ready to test your final model.

2. Choose a type of model/machine learning algorithm. For example, Random Forest.

3. Use k-fold cross-validation on your "training+validation" set to find the best hyperparameters. This is where GridSearch comes into play.

4. Once you've got the best hyperparameters, create a new model with these parameters and train it on the entire "training+validation" set.

5. Repeat steps 2-4 for the other types of models you're considering.

6. Once you've chosen your best model and trained it, evaluate it on the "test" set to estimate how well your model will likely perform on unseen data.

This way, you are making use of all your data effectively and also ensuring that your model is not overly optimistic. Note that this is a typical process, but you always need to be flexible and critical, and adapt the process to your particular needs and constraints.
<|end|>


