<|user|>
Elucidate the methodology involved in executing a value interchange between two distinct variables in the C++ programming language, specifically negating the use of an additional intermediary variable.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In C++, we can swap the values of two distinct variables without using an additional intermediary variable by using mathematical operations (addition and subtraction or multiplication and division) or bitwise XOR operation.

Addition and Subtraction:

```cpp
int a = 5, b = 10;
    
// Value swap
a = a + b;   // now a is sum of a and b
b = a - b;   // subtract b (original b) from a (sum) to get original a
a = a - b;   // subtract new b (original a) from a (sum) to get original b
```

Multiplication and Division:

```cpp
int a = 5, b = 10;
    
// Value swap
a = a * b;   // now a is a product of a and b
b = a / b;   // divide a (product) by b (original b) to get original a
a = a / b;   // divide a (product) by new b (original a) to get original b
```

Bitwise XOR:

```cpp
int a = 5, b = 10;
    
// Value swap
a = a ^ b;   // now a is resultant of a XOR b
b = a ^ b;   // XOR a (a XOR b) with b to get original a
a = a ^ b;   // XOR a (a XOR b) with new b (original a) to get original b
```
Remember that you need to be careful when using these methods. If you use addition and subtraction and the sum of the two numbers exceeds the maximum limit of the integer, it will cause an overflow error. If using multiplication and division and the product of the two numbers exceeds the maximum limit of the integer, it will also cause an overflow error. Division by zero also need to be taken care of. Similarly, while using Bitwise XOR, if a and b are pointing to the same location, then `a^b` will result into 0 and variable a will get 0 assigned.
<|end|>


