Create a C# function that not only finds the index of the first element of an array that is greater than a given value, but also captures the sum of the elements that come before it. When calling the function, provide parameter flexibility so it can handle an array of doubles, integers, or floats. Consider elements in this array that are divisible by a specific number and exclude them from your calculations.

For instance, if your array includes elements: {1.1, 2.00, 1.3, 9.7, 12.4} and the provided value is 5.0, your function should return the index of the first element greater than 5.0 (which is 9.7 with index 3) and the sum of the elements before it excluding any element divisible by a specific provided number (suppose the divisible number is 2).
<|end|>
<|asst|>
I assume the provided divisible number in your example is 2.0. Here's the C# function to achieve this using generics and tuples.

```Csharp
using System;
using System.Linq;

public static (int index, double sum) FindAndSum<T>(T[] arr, T val, T divisor) where T: IConvertible
{
    int firstOccurrence = -1;
    double sum = 0.0;
    double value = Convert.ToDouble(val);
    double divisorValue = Convert.ToDouble(divisor);
    
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.Length; i++)
    {
        double current = Convert.ToDouble(arr[i]);

        if (current % divisorValue != 0) // current value not divisible by divisor value
        {
            if (current > value && firstOccurrence == -1)
            {
                firstOccurrence = i;
            }
            if (firstOccurrence == -1 || i < firstOccurrence)
            {
                sum += current;
            }
        }
    }

    return (firstOccurrence, sum);
}
```

Here's how you would use it with your example data:

```Csharp
double[] arr = { 1.1, 2.00, 1.3, 9.7, 12.4 };
double val = 5.0;
double divisor = 2.0;

var result = FindAndSum(arr, val, divisor);

Console.WriteLine($"Index: {result.index}, Sum: {result.sum}");
```

This will output:

```
Index: 3, Sum: 2.4
```

The tuple `(3, 2.4)` is returned representing the index of the first element greater than 5.0 and the sum of all values before this index that are not divisible by 2.0. Note that the number 2.00 isn't included in the sum because it's divisible by 2.0. 

Please understand that due to the nature of floating point calculations, there may be small rounding errors in certain situations.
<|end|>


