Write a C program to read a list of names store them in an array and list them out.
Names: John, Mike, Peter
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a simple C program that meets your requirements. The names are hard-coded into the program and the program prints out each name.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

#define SIZE 3

int main() {
    // array to store names
    char *names[SIZE] = {"John", "Mike", "Peter"};
    
    // loop to print all names
    for(int i = 0; i < SIZE; i++) {
        printf("%s\n", names[i]);
    }
    
    return 0;
}
```

This code creates an array of 3 pointers to strings (names). The names "John", "Mike", and "Peter" are stored in the array. Then the program loops through each element in the array and prints the name.

If you want to input the names through the console, we need to allocate the memory necessary to store these names. Here is a program that reads the names :

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

#define SIZE 3
#define MAX_NAME_LENGTH 50

int main()
{
    // array to store names
    char *names[SIZE];

    // allocate memory and read names
    printf("Enter %d names:\n", SIZE);
    for (int i = 0; i < SIZE; i++)
    {
        names[i] = malloc(MAX_NAME_LENGTH * sizeof(char));
        scanf("%s", names[i]);
    }

    // print names
    printf("Here are the names you entered:\n");
    for (int i = 0; i < SIZE; i++)
    {
        printf("%s\n", names[i]);
    }

    // free allocated memory
    for (int i = 0; i < SIZE; i++)
    {
        free(names[i]);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This program will ask the user to enter 3 names. For each name, it will allocate enough space to store a string of 50 characters, then read the string from the user input. After reading all names, it will print them and finally, the program will free the memory that was allocated to store the names.
