<|user|>
I'm trying to write a function which works uses constant generics to select the size of an array. Using constant generics in the main function works like so.

fn main() {
    const N: usize = 100;
    // let primes = primes_up_to(10_000);
    let list = [1; N];
    print!("{:?}", list)
}


therefore to use a constant generic in a function I would expect:

fn const_generic_test(const LEN: usize) {
    let test_arr = [1, LEN];
    println!("{:?}", test_arr)
}


However, this doesn't work. How can I take a constant generic as a function argument?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can use constant generics as function parameters, but you are using it in the wrong way. You're trying to use the constant as the initial value for an array, but size of the array in Rust should be known at compile time, not runtime. The compiler won't be able to figure out the size of your array like that. 

Instead what you actually want is a constant generic argument for the function which will then be used to specify the size of the array. 

Here's an example of how to do it:

```rust
#![feature(const_generics)]
#![feature(const_evaluatable_checked)]

fn const_generic_test<const LEN: usize>() {
    let test_arr: [usize; LEN] = [1; LEN];
    println!("{:?}", test_arr)
}

fn main() {
    const_generic_test::<10>();
    const_generic_test::<20>();
}
```

In this function, `LEN` is a type constant which define the length of the array. Please note that for this you need two unstable features: `const_generics` and `const_evaluatable_checked`. You can activate these features by adding the `#![feature(const_generics)]` and `#![feature(const_evaluatable_checked)]` at the top of your file.

Note that using these features will make your program require a nightly version of Rust to compile.

Also note that the syntax for specifying the constant when calling the function is `<Constant>`, for example `const_generic_test::<10>()`.

Lastly, remember that the size of an array cannot be a run-time value, it must be known at compile time in Rust, hence this approach.
<|end|>


