I'm thinking this might be a quick and easy way to lower the form spam on our site just a little bit. The idea being that (I have read) spammers aren't running with javascript enabled. (Or at least they are accessing your website without running javascript. I.e., they aren't browsing up to it in IE or FF.

I can use .asp or .aspx.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, disabling form submissions from browsers where JavaScript is not enabled can help reduce form spam, as many spambot scripts do not execute JavaScript.

Here's a simple way to do it using ASP.NET (or ASPX). The idea is to include a hidden field in the form that is set by JavaScript when the form is loaded. On the server-side, you can then check whether this field is set and ignore the form submission if it isn't. 

In your HTML:

```HTML
<form id="yourForm" runat="server" onsubmit="return Validate();">
   <!-- Your form fields here -->
   <input type="hidden" id="isJsEnabled" runat="server" />
   <input type="submit" value="Submit" />
</form>
```

In your JavaScript:

```JavaScript
window.onload = function() {
   document.getElementById('isJsEnabled').value = 'true';
}
```

And then, in your ASP.NET code-behind:

```C#
protected void Page_Load(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
   if (!IsPostBack) return;

   if (isJsEnabled.Value != "true")
   {
      // Ignore the form submission
      return;
   }

   // Process the form submission
}
```

With this implementation, if JavaScript is not enabled, the hidden field `isJsEnabled` will not be set to "true" and the server-side code will ignore the form submission. Remember though, this won't stop all form spamming and there are bots that can execute JavaScript. 

For more thorough protection, consider implementing CAPTCHA, token validation, honeypot fields, or server-side form validation. Utilizing rate limiting on form submissions can also be effective. Please also note, this methodology falls in the category of Progressive Enhancement, i.e. its effectiveness is determined by the sophistication of the spambot.
<|end|>


