Taking into account the instruction embedded within the commentary, modify the accompanying Cascading Style Sheets (CSS) language such that the text affiliated with the identifier 'heading_text' transitions to a shade of green when a web element adorned with the class label 'selected' gets activated.
.selected {
	border: 1px solid green;
}
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In order to modify the text to change color when the 'selected' class gets activated, we can leverage the use of CSS pseudo-classes. In this case, we want to look for the ':active' pseudo-class. 

We attach this to '.selected' and then modify the CSS for 'heading_text'. Here's how you could do it:

```css
.selected:active #heading_text {
	color: green;
}
```
The '#heading_text' is an id selector in CSS which is used to select the element with the specified id. In this case, it's 'heading_text'.

The ':active' is a pseudo-class used to select and style the active link - a link the user is currently clicking on. In this case, it's attached to the 'selected' class to listen for when an element with this class gets activated.

The '.selected:active #heading_text' will select the element with the id = 'heading_text' nested within an element that has class = 'selected' when it has been activated.

To make a smooth transition instead of abrupt color change, you can add the transition property to #heading_text:

```css
#heading_text {
    transition: color 0.5s ease-in-out;
}

.selected:active #heading_text {
    color: green;
}
```
This will make the color of the 'heading_text' gradually change over half a second when 'selected' class gets activated.

Please note, this only works if 'heading_text' is a child of 'selected'. If this is not the case, JavaScript or jQuery will be required to achieve this.
<|end|>


