<|asst|>
Testing a Redux-based React app can be confusing, especially when using React hooks. Fortunately, your code base is organized well into smaller components, so it should be easier to test each part of your application.

I have written an example test to guide you. We'll use `@testing-library/react` and `@testing-library/user-event`.

First, install the necessary libraries:

If you're using Yarn:

```
yarn add @testing-library/react @testing-library/user-event
```

If you're using npm:

```
npm install --save-dev @testing-library/react @testing-library/user-event
```

Now, let's write a test for one of your components. Let's test the `TodoInput.js` file. Create a new `TodoInput.test.js` file in the same directory.

```jsx
import { render, fireEvent, screen } from '@testing-library/react';
import '@testing-library/jest-dom/extend-expect';
import TodoInput from './TodoInput';
import { TodoProvider } from '../context/TodoContext';

test('Should add a todo when the add button is pressed', () => {
  const fakeTodo = 'Buy groceries';

  // Render TodoInput inside the context provider
  render(
    <TodoProvider>
      <TodoInput />
    </TodoProvider>,
  );

  // Get the text input and the add button elements
  const input = screen.getByPlaceholderText(/add a todo/i);
  const button = screen.getByText(/add/i);

  // Simulate user typing a todo
  fireEvent.change(input, { target: { value: fakeTodo } });

  // Simulate user clicking the add button
  fireEvent.click(button);

  // Assert that the input value has been cleared just after submission
  expect(input.value).toBe('');
});
```

This test simulates a user typing into the TodoInput component and then clicking the add button.

Similarly, you can create tests for deleting todos and marking todos as completed.

To run your tests, you can use the `jest` command or adjust the `test` script in your `package.json` to "jest". Then, run the command `yarn test` or `npm run test`.

These tests are simple and directly check the DOM changes based on user's actions. For more advanced tests, you may want to mock your context and test how components interact with it.

Side Note: Your codebase is well-organized, but it's recommended to separate components from context logic (i.e., not using `useContext` within components). This provides a clear separation of concerns and makes testing easier.

I hope this provides you with a clear understanding of how to test your components and helps you move forward!
