I want to create a `margin` class in my custom CSS like `margin-left-x` and wish to have the left margin equal to that `x` value. Is this possible? More precisely I wish to do it as below:

.margin-left-x{
   margin-left: x%;
}


Brute force approach is to create separate CSS classes for each margin value but it becomes tedious and difficult to maintain, like:

.margin-left-2{
   margin-left: 2%;
}


.margin-left-5{
   margin-left: 5%;
}


.margin-left-20{
   margin-left: 20%;
}


Is there anyway I can make this dynamic with just a single CSS class?
In my HTML, I wish to use it in the `class` attribute directly, like below:

<div class="col-sm-4 col-md-4 margin-left-20">
 <p> Hello </p>
</div>
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can't achieve this directly through native CSS as it doesn't support dynamic class names like this. However, you can accomplish this through CSS pre-processor like Sass or Less or using JavaScript.

Here's the Sass example:

```sass
@for $i from 1 through 100 {
  .margin-left-#{$i} {
    margin-left: #{$i}%;
  }
}
```

The code above will generate CSS classes from `.margin-left-1` to `.margin-left-100` with the corresponding percentage for the left margin.

And here's how you can achieve this using JavaScript (or jQuery) :

```javascript
// Assuming you use jQuery
var el = $('.col-sm-4');
var classList = el.attr('class').split(/\s+/);
$.each(classList, function(index, item) {
   // Checking if a CSS class is starting with margin-left-
   if (item.indexOf('margin-left-') === 0) {
       // Removing margin-left- from the class name to get the value
       var value = item.replace('margin-left-', '');   
       $(el).css('margin-left', value+'%');
   }
});
```

But remember to not rely on JavaScript for everything CSS should normally do. There's a performance cost to run the script above for every element you want to control. Preprocessors like Sass, Less or creating specific CSS classes would be the optimal way. Use JavaScript way only if it's necessary and unavoidable.
<|end|>


