Here is how you could rewrite the jQuery code to pure JavaScript:

```javascript
var listItems = document.querySelectorAll("#list li");

listItems.forEach(function(listItem) {
  //some code 
});
```

The `document.querySelectorAll()` method returns all elements in the document that match a specified CSS selectors, which in this case, are the list items inside lists with the id "list". Therefore, it is equivalent to the jQuery `$("#list li")`.

The `forEach()` method executes a provided function once for each array element. It's used on arrays, and since `document.querySelectorAll()` returns a NodeList (which is array-like), it operates similarly to the jQuery `.each()`. 

Note: `NodeList.forEach()` is not supported in Internet Explorer. If you need to support that browser, you can convert the NodeList to an array before calling `forEach()`:

```javascript
var listItems = Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll("#list li"));

listItems.forEach(function(listItem) {
  //some code 
});
```
<|end|>


