I've seen this all over the place: 

> Download here!  SHA1 =
  8e1ed2ce9e7e473d38a9dc7824a384a9ac34d7d0

What does it mean? How does a hash come into play as far as downloads and... What use can I make of it? Is this a legacy item where you used to have to verify some checksum after you downloaded the whole file?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
That SHA1 code is essentially a digital fingerprint of the file you're downloading. It's derived from the contents of the file using the SHA-1 cryptographic hash function.

Checksums like these are used to verify the integrity of the file. When you download the file, you can calculate its SHA-1 hash on your own and compare it to the hash you see on the website. If the hashes match, you can be sure that the file wasn't corrupted or tampered with during transmission – the file on your computer is identical to the file on the server.

Checksum verification is still used today, especially in contexts where it's critical to ensure that the file hasn't been tampered with, such as when downloading system updates or software from the Internet. Even though SHA-1 is no longer considered secure against well-funded attackers, it's still perfectly fine for checking file integrity in most cases. Though, stronger hash functions like SHA-256 or SHA-3 are now preferred.

To use this, you'll need a tool that can compute SHA-1 hashes. Many operating systems come with one pre-installed. On macOS or Linux, you can simply open a terminal and type `shasum downloaded-file-name`, and on Windows, you can use `CertUtil -hashfile downloaded-file-name SHA1` in the Command Prompt. There are also third-party tools available for all platforms. 

If you're a casual user, you might not need to check the hash every time you download a file, especially if you're using a trusted network and download source. But it is good to know about and understand, as it's a useful tool to ensure files have been downloaded correctly and have not been tampered with.
<|end|>


