Create a validation program in JavaScript to check if an entered email address is valid.
The entered email address: example@example.com
<|end|>
<|asst|>
We use regular expressions (regex) to validate email addresses in JavaScript. The regex will test the format of the email if it is correct or not.

Here's a simple JavaScript function to validate an email address:

```javascript
function validateEmail(email) {
  var re = /^(([^<>()\[\]\\.,;:\s@"]+(\.[^<>()\[\]\\.,;:\s@"]+)*)|(".+"))@((\[[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\])|(([a-zA-Z\-0-9]+\.)+[a-zA-Z]{2,}))$/;
  return re.test(email.toLowerCase());
}

console.log(validateEmail('example@example.com'));  // It will return true because the email is valid
console.log(validateEmail('example@.com'));  // It will return false because the email is invalid
```

In the `validateEmail` function, we use a regular expression to check if the email is in a valid format. The regex will match the format of a standard email: some characters, followed by an @, followed by some characters, a dot, and finally some more characters. 

This function checks the email argument by running the `test` method with the given regex `re`. The method `test` returns either `true` (if the email matches the regex pattern and is therefore valid) or `false` (if it doesn't).

The function is case-insensitive, meaning it treats uppercase and lowercase letters as the same. This is achieved by using the `toLowerCase()` method which converts the email argument to all lowercase letters.
