<|end|>
<|asst|>
Your original code seems correct in logic and syntax which does indeed solve the given problem i.e., it will count the upper case vowels at even indices. However, it has some limitations. Because of this reason we should avoid checking the character at each index and running the modulo function to find whether it's even or not. 

Instead, we can leverage Python's slicing syntax to get all the characters at even indices (i.e., every other character starting from index 0), then iterate over these characters alone, checking if they are upper-case vowels.

Here is an improved version of the code:

```python
def count_upper_vowels(s):
    """
    This function counts the number of capital letters which are vowels on even indices in the string.
    Example:
    count_upper_vowels('aBCdEf') should return 1
    count_upper_vowels('abcdefg') should return 0
    count_upper_vowels('dBBE') should return 1
    """
    upper_vowels = 'AEIOU'
    even_letters = s[::2]
    counter = sum(1 for letter in even_letters if letter in upper_vowels)
    return counter
```

Please note that this new version of the function is not only conceptually easier to understand, it is also more efficient because it only iterates through half of the characters in the string (those at even indices) and does not need to calculate the modulo function at each iteration.
