The correct Python code is as follows:

```python
def queensAttacktheKing(queens, king):
    res = [None] * 8  # 8 directions
    for i, j in queens:
        x, y = king[0]-i, king[1]-j
        # Use the direction to index the position
        if x == 0:  # same row
            k = 3 if y < 0 else 7  # left or right
        elif y == 0:  # same column
            k = 1 if x < 0 else 5  # up or down
        elif abs(x) == abs(y):  # diagonal
            if x > 0 and y < 0: k = 0  # up-left
            elif x < 0 and y < 0: k = 2  # down-left
            elif x < 0 and y > 0: k = 4  # down-right
            else: k = 6  # up-right

        if res[k] is None or max(abs(x), abs(y)) < max(abs(king[0]-res[k][0]), abs(king[1]-res[k][1])):
            res[k] = [i, j]
    return [item for item in res if item is not None]
```
It seems like your original code attempts to solve a game problem where queens are attacking a king on a chessboard. The code organizes the eight possible directions into an eight element list (`res`) which can be populated with the positions of any queens that can attack the king. The code iterates through the list of queens, determining each queen's relative position to the king and replacing the stored position in `res` if the new queen is closer. The final line removes any None values from `res`, leaving only the positions of the queens that can attack the king. I recommend adding some comments to your code to make it more understandable and easier to debug.
