When reading about AUC/ROC there is often this notion that comes up that a perfect score is 1.0. I have two problems with that:

- There can be models that are 100% accurate but do not have an AUC of 1.0.
E.g.
Predictions = [0.4,0.35,0.49,0.96,0.98]
Ground truth = [0,0,0,1,1]. Thus the perfect threshold would be >0.49, however the AUC would be way less than 1.0. Am I wrong in this assumption?

- An AUC of 0.0 would be perfectly good, wouldn't it? It's just a perfect imperfect model, which would result in a very good model if I am not mistaken?


So what does the AUC really say about a model? My hypothesis: At best a good AUC tells you how decisive a model is.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
When measuring the performance of classification models, AUC (Area Under the Curve) of ROC (Receiver Operating Characteristic) curve is often used. The higher the AUC, typically the better the model is. But it isn’t as simple or straightforward as it may seem.

- The reason why a 100% accurate model doesn't have an AUC of 1.0 in your case is because AUC depends not just on getting the right answer, but also assigning higher probabilities to instances that are indeed more likely. So even if a model classifies all instances correctly, if it assigns less confident probabilities to some of them, this is a sign of less robustness and we can see it in a lower AUC score.

- An AUC of 0.0 indicates that the model is perfectly wrong – it classifies all positive instances as negative, and vice versa. You could trivially invert the predictions to achieve an AUC of 1.0, but the original model still exhibited poor discrimination by itself.

- The AUC really measures the quality as well as robustness of a model. The AUC essentially shows two things: (1) the ability of a model to rank positive instances higher than negative ones, and (2) the model's performance across various classification thresholds. So if a model has a high AUC, it means the model is not only getting a large number of predictions right, but also "confident" about these predictions.

In summary, AUC is a good way to measure the inherent classification capability of a model across all possible classification thresholds. This doesn’t mean models with a lower AUC cannot be good with proper calibration of the classification threshold, it just means a higher AUC model has an inherently stronger signal about the classes in the data.
<|end|>


