Could you expound on the procedural steps to utilize Google's Cloud Vision API, specifically for the complex endeavor of recognizing and differentiating distinct objects encapsulated within the realm of ultra-high-definition photographic visuals?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Google's Cloud Vision API harnesses the power of machine learning to analyze images and understand their content. It enables the categorization of diverse visual content into thousands of categories (like "boat", "lion"), detects individual objects and faces within images, finds and reads printed words contained within images, and more.

Here are the steps on how to use it to recognize and differentiate distinct objects in high resolution images:

1. Set up Google Cloud Project: Go to Google Cloud Console and create a new project. Give it an appropriate name and wait for Google Cloud to set it up. 

2. Enable Cloud Vision API: Once the project is set up, go to the "APIs & Services" dashboard, click on "+ ENABLE APIS AND SERVICES". From the list of available APIs select "Cloud Vision API" and enable it.

3. Create Credentials: In order to use the API, you need to create credentials. Under the "APIs & Services" dashboard, select "Credentials". Click "+ CREATE CREDENTIALS" and select "API key". A key will be generated which you will use later. 

4. Install and Import Required Libraries: Make sure you have the Google Cloud client library installed. You also need to set up the Google Application Credentials with the key file received when you created the credentials. You can install it by using pip (`pip install --upgrade google-cloud-vision`). Then, import it into your Python script (`from google.cloud import vision`).

5. Initialization: Instantiate a `vision.ImageAnnotatorClient` object that you will use to call the Vision API. 

6. Load Your Image: For high-resolution images, it's important to downsample the image to avoid exceeding the service's limit of 20MB per image. Once the image has been processed and downsized to an acceptable size, you can send it off to the Cloud Vision API.

7. Execute a request: With the image loaded, you can now use the Vision API's `detect()` or `annotate_image()` methods to analyze the image.

8. Handle Response: For each recognized object, the API will return a name, a score that represents the confidence of the recognition and the bounding polygon for the object. Process this information as needed.

9. Iterate for each object: You can now loop the request for as many objects as you want to differentiate.

The Google Cloud Vision API is powerful and versatile, but bear in mind that analyzing high-resolution images will consume more processing power and incur higher costs than analyzing lower-resolution images.

Note also that the API is machine-learning based and depends heavily on training data. Therefore, its recognition quality may vary depending on the complexity, objects, variety of angles or clarity in the images.
<|end|>


