Write a Java program that converts a multidimensional array of numbers to a list of lists of numbers. The program should also handle the case where the multidimensional array can have irregular shapes (i.e., not all sub-arrays have the same length).
int[][] arr = {{1,2,3}, {4,5}, {6,7,8,9}};
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a Java program that converts irregular multidimensional arrays to a list of lists:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] arr = {{1,2,3}, {4,5}, {6,7,8,9}};

        List<List<Integer>> list = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i=0; i<arr.length; i++) {
            List<Integer> subList = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int j=0; j<arr[i].length; j++) {
                subList.add(arr[i][j]);
            }
            list.add(subList);
        }

        System.out.println(list);  
    }
}
```

This program creates an ArrayList of ArrayLists, then iterates over the array to create ArrayLists from its sub-arrays and add them to the main ArrayList. The ArrayLists can handle varying lengths of sub-arrays, so it will effectively convert an array of irregular shape to a list of lists of its elements.

When you run this program, it will print:

```
[[1, 2, 3], [4, 5], [6, 7, 8, 9]]
``` 

which represents the array as a list of lists of numbers.
<|end|>


