Using Python's Turtle Graphics library, please engineer a geometric construction that manifests as a parallelogram, particularly taking the form of a rectangle. Keep in mind the need to solicit programming logic that reflects a fundamental comprehension of geometric principles intrinsic to the design of a parallelogram and particularly its special case, a rectangle.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is the python program using turtle library that draws a rectangle, which is a special case of parallelogram. A rectangle is a parallelogram with all four angles of 90 degrees.

```python
import turtle

# create turtle object
t = turtle.Turtle()

# set first direction for the turtle
t.right(90)

# draw the rectangle
for i in range(2):
    t.forward(100)
    t.right(90)
    t.forward(200)
    t.right(90)

# hide the turtle
t.hideturtle()

# keep the window open
turtle.done()
```

In above program, geometry of rectangle is applied. Rectangle has 4 sides and all angles are right angles (90 degree). Loop is run twice as after moving right and then forward, it completes two sides of the rectangle, so repeating it twice forms a rectangle.

You can modify length of sides in 'forward' as per requirement, just keep opposite sides as equal length to form a rectangle.

Each 'right(90)' turns the turtle 90 degrees to the right and 'forward(100)' or 'forward(200)' moves the turtle in the direction it's facing. Opposite sides of a rectangle are equal, so 'forward(100)' and 'forward(200)' are each run twice making the opposite sides of the rectangle equal.
<|end|>


