Develop an assembly language program to generate prime numbers up until a given number, n. Enhance it by making the program find the closest prime number larger than n, without using any built-in functions.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This question is asking for an assembly language (ASM) program that generates prime numbers until a given number, and also able to find the closest prime number larger than n. Here is a simple assembly code in MIPS assembly language.


```assembly
.data
n : .space 4
next_prime : .space 4        # to store the next prime number

.text
.globl main
main :
    la $a0, n
    li $a1, 4
    li $v0, 5                 # system call code for reading an integer
    syscall                   # generating prime numbers up to a given number n

    move $t0, $v0
    move $t1, $0              # to store the largest prime number so far
    move $t2, $0
generate_primes:
    beq $t2, $t0, find_next_prime
    addi $t1, $t1, 1
    li $t2, 0
    move $t3, $t1
check_divisor:
    beq $t3, $t2, generate_primes
    addi $t2, $t2, 1
    remu $t4, $t1, $t2
    bnez $t4, check_divisor   # Not a prime if remainder isn't zero
    j generate_primes         # a prime number found

find_next_prime:
    addi $t1, $t1, 1
    li $t2, 0
move_divisor:
    beq $t3, $t2, end
    addi $t2, $t2, 1
    remu $t4, $t1, $t2
    bnez $t4, move_divisor
    j find_next_prime

end: 
    la $a0, next_prime
    move $a1, $t1
    li $v0, 1                 # system call code for printing an integer
    syscall                   # print the next prime number

    li $v0, 10                # system call code for exiting the program
    syscall

```                  
This is a basic code and does not consider optimization and efficiency of the code. Also, it does not include error checking if the input is invalid or not. The closest prime number larger than n is also printed, as the question asks without using any pre-declared functions. Syntax and function may vary depending on different Assembly language (like x86, 8086, MIPS, ARM, etc.).
<|end|>


