No, `temp2` and `temp1` will not point to the same memory location after execution of the two lines.

In the first line, `temp2` is made to point to the same location as `temp1` is pointing, which is the address of `x`.

In the second line, `temp1` is assigning to point to the next node that it's current node (which is `x`) is pointing to (assuming nxt is potentially a pointer to the next node or next struct of type FunkyStruct in a list of such structs).

So now, `temp2` will be pointing to `x`, but `temp1` will be pointing to wherever `x.nxt` was pointing to. Hence, unless `x.nxt` was self-referential (meaning x.nxt was itself pointing to `x`), `temp2` and `temp1` will not be pointing to the same memory location.
<|end|>


