<|asst|>
Go (Golang) doesn't have a built-in library for data processing like Python's Dask or Pandas. However, you can import `encoding/csv` and `strconv` for CSV file reading and parsing. A simple equivalent code to perform grouping and mean calculation in Go may look as below:

Please install "gonum.org/v1/gonum/floats" first for float calculations.

```go
package main

import (
	"encoding/csv"
	"fmt"
	"log"
	"os"
	"strconv"
	"gonum.org/v1/gonum/floats"
)

func main() {
	f, err := os.Open("your_large_dataset.csv")
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}
	defer f.Close()

	reader := csv.NewReader(f)
	rawData, err := reader.ReadAll()
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}

	data := make(map[string][]float64)
	// Assuming the column to group by is the first one
	// and the values are in the second column
	for _, row := range rawData[1:] { // Exclude headers
		groupKey := row[0]
		value, err := strconv.ParseFloat(row[1], 64)
		if err != nil {
			log.Fatal(err)
		}

		if _, ok := data[groupKey]; ok {
			data[groupKey] = append(data[groupKey], value)
		} else {
			data[groupKey] = []float64{value}
		}
	}

	grouped := make(map[string]float64)
	for key, values := range data {
		grouped[key] = floats.Sum(values) / float64(len(values))
	}

	
	outputFile, err := os.Create("output.csv");
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}
	writer := csv.NewWriter(outputFile)
	// Write headers
	writer.Write([]string{"Group", "Mean"})
	// Write data
	for group, mean := range grouped {
	    writer.Write([]string{group, fmt.Sprintf("%f", mean)})
	}
	writer.Flush()

}
```

This Go example groups by the first column and compute mean values of the second column. You need to adjust this code according to your specific context. Also, please note that this code will load all CSV data into memory as like Dask, Go doesn't have a built-in lazy evaluation dataframe library. If you handle too large data which memory cannot contain, consider using a database or a tool designed for big data processing.
<|end|>


