<|asst|>
Below is a Java solution. The program uses StringBuilder's reverse function to reverse the Unicode characters. To handle edge cases, the program will return an empty string when the input is null or empty.

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(reverseTranspose(null)); // prints ""
        System.out.println(reverseTranspose("")); // prints ""
        System.out.println(reverseTranspose("Hello World!")); // prints "!dlroW olleH"
        System.out.println(reverseTranspose("😊 Hello, World! 🌎")); // prints "🌎 !dlroW ,olleH 😊"
        System.out.println(reverseTranspose("Gruß vom Krüger")); // prints "regürK mov ßurG"
    }

    /**
     * This method reverses a given string even if it includes unicode characters, emojis, etc.
     * 
     * @param str The string to be reversed.
     * @return The reversed string. If the input is null or empty, returns an empty string.
     */
    public static String reverseTranspose(String str) {
        if (str == null || str.isEmpty()) {
            return "";
        }

        // StringBuilder's reverse function works perfectly fine with emojis and unicodes.
        return new StringBuilder(str).reverse().toString();
    }
}
```

This program handles Unicode characters correctly because Java's String and StringBuilder are Unicode compatible. Unicode is a computing industry standard for the consistent encoding, representation, and handling of text expressed in most of the world's writing systems.
<|end|>


