What's the correct way to create bidirectional 1to1 mapping using Embeddable annotation? This one throws error
"EmpId has no persistent id property: Emp.id"

@Entity
public class Per implements Serializable {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    @Column(name = "per_id")
    private Long id;
 
    @OneToOne
    @JoinColumn(name = "per_id", referencedColumnName = "emp_id")
    private Emp emp;
 
}
 
@Embeddable
public class EmpId implements Serializable {
    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;
 
    @OneToOne(mappedBy = "emp")
    private Per per;
 
}
 
@Entity
public class Emp implements Serializable {
    @EmbeddedId
    private EmpId id;
}


I'd like to operate entities like

per.getEmp();
emp.getId().getPer();
<|end|>
<|asst|>
No, using the @Embeddable annotation like this is not correct. @Embeddable is used when a group of values is only related to the entities that refer to them and can be treated as a simple value – like a struct in C – rather than a link to another entity.

In your case, you have 2 entities (Per and Emp) having a bidirectional OneToOne association, so you should not use @Embeddable. 

You can do something like this:

```
@Entity
@Table(name="per")
public class Per implements Serializable {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue
    @Column(name = "id")
    private Long id; 

    @OneToOne(cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
    @JoinColumn(name = "emp_id")
    private Emp emp;

}

@Entity
@Table(name="emp")
public class Emp implements Serializable {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue
    @Column(name = "id")
    private Long id;

    @OneToOne(mappedBy = "emp")
    private Per per;

 }
```

Now, you can use the following commands as you provided:

```
per.getEmp();
emp.getPer();
```

It's important to remember to treat the bidirectional relationships properly, meaning it's imperative to synchronize both sides of the association because of how the ORM works. While it might seem that you only need to set one side and the ORM will do the rest of the work, that's not the case. The application itself should maintain the consistency of the domain model.
Here is the right way to synchronize both sides:

```
public class Per {
    // ...

    public void setEmp(Emp emp) {        
        if (emp == null) {
            if (this.emp != null) {
                this.emp.setPer(null);
            }
        } else {
            emp.setPer(this);
        }       
        this.emp = emp;
    }
}
```
<|end|>


