Write a Java program that generates a 2D array with 5 rows and 6 columns. Each entry in the array should be initialized with the product of its row and column numbers (considering 0-indexed). Additionally, you are given a piece of erroneous code to start with. 

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] array2D = new int[5][6];
        
        for(int i = 0; i <= 5; i++) {
            for(int j = 0; j <= 6; j++) {
                array2D[i][j] = i * j;
            }
        }

        for(int i = 0; i <= 5; i++) {
            for(int j = 0; j <= 6; j++) {
                System.out.print(array2D[i][j] + " ");
            }
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}
```

Identify the problem in the above code and rewrite it to resolve the issues. Be sure to pay attention to proper array initialization and bounds, and handle any possible exceptions. Additionally, your final solution should include code that displays the contents of your 2D array.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The given code is erroneous because the bounds used in the loop for filling and printing the array are incorrect. A 2D array declared as new int[5][6] has 5 rows and 6 columns. Therefore, the valid indices for rows are from 0 to 4 and those for columns are from 0 to 5. However, in the current code, the loop bounds are from 0 to 5 for rows and from 0 to 6 for columns. These are outside the actual array dimensions and will throw ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException. Here is a corrected version of the code:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] array2D = new int[5][6];
        
        for(int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            for(int j = 0; j < 6; j++) {
                array2D[i][j] = i * j;
            }
        }

        for(int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            for(int j = 0; j < 6; j++) {
                System.out.print(array2D[i][j] + " ");
            }
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}
```
In this version of the code, array bounds have been correctly set as 5 for rows and 6 for columns (i.e., the bounds are exclusive). Consequently, the possible ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException has been avoided.
<|end|>


