I know in certain circumstances, such as long running processes, it is important to lock ASP.NET cache in order to avoid subsequent requests by another user for that resource from executing the long process again instead of hitting the cache.

What is the best way in c# to implement cache locking in ASP.NET?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Locking cache in ASP.NET can indeed be a crucial part of managing your application’s performance. Here’s a simple implementation of locking in cache in ASP.NET using C#.

Use the `lock` statement in .NET to synchronize access to a single code block:

```csharp
public object GetFromCacheOrFetch(string cacheKey, Func<object> fetcher)
{
    object cachedItem = HttpRuntime.Cache[cacheKey];

    if (cachedItem == null)
    {
        var lockObj = new object();
        lockObj = HttpRuntime.Cache[cacheKey + "_lock"] ?? lockObj;
        
        lock (lockObj)
        {
            // Recheck if your data was populated into cache while waiting for the lock to release.
            cachedItem = HttpRuntime.Cache[cacheKey];
            if (cachedItem == null)
            {
                cachedItem = fetcher();
                HttpRuntime.Cache.Insert(cacheKey, cachedItem, null, Cache.NoAbsoluteExpiration, Cache.NoSlidingExpiration);
                
                // Store lock object in cache, which can be used next time.
                HttpRuntime.Cache.Insert(cacheKey + "_lock", lockObj);
            }
        }
    }

    return cachedItem;
}
```

This method takes a cacheKey string, which you’ll use as the unique identifier for your cache item, and a Func delegate, which will execute to fetch your data in the event that it’s not found in cache.

Note that directly locking on `HttpRuntime.Cache[cacheKey]` can cause issues since `HttpRuntime.Cache[cacheKey]` can become null after lock acquired and before entering the lock causing a runtime exception.

This method provides a Thread-Safe way to access and modify items in cache. If cache is empty, it fetches data using `fetcher`, then fills the cache. When a second thread accesses the method while cache is still empty but first thread already inside lock, the second thread waits at `lock(lockObj)`. As soon as the first thread releases lock, second thread enters lock and finds the cache is already filled, so it won't call fetcher again.

Answering the locking part, there are other ways like using `Mutex`, `Monitor`, `Semaphore` based on your specific needs. Also, when considering a distributed cache scenario, you would need a distributed lock strategy.

Please take care that lock should be used very carefully. A bad usage might lead to deadlocks in your web application causing performance issues.
<|end|>


