Create an advanced algorithmic process that locates the most extensive subsequence exhibiting a descending pattern within a numerical array.
input = [23, 21, 19, 22, 20, 18, 24, 25, 16, 15, 17, 14, 13, 12, 11]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a Python solution using dynamic programming:

```python
def longest_descending_subsequence(nums):
    n = len(nums)
    # List to store subsequence lengths
    lengths = [1] * n
    # List to store actual subsequences
    results = [[num] for num in nums]

    for i in range(1, n):
        for j in range(i):
            if nums[i] < nums[j]: # If a descending pattern is identified
                if lengths[j]+1 > lengths[i]: # If the new subsequence is longer
                    lengths[i] = lengths[j] + 1
                    # Update the results list
                    results[i] = results[j] + [nums[i]]

    # Locate the longest subsequence
    longest_length = max(lengths)
    index = lengths.index(longest_length)

    return results[index]

input = [23, 21, 19, 22, 20, 18, 24, 25, 16, 15, 17, 14, 13, 12, 11]
result = longest_descending_subsequence(input)
print(result) # prints [23, 21, 19, 18, 16, 15, 14, 13, 12, 11]
```
The function `longest_descending_subsequence(nums)` calculates the lengths of the longest ascending subsequences ending at each index of the input array, storing these lengths in `lengths`. The corresponding sequences are stored in `results`. The function then identifies the maximum length in `lengths` and returns the corresponding sequence from `results`.
<|end|>


