In JavaScript, I would like for you to construct a function that scrutinizes a provided string and establishes whether any possible permutation of the string's characters could potentially assemble a palindrome. Test this function with the string "carerac".
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, a basic logic to check if a permutation of the string can form a palindrome is that at most one character appears an odd number of times.

Here is a JavaScript function using a Hash table that serves your purpose:

```javascript
function canPermutePalindrome(s) {
    let countChars = {};

    // Count all characters in the string
    for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
        if (countChars[s[i]]) {
            countChars[s[i]]++;
        } else {
            countChars[s[i]] = 1;
        }
    }

    let oddCounts = 0;
    // Count characters that appear an odd number of times
    for (let key in countChars) {
        if (countChars[key] % 2 !== 0) {
            oddCounts++;
        }
    }

    // The string can form a palindrome if at most one character appears an odd number of times
    return oddCounts <= 1;
}

console.log(canPermutePalindrome('carerac'));
```

When you run this script, it should return `true` for the supplied 'carerac' input – as it could be rearranged to from a palindrome (e.g., 'racecar').
<|end|>


