Develop a method named "showAuthor" for the previously referenced "Novel" subclass, taking into account the necessary steps and procedures of function implementation within a subclass context.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To solve this problem, we first assume that there is a superclass named "Book" and a subclass named "Novel". 

Here's an example using Python:

```Python
# Superclass definition
class Book:
    def __init__(self, title, year_published):
        self.title = title
        self.year_published = year_published

# Subclass definition
class Novel(Book):
    def __init__(self, title, year_published, author, chapters):
        super().__init__(title, year_published)
        self.author = author
        self.chapters = chapters

    # Method to show author
    def showAuthor(self):
        return self.author
```

For Java, you can create the classes as follows,

```Java
// Base Class
public class Book {
    private String title;
    private int year_published;

    public Book(String title, int year_published) {
        this.title = title;
        this.year_published = year_published;
    }
    // getters and setters ...
}

// SubClass
public class Novel extends Book {
    private String author;
    private int chapters;

    public Novel(String title, int year_published, String author, int chapters) {
        super(title, year_published);
        this.author = author;
        this.chapters = chapters;
    }

    public String showAuthor() {
        return this.author;
    }

    // other getters and setters ...
}
```

This way, when you create an instance of the Novel class, you can apply the showAuthor() method to retrieve the author of that Novel. 

For example, in Python:
```Python
n = Novel("To Kill a Mockingbird", 1960, "Harper Lee", 31)
print(n.showAuthor())  # Output: Harper Lee
```
In Java:
```Java
Novel novel = new Novel("To Kill a Mockingbird", 1960, "Harper Lee", 31);
System.out.println(novel.showAuthor()); // Output: Harper Lee
```
