I'm developing a multi-threaded app for a Linux embedded platform.

At the moment I'm setting the stack size for each thread (via pthread_set_attr) to a fairly large default value. I would like to fine tune that value for each thread to something smaller to reduce my application's memory usage. I could go through the trial and error route of setting each thread's stack size to progressively smaller values until the program crashed, but the application uses ~15 threads each with completely different functionality/attributes so that approach would be extremely time consuming.

I would much rather prefer being able to directly measure each thread's stack usage. Is there some utility people can recommend to do this? (For example, I come from a vxWorks background and using the 'ti' command from the vxWorks shell directly gives stats on the stack usage as well as other useful info on the task status.)

Thanks
<|end|>
<|asst|>
One approach you could use is Valgrind's Massif tool which is a heap profiler. With it, you can monitor the heap and stack usage of your application. It will provide you with an output in regards to the peak stack usage so that you can fine-tune your stack allocation.

However, it is important to note that, since it's a dynamic analysis tool, it won't account for paths through the code that weren't taken during the analysis run but could be taken in a real-life situation, possibly leading to a larger stack size than measured.

Here's how you could measure stack usage with Massif:

```bash
valgrind --tool=massif --stacks=yes ./your_program
ms_print massif.out.*
```

The `--stacks=yes` option makes Massif profile stack sizes.

Another option is to use glibc's backtrace wrapper functions (backtrace and backtrace_symbols) to periodically monitor and log the depth of the stack on each thread. Variables containing the address of the bottom of the stack and the maximum depth of the stack would have to be updated on each function entry and exit. This method involves more legwork and possibly a performance hit, but would deliver exact data on each thread's stack usage.

Keep in mind, though, that the stack size doesn't just depend on how deep the function call stack is (which you can limit by avoiding recursive calls), but might also increase due to large local variables. In other words, even if your function call depth doesn't change, using a large local array could also cause a stack overflow.

Last but not least, please remember that stack overflows don't necessarily crash your program immediately, but could corrupt data and cause crashes or strange behavior later on, so don't rely on your program crashing to tune stack size.
<|end|>


