<|asst|>
The method you're describing is often referred to as iterative or recursive prediction. It can indeed be risky — small errors in early predictions can compound over time, affecting the quality of subsequent predictions.

References:

1. Zhang, G.P., Patuwo, B.E. & Hu, M.Y. A simulation study of artificial neural networks for nonlinear time-series forecasting. Comput Oper Res 28, 381–396 (2001). 
      - This study discusses a simulation of artificial neural networks for nonlinear time-series forecasting. It touches on the drawback you mentioned wherein inaccurate early predictions can cause deteriorations in later steps.

2. Hyndman, R.J., & Athanasopoulos, G. (2021) Forecasting: principles and practice
      - This is a comprehensive book on forecasting methods in which you can find a section related to iterated forecasting.

3. K. H. Pollock (1982) A Model for the Prediction of the Recursive Residuals from a Gaussian Time Series, Biometrika, Volume 69, Issue 1, Pages 239–245
      - This paper describes a model for recursively predicting residuals of a Gaussian time series.

4. Sola, J., Sevilla, J. (1997) Importance of Input Data Normalization for the Application of Neural Networks to Complex Industrial Problems, IEEE Transactions on Nuclear Science, Volume 44, Issue 3, Pages 1464 - 1468
      - This paper shows how recursive multi-step prediction accuracy can be improved by normalizing the input data and using a complex network architecture.

5. Luis Fernandez-Luque, Randi Karlsen, Jason Bonander (2011) Insights from analyzing and modelling health social media platforms. Journal of Medical Internet Research, Volume 13, Issue 3
      - This paper provides a perspective on modelling recursive predictions using social media data.

Moreover, to mitigate the risks of compounding error, you should consider using methods that are designed to predict multiple steps ahead (like "Direct Multi-step Forecasting") instead of iteratively predicting one step at a time.

1. J. Benesty, M. M. Sondhi and Y. A. Huang. "Springer Handbook of Speech Processing", Chapter 50, Adaptive Multi-Step Ahead Neural Network Predictions of Speech Signals (pages 705-729).
