Construct a rudimentary Vue component, incorporating a reactive state variable, and devise a function that enables its alteration, ensuring the component's reactivity is maintained throughout the process.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is a simple Vue.js component which contains a reactive state variable (data) name, and a method (changeName) to alter its value:

```javascript
<script>
    export default {
        name: "HelloWorld",
        data() {
            return {
                name: 'Vue.js'
            }
        },
        methods: {
            changeName(newName) {
                this.name = newName;
            }
        },
    }
</script>
```

Here, `data() { return { name: 'Vue.js' } }` is a reactive object that holds our component's local state. Any changes to properties of this object will be reactive, which means Vue will automatically update the DOM when these properties change.

The `changeName(newName) { this.name = newName; }` is a method changing the reactive `name` property. It will cause Vue to update any references to `name` in the template.

Whenever you call `this.changeName('New Name')`, Vue will automatically update every location where `name` is referenced/reused, ensuring that the component's reactivity is maintained at all times.

Here's a simple usage example in the template section:

```html
<template>
    <div>
        <h1>Hello, {{ name }}!</h1>
        <button @click="changeName('New Vue')">Change Name</button>
    </div>
</template>
```

In this template, {{ name }} will be replaced with the value of `name` from the component's data. When you click the button, the `changeName` function is called with 'New Vue' as its argument, and it changes the `name` to 'New Vue'. The displayed name in the `<h1>` tag also updates automatically because Vue reacts to this change.
<|end|>


