Consider a complex object structure in JavaScript where multiple levels of nested objects exist. Write a function in JavaScript that can interchange data between two given paths of this object. The function should take in three arguments - the main object, the path to the first variable, and the path to the second variable, and then interchange the data between these variables. Paths will be provided as a string. Coding algorithms to handle different forms of error, such as path not existing, would be an added advantage. 

For example, if we have an object like this: 
```
var obj = {
  personal: {
    name: 'John',
    details: {
      age: 30,
      address: '123 St'
    }
  },
  job: {
    title: 'Engineer',
    experience: 5
  }
}
```
and if we want to interchange `personal.details.age` with `job.experience`, your function should modify the object as follows:

```
{
  personal: {
    name: 'John',
    details: {
      age: 5,
      address: '123 St'
    }
  },
  job: {
    title: 'Engineer',
    experience: 30
  }
}
```

Note: Avoid changing any other information in the object except for the variables that are required to be interchanged. Complex error handling should be included for situations like when the given path does not exist within the object. Moreover, keep the code as efficient as possible.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To solve this problem, the function provided below will use the `get` function to get the initial values at the given paths and then the `set` function to set the values at the other paths. Exception handling is done by catching any potential errors that might emerge.

This is the function written in JavaScript:

```javascript
function getPath(obj, path) {
    let pathArr = path.split('.');
    for (let i = 0; i < pathArr.length; i++) {
        if (obj[pathArr[i]] !== undefined) {
            obj = obj[pathArr[i]];
        } else {
            throw new Error(`Path ${path} does not exist`);
        }
    }
    return obj
}
 
function setPath(obj, path, value) {
    let pathArr = path.split('.');
    for (let i = 0; i < pathArr.length - 1; i++) {
        if (obj[pathArr[i]] !== undefined) {
            obj = obj[pathArr[i]];
        } else {
            throw new Error(`Path ${path} does not exist`);
        }
    }
 
    if (obj[pathArr[pathArr.length-1]] !== undefined) {
        obj[pathArr[pathArr.length-1]] = value;
    } else {
        throw new Error(`Path ${path} does not exist`);
    }
}
 
function interchange(obj, path1, path2) {
    try {
        let temp = getPath(obj, path1);
        setPath(obj, path1, getPath(obj, path2));
        setPath(obj, path2, temp);
    } catch (e) {
        console.error(e.message);
    }
}
```

You can use `interchange(obj, 'personal.details.age', 'job.experience');` to update the `age` and `experience` values of the `obj` object. Error messages will be outputted to the console if the provided paths do not exist in the given object.
<|end|>


