I'm wanting extra security for a particular point in my web app. So I want to lock the database (SQL Server 2005). Any suggestions or is this even necessary with SQL Server?



The query is failing silently with no errors messages logged, and does not occur inside of a transaction.



I never was able to solve the problem, however what I wound up doing was switching to MySQL and using a transactional level query here. This was not the main or even a primary reason to switch. I had been having problems with SQL Server and it allowed me to have our CMS and various other tools all running on the same database. Previous we had a SQL Server and a MySQL database running to run our site. The port was a bit on the time consuming however in the long run I feel it will work much better for the site and the business.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Switching to MySQL is a tough decision and can indeed be time consuming in the short run, but if it provides the additional security and functionality you need, then it might be worth it.

However, coming back to your question about SQL Server - there's a locking hierarchy in SQL Server (2005 and later) that would cater to your need if you've set it up right. There are three lock modes - 'Shared', 'Exclusive' and 'Update'. The locks could be applied at different levels - row, page or table.

In order to ensure the performance and concurrency, SQL Server would generally put shared locks (which allow concurrent transactions) on read operations and exclusive locks (which disallow) on write operations. An update lock is a hybrid which starts off as shared lock and escalates to exclusive when necessary.

But if you want to override this, you can do it. Let's say you want to ensure that no other transaction can access some data that's being updated currently, you can use the following query: 

```
BEGIN TRAN
  SELECT * FROM tablename WITH (TABLOCK, HOLDLOCK) WHERE condition...
  -- Do your thing
COMMIT
```

The TABLOCK hint puts a lock on the table, and the HOLDLOCK hint ensures that the lock is held until the transaction is completed. Basically, this reduces concurrency but increases the isolation.

Please note though, this might not be ideal in high-load, high-concurrency systems, as it might lead to reduced performance. So use with discretion. 

And of course, always put your database interactions inside transaction. That's one sure way to maintain data integrity whatever be your database platform.
<|end|>


