I need to pass a regex substitution as a variable:

sub proc {
    my $pattern = shift;
    my $txt = "foo baz";

    $txt =~ $pattern;
}

my $pattern = 's/foo/bar/';
proc($pattern);


This, of course, doesn't work. I tried eval'ing the substitution:

eval("$txt =~ $pattern;");


but that didn't work either. What horribly obvious thing am I missing here?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To dynamically replace matched string in Perl, you have to use the `eval` function as follows:

```perl
sub proc {
    my $pattern = shift;
    my $txt = "foo baz";

    eval "\$txt =~ $pattern";

    print $txt;
}

my $pattern = 's/foo/bar/';
proc($pattern);
```

Please note: 

It is advisable to use the `eval` function with caution because it can execute any arbitrary Perl code, which can become a potential security risk.

In the above code, the `eval` function will replace 'foo' with 'bar' in the $txt string. Always make sure that any user data in the `eval` function is properly validated or sanitized to prevent code injection attacks. 
For instance, if $pattern was set to `'system("delete all files")'`, the eval would actually run that as Perl code, with potentially disastrous consequences.

Creating code (like a regex, in this case) out of potentially untrusted user input is known as 'eval injection', and is a known security issue similar to SQL injection. Be very sure you trust the source of the code you're eval'ing, or evaluate only a limited set of known-safe input.
<|end|>


