<|asst|>
Here is a simple jQuery plugin that does exactly that:

```js
(function ($) {
    $.fn.loadData = function(url, success) {
        var table = this;
        $.ajax({
            url: url,
            method: 'GET',
            dataType: 'json',
            success: function(data) {
                success(data);
                var keys, html = '<table>';

                data.forEach(function(item) {
                    keys = keys || Object.keys(item);
                    html += '<tr>';
                    keys.forEach(function(key) {
                        html += '<td>' + item[key] + '</td>';
                    });
                    html += '</tr>';
                });

                html += '</table>';
                table.html(html);
            },
            error: function(error) {
                console.log(error);
            }
        });

        return this;
    };
}(jQuery));
```

You can use this jQuery plugin like this:

```js
$('#myTable').loadData('/api/mydata', function(data) {
    console.log('Data loaded', data);
});
```

This code will make a GET request to your API endpoint (/api/mydata) and, once the promise is resolved successfully, will call the provided callback with the resultant data. After that, it will generate an HTML table from the JSON data and append that to the selector element.

Keep in mind that the JSON data should be an array of objects, with each object's properties represented as the cell of the table. Also, error handling is very simple here— in a real-world scenario you'd probably want to provide more useful feedback to users in case of an error.
