<|user|>
This is an interview question:
Imagine you have a double knock-out barrier option: the current spot is 100, the lower barrier is 80, and upper barrier is 120. The barrier is continuous, meaning that once the spot goes out side the range of 80-120 anytime before time to maturity, you got nothing. If the spot stays within the range 80-120 the whole time before the option matures, you will get paid a fixed amount of 10 as the final payoff.
The question is, if you need to price this option, which volatility model will give a higher option price? Local vol or Stoch vol? Why?
The interviewer said it's the Stoch vol gives a higher option price, but I didn't get the reason.
Does anyone know why?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The reason that a stochastic volatility model would typically give a higher option price for a double knock-out barrier option has to do with the fundamental differences between local and stochastic volatility models.

Local volatility models assume that the volatility of the underlying asset is a deterministic function of the asset price and time. This means that the volatility is consistent and only varies with the asset's price level or the time, not randomly. 

On the other hand, stochastic volatility models assume that the volatility of an asset can change randomly over time, behaving according to its own stochastic process. This introduces an additional source of uncertainty compared to local volatility models.

In the case of a double knock-out barrier option, the option becomes worthless once the underlying asset price crosses either the upper or lower barrier. Therefore, increased volatility (or the potential for increased volatility, in the case of a stochastic volatility model) adds more risk that the barrier will be crossed, thereby "knocking out" the option earlier. 

Thus, under a stochastic volatility model, this option would have a higher price due to the increased risk of it being knocked out earlier due to the potential for greater changes in volatility. Price of an option is a reflection of risk, and higher risk implies higher option price under the risk-neutral pricing framework. The potential for sudden and large changes in the underlying asset's price increases both the probability of a payout (if the barriers are not hit) and expected size of that payout (since the asset's price can potentially be further away from the barriers when the option matures), which combined contribute to a higher option price. 

This question would likely be part of a broader discussion of the advantages and disadvantages of different types of volatility models in option pricing.
<|end|>


