What is the best way to make a `<div>` fade away after a given amount of time (without using some of the JavaScript libraries available).

I'm looking for a very lightweight solution not requiring a huge JavaScript library to be sent to the browser.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can use native JavaScript to achieve this effect. 

Here's a simple example where a `div` fades away after 5 seconds:

HTML:
```html
<div id="myDiv" style="opacity: 1">Hello, world!</div>
```

JavaScript:
```javascript
function fade(element) {
    let op = 1;  // initial opacity
    let timer = setInterval(function () {
        if (op <= 0.1){
            clearInterval(timer);
            element.style.display = 'none';
        }
        element.style.opacity = op;
        element.style.filter = 'alpha(opacity=' + op * 100 + ")";
        op -= op * 0.1;
    }, 50);
}

window.onload = function() {
    setTimeout(function() {
        fade(document.getElementById('myDiv'));
    }, 5000);  // 5 seconds
}
```

You use a `setInterval` function to gradually decrease the `opacity` of the `div`. Once the opacity reaches 0.1, you clear the interval and set the `display` of the `div` to 'none'.

The `setTimeout` function is used to call the `fade` function after the specified amount of time (in this case, 5 seconds). You use `window.onload` to start the timer once the document is fully loaded. 

Just adjust the time on the `setTimeout` call to change how long before the `div` starts to disappear. Adjust the interval time and the amount you decrease the opacity each time to control how quickly the `div` fades away.
<|end|>


