Implement the k-means clustering algorithm to segment the iris dataset procured from https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/datasets/iris into distinct groups. Subsequently, employ a scatter plot to graphically illustrate the distribution of these formed clusters.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is a Python implementation of k-means clustering applied to the iris dataset and a scatter plot of the clusters:

```python
from sklearn.cluster import KMeans
from sklearn import datasets
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import pandas as pd

# Load the iris dataset
iris = datasets.load_iris()
X = iris.data
y = iris.target

# Fit the KMeans model to the iris dataset
model = KMeans(n_clusters=3)
model.fit(X)

# Retrieve cluster labels and centroids
labels = model.labels_
centroids = model.cluster_centers_

# Create a DataFrame with labels and species
df = pd.DataFrame({'labels': labels, 'species': y})

# Create crosstab to verify the divided clusters
ct = pd.crosstab(df['labels'], df['species'])

# Scatter plot - Sepal Length and Sepal width
plt.scatter(X[labels == 0, 0], X[labels == 0, 1], s=50, color='blue', label = 'Iris-setosa')
plt.scatter(X[labels == 1, 0], X[labels == 1, 1], s=50, color='green', label = 'Iris-versicolor')
plt.scatter(X[labels == 2, 0], X[labels == 2, 1], s=50, color='red', label = 'Iris-virginica')

# Plotting the centroids of the clusters
plt.scatter(centroids[:, 0], centroids[:,1], s = 50, color = 'yellow', label = 'Centroids')
plt.legend()
plt.show()
```

This code starts out by loading the iris dataset, downloading if it's not already present, from sklearn's suite of test datasets. Then it fits a `KMeans` model to the iris data and extracts predicted cluster labels and the locations of the cluster centroids. 

The scatter plot uses these labels to determine which cluster each data point belongs to when choosing its color. The centroids of the clusters are then plotted on the scatter plot as yellow dots.

Please note, the Iris dataset is a labeled dataset. Hence, it is possible to validate the accuracy of the cluster labels with respect to the original labels. However, k-means is an unsupervised learning algorithm generally used when such labeled data is not available.
<|end|>


