I'm trying to write a function that formats every (string) member/variable in an object, for example with a callback function. The variable names are unknown to me, so it must work with objects of all classes.

How can I achieve something similar to `array_map` or `array_walk` with objects?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To run a function or callback over all member variables of an object, you could use `array_walk` after converting the object to an array using `get_object_vars`. However, this would convert the object to an array, which might not be what you desire. 

If you want to keep the object as an object, you could do something like the following:

```php
function object_walk($object, $callback) {
    $reflection = new ReflectionObject($object); 
    $properties = $reflection->getProperties();

    foreach($properties as $property) {
        $property->setAccessible(true); // If you want to access private/protected properties
        $value = $property->getValue($object);

        if (is_string($value)) {
            $property->setValue($object, call_user_func($callback, $value));
        }
    }
}
```

This function uses PHP's `ReflectionObject` and `ReflectionProperty` classes to get and modify the properties of the object, even if they are private or protected. The `$callback` will be applied to all string properties.

You can use it like this:

```php
$myObject = new MyObject();
object_walk($myObject, function($str) {
    return strtoupper($str);
});
```

This will replace every string property with its uppercase version. Obviously, you could define your own callback that does some other kind of formatting or processing.
<|end|>


