A few weeks ago, I was assigned to evaluate all our programmers. I'm very uncomfortable with this since I was the one who taught everyone the shop's programming language (they all got out of college not knowing the language and as luck would have it, I'm very proficient with it.). On the evaluation, I was very biased on their performance (perfect scores).

I'm glad that our programming shop doesn't require an average performance level but I heard horror stories of shops which do require an average level.

My question are as follows: 


1. As a programmer, what evaluation questions would you like to see?
2. As a manager, what evaluation questions would you like to see?
3. As the evaluator, how can you prevent bias in your evaluation?
4. I would love to remove the evaluation test. Is there any advantages to having an evaluation test? Any disadvantage?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
1. As a programmer, I would like to see evaluation questions that revolve around problem-solving efficiency, code quality, adaptability to new technologies, how one handles bugs and errors, programming methodology, collaboration and communication skills with the team, how one keeps up with updated knowledge and skills, and time management. Also, questions related to understanding the business, clients and customer needs are essential too. 

2. As a manager, I would focus evaluation questions on areas that assess a programmer's task completion rate, quality of work, creativity and innovative skills, technical skills, knowledge about the company's systems and projects, ability to meet deadlines, teamwork, problem-solving skills, and adaptability.

3. As an evaluator, avoid bias by keeping strict focus on the evaluation criteria itself rather than personal relationships or past experiences. Using an objective scoring system, maintaining consistency, involving others in the evaluation process, and seeking feedback from a diverse group can all help ensure evaluations are fair and unbiased. 

4. There are several advantages of having an evaluation test. It can help identify strengths and weaknesses, measure skill level, set benchmarks, identify training needs, measure improvement, ensure productivity, and find opportunities for employee development. The disadvantages include the risk of creating a stressful work environment, discouraging creative thinking, focusing too much on individual performance rather than teamwork, and the potential for biased evaluations.
<|end|>


