Is there a way to convert a HTML string into a Image .tiff file?

I am using C# .NET 3.5. The requirement is to give the user an option to fact a confirmation. The confirmation is created with XML and a XSLT. Typically it is e-mailed.  

Is there a way I can take the HTML string generated by the transformation HTML string and convert that to a `.tiff` or any image that can be faxed?

3rd party software is allowed, however the cheaper the better.

We are using a 3rd party fax library, that will only accept `.tiff` images, but if I can get the HTML to be any image I can covert it into a `.tiff`.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can use a tool called PhantomJS to capture the HTML into an image. PhantomJS is a 'headless' browser which you can control with a script. It is capable of producing a TIFF file of the render.

You would need to run Apache or similar on the computer running the script, so as that PhantomJS can access the rendered HTML.

Here is a script you could use with PhantomJS to save the HTML as image:

```javascript
var page = require('webpage').create();
page.open('http://localhost/yourfile.html', function() {
  page.render('yourfile.tif');
  phantom.exit();
});
```

That will create a 'screen shot' of the webpage as it would be rendered in the PhantomJS browser.

An important note: PhantomJS is a WebKit browser, just like Google Chrome, which means, if your code is specific to Internet Explorer (or any other browser), it might not display as you expect. Furthermore, this will only create a 'screen shot' of what fits on the page, it will not manage to fit an entire page content into one visible page, as it merely captures what PhantomJS 'sees'.

For .NET you need to use a wrapper like NReco or Selenium WebDriver.

The downside of using PhantomJS is that it may take a little more work to get setup initially since it's a command line tool. But with .NET wrapper, it is much easier to implement in your c# code.

Once you got the image, you can convert it to .tiff file using System.Drawing library like this:

```csharp
Bitmap bitmap = new Bitmap("yourfile.png");
bitmap.Save("yourfile.tiff", ImageFormat.Tiff);
```

Or use a third-party tool like ImageMagick.NET.
<|end|>


