<|user|>
Employ a regular expression to devise a pattern that identifies a character sequence of arbitrary length, culminating in the consecutive letters "o" and "o".
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can achieve this using a regular expression in Python. A regular expression is a sequence of characters that forms a search pattern. They can be used to check if a string contains the SPECIFIC pattern or not. Python offers a library called re to work with RegEx.

Here's a python script to solve this problem:

```python
import re

def find_pattern(text):
    pattern = '.*oo'
    if re.search(pattern, text):
        print("The pattern was found in the text!")
    else:
        print("The pattern was not found in the text!")

find_pattern('helloo')
find_pattern('good')
```

In this script, '.*oo' is the regular expression pattern. The dot (.) is used to match any character (except newline). The star (*) means the previous character/concept could be repeated any number of times, including zero. And 'oo' is the actual character pattern we are looking for. 

So, in English, this would be "Look for anything that ends with 'oo'". 

When the function find_pattern is called with a string parameter, the method re.search will return a match object if the regex pattern is found in the string. If it is not found, it will return None.

In the example above, 'helloo' does end with 'oo', so "The pattern was found in the text!" is printed. 'good' does not end with 'oo', so "The pattern was not found in the text!" is printed.
<|end|>


