Upgrade the current code challenge:

Now your task is to upgrade the function that receives a string and two integers X & Y as an argument and gives back the sum of the ASCII codes of the uppercase alphabets present in the string after substrracting the ASCII value of the Xth character and adding the ASCII value of the Yth character in the string. If X or Y is not a valid index, ignore them.

Here, we assume string indexing starts from 0.

  Examples:
      digitSum("", 2, 3) => 0
      digitSum("abAB", 1, 2) => 178
      digitSum("abcCd", 0, 4) => 132
      digitSum("helloE", 5, 1) => 65
      
Your function starts with:
const digitSum = (s, X, Y) => {
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is the upgraded function:

```javascript
const digitSum = (s, X, Y) => {
    let sum = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++){
        if (s[i] === s[i].toUpperCase() && s[i] !== s[i].toLowerCase()){
            sum += s[i].charCodeAt();
        }
    }
    if (X >= 0 && X < s.length){
        sum -= s[X].charCodeAt();
    }
    if (Y >= 0 && Y < s.length){
        sum += s[Y].charCodeAt();
    }
    return sum;
}
```
What we have done here is that we've gone through each character in the string with a for loop and checked if that character is uppercase (which we've done by comparing the character to its upper and lower case forms), if it is, then we add its ASCII value (which we get from `charCodeAt()`) to our sum.

After going through all characters in the string, we subtract the ASCII value of the Xth character (if X is a valid index) and add the ASCII value of the Yth character (if Y is a valid index) to our sum.

Then finally we return our sum.
<|end|>


