Is it realistic to try and learn and code a Flex 3 application without purchasing FlexBuilder? Since the SDK and BlazeDS are open source, it seems technically possible to develop without Flex Builder, but how realistic is it.

I would like to test out Flex but don't want to get into a situation where I am dependent on the purchase of FlexBuilder (at least not until I am confident and competent enough with the technology to recommend purchase to my employer).

I am experimenting right now, so I'm taking a long time and the trial license on my Windows machine has expired. Also Linux is my primary development platform and there is only an alpha available for Linux.

Most of the documentation I've found seem to use Flex Builder.

Maybe I should use Laszlo...
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, it is definitely possible to develop Flex applications without using FlexBuilder, although it will require more work.

In fact, a considerable number of developers start learning and developing with the open-source Flex SDK and compile their applications with MXMLC, the command-line compiler that comes with the SDK. 

To aid with coding, you can use a text editor or even a more complex IDE that supports ActionScript. Some developers use IntelliJ IDEA, which supports Flex/ActionScript development. Other options include Eclipse with the AXDT plugin, and FlashDevelop for Windows. These provide features like code completion and debugging tools. 

The main benefits FlexBuilder provides are visual layout tools, additional coding help, and integrated debugging/profiling. If you are familiar with Java, using Flex SDK without Flex Builder is a bit like developing Java without using an IDE like Eclipse or IntelliJ: lower-level, more control, but more manual work. 

Think of it this way: FlexBuilder is a luxury that makes development easier, rather than a necessity.

Remember that FlexBuilder is available free for education, unemployed developers, and open source projects. 

If you find yourself struggling with just the Flex SDK and a text editor, you may want to examine the trial version of FlexBuilder or free alternatives like FlashDevelop before trying out entirely different platforms such as Open Laszlo.
<|end|>


