What is the Go-compatible version of this code?
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;

public class Solution {
    public List<Integer> common(List<Integer> l1, List<Integer> l2) {
        HashSet<Integer> set1 = new HashSet<>(l1);
        HashSet<Integer> set2 = new HashSet<>(l2);
        set1.retainAll(set2);
        List<Integer> intersectionList = new ArrayList<>(set1);
        Collections.sort(intersectionList);
        return intersectionList;
    }
}
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is the Go-compatible version of the given Java code, but note Go does not have set data type so we need to implement this feature manually using a map.

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sort"
)

func common(l1 []int, l2 []int) []int {
	set1 := make(map[int]bool)
	set2 := make(map[int]bool)

	for _, v := range l1 {
		set1[v] = true
	}

	for _, v := range l2 {
		set2[v] = true
	}

	intersectionList := make([]int, 0)
	for v := range set1 {
		if set2[v] {
			intersectionList = append(intersectionList, v)
		}
	}

	sort.Ints(intersectionList)

	return intersectionList
}

func main() {
	l1 := []int{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
	l2 := []int{4, 5, 6, 7, 8}
	fmt.Println(common(l1, l2))
}
```

This Go code creates a function common which accepts two integer slices, treats them as sets (performing set operations using map datatype), calculates their intersection, sorts the resulting slice, and finally returns it. This does exactly the same as the given Java code.

The final print line in the main function is for testing purposes and will output the common integers in both lists.
<|end|>


