Swift for TensorFlow does not contain direct equivalents for every Keras function, and how to design and train models might be slightly different. Here is an equivalent Swift code using Swift For TensorFlow:

```swift
import TensorFlow
import Datasets

enum ConvBlock: Layer {
    typealias Input = Tensor<Float>
    typealias Output = Tensor<Float>
    
    var conv: Conv2D<Float>
    var batchNorm: BatchNorm<Float>
    var dropout: Dropout<Float>
    var leakyRelu: LeakyReLU<Float>
    
    init(filters: Int, kernelSize: (Int, Int), strides: (Int, Int)) {
        conv = Conv2D(filterShape: (kernelSize.0, kernelSize.1, 3, filters), strides: strides, padding: .same)
        batchNorm = BatchNorm(featureCount: filters)
        dropout = Dropout(probability: 0.5)
        leakyRelu = LeakyReLU(alpha: 0.2)
    }
    
    @differentiable
    func callAsFunction(_ input: Input) -> Output {
        return input.sequenced(through: conv, leakyRelu, batchNorm, dropout)
    }
}

let batchSize = 32
let epochCount = 20
let outputFolder = "./output/"

let cifarDataset = CIFAR10()
let testBatches = cifarDataset.testDataset.batched(batchSize)

var model = ConvBlock(filters: 32, kernelSize: (3, 3), strides: (1, 1))
let optimizer = Adam(for: model)

for epoch in 1...epochCount {
    for batch in cifarDataset.trainingDataset.batched(batchSize) {
        let (images, labels) = (batch.data, batch.label)
        let (loss, gradients) = valueWithGradient(at: model) { model -> Tensor<Float> in
            let logits = model(images)
            return softmaxCrossEntropy(logits: logits, labels: labels)
        }
        
        optimizer.update(&model, along: gradients)
    }
    
    var testLoss: Float = 0
    var testAccuracy: Float = 0
    for batch in testBatches {
        let (images, labels) = (batch.data, batch.label)
        let logits = model(images)
        testLoss += loss(logits: logits, labels: labels)
        testAccuracy += accuracy(predictions: logits.argmax(squeezingAxis: 1), truths: labels)
    }
    
    print("Epoch: \(epoch), Loss: \(testLoss), Accuracy: \(testAccuracy)")
}
```
Please note that the functionality is not identical as I used `softmaxCrossEntropy` loss and `accuracy` method for simplicity, you may adjust the function according to your needs.

Also, the size of the filters(3 in our case) is hard-coded, so replace '3' with your actual input channels. I am also not sure about your exact model architecture, this example just converts the incoming images tensor by a convolution-batch normalization-dropout-LeakyReLU sequence.

Please install the required dependencies to use TensorFlow with Swift. In summary, please adjust this example depending on your specific use case and exact architecture of your model. It gives you a basic idea of how to set up a similar model in Swift.
