Sure! Here is an example how you can implement it with JavaScript and CSS.

Firstly, let's create CSS file named styles.css:

```css
body {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
  height: 100vh;
  margin: 0;
  background-color: #fff;
  font-family: "Arial", sans-serif;
}
```

Then, let's create HTML file named index.html:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
    <head>
        <meta charset="UTF-8">
        <title>Random Background Color</title>
        <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
    </head>
    <body>
        <h1>Refresh the page to change the background color</h1>
        <script src="script.js"></script>
    </body>
</html>
```

Lastly, let's create JavaScript file named script.js:

```javascript
document.body.style.background = getRandomColor();

function getRandomColor() {
    var letters = '0123456789ABCDEF';
    var color = '#';
    for (var i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
        color += letters[Math.floor(Math.random() * 16)];
    }
    return color;
}
```

The JavaScript file creates a function "getRandomColor" that makes a 6 character long hexadecimal number which can be used for color representation in HTML. Then immediately when the document loads, it is setting the background color of body to random color returned by getRandomColor function.
