I have two predictors and five dependent variables. I'm trying to figure out what the critical alpha cutoff for Mahalanobis distance is for a model with 5 DVs to check for assumption of multivariate outliers before my MANOVA. Can't seem to find the answer anywhere, could anyone help?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The critical value of alpha cutoff for Mahalanabis Distance is usually found from the Chi-Square distribution table. The degrees of freedom for the chi square distribution should be equal to the number of dependent variables, which is 5 in your case.

If you are working with an alpha level of 0.05, look up the corresponding value in the Chi-Square table for 5 degrees of freedom. It is approximately 11.07.

Thus, if the calculated Mahalanobis Distance for any observation in your sample is greater than 11.07, it would be considered an outlier at the 0.05 level. However, it's up to you how conservative or liberal you want to be about labeling an observation as an outlier.
  
Always remember to consider your specific research context when deciding. For example, in very sensitive contexts (e.g. medical or safety research), you might want to be more conservative and lower your alpha level to be more certain about your conclusions.
  
Also do note that, just because a data point is an outlier according to statistical criteria doesn't necessarily mean it should be removed from the analysis; it might represent a true but extreme case. Always use your substantive knowledge about the research subject along with statistical guidelines when handling statistical outliers.
<|end|>


