Within the context of a singular collection of fiscal coin units, your task is to determine the least number of these coins required to amass an exact total monetary value. This task must be carried out using a systematic, logical approach, ensuring each step of the thought process is clear and comprehensible. Moreover, consider possible irregularities and the influence of various combinations of coin units, while simultaneously pondering the implications of the task in terms of computational complexity and operational efficiency. Factor in the potential strain of the problem on computational resources and the scalability of the solution, while also contemplating the effect of modifying coin units on the overall problem-solving approach. Furthermore, reflect on the possibility for improvement and the role of dynamic programming in achieving the most effective solution.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This problem is essentially a minimum coin change problem. The task is to determine the least number of coins to make a certain value. Let us assume a function minNumCoins(v) that returns the minimum number of coins required to make a change for the value v. 

The systematic logical approach would be to start from the smallest possible value 0, and build up the solution to our final desired value, v. For this, we make use of a table, minCoins[] such that minCoins[i] stores the minimum number of coins required for value i.

Since we start from 0, for this value, we don't need any coins, so minCoins[0] = 0. For all other values i>0, we set minCoins[i] = ∞ to indicate that the value cannot be achieved with any coins.

Let c[] be the array that contains the available coin values. For each coin, with value c[j], we go through the values c[j] to v, and for each value i, we set minNumCoins[i] = min(minNumCoins[i], minNumCoins[i-c[j]] + 1), which means we are choosing the coin c[j] and reducing the problem to minNumCoins[i - c[j]].

This approach of solving a complex problem by breaking it down into simpler sub-problems and using the solutions to the sub-problems to construct a solution to the original problem is Dynamic Programming.

The complexity of this algorithm is O(v*n) where v is the total desired value and n is the number of different coin units. This is not the most efficient possible but it provides a good balance between time complexity and space complexity. The space complexity of this algorithm is O(v), which is the space needed to store the minCoins table.

Now, the task of handling irregularities and experimenting with various combinations of coin units fall in the category of handling edge cases. For example, for some coin systems, the standard approach of using the highest valued coin as much as possible (greedy algorithm) may not give the minimum number of coins. Another irregularity could be having a coin system where not all values are achievable. Dynamic programming handles these cases elegantly, but it might come with an increase in time complexity. 

Furthermore, if we modify the coin units, our solution stays the same, we just need to rebuild our minCoins table according to the new coin system. 

This problem is well-suited for dynamic programming and its performance could be further improved by introducing a mechanism to stop early when an optimal solution is found or using a more efficient data structure to find the minimum coin unit.
<|end|>


