I have this deep neural network model with an integer label to predict. The label is heavily skewed so we cap the labels at some value (let's say 90 %ile).
Now when we build and run the model, it performs well in general. But in online experiment shows degradation in business metrics for a fraction of users that have high value labels.
If we don't cap the label, the business metrics gets skewed for users with low number of activities.
What are my best options to deal with such issue? Adding a new feature? Multi tower learning? Any idea can be super helpful.
Thanks.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Providing an exact solution without in-depth information about your model and data might be challenging. However, on a high level, some suggestions are as follows: 

1. Stratified Sampling: In your training data creation, use a stratified sampling method to ensure that all ranges of your target variable are equally represented.

2. Ensemble Models: Train separate models for each class/label, then ensemble them together. This concept is quite similar to one-versus-all in multiclass SVMs. You'd have a different architecture for low, medium, and high value labels.

3. Multi Task Learning: The idea behind this is to use a shared representation to learn multiple tasks at the same time. By sharing representations among related tasks, you can enable the model to generalize better on the main task.

4. Adding a new feature: This can certainly help. If there is external information which you think might help the model better understand the higher value labels, it might be worth to add it to your model. 

5. Data Transformation: Caping a skewed distribution may not always be the best approach. Using a logarithm or square root function to transform the labels into a more normal distribution might allow the model to better learn from these extremes.

6. Weighted Loss Function: Higher weights can be assigned to the predictions of high value labels. This informs the model to minimize the error of these instances comparitively more than instances with lower value labels.

These are some approaches you can try. Testing and validation would help you determine which works best for your case. Additionally, it might also be worth researching further into handling skewed data in machine learning models, as there is extensive literature on this topic that might provide more insights.
<|end|>


