I'm running an c# .net app in an iframe of an asp page on an older site. Accessing the Asp page's session information is somewhat difficult, so I'd like to make my .net app simply verify that it's being called from an approved page, or else immediately halt.

Is there a way for a page to find out the url of it's parent document?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
No, you can't directly get the parent document URL as it belongs to different domain because of Same-Origin Policy(HTTP), i.e. a web application cannot make requests to a different domain than the one it originated from. 

There is also an additional concern regarding security and privacy. Allowing any embedded site to gather information about the URL of the parent document could potentially reveal private user data in scenarios where such information is kept in URLs. 

What you can do is to securely pass the necessary information during the communication between the asp page and the .NET app. Depending on the specific requirement and the problem you're trying to solve, there could be several methods. 

Sometimes the information can be passed through URL parameters when calling the .NET app. However, this is not a secure way to transmit sensitive information like authentication data, and is not recommended for such cases. 

Alternatively, server-side verification is often used. The ASP page could send an authenticated server-to-server request to the .NET app to check the session status or transmit necessary data. Database solutions can also be used, where the data is stored into a shared database and accessed where needed based on user session ID or other identifiers.

But all of them needs changes on both ASP site and .NET app, the .NET app itself cannot figure out if it's embedded in a known parent.
<|end|>


