<|user|>
From this answer [https://stats.stackexchange.com/a/437474/346940](https://stats.stackexchange.com/a/437474/346940) seems that batch norm scales the standardized input by a factor $ \beta $... why don't we restrict this $\beta$ to be greater than zero?
if with one gradient update we pass from a $\beta > 0$ but very close to zero, to one small and negative, the input of the following layer will be flipped all of a sudden
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The main reason why we don't restrict beta to be greater than zero in batch normalization is because we want our models to learn the best possible parameters to minimize a certain loss function. This means that the model must have the flexibility and instability to explore the whole parameter space, including negative values.

There's no universal rule that negative scaling factors will produce worse results than positive ones. Flipping the input of the subsequent layer might actually produce better features for certain specific problems. This is part of the non-linearity and interaction of parameters in neural networks that make them so powerful.

In practice, weights in neural networks can vary dramatically, and instabilities like vanishing or exploding gradients are usually not due to negative scaling factors in batch normalization, but more due to factors like architecture, learning rate, etc. 

Having said that, you could certainly apply this restriction if there was a clear motivation to do so in a specific context (e.g., you know that your input and output should both be positive). But you'd run the risk that the restricted model might not now find the global or best possible minimum, or will simply take longer to do so.

In most situations, allowing beta to range freely across all real numbers usually gives the network the best chance to find the most favorable parameters given the selected architecture and training set.
<|end|>


