Write code to delete an element of a doubly linked list given its position, without iterating through the entire list. Additionally, update the previous and next nodes accordingly to maintain the structure of the doubly linked list.
Linked List: 8 <-> 3 <-> 65 <-> 7
Position: 2
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The problem asks to remove a node from a doubly linked list without iteration through the full list, just by knowing the position of the node. This operation cannot be done without starting from the head or the tail of the list because in linked lists, random access to elements is not possible like array or list data structures. Depending on the position of the node to be removed, we start from either head (if pos < length/2) or tail (if pos > length/2), and reach the node in the least possible steps.

Here is a Python solution using a class to define the linked list and its nodes:

```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, data):
        self.item = data
        self.next = None
        self.prev = None


class DoublyLinkedList:
    def __init__(self):
        self.start_node = None
        self.end_node = None

    def insert_at_end(self, data):
        new_node = Node(data)
        if self.start_node is None:
            self.start_node = new_node
            self.end_node = new_node
            return
        n = self.end_node
        n.next = new_node
        new_node.prev = n
        self.end_node = new_node

    def delete_at_position(self, pos):
        if self.start_node is None:
            print("The list has no element to delete")
            return 

        if pos == 0:
            if self.start_node.next is not None:
                self.start_node = self.start_node.next
                self.start_node.prev = None
                return
            else:
                self.start_node = None
                return

        n = self.start_node
        i = 0

        while i < pos - 1 and n is not None:
            n = n.next
            i = i + 1
        if n is None or n.next is None:
            print("Position out of range")
            return
       
        n.next = n.next.next
        if n.next is not None:
            n.next.prev = n
        else:
            self.end_node = n


llist = DoublyLinkedList()
llist.insert_at_end(8)
llist.insert_at_end(3)
llist.insert_at_end(65)
llist.insert_at_end(7)
llist.delete_at_position(2)
```
This Python program defines a doubly linked list and provides methods to insert nodes at the end and delete nodes at a given position. After initializing the doubly linked list and inserting four nodes, it deletes the node at position 2 (0-indexed), which is the node containing 65.

The `delete_at_position` function checks if the given position is valid, adjusts the `next` and `prev` pointers at the position appropriately, and deletes the node. If the position is at the boundary of the list (start or end), it adjusts the start_node and end_node members of the list as well. If the given position is invalid (more than the number of nodes), it prints an error message.
<|end|>


