<|user|>
Design a data structure to store and efficiently query a dictionary with 100000 English words as keys.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Data Structure: Trie

Trie, also known as prefix tree, is an ordered multi-way tree structure that is used to store a dynamic set or associative array where the keys are usually strings. It is highly efficient for querying and searching the data.

The root of a Trie contains an empty character. It contains multiple child nodes where each node consists of two parts: a set of pointers to its child nodes, and a boolean flag to indicate whether the node corresponds to the end of the word.

Design:

1. Nodes: Each node will contain an array of size 26 (for 26 English letters), where each index represents the letter. Index 0 represents 'a', 1 represents 'b', and so on till index 25 which represents 'z'.

2. The end of a word: I will maintain a boolean flag 'endOfWord' at each node. If the flag is true, this means that the word ends at that node.

3. Hash function: I will not need a separate hash function in the case of Trie, as the key will be treated as string and the string characters will be directly inserted into the Trie.

4. Insertion: For inserting the word, I will start from the root and for each character, I will check if the link exists in the trie node, if not I will create one. After processing each character in the word, I'll make 'endOfWord' true for the last character of the word.

5. Searching/Querying: For searching words, I will start from the root node and navigate character by character. If anytime, there’s a character which is not present, I’ll return false. Even if the link is there and 'endOfWord' is true, then the word is present in our trie.

6. Deletion: For deleting a word from the Trie, we have three scenarios:
    - If the word is a prefix of another word, just unmark the endOfWord field.
    - If the Trie contains the word but not any word which is a prefix of this word, remove the node.
    - If the word present in the Trie is a part of another long word in the Trie, don’t remove the node. Just unmark the endOfWord field.

Complexity:

1. Time complexity: For both insert or search, the time complexity is O(n), where n is the length of the word.
2. Space complexity: Since each node has a constant number of pointers (26 in our case), we just need to worry about the number of nodes. In the worst case, a completely unbalanced trie could be a linked list, hence O(n).

This Trie data structure is very efficient and suitable for storing and querying English words. It provides auto-complete feature, prefix searches which are very useful in many applications like search-engines, spell corrections, etc.
<|end|>


