Here is the translated version of your code:

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;

fn remove_duplicates(numbers: Vec<i32>) -> Vec<i32> {
    //Creating a map to store the frequency of elements in numbers
    let mut count_map: HashMap<i32, i32> = HashMap::new();
    for &num in &numbers {
        *count_map.entry(num).or_insert(0) += 1;
    }
    //Creating a temporary vector to store unique elements
    let mut result = Vec::new();
    for &num in &numbers {
        if count_map[&num] == 1 {
            result.push(num);
        }
    }
    result
}
```

The code uses Rust's `HashMap` type (equivalent to C++'s `map`) to count how many times each number appears in the vector. `HashMap::entry()` method is used here to fetch an existing key or create it if it doesn't exist. 

The function signature in Rust includes the type of the argument and return value. In this case, `numbers` is a `Vec<i32>` (equivalent to C++'s `vector<int>`), and the function also returns a `Vec<i32>`. 

`Vec::push()` is equivalent to C++'s `vector::push_back()`, and used to add an element to the end of the vector.

Also, in the loops, `&num` is used to get a reference to each item in numbers and `&num` is used as a key in the HashMap.
