I need to run JET templates on a EMF model metadata - i.e. the model itself (not data) is input to my JET template.

More practically -  I want generate non java code, based on EMF templates.

How I do it?

Thank you
<|end|>
<|asst|>
There are several steps to execute your intent. Here are step-by-step instructions that may help you to run JET templates on an EMF model metadata.

Step 1: Create an EMF model
First, create a new EMF project by File -> New -> Other -> Eclipse Modeling Framework -> EMF Project.  Give a unique name to your project, select the model importer type as XML Schema (or relevant importer based on model data type), browse your XML schema to use and proceed with default configuration until it finishes.

Step 2: Open JET Transformation Project
Next, create a new JET Transformation project. Go to  File -> New -> Other -> Transform ->JET Transformation Project. This project will create a plugin where you can specify your input and templates to generate code.

Step 3: Write your JET Template
Now you are ready to write the JET template that will convert the EMF model into desired code. Right click on the "transform" folder in the JET transformation project -> New -> Other -> JET -> JET Template. This will open a new editor where you can directly write your templates.

Step 4: Create the Main.jet
Right-click on your transformation project -> New -> Main.jet. In this file you'll import your EMF model and run the template you've created in the previous step. Your code will look something like this:
```
<%@jet package="your.package.name" class="Main" skeleton="transform.template.skeleton" %>
<%-- Import EMF model --%>
<%your.emf.package.*; %>

<%
// get the EClass from the model
EClass yourModelClass = your.package.getYourPackage().getYourModel();

// run your template
your.template.package.YourTemplate.generate(yourModelClass);
%>
```

Step 5: Run your JET template
Finally, you can run your JET template. The easiest way to test is by right clicking your transformation project -> Run As -> Eclipse Application.

Keep in mind that creating JET templates may require a good amount of understanding of EMF, the template language (Java), and the structure of your EMF model. Manual adjustments may be required to perfectly fit your model.
<|end|>


