<|asst|>
Sure, here is the translated code in Lua:

```lua
function filter_and_group_chars(text)
    local consonants = "bcdfghjklmnpqrstvwxyz"
    local filtered_text = {}
    
    -- filter consonants
    for i = 1, #text do
        local char = text:sub(i,i):lower()
        if consonants:find(char, 1, true) then
            table.insert(filtered_text, char)
        end
    end

    -- count frequency
    local char_freq_dict = {}
    for _, v in ipairs(filtered_text) do
        char_freq_dict[v] = (char_freq_dict[v] or 0) + 1
    end

    -- sort by frequency and alphabetical order
    local sorted_chars = {}
    for k in pairs(char_freq_dict) do
        table.insert(sorted_chars, k)
    end
    table.sort(sorted_chars, function(a, b)
        if char_freq_dict[a] == char_freq_dict[b] then
            return a < b
        else
            return char_freq_dict[a] > char_freq_dict[b]
        end
    end)

    -- combine chars
    local result = ""
    for _, char in ipairs(sorted_chars) do
        result = result .. char:rep(char_freq_dict[char])
    end

    return result
end
```

Please keep in mind that this is quite literal translation and string operations in Lua can be slow.

Lua also lacks some features so the code translation may not look as neat as the Python version. It also doesn't have list comprehension and the ternary operator (`a if condition else b`) like Python, so some parts may look a little bit unfamiliar.
<|end|>


