Develop a program in which you must create a switch statement (or equivalent, depending on the language) to modify inputs of strings into their corresponding enum types. The enum should represent a classification of colors. 

In addition, implement error handling for cases where the input string does not match any enum type. The function receiving the string input should be case insensitive, implying that it should not matter whether the string is in upper, lower, or mixed case. 

Lastly, to ensure your function is working correctly, you must also create a series of unit tests which cover all the possible edge cases. This should include, but not be limited to, the color string in different cases, an empty string, null inputs, and inputs which do not match any enum.

Input: "Blue", "", null, "Purple", "bLuE", "BLUE". 

You should utilize the following enumeration for the task:

enum Color { RED, BLUE, GREEN, YELLOW }
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a solution using JavaScript:

```javascript
// Enum Type
var Color = {
  RED: "RED",
  BLUE: "BLUE",
  GREEN: "GREEN",
  YELLOW: "YELLOW",
};

// Function to map string to enum
function stringToEnum(input) {
  try {
    if (input == null || input.trim() === "") {
      throw new Error("Input is null or empty");
    }

    let color = Color[input.toUpperCase()];
    if (color === undefined) {
      throw new Error("Invalid color entered");
    }

    return color;
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(error.message);
    return null;  // return null in case of an error
  }
}

// Testing
console.log(stringToEnum("Blue")); // BLUE
console.log(stringToEnum("")); // Input is null or empty
console.log(stringToEnum(null)); // Input is null or empty
console.log(stringToEnum("Purple")); // Invalid color entered
console.log(stringToEnum("bLuE")); // BLUE
console.log(stringToEnum("BLUE")); // BLUE
```

In the above solution, an object is used to emulate an enum since JavaScript does not natively support enums. The function `stringToEnum` attempts to convert the string input into the corresponding enum value. If the input is `null`, empty, or does not match an enum value, an error is thrown and handled accordingly. The function is case insensitive due to the use of `toUpperCase`.

The bottom part of the code tests the function using various edge cases.
