Develop a Python-based software component to invert both an input string and its individual constituents, discarding any characters that aren't part of the standard English alphabet. The output, mirroring the reversed words, should exclusively contain lowercase letters. The internal Python reverse() function and slicing techniques are not permitted for use in the creation of this code. Complement the solution with a quantitative representation of its temporal complexity.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, I will use a stack-based approach to create this software component. Here's the Python code:

```python
def invert_string(s):
    result = ""
    stack = []
    for char in s:
        if char.isalpha():
            stack.append(char.lower())
        elif stack:
            while stack:
                result += stack.pop()
            result += " "
    while stack:
        result += stack.pop()
    return result

print(invert_string("Hello World!"))
```
This function works by iterating over each character in the input string. If the character is an alphabetic character, it is converted to lowercase and added to the stack. If it's not (for example, it might be a space character), all characters currently on the stack are popped off (removed from the stack and appended to the end of the result string). This continues until all characters from the input string have been processed. At the end, any characters that remain on the stack are popped off.

When run with the input `"Hello World!"`, the function ships the string `"olleh dlrow"`.

Regarding time complexity, the function runs in O(n) time, where n is the size of the input string. This is because it performs a constant amount of work for each character in the input string. The space complexity is also O(n) as in the worst case scenario the stack might contain all the characters from the input string.
<|end|>


