<|user|>
I'm using LINQ to SQL classes in a project where the database design is still in a bit of flux.

Is there an easy way of synchronising the classes with the schema, or do I need to manually update the classes if a table design changes?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, there are ways to synchronize your LINQ to SQL classes with your database schema, though they might not be as automated as you'd hope.

1. **Manually Update**: You can manually update your LINQ to SQL classes (.dbml file) by deleting the existing table and then drag-and-dropping the updated table from Server Explorer.
2. **SqlMetal Command Line Tool**: This tool is part of .NET Framework SDK and can be found at (C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft SDKs\Windows\v10.0A\bin\NETFX 4.8 Tools) on a standard Windows 10 installation. You can use it to generate a new .dbml file with updated schema from your database, but it doesn't offer a way to 'update' an existing .dbml file.
3. **SQL Server Database Project**: If you are using Visual Studio 2012 or later, consider having a SQL Server Database Project that import your database schema. So, whenever you change your database schema, you can import the updated schema into your project and the corresponding .dbml file will get updated. But, you will need to do it manually.
4. **Third-party Tools**: There are some third-party tools available, like SqlSharpener or Linq-to-Sql Alternatives (PLINQO for Linq-to-Sql) from CodeSmith tools, that provide features such as regenerating LINQ to SQL .dbml files from a schema.

In general, it requires some level of manual work to synchronize your LINQ to SQL classes with the database schema. Always remember to backup your code before performing these operations, as you may lose any customizations made to the classes.
<|end|>


