<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, here is a simple HTML script to generate a checkbox with adjacent label reading "Remember me". 

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<body>

<form action="">
  <input type="checkbox" id="remember" name="remember" value="remember">
  <label for="remember"> Remember me</label><br>
</form>

</body>
</html>
```

Just a few explanations of the script above:

- `<form>` element is used to create an HTML form for user input.

- `<input>` element is used to create interactive controls for web-based forms in order to accept data from the user; here we specify its `type` as `'checkbox'`.

- `id` attribute of the `<input>` tag is used to associate the checkbox with the label.

- `<label>` element is used to provide a text description for the checkbox. The `for` attribute of the `<label>` tag should be equal to the ID value of the `<input>` element to bind them together.
