I have this function in my code:

const handlePlay = () => {
    const audio = audioRef.current
    if (audio!.paused) {
      audio.play()
      setIsPlaying(true)
    } else {
      audio.pause()
      setIsPlaying(false)
    }
  }


When I try to compile that on Vercel, it fails with `Type error: 'audio' is possibly 'null'.`.
When I force `audio` to not be null like this:

const handlePlay = () => {
    const audio = audioRef.current
    if (audio!.paused) {
      audio!.play()
      setIsPlaying(true)
    } else {
      audio!.pause()
      setIsPlaying(false)
    }
  }


It does not like that either, saying: `Property 'play' does not exist on type 'never'.`
How can I compile this?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The problem with your code is that `audioRef.current` can potentially be `null`, but your code does not account for that possibility at all. When you use `!.` you're telling TypeScript to assume it will never be `null` or `undefined`, but your code doesn't actually make sure of that, hence the error.

In your second code, you're trying to call `.play()` on `audio!`, but this tells TypeScript to assume that `audio` can't be anything except `null` or `undefined` which obviously doesn't support the `.play()` method, causing TypeScript to raise an error as a result.

To fix this issue, you should check if `audio` is `null` before trying to call `.play()` or `.pause()` on it:

```javascript
const handlePlay = () => {
    const audio = audioRef.current
    if (audio) {
        if (audio.paused) {
            audio.play()
            setIsPlaying(true)
        } else {
            audio.pause()
            setIsPlaying(false)
        }
    }
}
```

In this version of your function, I first check if `audio` is `null` or `undefined` before trying to access any of its properties or call any of its methods. This ensures that you'll never get a "cannot read property of null" error at this point in your code.
<|end|>


