I am new to Deep learning. I want to understand when it is a good time to stop training and the tweaks that should be made. Thank you.
1) I understand that if val_loss increases but val is flat, we are over-fitting. Eg in
Image 1:
[](https://i.stack.imgur.com/gKuVY.png)
So do we tweak the network to be less complex (eg reduce the layers, neurons), or reduce the epoch?
2) If both the val_loss and loss have flatten, but the val_loss remains above the loss.
[](https://i.stack.imgur.com/S5Hyj.png)
Does this mean we are over-fitting? Do we increase the epoch until the val_loss and loss are almost the same?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
1) If your validation loss starts to increase while your training loss continues to decrease, it indicates that your model is overfitting to your training data. Meaning that it performs well on the data it was trained on but not on new unseen data. If this is the case, reducing the complexity of your network (e.g., lowering number of layers or neurons) or implementing regularization techniques can help. Another option is to stop your training earlier, or at the point when your validation loss is at its lowest. This is called Early Stopping. 

2) If both your training and validation losses have plateaued, but your validation loss sits higher than your training loss, this likely indicates that your model is underfitting the validation data. This means your model could be too simple to capture all of the patterns in the data.

Although there is not a hard rule that training and validation loss need to be almost the same, the closer they are (given they are at a reasonably low value), the better your model arguably is, because it shows that your model is generalizing well. This is often the ideal scenario, but it's not always achievable depending on the complexity of the problem and the amount/quality of the data.

There are few ways to address this:

- Increase the complexity of your model: This could mean adding more layers or neurons per layer to your network.
- Train for more epochs: Sometimes, your model might just need a bit more time to learn from the data.
- Increase the quality or quantity of your training data: More diverse training data will often improve a model's validity.
- Use Transfer Learning: Pre-trained models could improve the performance, as they have learnt significant features from large datasets.
  
Remember machine learning is an iterative process, if one change doesn't bring improvement, revert the change and try something else. You'd also want to make sure the improvements in the loss metrics corresponds to improvements in the actual metrics that you care about (accuracy, F1 score, etc.).
<|end|>


