This is one of my model variants. It achieves an AUC score of 0.73. Another one of my model variants achieves an AUC score of 0.7265. Below is the the confusion matrix -
[](https://i.stack.imgur.com/kIjY2.png)
Like many problems, the minority class(positive class) represents the customers I'd like to target. But having many false positives is going to cost me money.
Q - how to select a model and how such a massive difference in confusion matrix gives similar AUC scores?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The Area Under the Curve (AUC) score refers to the area under the Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) curve. The ROC curve plots the True Positive Rate (TPR) against the False Positive Rate (FPR) at different threshold settings. The AUC score provides an aggregate measure of the model's performance across all possible classification thresholds. Therefore, one model may be better at a specific threshold, but overall, they may end up having similar AUC scores.

The difference in confusion matrices might be due to the difference in threshold cut-off used in your models. One model might be more conservative, predicting fewer positives (both true and false), while the other predicts more positives overall. Therefore, even if the AUC scores are similar, the business impact can be very different depending on the cost of false positives.

To decide which model to use, you should consider your specific business context. For example, if it's more costly to have a false positive (i.e., you spend a lot of resources on a customer who's not interested), then you should choose the model with fewer false positives. On the other hand, if you're more concerned about missing potential customers (i.e., false negatives), you might prefer the model that predicts more positives.

Another approach involves optimizing for a different metric that takes into account the costs/benefits for your specific problem (e.g., F1-score or precision-recall AUC). You can also tune your models to adjust their threshold to your specific business needs.
<|end|>


