i'm interested in the perl translation of this code:
import java.util.*;

public class Solution {
    public boolean isMonotonic(List<Integer> list) throws IllegalArgumentException {
        if (list == null || list.size() <= 1) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("List should have at least two elements for comparison.");
        }

        int initialComparison = list.get(0).compareTo(list.get(1));
        if (initialComparison == 0) {
            // Both the elements are the same, so we find the next different elements in the list
            Optional<Integer> comparisonResult = findFirstNonZeroComparisonFromIndex(list, 2);
            if (comparisonResult.isPresent()) {
                initialComparison = comparisonResult.get();
            }
        }

        if (initialComparison < 0) {
            return isStrictlyIncreasing(list);
        } else {
            return isStrictlyDecreasing(list);
        }
    }

    private boolean isStrictlyIncreasing(List<Integer> list) {
        for (int i = 0; i + 1 < list.size(); i++) {
            if (list.get(i).compareTo(list.get(i + 1)) > 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    private boolean isStrictlyDecreasing(List<Integer> list) {
        for (int i = 0; i + 1 < list.size(); i++) {
            if (list.get(i).compareTo(list.get(i + 1)) < 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    private Optional<Integer> findFirstNonZeroComparisonFromIndex(List<Integer> list, int fromIndex) {
        for (int i = fromIndex; i + 1 < list.size(); i++) {
            int comparison = list.get(i).compareTo(list.get(i + 1));
            if (comparison != 0) {
                return Optional.of(comparison);
            }
        }
        return Optional.empty();
    }
}
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a translation of the Java code to Perl. Perl doesn't really have classes in the same way that Java does, so this is just a collection of subroutines. Perl also doesn't have a concept similar to Java's `Optional`, so instead `undef` is used.

```perl
use strict;
use warnings;

sub is_monotonic {
    my @list = @_;

    die "List should have at least two elements for comparison."
        if @list < 2;

    my $initial_comparison = $list[0] <=> $list[1];

    if($initial_comparison == 0) {
        my $comparison_result = find_first_non_zero_comparison_from_index(@list, 2);
        $initial_comparison = $comparison_result if defined $comparison_result;
    }

    if ($initial_comparison < 0) {
        return is_strictly_increasing(@list);
    } else {
        return is_strictly_decreasing(@list);
    }
}

sub is_strictly_increasing {
    my @list = @_;

    for my $i (0 .. $#list - 1) {
        return 0 if $list[$i] > $list[$i + 1];
    }

    return 1;
}

sub is_strictly_decreasing {
    my @list = @_;

    for my $i (0 .. $#list - 1) {
        return 0 if $list[$i] < $list[$i + 1];
    }

    return 1;
}

sub find_first_non_zero_comparison_from_index {
    my @list = @_;
    my $from_index = pop @list;

    for my $i ($from_index .. $#list - 1) {
        my $comparison = $list[$i] <=> $list[$i + 1];

        if($comparison != 0) {
            return $comparison;
        }
    }

    return undef;
}
```
In order to call `is_monotonic`, you would provide a list of values. For example,

```perl
my @numbers = (1, 2, 3, 4, 5);
print is_monotonic(@numbers);
```
<|end|>


