&lt;p&gt;A triomino is a geometric figure composed of three squares conjoined at their edges. There exist two fundamental forms of this shape:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p class=&#34;center&#34;&gt;&lt;img src=&#34;resources/images/0161_trio1.gif?1678992055&#34; class=&#34;dark_img&#34; alt=&#34;&#34;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When considering all potential orientations, the number of unique forms increases to six:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p class=&#34;center&#34;&gt;&lt;img src=&#34;resources/images/0161_trio3.gif?1678992055&#34; class=&#34;dark_img&#34; alt=&#34;&#34;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Any grid of dimensions $n$ by $m$, where the product $n \times m$ is divisible by $3$, can be completely covered with triominoes.&lt;br&gt;
If we take into account tilings that are merely reflections or rotations of other tilings as distinct, there are $41$ unique ways to tile a $2$ by $9$ grid using triominoes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p class=&#34;center&#34;&gt;&lt;img src=&#34;resources/images/0161_k9.gif?1678992055&#34; class=&#34;dark_img&#34; alt=&#34;&#34;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Given these conditions, can you determine the number of distinct ways a $9$ by $12$ grid can be tiled using triominoes?&lt;/p&gt;
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The task can be solved by employing dynamic programming approach. We define `f(m, mask)` as the number of ways to cover a grid of size `m` by `m` with a mask `mask`, that is, represents which cells of the last line are covered (if i-th bit in `mask` is `1`, then cell is covered). Note, that in the case of `mask = 2^m - 1`, we start to consider the next line. Thus, we can iterate over all `mask1` of the next line, that can be reached from current `mask`, and add `f(mask1)` to the current `f(mask)`.

Since this problem has a large input size - '9 x 12 grid', the naive approach here wouldn't work in feasible time.
We need to use the bit manipulation to store the different forms of triominoes and memoization to reduce the re-computation of the same sub-problem which comes under dynamic programming. So we need a dictionary to store these values and a recursive function that checks if this configuration is already computed or not. 

1. If it's already computed, return the result directly from the dictionary.
2. If not, compute it and store it in the dictionary, then return it from there.

The Python function below uses bitwise operations to iterate through possible combinations of filling the grid. Then with recursion, it computes and stores the result in the dictionary ways{} to avoid recalculation.

```python
def ways(n, m, bmp, lst):
    if (n, m, bmp) in lst:
        return lst[n, m, bmp]
    else:
        if n == 0 and m == 0:
            if bmp == (1 << 9) - 1:
                return 1
            else:
                return 0
        elif m == 0:
            return ways(n - 1, 11, bmp, lst)
        elif (bmp >> m) & 1 == 1:
            return ways(n, m - 1, bmp, lst)
        else:
            new_bmp = bmp | (1 << m)
            answer = ways(n, m - 1, new_bmp, lst)
            if (bmp >> (m - 1)) & 3 != 3 and m > 1:
                new_bmp = bmp | (3 << (m - 1))
                answer += ways(n, m - 2, new_bmp, lst)
            if m < 11 and (bmp >> (m + 1)) & 3 != 3:
                new_bmp = bmp | (3 << m)
                answer += ways(n, m - 1, new_bmp, lst)
            if (bmp >> (m - 2)) & 7 != 7 and m > 2:
                new_bmp = bmp | (7 << (m - 2))
                answer += ways(n, m - 3, new_bmp, lst)
            if m < 10 and (bmp >> (m + 2)) & 7 != 7:
                new_bmp = bmp | (7 << m)
                answer += ways(n, m - 1, new_bmp, lst)
            lst[n, m, bmp] = answer
            return answer
lst = {}
print(ways(8, 11, (1 << 9) - 1, lst))
```

The dynamic programming approach provides an optimized solution by reducing the time complexity. Reasoning the optimal substructure and overlapping sub-problems of this problem and using a dictionary for memoization lead to an efficient and feasible solution.
<|end|>


