Write a SQL query to find the average price of all unique items in the "orders" table, grouped by customer id and item category. Additionally, only include averages for categories which have more than five unique items purchased across all customers.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Assuming we have a table named "orders" and this table contains the columns "customerId", "itemCategory", and "price", here's a SQL query that will solve the problem:

```
WITH Aggregated AS (
  SELECT customerId, itemCategory, AVG(price) as averagePrice
  FROM (
    SELECT DISTINCT customerId, itemCategory, price
    FROM orders
  ) individual_orders
  GROUP BY customerId, itemCategory
),

categories_count AS (
  SELECT itemCategory, COUNT(DISTINCT price) as item_count
  FROM orders
  GROUP BY itemCategory
  HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT price) > 5
)

SELECT A.customerId, A.itemCategory, A.averagePrice
FROM Aggregated A
INNER JOIN categories_count C ON A.itemCategory = C.itemCategory;
```

In this query, we first created a subquery ("Aggregated") to calculate the average prices for each customer per item category. Of note is that we used "SELECT DISTINCT customerId, itemCategory, price" to only select unique items. We then grouped these results by both "customerId" and "itemCategory".

Then, we have another subquery ("categories_count") to count the number of unique items in each category across all customers.

In the final query, we joined these two subqueries, filtering the results to only include categories which have more than five unique items purchased as set in the subquery "categories_count".
<|end|>


