<|asst|>
You can navigate through your code in Visual Studio Code in a very similar way to Visual Studio. The difference is that Visual Studio Code uses "Go to Definition" and "Peek Definition" instead of active links to sources. 

Here are a couple of ways you can navigate through your code using your mouse:

1. When you hover over something with your mouse, like a method or variable, it will show a pop-up with information about it. You can then `Ctrl + click` (or `Command + click` on Mac) it to go to its definition.

2. You can right-click on the method or variable and choose "Go to Definition" or "Peek Definition" from the context menu.

3. If you want to see all the places where a symbol is used, you can right-click on it and choose "Find All References" or "Peek References".

4. There is also the "Outline" view which provides a tree view of the symbol structure of the current file. You can click on any symbol to navigate to it.

Visual Studio Code also supports "breadcrumbs" which shows the current location in a file and allows you to navigate back to parent symbols. This might be what you mean by seeing the "active link to the source". You can enable it by going to Settings (File > Preferences > Settings or use the shortcut `Ctrl + ,`) and enable "Editor: Breadcrumbs".

Please note that to enable these features in Visual Studio Code with a .NET project, you will need to install the C# extension from Microsoft. The C# extension provides rich support for .NET development.
