```csharp
//convert binary data to base64 string
string base64 = Convert.ToBase64String(file);

//your existing code to set up the SqlCommand, SqlConnection, etc.

//set up the SQL parameter and add it to the command
SqlParameter param = new SqlParameter("@rowColumn", SqlDbType.NVarChar, -1);
param.Value = base64;
cmd.Parameters.Add(param);

//proceed with the update command...
```

When you want to read the data back out, you'll need to convert it back:

```csharp
//get the base64 string from the database
string base64 = (string)reader["rowColumn"];

//convert the base64 string back to a byte array
byte[] file = Convert.FromBase64String(base64);
```
Please note, storing large binary data, like zip files, is generally not recommended in SQL Server, as it can lead to performance issues. Instead, consider storing the files on disk or blob storage and keeping just the file paths or URLs in the database.
