Write a JavaScript function to transform every string element within a specific array into upper case characters, but also, if a string element contains a numerical digit, remove the digit before transforming the string to upper case. The function should throw an error if the array contains a non-string data type.

For example, given the array: ["this", "is", "an", "example1"]
The expected return is: ["THIS", "IS", "AN", "EXAMPLE"] 

Additionally, given the array: ["this", "is", true, "example1"]
The function should throw an error as the array contains non-string data type.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a JavaScript function that performs the described functionality:

```javascript
function transformArray(array) {
    for (let index in array) {
        let item = array[index];
        if (typeof(item) !== 'string') {
            throw new Error('Array contains non-string data type');
        }
        array[index] = item.replace(/\d+/g, '').toUpperCase();
    }
    return array;
}
```

You can test it with:

```javascript
console.log(transformArray(["this", "is", "an", "example1"]));
```

This should log ["THIS", "IS", "AN", "EXAMPLE"] to the console.

And with:

```javascript
console.log(transformArray(["this", "is", true, "example1"]));
```

This should throw an error that says 'Array contains non-string data type'.
<|end|>


