I'm working on a casual game on XNA with the intention of deploying to the Xbox 360.  I'm not going to have access to hardware for a few weeks and I'm on a tight deadline, so I'd like to test that a few things -- Boo and a few custom libraries -- will work properly on the 360.  If they don't, I need to work around them sooner rather than later, so testing this is quite important.

With that explained, is there a way I can go into a 'simulator' of sorts to run code on the .NET Compact Framework for 360 before actually deploying to the 360?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Unfortunately, no such simulator exists for XNA and the Xbox 360. When you develop with XNA, you are using a version of .NET specifically tailored to be able to run on the 360's hardware, dubbed the .NET Compact Framework.

Although you can run your game in Windows as a standalone application during development, it doesn't really simulate the .NET Compact Framework of the Xbox 360. While it allows you to test most parts of your game without much problem, when it comes to platform-specific features or performance considerations things might behave differently on a real Xbox 360.

One approach would be to try and isolate the potential problem in a small standalone code. Then, when you get actual access to hardware, testing that small standalone code might at least give you a good hint about rest of the project.

Alternatively, it might be best to ask on game development forums or Microsoft's XNA community for people that have encountered the same situations or used the same libraries. 

Remember that the major differences between .NET and .NET compact framework 2.0 are threading, reflection and missing coupling between user-interface and other elements. For more details, you can refer to Microsoft's documentation or third party resources.

In consequences, unless you have access to an actual Xbox 360, it could be a bit of a gamble. You can only make sure your code fits the Compact .NET restrictions and hope it will work.
<|end|>


