3. [MVVM pattern](https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/hh848246.aspx): If you're learning WPF, you should focus on learning the MVVM pattern as well as it's commonly used in WPF applications for separating application's logic from the UI.

4. [Pluralsight WPF Fundamentals](https://www.pluralsight.com/courses/wpf-fundamentals-csharp-xaml): This course from Pluralsight can help set you up nicely if you're new to WPF. (Note - This is a paid resource, but it provides a good structured method of learning, and you can try the trial period.)

5. [Pro WPF 4.5 in C#](https://www.amazon.com/Pro-WPF-4-5-Matthew-MacDonald/dp/1430243651): It's arguably the best book out there for WPF. It's a bit old, but WPF hasn't changed too much in recent years, and the book does a great job of explaining things.

6. [WPF free online tutorial by tutorialspoint](https://www.tutorialspoint.com/wpf/index.htm): Tutorials Point has a very good set of tutorials for free for beginners. The chapters are easy to understand and you can start quickly.

Remember, WPF has a steep learning curve at the beginning because it introduces a new way of thinking about UI (especially with data binding), but once you master the basics you will appreciate its power and flexibility. Good luck with your learning journey!
