I'm trying to confirm a user a unplugged my embedded device from a network before performing some maintenance. I'm considering "ping"ing all IP address on my sub-net, but that sounds crude. Is there a broadcast/ARP method that might work better?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, ARP (Address Resolution Protocol) can be a better solution in this case. You could use this protocol to create an ARP request for your device's IP. If you do not get a reply, that would mean the device is disconnected.

How to use ARP?

You can use the arp-scan tool, which is available in Linux. It sends ARP packets to all the hosts on the local network and displays any responses that are received, showing you which devices are set to which IP addresses.

Example:
```
arp-scan --interface=eth0 --localnet
```

Please replace eth0 with your network interface name.

This should provide a quicker and more accurate means of determining whether your device has been taken offline than mass-pinging an entire subnet would. Additionally, this approach is generally less prone to errors and false positives, since devices may block ICMP ping requests for security reasons, whereas ARP is a necessary part of the network stack and is usually available. 

However, remember ARP only works on a local network as it is a non-routable protocol.

For networks beyond the local one, you can resort to the ICMP or TCP pings. For the latter you might find nmap tool helpful. 
Remember though these methods are more prone to false readings due to some user side security settings. 

If possible, the ultimate solution for future situations like these might be implementing a small software client into your embedded device that could report it's state back to a server, but this of course heavily depends upon your device specifications and capabilities.
<|end|>


