<|asst|>
You can solve this problem by iterating over the input array and for each string, you count the number of odd numbers.

You can use the Array `map` method to perform an operation on each string. In this operation, you convert the string to an array of digits with the `split` method, then filter this array to only include the odd digits. The number of odd digits is the length of this filtered array.

Here's the completed JavaScript code:

```javascript
const oddCount = (lst) => {
  return lst.map((str, index) => {
    let oddDigitsCount = str.split('').filter(digit => digit % 2 !== 0).length;
    return `the number of odd elements ${oddDigitsCount}n the str${oddDigitsCount}ng ${oddDigitsCount} of the ${oddDigitsCount}nput.`;
  });
}
```

This function uses string interpolation (`${}` syntax) to include the count of odd digits in the sentences it builds.

For example, `oddCount(['1234567'])` would count 4 odd digits (1, 3, 5, 7), so it would return `["the number of odd elements 4n the str4ng 4 of the 4nput."]`.
