Generate two regular expressions in JavaScript. The first one should check if a string contains only alphabets with uppercase and lowercase letters. The second one should check if a string has alphabets in alternating case (each letter alternatively in uppercase and lowercase). Your program should also handle and provide meaningful feedback to the user in case of empty input.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can use JavaScript's Regular Expressions, or RegEx's, to solve this problem. Here's a simple implementation:

```javascript
// Check for only alphabets in a string, both uppercase and lowercase.
function checkAlphabetsOnly(text) {
  const regex = /^[a-zA-Z]+$/;
  if (text === '') {
    return 'Input field is empty. Please enter a valid text.';
  } else if (regex.test(text)){
    return 'Valid: String contains only alphabets (uppercase and lowercase).';
  } else {
    return 'Invalid: String contains non-alphabet characters.';
  }
}

// Check for alternating case in a string
function checkAlternatingCase(text) {
  const regex = /^([a-z][A-Z])*([a-z][A-Z]|[a-z]|[A-Z])$/;  
  if (text === '') {
    return 'Input field is empty. Please enter a valid text.';
  } else if (regex.test(text)){
    return 'Valid: String has alphabets in alternating case.';
  } else {
    return 'Invalid: String does not have alphabets in alternating case.';
  }
}

```
The first function `checkAlphabetsOnly` returns whether an input string consists only of alphabet characters (both uppercase and lowercase). The second function `checkAlternatingCase` verifies if the alphabets in the input string follow an alternating case.

In both functions, we use regular expressions to match the input with the desired pattern. Regular expressions are patterns used to match character combinations in strings. The caret `^` symbol means the beginning of the line, the dollar sign `$` means the end of the line, and the `[a-zA-Z]` pattern matches any uppercase or lowercase letter. The asterisk `*` means zero or more of the preceding element.

The `test` method of a regular expression object tests for a match in a string. It returns `true` if it finds a match, otherwise it returns `false`.
<|end|>


