If I am trying to determine the read speed of a drive, I can code a routine to write files to a filesystem and then read those files back. Unfortunately, this doesn't give an accurate read speed because Windows does disk read caching. 

Is there a way to flush the disk read cache of a drive in C# / .Net (or perhaps with Win32 API calls) so that I can read the files directly from the drive without them being cached?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In Windows, there is no programming interface provided for explicitly purging the disk read cache. Windows manages it automatically.

However, to get a more accurate measure of disk read speed, you can minimize the effects of read caching by using the `FILE_FLAG_NO_BUFFERING` flag while reading files through Win32 API calls. This flag indicates that the file is opened without system caching.

Please note that this approach comes with certain restrictions, such as you can only read an entire sector at a time (i.e., your read size must be a multiple of the sector size).

Here is an example of creating a file with no buffering:

```csharp
uint FILE_FLAG_NO_BUFFERING = 0x20000000;
var handle = CreateFile(@"C:\Temp\file.txt", FileAccess.Read, FileShare.None, IntPtr.Zero, FileMode.Open, FILE_FLAG_NO_BUFFERING, IntPtr.Zero);
var fs = new FileStream(handle, FileAccess.Read);
```

Unfortunately, this functionality is not directly available in C#, you need to use a PInvoke declaration to make this call to the Win32 API, here's an example:

```csharp
[DllImport("kernel32", SetLastError = true)]
static extern unsafe SafeFileHandle CreateFile(
   string FileName,           // file name
   uint DesiredAccess,        // access mode
   uint ShareMode,            // share mode
   IntPtr SecurityAttributes, // Security Attributes
   FileMode CreationDisposition, // how to create
   uint FlagsAndAttributes,   // file attributes
   IntPtr hTemplateFile       // handle to template file
);
```

Then, to read from the file, iterate in chunks the size of the disk's sectors. This will give you a more accurate measurement of disk read speeds as it bypasses Windows' disk read cache.

Lastly, bear in mind that not all disk drives will have the same sector size and you'll need to account for that when you perform your reads.

Remember to dispose of the `fs` FileStream object and `handle` SafeFileHandle object to close the file and release all system resources associated with the file when you have done your measurement.
<|end|>


