<|asst|>
Doze mode is an Android system feature introduced in Android 6.0 (Marshmallow) to save battery life when a phone is idle. It restricts the network and CPU activity of apps running in the background, thus affecting the execution of Observables and other similar tasks.

Unfortunately, RxJava doesn't have inherent support to handle doze mode scenarios. 

As for your case, you can consider the following options:

1. AlarmManager: Use the Android AlarmManager to perform periodic tasks. AlarmManager is more reliable when it comes to starting tasks at a specific time or when the device is in doze mode. However, use it judiciously as it can affect battery life.

2. JobScheduler: Use the Android JobScheduler for tasks that need to be done periodically or under specific conditions. It is usually more reliable and battery-friendly than AlarmManager. In addition, JobScheduler is more efficient for executing background tasks that need to be done periodically or under certain network connectivity conditions.

3. WorkManager: Introduced in Android 8.0 (Oreo), this is an all-in-one solution that helps satisfy a variety of use cases, and is backwards-compatible.

However, keep in mind to always follow the best practices to optimise battery consumption while performing periodic background tasks. Android documentation provides extensive information on these aspects. 

Remember not to rely on the onSuccess method of Observables for crucial tasks because the OS may not allow it to execute in certain modes like doze. Instead, rely on system services like AlarmManager, JobScheduler or WorkManager that are designed to handle these conditions.
<|end|>


