expecting output =>

List subLists =[[1,2,3,4],[30,31],[50,51],[100]];


basic criteria, subtraction result of each in between values of subgroups should be less than 10
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In the provided input list, the groups have been divided based on the criterion given, i.e., the difference between consecutive numbers should be less than 10. For this, you can use a programming approach preferably in java.

Here is a java code for this task:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    List<Integer> integerList = Arrays.asList(1, 2, 3, 4, 30, 31, 50, 51, 100);
    List<List<Integer>> subLists = getSublists(integerList);
    System.out.println(subLists);
  }
  
  public static List<List<Integer>> getSublists(List<Integer> list) {
    List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
    List<Integer> sublist = new ArrayList<>();

    for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++) {
      if (i == 0) {
        sublist.add(list.get(i));
      } else {
        if (list.get(i) - list.get(i - 1) < 10) {
          sublist.add(list.get(i));
        } else {
          result.add(sublist);
          sublist = new ArrayList<>();
          sublist.add(list.get(i));
        }
      }
    }

    result.add(sublist); 

    return result;
  }
}
```

When you run this code, it will give you the desired result:

`[[1, 2, 3, 4], [30, 31], [50, 51], [100]]`

In this code, the java standard ArrayList and List are used. The function getSublists() applies the logic on each element and divides into sublists whenever the subtraction result is >= 10.
