The original code suffers from two mistakes:

1. In Python, the 'range' function is exclusive of the ending index. So, 'range(n)' generates sequence from 0 to n-1. For summing the first 'n' natural numbers and squares, you want to include 'n' in your sequence. The correct way is to use 'range(1, n+1)'.

2. It initializes the sums (total_sum and total_sq_sum) to 0. We also have to include 'n' into the sum, so the initialization of the sums should be after the 'range' definition.

