This probably is a very basic question and I might be missing something on the definition, but does cross-sectional data have more than one point per user?
In other words, suppose I have a dataset of (users x items) interactions with no ordering of interactions/dates, would this be called a Panel dataset or a Cross sectional dataset if I have one data point per user? And would this be panel or cross sectional, if I have more than one points per user?
Thank you.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Cross-sectional data typically refers to data that is collected at a single point in time, regardless of how many points per user there are. So, if you have one data point per user or more, but all the data was collected at once (or roughly the same time), you'd call this a cross-sectional dataset. 

On the other hand, panel data (also known as longitudinal data), is data collected on the same individuals over time or at different points in time. 

If you have a dataset with multiple interactions per user, but the timestamps for those interactions are irrelevant or non-existent, it's a bit of a gray area. Some might still consider this cross-sectional, especially if the dataset is being used to examine a single point in time, while others might consider it panel data due to the repetition of users. 

Ultimately, it can depend on how exactly the dataset is being used and what questions are being asked of the data.
<|end|>


