```
function printOddNumbers2DMatrix(matrix){
    for(var i=0; i<matrix.length; i++){
        for(var j=0; j<matrix[i].length; j++){
            if(matrix[i][j] % 2 = 0){
                console.log(matrix[i][j]);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Note: This question requires a good understanding of JavaScript, recursion, and iterative approaches to problem-solving. Implement a version of this function using iteration and another using recursion. Remember, the ultimate goal is to find the most efficient solution.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a correctly implemented function of the erroneous code you provided with a change in the if condition,:
```JavaScript
function printOddNumbers2DMatrixIterative(matrix){
    for(var i=0; i<matrix.length; i++){
        for(var j=0; j<matrix[i].length; j++){
            if(matrix[i][j] % 2 !== 0){
                console.log(matrix[i][j]);
            }
        }
    }
}
```
The reason this code before was wrong because it used a single '=' in the if condition, which is an assignment operator, while we need the '!=' or '!==' (not equal to) operator for the if condition. The operator '==' checks whether the values are equal, and the operator '===' checks whether the values and their types are equal.

Finally, let's also implement the function using recursion:
```JavaScript
function printOddNumbers2DMatrixRecursive(matrix, i = 0, j = 0){
  if(i < matrix.length){
    if(j < matrix[i].length){
      if (matrix[i][j] % 2 !== 0){
        console.log(matrix[i][j]);
      }          
      printOddNumbers2DMatrixRecursive(matrix, i, j + 1);
    } else {
      printOddNumbers2DMatrixRecursive(matrix, i + 1, 0);
    }
  }
}
```
This recursive function considers the sub-problem of checking the current element, printing if it's odd, then invoking the same function on the next element. If the end of a row is reached, the function is invoked on the first element of the next row.

Both codes work well with reasonably sized inputs, however, for large inputs, the iterative approach is generally more efficient due to recursion overhead and possible stack overflows if the recursion is too deep.
<|end|>


