Write an SQL query for finding the average salary of employees in a table whose age is greater than 25, excluding those with duplicate names and considering only full-time employees.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Suppose you have a table `Employees` like this:

| id (PK) | name | age | salary | employment_type |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | John | 30 | 3000 | full-time |
| 2 | Mike | 28 | 3500 | part-time |
| 3 | Emma | 26 | 4000 | full-time |
| 4 | John | 32 | 4500 | full-time |
| 5 | Adam | 24 | 2500 | full-time |
| 6 | Emma | 27 | 4200 | full-time |

Here is the SQL query to find the average salary of employees who are older than 25, excluding those with duplicate names and considering only full-time employees:

```sql
SELECT AVG(salary)
FROM (
  SELECT name, avg(salary) as salary
  FROM Employees
  WHERE age > 25 AND employment_type = 'full-time'
  GROUP BY name
  HAVING count(id) = 1
) as subquery;
```

In this query, we first filter out the employees who are older than 25 and are full-time employees. Then, we group the remaining employees by their names and calculate the average salaries for each group. 
We will then further filter out the groups that have only one member (no duplicates).
Finally, we calculate the average salary from these remaining (non-duplicate, full-time, older than 25) employees.
<|end|>


