Creating a mirage or distortion effect may require more advanced SVG manipulation, or perhaps the implementation of CSS animations. You need to distort, scale or skew the SVG element on a timeline and animate it. 

Unfortunately, representing this with texts is more challenging and applying the concept directly on an SVG element would be more appropriate.

A simplified example would illustrate by using CSS animation with filters like hue-rotate and brightness to achieve a shimmering effect:

```CSS
@keyframes shimmer {
  0% {
    filter: brightness(1) hue-rotate(0deg);
  }
  50% {
    filter: brightness(1.2) hue-rotate(20deg);
  }
  100% {
    filter: brightness(1) hue-rotate(0deg);
  }
}

svg {
  animation: shimmer 2s infinite;
}
```

This will cause the SVG to cycle through different colors and brightness levels, creating a shimmering effect. 

To create a distorted effect, you can use the SVG `<feTurbulence>` and `<feDisplacementMap>` filter primitives, but it gets quite complex, especially if you want to animate it. 

Because of the complexity of these effects, you can consider using a library like GreenSock (GSAP) which can help you create more advanced SVG animations with displacement maps and other effects. 

Note: For complex visual effects like a mirage, consider using Canvas or WebGL. These provide greater power and flexibility for visual images and animations.
<|end|>


