Setting up Team Foundation Server (TFS) may not be overly complex, but it can seem overwhelming for someone without prior experience. The process involves several steps such as installing prerequisites, setting up SQL Server, installing TFS, configuring TFS and more. Instructions given in the Microsoft documentation are pretty comprehensive and easy-to-follow.

You can check the following resources for instructions on setting up TFS:

1. Official documentation: Microsoft provides an in-depth guide on how to install TFS on their official documentation website:
https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/server/install/get-started?view=azure-devops

2. Video tutorial: Pluralsight has a detailed, step-by-step course on setting up TFS for those who prefer video tutorials. Though, it's a paid subscription service but you can use their trial version. Here is the link: https://www.pluralsight.com/courses/team-foundation-server-2013-new-features. 

3. Blogs and Forums: There are several blog posts and threads on platforms like StackOverflow and other technology forums which share the practical experiences, common challenges and their resolution of setting up TFS. These platforms can be helpful to understand the practical challenges you might face during the setup.

For servers, Microsoft recommends at least 2 Gigahertz (GHz) processor, 10 GB of hard disk space and 2 GB of RAM, though these requirements may vary depending on the scale of projects your developers work on. 

For a team size of 5-10, a dedicated server with an i7 Processor, 16GB of RAM, 1TB HDD would provide a good starting point. However, these are high-level estimates and actual needs may vary based on the exact use-case and workload. It's always a good idea to monitor server performance and adjust based on actual requirements.

Remember to back up often and consistently manage your server to prevent data loss and ensure smooth running of TFS.
