const wordsInSentence = (sentence) => {
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a revised version of the function, in Javascript, which fulfills the specifications and constraints:

```javascript
/** Auxiliary function that checks if a number is prime. */
const isPrime = (number) => {
    if (number < 2) return false;
    for(let i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(number); i++) {
        if (number % i === 0) return false;
    }
    return true;
};

/** Primary function that filters the input sentence. */
const wordsInSentence = (sentence) => {
    // Validation of the input sentence.
    if (!sentence || sentence.length === 0 || sentence.length > 100) return null;
    if (/[^a-zA-Z\s]/.test(sentence)) return null;
    
    // Splitting sentence into words.
    const wordsArray = sentence.split(' ');
    const primeWordsArray = wordsArray.filter(word => isPrime(word.length));
    
    // Joining prime words back into a sentence.
    return primeWordsArray.join(' ');
};
```

This code implements an auxiliary ```isPrime function``` that checks if a given number is prime. Then, in the ```wordsInSentence``` function, validates if the sentence meets the defined constraints. Subsequently, it splits the sentence into words, filters out non-prime length words, and combines the remaining words into a single sentence that will be returned.
<|end|>


