This is for multiclass classification. Before tuning the n_neighbors for KNN, these were the results:

    Train Accuracy:  99.54%
    Test Accuracy:  99.58%
    ROC AUC Score: 99.86%


After finding the optimum n_neighbors, these were the results:

    Train Accuracy:  99.64%
    Test Accuracy:  99.67%
    ROC AUC Score: 99.82%


My recall score went from 0.996 to 0.997. As we can see, the results improved without overfitting. But why did my ROC AUC score went down by 0.04? I thought the ROC AUC score increases when the model improves? My confusion matrices also improved:
Before tuning:[](https://i.stack.imgur.com/uEEqg.png)
After tuning:[](https://i.stack.imgur.com/puno9.png)
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Your observations raise a very valid question, as normally the ROC AUC score is expected to increase if the model improves.

However, there are cases where we may see such situations. The reason ROC AUC might decrease even when the model's accuracy and recall are increasing is because ROC AUC considers all possible thresholds for classification, while accuracy, precision, and recall scores typically consider a specific single threshold (often 0.5).

Changes in the model parameters might create changes that are beneficial at the particular threshold but are actually less effective when you consider all possible thresholds. 

Another possible explanation could be that the ROC AUC score, which summarises the trade-off between the true positive rate and false positive rate for different decision thresholds, depends on the distribution of the scores and not only on the final decision. Even a very tiny shift in the scores of the negative class can alter the AUC a lot, without significantly affecting accuracy or recall. 

A fall in ROC AUC score with increase in accuracy might indicate that the model's score distribution is less 'discriminative' than before, even though overall it is more accurate.

Lastly, remember that these measures provide different perspectives on the model's performance, and it is possible for them to provide seemingly contradictory information. It's part of the reason why you should consider multiple measures when evaluating your model. 

A minor drop in ROC AUC score along with an increase in recall and accuracy is generally not a cause for concern. But if this gap keeps growing with each tuning, it signifies that the model is improving its performance only on a specific threshold and deteriorating overall.
<|end|>


