In probability theory, convolution extends to produce the distribution of the sum of two independent random variables. I've only ever seen this in the context of univariate random variables.
However, the more general engineering interpretation of convolution is a signal operator/filter. In convolutional neural nets, this seems to be the main interpretation: we can produce common signals throughout, say, an image. But I am wondering if there is a probabilistic formulation/interpretation of this as there is in probability theory? Intuitively I wouldn't think so since I'm not sure how to interpret a 2D convolution probabilistically.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In the context of convolutional neural networks (CNNs), the process of convolution certainly has a different meaning compared to the probability theory.

CNNs use the convolution operation as a means of "scanning" an image, looking for localized features in the image, such as edges or shapes, depending on the complexity of the layers. One might say that it's conceptually similar to a sliding window technique which systematically applies a filter or kernel throughout the image, pixel by pixel. 

This version of convolution, though it doesn't have a probabilistic interpretation in the same sense as in probability theory, does have a probabilistic aspect. Filters (or kernels) that a CNN learns are based on statistical optimization techniques that tries to minimize error rates on training data. So the pattern that are learned are statistical in nature, and inherently probabilistic, though not in the classical sense used in probability theory of convolution of two independent random variables.

Finally, it is possible to have probabilistic convolutions in a sense, especially when dealing with Bayesian Convolutional Neural Networks where weights of networks are modelled with some probability distribution, but this strays quite far from the source of your question and more towards the Bayesian interpretation of neural networks. So to wrap up, while there is not a one-to-one mapping of the concept of convolution from the realms of signal processing to probability theory, both have probabilistic aspects in their operation and learning processes.
<|end|>


