In C, the task takes a little bit more effort than it would in JavaScript. And, please note that C doesn't natively support Boolean data type. Instead, it treats 0 as false and any non-zero value as true.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <ctype.h>

void removeVowelsAndTransformCase(char *text, int changeCase) {
    int length = strlen(text);
    char reversedText[length + 1];
    int j = 0;
    
    for(int i = length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        char current = text[i];
        if(strchr("aeiouAEIOU", current) == NULL) {
            reversedText[j++] = changeCase ? toupper(current) : tolower(current);
        }
    }
    
    reversedText[j] = '\0';  // Null terminate the reversed text
    printf("%s\n", reversedText);
}

int main() {
    removeVowelsAndTransformCase("", 1);
    removeVowelsAndTransformCase("abcdef", 0);
    removeVowelsAndTransformCase("abcdef", 1);
    removeVowelsAndTransformCase("aaBAA", 0);
    removeVowelsAndTransformCase("zbcd", 1);
    return 0;
}
```

In C, a string is an array of characters. The function removeVowelsAndTransformCase walks the given text backwards, does the required transformations and stores the result in the array reversedText. Finally the reversedText is null terminated and then printed.
