Using JavaScript, write a function that not only displays the current time in the format "hh:mm:ss", but also returns the time in the same format for any valid JavaScript Date object passed as a parameter to the function. Also, make sure your function can handle displaying both 12-hour (AM/PM) and 24-hour time standards as an additional argument passed to it.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a JS function that can handle this:

```javascript
function getFormatedTime(dateObj = new Date(), is12HourFormat = false) {
    let hours = dateObj.getHours();
    let minutes = dateObj.getMinutes();
    let seconds = dateObj.getSeconds();
  
    if (is12HourFormat) {
      let am_pm = hours >= 12 ? 'PM' : 'AM';
      hours = hours % 12;
      hours = hours ? hours : 12; // the hour '0' should be '12'
      hours = hours < 10 ? '0' + hours : hours;
      minutes = minutes < 10 ? '0' + minutes : minutes;
      seconds = seconds < 10 ? '0' + seconds : seconds;
      let strTime = hours + ':' + minutes + ':' + seconds + ' ' + am_pm;
      return strTime;
    } else {
       // for 24 hour format
      hours = hours < 10 ? '0' + hours : hours;
      minutes = minutes < 10 ? '0' + minutes : minutes;
      seconds = seconds < 10 ? '0' + seconds : seconds;
      let strTime = hours + ':' + minutes + ':' + seconds;
      return strTime;
    }
}
```

This function takes two parameters: a Date object and a Boolean value. The Date object defaults to the current date and time if no argument is passed. The Boolean value indicates whether the time is displayed in the 24-hour format (false) or the 12-hour format (true). If this argument is omitted, it defaults to false, i.e., the 24-hour format.

You just need to call this function and pass the parameters as per your requirements.

Note: This function assumes you are passing a valid Date object. It will not check for invalid or improperly formatted dates. It is user's responsibility to pass a correct Date object to the function.
