<|asst|>
In Lua, you can use a for loop to iterate through the nested table (equivalent of arrays or vectors in C++) and you can directly compare the original and reversed array as they are tables in lua. Here's how you can do it:

```lua
-- Function to flatten a nested table
function flatten(vec)
    local result = {}
    for _, el in ipairs(vec) do
        for _, val in ipairs(el) do
            table.insert(result, val)
        end
    end
    return result
end

-- Function to check if array is a palindrome
function palindrome(l)
    -- Flatten the multidimensional array
    local flat = flatten(l)
    -- Compare the original array with its reverse
    local reversed = {}
    for i=#flat, 1, -1 do
        table.insert(reversed, flat[i])
    end
    for i=1, #flat do
        if flat[i]~=reversed[i] then
            return false
        end
    end
    return true
end

print(palindrome({{1, 2}, {3, 2, 1}}) and "true" or "false")
print(palindrome({{1, 20, 4}, {10}}) and "true" or "false")
print(palindrome({{4, 4, 2}, {2, 4, 4}}) and "true" or "false")
```

This program defines two functions: `flatten()` and `palindrome()`. `flatten()` takes a nested table and returns a one-dimensional table that is a flattened version of the input. `palindrome()` checks if a flattened version of the input is a palindrome, that is, whether it is the same forward and backward.

The `print()` statements test these functions on some hard-coded input and print the result.
