I have read some paper like [https://arxiv.org/abs/1011.6402](https://arxiv.org/abs/1011.6402) , which need transaction to construct order flow imbalance.
[](https://i.stack.imgur.com/rsDTf.png)
The [binance api](https://www.binance.com/en/landing/data) only have trades(Executed transactions) for spot data.
So I wonder if we can reconstruct a order flow imbalance only from trade data .
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To reconstruct order flow imbalance using only trade data can be challenging. Order flow imbalance is typically constructed by observing a series of transactions and determining whether the majority were buyer-initiated or seller-initiated. 

However, with only trade data, you don't have explicit information about whether a trade was initiated by a buyer or a seller - just the fact that a trade happened. Therefore, you might have to make some assumptions or use certain estimation techniques which could introduce inaccuracies.

One common technique is to use the "Lee and Ready" algorithm, which determines whether a trade is buyer- or seller-initiated based on the trade price in comparison to the midpoint of the bid-ask spread at the time of the trade. However, this also requires access to bid-ask spread data.

In summary, while it is technically possible to estimate order flow imbalance from trade data, it is best to consider using bid-ask spread data or order book data if it's accessible as it is more accurate.
<|end|>


