Construct a JavaScript function, embedded within an HTML webpage, that assesses the robustness of an end-user's bespoke password, offering commentary based on the security level.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a simple example of a program that could accomplish this using plain HTML and JavaScript. This program will consider password length, presence of numerics, lower case letters, upper case letters, and special characters for scoring password strength.

```HTML
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<body>
    <label for="pwd">Password:</label> <br>
    <input type="password" id="pwd" name="pwd"> <br>
    <button onClick="evaluate()">Check Strength</button> <br>
    <p id="strength"></p>

    <script>
        function evaluate() {
            let pwd = document.getElementById('pwd').value;
            let strength = {
                length: pwd.length > 7,
                numeric: /\d/.test(pwd),
                lowerCase: /[a-z]/.test(pwd),
                upperCase: /[A-Z]/.test(pwd),
                specialChar: /[!@#$%^&*()_+\-=\[\]{};':"\\|,.<>\/?]+/.test(pwd),
            };

            let score = Object.values(strength).reduce((a, b) => a + b);
            let comment = (score < 3 ? "Weak Password" : score < 5 ? "Good Password" : "Strong Password");
            document.getElementById('strength').innerText = comment + " (" + score + "/5)";
        }
    </script>

</body>
</html>
```
In this simplistic scoring, a strong password can be a combination of at least 5 of the following: a length over 7, numeric characters, lower case letters, upper case letters, and special characters. A weak password contains fewer than 3 of the aforementioned qualities, and a good password falls in between. 

Please note that the provided JavaScript function along with regex usage are quite simple and may not cover all security needs. Password strength and requirements vary greatly depending on the specific application or system where they are being used. 

Some other important factors to consider that are not covered by this simple function are resistance to dictionary attacks (common passwords), the use of common personal information, ease of guessability, repetitive or sequential characters, etc. It may be better to use a well-established library or to follow widely accepted standards for password complexity validation.
<|end|>


