<|asst|>
In your loop where you are checking for events, you have placed the screen.blit and pygame.display.update() functions outside it. This means these two pieces of code only will get executed once and then your program will be stuck on the event loop until you manually quit. Your screen.blit and pygame.display.update() should be inside your while loop to constantly update your screen.

Here is your corrected code:

```python
import pygame
pygame.init()

# Set up some constants
WIDTH, HEIGHT = 3840, 2160
CAPTION = "GermanBall"
BG_PATH = "Tan.jpg"

# Initialize the game window
screen = pygame.display.set_mode((WIDTH, HEIGHT))
pygame.display.set_caption(CAPTION)

# Load the background image
bg = pygame.image.load(BG_PATH)

running = True
while running:
    for event in pygame.event.get():
        if event.type == pygame.QUIT:
            running = False
    
    # Draw the background
    screen.blit(bg, (0, 0))

    # Update the display
    pygame.display.update()

pygame.quit()
```
Please also note the pygame.init() at the beginning, which ensures all necessary Pygame modules get initialized. When you stop running the game, you may also call pygame.quit() to close the Pygame window and clean up resources.
<|end|>


