As a language model AI I'm a text based model and not able to create graphical visuals, diagrams or drawings including python packages like bpmn_pgv to generate graphical data such as BPMN diagrams.

However, I can guide you on how to do it:

```python
from bpmn_python import bpmn_diagram_rep as diagram
from bpmn_python import bpmn_diagram_layouter
from bpmn_python import bpmn_diagram_visualizer
from bpmn_python import bpmn_diagram_rep as diagram

# Create new BPMN diagram
bpmn_graph = diagram.BpmnDiagramGraph()

# Define process
bpmn_graph.create_new_diagram_graph(diagram_name="myDiagram")
process_id = bpmn_graph.add_process_to_diagram()

# Add start event

start_id = bpmn_graph.add_start_event_to_diagram(process_id, start_event_name="start_event", )

# Add clusters
[cluster1_id, _] = bpmn_graph.add_subprocess_to_diagram(process_id, [process_id + "_task_1"], "Secretaría Ejecutiva", False)
[cluster2_id, _] = bpmn_graph.add_subprocess_to_diagram(process_id, [process_id + "_task_2"], "Presidencia", False)
[cluster3_id, _] = bpmn_graph.add_subprocess_to_diagram(process_id, [process_id + "_task_3"], "Presupuesto/Administración", False)

# Add tasks to each cluster
secretaria_task_id = bpmn_graph.add_task_to_diagram(process_id, task_name="secretaria_task")
presidencia_task_id = bpmn_graph.add_task_to_diagram(process_id, task_name="presidencia_task")
presupuesto_task_id = bpmn_graph.add_task_to_diagram(process_id, task_name="presupuesto_task")

# Add edges within clusters
bpmn_graph.add_sequence_flow_to_diagram(process_id, start_id, secretaria_task_id)
bpmn_graph.add_sequence_flow_to_diagram(process_id, secretaria_task_id, presidencia_task_id)
bpmn_graph.add_sequence_flow_to_diagram(process_id, presidencia_task_id, presupuesto_task_id)

# Add nodes for SIGEFOMDES Administración and SISAC
SIGEFOMDES_id = bpmn_graph.add_task_to_diagram(process_id, task_name="SIGEFOMDES Administración")
SISAC_id = bpmn_graph.add_task_to_diagram(process_id, task_name="SISAC")

# Connect nodes to clusters with dashed edges
bpmn_graph.add_sequence_flow_to_diagram(process_id, cluster1_id, SIGEFOMDES_id, sequence_flow_name='flow_1', is_default=False, condition_expression=None, show=False, is_dashed=True)
bpmn_graph.add_sequence_flow_to_diagram(process_id, cluster2_id, SISAC_id, sequence_flow_name='flow_2', is_default=False, condition_expression=None, show=False, is_dashed=True)

# Save diagram as PNG
bpmn_diagram_diagram_visualizer.generate_diagram_png_file(bpmn_graph,'proc4')

# Save diagram as DOT
bpmn_diagram_diagram_visualizer.generate_diagram_dot_file(bpmn_graph, "proc4")
```
The above python script will create a BPMN diagram and save it as a PNG file named "proc4.png" as well as a DOT file named "proc4.dot". However, you need to make sure you have installing the necessary packages by running `pip install bpmn_python` in your python environment.

One last thing to note is that the bpmn_python library is not always accurate in conforming to BPMN standard especially with complex diagrams. If you're looking to create diagrams that strictly follow BPMN standards, you might want to use more mature software, possibly GUI based.
<|end|>


