can you make this snippet rust-ready?
def advanced_roman_to_int(roman):

    roman_numerals = {'i': 1, 'v': 5, 'x': 10, 'l': 50, 'c': 100, 'd': 500, 'm': 1000}
    integer = 0
    i = 0

    while i < len(roman):
        if i+1 < len(roman) and roman_numerals[roman[i]] < roman_numerals[roman[i+1]]:
            integer += roman_numerals[roman[i+1]] - roman_numerals[roman[i]]
            i += 2
        else:
            integer += roman_numerals[roman[i]]
            i += 1
    return integer

# Test cases:
print(advanced_roman_to_int('mdvii')) # Output: 1507
print(advanced_roman_to_int('mmxlvii')) # Output: 2047
print(advanced_roman_to_int('mmdxxvi')) # Output: 2526
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, here's how you can implement this function in Rust:

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;

fn advanced_roman_to_int(roman: &str) -> i32 {
    let mut roman_numerals = HashMap::new();
    roman_numerals.insert('i', 1);
    roman_numerals.insert('v', 5);
    roman_numerals.insert('x', 10);
    roman_numerals.insert('l', 50);
    roman_numerals.insert('c', 100);
    roman_numerals.insert('d', 500);
    roman_numerals.insert('m', 1000);

    let mut integer = 0;
    let mut i = 0;
    let chars: Vec<char> = roman.chars().collect();
    
    while i < chars.len() {
        if i+1 < chars.len() && roman_numerals[&chars[i]] < roman_numerals[&chars[i+1]] {
            integer += roman_numerals[&chars[i+1]] - roman_numerals[&chars[i]];
            i += 2;
        } else {
            integer += roman_numerals[&chars[i]];
            i += 1;
        }
    }
    
    integer
}

fn main() {
    println!("{}", advanced_roman_to_int("mdvii")); // Output: 1507
    println!("{}", advanced_roman_to_int("mmxlvii")); // Output: 2047
    println!("{}", advanced_roman_to_int("mmdxxvi")); // Output: 2526
}
```
This code works similarly to the Python snippet you provided. It first creates a HashMap to store the values of Roman numerals, then it iterates over the input string, adding up the values of the numerals, taking into account the special subtractive notation of Roman numerals. 

Remember that in Rust, we need to specify the type for each variable and need to use a vector of characters instead of string for iterating over in index-based way. We also have to change the function definition slightly to work with Rust syntax. 

Please note that this code expects all the characters in the string to be lower case and valid Roman numerals; otherwise, it may panic due to unwrapping of None values. If the input str may include upper case letters or invalid characters, you'll need to add extra error checking.
<|end|>


