Transform the provided complex nested if-else based code snippet, containing specific equality as well as inequality conditions and logical AND, OR operations for multiple variables including 'x', into an equivalent switch-case statement structure. It should also include suitable error handling scenarios whenever required.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
It is important to note that switch-case statements can't handle inequality conditions and logical AND, OR operations directly as the if-else statements do. Switch statements evaluate an expression and try to find a match among a list of constants (or expressions that can be evaluated at compile time as constants).

Additionally, switch-case is not applicable to all data types. For instance, it cannot be used with float or double.

For these reasons, your complex nested if-else statement with multiple conditions and boolean operations might not be able to be converted into a switch-case statement.

However, under certain circumstances where you are dealing with equalities and the data types are compatible, you can use switch-case. Here is a sample switch-case statement in C++:

```c++
void process(int x) {
    // Input validation
    if (x < 0 || x > 10) {
        throw std::invalid_argument("Input out of range");
    }

    switch (x) {
        case 1:
            // Code for case x = 1
            break;
        case 2:
            // Code for case x = 2
            break;
        case 3: 
            // Code for case x = 3
            break;
        // other cases

        default:
            // default code if none of the cases is satisfied
            throw std::runtime_error("Unhandled case");
    }
}
```

For stricter type control and to ensure comparability, considering using enums instead of integers when using switch-case.

But if you are looking to find an equivalent switch-case statement for your complex nested if-else condition, unfortunately, you might not find one due to the limitations of the switch-case construction.
<|end|>


