Write a Java program that uses a loop to print out the squares of numbers from 1 to 20, but in reverse order (e.g., starting from 20^2 and ending with 1^2). Additionally, add error handling to ensure that no negative squares are printed. You may use the following piece of erroneous code as a reference:

```java
public class PrintSquares {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
            int square = i * i;
            System.out.println(square);
        }
    }
}
```

The code above prints the squares of numbers from 1 to 10 in ascending order, instead of squares of numbers from 1 to 20 in descending order, as required by the updated question.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's the corrected Java program:

```java
public class PrintSquares {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int i = 20; i >= 1; i--) {
            int square = i * i;
            if (square >= 0) {
                System.out.println(square);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This corrected program uses a for loop from 20 to 1 in descending order (i.e., it starts from 20 and moves down to 1). It then calculates the square of each number and prints it. The if statement checks if the square is non-negative (which should always be true in this case) before printing it.
<|end|>


