I am trying to optimize some stored procedures on a SQL Server 2000 database and when I try to use SQL Profiler I get an error message "In order to run a trace against SQL Server you have to be a member of sysadmin fixed server role.". It seems that only members of the sysadmin role can run traces on the server (something that was fixed in SQL Server 2005) and there is no way in hell that I will be granted that server role (company policies)

What I'm doing now is inserting the current time minus the time the procedure started at various stages of the code but I find this very tedious 

I was also thinking of replicating the database to a local installation of SQL Server but the stored procedure is using data from many different databases that i will spend a lot of time copying data locally

So I was wondering if there is some other way of profiling SQL code? (Third party tools,  different practices, something else )
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, there are a few third-party tools and practices you can use for profiling SQL server. Here are some recommended ones:

1. **Redgate's SQL Monitor**: This tool provides full coverage for your SQL servers. It gives SQL server performance monitoring, analysis, and alerts.

2. **SolarWinds Database Performance Analyzer**: It offers comprehensive database performance monitoring and analysis to ensure the performance of your SQL Server instances, and identify bottlenecks in your databases.

3. **ApexSQL Monitor**: It's a SQL server monitoring tool that provides a wide range of metrics and a detailed analysis of SQL server performance.

4. **SQL Server Query Store**: For SQL Server versions 2016 and later, Query Store provides a built-in way to track query performance over time. It's a powerful feature used for troubleshooting and optimizing SQL queries.

5. **Use SQL Server Execution Plans**: The execution plans provide a wealth of information about how SQL Server will execute a given query, and subsequently how well that execution was performed. You can analyze execution plans to find performance bottlenecks.

6. **Use SET STATISTICS IO, TIME ON**: This method gives you a detailed breakdown of several key performance statistics for a particular SQL query. This is likely the most similar to what you're currently doing, but is a more robust implementation of tracking query execution time on a granular level.

Note that most of these options also require certain permissions, so you would need to check which one is feasible within your company policies. In general, optimizing SQL code involves writing efficient SQL queries, indexing, partitioning, etc. Profiling SQL code can help identify problematic SQL code that may need to be optimized for better performance.
<|end|>


