I'm creating a plugin framework, where my application loads a series of plugin DLL's, then creates a new window and pass this new window's handle to the plugin. The plugin can, then, use this handle to create their own GUI.

Everything seems to be working very well. The only problem is that when I press TAB on a plugin widget (An editbox, for example), it doen't jump to another widget. I figured out that some Windows messages are passed, and some others aren't. The WM_KEYDOWN is passed for other keys, because I can type on the editbox, but this message doesn't handle TAB key.

Hope somebody has a hint.

I'm using Borland VCL with CBuilder, but I think I could use any framework under WIN32 to create these plugins, since they never know how their parent windows were created.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This is a behavior of Windows itself. The TAB key is handled at a higher level because it's a control key, it's used for navigation among controls in the same window (more precisely, in the same dialog box). If a window is not defined as a dialog box, then the operating message pump doesn't perform this kind of processing.

In others words, the way you are managing your plugins' GUI by passing a raw window handle and having each plugin manage its own controls independently means they are not part of the same dialog box and therefore not aware of each other, hence when you press TAB, there's no list of controls to navigate through. 

To make tabbing work conveniently and intuitively among your plugin widgets, some potential solutions could be:

1. Defining your plugin GUIs as dialog boxes (so not just standard windows) and ensuring they implement the necessary processing to deal with WM_NEXTDLGCTL message. 

2. Implementing custom message pumping and tab handling in your application. Yes, it can get complex as you need to manage the tab order and each widget type's specific behavior.

3. Using a higher-level UI framework that abstracts these details away for you, which can handle and track tab ordering, etc., but it can impose constraints on what can be done or requires the plugins to use the same framework.
<|end|>


