You can approach this in JavaScript by creating an object with methods which execute different operations based on the supplied conditions. Consider the example below:

```javascript
let condition_based_operation = {
  add: function(x, y) {
    return x + y;
  },
  subtract: function(x, y) {
    return x - y;
  },
  multiply: function(x, y) {
    return x * y;
  },
  divide: function(x, y) {
    if(y != 0) {
      return x / y;
    } else {
      return "Cannot divide by zero";
    }
  },
  execute: function(operation, x, y) {
    switch(operation) {
      case "add":
        return this.add(x, y);
      case "subtract":
        return this.subtract(x, y);
      case "multiply":
        return this.multiply(x, y);
      case "divide":
        return this.divide(x, y);
      default:
        return "Invalid operation";
    }
  }
};

// Test:
console.log(condition_based_operation.execute("add", 2, 3));           // 5
console.log(condition_based_operation.execute("subtract", 5, 2));      // 3
console.log(condition_based_operation.execute("multiply", 3, 4));      // 12
console.log(condition_based_operation.execute("divide", 8, 2));        // 4
console.log(condition_based_operation.execute("divide", 8, 0));        // Cannot divide by zero
console.log(condition_based_operation.execute("invalid", 8, 0));       // Invalid operation
```
In this example, we've created an object `condition_based_operation` with multiple methods to do arithmetic operations. We also have a `execute` method that uses a `switch` statement to decide which operation to perform based on the supplied condition.

In the switch condition, `this` refers to the current object, so it allows you to refer to the methods within the same object.

You can modify this `execute` method further according to the specific conditional circumstances you want to deal with. Note that 'add', 'subtract', 'multiply', 'divide' would be your "conditions" in this case and depending on each one, different operations are performed.
<|end|>


