Write a Java program to filter out the common characters in two given strings. The program should also provide the count of these common characters and it must be case sensitive (for example, 'a' and 'A' should be considered as two different characters). Additionally, the program should not use any in-built or third-party library methods for this task.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is the Java solution for it. This solution iterates over each characters of both strings and stores them in two separate boolean arrays to keep track of the characters present in those strings. Later, it iterates over these boolean arrays to find common characters in both strings. The complexity of this solution is O(n + m), where n and m are lengths of given strings respectively.

```java
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str1 = "Programming";
        String str2 = "Testing";
        filterCommonCharactersAndCount(str1, str2);
    }

    public static void filterCommonCharactersAndCount(String str1, String str2) {
        boolean[] str1Characters = new boolean[256];
        boolean[] str2Characters = new boolean[256];

        for (int i = 0; i < str1.length(); i++) {
            str1Characters[str1.charAt(i)] = true;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < str2.length(); i++) {
            str2Characters[str2.charAt(i)] = true;
        }

        int commonCharactersCount = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 256; i++) {
            if (str1Characters[i] && str2Characters[i]) {
                System.out.println((char) i);
                commonCharactersCount++;
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Count of common characters: " + commonCharactersCount);
    }
}
```

In the above program, change the `str1` and `str2` variables to test with different strings. This program will print each common character and their count separately. If you want to keep track of each common character's count separately, you will need to use integer arrays instead of boolean. 

Please note that this solution assumes that the given strings contains only ASCII characters (0-255). If they can contain unicode characters, you will have to use a different data structure (like a map) that can handle a larger range of characters.
