Creating a Java program to solve for a particular equation.
Equation: x^4 + 5x^2 - 7
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Solving this equation numerically in Java can be done with either an iterative method like Newton-Raphson's method or a root-finding library like Apache Commons Math. Here is a simple example using Newton-Raphson's method:

```java
public class Main {

    private static final double EPSILON = 0.0001;

    // Function to get value of f(x) = x^4 + 5x^2 - 7
    private static double f(double x) {
        return x*x*x*x + 5*x*x - 7;
    }

    // Derivative of the above function which is 4x^3 + 10*x
    private static double g(double x) {
        return 4*x*x*x + 10*x;
    }

    private static void newtonRaphson(double x) {
        double h = f(x) / g(x);
        while (Math.abs(h) >= EPSILON) {
            h = f(x) / g(x);
            x = x - h;
        }

        System.out.print("The root is: "+ Math.round(x * 1000.0) / 1000.0);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        double x = -20;  // Initial values assumed, you may need to change for different equations
        newtonRaphson(x);
    }
}
```

Please note that this sort of equation usually doesn't have an algebraic (exact and in simple form) solution and so we use numerical methods which give approximate solutions instead. Also, be aware that this code doesn't check if the divided by zero situation occurs.

Newton-Rapshon's method for root finding might not work in some cases (e.g. stationary points, inflection points, roots of multiplicity higher than one). It's also dependent on the "initial guess" of the root value. Without more sophisticated methods or specific conditions, we cannot guarantee it will always find a root.
<|end|>


