<|asst|>
The CSS properties are incorrectly written. The correct property is `background-color`. Spelling errors are identified as incorrect HTML or CSS terminology. Also, CSS property names are case-sensitive and should be all lower case. Here is how to correct it:

```css
#foobar {
  background-color: white;
}

button {
  background-color: white;
}

.foo {
  background-color: white;
}

div {
  background-color: white;
}
```

To change background color to teal:

```css
#foobar {
  background-color: teal;
}

button {
  background-color: teal;
}

.foo {
  background-color: teal;
}

div {
  background-color: teal;
}
```

Please note that setting background color of `div` to teal will also affect any nested `div` elements. Also, this code assumes that the classes or IDs being styled are meant to be globally. 

Best practice for writing CSS would be more modular, where you don't use blanket element selectors like `button` or `div` or overarching IDs that might affect areas you don't intend to. Assign unique classes to elements you want to style, for instance `.button-teal, .div-teal`, etc. That way, you can control exactly which elements get the teal background.
