To construct a webpage as described, one can use HTML for displaying the webpage, JavaScript for sorting the array and outputting the result, and CSS for some basic styling.

Here is an example of how to achieve this:

HTML:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <style>
        .result {
            font-size: 20px;
            margin-top: 20px;
        }
    </style>
    <script src="script.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
    <div>
        <label>Input your array: </label>
        <input id="array_input" type="text" placeholder="e.g., 11,3,8,7,1,15">
        <button onclick="sortAndDisplay()">Sort and display</button>
    </div>
    <div id="result" class="result"></div>
</body>
</html>
```
Note: User should insert the numbers in the array as a comma-separated string.

And here is the JavaScript part:

JavaScript:
```javascript
function sortAndDisplay() {
    const arrayString = document.getElementById('array_input').value;
    const array = arrayString.split(',').map(Number).sort((a, b) => b - a);

    document.getElementById('result').innerHTML = 'Sorted Array: ' + array.join(', ');
}
```
This script uses built-in sort() function of JavaScript, which sorts the elements in the array in place and returns the array. The array is sorted according to the return value of the compare function provided to sort() function. Here, (a, b) => b - a is used for sorting in descending order.

You can add this JavaScript code in an external file called "script.js" or embed in the HTML file between script tags. 

Lastly, please note that in a real-world application you would potentially also want to handle invalid input, such as non-numeric values, but this is beyond the scope of this problem.
