The webpage is experiencing erratic behavior with the alignment of certain graphical elements. We believe the issue originates with six circles, specified with a diameter of 150px each, which are supposed to be arranged in a hexagonal grid pattern. However, these circles are misaligned and not creating the desired hexagonal pattern.

Given below is the CSS code currently use:

```CSS
.circle {
  width: 150px;
  height: 150px;
  background-color: blue;
  border-radius: 50%;
}
.container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
  grid-gap: 10px;
}
```

Analyze the erroneous CSS code provided, identify the mistakes, and correct them. Your modified CSS coding syntax should organize these six circles in a geometric configuration reminiscent of a hexagonal pattern. Consider any necessary adjustments to the 'grid-gap' property or introduction of new properties to achieve the desired pattern without affecting the specified diameter of the circles. Additionally, provide a brief explanation of the issues in the original CSS code and how your corrections solve them.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The erroneous CSS code provided did not account for the hexagonal pattern needed and instead, placed the circle elements in a simple grid with 3 equal fractions and a grid-gap of 10px. With this configuration, the circles will simply line up in rows, not forming the desired hexagonal pattern.

To fix this issue, you need to adjust the grid configuration and introduce pseudo-elements to create a hexagonal pattern of circles. Below is the modified CSS code:

```CSS
.circle {
   width: 150px;
   height: 150px;
   background-color: blue;
   border-radius: 50%;
}
.container {
   display: grid;
   grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 150px);
   justify-content: center;
   grid-row-gap: 75px;
   margin-top: -75px;
}
.container > :nth-child(4n+2),
.container > :nth-child(4n+3) {
   margin-top: 75px;
}
.container > :nth-child(4n+1),
.container > :nth-child(4n+4) {
   margin-top: -75px;
}
```
In the corrected code, we first set a grid with 4 equal width columns of 150px each and center contents. We then set the grid row gap as half of the circle diameter i.e. 75px.

Next, we manipulate the `margin-top` property of the circles to create the hexagonal pattern. For every 2nd and 3rd circle in each 4-circle series (0-indexed), we add a margin-top of 75px. For every 1st and 4th circle in each 4-circle series, we add a negative margin-top of -75px.

This code will generate a hexagonal grid pattern among the six circles without changing their size. It tackles the issue of incorrect grid pattern in the original code and introduces necessary pseudo-elements to fix the alignment.
<|end|>


