+----+--------+-------+---------+
| 1  | David  | 21    | USA     |
| 2  | Emily  | 17    | UK      |
| 3  | Rachel | 23    | France  |
| 4  | John   | 12    | USA     |
+----+--------+-------+---------+

'country_age_of_majority' table:

+---------+---------------+
| country | majority_age  |
+---------+---------------+
| USA     | 18            |
| UK      | 16            |
| France  | 18            |
+---------+---------------+
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can solve this problem with the SQL JOIN command to combine data from both of the tables. Here is the SQL command to solve the problem:

```sql
SELECT individuals.name
FROM individuals
JOIN country_age_of_majority
ON individuals.country = country_age_of_majority.country 
WHERE individuals.age > country_age_of_majority.majority_age
```

This command will join the 'individuals' table with the 'country_age_of_majority' table based on the 'country' column. It will then select the names of all individuals where their age is greater than the majority age of their respective countries.
<|end|>


