I want to create a simple http proxy server that does some very basic processing on the http headers (i.e. if header x == y, do z).  The server may need to support hundreds of users.  I can write the server in C# (pretty easy) or c++ (much harder).  However, would a C# version have as good of performance as a C++ version?  If not, would the difference in performance be big enough that it would not make sense to write it in C#?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
If you are proficient in both C# and C++, then it depends on your needs and situation. 

C# is much easier to write, debug, and maintain than C++. With proper coding and good memory management, a C# app can run very efficiently, especially considering it's running on the .NET runtime, which offers garbage collection and other features to help optimize program performance. 

However, C++ usually offers better performance because it allows more direct control over hardware and system level resources. It can executes closer to the hardware due to absence of the additional layers in between. So a well-written C++ program could run faster and more efficiently than a similar program written in C#.

That said, for a simple HTTP proxy server that does some basic processing on the HTTP headers, the most demanding part might be dealing with network connections rather than processing, so the advantage of the performance of C++ might not be that significant. Using C#, you can take advantage of various libraries and features that would make your life easier developing the server, such as async/await for handling potentially long IO operations, and other high level networking libraries.

Many modern high-performance server systems, including many used by massive scale companies, are written in managed, garbage collected environments - like Java and C#. Microsoft's own ASP.NET is capable of serving thousands of requests per second in C#.

So, it's not just about performance. It's about productivity, maintainability, hiring (more developers know C# than C++), etc. In your use case, unless you're targeting extremely high loads, C# should be totally fine. The best part of it, you gain speed in development and debugging phase which is very crucial. But at the end, it would be really helpful if you do a prototyping on both of them and analyze the CPU and memory usage.
<|end|>


