Can you give me a Go translation of this code?
++
vector<int> get_odd_collatz(int n){
    vector<int> odds;
    while (n != 1) {
        if (n % 2 != 0) // check if odd
        {
            odds.push_back(n);
        }
        n = n % 2 ? n*3 + 1 : n / 2; // collatz function
    }
    odds.push_back(1); // push 1 to the end of the list
    sort(odds.begin(), odds.end()); // sort the list in ascending order
    return odds;
}
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is the Go translation of the given C++ code:

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sort"
)

func getOddCollatz(n int) []int {
	var odds []int
	for n != 1 {
		if n%2 != 0 { // check if odd
			odds = append(odds, n)
		}
		if n%2 == 0 {
			n = n / 2
		} else {
			n = 3*n + 1
		} // collatz function
	}
	odds = append(odds, 1) // append 1 to the end of the list
	sort.Ints(odds) // sort the list in ascending order
	return odds
}

func main() {
	fmt.Println(getOddCollatz(10))
}
```

Note:
Go does not directly support vector like C++. Vector in C++ can be implemented as a slice in Go.
Go does not have classes, it does not support method like `push_back()` in C++. Instead, you can use `append()` function in Go for appending an item to a slice.
To sort a slice in Go, you can use `sort.Ints()` from the `sort` package.
<|end|>


