<|asst|>
Here is a basic example of a code segment in Java where we use `MessageDigest` to use SHA-1 hashing algorithm for the string "password123". 

Please note that the SHA-1 is no longer considered secure against well-funded attackers. This is a simple example and shouldn't be used for storing passwords since it's not a secure way. You should consider using stronger hash functions (e.g., SHA-256) and adding salt for storing a password or any important data.

```java
// import necessary classes
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String password = "password123";

        try {
            // Create MessageDigest instance for SHA-1
            MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-1");
            
            // Add password bytes to digest
            md.update(password.getBytes());

             // Get the hash's bytes
            byte[] bytes = md.digest();

            // This will convert the bytes to hexadecimal
            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
            for (int i = 0; i < bytes.length; i++) {
                sb.append(Integer.toString((bytes[i] & 0xff) + 0x100, 16).substring(1));
            }

            // Get complete hashed password in hexadecimal format
            String generatedPassword = sb.toString();

            System.out.println("Generated password hash: " + generatedPassword);
        } 
        catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

In this code, the MessageDigest class is used from java.security package which provides applications the functionality of a message digest algorithm, such as SHA-1. Please make sure to handle the NoSuchAlgorithmException in the method where this code is used.
<|end|>


