<|asst|>
GitHub Gist
Yes, here is the code in C++:

```cpp
#include<iostream>

int fibfib(int n) {
    if(n < 3) {
        if(n < 0) {
            return -1;
        } else {
            return (n == 2) ? 1 : 0;
        }
    }

    int a = 0;
    int b = 0;
    int c = 1;
    int d;

    for(int i = 3; i <= n; i++) {
        d = a + b + c;
        a = b;
        b = c;
        c = d;
    }

    return d;
}

int main() {
    int n;
    std::cout<<"Enter a number: ";
    std::cin>>n;
    std::cout<<fibfib(n)<<std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

Note: The code has changed from C to C++ style, using `std::cout` and `std::cin` rather than `printf` and `scanf`. Moreover, the main function has been added to make the C++ code executable. The changes do not affect the algorithm of `fibfib(int n)`.
