In the documentation bout recursive wildcards: [https://firebase.google.com/docs/firestore/security/rules-structure#recursive_wildcards](https://firebase.google.com/docs/firestore/security/rules-structure#recursive_wildcards)
We have the following:

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    // Matches any document in the cities collection as well as any document
    // in a subcollection.
    match /cities/{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if <condition>;
    }
  }
}


What is the purpose of the line `match /databases/{database}/documents {`? I thought I could only create one database per project in Firestore. Does this mean I can create database A and database B within the same project? Do I even need this line if I only want to have 1 database?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
No, currently Google Firestore only allows you to have one database per Firebase project. This is a design decision made by the Firebase team.

The line `match /databases/{database}/documents {` is not about multiple databases within a project. Rather it's part of Firestore's security rule syntax.

Firebase Firestore is designed as a multi-tenant system, which means multiple databases can exist under the Firebase platform (but still one database per project). When specifying security rules, `match /databases/{database}/documents {` is simply a pathing syntax Google enforces to ensure the following rules apply to the correct database under the Firebase platform, even though practically you can only have one Firestore database per project.

Even if you have a single database, which is the case always for Firestore at this moment, you still need to use this syntax in order to specify security rules for your Firestore database.

In summary, each Firestore project comes with a single Firestore database, however, the way rules are structured and read require this syntax to ensure accuracy and avoid potential confusion in a multi-tenant system.
<|end|>


