Develop a C++ program that not only calculates the Fibonacci sequence but also returns the index of a given number if it exists in the sequence. If the provided number is not in the Fibonacci sequence, the program should return -1. Take into account optimizing your solution for larger Fibonacci numbers for efficient computation.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
```C++
#include<iostream>
#include<vector>
#include<cmath>
using namespace std;

int fibonacciIndex(int num) {
    if (num < 0)
        return -1;

    int fib_2 = 0;  // second to last fibonacci number
    int fib_1 = 1;  // last fibonacci number
    int index = 1;

    while(true) {
        int fib = fib_1 + fib_2;

        if (num == fib)
            return index+1;
        if (fib > num) 
            return -1;

        fib_2 = fib_1;  
        fib_1 = fib;
        index++;
    }
}

int main() {
    int num;
    cout<<"Enter a number to find in Fibonacci series: ";
    cin>>num;
    int index = fibonacciIndex(num);
    if (index == -1)
        cout<< num << " is not in the Fibonacci sequence." << endl;
    else
        cout<< num << " is at index " << index << " in the Fibonacci sequence." << endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This program calculates Fibonacci numbers and keeps searching for the given number until the calculated Fibonacci number exceeds the given number. It uses a loop to generate the Fibonacci sequence. Here, `fib_2` and `fib_1` store the two preceding terms in the Fibonacci sequence, and `fib` equals their sum (i.e., the next Fibonacci number). The variable `index` stores the current index in the Fibonacci sequence. This program is efficient even for large Fibonacci numbers because it calculates the Fibonacci numbers iteratively and has constant space complexity. Also, it terminates as soon as it finds the given number or finds a Fibonacci number greater than the given number.
<|end|>


