Develop an Oracle SQL code segment that executes a calculation to pinpoint the arithmetic mean value representing the cost of an individual product.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can use the AVG() function in Oracle SQL to calculate the arithmetic mean of a column. See the example below:

Note: Replace "ProductCost" with the real name of the column that contains the cost of individual product, and replace "Products" with the real name of the table that contains your products information.

```SQL
SELECT AVG(ProductCost) AS AverageProductCost
FROM Products;
```

This script will return the arithmetic mean of all values in the "ProductCost" column from the "Products" table.

Please adjust according to your actual database schema. The result will be the average cost of an individual product. 

If you also need to count nil and amount zero records while calculating the average, you need to handle it separately as by default the AVG() function does not count NULL values. For this, you can use the NVL function:

```SQL
SELECT AVG(NVL(ProductCost, 0)) AS AverageProductCost
FROM Products;
```

The NVL function replaces null values with 0, so that they are included in the average calculation.
