I have built a basic data entry application allowing users to browse external content in iframe and enter data quickly from the same page. One of the data variables is the URL.
Ideally I would like to be able to load the iframes current url into a textbox with javascript. I realize now that this is not going to happen due to security issues.

Has anyone done anything on the server side? or know of any .Net browser in browser controls. The ultimate goal is to just give the user an easy method of extracting the url of the page they are viewing in the iframe It doesn't necessarily HAVE to be an iframe, a browser in the browser would be ideal.

Thanks,
Adam
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You're right that due to same-origin policy, you can't access the URL of a page loaded inside an iframe if it is from a different origin. The server side also isn't going to be able to help you with this as this is a client-side issue. 

There's a partial workaround, but it relies on the URLs being part of a pre-defined set known to your application:

- Instead of navigating directly to the URLs, you could navigate to a server-side script in your application that takes the destination URL as a parameter, records it as the last visited URL for that user, and then redirects to the actual URL.
- The next time you load the page containing the iframe, you could use a bit of server-side code to set the initial value of the textbox to the last visited URL for that user.

It's not the most elegant solution, but it would work as long as you're always navigating via links/redirects that you control and you're able to store and retrieve the last visited URL for each user.

As for the "browser in a browser" approach, it would have to deal with the same same-origin policy restrictions as an iframe. There are services that let you embed a "web browser" on your page, like Browserling, but these also have their limitations, the main one being that they don't give you access to the URL or any other details of the page being viewed. 

In conclusion, it would be difficult (likely impossible) to achieve exactly what you're asking for due to fundamental web security restrictions.
<|end|>


