I get 'access denied' when using a shell script in cygwin.
Using stty > /dev/ttyS6 (for COM6).
Yet python scipts work fine, no access problem.
Have checked that I am i dialup group in cygwin.
Checked permissions on /dev/ttyS5
Can't find /etc/groups to check dialup is there.
Can't see if I am in dialup.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here are a few suggestions:

1. Check if your user account has permission for the device /dev/ttyS5. You may need to add your user to the appropriate group that has access to the device.

2. If you have another program using the COM port (like putty or other), it will lock the port and not allow you to use it. Make sure that no other program is using the port.

3. If you are trying to use the stty command in a shell script, make sure that you are running your shell scipt as an administrator (or with sufficient permissions) to be able to access the serial port.

4. Verify that your COM port is working properly. You can do this by running the following command in the cygwin terminal:

```bash
dmesg | grep ttyS
```

5. Make sure your user is a part of 'tty' group as well. Check by typing `groups` in the terminal.

6. Cygwin does not use /etc/groups. Instead, it bases its permissions on your Windows user permissions. Therefore, make sure your Windows user has required permissions.

7. You can try using the 'chmod' command to change the permissions of the device file:

```bash
chmod 666 /dev/ttyS5
```

8. Try running your commands in Cygwin as administrator. Sometimes certain commands cannot execute properly without admin rights.

9. Make sure the device file for the COM port exists. You can verify this by running:

```bash
ls /dev/ttyS*
```

This should list out all serial ports.

If after trying these things, you're still running into issues, there may be another problem with your setup. Remember, always make a backup before changing permissions or other similar operations.
<|end|>


