<|user|>
Design a Fibonacci sequence generator using a unique base-64 algorithm and maintaining a 20 character alphanumeric output in Ruby.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a Ruby program that "solves" this task.

This program generates the Fibonacci sequence using Ruby's features. The Fibonacci sequence is generated up to a number that when encoded in base64 is less than 20 alphanumeric characters. Ruby's built-in function Base64 is used for the encoding.

Please kindly note that base64 symbols include a few more symbols other than alphanumeric. Hence you might see a few "+", "/" or "==" in the generated sequences. This can't be avoided with your conditions as base64 inherently uses these symbols. 

```ruby
require 'base64'

def generate_fibonacci_base64
  fib_seq = [1, 1]
  
  while (base64_length = Base64.encode64(fib_seq[-1].to_s).strip.length) < 20
    fib_seq << fib_seq[-1] + fib_seq[-2]
  end
  
  fib_seq.pop while Base64.encode64(fib_seq[-1].to_s).strip.length > 20
  fib_seq.map { |num| Base64.encode64(num.to_s).strip }
end

generate_fibonacci_base64.each do |base64_num|
  puts base64_num
end
```
In the code:

1) 'base64' library is included to use the Base64 features.
2) A function 'generate_fibonacci_base64' is defined. 
3) We're starting the fibonacci sequence with [1, 1]. We then enter a loop, where for each iteration we're appending to the 'fib_seq' array the sum of the last 2 elements.
4) The loop will continue running until the last element, converted to base64, is of length 20 or more (break condition).
5) After the we break out of the loop, we are calling "pop" on the 'fib_seq' array while the base64 converted last element has length > 20. This will remove the last element of the array unless it fits the length requirement.
6) At the end, we're mapping through the final array and converting each number to a base64 string.
7) The function 'generate_fibonacci_base64' is then called and for each element returned, that string is printed.
<|end|>


