A rectangle has sides that measure 18 cm and 30 cm. The length is increased by 15% and the breadth decreased by 10%. What is the new area of the rectangle? Incorporate edge case handling for negative percentage values.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The original area of the rectangle is 18 cm * 30 cm = 540 cm^2.

If the length is increased by 15%, the new length is 18 cm * (1 + 15/100) = 20.7 cm.

If the breadth is decreased by 10%, the new breadth is 30 cm * (1 - 10/100) = 27 cm.

Thus, the new area of the rectangle equals 20.7 cm * 27 cm = 559.9 cm^2.

However, it's logical that any decrease in percentage exceeds 100%. Similarly, an increase in percentage below -100% doesn't make any sense either. The reason is simple: you can't decrease a value by more than 100% because it would mean attaining a negative value which is unrealistic for physical quantities like length or breadth. And if you say that you're increasing a value by less than -100%, it means you're actually decreasing it, and again, we land at the point mentioned above.

So, if any such values are encountered, they should be treated as incorrect inputs. An easy way to handle it programmatically might be to put conditions before calculating the new length and breadth to check if the increase or decrease percentages fall inside the realistic range (-100%, 100%) and proceed only if the condition is true. Otherwise, an error message can be displayed to the user. Or instead of an error message, you can automatically set any decrease greater than 100% to be 100%, and any increase less than -100% to be -100%, therefore avoiding negative dimensions.
<|end|>


