Design a scalable, serverless model server on AWS Lambda to serve multiple machine learning models. These models must have different input requirements and should be dynamically allocated based on the request. The server should not only be able to support version control for various models and their efficient updates, but also rollbacks in case of faulty updates. 

Your task also includes integration of exception handling, implementing comprehensive logging detailing each step of the data processing and model serving pipeline. You must also automate anomaly detection and setup alerting parameters in these logs for any potential issues in real-time.

The solution must be securely designed to avoid unauthorized access to the model or its data. This includes managing identity and access management (IAM), implementing secure secret management, and enabling encryption for data at rest as well as in transit.

It it highly recommended that you show how you would avoid overfitting during model training in the face of imbalance or sparse data. 

The solution should be given with Infrastructure as Code (IaC) using AWS CloudFormation or the Serverless Framework. Make sure to include scaling policies and cost optimization measures into your infrastructure design. Provide explanation of all the components and decision-making process involved in your design. 

Additional requirements include:
- Ability to efficiently handle high throughput without impacting latency.
- Model evaluation and monitoring strategy for ongoing model performance and drift detection.
- Integration of AWS Lambda with other services like AWS API Gateway, AWS S3, AWS SNS etc.
- Capability to perform A/B testing for different models.
- Write effective tests for your AWS Lambda functions and provide a testing strategy. 

You are expected to provide a thorough design document guided by best practices, focusing on scalability, efficiency, cost-effectiveness, and security.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This is a broad and complex question, calling for deep expertise and experience in AWS services, Machine Learning, and Infrastructure as Code. A detailed solution would span several pages. However, here's a high-level solution:

We will need:

1. AWS S3: used to store models and data.
2. AWS Lambda: This is the core of our solution. Serve models and load them dynamically based on the request.
3. AWS API Gateway: to expose a REST API that applications can use.
4. AWS IAM: To handle authorization and identify validation.
5.BlazingText from AWS Sagemaker to solve the overfitting problem and handle imbalance or sparse data.
6. AWS SNS (Simple Notification Service) for real-time anomaly detection alerts.
7. AWS CloudTrail and AWS CloudWatch for logging.
8. AWS KMS (Key Management Service): For encryption, decryption and control access to data.

We start by storing all versions of models on S3 with the correct versioning policy. AWS Lambda would then load models dynamically from S3. 

Identity and Access Management (IAM) roles would be created for Lambda, API Gateway and S3 to give minimum privileges necessary (principle of least privilege), thus, enhancing security. 

A Lambda Layer can be used for common dependencies across models. It will help us to reduce deployment package size.

For version control, we can implement a system where each version of the model has a unique name and is stored separately. Any update will not overwrite the existing model but instead, a new model version will be created. 

Amazon CloudWatch Logs can be used for logging where each step of the model serving pipeline and data processing is logged including any errors. AWS CloudTrail can be used for auditing purpose.

Anomaly detection and alerting can be addressed by AWS CloudWatch. Metrics can be established with CloudWatch logs and alerts sent via SNS.

Serverless Framework or AWS CloudFormation will be used to create IaC. This will make it easy to replicate the infrastructure across different AWS accounts or regions.

For efficiency during high throughput, you could employ the throttling property of AWS Lambda and as well as the asynchronous invocation of Lambda to queue requests and handle them systematically to avoid overloading Lambda. 

For model performance evaluation and drift detection, set up CloudWatch monitoring on the Lambda invocations, error rates, and latency so you can track how well your model and infrastructure are performing over time.

If you want to perform A/B testing for different models, consider using AWS Lambda Aliases and Versions. You can have different versions of the same function and use aliases to route different percentages of traffic to different versions.

The Serverless Framework or AWS CloudFormation has built-in support for scaling configuration and cost optimization.

For secure secret management, we can use AWS Secrets Manager or Parameter Store.

For testing, leverage the Serverless Framework's or AWS SDK's integration with existing testing tools like Mocha and Chai for unit testing and integration testing. Write tests that verify your function's behavior, using your function's CloudFormation stack outputs to write meaningful assertions. 

Lastly, all data at rest (in S3) and in transit between Lambda and API Gateway/S3/SNS/CloudWatch will be encrypted using AWS KMS.

Please note that this is a high-level overview. A full blown solution would require detailed understanding of the application and actual ML Models requirements.
<|end|>


