In the context of Java programming, please write a method that removes an element from a singly linked list data structure. The method should also handle exceptions for cases where the element to be removed does not exist in the list. You will be provided a piece of code below:

```
public class Node {
    int data;
    Node next;

    public Node(int data) {
        this.data = data;
    }
}

public class LinkedList {
    Node head;

    public void remove(int data) {
         //Your code goes here
    }
}
```

Based on the above code, write down the implementation of the remove method. Additionally, elaborate the step-by-step process when writing the code. Also, implement a solution using an iterative approach specifically.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The remove method implementation:

```java
public void remove(int data) {
    //If the list is empty, we cannot remove any element, hence we throw an exception
    if (head == null) {
        throw new RuntimeException("Cannot remove from empty list");
    }
    //If the head node is the one to be removed
    if (head.data == data) {
        head = head.next;
        return;
    }
    Node current = head;
    while (current.next != null) {
        //If the node next to current node is the one to be removed
        if (current.next.data == data) {
            current.next = current.next.next;
            return;
        }
        current = current.next;
    }
    //If value not found in list, throw exception.
    throw new RuntimeException("Cannot find element in list");
}
```

## Step-by-step process:

1. Check if the head of the linked list is null. If so, the list is empty, and removing an element is impossible, so we throw a RuntimeException.

2. If the head of the list contains the data to be removed, reassign head to the second node (i.e., head.next). The first node is no longer referenced and will be garbage-collected.

3. If the data to be removed is not in the head, initiate a "current" node starting from the head of the list. This node will be used to traverse the list.

4. Traverse the list by continuously reassigned "current" to the next node. During traversal, always checking if the next node contains the data to be removed.

5. If a node containing the data is found (i.e., current.next.data == data), skip over this node by linking its predecessor with its successor. This removes the node from the list by severing all links to/from it, causing it to be garbage-collected.

6. Traverse the list until reaching the end (i.e., current.next == null). If the data was not found in the list, throw a RuntimeException.

7. The removal is now completed. The method does not return any value (it's void) because it modifies the list directly.
<|end|>


