In order to morph a shape in an SVG file, you have to understand the anatomy of an SVG. The "path" typically defines the shape of your object. Ideally, you will want to start with two SVGs where the start shape and end shape have the same number of points for a smooth transition.

As we see in this case, the shape is defined in this specific path:

```svg
<path d="M2 4C2 2.89543 2.89543 2 4 2H28C29.1046 2 30 2.89543 30 4V28C30 29.1046 29.1046 30 28 30H4C2.89543 30 2 29.1046 2 28V4Z" fill="url(#paint0_linear_18590_3220)"/>
```

This is a quite complex path based on Bezier curves and lines to create a rectangular shape. To morph it into another shape, the easiest way you can do is to replace this path with another SVG's path data, which represents the shape you want to morph to.
  
If you want to do it programmatically, libraries such as MorphSVGPlugin by GSAP or anime.js can help to animate and morph an SVG. It typically works by changing the `d` attribute of the path over time.

However, bear in mind that SVG transformations can become complex depending on how different the start and end shapes are. Changes are easier if the two shapes have a similar number of points. For complex shapes you might need specialized software like Inkscape, Adobe Illustrator or an online tool like Shape Shifter by Alex Lockwood.
<|end|>


