<|user|>
In our SpringBoot application we use Stored Procedures and Spring.datasource.hikari.data-source-properties.queryTimeOut is set to 60 seconds
But after the queryTimeout the Application receives timeout exception but still the SP continues to run in the DB.
How to terminate the SQL execution once the timeout occurs
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You are currently setting the `queryTimeout` property of your datasource, which means that your application will throw a `SQLTimeoutException` if the SQL command takes longer than the specified timeout. But this property doesn't cancel the DB operation, it means that client (your application) stops waiting for the response but on the DB server side the operation still continues.

To prevent this behavior you may need to signal the database to cancel the operation if the client has stopped waiting.

The cancellation of the operation is highly dependent on the Database and the JDBC driver that you are using. Also note that not all JDBC drivers support query cancellation.

However, for an example if you are using `Spring JdbcTemplate`, you can use the `JdbcTemplate` execute methods that take a `StatementCallback` argument.

This could be something like:

```java
jdbcTemplate.execute(new StatementCallback<Object>() {
    public Object doInStatement(Statement stmt) throws SQLException {
        stmt.setQueryTimeout(60); //In case of using HikariCP, this line may not be needed as it overrides the conf from the datasource.
        return stmt.execute(/*SP call*/);
    }
});
```
This way, `Spring` should call `cancel` method of `Statement` when a timeout happens. But again, the JDBC driver needs to support this, otherwise nothing will happen. And even if it does, it depends on the database whether it will actually stop executing the statement. 

In conclusion, even if the proper steps are taken on the application side, the database must also cooperate otherwise the statement will keep executing. Make sure you understand the implications, advantages and limitations of `setQueryTimeout` / `cancel`.
<|end|>


