I am looking to write some C# code for linux/windows/mac/any other platform, and am looking for best practices for portable code.

Project [mono](http://go-mono.org) has some great [porting](http://www.mono-project.com/Category:Porting) resources.

What are the best practices for portable C#?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
1. **Avoid platform-specific libraries**: Stick to .NET Standard libraries as much as possible to ensure compatibility across multiple platforms. 

2. **Avoid P/Invoke**: Platform Invoke (P/Invoke) is a technique of invoking OS-specific APIs, and this makes the code non-portable.

3. **Use conditional compilation**: In cases where you must use platform-specific code, use conditional compilation directives to compile the right code block for each platform

4. **Cross-platform paths**: Use `Path.Combine` instead of hardcoding path delimiters.

5. **Prefer built-in types**: Always prefer built-in types over types provided by a specific framework.

6. **Platform-agnostic packets**: Use NuGet packages which are platform-agnostic.

7. **Avoid hard-coding conventions**: Different OSes have different conventions, like line endings, environment variables, etc. Avoid hardcoding these conventions.

8. **Dispose properly**: Dispose of your system resources properly so that system resources do not leak. 

9. **Avoid platform specific coding styles**: Always use { } for if statement instead of relying on indentation which may change across different IDEs

10. **Use a Cross-Platform UI Framework**: If your application has a graphical user interface, you might need to ensure that your UI library supports all the desired platforms. Libraries like Xamarin.Forms, Uno Platform, or Avalonia can help in creating cross-platform UIs.

11. **Testing**: Last, but most important, test your application thoroughly on all platforms. 

These practices will help you ensure that your C# code is portable and runs without modifications on all major platforms.
<|end|>


