Enhance the efficiency of the ensuing C code, ensuring it executes at a swifter pace, while preserving its original functionality. Your task involves multi-step reasoning: identify potential bottlenecks, propose modifications, and explain how these changes improve performance.
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;

int main() {
 int i, j;
 int sum = 0;
 int a[1000][1000];

 for (i = 0; i &lt; 1000; i++) {
  for (j = 0; j &lt; 1000; j++)
   a[i][j] = i + j;
 }

 for (i = 0; i &lt; 1000; i++) {
  for (j = 0; j &lt; 1000; j++)
   sum += a[i][j];
 }

 printf(&#34;Sum=%d&#34;, sum);
 return 0;
}
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here are some optimization steps in C:

1. Optimize array access: Arrays in C are row-major. This means that consecutive elements of the rows of the array are contiguous in memory. Currently, the code is traversing the array in column order, which can lead to poor cache utilization. By traversing the array in row order instead, we can better utilize cache locality and potentially achieve a significant speedup.

2. Integer values i and j might be made unsigned, freeing up one bit for larger numbers and iterating over larger arrays.

3. It may be possible to combine the two nested loops into one, calculating and accumulating the sum at the same time as the array cells are initialized. This would reduce the number of array accesses, which could potentially be expensive in terms of memory latency.

Here's how the optimized code might look:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
 unsigned int i, j;
 int sum = 0;

 for (i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
  for (j = 0; j < 1000; j++) {
   sum += i + j;
  }
 }
 printf("Sum=%d", sum);
 return 0;  
}
```

Note that these suggestions are only potentially able to optimize the program, and actual performance will depend on the architecture of the specific machine the code is running on.
<|end|>


