When trying to enter a SQL query with parameters using the Oracle OLE DB provider I get the following error:

> Parameters cannot be extracted from the SQL command. The provider might not help to parse parameter information from the command. In that case, use the "SQL command from variable" access mode, in which the entire SQL command is stored in a variable.
  ADDITIONAL INFORMATION:
  Provider cannot derive parameter information and SetParameterInfo has not been called. (Microsoft OLE DB Provider for Oracle) 

I have tried following the suggestion here but don't quite understand what is required:[Parameterized queries against Oracle](http://microsoftdw.blogspot.com/2005/11/parameterized-queries-against-oracle.html)

Any ideas?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The error message suggests that the Microsoft OLE DB Provider for Oracle cannot parse the parameter information from the SQL command. This could be due to syntax problem or provider limitation. 

One suggested workaround is to use the "SQL command from variable" access mode. In this mode, you define the entire SQL command, including parameters, in a variable. This way, the provider does not need to parse the command or extract parameters from it.

Here are the steps to create and use a SQL command from a variable:

1. Create a new variable. Navigate to the Variables pane in your SSIS package, and select "Add Variable". 

2. In the 'Properties' pane for the new variable, set the 'Data Type' to 'String'. Then, enter your SQL command as the 'Value'.

3. Now, in your Data Flow Task, instead of entering the SQL Command directly in the OLE DB Source, select 'SQL command from variable' as the Data access mode. In the 'Variable Name' dropdown, select the variable you just created.

If you still want to use parameterized queries, please ensure you're using the right parametrization syntax that Oracle OLE DB Provider understands. Use "?" for placeholders in the query and make sure your parameters are provided in the right order.

Remember that not all OLE DB providers support parameterized queries. If you continue to experience issues, it may be worth considering moving to the Oracle .NET Provider for Oracle, which has more robust support for parameterized queries.
<|end|>


