Design and write an SQL query to delete a row in table "book" with an id of "100", under the condition that the row must have an "is_available" column set to "1" (which implies that the book is available) and the publication year of the book must be greater than "2010". Additionally, after deleting the desired row from the "book" table, also delete any associated records from the "author_book" table where the "book_id" matches "100".
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Assuming that you're working with SQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL, or some similar system with some standard constraints, here's one way you might write your SQL commands:

```sql
-- Delete from "book" table
DELETE FROM book 
WHERE id = 100 
AND is_available = 1 
AND publication_year > 2010;

-- Delete from "author_book" table
DELETE FROM author_book 
WHERE book_id = 100;
```

This first command manages the conditionally removing of the row of interest from the "book" table. It only does so for the record that all of id is 100, is_available is 1, and the publication_year is greater than 2010.

The second command is fairly straightforward -- it just removes all rows from the "author_book" table where book_id is equal to 100.

Always remember that deletion is permanent and not undo-able in most SQL systems, so use these commands wisely!
<|end|>


