Compose a JavaScript function that takes in an array of N integers. This function should not only identify the smallest integer, but also count the frequency of this smallest integer throughout the array. It should then return this smallest integer and its frequency as an object. Error handling should be included to account for instances where the array is empty or doesn't contain any integer values. Consider optimizing your function for efficiency in terms of time complexity.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a JavaScript function that accomplishes the mentioned requirements:

```Javascript
function countSmallInt(arr) {
  // error handling for empty array or non-integer values
  if (!Array.isArray(arr) || arr.length === 0 || !arr.every(Number.isInteger)) {
    throw new Error('Input should be an array of integers.')
  }

  // initialize smallest integer and its frequency
  let smallest = arr[0];
  let frequency = 0;
 
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (arr[i] < smallest) {
      smallest = arr[i];
      frequency = 1;
    } else if (arr[i] === smallest) {
      frequency++;
    }
  }
  
  return {"smallestInt": smallest, "frequency": frequency};
}

```
The above JavaScript function takes an array of integers as input, then iterates over the whole array, identify the smallest number and count the frequency of that number. If the array is empty or contains non-integer values, an error will be thrown.

The time complexity of this function is O(N), where N is the length of the array. This is because each item of the array has to be visited at least once to determine the smallest integer and its frequency.
This solution is highly optimized in terms of time complexity as it only requires a single iteration through the array.
<|end|>


