show me the syntax for this in php:
import java.util.*;
import java.lang.*;

class Solution {
    public boolean isHappy(String s) {
        if (s.length() < 3) {
            return false; // less than three letters
        }
        
        String firstSequence = s.substring(0, 3);
        
        if (!areAllCharsUnique(firstSequence)) {
            return false; // first three letters are not distinct
        }

        if (s.length() % 3 != 0) {
            return false; // length not multiple of 3, cannot be happy
        }
        
        for (int i = 3; i < s.length(); i += 3) {
            String sequence = s.substring(i, i + 3);
            if (!areAllCharsUnique(sequence) || !sequence.equals(firstSequence)) {
                return false; // a sequence has same letter or different sequence
            }
        }

        return true; // passed all tests, string is happy
    }

    public static boolean areAllCharsUnique(String str) {
        Set<Character> set = new HashSet<>();
        for (char c : str.toCharArray()) {
            if (!set.add(c)) {
                return false; // repeated character, not unique
            }
        }
        return true; // all characters are unique
    }
}
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's the equivalent PHP code for your Java class:

```php
<?php

class Solution {
    public function isHappy($s) {
        if (strlen($s) < 3) {
            return false; // less than three letters
        }
        
        $firstSequence = substr($s, 0, 3);
        
        if (!$this->areAllCharsUnique($firstSequence)) {
            return false; // first three letters are not distinct
        }

        if (strlen($s) % 3 != 0) {
            return false; // length not multiple of 3, cannot be happy
        }
        
        for ($i = 3; $i < strlen($s); $i += 3) {
            $sequence = substr($s, $i, $i + 3);
            if (!$this->areAllCharsUnique($sequence) || $sequence != $firstSequence) {
                return false; // a sequence has same letter or different sequence
            }
        }

        return true; // passed all tests, string is happy
    }

    public function areAllCharsUnique($str) {
        $chars = str_split($str);
        $set = array_unique($chars);
        if (count($set) != strlen($str)) {
            return false; // repeated character, not unique
        }
        return true; // all characters are unique
    }
}
?>
```
Please notice that in PHP counting starts from index zero, so it is not necessary to subtract one from the ending index in substring operations. Therefore, in substr($s, $i, $i + 3), the 3 doesn't need to be decreased by 1 which is a common practice in languages like Java.
<|end|>


