Devise a procedural, algorithmic framework aimed at diligently discerning and evaluating the disparities within two separate declarations, producing a systematic compilation of unique, non-duplicate terminology present exclusively within each individual context. 
sentence1 = "The blue whale sprints towards the horizon"
sentence2 = "The cheetah dashes across the savannah"
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can solve this problem using Python programming language by converting the sentences into sets, after cleaning and tokenizing them. And then you can simply do operations like set difference to get the unique words present in each sentence. Here is an example of how to do that:

```python
# Define the sentences
sentence1 = "The blue whale sprints towards the horizon"
sentence2 = "The cheetah dashes across the savannah"

# Tokenize the sentences into words and convert them to sets
set1 = set(sentence1.lower().split())
set2 = set(sentence2.lower().split())

# Get the unique words in each sentence
unique_in_sentence1 = set1 - set2
unique_in_sentence2 = set2 - set1

print("Unique words in sentence 1:", unique_in_sentence1)
print("Unique words in sentence 2:", unique_in_sentence2)
```

In this Python script, first, we define the sentences. Then, we convert the sentences into a set of words (tokens) by using the `split()` function, which splits a sentence into words based on spaces, and the `set()` function, which creates a set of the words. We also convert all words into lowercase to make the comparison case-insensitive. 

Finally, we compute the set difference between the sets of words (i.e., we find words that are in one set but not in the other). The result is the unique words in each sentence. We print both sets of unique words to the console.
<|end|>


