Construct a web-based platform utilizing the Ruby on Rails framework. This platform should encompass a landing page, an administrative interface for the regulation of user profiles, and a dedicated webpage for the generation and modification of blog entries.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Creating a web-based platform using Ruby on Rails requires a basic understanding of Ruby programming and a thorough understanding of the Rails framework. Here's a step-by-step guide to building the requested platform:

1. Initial Development Environment Setup:
   Before you start coding, you'll need to have Ruby and Rails installed on your PC. I would recommend using the Ruby Version Manager (RVM) for this purpose as it makes managing multiple Ruby versions easier.

2. Create New Rails Project:
   Navigate to your workspace from the terminal and type the following commands to create a new Rails app:
   
    ```bash
    rails new BlogPlatform
    cd BlogPlatform
    ```

3. Database Setup:
   Rails comes with SQLite by default, but you can use any database. To set up a database, go to 'config/database.yml' and update it with your database information.

4. Generate Rails Scaffold:
   A scaffold in Rails is a full set of model, database migration, controller, views and tests. Use the following command to generate the scaffold:
   
    ```bash
    rails generate scaffold Blog title:string content:text 
    rails db:migrate
    ```

5. User Model and Authentication:
    Use Devise gem for user management and authentication. Add the gem to your Gemfile then bundle install. Generate Devise models for User and run migration.

    ```bash
    gem 'devise'
    bundle install
    rails generate devise:install
    rails generate devise User
    rails db:migrate
    ```

6. Create Home Page:
    You can create the home page by updating your routes in 'config/routes.rb' file and creating appropriate action in Application controller:
   
    ```ruby
    root to: "application#home"
    ```

7. Create Administrative Interface:
   Active admin gem is a good choice for easy admin interface. Add gem to your Gemfile and bundle install.

    ```bash
    gem 'activeadmin'
    bundle install
    rails generate active_admin:install
    rails db:migrate
    ```
   
   To customize the user interface and user control, delve into the 'app/admin' directory.
   
8. Blog Entries Setup:
    Use the previously generated scaffold for Blog. You can now create, modify and delete a blog entry.

9. Polish the UI:
   To enhance the UI, add Bootstrap to your application. Add 'gem bootstrap' into Gemfile, then run bundle install. 

Once you've completed the above steps, you should have a functioning Ruby on Rails web-based platform. Always note that this is a barebones, basic setup. For a production-ready application, a lot more considerations need to be put into security, error handling, and performance. Remember to utilize suggested RoR best practices and use automated testing to ensure your code works as intended.
