I looking into [Purgecss webpack docs](https://purgecss.com/plugins/webpack.html) and I'm a bit confused.
Why do I need to specify `paths` in the plugin options?

new PurgeCSSPlugin({
      paths: glob.sync(`${PATHS.src}/**/*`, { nodir: true }),
    }),


Or in postcss:

purgecss({
    content: ['./src/**/*.html']
 })


By do this, I tell purgecss look into those HTML files, and that javascript files.
But when I using webpack it have all the source files that app needs. so Purgecss can't able to looking into HTML or js that it bundle?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
PurgeCSS works by comparing your HTML (and JavaScript) files with your CSS files to determine which CSS classes are actually being used in your HTML and JavaScript, and then removes any unused CSS from your bundled CSS files.

Even though Webpack indeed knows all of your application files, PurgeCSS itself needs to be explicitly told what values to look for. For doing this, you pass the `paths` option to the PurgeCSS plugin. This property contains a list of all the files in your project that you want PurgeCSS to analyze for used CSS selectors.

So basically, PurgeCSS is looking for a list of files to analyze and that's why you provide it with a list of file paths. It uses this to search for any classes, id's, or html tags that are used in your project. When it doesn't find a css class in the list of files you provided, this css class is considered as "unused" and therefore removed from your final, production CSS files to make them lighter.

While Webpack manages and processes files, it does not check which classes are used across your CSS and HTML/JS. That's where PurgeCSS comes in - it cross references the classes used in your JS/HTML with your CSS to strip out any unnecessary styles.
<|end|>


