Problem:
I have the summary statistics of two different samples, specifically the median, interquartile range (IQR), and the number of observations (n). I want to perform a non-parametric Mann-Whitney U test considering the number of observations. I need help in calculating the U statistic.
A:
&lt;code&gt;
import numpy as np
import scipy.stats
amedian = 5.6
aiqr = 2.3
anobs = 35
bmedian = 7.2
biqr = 3.1
bnobs = 45
&lt;/code&gt;
U_statistic = ... # put solution in this variable
BEGIN SOLUTION
&lt;code&gt;
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Unfortunately, it is not possible to calculate the Mann-Whitney U-statistic using just the median, interquartile range (IQR), and number of observations. The Mann-Whitney U test requires the actual data points from both samples, not just the summary statistics. 

The main reason for this is that the U-statistic is calculated by comparing each observation from one sample with each observation from the other sample, which you can't do with just the summary values.

The only solution would be to obtain the raw data for both sets and then perform the Mann-Whitney U test. 

Here's an example of how you would conduct this test if you had the full data:

```python
from scipy.stats import mannwhitneyu

# assuming data_a and data_b are numpy arrays containing your data points
u_statistic, p_value = mannwhitneyu(data_a, data_b)

print('U-Statistic:', u_statistic)
print('P value:', p_value)
```
<|end|>


