Create a full-featured browser application in JavaScript that not only toggles a dark-mode but also switches other UI elements according to the chosen mode. Additionally, allow for local storage of user preferences so the user's mode choice is remembered for future browsing sessions. Implement this while ensuring best performance and accessibility.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Creating a full-featured browser application with dark-mode toggle, other UI switches, and local storage preferences is a quite broad task that would need a robust knowledge of JavaScript, HTML, CSS, and web storage API. Though the full application cannot be demonstrated here due to its complexity, I will give you a simple starting point for implementing a dark mode toggle and storing user preferences using local storage.

HTML:
```html
<button id="toggleBtn">Toggle Dark Mode</button>
```

CSS:
```css
body {
  transition: background-color 0.5s ease;
}

body.light-mode {
  background-color: #fff;
  color: #000;
}

body.dark-mode {
  background-color: #000;
  color: #fff;
}
```

JavaScript:
```js
window.onload = function() {
  // Check if a mode is saved in local storage
  if (localStorage.getItem('mode') === 'dark') {
    document.body.classList.add('dark-mode');
  } else {
    document.body.classList.add('light-mode');
  }
  
  // Get our button element
  var button = document.getElementById('toggleBtn');
  // Add a click event listener
  button.addEventListener('click', function() {
    // If dark mode is on...
    if (document.body.classList.contains('dark-mode')) {
      // Turn it off
      document.body.classList.remove('dark-mode');
      document.body.classList.add('light-mode');
      // And save the preference to local storage
      localStorage.setItem('mode', 'light');
    } else {
       // Otherwise turn dark mode on
      document.body.classList.remove('light-mode');
      document.body.classList.add('dark-mode');
      // And save the preference to local storage
      localStorage.setItem('mode', 'dark');
    }
  });
}
```

In a comprehensive implementation, you'd want to consider dividing your JavaScript code into modules, implementing a proper design pattern (like MVC or MVVM), and using a CSS preprocessor (like SASS or LESS) for easier CSS development. Tabs, bookmarks, and other features of a full-featured browser application would require significant additional work. 

For accessibility, you should ensure that all elements have proper alt text, use semantic HTML5 tags for better screen reader support, and ensure color contrast is sufficient in both light and dark modes.

Remember that local storage is limited (usually a few MB), and is not a secure way to store sensitive information, as it can be accessed via the client side quite easily. It is best used for non-essential, preference-based data like this.
<|end|>


