How can I use sed command to replace a specific line in a text file?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can use `sed` command for replacing a specific line in a text file by referring to line number and using the `s/old-text/new-text/` syntax. Here is a simple example:

```bash
sed '3 s/foo/bar/' filename
```

This command replaces 'foo' with 'bar' in the 3rd line of the file. The changes aren't saved back to the file but they are just printed to standard output.

If you want to save the changes back to the file, you can use `-i` option:

```bash
sed -i '3 s/foo/bar/' filename
```

This will change the 3rd line of the file "filename", replacing 'foo' with 'bar', and it will save the changes back to the file.

Note, advanced regular expressions and matching rules can also be applied in the `s/old-text/new-text/` part of the `sed` command.
<|end|>


