Should I expose a strongly typed dataset from a webservice and bind it directly in a client? or are there more sensible ways for asmx web services? I am doing CRUD operations (Create, Read, Update, Delete). 

I find working with datasets to be frustrating and difficult to work with when for example when inserting into a table within it for instance. It doesn't seem logical to ship a whole dataset to and forth when only inserting one record or when only getting one record from a particular table within the dataset.

Is there a better way?

Should I perhaps be converting to objects and use objects over the webservice? Doing conversions all over the place to get objects passed around is perhaps just as tedious?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
It's generally not considered a good practice to expose strongly typed datasets from web services. This is mainly due to the fact that datasets are a .NET specific form of data representation and can make your service harder to consume from other platforms if not impossible.

For web services, it's generally better to design your methods to accept and return simple data types or custom objects. 

One approach could be to create Data Transfer Object (DTO) classes to mimic your data sets and serialize these to XML or JSON when transmitting over the network through your service. A DTO is an object that carries data between processes. The main benefit of DTOs is that you can encapsulate the complex data structures and provide a more simplified view to the client. 

This way you can separate your service API from your internal data representation and make your service easier to use.

For general CRUD operations, the request/response would generally include objects that reflect the fields of a given item in the database. This would avoid the need to ship an entire dataset back and forth, and instead you would only be sending the data necessary for a given operation.

Moreover, with this approach, you can also have stringent control over the data and can implement validation checks before the data is sent over the network or before it gets persisted in the database.
<|end|>


