```python
# Convert the dataset to pandas DataFrame and rename the columns for convenience
df = pd.read_csv('https://public.opendatasoft.com/explore/dataset/air-passenger-traffic-in-france/download/?format=csv&timezone=Europe/Berlin&lang=en&use_labels_for_header=true&csv_separator=%3B')
df.columns = ['Airport', 'Year', 'Month', 'Freight traffic', 'Passenger Count', 'Movement Count']

# 1. Airport with the highest average monthly traffic:
average_monthly_traffic = df.groupby('Airport')['Passenger Count'].mean()
max_airport = average_monthly_traffic.idxmax()
print('Airport with the highest average monthly traffic:', max_airport)

# 2. Passenger traffic trend over the years for the top 3 busiest airports:
top3_airports = df.groupby('Airport')['Passenger Count'].sum().nlargest(3).index
for airport in top3_airports:
    subset = df[df['Airport'] == airport]
    plt.figure(figsize=(10,5))
    sns.lineplot(x='Year', y='Passenger Count', data=subset)
    plt.title(f'Passenger traffic trend for {airport}')
    plt.show()

# 3. Correlation between the year and the number of passengers:
correlation = df['Year'].corr(df['Passenger Count'])
print('Correlation between the year and the number of passengers:', correlation)

# Note: In the original question, it wasn't clear whether the analysis should be done on 'Freight traffic', 'Passenger Count', or 'Movement Count'. I assumed 'Passenger Count' based on the given code snippet.
```

I have used line plots to visualise the trends as they clearly show how the data changes over time. Correlation is represented as a number, if it is clear whether the correlation is positive or negative and how strong it is.
