I will need to use weekly ATR (ATR computed using weekly HLC data) to compute some technical indicators on a daily time scale. But instead of directly computing the weekly ATR, it is more convenient to compute daily ATR (ATR computed using daily HLC data), and the quote data are in daily time frame. Is there a straightforward relation between the two (for example, weekly ATR may be approximately daily ATR times a constant)?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
While there is no exact straightforward equation to convert daily ATR to weekly ATR, you can use an approximation that could serve your purpose. 

Average True Range (ATR) is a measure of volatility that is typically calculated over 14 periods (whether those are days, weeks, months, depends on the chart), and it takes into account any gaps in the price movement from one period to the next.

Assuming that the intraday volatility is iteratively added up without any weekend effects, you can take the square root of the number of trading days in a week (usually 5). That's because volatility (e.g., standard deviation, and likewise ATR) is root-time scalable in geometric Brownian motion (which is a common model for stock prices). Mathematically, the weekly ATR ≈ daily ATR * sqrt(5). 

Remember this is a simplification and actual results may vary. The market volatility can change significantly from day to day due to a multitude of factors. Weekly ATR calculation would naturally include these changes while our approximation method may not fully capture them. 

Also note that some people do average 5 daily ATRs to estimate a weekly ATR, but the result is not the same as a true weekly ATR. They behave similarly, but their absolute levels will be different.
<|end|>


