In C# I can use the FileSystemWatcher object to watch for a specific file and raise an event when it is created, modified, etc.

The problem I have with this class is that it raises the event the moment the file becomes created, even if the process which created the file is still in the process of writing. I have found this to be very problematic, especially if I'm trying to read something like an XML document where the file must have some structure to it which won't exist until it is completed being written.

Does .NET (preferably 2.0) have any way to raise an event after the file becomes accessible, or do I have to constantly try reading the file until it doesn't throw an exception to know it is available?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
There is no option in FileSystemWatcher to raise an event only when the file is completely written. You have to implement your own mechanism. 

A typical method to handle this scenario involves checking if the file can be opened exclusively. If not, then you can infer that the file is still being written to. Once the file can be opened exclusively, then it's safe to read from the file.

Here is sample code demonstrating this:

```csharp
public void OnChanged(object source, FileSystemEventArgs e)
{
    // Only react if the file event is a creation
    if (e.ChangeType == WatcherChangeTypes.Created)
    {
        FileInfo fileInfo = new FileInfo(e.FullPath);
        bool fileAccessible = IsFileAccessible(fileInfo);

        while (!fileAccessible)
        {
            Thread.Sleep(500); // Wait before retrying
            fileAccessible = IsFileAccessible(fileInfo);
        }

        // The file is now available for reading
    }
}

private bool IsFileAccessible(FileInfo file)
{
    FileStream stream = null;

    try
    {
        stream = file.Open(FileMode.Open, FileAccess.ReadWrite, FileShare.None);
    }
    catch (IOException)
    {
        // The file is unavailable because it is:
        // still being written to
        // or being processed by another thread
        // or does not exist (has been deleted)
        return false;
    }
    finally
    {
        if (stream != null)
        {
            stream.Close();
        }
    }

    // File is available
    return true;
}
```

In this approach, a small pause has been introduced between retries, this can prevent a lot of unnecessary CPU usage. Also, you may want to limit the number of retries or introduce a timeout, so you are not waiting indefinitely for the file to become accessible.
<|end|>


