I'm confronted with a challenge: Substituting Employee ID with a Distinct Identifier. The details are as follows: SQL Schema.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
If you want to replace your existing Employee ID field with a distinct identifier, this can be achieved through using the `ALTER TABLE` command to add a new column, combined with the `NEWID()` function to generate a unique value for each record (assuming you're using Microsoft SQL Server).

Here is an example SQL script that performs these changes:

```sql
-- Add a new column for the unique identifier
ALTER TABLE YourTableName
ADD NewEmployeeID uniqueidentifier;

-- Populate the new column with unique values for each record
UPDATE YourTableName
SET NewEmployeeID = NEWID();

-- If you want to remove the old column after you've confirmed the new ones are correct
-- ALTER TABLE YourTableName
-- DROP COLUMN EmployeeID;

-- If you want to rename the new column to the old column name
-- EXEC sp_rename 'YourTableName.NewEmployeeID', 'EmployeeID', 'COLUMN';
```

Please replace "YourTableName" with the name of your table. 

It's generally a good idea to not delete the old column immediately, just in case there are issues with the new one. After verifying the new column contains the expected values, you can use the commented out `DROP COLUMN` command to remove the old column.

As always, remember to backup your database before performing major schema changes.

Also, be aware that the `NEWID()` function generates uniqueidentifer of type GUID. If you need a distinct numeric identifier, please use `ROW_NUMBER()` function instead.
<|end|>


