Devise a function using C++ which identifies and brings forth the largest and smallest elements within a provided data array. Implement this without utilizing pre-existing sort function, and include functionality to handle potential duplicate elements and empty arrays.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a simple C++ function which finds the biggest and smallest elements in the array. This function uses a single pass (linear search) approach to find these elements. It also handles the case when array is empty:

```cpp
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

void findMinMax(int arr[], int len, int& minVal, int& maxVal) {
    if(len == 0) {
        cout << "Array is empty." << endl;
        return;
    }
    minVal = arr[0];
    maxVal = arr[0];
    for(int i=1; i<len; i++){
        if(arr[i] < minVal) 
            minVal = arr[i];
        if(arr[i] > maxVal)
            maxVal = arr[i];
    }
}

int main() {
    int arr[] = {3, 1, 2, 5, 4, 7, 6, 9, 8, 0};
    int minVal, maxVal;
    findMinMax(arr, sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]), minVal, maxVal);
    
    cout << "Minimum value in the array is " << minVal << endl;
    cout << "Maximum value in the array is " << maxVal << endl;

    return 0;
}
```
This program first declares and initializes an array, then it calls the findMinMax function passing array, its length and two integer variables minVal and maxVal to store the minimum and maximum values. If the array is empty, i.e., its length is zero, the function prints a message and returns immediately. Otherwise, it assumes the first element as the minimum and maximum and then processes each remaining element once comparing it with minVal and maxVal.

After calling the findMinMax function, the program prints the minimum and maximum elements in the array.
<|end|>


