Using toad and an oracle database, how can I call a sp and see the results by passing an array to one of the parameters of the sp?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Unfortunately, Oracle doesn't support passing an array from SQL or from a program (like toad) directly to a stored procedure. Here's one way you might work around it:

1.Create an Oracle type (an array type) that you'll use to pass data.

```sql
CREATE OR REPLACE TYPE number_array AS VARRAY(1000) OF NUMBER;
```

2.Use the type in your stored procedure.

```sql
CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE myProc(
    p_array IN number_array,
    p_result OUT SYS_REFCURSOR)
IS
BEGIN
    -- Your procedure body here
    OPEN p_result FOR
    SELECT column FROM table WHERE id IN (SELECT * FROM TABLE(p_array));
END;
```

3.Then from Toad you can create an anonymous PL/SQL block that creates the array, calls the procedure, and fetches from the ref cursor.

```sql
DECLARE
  l_array number_array := number_array(101, 102, 103);
  l_result SYS_REFCURSOR;
BEGIN
  myProc(l_array, l_result);
  -- Fetch from the cursor and display the result
  -- This only works if your procedure is returning one value.
  -- If it's more than one value, use BULK COLLECT
  LOOP
    FETCH l_result INTO l_var;
    EXIT WHEN l_result%NOTFOUND;
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Result: ' || l_var);
  END LOOP;
  CLOSE l_result;
END;
```

4.You need to enable output in Toad to see the DBMS_OUTPUT result. In the DBMS Output window, click on the "green plus" button and then execute your pl/sql.

Please note that your procedure and logic may require adjustments to this base structure. This is just a generic example. Also, bear in mind that DBMS_OUTPUT has limitations and should really be used for debugging and not as a routine method of getting large amounts of data out of PL/SQL blocks or procedures.
<|end|>


