Design a Python function that effectively computes the mean value of both the three largest as well as three smallest numerical entities present within a defined listing.
list = { 14, 25, 9, 41, 33, 81, 95, 12, 59, 88, 66 }
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In Python, you can achieve this by making use of the built-in "sorted" and "sum" functions. Here's how you can do it:

```python
def get_avg_three_largest_smallest(numbers):
  sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers) # Numbers are sorted in ascending order

  # Get the three smallest numbers
  three_smallest = sorted_numbers[:3]

  # Get the three largest numbers
  three_largest = sorted_numbers[-3:] 

  # Calculate the average of the three smallest and three largest numbers
  avg_smallest = sum(three_smallest) / len(three_smallest)
  avg_largest = sum(three_largest) / len(three_largest)

  return avg_smallest, avg_largest

numbers = [14, 25, 9, 41, 33, 81, 95, 12, 59, 88, 66]
avg_smallest, avg_largest = get_avg_three_largest_smallest(numbers)

print('Average of three smallest numbers:', avg_smallest) 
print('Average of three largest numbers:', avg_largest) 
```

This function first sorts the list of numbers in an ascending order, then it calculates the mean for the three smallest numbers (the first three numbers in the sorted list) and the three largest numbers (the last three numbers in the sorted list). It then returns these two averages.
<|end|>


