Below is an example that uses jQuery and DataTables library which is compatible across different web browsers and has built-in table sorting functionality. Here is a basic outline of the steps we will follow:

1. Create an HTML document.
2. Create a JavaScript function that takes JSON data and dynamically generates a table.
3. Use DataTables library to add sorting functionality to the table.
4. Write a simple unit test using Jasmine framework to validate the correct rendering and functionality of the table.

Here is the sample code:

HTML code:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="https://cdn.datatables.net/1.10.22/css/jquery.dataTables.css">
    <script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.5.1.js"></script>
    <script src="https://cdn.datatables.net/1.10.22/js/jquery.dataTables.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
    <table id="myTable"></table>
    <script src="main.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

JS code in main.js:

```javascript
// Sample JSON data
var jsonData = [
    { "Name": "Michael", "Age": "35", "Job": "Developer" },
    { "Name": "Sarah", "Age": "42", "Job": "Designer" },
    // ...
];

$(document).ready(function() {
    // Generate the table
    var table = $('#myTable').DataTable({
        data: jsonData,
        columns: [
            { title: "Name", data: "Name" },
            { title: "Age", data: "Age" },
            { title: "Job", data: "Job" }
        ]
    });

    // Add sorting functionality
    $('#myTable').on( 'click', 'tr', function () {
        $(this).toggleClass('selected');
    });
});
```

To validate the rendering and functionality, Jasmine, a behavior-driven JavaScript framework can be used for unit testing. Here is a basic example:

```javascript
describe("DataTable Test Suite", function() {
    var table;

    beforeEach(function() {
        table = $('#myTable').DataTable();
    });

    it("should correctly render rows from JSON data", function() {
        expect(table.rows().count()).toEqual(jsonData.length);
    });

    it("should have sorting functionality", function() {
        // Assuming column 0 is 'Name'
        table.order([0, 'asc']).draw();
        expect($(table.row(0).node()).text()).toEqual(jsonData[0].Name);

        table.order([0, 'desc']).draw();
        expect($(table.row(0).node()).text()).toEqual(jsonData[jsonData.length - 1].Name);
    });
});
```

Please make sure to replace the sample JSON data with your actual data and adjust test cases according to your actual requirements. Running this HTML file in a web browser will create a data table from JSON data that can be sorted by clicking on the column headers. Jasmine tests should pass if the data is correctly rendered into the table and the sorting functionality is working properly.
