Develop a sophisticated object-oriented structure that expands on the provided prototype. Additionally, implement encapsulation, inheritance, and polymorphism in your structure along with suitable methods based on object behavior. Ensure to account for edge cases and exceptions. Here is the prototype:

```java
class Archetype {
	public String cognomen;
	public int annum;
	public String sex;
	
	public Archetype(String cognomen, int annum, String sex) {
		this.cognomen = cognomen;
		this.annum = annum;
		this.sex = sex;
	}
}
```

Furthermore, add a new class to the structure, `DerivedClass`, which takes the `Archetype` class as a base and extends it with additional attributes and methods. An example of an attribute could be `employmentStatus`, and methods could be `findJob()` or `quitJob()`. As a challenge, construct at least one method that overrides the `Archetype`'s method and alters its original behavior. Implement the use of interfaces if necessary. 

Lastly, create a main function to instantiate these classes and demonstrate their interactions and functionalities.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a simple structure that fulfils your requirement:

```java
interface Employable {
    void findJob(String job);
    void quitJob();
}

class Archetype {
    private String cognomen;
    private int annum;
    private String sex;
    
    public Archetype(String cognomen, int annum, String sex) {
        this.cognomen = cognomen;
        this.annum = annum;
        this.sex = sex;
    }

    public String getCognomen() {
        return this.cognomen;
    }

    public void setCognomen(String cognomen) {
        this.cognomen = cognomen;
    }

    public int getAnnum() {
        return this.annum;
    }

    public void setAnnum(int annum) {
        this.annum = annum;
    }

    public String getSex() {
        return this.sex;
    }

    public void setSex(String sex) {
        this.sex = sex;
    }

    public void printInfo() {
        System.out.println("Name: " + this.cognomen);
        System.out.println("Age: " + this.annum);
        System.out.println("Sex: " + this.sex);
    }
}

class DerivedClass extends Archetype implements Employable {
    private boolean employmentStatus;
    private String job;

    public DerivedClass(String cognomen, int annum, String sex) {
        super(cognomen, annum, sex);
        this.employmentStatus = false;
    }

    public void findJob(String job) {
        this.job = job;
        this.employmentStatus = true;
    }

    public void quitJob() {
        this.job = "";
        this.employmentStatus = false;
    }

    @Override
    public void printInfo() {
        super.printInfo();
        System.out.println("Employment Status: " + (this.employmentStatus ? "Employed" : "Unemployed"));
        if(this.employmentStatus) {
            System.out.println("Job: " + this.job);
        }
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        DerivedClass person = new DerivedClass("John", 25, "Male");

        person.printInfo(); // Outputs information without job
        person.findJob("Engineer"); 

        System.out.println();
        person.printInfo(); // Outputs information with job

        System.out.println();
        person.quitJob();
        person.printInfo(); // Outputs information after quitting job
    }
}
```

This solution expands on the provided `Archetype` class with access modifiers for private attribute instances and public getter and setter methods. This is a principle of encapsulation, where the `Archetype` class's internal state is being hidden from outside classes.

The class `DerivedClass` extends `Archetype` and interfaces `Employable`. It overwrites the `printInfo()` method of `Archetype`, demonstrating polymorphism, and adds the `findJob()` and `quitJob()` methods along with `employmentStatus` property according to the requirements. 

Lastly, a `main()` function is added to demonstrate these principles in action.
<|end|>


