In addition to the above, the webpage should also include a navigation bar with four different sections i.e., Home, About, Services, Contact. When a user hovers over these sections, the color of the text should change. The phrase "Hello World!" should change color every 2 seconds using JavaScript. Use external stylesheets (CSS) and scripts (JavaScript) to accomplish this task.

Furthermore, add an option to change the language of "Hello World!" to five different languages. Create and utilize JSON objects containing translation data and use jQuery to alter the text when a different language is selected. Also, ensure your webpage is accessible and meets the WCAG 2.1 AA accessibility standards.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The problem can be translated into the following files:

index.html
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
    <script src="https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/jquery/3.5.1/jquery.min.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
    <nav>
        <ul>
            <li>Home</li>
            <li>About</li>
            <li>Services</li>
            <li>Contact</li>
        </ul>
    </nav>

    <h1 id="msg">Hello World!</h1>

    <select id="lang">
        <option value="en">English</option>
        <option value="es">Spanish</option>
        <option value="fr">French</option>
        <option value="ge">German</option>
        <option value="it">Italian</option>
    </select>

    <script src="scripts.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```
styles.css
```css
body {
    background: linear-gradient(to right, yellow, orange);
    padding: 10px;
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}

nav ul {
    list-style: none;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-around;
}

nav ul li:hover {
    color: blue;
} 

h1 {
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 50px;
}
```
scripts.js
```javascript
let translations = {
    "en": "Hello World!",
    "es": "¡Hola Mundo!",
    "fr": "Bonjour le monde!",
    "ge": "Hallo Welt!",
    "it": "Ciao Mondo!"
}

const colors = ['red', 'blue', 'green', 'purple', 'pink'];
let i = 0;

setInterval(function(){
    document.getElementById('msg').style.color = colors[i % colors.length];
    i++;
}, 2000);

$('#lang').change(function() {
    $('#msg').text(translations[this.value]);
});
```
This code creates a webpage with a gradient background, a nav bar, a dynamic "Hello World" message, and a language selector. The color of the "Hello World" text changes every 2 seconds and the "Hello World" message changes to a different language every time the value of the language selector is changed.

As for WCAG 2.1 AA accessibility standards, you would need to add things like meaningful alternative text for images, labels or instructions for user input, structure the content properly, offer several ways to navigate the page, and more. These requirements depend heavily on specific content of your page, so without further information, it's impossible to fully implement it in this example.
