You are given a 2D array. Your task is to create a JavaScript function that finds the maximum value within the 2D array and also returns the indices at which this max value resides. If the largest number repeats, return the indices of all its positions.

The defined function must be concise and optimized. Additionally, for added complexity, handle potential error cases such as an empty array, incorrectly defined array (Not a 2D array), or an array that has non-numeric values. Comment your function to explain your logic and reasoning behind certain decisions. 

References, helper methods, or recursive approaches (if needed) can be used but must be explained thoroughly.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a simple JavaScript function that fulfills the outlined requirements. It initially sets the max value to negative infinity and iterates over each element of the 2D array to find the max value and its indices.

```javascript
function findMaxAndIndices(matrix) {
  // handle potential error cases
  if (!Array.isArray(matrix) || matrix.length === 0 || !Array.isArray(matrix[0])) {
    return 'Invalid input. Please provide a non-empty 2D array.';
  }

  let maxValue = -Infinity;
  let indices = [];

  // loop through 2D array
  for (let i = 0; i < matrix.length; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < matrix[i].length; j++) {
      // only consider numeric values
      if (typeof matrix[i][j] !== 'number') continue;

      if (matrix[i][j] > maxValue) {
        // if a new max value is found, update maxValue and clear indices array
        maxValue = matrix[i][j];
        indices = [[i, j]];
      } else if (matrix[i][j] === maxValue) {
        // if current value is equal to maxValue, add its indices to the array
        indices.push([i, j]);
      }
    }
  }

  // return null if no numeric value is found
  if (maxValue === -Infinity) return 'No numeric value found in the array.';

  return { maxValue, indices };
}
```

This function starts with setting the `maxValue` to `-Infinity` to ensure that it'll be updated to the first numeric value encountered in the array.

For error checking, it confirms the input is a 2D array by checking initially if it's an array, is non-empty, and its first item is also an array. Note that this basic check assumes the array is either entirely correct (is a 2D array) or not - it doesn't validate every item.

It discards non-numeric values when looking for `maxValue`, but if the matrix only has non-numeric values, it doesn't treat it as an error, instead it returns a statement indicating no numeric values were found.

When a higher value is found, it replaces `maxValue` and clears `indices`. When a value equal to the current `maxValue` is found, it adds its index to `indices`.

It finally returns the `maxValue` and `indices` as an object. If no numeric value was found, currentValue would still be -Infinity, so a different message is returned accordingly. The object format will allow easy access to the max value and its indices separately later on. The index order in the output is [rowIndex, columnIndex], which is a common way of representing matrix coordinates.
<|end|>


