Here is the C++ code that fulfills the requirement you have described:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    for(int i = 1; i <= 100; i++) {
        if(i % 3 == 0 && i % 5 == 0) {
            cout << "FizzBuzz" << endl;
        } else if(i % 3 == 0) {
            cout << "Fizz" << endl;
        } else if(i % 5 == 0) {
            cout << "Buzz" << endl;
        } else {
            cout << i << endl;
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
```

This program uses a for loop to iterate through numbers 1 to 100. Then using the modulus operator, it checks if the number is divisible by both 3 and 5, in which case it prints "FizzBuzz". If it is divisible only by 3 it prints "Fizz", and if it's divisible by 5 it prints "Buzz". If the number is not divisible by either 3 or 5, it prints the number itself. Following this, the loop moves to the next iteration until it reaches 100. 

You can run this program in any C++ environment to see the results. The program goes on checking each condition in the loop and gives the output based on the condition met.
