<|asst|>
Testing WebControls directly can be quite tricky. There are usually a couple ways you can approach this. One is to change the methods you want to test to public. If they were only put as private or protected for encapsulation, but not for security, then this can sometimes be an acceptable solution - although it can lead to other problems down the line when the "public" methods that should really be private or protected are misused.

Another approach is to use PrivateObject or PrivateAccessor, depending on what version of Visual Studio you're using. These helper classes allow you to unit test private and protected methods directly, through reflection. However, this can be complicated and messy, especially when you get into testing nested private classes or complex hierarchies of classes.

The best way to go about it is to do a little bit of design refactoring to make your code more testable. This usually involves abstracting out the parts of the code that interact with the WebControls into separate classes or methods that can be tested in isolation. 

For example, you might create a separate class that handles all the logic for your WebControl, leaving the WebControl itself very simple and straightforward. This new class would have public methods that can be tested.

One more thing you might consider is using a testing tool such as Selenium. Selenium automates browsers, and can be used for unit testing just like NUnit. This might be overkill if you're just unit testing one method at a time, but if you're doing more complex integration testing it can be a huge time saver.

Lastly, the Microsoft Fakes Framework is a relatively new library (bundled with Visual Studio 2012 and later) that provides powerful shimming and stubbing capabilities, which could potentially be used to more easily test WebControl-behavior, but it requires a different way of thinking about how to structure your tests.
<|end|>


