Is it possible to remote-debug a Visual C++ 6.0 application running on a Windows NT machine from a developer workstation running Windows XP? If so, is there a procedure written up somewhere?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, it's possible to remote-debug a Visual C++ 6.0 application running on a Windows NT machine from a developer workstation running Windows XP. Here's a general procedure:

1. Install the remote debugging components on the target machine (the Windows NT machine where your application is running). You can get these from your Visual Studio 6.0 installation CD or ISO - they're available under \common\tools\cpu.

2. On your Windows XP machine, start Visual Studio 6.0, load your project.

3. In the Debug menu, select "Remote Debug". First, set up a new "Debugging Connection" - specify the connection protocol (TCP/IP) and the name or IP address of the target machine.

4. After setting up the connection, select "Go and Attach" from the Debug menu. You will be asked to specify the path to the executable on the target machine that you want to debug.

5. Assuming all goes well, you should now be in remote debugging mode. Breakpoints, watches, etc. should all work as if you were debugging on your local machine.

6. If you're having problems, make sure the TCP/IP protocol has been installed on both machines, and that both machines can see each other on the network.

7. There could be several other issues which may arise - firewall settings may block the necessary ports, DCOM settings need to be adjusted on both machines etc. - these would be specific to your individual setup and may require additional troubleshooting.

Remember, VS 6.0 and Windows NT are quite old, so you may run into additional issues related to the age of the software.

Note: These steps are a high level guideline based on general practice from the time when these tools were new. They may not cover every single situation, as each developer workstation can have its own unique settings and infrastructure. Furthermore, the tools mentioned are quite old and may suffer compatibility issues with newer operating systems. You may have to troubleshoot to find solutions to specific technical issues. 

Also, consider upgrading your development environment and tools to more modern ones as they offer better features, support, and security improvements.
<|end|>


