Using Prototype 1.6's "new Element(...)" I am trying to create a <table> element with both a <thead> and <tbody> but nothing happens in IE6.


var tableProto = new Element('table').update('<thead><tr><th>Situation Task</th><th>Action</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a</td><td>b</td><td>c</td></tr></tbody>');



I'm then trying to inject copies of it like this:


$$('div.question').each(function(o) {
  Element.insert(o, { after:$(tableProto.cloneNode(true)) });
});



My current workaround is to create a <div> instead of a <table> element, and then "update" it with all of the table HTML.

How does one successfully do this?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Internet Explorer (especially earlier versions like IE6) have many quirks when it comes to creating and manipulating table elements with Javascript. You often cannot modify or create `tbody`, `thead`, or `tr` elements directly.

Instead of using the `.update()` method, a more successful approach could be to create each of the elements one by one and using `.appendChild()` to add them.

Here's how you can make it work:

```
// Create the table
var table = new Element('table');

// Create the thead and append it to the table
var thead = new Element('thead');
table.appendChild(thead);

// Create a row and append it to the thead
var row = new Element('tr');
thead.appendChild(row);

// Create the cells for the row
['Situation Task', 'Action', 'Result'].each(function(text) {
  var cell = new Element('th').update(text);
  row.appendChild(cell);
});

// Create the tbody and append it to the table
var tbody = new Element('tbody');
table.appendChild(tbody);

// Create a row and append it to the tbody
var row = new Element('tr');
tbody.appendChild(row);

// Create the cells for the row
['a', 'b', 'c'].each(function(text) {
  var cell = new Element('td').update(text);
  row.appendChild(cell);
});

// Then you can append the table as you want
$$('div.question').each(function(o) {
  o.insert({
    after: table.cloneNode(true)
  });
});
```

This should give you a more consistent result across different browser versions and engines. Be reminded to always test across different browser versions and engines when dealing with DOM manipulation. 

