Given a piece of faulty HTML code, identify the issues, debug it, and create a proper HTML code snippet for an unordered list with each item containing an embedded hyperlink. The hyperlink must make use of relative pathing. Additionally, make sure your code is well-structured and semantic for assistive technologies.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Consider this as the given faulty HTML code:

```html
<ul
  <li><a href"page1.html">Item 1</a
  <li><a href"page2.html"Item 2</a>
  <li><a Item 3</a href="page3.html">
</ul
```

Several issues can be identified:

1. The starting `<ul` and ending `</ul` tags are not closed properly.
2. The first and second `<li` are not closed properly.
3. The first `<a` tag is not closed properly.
4. The second hyperlink misses closing bracket and greater than sign after "href attribute.
5. The third hyperlink structure is wrong, where href attribute is after the anchor text.

Here's the corrected version of the faulty HTML:

```html
<ul>
  <li><a href="page1.html">Item 1</a></li>
  <li><a href="page2.html">Item 2</a></li>
  <li><a href="page3.html">Item 3</a></li>
</ul>
```

For further improved accessibility, we can add 'alt' attributes to anchor tags for users who use screen readers:

```html
<ul>
  <li><a href="page1.html" alt="Link to Item 1">Item 1</a></li>
  <li><a href="page2.html" alt="Link to Item 2">Item 2</a></li>
  <li><a href="page3.html" alt="Link to Item 3">Item 3</a></li>
</ul>
```

Remember, alt tags should describe the destination of the link or the purpose of the link.
Please note that "alt" attribute is not valid for "a" tag. It is better to use "title" attribute. 

