Create a distinct 8-character identifier composed of both numbers and letters utilizing the Java programming language. Additionally, implement a mechanism to ensure an identifier is not repeated. Validate the identifier with an external rest API with your preferred http client. The API will return true if the id is unique and false if not. The program should only end when a new unique identifier has been generated and validated. Handle any potential exceptions such as network issues and API errors.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a simple Java program that generates a unique 8-character identifier, checks its uniqueness through a REST API call and handles potential exceptions.

Please note this is a skeleton program, and the actual API endpoint, request method, and other details should be replaced with the actual API information.

```
import java.util.*;
import org.apache.http.*;
import org.apache.http.client.methods.*;
import org.apache.http.impl.client.CloseableHttpClient;
import org.apache.http.impl.client.HttpClients;
import org.apache.http.util.EntityUtils;

public class UniqueIdGenerator {

    private static final String API_ENDPOINT = "http://myapi.com/checkId"; // replace with actual API endpoint

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String uniqueId = "";
        boolean isUnique = false;

        while(!isUnique){
            try {
                uniqueId = generateUniqueId();
                isUnique = validateId(uniqueId);
            } catch (Exception e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
                System.out.println(e.getMessage());
            }
        }

        if (isUnique){
            System.out.println("Generated unique id : " + uniqueId);
        }
    }

    private static String generateUniqueId() {
        String alphanumericStr = "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789";
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(8);
        for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
            int index = (int)(alphanumericStr.length() * Math.random());
            sb.append(alphanumericStr.charAt(index));
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }

    private static boolean validateId(String id) throws Exception {
        CloseableHttpClient httpClient = HttpClients.createDefault();
        HttpGet request = new HttpGet(API_ENDPOINT + "/" + id);
        request.setHeader("Content-Type", "application/json");
        CloseableHttpResponse response = httpClient.execute(request);
        
        if (response.getStatusLine().getStatusCode() != HttpStatus.SC_OK) {
            throw new Exception("API request failed. Received HTTP status: " + response.getStatusLine().getStatusCode());
        }
        
        String responseBody = EntityUtils.toString(response.getEntity());
        EntityUtils.consume(response.getEntity());

        response.close();
        httpClient.close();

        return "true".equals(responseBody);
    }
}
```
This program might encounter issues such as a bad API endpoint URL, no network connectivity, API endpoint down, or internal server errors (http 500 errors). Exception handling code is included to print the relevant error message and continue with the next iteration until a unique identifier is found.

The program generates a random unique identifier and makes a Rest API call to check if the identifier is unique or not. The API is expected to return a text response of "true" or "false". If the identifier is unique, the while loop is exited, and the unique identifier is printed.
