change the following snippet to rust:
script
const overlapAndFactorial = (interval1, interval2) => {
    // Find the overlap
    const start = Math.max(interval1[0], interval2[0]);
    const end = Math.min(interval1[1], interval2[1]);

    // If there is no overlap
    if (start > end) {
        return -1;
    }

    // Calculate the length of the overlap
    const length = end - start;

    // Calculate the factorial of the length
    let factorial = 1;
    for(let i = 2; i <= length; i++) {
        factorial *= i;
    }

    return factorial;
}
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is how you can write it in Rust:

```rust
fn overlap_and_factorial(interval1: (i32, i32), interval2: (i32, i32)) -> i32 {
    // Find the overlap
    let start = interval1.0.max(interval2.0);
    let end = interval1.1.min(interval2.1);

    // If there is no overlap
    if start > end {
        return -1;
    }

    // Calculate the length of the overlap
    let length = end - start;

    // Calculate the factorial of the length
    let mut factorial = 1;
    for i in 2..=length {
        factorial *= i;
    }

    factorial
}
```

Instead of using an array for the intervals, this version of `overlap_and_factorial` uses a tuple. We can utilize the `min` and `max` methods in Rust, allowing us to avoid importing a module for this (like `Math` in the JavaScript version). The for loop syntax is similar, with the range `2..=length` meaning "from 2 to length inclusive" in Rust.
<|end|>


