Here's a simple example of the task in React using functional components and hooks:

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const TextInputs = () => {
  const [inputValues, setInputValues] = useState({ field1: '', field2: '' });

  const handleInputChange = (e) => {
    setInputValues({ ...inputValues, [e.target.name]: e.target.value });
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <input
        type="text"
        name="field1"
        value={inputValues.field1}
        onChange={handleInputChange}
        placeholder="input 1"
      />
      <input
        type="text"
        name="field2"
        value={inputValues.field2}
        onChange={handleInputChange}
        placeholder="input 2"
      />
    </div>
  );
};

export default TextInputs;
```

In this component, the `useState` hook is used to manage the state of the two input fields. The `handleInputChange` function updates the state when the input fields are changed. The `useState` hook's initial state is an object, where each key corresponds to a field in the form (in this case, `field1` and `field2`). It makes it easy to add more fields to the form if needed. The `name` attribute on each input field matches the keys in the state object, and is used by the `handleInputChange` function to determine which field to update.
<|end|>


