Could you please suggest some good textbooks on the subject of `Design of Experiments` particularly covering below topics:
Inference in linear models, One and two-way ANOVA, Standard orthogonal and non-orthogonal designs, Analysis of general block designs, Factorial experiments etc.
It would be helpful to have the names of textbook covering above topics with lots of examples.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
1. "Design and Analysis of Experiments" by Douglas C. Montgomery: This book is a classic in the field and covers all the topics you listed. It is well-written and contains numerous examples to aid understanding.

2. "Experimental Design and Statistical Analysis" by Kirk and Roger E. Kirk: This is another comprehensive and authoritative source about the design of experiments in statistics. 

3. "Statistics for Experimenters: Design, Innovation, and Discovery" by George Box, J. Stuart Hunter, and William G. Hunter: This text is also recommended for your topics as it puts emphasis on the design of experiments besides covering the basic and advanced statistical techniques.

4. "The Design and Analysis of Industrial Experiments" by Davies, O.L.: This is a classic book on the subject. It covers the theoretical underpinnings but also focuses heavily on practical applications.

5. "Design of Experiments: Statistical Principles of Research Design and Analysis" by Robert O. Kuehl: This book includes multiple examples and is written in an accessible style, making it ideal for self-study.

6. "Design and Analysis of Experiments, Special Designs and Applications" by Klaus Hinkelmann and Oscar Kempthorne: The third volume in this series, it has chapters specifically devoted to one and two-way ANOVA, amongst other topics.

7. "Design of Experiments: An Introduction Based on Linear Models" by Max Morris: It provides a balanced presentation of theory and applications and has plenty of practical examples. 

8. "Design and Analysis of Experiments with R" by John Lawson: This book presents a broad range of these designs, and complete analyses of the designs are carried out using R. 

9. "Design of Experiments: A No Name Approach" by Thomas Lorenzen and Virginia Anderson: This book details systematic design and analysis of experiments and provides ample case studies and exercises. 

10. "An Introduction to the Design and Analysis of Experiments" by George C. Canavos: This book emphasizes the strategy of experimentation, data analysis, and the interpretation of experimental results, while explaining concepts in simple language. 

Please check which book caters to your specific needs and depth of study.
<|end|>


