<|user|>
Generate a code to remove duplicates from an unsorted linked list.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In Python, you can remove duplicates from an unsorted singly linked list using hash set:

```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, data):
        self.data = data
        self.next = None

def remove_duplicates(head):
    if head is None:
        return head

    # Create an empty set to store visited nodes
    visited = {head.data}

    current = head
    while current.next:
        if current.next.data in visited:
            current.next = current.next.next
        else:
            visited.add(current.next.data)
            current = current.next

    return head

# Test
head = Node(1)
head.next = Node(3)
head.next.next = Node(2)
head.next.next.next = Node(1)
head.next.next.next.next = Node(3)

remove_duplicates(head)

# Print linked list
while head:
    print(head.data, end=" ")
    head = head.next
```

This code works by initially checking if the head of the linked list is None. If it is, the function immediately returns as there's nothing to process. Then, it creates an empty set `visited` for storing the nodes already seen.

It will then start iterating over the linked list. For the duplicate values, it will update the `next` value of the current node to bypass the following node. If the node hasn't been visited previously, then it will add its value to the `visited` set and move on to the next node.

In the end, it will return the head of the modified linked list which doesn't have any duplicate values. The linked list is then printed to demonstrate the function's effectiveness. This example assumes a singly linked list, but it can be easily adjusted for doubly linked lists.
<|end|>


