<|asst|>
This is a broad task that requires knowledge of programming, web scraping, data processing and visualization. I'll use Python's pandas library for data handling, requests for downloading the data and matplotlib for visualization. I cannot run this code here but I can provide a skeleton to help guide you. Please adapt and run it in your local environment.

```python
import pandas as pd
import requests
from io import StringIO
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

# Function to download and load the data into a pandas DataFrame
def load_data(url):
    response = requests.get(url)
    data = response.text
    # The data is tab-delimited, and missing values are denoted by 'NaN'
    df = pd.read_csv(StringIO(data), delimiter='\t', na_values='NaN',
                     comment='%', header=None, usecols=[0, 1, 2, 3],
                     names=['Year', 'Month', 'Day', 'AvgTemperature'])
    return df

# Loading the data
url = "http://berkeleyearth.lbl.gov/auto/Global/Complete_TAVG_latest.txt"
df = load_data(url)

# Select a method to determine the hottest city
def get_hottest_cities(df, method='average'):
    # Handle ties for the hottest city
    if method == 'median':
        df_group = df.groupby('Year')['AvgTemperature'].transform('median')
    elif method == 'max':
        df_group = df.groupby('Year')['AvgTemperature'].transform(max)
    else: # default to 'average'
        df_group = df.groupby('Year')['AvgTemperature'].transform('mean')

    df['Temperature'] = df_group
    hottest_cities = df.sort_values('Temperature', ascending=False).drop_duplicates('Year')
    return hottest_cities

# Display top 5 hottest cities
def display_top5(hottest_cities):
    top5 = hottest_cities.head(5)
    print("Top 5 hottest cities:")
    print(top5)

# Display temperature trend over the years for each city
def plot_temp_trend(df, city):
    city_data = df[df['City'] == city]
    city_data.plot(x='Year', y='AvgTemperature')
    plt.title('Temperature Trend Over Years for {}'.format(city))
    plt.xlabel('Year')
    plt.ylabel('Average Temperature')
    plt.show()

# Test the functions
hottest_cities = get_hottest_cities(df, method='average')
display_top5(hottest_cities)
for city in hottest_cities['City'].unique():
    plot_temp_trend(df, city)
```
This code is basic and may not cover all possibilities or edge cases, please adapt it as needed. Note that I assume there are 'City' columns not mentioned in your request, you should adjust based on the exact format of the dataset. Also, for the sake of brevity, only plotting for hottest cities is shown. You may want to add conditions and checks to handle other scenarios. Also, remember to add any additional handling for ties in temperature. This code does not handle ties.
