I'd want to show that the behavior of our customers with the most customer support follows a different trend than our overall customers (with less support). As you can imagine, a linear fit to Time Series data with seasonality is going to lead to pretty bad r and p values. Therefore, my plan is to substract the linear fit from the datapoints and pick a few frequencies from a fourier transform.
My question is, can I argue, that the linear trend on its own is reasonable, when I show that the combination of the trend + fourier transform gets a decent fit (can I take the metrics of the combined fit?)? My goal would be to compare the trendlines of both customer groups (eventhough they are going to be pretty similar) to show our management that the support team is doing a great job.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Your approach seems fundamentally sound. Here are some suggestions on how you might proceed.

Firstly, you would want to establish a baseline trend for both groups of customers (those with more customer support and those with less). This will give you an idea about the overall directionality and trend that your customer behavior is following.

Next, by removing this linear trend from your time series data, you essentially deseasonalize it. This is an important stage in time series analysis which can indeed reveal more granular behavioral patterns.

Finally, by applying a Fourier transform to your deseasonalized data, you seek to identify the underlying cyclical patterns and oscillations. This allows you to extract the dominant frequencies (i.e., cycles) from your time series data.

This combination of linear trend and Fourier analysis should provide a clearer picture of how customer behavior changes over time for both of your customer groups. By comparing these results, you should be able to determine if there are significant differences between the groups.

As for whether the linear trend on its own is reasonable, the answer depends on how well it fits your initial data. But, as you plan to use it just as a part of your analysis, it would be justified as long as it helps to clarify and reveal the underlying patterns in your data.

However, be aware that all of these are contingent on your data quality and the appropriateness of these techniques to your particular dataset. Without specific details on your data, this suggestion remains mostly hypothetical.

