<|user|>
What impact will increasing the ℓ2 regularization coefficient have on the bias and variance of an overfitting linear regression model in a straightforward estimation task?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Increasing the ℓ2 regularization coefficient increases the bias and decreases the variance of an overfitting linear regression model.

Regularization works by adding a penalty term to the objective function which decreases model complexity. In the context of regression analysis, this means the estimated regression coefficients are smaller.

ℓ2 regularization results in a trade-off: it introduces bias into the estimates, but reduces their variance. As the regularization coefficient increases, this shrinks the estimates towards zero. Hence, bias is increased.

Meanwhile, variance is reduced because overfitting, which occurs when a model is overly sensitive to fluctuations in the training dataset, is mitigated. This consequently stabilizes the estimates across different samples. Therefore, increasing the ℓ2 regularization term reduces variance. 

Summarily, the net effect of increasing the ℓ2 regularization coefficient on an overfitting linear regression model is an increase in bias and decrease in variance.
<|end|>


