Create two classes in Java, named Rectangle and Square. The Rectangle class should have length and width attributes. Initialize these attributes via a constructor and provide getters and setters for them. Include a method to calculate its area.

For the Square class, inherit the properties from the Rectangle class and add an additional method to ensure the length and width are equal when a new object is constructed. Override the area method in the Square class, adjusting it appropriately for a square's properties. As an added challenge, include a check in the Rectangle class to ensure that the length and width are always positive, non-zero values. For the Square class, ensure that this condition applies for its sides too.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a simple way to solve the problem using Java:

```java
class Rectangle {
    protected double length;
    protected double width;

    public Rectangle(double length, double width) {
        if(length > 0 && width > 0) {
            this.length = length;
            this.width = width;
        } else {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Length and width must be positive, non-zero values.");
        }
    }

    public double getLength() {
        return length;
    }

    public void setLength(double length) {
        if(length > 0) {
            this.length = length;
        } else {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Length must be a positive, non-zero value.");
        }
    }

    public double getWidth() {
        return width;
    }

    public void setWidth(double width) {
        if(width > 0) {
            this.width = width;
        } else {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Width must be a positive, non-zero value.");
        }
    }

    public double area() {
        return length * width;
    }
}

class Square extends Rectangle {
    public Square(double side) {
        super(side, side); // Pass the side length to the Rectangle's constructor as both the length and width
    }

    public void setSide(double side) {
        if(side > 0) {
            setWidth(side); 
            setLength(side); 
        } else {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Side length must be a positive, non-zero value.");
        }
    }

    public double getSide() {
        return getWidth(); // Or getLength(), they are the same in a square
    }

    public double area() {
        return length * length; // Or width * width, they are the same in a square
    }
}
```

This approach creates a `Rectangle` class with length and width attributes, which are set via the constructor. The constructor checks if the length and width are greater than zero before setting them.

The `Rectangle` class has getters and setters for length and width, and a method to calculate the area of the rectangle.

The `Square` class extends `Rectangle`, meaning it inherits all of Rectangle's methods and attributes. The square's constructor passes the side length to the rectangle's constructor twice - once for the length, and once for the width - to create a rectangle with equal sides, i.e., a square.

In the `Square` class, the area method is overridden to use the squares' side length. A getter and setter for the side length are also added for convenience. The setter updates both the length and width of the `Rectangle`, again ensuring we have a perfect square at all times. 

Also note that we use the `getWidth()` method to return the length of the side in `Square` class as in case of `Square`, length equals width. We could have equally used `getLength()` instead. The `setSide` method also sets both `length` and `width` in `Square` class to ensure it remains a square. In both cases, an `IllegalArgumentException` is thrown when the sides are set to non-positive values.
<|end|>


