I'm trying to store a model to the redis that has 3 string fields, each one is serialized JSON
I have no problem in setting it and I can see it in redis insight
but when getting it from redis it fires error JSON exception when digging deep into it, the problem with those string fields that contains JSON

[Document(Prefixes = new[] { "Demo" })]
public class Demo
{
    [Indexed]
    public string ReferenceNumber { get; set; }  
    [Indexed]
    public string Note { get; set; }
    [Indexed]
    public string ItemsData { set; get; }  <-- those the string fields that contains json
    [Indexed]
    public string SkillsData { get; set; }
    [Indexed]
    public string Data { get; set; }
}


I'm using redis OM with Dot Net Core 6.
Furthermore, I tried to change the document type from hash to JSON and nothing happened
<|end|>
<|asst|>
When storing complex data structures (JSON, in this case) as a string in Redis, you are responsible for correctly handling the serialization and deserialization process because you are essentially telling Redis to treat the data as a regular string.

Unfortunately, `JSONException` is a fairly generic error, so it can be caused by various problems in the data. Some of the common ones include:
- An unexpected character or end of data when reading the JSON
- Invalid format (missing closing braces or brackets, unquoted property names, etc.)
- The JSON text represents a different .NET type (an array when you're expecting an object, for instance)
- Value not matching the expected type (a string where an integer is expected, for instance)

You might want to investigate which exact part of the JSON data is causing the problem. You might want to print out the exact JSON string that you're trying to deserialize, and manually verify if it's in the correct format.

The `JsonSerializer` class from `System.Text.Json` is commonly used for serializing/deserializing JSON data in .NET Core 6.

Here is a simple sample: 

```C#
string jsonString = JsonSerializer.Serialize(myObject); // serialize
var myObject = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<MyClass>(jsonString); // deserialize
```
If you are not handling the deserialization on your own and Redis OM is supposed to take care of it for you, you might want to check if it's indeed capable of handling complex structures in the way you expect.

Please note that although RedisOM has built-in JSON serialization functionality, it cannot natively serialize and deserialize JSON within string fields of a type. If you want to get these fields as specific types (as a `List<Item>`, for example) you should modify these properties' types to the correct types you want (not string) then manually serialize them before setting them in Redis, and they should automatically be deserialized into these types when getting the objects from Redis.
<|end|>


