Within the specialized domain of PostgreSQL database schema architecture, devise two separate data storage units, specifically denoted as &amp;amp;#34;music_composers&amp;amp;#34; and &amp;amp;#34;music_compositions&amp;amp;#34;. For musical pieces crafted by a solitary composer, consolidate the exhaustive data pertaining to the respective composer into the &amp;amp;#34;music_compositions&amp;amp;#34; storage unit. In contrast, for musical pieces that are the result of joint endeavors of multiple composers, merely incorporate the exclusive identifier associated with each composer within the &amp;amp;#34;music_compositions&amp;amp;#34; storage unit.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, here's how you might create these Postgres tables. 

First, let's create the table "music_composers". This table will store information about each composer:

```python
CREATE TABLE music_composers (
    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    first_name VARCHAR(100),
    last_name VARCHAR(100),
    birth_date DATE,
    death_date DATE,
    nationality VARCHAR(100)
);
```

For "music_compositions", there might be one or several composers per composition. To accommodate for this, we'll include a linking table "composition_composer":

```python
CREATE TABLE music_compositions (
    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    title VARCHAR(255),
    compose_date DATE,
    genre VARCHAR(100),
    duration TIME,
    composer_id SERIAL REFERENCES music_composers(id)
    -- this field refers a solitary composer, 
    -- in case of joint endeavors, this field will be NULL
);

CREATE TABLE composition_composer (
    composition_id SERIAL REFERENCES music_compositions(id), 
    composer_id SERIAL REFERENCES music_composers(id)
    -- this table handles multiple composers associated with one piece,
    -- for joint endeavors, a new row will be added for each composer
);
```

Now you can track all composers and their compositions, whether they are responsible for the compositions individually or as part of a group.
<|end|>


