I need to compress portions of our application's network traffic for performance.  I presume this means I need to stay away from some of the newer algorithms like bzip2, which I think I have heard is slower.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, you're correct, while bzip2 does feature better compression ratios than other algorithms, it is computically intensive, therefore slower. Instead, I would recommend using Zlib or LZ4.

Zlib is known for its good balance of compression ratio and speed. It reliably speeds up network transmission with minimal processor usage.

LZ4, another popular choice, is one of the fastest compressors available. It offers satisfactory compression ratios but prioritizes speed, making it a good choice for reducing network traffic.

However, the best choice would also depend highly on the specific details of your application and data, such as data type, repetition of data, network speed, and available processor power. You may need to experiment with several options and benchmark their performance in your specific application/environment to come up with the best solution for your needs.
<|end|>


