Construct a Vue.js component that dynamically fetches and displays Open Graph protocol metadata from a user-inputted URL.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can create a Vue.js component to fetch and display Open Graph protocol data from a URL inputted by the user. Here's an example using Vue.js and Axios, a popular promise-based HTTP client for the browser and Node.js.

Let's create a component named OGMetaDisplay.vue.

```vue
<template>
  <div>
    <input v-model="url" @input="fetchOGData" />
    <div v-if="ogData">
      <p><strong>OG Title:</strong> {{ ogData.ogTitle }}</p>
      <p><strong>OG Description:</strong> {{ ogData.ogDescription }}</p>
      <p><strong>OG Image:</strong> {{ ogData.ogImage }}</p>
      <p><strong>OG Url:</strong> {{ ogData.ogUrl }}</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
import axios from 'axios';
export default {
  data() {
    return {
      url: '',
      ogData: null
    };
  },
  methods: {
    async fetchOGData() {
      try {
        const response = await axios.get(`your-server-side-script?url=${this.url}`);
        this.ogData = response.data;
      } catch (error) {
        console.error(error);
      }
    }
  }
};
</script>
```

You will need to replace `your-server-side-script` with a URL that points to a server-side script that fetches Open Graph data from the provided URL. Due to CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) restrictions on the browser, it's not possible to fetch data directly from a different domain.

The server-side script will fetch the URL response and scrape Open Graph meta tags from HTML, then return this data in the JSON format. 

Please note that this is just a simple implementation. More complicated Open Graph objects could include multiple instances of certain properties (like multiple images), so you might need to tweak the `fetchOGData` function and the template accordingly.
<|end|>


