Formulate a Java method that receives a textual input, also handling special characters and punctuation, and registers each entity of length n or superior within that text. Including numerical values. Here's the text: "Welcome to the programming world, 12345!" Note that 'n' is defined as 5.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a code example in Java that fulfills these requirements:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.regex.Matcher;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String text = "Welcome to the programming world, 12345!";
        List<String> recordedEntities = recordEntities(text, 5);
        for (String record : recordedEntities) {
            System.out.println(record);
        }
    }

    public static List<String> recordEntities(String text, int n) {
        List<String> entities = new ArrayList<>();
        Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile("\\b\\w{5,}\\b");
        Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(text);

        while (matcher.find()) {
            if (matcher.group().length() >= n) {
                entities.add(matcher.group());
            }
        }
        return entities;
    }
}
```

This code first defines the `recordEntities` method, which matches all alphanumeric strings (including underscores) of length 5 or more using a regex pattern. It's important to note that the term "entity" refers to alphanumeric strings in the context of text processing. The matched entities are stored in a list as they are found and the method returns this list.

In the main method, example text is used to demonstrate the `recordEntities` method. The resulting list of entities, each having at least 5 characters, is printed to the console. 

With the example text "Welcome to the programming world, 12345!", and a value of 5 for 'n', the output is:
```
Welcome
programming
world
12345
```
<|end|>


