Write a Pascal program to find the first 1000 prime numbers.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a simple Pascal program which prints out the first 1000 prime numbers:

```pascal
program PrimeNumbers;

type
    primesarray = array [1..1000] of longint;

var
    primes: primesarray;
    numprimes, i, candidate: integer;
    prime: boolean;

begin
    primes[1] := 2;
    numprimes := 1;
    candidate := 3;
    writeln('The first 1000 prime numbers are: ');
    writeln('2 ');
    while numprimes < 1000 do
    begin
        prime := true;
        i := 1;
        while (i <= numprimes) and (prime) do
        begin
            if (candidate mod primes[i] = 0) then
                prime := false;
            i := i + 1;
        end;
        if prime then
        begin
            numprimes := numprimes + 1;
            primes[numprimes] := candidate;
            writeln(candidate);
        end;
        candidate := candidate + 2;
    end;
end.
```

This program works by maintaining an array to hold all the prime numbers that it finds until it finds the 1000th prime. For each candidate number, it checks if the candidate can be divided by any of the previously found prime numbers. If it can, then the candidate is not prime, and the program proceeds to the next candidate. If it cannot, then the candidate is prime, and the program stores this prime in the prime array, then proceeds to the next candidate. This process repeats until the 1000th prime number is found.
<|end|>


