    <h2>Fibonacci sequence Generator</h2>
    <p>Enter count: </p>
    <input type="number" id="fibCount" min="0">
    <button onClick="printFib()">Generate</button>
    <p id="fibSequence"></p>
</body>
</html>
```
JavaScript code:

```javascript
function printFib() {
    var n = document.getElementById('fibCount').value;
    var fibSeq = fibonacci(n);
    document.getElementById('fibSequence').innerHTML = fibSeq.join(", ");
}

function fibonacci(n) {
    var fib_array = [0, 1];
    if(n <= 0)
      return "Input should be greater than 0!";
    else if(n==1)
      return [0]; 
    else if(n==2)
      return fib_array;
    else {
      for(var i = 2; i < n; i++){
        fib_array[i] = fib_array[i - 2] + fib_array[i - 1];
      }
      return fib_array;
    }

}
```

The erroneous piece of code in the question does not correctly calculate the Fibonacci sequence because it's not adding the last two numbers of the sequence to get the next number. It is simply copying the second-last number (at position [i-2]) to the current position (i). Therefore, from the 3rd term onwards, the array only contains zeroes, which is incorrect.

Here is the corrected function:

```javascript
function fibonacci(n){
    var fib_array = [0, 1];
    if(n <= 0)
      return "Input should be greater than 0!";
    else if(n==1)
      return [0]; 
    else if(n==2)
      return fib_array;
    else {
      for(var i = 2; i < n; i++){
        fib_array[i] = fib_array[i - 2] + fib_array[i - 1];
      }
      return fib_array;
    }
}
```
