Construct a multifaceted CSS stylesheet, complemented by JavaScript coding, that ingeniously manipulates the backdrop chromaticity of a webpage employing a bespoke and random color decision mechanism every instance the digital platform experiences a rejuvenation or refresh, alongside ensuring the selected color doesn't repeat consecutively for improved user experience.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This task is straightforward and can be accomplished using JavaScript and CSS. Here is a simple implementation:

Firstly, you need to create an HTML file and enable JavaScript.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="styles.css">
  <script src="script.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
</body>
</html>
```

In your CSS (styles.css), create a few classes for the background colors:

```css
body {
  transition: background-color 0.5s ease;
}

.color1 {
  background-color: #ff7b7b;
}

.color2 {
  background-color: #ff5733;
}

.color3 {
  background-color: #ffc300;
}

.color4 {
  background-color: #DAF7A6;
}

.color5 {
  background-color: #5DADE2;
}
```

In your JavaScript file (script.js), implement the logic for choosing a random background color that is not the same as the current one:

```js
var body = document.body;
var previousColor = '';

function selectRandomColor() {
  var colors = ['color1', 'color2', 'color3', 'color4', 'color5'];
  var currentColor = colors[Math.floor(Math.random() * colors.length)];

  // in case selected color is same as previous, call the function again
  if (previousColor == currentColor) {
    selectRandomColor();
  } else { 
    previousColor = currentColor;
    body.className = currentColor;
  }
}

// on page load or refresh, change the color
window.onload = selectRandomColor;
```

This script picks a random color from the array of colors at every page load/refresh and checks if it's the same as the previous background color. If not, it sets that color as the background. If it is, it recalls the function to pick a different color. This way, there won't be repetition of colors consecutively. Also, we are smoothly changing the background color using the `transition` property in the CSS.
