I don't see any obvious way to import ICS files into Google Calendar from the API docs here:

[http://code.google.com/apis/calendar/developers_guide_protocol.html](http://code.google.com/apis/calendar/developers_guide_protocol.html)

And I'd greatly prefer not to have to parse them myself just to send the appointments into GCal.  I'm looking for a programmatic solution, not something like import plugins for Thunderbird, Outlook, etc.  Third party APIs to do the ICS parsing are acceptable, in any language.  Any ideas?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Google's official Calendar API doesn't support importing ICS files directly. If you want to import ICS files programmatically, you must parse the file and convert the data to the format Google understands. 

One possible solution would be to use a third-party library or tool that can parse ICS files and return you a format that Web APIs will accept. Most of these libraries would allow conversion of ICS data to JSON, which you would then be able to import into Google Calendar through its APIs.

One such library in JavaScript/Node.js is `ical.js`. You can parse an ICS file with it and handle the resulting data as shown in the following example:

```javascript
var ICAL = require('ical.js');

fs.readFile('path_to_your_file.ics', 'utf8', function(err, data) {
  if(err){
    console.log('Error: ', err);
    return;
  }

  var jcalData = ICAL.parse(data);
  var comp = new ICAL.Component(jcalData);
  
  // If it's a VEVENT
  comp.getAllSubcomponents('vevent').forEach(function(event) {
    console.log('Event start time: ', event.getFirstPropertyValue('dtstart'));
    console.log('Event summary: ', event.getFirstPropertyValue('summary'));
  
    //Your datatype conversion logic here
  });

  // Add your logic to use Google API with the converted data
});
```

For Python, there's a library called `icalendar` that you can use to parse an ICS file and then use Google's Calendar API to create events based on the parsed data:

```python
from icalendar import Calendar
import requests

r = requests.get('http://example.com/path_to_your_file.ics')
cal = Calendar.from_ical(r.text)

for event in cal.walk('vevent'):
    summary = event.get('summary')
    start = event.get('dtstart').dt
    end = event.get('dtend').dt
    #you can now use these variables to create an event using Google's Calendar API

```

Remember that Google's API expects Date-Time values in RFC3339 format, so you may need to convert date-time values from the ICS file to this format before sending it to Google. Following third party libraries support ics file parsing and conversion:

- `ical.js` (JavaScript)
- `icalendar` (Python)
- `biweekly` (Java)
- `vobject` (Python)
- `ical-ruby` (Ruby)
<|end|>


