Rewrite the following JavaScript code to use a switch statement in place of multiple if-else if conditions. 

let animal = "dog";

if (animal == "cat") {
  console.log("Meow");
} else if (animal == "dog") {
  console.log("Woof");
} else {
  console.log("Unknown animal");
}
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can rewrite the given JavaScript code using a switch statement like so:

```javascript
let animal = "dog";

switch (animal) {
  case "cat":
    console.log("Meow");
    break;
  case "dog":
    console.log("Woof");
    break;
  default:
    console.log("Unknown animal");
}
```
In the switch statement, each case indicates a possible value for the variable (in this case, "animal") and the code to be executed if the variable's value matches that case. The optional default case is executed if none of the cases match.
<|end|>


