Create a dynamic webpage that allows users to input a list of elements, then apply CSS to change the background color of the second element in the list and store the modified list for later retrieval.

Requirements:
1. Create an input field and a submit button to allow users to input a list of elements.
2. Upon submission, use JavaScript to parse and store the list as an array.
3. Create a function to identify and change the background color of the second element in the list using CSS.
4. The function should check if the list has at least two elements; if not, display a warning message to the user.
5. Store the modified list in a separate array and display the updated list on the webpage.
6. Add a feature that allows users to view both the original and modified lists for comparison.
7. Implement the ability to undo any changes made, reverting the list back to its original state.
8. Optimize the solution for minimal time and space complexity, and ensure compatibility across different web browsers and devices.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can achieve this using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Here's one example of how you can implement this:

1. First, create a simple HTML structure with an input field, a submit button, a warning message container, and two containers to display the original and modified lists:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>List Modifier</title>
</head>
<body>
    <input type="text" id="inputList" placeholder="Enter comma-separated list">
    <button onclick="submitList()">Submit</button>
    <p id="warningMessage" style="display:none">List must have at least two elements.</p>
    <h3>Original List:</h3>
    <ul id="originalList"></ul>
    <h3>Modified List:</h3>
    <ul id="modifiedList"></ul>
    <button onclick="undoChanges()" id="undoBtn" style="display:none">Undo Changes</button>
    <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

2. Now create a CSS file named `style.css` to style the background color of the second element in the list:

```css
.second-item {
    background-color: yellow;
}
```

3. Create a JavaScript file named `script.js`. This file will handle all the logic to manipulate and store the lists:

```javascript
let originalArray = [];
let modifiedArray = [];

// Submit list function
function submitList() {
    const inputList = document.getElementById('inputList');
    const inputValue = inputList.value;
    originalArray = inputValue.split(',');
    
    if (originalArray.length < 2) {
        showWarning(true);
    } else {
        showWarning(false);
        modifiedArray = changeBackgroundColor(originalArray);
        displayList('originalList', originalArray);
        displayList('modifiedList', modifiedArray);
        document.getElementById('undoBtn').style.display = 'inline';
    }
}

// Show or hide warning message
function showWarning(show) {
    const warningMessage = document.getElementById('warningMessage');
    warningMessage.style.display = show ? 'block' : 'none';
}

// Change the background color of the second element in the list
function changeBackgroundColor(arr) {
    let modArray = [...arr];
    modArray[1] = `<span class='second-item'>${modArray[1]}</span>`;
    return modArray;
}

// Display the list on the webpage
function displayList(listId, arr) {
    const listElement = document.getElementById(listId);
    listElement.innerHTML = '';

    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
        const listItem = document.createElement('li');
        listItem.innerHTML = arr[i];
        listElement.appendChild(listItem);
    }
}

// Undo changes and revert the list back to its original state
function undoChanges() {
    document.getElementById('modifiedList').innerHTML = '';
    document.getElementById('undoBtn').style.display = 'none';
}
```

4. To optimize the solution, the JavaScript functions are kept simple with minimal loops, and the CSS style is applied directly to the second element in the list without any complex selectors. The solution should work across different web browsers and devices.

Now your dynamic webpage should meet all the requirements.
<|end|>


