I am working on a multilabel classification problem with both continuous and categorical features. For a single label problem, I might make use of a supervised encoder for my categorical features such as a TargetEncoder, CatBoost, etc. However, I don't know if these encoders can handle multilabel samples. Are there categorical encoders that are extensible to multilabel data? Also happy to look at any literature published on this specifically if people know of any.
Note: The categorical encoders in the scikit contrib category encoders package seem to be extensible to multilabel problems via the polynomial wrapper. However, I'm not sure if I'm interpreting the functionality correctly.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You're correct that typical categorical encoders are most straightforwardly applied to single label classifiers.

In multilabel classification problems, the same feature might contribute differently to the prediction of each label, hence the encoding might differ label by label. A straightforward but somewhat computationally expensive way of extending target encoders to the multilabel case is to simply create an encoder per label.

A downside of this is that you lose the ability to leverage potential correlations between your labels.

Depending on your specific problem, it may be possible to stick with simpler encoding methods. One-hot encoding is a simple method, which doesn't have to be adapted for multilabel problems and has the benefit of not introducing any arbitrary ordering into your categories. This could be particularly beneficial if your labels are not ordered.

In terms of literature, multilabel classification with categorical features is a bit niche and does not have a lot of specific studies. You might find more generalized multilabel classification studies, which might include sections discussing categorical features. The book "Learning from Multi-Label Data" by Grigorios Tsoumakas and Ioannis Katakis is a nice resource on various multilabel learning methods and might have some information on handling categorical features.
  
However, a lot of the specifics will likely depend on the details of your particular problem. Types of categories, how many categories there are, how they're distributed, their relationship to the labels, and many other factors will determine what method is best for encoding them.

In the end, experimentation might be your best approach: try several different encoding methods and see which one works best in conjunction with your chosen multilabel classification model.
<|end|>


