Design a program in JavaScript to remove the last instance of a given character from a given string.
Hello, world
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can use the combination of `lastIndexOf()` and `slice()` method to remove the last instance of a given character from a given string.

Here's the JavaScript function to remove the last instance of a given character:

```JavaScript
function removeLastInstance(str, charToRemove) {
  // Get last position of charToRemove
  const position = str.lastIndexOf(charToRemove);

  // If character is not found, return original string
  if (position === -1) {
    return str;
  }

  // Part of the string before the charToRemove
  const start = str.slice(0, position);

  // Part of the string after the charToRemove
  const end = str.slice(position + 1);

  // Returning result string
  return start + end;
}

console.log(removeLastInstance('Hello, world', 'l')); // output: "Hello, word"
```

This program works by finding the last index of the given character in the string, split the string into two parts at this index (the start and the end), and then concatenate the two parts together to form the new string. This effectively removes the last instance of the given character from the string. 

You can change the second parameter in `removeLastInstance('Hello, world', 'l')` to the character you want to remove. In this case, it removes the last 'l' character.
<|end|>


