Like in Windows Forms:

Dim myForm as New AForm(Constr-arg1, Constr-arg2)

myForm.Show

... is there a similar way to Load a Page in ASP.Net. I would like to overload the Page Constructor and instantiate the correct Page Contructor depending on the situation.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
No, there isn't a way to directly instantiate a page with a constructor in ASP.NET. The reason for this is because the life-cycle of a Page in ASP.NET is managed by the framework. It creates an instance of the page class and calls the load event, handles the postback events and finally renders the page.

But if you have a specific need in your code to call a different method each time a Page is loaded, depending on certain conditions, you can use the `Page.Load` event.  You can place the conditional logic inside the `Page_Load` method and call the appropriate method from there.

For example:

```
Protected Sub Page_Load(ByVal sender As Object, ByVal e As System.EventArgs) Handles Me.Load
        If Not Page.IsPostBack Then
            If Condition1 Then 
               Method1()
            End If
            If Condition2 Then
               Method2()
            End If
        End If
End Sub
```

Each time the page is loaded, this code will be called, and the appropriate method will be executed.

Alternatively, you can also use `Session` or `ViewState` to pass values between pages. 

ASP.NET Web Forms also supports QueryString which can be used to send values from one page to another. These values are appended to the end of the URL. The key-value pairs are separated by an '&'. 

If use server.Transfer between pages is also a option. It preserves the state of all controls on the source page. 

Those are the ways that values are passed from page to page in ASP.NET. You cannot pass values using overloaded constructors.
<|end|>


