<|user|>
Using JS SingleR client v7.0. Server side is ASP.NET Core V5.0.
When inspecting the server side I get the same cookie value from client for every hub method call equal to that of the cookie value received during the initial connection. This is true even though the cookie value on client has been updated and changed in the time between the connection creation and subsequent method calls to the hub.
If I reestablish the connection, the new cookie does get sent but then again it becomes stale as it is never updated on subsequent hub method calls.
From what I understand WebSockets should send the client cookie every time through it's header but seems to keep providing the old cookie. Is there some setting in SignalR that does some caching on the cookie per connection?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The problem you're encountering is not specific to SignalR, but rather to how WebSockets work. When a WebSocket connection is established, it uses an HTTP handshake. During this handshake, cookies are sent and can be modified, as with any other HTTP request. However, after this handshake, a persistent connection is established which doesn't adhere to the HTTP request/response model. 

This means any subsequent communication over the WebSocket connection (like SignalR hub method calls) won't include cookies. Therefore, if you're changing a cookie after the WebSocket connection is established, these changes won't be reflected in the WebSocket communication. The WebSocket doesn't not provide any mechanism to send or receive cookies apart from at connection time during the HTTP handshake.

So in a nutshell:

- During the WebSocket connection establishment, it behaves just like an HTTP request and includes all the cookies just like HTTP would.
- After the WebSocket connection is made, no further cookies are sent or received because it no longer uses HTTP.

There isn't any setting in SignalR to change this behavior as it's fundamentally due to how WebSockets work. If you need to pass some updated state along with your Hub method calls, I would suggest passing this as method parameters rather than relying on cookies. Alternatively, if the updated cookie is very important, you may have to disconnect and reconnect to cause a new handshake and send the updated cookies.
<|end|>


