Is there a specfic [Gang Of Four Design Pattern](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Design_Patterns) that you frequently use, yet hardly see used in other peoples designs?  If possible, please describe a simple example where this pattern can be useful.  It doesn't have to necessarily be a Gang Of Four pattern, but please include a hyperlink to the pattern's description if you choose a non-GoF pattern.

Put another way:
<|end|>
<|asst|>
One design pattern I find quite useful but often overlooked is the [Flyweight Pattern](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flyweight_pattern), one of the Gang of Four structural design patterns. This pattern is best suited for situations where a large number of objects of a class are to be created, but most of them are identical and can share common data.

The main idea of Flyweight Pattern is to minimize memory use by sharing as much data as possible with similar objects.

For an simple example, consider a text editor that allows to change font type and color of text. Different combinations of font type and color could be saved as separate objects. But if there are many such objects, memory usage would be high. Instead, using Flyweight pattern, each unique font type-color combination becomes a flyweight object, and the text document only needs to reference these flyweights, drastically reducing memory usage. 

Rather than creating new objects each time these combinations are used, we simply pull the existing ones from a pool and reference them. Sure, we need a bit more computational time for looking up the pooled objects, but the savings on memory can be considerable, particularly for large applications or in cases where the created objects are significantly large.

It's not a pattern you will use in every project, but in the right context, it can make a big difference.
<|end|>


