Craft a piece of software that detects the minimal distinctive subset of characters positioned in the same order within a tandem of typographical series inputted.
input1 = "ALGORITHMS", input2 = "LOGARITHM"
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This problem is about finding the Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) in two typographical strings. The LCS problem is a classic computer science problem, which can be solved using dynamic programming. 

Here is a Python solution to this problem:

```python
def minimal_distinctive(input1, input2):
    # the dp table will have len(input1) + 1 rows and len(input2) + 1 columns.
    dp = [[None]*(len(input2)+1) for i in range(len(input1)+1)]
 
    # initialize the first row and first column as 0.
    for i in range(len(input1) + 1):
        for j in range(len(input2) + 1):
            if i == 0 or j == 0 :
                dp[i][j] = 0
            elif input1[i-1] == input2[j-1]:
                dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1]+1
            else:
                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1])
 
    # the value of the bottom right corner of the matrix
    # is the length of the LCS.
    lcs_length = dp[len(input1)][len(input2)]
 
    # Create a character array to store the lcs string
    lcs = [''] * (lcs_length+1)
    lcs[lcs_length] = ''

    # Start from the right-most bottom-most corner and
    # one by one store characters in lcs[]
    i = len(input1)
    j = len(input2)
    while i > 0 and j > 0:

        # If current character in input1 and input2 are same,
        # then current character is part of LCS
        if input1[i-1] == input2[j-1]:
            lcs[lcs_length-1] = input1[i-1]
            i-=1
            j-=1
            lcs_length-=1

        # If not same, then find the larger of two and
        # go in the direction of larger value
        elif dp[i-1][j] > dp[i][j-1]:
            i-=1
        else:
            j-=1
 
    lcs_string = "".join(lcs)
    return lcs_string
 
  
# Driver program
input1 = "ALGORITHMS"
input2 = "LOGARITHM"
 
print("The minimal distinctive subset is " + minimal_distinctive(input1, input2))
```

This Python code creates a two-dimensional array (or matrix), which covers all the possible combinations of input1 and input2 characters. Each cell in the matrix contains the length of the longest common subsequence up to that point. The value in the bottom right corner of the matrix is the length of the longest common subsequence for the full strings. All characters in the longest common subsequence stay in the same order they appear in the original strings.
<|end|>


