The issue you're facing is due to not taking the year into consideration when you reduce months. Here's the detail on how you can correct your existing code with handling years as well:

You can use a decrement operator alongside `months.ago` from the ActiveSupport (part of Rails) utility to return dates from the past. Here's the ruby code:

```ruby
require 'date'
require 'active_support/all'

date = '2023-02-01'.to_date  # Assume '2023-02-01' is the date you provide

(0..2).map { |i| (date - i.months).beginning_of_month.to_s }
```

Here's how this works:

1. `0..2` generates a range from 0 to 2. It will give you the past 3 months.

2. `date - i.months` subtracts i months from the given date. For i from 0 to 2, this will give you the same month, one month ago, and two months ago.

3. `beginning_of_month` returns the date with day set to 1. This will ensure that the returned date's day is always the 1st of the month.

4. `.to_s` converts the date back to a string, so that the returned array elements are all strings.

Note: Remember to include `require 'active_support/all'`, if you are trying to run this outside of Rails. If you are using Rails, `require 'active_support/all'` is not necessary.
