Here is the corrected and optimized version of the given JavaScript code:

```javascript
document.getElementById('redirectBtn').addEventListener('click', function(event) {
    event.preventDefault();
    let username = document.getElementById('username').value;
    let password = document.getElementById('password').value;

    if (inputValid(username) && inputValid(password)){
        window.location.href = "https://www.someurl.com";
    } else {
        alert("Invalid credentials");
    }
});

function inputValid(input) {
    return input && input.trim() !== ""; 
}
```

Explanation of the changes:

1. The function should be associated with a button click event for initiating the redirection only after an user action. So, I have added an event listener to a button with ID 'redirectBtn'. Use the actual ID of your button.

2. The redirection is assigned incorrectly in the original script. The correct way to redirect to a new URL using JavaScript is by assigning the URL to `window.location.href`.

3. The `inputValid` function checks if the input is not null and not an empty string. So, it can be simplified using truthy value concept in JavaScript. An empty string is considered falsy. This way, we're also considering spaces as invalid inputs.
