import javax.imageio.ImageIO;
import java.awt.image.BufferedImage;
import java.io.File;
import java.io.IOException;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
                // Load the image
                BufferedImage img = ImageIO.read(new File("image.jpg"));

                // Specify the start point and size of the cropping rectangle
                int startX = 150;
                int startY = 150;
                int cropWidth = 400;
                int cropHeight = 400;

                // Check if start points and size are within the image constraints
                if(startX < 0 ) startX = 0;
                if(startY < 0 ) startY = 0;
                if(startX + cropWidth > img.getWidth()) cropWidth = img.getWidth() - startX;
                if(startY + cropHeight > img.getHeight()) cropHeight = img.getHeight() - startY;

                // Crop the image
                BufferedImage imgCropped = img.getSubimage(startX, startY, cropWidth, cropHeight);

                // Save the cropped image to a new file
                // set output file format end part of the filename like ".png"
                ImageIO.write(imgCropped, "png", new File("image_cropped.png"));

        } catch (IOException e) {
            // handle the exception
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

In Java, unlike Python, we don't have in-built function to display the image directly. So, I've written the code to save it as a new image. You can open and view the image from your system. 

Remember that Java ImageIO doesn't support all image types. For instance, it supports reading gif, jpg, png type images but not writing all types of images. You should use related library to java like OpenCV for complex image processing tasks.
<|end|>


