```python
import pandas as pd
import geopandas as gpd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from sklearn.cluster import DBSCAN
from geopandas.tools import geocode
from shapely.geometry import Point
import seaborn as sns


# Load the data
url = 'https://data.seattle.gov/api/views/65db-xm6k/rows.csv'
data = pd.read_csv(url)

# Clean data and convert to GeoDataFrame
data = data.dropna(subset=['Longitude', 'Latitude'])
gdf = gpd.GeoDataFrame(data, geometry=gpd.points_from_xy(data.Longitude, data.Latitude))

# Perform DBSCAN clustering
db = DBSCAN(eps=0.3, min_samples=10).fit(gdf[['Longitude', 'Latitude']])
gdf['cluster'] = db.labels_

# Plot
fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(10,10))
gdf.plot(column='cluster', ax=ax, legend=True)
plt.show()

# Analysis
# Peak times and days
data['Datetime'] = pd.to_datetime(data['Datetime'])
data['Hour'] = data['Datetime'].dt.hour
data['Day'] = data['Datetime'].dt.dayofweek
hourly_calls = data['Hour'].value_counts().sort_index()
daily_calls = data['Day'].value_counts().sort_index()

print("Peak hour for calls:", hourly_calls.idxmax())
print("Peak day for calls:", daily_calls.idxmax())

# Zone-wise distribution
zone_calls = data['Zone'].value_counts()
print("Most calls by zone:\n", zone_calls)

# Types of emergencies/services mainly requested
type_calls = data['Type'].value_counts()
print("Most called for services/emergencies:\n", type_calls[:5])
```

This code will first load the data, convert it to a `GeoDataFrame` (which allows for geographical operations), and perform DBSCAN clustering based on the geographic locations of the calls. It will then plot the clusters on a scatter plot.

It will also analyze the data to determine the peak hours and days for calls, the distribution of calls by zone, and the most common types of services or emergencies requested. You might want to modify these parts depending on the actual column names in the dataset.

Again, this script is simplistic and may need further adjustments based on dataset characteristics. Also, installing geopandas and its dependencies might be quite difficult because it needs a lot of GIS (Geographical Information System) libraries. It would be good to keep this in mind.
