Create a HTML page that allows users to filter a table with data based on user input.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's a simple HTML page with JavaScript that enables a user to filter a table. 

This page has a table of five people with names and ages, and there's an input field too where the user can enter any text to filter the table.

Please note that this is a very basic example and may not cover more complex filters.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>

<head>
    <style>
        table, th, td {
            border: 1px solid black;
        }
    </style>
</head>

<body>
    <h2>Filterable Table</h2>

    <input type="text" id="myInput" onkeyup="filterTable()" placeholder="Search for names..">

    <table id="myTable">
        <tr class="header">
            <th style="width:60%;">Name</th>
            <th style="width:40%;">Age</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>John Doe</td>
            <td>30</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Jane Doe</td>
            <td>25</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Paul Stevenson</td>
            <td>50</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Emma Brown</td>
            <td>23</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Tom Hanks</td>
            <td>58</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <script>
        function filterTable() {
            var input, filter, table, tr, td, i, txtValue;
            input = document.getElementById("myInput");
            filter = input.value.toUpperCase();
            table = document.getElementById("myTable");
            tr = table.getElementsByTagName("tr");

            for (i = 0; i < tr.length; i++) {
                td = tr[i].getElementsByTagName("td")[0];
                if (td) {
                    txtValue = td.textContent || td.innerText;
                    if (txtValue.toUpperCase().indexOf(filter) > -1) {
                        tr[i].style.display = "";
                    } else {
                        tr[i].style.display = "none";
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    </script>

</body>

</html>
```

In this design, the function `filterTable()` is called whenever a user types in the input field (`onkeyup="filterTable()"`). This function finds the rows of the table (`document.getElementById("myTable").getElementsByTagName("tr")`) and checks if the text content of the first `td` of each row includes the input value. If it does, the style of the row remains unchanged. If it doesn't, the row is set to not display (`tr[i].style.display = "none"`). This gives the effect of a filter.
