Create a JavaScript function that takes an integer as a parameter, multiplies it by 2 and stores it in a variable, only if the passed integer is within the range of 1 to 10 inclusive. Furthermore, the function should return the variable value only if it is an even integer, otherwise it should throw an error. Apply error handling to this function.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can solve this problem by using JavaScript's built-in functions and error handling mechanisms. Here's a simple JavaScript function with error handling that fulfills the requirements:

```javascript
function processInteger(n) {
    // Check if n is within range 1-10
    if (n < 1 || n > 10) {
        throw new Error('The provided integer is not in the range of 1 to 10.');
    }
  
    // Multiply n by 2
    var result = 2 * n;
  
    // Check if result is even
    if (result % 2 !== 0) {
        throw new Error('The result is not an even number.');
    }
  
    // Return the result
    return result;
}
```

You can then use this function within a try-catch block to handle any potential errors:

```javascript
try {
    console.log(processInteger(5)); // Should return 10
    console.log(processInteger(7)); // Should throw error 'The result is not an even number'
    console.log(processInteger(11)); // Should throw error 'The provided integer is not in the range of 1 to 10'
} catch (error) {
    console.error(error.message);
}
```
The processInteger() function first checks the range of the input integer. If the integer is not within the range of 1 to 10, it throws an error. Then, it multiplies the integer by 2 and checks if the result is an even number. If it's not, it throws an error again. Finally, if no errors are thrown, it will return the result.
