Calculate the product of elements in the provided multi-dimensional list based on the specified criterion. Take into consideration only distinct elements from all sub-arrays, ignore duplicate entries. Also, make sure you're handling the case of empty sub-arrays.
List: [[2, 3, 4, 7, 8, 10], [2, 9, 15, 7], [3, 4, 7], [], [4, 20, 10, 2]]
Criterion: x % 3 == 1
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a Python solution for your problem.
This solution works by first flattening the list, removing duplicates and then finding the product of the elements which fulfill the specified criterion.

```python
# Given data
lst = [[2, 3, 4, 7, 8, 10], [2, 9, 15, 7], [3, 4, 7], [], [4, 20, 10, 2]]

# Flattening the list and removing duplicate elements
flattened_list = list(set([num for sublist in lst for num in sublist]))

# Finding the product of the elements which fulfill the criterion
product = 1
for num in filtered_list:
    if num % 3 == 1:
        product *= num
print(product)
```

This script will iterate through each sub-list in the list, flattening it and removing duplicates. Then for each number in the new list, it will check if the number is congruent to 1 modulo 3 (x % 3 == 1) and if the criterion is met it will multiply the current product by that number.

It should be noted that the use of set to remove duplicates from the list will also destroy the original order of the elements, but it doesn't appear order matters for your use case.
