Craft a function using Java programming language that will not merely output the square of a given integer parameter but also performs a thorough validation to confirm whether the input is indeed an integer before eventually calculating its square.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is a simple function in Java that accomplishes this:

```java
public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(squareOfInteger(5));  // Outputs: 25
        System.out.println(squareOfInteger(null));  // Outputs: integer is null
    }

    public static String squareOfInteger(Integer num) {
        if (num == null) {
            return "Input integer is null";
        }
        return String.valueOf(num * num);
    }
}
```

In this function 'squareOfInteger', we're initially checking if the input parameter 'num' is null. If it is, then we're returning a message "Input integer is null" indicating that there was no valid integer provided. If the 'num' is not null, then we're calculating the square (num \* num) and returning it. Note that we're returning a string just to be able to return both integers and string messages from this function. Normally, we would have the function return an integer.

Note, in Java all integer-primitive-passed-as-parameter are indeed integers because Java is STONGLY TYPED language and it doesn't perform automatic type conversions as done by some other languages. The number validation is implicitly done by the language and we only have to possibly handle null values if Integer object is used.

This function only handles null Integer inputs because primitive variables like int cannot be null in Java. If you want to ensure inputs like Strings and others, it's better to take type as Object and then perform instanceOf check. This is not generally a good practice as you try to ensure your code receives the kind of inputs it expects.

If you still want to ensure it, here is the code sample:

```java
public static String squareOfInteger(Object num) {
    if(num instanceOf Integer) {
        Integer n = (Integer) num;
        return String.valueOf(n * n);
    } else {
        return "Input is not Integer";
    }
}
```
<|end|>


