Sure, here's an example of how you can morph an SVG shape. In simple terms, morphing SVGs involves manipulating the d attribute of the SVG's path elements over time to achieve the final shape. Below is CSS code to demonstrate an example.

First, We use SVG path as a variable (Let's say to Square shape).

```html
<svg id="shape" width="32" height="32" viewBox="0 0 100 100" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
<path id="shape-path" d="M10 10 H 90 V 90 H 10 Z" fill="#FCD53F"/>
</svg>
```

Here's how you can apply CSS animations to the SVG:

```css
@keyframes morph {
    0% {d: path("M10 10 H 90 V 90 H 10 Z");}
    50% {d: path("M50 50 m -50, 0 a 50,50 0 1,0 100,0 a 50,50 0 1,0 -100,0");}
    100% {d: path("M10 10 H 90 V 90 H 10 Z");}
}

#shape-path {
    animation: morph 5s infinite;
}
```

In this code above, we set up a keyframe animation called morph that changes the d path of the SVG shape over time. The animation is then applied to the path element with the id of 'shape-path', and the animation plays indefinitely (infinite), with each cycle lasting 5 seconds (5s).

Please note that CSS animation with path() for 'd' attribute does not work on all browsers and could not work properly. An alternative would be javascript libraries like GSAP or anime.js for SVG morphing effect.
<|end|>


