Yes, it's possible! You can use ruby variable into backticks to execute an OS command, here is how to do that:

```ruby
#!/usr/bin/env ruby
directory = '/home/paulgreg/'
`ls #{directory}`
```

Remember that the value returned by the backticks is the output of the command being run. So, you could capture this into a variable and use it later in your script.
```ruby
output = `ls #{directory}`
puts output
```
Please note that when using interpolated strings within command execution (both system and `backticks`), be wary about potential shell injection vulnerabilities if the interpolated value come from an untrusted source (like user input, web request...). You might want to sanitize/escape these values properly before using them if such potential exists.
