Develop a Ruby code segment to analyze the strength of a supplied password, complying with detailed safety standards.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In Ruby, you can use regular expressions (Regexp) to analyze a given password and determine its strength. Here's a basic strength-testing code segment that checks for the major characteristics of a strong password:

```ruby
def password_strength(password)
  # Check length
  if password.length < 12
    return "Password is too short. It should be at least 12 characters."
  end

  # Check for number
  unless password.match(/\d/)
    return "Password should contain at least one number."
  end

  # Check for lowercase letter
  unless password.match(/[a-z]/)
    return "Password should contain at least one lowercase letter."
  end

  # Check for uppercase letter
  unless password.match(/[A-Z]/)
    return "Password should contain at least one uppercase letter."
  end

  # Check for special character
  unless password.match(/[!@#$%^&*()_+\-={}|:;"'<,>.?\/\\]/)
    return "Password should contain at least one special character."
  end

  "Password is strong!"
end

puts password_strength('testA1!123456') # This will output: Password is strong!
```

Remember that these are very basic checks; real-word password strength checks can involve more complex factors. 

Also keep in mind that storing passwords securely is equally important as requiring strong passwords. Always hash + salt passwords, don't store them in plain text. Security is a complex field, always consult with a security expert when dealing with user data.
<|end|>


