Create a JavaScript program to generate an Oxford comma-separated list.
["apples", "oranges", "bananas"]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, here is a JavaScript function to generate an Oxford comma-separated list from an array of items:

```javascript
function oxfordCommaList(arr) {
    if (arr.length === 2) {
        return arr.join(' and ');
    } else if (arr.length > 2) {
        let last = arr.pop();
        return arr.join(', ') + ', and ' + last;
    } else {
        return arr[0] || '';
    }
}

console.log(oxfordCommaList(["apples", "oranges", "bananas"]));
```

This function first checks the length of the array. If it's 2, it will return a string with "and" connecting the two items. If it's more than 2, it will pop off the last item, join the remaining items with commas, then add ", and " plus the last item. If there's only 1 item, it will return that item, and if the array is empty it will return an empty string.
<|end|>


