Design a real-time data processing system using Apache Storm for a high-frequency trading firm. This system should extract data from numerous simultaneous sources such as real-time stock market prices, trading details, transaction records, and account information. Process this data in real-time while handling the large volume of high-speed transactions, and provide predictive analytics based on this information. Transmit this processed data to a Big Data platform.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Designing a complete system is beyond the scope of this service. However, here's an outline of how you can approach building a real-time data processing system using Apache Storm for a high-frequency trading firm.

1. Data Extraction:

You can use the Kafka Spout which is available in Apache Storm to read data from various sources like real-time stock market prices, trading details, transaction records, and account information. Kafka serves as a buffer system that allows you to handle the surge in the data volume. You will need to write the Kafka producer code which pushes data from heterogeneous sources into Kafka topics.

2. Data Processing:

You can use Apache Storm for real-time data processing. A basic Storm topology consists of Spouts (source of streams), Bolts (processing of data), and Streams (channel of data between Spouts and Bolts). For example, Spouts can get the data from Kafka topics and Bolts can process it real-time based on your logic.

You can also use Trident API for stateful stream processing. Trident functions allow you to implement complex business processing logic. In Trident, you deal with batches of tuples and there is built-in support for persisting aggregations to databases.

3. Real-Time Analytic:

Apache Storm is integrated with machine learning libraries, so you can use these to perform predictive analytics on the incoming data, then provide the results to the high-frequency trading system that can then make instant decisions based on these real-time analytics.

4. Big Data Platform:

Apache Storm can be set up to write the processed output data to any big data platform such as Hadoop HDFS or NoSQL databases.

Below is a simple pseudo-code for extracting data from Kafka and processing it using Storm:

```java
// Define a new KafkaSpout and enter the details
KafkaSpout kafkaSpout = new KafkaSpout(new SpoutConfig(
  new ZkHosts("localhost:2181"),
  "myTopic",
  "/my/dir",
  "myId"
));

// Define a new bolt that processes incoming data
BoltExecutor myBolt = new BoltExecutor(new MyBolt());

// Create a topology
TopologyBuilder builder = new TopologyBuilder();

// Attach the kafka spout to the topology
builder.setSpout("kafka-spout", kafkaSpout);

// Attach your bolt to the topology
// We are shuffling the data meaning we are grouping it randomly and sending it to the bolt
builder.setBolt("my-bolt", myBolt).shuffleGrouping("kafka-spout");

// Create the config for the topology
Config conf = new Config();

// Submit the topology to the cluster
StormSubmitter.submitTopology("my-topology", conf, builder.createTopology());
```

The above code will process data real-time. You have to add code for your data processing/validation logic into MyBolt class and tune the Storm and Kafka configurations based on your requirements.

This is a simplified and foundational representation of what a real-world scenario would look like. In production, considerations for error handling, failover mechanisms, and security measures would be necessary.
<|end|>


