<|user|>
Generate a Java solution that determines the longest increasing sequence of numbers in an array.
[9, 4, 2, 5, 7, 1, 6, 8]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a simple solution using Dynamic Programming. This solution iterates the array and keeps track of the longest increasing sequence encountered so far.

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] array = {9, 4, 2, 5, 7, 1, 6, 8};
        System.out.println(longestIncSequence(array));
    }

    public static int longestIncSequence(int[] nums) {
        if (nums == null || nums.length == 0) {
            return 0;
        }
        int[] dp = new int[nums.length];
        dp[0] = 1;
        int max = 1;
        for (int i = 1; i < dp.length; i++) {
            dp[i] = 1;
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                if (nums[i] > nums[j]) {
                    dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1);
                }
            }
            max = Math.max(max, dp[i]);
        }
        return max;
    }
}
```
In the above solution, we loop through the input array. For each element, we compare it with all the elements before it. If the current element is greater than the previously considered elements, we update the longest sequence at the current position.

Finally, we maintain a variable `max` to store the longest increasing sequence discovered so far.

Please note that this solution returns the length of the longest increasing sequence, not the sequence itself. If you want the sequence instead, you would need to modify the code to keep track of the sequence elements. Also, this solution assumes that the increasing sequence does not have to be contiguous. If you want the longest contiguous increasing sequence, you would need a different approach.
<|end|>


