Create a JavaScript code to accept a number and check if it is a prime number. Additionally, implement error handling to reject non-numeric or negative inputs, and optimize for large numbers.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a simple JavaScript code optimized with error handling and checking for prime numbers:

```javascript
function checkPrime(n) {
    // Error handling for non-numeric or negative inputs
    if (typeof n !== 'number' || n <= 1) {
        return 'Input must be a positive number greater than 1';
    }

    // Optimization for large numbers and check for prime numbers
    // 2 is the smallest prime number
    if (n === 2) {
        return true;
    }

    // Even numbers other than 2 are not prime
    if (n % 2 === 0) {
        return false;
    }

    let sqrt = Math.sqrt(n);

    // Only check up to the square root of n for further optimization
    for(let i = 3; i <= sqrt; i += 2) {
        if (n % i === 0) {
            return false;
        }
    }

    return true;
}

console.log(checkPrime(1));  // Returns: 'Input must be a positive number greater than 1'
console.log(checkPrime("abc"));  // Returns: 'Input must be a positive number greater than 1'
console.log(checkPrime(-10));   // Returns: 'Input must be a positive number greater than 1'
console.log(checkPrime(2));   // Returns: true
console.log(checkPrime(4));   // Returns: false
console.log(checkPrime(17));  // Returns: true
console.log(checkPrime(1000000007)); // Returns: true
```

This script first checks if the input is a number and is greater than 1. Then it starts checking if the number is divisible by any number up to the square root of the number. This helps in optimized checking for large numbers, since for any number 'n', if n is not a prime, it will have a factor less than or equal to square root of 'n'. So this eliminates a lot of unnecessary computations for larger numbers.

Please note that prime numbers have to be greater than 1, so this function by default rejects 1 as a prime number. 

Also note: JavaScript has a maximum safe integer limit. For numbers beyond this limit, the prime check may not be accurate. You may use BigInt for numbers beyond the safe integer limit. However, the given solution does not incorporate use of BigInt.
<|end|>


