<|asst|>
You can create an equivalent JavaScript function as follows:

```javascript
function productInRangeExtended(l, min_val, max_val) {
    // get the product of all elements
    let total_product = l.reduce((a, b) => a * b, 1);
    // if the total product is not in range, then first part of result is False
    let r_all_in_range = min_val <= total_product && total_product <= max_val;
    // now we move on to the second part
    let r_individuals_in_range = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < l.length; i++) {
        // get the product excluding the ith element
        let product_excluding_i = Math.floor(total_product / l[i]);
        // if this product is in the range, add the ith element to the list
        if (min_val <= product_excluding_i && product_excluding_i <= max_val) {
            r_individuals_in_range.push(l[i]);
        }
    }
    return [r_all_in_range, r_individuals_in_range];
}
```
This JavaScript function operates similarly to the original Python function. It gets the product of all elements, checks if that product falls within the specified range, and then computes products which omit one element, checking each to see if it falls within the range and adding corresponding elements to a new list which it returns along with the boolean value computed earlier. Note that, unlike Python, JavaScript uses `&&` for logical and operation.
