<|user|>
Design commands in SQLite not only to alter the cryptocurrency wallet addresses of customers recorded in the specified database but also to create a backup table prior to alterations for security purposes. Furthermore, add a function that allows tracking of when these alterations were made. 

Database specification: client_base 
Parameter for Cryptocurrency Wallet: crypto_wallet 
Additional Parameters: alteration_timestamp, backup_table

Note: You are to also consider updating the "alteration_timestamp" each time a record is updated and the "backup_table" should be a replica of the original table before alterations.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is a series of SQLite commands that you would run to meet these conditions. SQLite doesn't directly support Date and Time data types, it stores dates internally in a few different formats. However, it has some built-in functions that make it easy to work with dates and times.

Step 1: To Create a Backup table:

```SQLite
CREATE TABLE backup_table AS SELECT * FROM client_base;
```

Step 2: Add column "alteration_timestamp" to client_base

```SQLite
ALTER TABLE client_base ADD COLUMN alteration_timestamp DATETIME;
```

Step 3: Create a Trigger that will update "alteration_timestamp" and copy data to backup_table every time "crypto_wallet" is updated.

```SQLite
CREATE TRIGGER update_crypto_wallet
AFTER UPDATE OF crypto_wallet ON client_base
FOR EACH ROW
BEGIN
   UPDATE client_base SET alteration_timestamp = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP WHERE ROWID = NEW.ROWID;
   INSERT INTO backup_table SELECT * FROM client_base WHERE ROWID = NEW.ROWID;
END;
```

With this implemented, every time you execute an SQL statement that updates the "crypto_wallet" value, your "alteration_timestamp" will be updated with the current timestamp and the entry will be added to the "backup_table". NOTE that trigger names must be unique per database.

Make sure you have the right privileges to execute these commands and bear in mind SQLite may behave differently across different systems and with different versions.
