Develop the logic which given an array of integers, return a single integer which is the sum of all unique elements of the array.
arr = [1,3,3,3,3,7,7,9]
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a Python implementation of the logic:

```python
def sum_of_unique_elements(arr):
    # Create an empty dictionary to store the occurrences of each integer
    dic = {}

    # Iterate over the elements of the array
    for element in arr:
        # If the element is already in the dictionary, increment its count
        if element in dic:
            dic[element] += 1
        # If the element is not in the dictionary, initialize its count to 1
        else:
            dic[element] = 1

    # Initialize the sum to 0
    sum = 0

    # Iterate over the elements of the dictionary
    for key, value in dic.items():
        # If the count of an element is 1 (i.e., it is unique), add it to the sum
        if value == 1:
            sum += key

    # Return the sum
    return sum

# Test the function with the example array
arr = [1,3,3,3,3,7,7,9]
print(sum_of_unique_elements(arr))  # Output: 10
```

This function works by first counting the occurrences of each integer in the array, and then adding up only the integers that occur exactly once (i.e., the unique integers). The time complexity is O(n), where n is the length of the array, because it makes a single pass over the array to count the occurrences, and a single pass over the dictionary to sum the unique elements. The space complexity is O(n) for the dictionary.
<|end|>


