When the content exceeds the tag width, the content will auto break into new line.
How to split the tag content by line?
Expect Result: each line content
Same question but not solved: [Read text within an HTML <p> tag line by line in JavaScript](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/22854975/read-text-within-an-html-p-tag-line-by-line-in-javascript)
<|end|>
<|asst|>
There isn't a direct way to read the text within an HTML tag line by line since HTML doesn't preserve line breaks naturally. However, there are few alternative ways to accomplish this, including:

**1. Using `<br/>` to manually insert line breaks and splitting by it**

This requires manual work (or alternatively server-side scripting) to insert `<br/>` where you want manual breaks.

JS:
```javascript
var text = document.querySelector("p").innerHTML;
var lines = text.split("<br>");
```

HTML:
```html
<p>Line 1<br>Line 2<br>Line 3</p>
```

**2. Using the `white-space: pre-line;` CSS property**

This will force HTML to respect line breaks, but still doesn't give you a way to read the content line by line from JS or read those line breaks directly.

CSS:
```css
p {
  white-space: pre-line;
}
```

HTML:
```html
<p>Line 1
Line 2
Line 3</p>
```

**3. Use JavaScript to measure the height of the element**

This is very complicated and can be inaccurate, but in theory you could compare the height of the full `p` tag to the height of one line of text (by creating a separate `p` element with only one line of text) and estimate the number of lines.

Unfortunately, those are more or less the options available right now. HTML and JS weren't really designed with the intention of being able to read text by lines like this. You could also consider using a `<textarea>` instead of a `p` tag, which does allow for line breaks. This would mean changing your styling though, and wouldn't be a good choice if you don't want users to be able to edit the text.
<|end|>


