Lets start with the first bit which is stripping HTML tags from text with Python and then I will explain how to deal with erroneous tags. 

Here's how to write a function using python and beautifulsoup library.

```python
from bs4 import BeautifulSoup

def strip_html_tags(text):
    soup = BeautifulSoup(text, "html.parser")
    stripped_text = soup.get_text()
    return stripped_text
```
Now, moving forward to handling erroneous tags. A correct solution here strongly depends on what kind of errors are expected to be seen. 

[because trying to parse and clean erroneous HTML files is a problem in itself, e.g fixing a tag mismatch]

Based on the example given, There's an '<i>' tag instead of an '<b>' tag. Now, handling these errors would need human intervention to set rules. In the case of a common error like tag mismatch, BeautifulSoup would handle it appropriately and wouldn't break our function.

Alternatively, you can use python-html-sanitizer package to remove erroneous tags. It will also help you cleanse your HTML to avoid XSS attacks.

```python
from html_sanitizer import Sanitizer

s = Sanitizer()
s.tags = []  # Specify which tags to keep.
s.strip = True  # When tags are removed the contents are kept and added back to the parent tag (default: False).

# This will remove erroneous tags too along with normal tags
clean_html = s.sanitize(your_html)
```

In the solution above, `s.tags = []` specifies to remove all tags and to keep content.

Please note: Without concrete code examples of erroneous tags, it's difficult to provide a concrete solution. To handle erroneous tags, you may need to add special case considerations and potentially use regex pattern matching to flag, remove, or correct any erroneous tags. HTML parsing and cleaning, is not trivial and better tools like BeautifulSoup and python-html-sanitizer are available to do it in a much simpler and efficient way.
