A few months back my team switched our source control over to [Apache Subversion](http://subversion.apache.org/) from [Visual SourceSafe](http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/vs2005/aa718670.aspx), and we haven't been happier.  

Recently I've been looking at [Team Foundation Server](http://tfspreview.com/), and at least on the surface, it seems very impressive.  There is some great integration with Visual Studio, and lots of great tools for DBAs, testers, project managers, etc.

The most obvious difference between these two products is price.  It's hard to beat Apache Subversion (free).  Team Foundation Server is quite expensive, so the extra features would really have to kick Subversion in the pants.

- - -
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Now, while I have been using Subversion, a few of the features from Team Foundation Server that have caught my attention include:

1. **Traceability**: Team Foundation Server provides end-to-end traceability for all work items and source code.
      
2. **Work Item and Bug Tracking**: This feature comes out of the box with Team Foundation Server and gives teams the ability to track all work within a single tool.
 
3. **Continuous Integration**: Team Foundation Server supports continuous integration builds which mean that the build process can be triggered every time someone checks in code.
      
4. **Comprehensive Reporting**: TFS comes with a set of inbuilt reports that provide historical trending, traceability and project management tracking.
   
5. **Integration with Office Tools**: Team Foundation Server connects with various Microsoft applications like Excel and Project to help teams create and manage reports, statistics and work items.
  
6. **Gated Check-In**: This is a feature where you can set up a policy that the code must build without errors before it is committed. This can be very helpful in preventing broken builds.

While Apache Subversion is a popular open-source platform and is widely used for its simplicity and integration with various third-party tools, Team Foundation Server is a end-to-end solution that covers everything from version control, project management to continuous integration and reporting. However, all these features come at a cost.

If your team is on a tight budget, Subversion may still be the better option. If budget is not a factor and you are really willing to go the extra mile for better integration and comprehensive features, then Team Foundation Server is worth considering. It all depends on your specific needs.
<|end|>


