Create a Node.js web server for a simple contact list management application. This server should have routes to allow users to add, update, delete, and list contacts in the database.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is an example of how you could structure a Node.js server using Express.js and a hypothetical database client (like MongoDB, PostgreSQL, etc) for a contact list management application. Let's assume that a "contact" has a unique "id", a "name", "email", and "phone" number:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

// Hypothetical database client
const db = require('./db');

app.get('/contacts', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const contacts = await db.getContacts();
    res.json(contacts);
  } catch (err) {
    res.status(500).send('Error retrieving contacts');
  }
});

app.post('/contacts', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const { name, email, phone } = req.body;
    const newContact = await db.addContact(name, email, phone);
    res.json(newContact);
  } catch (err) {
    res.status(500).send('Error adding contact');
  }
});

app.put('/contacts/:id', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const { name, email, phone } = req.body;
    const updatedContact = await db.updateContact(req.params.id, name, email, phone);
    res.json(updatedContact);
  } catch (err) {
    res.status(500).send('Error updating contact');
  }
});

app.delete('/contacts/:id', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    await db.deleteContact(req.params.id);
    res.status(204).send();
  } catch (err) {
    res.status(500).send('Error deleting contact');
  }
});

const port = process.env.PORT || 3000;
app.listen(port, () => console.log(`Server is listening on port ${port}`));
```

This above server uses Express.js, a popular web framework for Node.js. We use `app.get()`, `app.post()`, `app.put()`, and `app.delete()` to create HTTP routes for listing, creating, updating, and deleting contacts, respectively. We utilize `req.body` to access JSON data sent in a request, and `req.params.id` to access the "id" parameter in the URL path.

Note: This code is just a basic example and doesn't include validation or error handling that a real-world application should have. Also, the methods `db.getContacts()`, `db.addContact()`, `db.updateContact()`, and `db.deleteContact()` are just placeholder methods and need a real implementation based on the selected database client.
<|end|>


