I declared a float variable, say `a`, then I assigned 57.00 to it. When I wanted to print the 'a' using  object, the stream seemed to print only 57 without the decimal point.
So, the solution to making the decimal point printed is adding  manipulator after cout. Example: `cout << showpoint << fixed << setprecision(2) << a << endl;`
The function  here is used together with  to specify the number of digits to be printed after the decimal point. After googling for a while, I found that  and  are a part of `<ios>` header file.
So, why don't we have to import this header? Without importing it, the code worked just fine.
And importing `<iostream>` is all I need to make these stream manipulators worked as intended.
Is `<ios>` header automatically imported whenever and wherever the `<iostream>` header is imported?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, the `<ios>` header file is indeed included when you include `<iostream>`. 

When you use `#include <iostream>`, the compiler includes the code from `iostream` which itself includes the code from `<ios>` along with other necessary header files like `<streambuf>`, `<istream>`, `<ostream>` and `<iosfwd>`. 

That's why you can use functions and manipulators defined in `<ios>` without explicitly including the `<ios>` header file when you have already included `<iostream>`. However, it's not a bad idea to include `<ios>` explicitly if your code directly depends on it, in case the implementation of `<iostream>` changes in the future.
<|end|>


