Generate a HTML page with a table of contents that links to sections on the same page. Additionally, implement a feature that highlights the current section in the table of contents as the user scrolls through the page.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Creating a page with such features requires adequate knowledge of HTML, CSS, and JavaScript or jQuery (a fast, small, and feature-rich JavaScript library). Here's an example of how you can implement that:

HTML:

```HTML
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title>Page with TOC</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="style.css">
  </head>
  <body>
    <div id="toc">
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#Section1">Section 1</a></li>
        <li><a href="#Section2">Section 2</a></li>
        <li><a href="#Section3">Section 3</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <main>
      <section id="Section1">
        <h1>Section 1</h1>
        <p>Content of section 1</p>
      </section>
      <section id="Section2">
        <h1>Section 2</h1>
        <p>Content of section 2</p>
      </section>
      <section id="Section3">
        <h1>Section 3</h1>
        <p>Content of section 3</p>
      </section>
    </main>
    <script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.6.0.min.js"></script>
    <script src="script.js"></script>
  </body>
</html>
```

CSS (style.css):

```CSS
#toc {
  position: fixed;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
}

#toc ul li a {
  color: black;
}

#toc ul li a.highlighted {
  color: red;
}
```

JavaScript (script.js):

```JavaScript
jQuery(document).ready(function($) {
  var alterClass = function() {
    var scroll_pos = $(window).scrollTop();
    $('section').each(function() {
      var curr_section = $(this);
      var section_top = curr_section.offset().top;
      var id = curr_section.attr('id');
      var nav_btn = $('#toc a[href^="#'+id+'"]');
      if (scroll_pos >= section_top) {
        $('#toc a').removeClass('highlighted');
        nav_btn.addClass('highlighted');
      }
    });
  };
  $(window).scroll(alterClass);
  alterClass();
});
```

This example adheres to your requirements. The JavaScript code:

- grabs the vertical scroll position.
- checks which section is currently visible on screen (based on scroll position).
- alters the 'highlighted' class of table of content links appropriately.

But remember that DOM manipulation on every scroll event such as this can lead to performance issues. You might consider using a library like Lodash to 'throttle' or 'debounce' the function that is executed on scroll. In real-world use, it's best to investigate performance and ensure it meets your needs.
<|end|>


