Write a program in Java that accepts multiple pairs of integers as input and calculates their least common multiple. Your solution should implement and make use of Euclidean algorithm to find the greatest common divisor as part of your least common multiple calculation. Additionally, your program should be able to handle and validate any wrong inputs such as negative numbers, zero and non-integer inputs.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Below is a simple Java code which solves the task. The following program uses Euclidean algorithm for the calculation of the greatest common divisor (GCD) and then finds the least common multiple (LCM) using the formula (a*b)/gcd(a,b).

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        String input;
        while(true) {
            try {
                System.out.println("Enter a pair of integers (or type 'exit' to finish): ");
                input = scanner.nextLine();
                if(input.equalsIgnoreCase("exit")) break;

                String[] pair = input.split(" ");
                int num1 = Integer.parseInt(pair[0]);
                int num2 = Integer.parseInt(pair[1]);

                if(num1 <= 0 || num2 <= 0) {
                    System.out.println("Error! Only positive integers are allowed.");
                    continue;
                }

                int gcd = gcd(num1, num2);
                int lcm = (num1 * num2) / gcd;

                System.out.println("LCM of " + num1 + " and " + num2 + " is: " + lcm);
            }
            catch(NumberFormatException ex){
                System.out.println("Error! Only integer values are allowed.");
            }
            catch(IndexOutOfBoundsException ex){
                System.out.println("Error! Provide two integers.");
            }
        }
    }

    private static int gcd(int a, int b) {
        if (b == 0) return a;
        return gcd(b, a % b);
    }
}
```
The above program will keep accepting pairs of integers from the user until the user types 'exit'. For each pair, the program calculates and prints their LCM. Any invalid inputs like negative numbers, zero, non-integer inputs, or a wrong number of inputs are handled and the user is warned with an appropriate error message.
<|end|>


