I have hierarchical time series data and I generate forecasts at the lowest level in the hierarchy. I am looking for a method to calculate the error of time series forecasts on each level in the hierarchy.
Let's assume that the hierarchy is as follows:
City - Region - Country - Continent
I have forecasts for each city and I calculate MAE of my forecasts on this level. How should I calculate the error on the Region level? Should take the weighted average of the MAEs calculated on city level or should I calculate the forecasts on Region level first(by summing up the forecasts on city level) and then calculate the MAE?
I hope my question is understandable. Thank you
<|end|>
<|asst|>
If you want to measure the forecast error on each level in the hierarchy, you should first aggregate the forecasts for each level and then calculate its Mean Absolute Error (MAE). So for the Region level, you would first sum up the forecasts for all cities within a region, then compare those sums to the actual data for the region, and then calculate the MAE.

Taking a weighted average of the MAEs at the city level will give you an indication of how good your forecasts are at the city level, but it doesn't necessarily reflect how accurate your regional forecasts are, because it doesn't take into account the correlations and shifting patterns between cities within a region.

So, calculate forecasts on each hierarchical level first (e.g., Region level = sum of forecasts on City level) and then calculate the MAE. This way, you are properly accounting for the hierarchical nature of your data.
<|end|>


