I'm not clear on the differences between the "current" version of Ruby (1.8) and the "new" version (1.9). Is there an "easy" or a "simple" explanation of the differences and why it is so different?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, I'll try to provide a simplified explanation. When a programming language like Ruby evolves from one major version to another, the changes can be quite significant. Though parts of the syntax and the language structure remain the same, many new features are incorporated and old functionalities are updated or removed. 

Here are the most significant differences between Ruby 1.8 and Ruby 1.9:

1. Syntax and Language Features: Ruby 1.9 introduced several new language features and improved syntax related to block variables, lambda functions, and case statements. Further, Ruby 1.9 makes "print" statements private.

2. Speed and Performance: Ruby 1.9 introduced a new execution environment called YARV (Yet Another RubyVM), which significantly improved the execution speed of Ruby programs.

3. Character Encoding: Ruby 1.8 used ASCII as default character encoding while Ruby 1.9 changed this to support multiple encodings, which is very useful as it allows internationalization of Ruby applications.

4. String and Regular Expression: In Ruby 1.9, Strings become encoding aware, which is a departure from Ruby 1.8 where a String was just a sequence of bytes.

5. Library and Built-in Methods: Ruby 1.9 added several new libraries and built-in methods, also some methods in Ruby 1.8 were removed in 1.9.

6. Hashes and Ordering: In Ruby 1.9, hashes maintain their order, which was not the case in Ruby 1.8.

Remember, these changes were made to advance the capabilities of Ruby to match other contemporary languages, and yet retain its simplicity and elegance. Although transitioning from Ruby 1.8 to 1.9 might initially cause some compatibility issues, in the long run, it allowed Ruby to become more powerful and versatile.
<|end|>


