I writing a report in Visual Studio that takes a user input parameter and runs against an ODBC datasource. I would like to write the query manually and have reporting services replace part of the where clause with the parameter value before sending it to the database. What seems to be happening is that the `@parmName` I am assuming will be replaced is actually being sent as part of the SQL statement. Am I missing a configuration setting somewhere or is this simply not possible?

I am not using the filter option in the tool because this appears to bring back the full dataset from the database and do the filtering on the SQL Server.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
For Visual Studio and SQL Server Reporting Services (SSRS) to replace the `@parmName` with the actual value in the SQL query, you need to specify the parameter in the SQL query itself.

Here's an example:

```sql
SELECT 
    column1,
    column2,
    column3
FROM 
    YourTable
WHERE
    column1 = @parmName -- this is where the parameter is used
```

Then in the Parameters section of the dataset, you need to add a parameter with the 'Name' as `parmName` (without the @ sign) and specify that it is a 'Text' datatype. In the 'Value' field for the parameter, you can specify the value as `[@parmName]` (since this is how you fed it through a user input).

This should replace the `@parmName` in your SQL query with the actual value before executing the SQL statement. 

The tool doesn't substitute the parameter value in the SQL string - it binds it to the statement when it's actually executed. 

Also, make sure you have the `"Use single transaction when processing the queries"` box checked in the dataset properties. That can sometimes cause issues with parameters not being recognized correctly if it's not checked. 

The filter option you mentioned would indeed perform the filtering on the SQL server, which may not be efficient if you are working with a large dataset, so passing the parameter value directly in the SQL query is a good alternative.
<|end|>


