<|asst|>
Creating a reflection effect in SVG involves creating a slightly transparent, upside-down version of the original shape or image. We can achieve this by using the `scale` and `translate` transformations along with gradient fills for making the reflection fade away.

Here is an example of how you can create a reflection:

```html
<svg width="32" height="64" viewBox="0 0 32 64" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
    <g id="main">
        <!-- Your original paths here -->
        <path /* ...original path attributes... */ />
    </g>
    <defs>
        <linearGradient id="Gradient" x1="0" x2="0" y1="0" y2="1">
            <stop offset="0%" stop-color="#9B9B9B" stop-opacity="0.5"/>
            <stop offset="100%" stop-color="#9B9B9B" stop-opacity="0"/>
        </linearGradient>
    </defs>
    <use id="reflection" xlink:href="#main" transform="scale(1, -1) translate(0, -64)" fill="url(#Gradient)"/>
</svg>
```

This will create a gradient that goes from semi-transparent (#9B9B9B with opacity 0.5) to fully transparent (#9B9B9B with opacity 0), applied to the reflected version of your SVG path.

You will need to adjust the viewBox, height, and translate values to make sure the reflection aligns correctly with the original image. Also remember to replace the "/* ...original path attributes... */" with your original path attributes. 

Please note that this is a simplified example and might not work perfectly with complex SVG shapes.
<|end|>


