I'm planning on creating a game that contains a landscape with objects on it. The landscape will be defined using a heightfield, and the objects will move about on top of, and fly over the terrain. If you've ever played the old games [Marble Madness](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marble_Madness) and [Virus/Zarch](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zarch), that's the kind of complexity and style I'm trying to create.

I've seen various physics engines on the Internet, and a few tutorials about how to render heightfields as terrain, but they either lack documentation or seem overly complex for what I need.

All I need is a way to draw a heightfield, place 3D objects on it and then make them roll down the hills, or fly around in the sky. I don't mind making my own code to do this, so any relevant tutorials would be good too.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
It sounds like you're looking for a balance between simplicity and realism. While full physics engines like PhysX, Bullet or Havok could certainly handle this, they could be overkill and will require quite a bit of learning and setup.

For terrain creation, a common approach is to use a heightmap, which is effectively a grayscale image where the brightness of each pixel represents the height of the terrain at that spot. There are plenty of tutorials available online on how you can handle such terrain rendering; here are a few good ones:

1. [Lighthouse 3D’s Terrain Tutorial](http://www.lighthouse3d.com/opengl/terrain/): This tutorial focuses on building a heightmap-based terrain in OpenGL. It discusses heightmap theory and gives you a practical, step by step guide to implement it in OpenGL.

2. [Riemer’s Heightmap Tutorial](http://www.riemers.net/eng/Tutorials/DirectX/Csharp/Series1/terraintutorial.php): This tutorial focuses on Direct X/C#, but the main concepts should be applicable in any language/graphics API.

For collision detection and rolling physics, you could use a more simplified physics engine like BEPU, depending upon the realism you want. You need to check if an object is colliding with the terrain, and if so, respond accordingly (like rolling down the hill, etc.). Here's a simple [tutorial](http://stemkoski.github.io/Three.js/Collision-Detection.html) on basic collision detection in Three.js which might help.

To make objects fly around in the sky, you would simply update their position over time according to some predetermined flight path, or use a simplex noise to create more realistic, random flight paths.

That said, if you are open to game engines, Unity or Unreal Engine are power-packed with these functionalities, and their vast communities have numerous relevant tutorials that would be easy to follow. They might save you from coding everything from scratch.

Remember, with either approach, you will have to deal with a math-heavy subject, and understanding concepts like vectors and matrices is essential. Good luck with your game development project!
<|end|>


