Provided with the extensive corpus of data pertaining to SARS-CoV-2 infection incidence, execute the necessary protocols to procure said data corpus and furnish a concise, factual narrative describing data elements. Subsequently, propose an innovative, yet efficacious, data visualization technique capable of accurately encapsulating the progressive escalation in infection numbers.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Data Procurement:

The data related to SARS-CoV-2 (COVID-19) can be acquired from multiple sources: 

1. Government Health Departments: Almost all national and regional governments maintain a daily record of COVID-19 cases in their jurisdiction. Data from these sources can be useful but might lack standardization compared to other sources.
   
2. Global databases: Organizations such as the World Health Organization (WHO) and John Hopkins University provide a comprehensive global repository of COVID-19 data.
   
3. COVID-19 Data APIs: Several organizations and individuals have created APIs that allow data to be pulled in a structured and standardized manner. Examples of this include the COVID-19 Data API provided by about-corona.net and the API provided by covid19api.com.

Data Narrative:

The data on SARS-CoV-2 infection rates typically contain elements such as:

1. Date: The date when the count was taken.

2. Geographical information: This could be the name of the country, state, or city where the cases were reported.

3. Case count: The total number of confirmed cases up to that date.

4. Death count: The total number of confirmed deaths related to infection up to that date.

5. Recovered count: The number of individuals reported to have recovered from the infection up to that date.

6. Active cases: The number of ongoing active cases on that particular date.

Proposed Data Visualization:

One way to effectively display the progression of the infection rate is through a time series plot, where the x-axis represents time (in days, weeks, or months) and the y-axis represents the number of confirmed cases.

1. Animated Time-Series Map: An innovative way to visualize the data could include an animated time-series map where each country (or region) changes color intensity based on the count of confirmed cases over time. 

2. Multi-Level Treemap: A treemap offers a hierarchical view of your data and makes it easy to spot patterns, such as which items are a store's best sellers. The tree branches are represented by rectangles and each sub-branch is shown as a smaller rectangle.

3. Heatmaps: Heatmaps are another innovative visual representation. The data can be plotted on a grid where each cell's color represents the number of cases.

4. Interactive Curve Graph: Provide an interactive curve graph which enables the viewers to select their region of interest to see the infection rate.

5. Augmented Reality (AR): This is the most innovative and immersive method to represent and explore the COVID-19 data. It helps the users to examine the data by moving, stretching, and compressing 3D visualizations.

Creating innovative visualizations not only helps in grasping the situation quickly but also assists policymakers and researchers in analyzing the patterns and making judgments. Remember, while creating these visualizations, always consider the right color palette, scaling, orientation and labeling to enhance comprehension.
<|end|>


