Compose an assembly language code sequence to evaluate the equivalence or disparity of two supplied integers.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, below is a very basic assembly language code using NASM to compare two integers. Please note that you should adjust the code based on your OS and assembler:

```asm
section .data
    num1 db 10 ;first number to compare
    num2 db 20 ;second number to compare
    equal db 'Numbers are equal',0
    unequal db 'Numbers are not equal',0

section .text
    global _start

_start:
    ; load the first number to register AL
    mov al, [num1]

    ; compare AL with the second number
    cmp al, [num2]

    ; if they are equal, jump to equal block
    je equal_block

    ; they are not equal, print the message and jump to exit
    mov eax, 4 ; system call number (sys_write)
    mov ebx, 1 ; file descriptor (stdout)
    mov ecx, unequal ; message to print
    mov edx, 19 ; message length
    int 0x80 ; call kernel
    jmp exit

equal_block:
    ; they are equal, print the message
    mov eax, 4 ; system call number (sys_write)
    mov ebx, 1 ; file descriptor (stdout)
    mov ecx, equal ; message to print
    mov edx, 16 ; message length
    int 0x80 ; call kernel

exit:
    ; cleanup and exit
    mov eax, 1 ; system call number (sys_exit)
    xor ebx, ebx ; exit code
    int 0x80 ; call kernel
```

This program assumes that the numbers are in the range of 0 to 255 (as it uses 8-bit registers), and that int 0x80 will handle system calls on your operating system. It prints 'Numbers are equal' if the two numbers are equal and 'Numbers are not equal', otherwise. Consider it as the basic way to compare integers and print messages, more advanced and better techniques can be performed as per requirement.

Remember, Assembly language is dependent on architecture. It is not universally applicable to all systems. This example works for systems that support the Netwide Assembler (NASM) with an x86 architecture.
<|end|>


