Create a web application using ASP.NET Core that can access external weather data for a given city.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To create this application, you need to integrate a weather data API into your application. Here we will be using OpenWeatherMap API because it's free and provides useful weather data.

Step 1. Create a new ASP.Net Core project

Open Visual Studio > Create a new project > ASP.NET Core Web Application > Name it as per your preference > Web Application.

Step 2. Install necessary Nuget packages

To call OpenWeatherMap API from your application, a helpful Nuget package to install is `Microsoft.AspNet.WebApi.Client`. Open Package Manager console (Tools > NuGet Package Manager > Package Manager Console) then enter:

```dotnetcli
Install-Package Microsoft.AspNet.WebApi.Client
```

Step 3. Get your API Key

Go to https://openweathermap.org/, sign up and get your API key.

Step 4. Create a WeatherService.cs file in the root directory

This file encapsulates the call to the OpenWeatherMap API. 

```csharp
public class WeatherService
{
	private readonly HttpClient _httpClient;
	private const string ApiKey = "<Your_API_Key>";
	private const string BaseUrl = "http://api.openweathermap.org/data/2.5";

	public WeatherService(HttpClient httpClient)
	{
		_httpClient = httpClient;
	}

	public async Task<WeatherForecast> GetForecastAsync(string city)
	{
		var response = await _httpClient.GetAsync($"{BaseUrl}/weather?q={city}&appid={ApiKey}&units=metric");

		response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();

		return await response.Content.ReadAsAsync<WeatherForecast>();
	}
}
```

Step 5. Create WeatherForecast.cs file

This class represents the data that the API returns.

```csharp
public class WeatherForecast
{
    public Main Main { get; set; }
    public List<Weather> Weather { get; set; }
}

public class Main
{
    public double Temp { get; set; }
}

public class Weather
{
    public string Description { get; set; }
}
```

Step 6. Create a Controller 

Create a `WeatherController` with Action `Index` which takes city as input and calls the `GetForecastAsync` method to get weather data.

```csharp
public class WeatherController : Controller
{
    private readonly WeatherService _weatherService;

    public WeatherController(WeatherService weatherService)
    {
        _weatherService = weatherService;
    }

    public async Task<IActionResult> Index(string city)
    {
        var weather = await _weatherService.GetForecastAsync(city);

        return View(weather);
    }
}
```

Step 7. Create a View

Create an Index.cshtml view under Views > Weather and display the data accordingly.

```html
@model WeatherForecast

<div>
    <h3>@Model.Main.Temp &deg;C</h3>
    <h3>@Model.Weather[0].Description</h3>
</div>
```

Step 8. Register WeatherService

In Startup.cs, register `WeatherService` in the `ConfigureServices` method, so it can be injected into the controllers using ASP.NET Core's dependency injection.

```csharp
public void ConfigureServices(IServiceCollection services)
{
    services.AddHttpClient<WeatherService>();
    services.AddControllersWithViews();
}
```

Step 9. Run the Application

Now, you can run the application. Navigate to /Weather?city=<> and see the weather data for the city you input.
<|end|>


