Here is a streamlined version of your initial code using list comprehension, which reduces redundancy:

```python
def sum_of_lists(lists):
  return sum([item for sublist in lists for item in sublist])
```

It does the same thing as your original code but in less lines. It first iterates through the lists, then through each element in each list, then sums all the elements. It is equivalent to your original code but is more "Pythonic", reduces redundancy, and performs quicker.
