Building a responsive and dynamic speech bubble involves creating HTML, CSS and a bit of JavaScript. Here's a simple example:

HTML:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Dynamic Speech Bubble</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>
    <div id="speech-bubble" class="speech-bubble">
        <span id="bubble-text">Hello World!</span>
    </div>
    <input type="text" id="user-input" placeholder="Type your text here">
    <button onclick="changeText()">Change Text</button>

    <script src="app.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

CSS (`style.css`):
```css
.speech-bubble {
    position: relative;
    background: #00aabb;
    border-radius: .4em;
    width: fit-content;
    padding: 10px;
    color: white;
    margin-bottom: 1.5em;
}

.speech-bubble:after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    right: 0;
    top: 50%;
    width: 0;
    height: 0;
    border: 20px solid transparent;
    border-left-color: #00aabb;
    border-right: 0;
    border-bottom: 0;
    margin-top: -10px;
    margin-right: -20px;
}

@media (max-width: 800px) {
  .speech-bubble {
    width: 80%;
  }
}
```

JavaScript (`app.js`):
```javascript
function changeText() {
    var userInput = document.getElementById('user-input').value;
    document.getElementById('bubble-text').innerHTML = userInput;
}
```

This code first defines a speech bubble in HTML with an associated CSS style, and also includes an input field and a button. When the button is clicked, the JavaScript code captures the value from the input field and changes the text inside the speech bubble to match this input.
