I am new in Vue.js. There is my question:
I try this:

<li v-for="(msg,index) in system_message" :class="index">


in order to create different className like 0,1,2,3 for every li element.
but v-bind does not work in this way. class name remain empty.
how do I use setAttribute function in Vue?
thanks!
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Actually, the way you're trying to use the `v-bind` directive is correct; however, the issue is related to the CSS class naming convention. 

The class name that starts with a number is not considered valid in CSS. This is the reason why you're experiencing this issue, Vue.js or any other framework or library will make sure that the class names are valid and hence you're not seeing class names like 0, 1, 2 assigned to your elements.

Fortunately, Vue provides several ways to bind class styles dynamically.

One alternative is to prefix the index with string like this:

```html
<li v-for="(msg,index) in system_message" :class="'class-' + index">
```

This will generate classes like class-0, class-1, class-2, etc.

Another way is to use Object Syntax for class binding in this case:

```html
<li v-for="(msg,index) in system_message" :class="{[`index-${index}`]: true}">
```

This will generate classes like index-0, index-1, index-2, etc. Notice that to interpolate the index variable into the string, we're using backticks that allow us to use the `${}` brackets for variable interpolation. This is a feature of JavaScript ES6 template literals.

In short, you do not need `setAttribute` function in Vue.js as Vue provides its own ways (like `v-bind` or `:` shortcut) to bind attributes or class styles.
<|end|>


