Using p5js a circle gets created:

EarthSize = createVector(width/3, height/3);
Eclipse(200, 200, EarthSize.x, EarthSize.y);


I want to get the size in radius of the circle.
off course I can use width/3 similar to what the vector is using but can I get this directly from the EarthSize vector?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The EarthSize vector contains the x and y coordinates used to draw the eclipse (circle in this case), not its radius. Since the width and height are divided by 3, it means that the drawn circle's diameter is one third of the canvas size. In p5.js, the ellipse function's third and fourth parameters refer to width and height, or in case of circle - diameter.

So to get the radius, you'll still have to use the diameter scaled down.

```jsx
let radius = EarthSize.x / 2; 
```

In your example, `EarthSize.x` and `EarthSize.y` both give the diameter, and dividing by 2 will yield the radius. You can't get the radius directly from the EarthSize vector, because it's just holding data about diameter (as used to draw the circle). 

In vector graphics (like p5.js) an ellipse will be drawn from the center, rather than the top left. That's why the diameter is calculated first, because internally it'll draw the shape in both directions from the center.
<|end|>


