Can you tell me what is the difference between  and  in software development?

I am confused. Abstraction hides detail implementation and
information hiding abstracts whole details of something.

 I found a good answer for these three concepts. [See the separate answer below](https://stackoverflow.com/a/8694874/240733) for several citations taken from [there](http://web.archive.org/web/20080906224409/http://www.itmweb.com/essay550.htm).
<|end|>
<|asst|>
"Abstraction" and "Information Hiding" are somewhat related concepts in software development. 

Abstraction, in context of software development, is a process where simple, intuitive, user-oriented concepts are created from complex system details. Abstractions provide a simplified picture of a system by removing or suppressing details that are not relevant for certain tasks or purposes. This means you minimize the complexity of the system by focusing on a higher level of idea. For example, when you style a text to be bold in Word software, you are not concerned with how the software implements this feature in the background, because it's abstracted away from you; you just know that the feature will make the text bold.

Information hiding, on the other hand, is a process of enclosing the details of an object and revealing only what is necessary. This principle prevents data from unnecessary access by the user. For example, encapsulation in object-oriented programming is a way of information hiding, where the details of a class are hidden and an interface is exposed for the users to interact with that class. 

In summary, abstraction is more about simplifying complex systems by representing them with higher-level ideas while information hiding is about encapsulating the details of an object and exposing only necessary parts to make the system safer and easier to use.
<|end|>


