Here's the equivalent Lua code:

```lua
-- Define a function to implement bubble sort on a given array
function bubbleSort(arr)
    local len = #arr

    -- Bubble Sort (Repeatedly swapping the adjacent elements if they are in wrong order)
    for i = 1, len do
        for j = 1, len-i do
            if arr[j] > arr[j+1] then 
                arr[j], arr[j+1] = arr[j+1], arr[j] -- Swap the elements
            end
        end
    end

    return arr
end

-- Define the function to rearrange the sorted list in a Unique BST sort order
function unique_BST_sort(lst)
    local sorted_list = bubbleSort(lst) -- Sort the original list
    local result = {} -- Buffer to hold the resulting list

    -- Continue until the sorted list is empty
    while #sorted_list > 0 do
        table.insert(result, table.remove(sorted_list, 1)) -- Add and remove first element from sorted list to result

        -- If there are still elements left in the sorted list
        if #sorted_list > 0 then
            table.insert(result, table.remove(sorted_list)) -- Add and remove last element from sorted list to result
        end
    end

    return result
end
```

This Lua script performs bubble sort first on the input list, and then rearranges it according to the logic specified in your Python function. Please note that Lua uses 1-based indexing as opposed to Python's 0-based indexing. Also, instead of Python's `pop()`, Lua's `table.remove()` function is used to remove and return a specific element from the table.
<|end|>


