Describe and provide a pseudocode of the following heuristic optimization algorithm. Modify the algorithm to incorporate a condition to handle the problem of local maximum in complex landscapes. 
[Algorithm Name] 
Advanced Hill Climbing Algorithm
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Algorithm Description:
The Advanced Hill Climbing Algorithm is a modern version of the classic hill-climbing algorithm found in artificial intelligence and optimization problems. This heuristic algorithm starts with a random solution, then attempts to systematically find a better solution by changing a constituent element of the solution. If this operation leads to a superior solution, an incremental change is made to the new solution. The process is repeated until no more improvements can be made, at which point the algorithm has reached the local maximum. Given that this method is susceptible to getting stuck at local maxima, alterations may be included to solve for more complex landscapes.

Algorithm Pseudocode:
```
Procedure AdvancedHillClimbing(problem)
    current <- makeNode(problem.initialState)
    loop do
        neighbor <- a randomly selected successor of current
        if neighbor has no successors then 
            return current.state
        if neighbor.value > current.value then 
            current <- neighbor
    end loop
EndProcedure
```
Problem of Local Maximum:

The local maximum problem occurs when the algorithm converges onto a solution that is the most optimal within the immediate vicinity (the local maximum), but not necessarily the most optimal overall (the global maximum). To overcome this issue, it is suggested to include a condition in the pseudocode that allows occasional transitions to lesser-valued states, with the probability of these transitions decreasing over time. This strategy is known as "simulated annealing".

Modified Pseudocode with Simulated Annealing:
```
Procedure AdvancedHillClimbing(problem)
    current <- makeNode(problem.initialState)
    T <- a large number
    ratio <- a small positive fraction
    loop do
        T <- T * ratio   # Decrease the temperature
        if T is very small then 
            return current.state
        neighbor <- a randomly selected successor of current 
        ΔE <- neighbor.value - current.value
        if ΔE > 0 then 
            current <- neighbor
        else if random() < exp(ΔE / T) then 
            current <- neighbor
    end loop
EndProcedure
```

In the modified pseudocode, T represents the initial temperature (set to a large value) which gradually decreases by a ratio over time (simulating the process of annealing). The if-statement with the random number and the exponential function allows the algorithm to sometimes transition to states with lower values to avoid getting stuck at local maximum.
<|end|>


